/* AQA GCSE Religious Studies A (8062) — room kits built by hand in a Claude Code session: written by
   Opus 5 subagents to the contract in src/kit-validator.js, every question re-solved and the lesson read against
   the specification map by a fresh Opus 5 subagent before shipping. See .claude/skills/course-builder/references/depth.md. */
module.exports = { ID: 'AQA-8062', KITS: {"3.1.1":{"id":"AQA-8062","topic":"3.1.1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Buddhism is one of the seven religions Component 1 sets, and a student who chooses it answers two five-part questions on it: one on Beliefs and teachings and one on Practices, 24 marks each, with the three SPaG marks riding on the Beliefs evaluation. Everything in this room is examined through the same five parts - a tick, two bare points, two contrasts, two teachings with a source, and a twelve-mark judgement - so the work is to hold the Dhamma, the Buddha's life, the Four Noble Truths, worship, meditation, death rites, festivals and Buddhist ethics precisely enough to name a Theravada and a Mahayana view of the same thing and say where each comes from.","idea":[{"h":"Sources, influence and divergence: the three demands behind every question","code":"3.1.1 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Buddhism","t":"Three standing demands sit behind every Buddhism question. Sources: every belief and practice must be traced to a Buddhist source of wisdom and authority, and the fifth mark of the five-mark question is given for exactly that - a prescribed text such as Dhammapada 190-191 or Jataka 075, the Three Refuges, the five precepts as a recited formula, or the teaching of a named tradition. Influence: what believers actually do, give, avoid or gather for because of the belief. Divergence: Theravada, Mahayana, Zen and Pure Land are named in the specification, so 'Buddhists believe' is never a safe sentence. A top-level answer names the tradition, cites the source and says what follows for a Buddhist's life."},{"h":"Dhamma (Dharma): the teaching and the truth it describes","code":"3.1.1.1 — The concept of Dhamma (Dharma)","t":"Dhamma means two things at once: the body of teaching the Buddha gave, and the way things actually are, which that teaching describes. It is not revelation from a god - Buddhists say the Buddha discovered it rather than invented it, and that it would hold whether or not a Buddha ever taught it. That is why Dhamma is the second of the Three Refuges, alongside the Buddha and the Sangha, and why everything that follows in Key Beliefs - dependent arising, the Three Marks, the Four Noble Truths - is content of the Dhamma rather than a separate topic. A top-level answer uses Dhamma as a frame: it explains a belief as part of one account of reality rather than as an isolated doctrine."},{"h":"Dependent arising (paticcasamupada): nothing stands on its own","code":"3.1.1.1 — The concept of dependent arising (paticcasamupada)","t":"Everything that exists arises because of conditions and ceases when those conditions cease, so nothing has independent or permanent existence of its own; the Pali formula is usually summarised as 'when this is, that comes to be'. Two consequences matter for the exam. It underwrites anicca and anatta: what depends on changing conditions cannot be permanent, and a person analysed this way has no fixed self behind the parts. And it makes the third Noble Truth possible: if dukkha arises from craving, then removing craving removes dukkha, which is what the Buddhist path attempts. A top-level answer uses dependent arising as a reason rather than a label, explaining why a belief follows instead of only naming the term."},{"h":"The Three Marks of Existence: anicca, anatta, dukkha","code":"3.1.1.1 — The Three Marks of Existence: anicca, anatta, dukkha","t":"Anicca is impermanence: everything conditioned changes, so nothing can be held. Anatta is no fixed self: what a person calls 'I' is a changing process, analysed in Theravada as the five aggregates, with no unchanging soul standing behind them. Dukkha covers both plain suffering - birth, illness, old age, death - and the wider unsatisfactoriness of a life built on things that will not last. The three are stated as facts about all conditioned existence rather than as opinions about it, which is why they are called marks and not beliefs. A top-level answer shows how they interlock: because things are impermanent and are not a self, clinging to them cannot satisfy, so dukkha follows."},{"h":"The person: Five Aggregates in Theravada, sunyata and Buddha-nature in Mahayana","code":"3.1.1.1 — The human personality, in the Theravada and Mahayana traditions","t":"Theravada analyses the person into the five aggregates (skandhas): form, sensation, perception, mental formations and consciousness. There is no sixth thing, no self, behind them - the person is the bundle, working together and changing constantly. Mahayana pushes further with sunyata, emptiness: not only persons but all things lack inherent, independent existence, and because nothing is fixed in what it is, all beings carry Buddha-nature and the possibility of attaining Buddhahood. The specification names this as one of the differences that must be studied, so it is prime material for 'Explain two contrasting'. A top-level answer sets the two side by side, names the tradition holding each, and does not blend them into one Buddhist view."},{"h":"Human destiny: Arhat, Bodhisattva, Buddhahood and the Pure Land","code":"3.1.1.1 — Human destiny","t":"Theravada holds up the Arhat, a 'perfected person' who has destroyed craving, reached nibbana and will not be reborn; the goal is reached by personal effort, and Dhammapada 276, that the Buddhas only point the way, is the usual support. Mahayana holds up the Bodhisattva, who vows to become a Buddha for the sake of all beings and postpones final release to help them - Avalokiteshvara for compassion, Manjushri for wisdom, Tara in Tibetan traditions. Beyond both stands Buddhahood itself, and Pure Land Buddhists trust Amitabha (Amida) Buddha for rebirth in the Pure Land, where Enlightenment becomes attainable. A top-level answer weighs self-effort against compassion for others rather than listing the ideals."},{"h":"The Buddha's life: luxury, the Four Sights, asceticism, Enlightenment","code":"3.1.1.1 — The Buddha's life and its significance","t":"Siddhartha Gautama was born into a ruling family in northern India and grew up in luxury, shielded from suffering. Riding out, he saw the Four Sights the specification lists - illness, old age, death and a holy man, prescribed with Jataka 075 - and left the palace to find an answer. Six years of extreme asceticism with five companions nearly killed him and taught him nothing, so he ate again and took the Middle Way between indulgence and self-denial. Under the Bodhi tree at Bodh Gaya he reached Enlightenment and became the Buddha, the awakened one, and preached his first sermon in the Deer Park at Sarnath. A top-level answer reads each stage as an argument: luxury does not satisfy, the Sights show dukkha is universal, asceticism fails too, and the Middle Way works."},{"h":"The Four Noble Truths and the Eightfold Path","code":"3.1.1.1 — The Four Noble Truths","t":"Dukkha: life as lived is unsatisfactory, and the specification asks for different types - ordinary pain, the suffering of change, and the deeper unease of conditioned existence. Samudaya: the cause is craving (tanha), driven by the Three Poisons of ignorance, greed and hate, shown as pig, cockerel and snake at the hub of the Wheel of Life. Nirodha: end the craving and dukkha ends, in nibbana, which Buddhists interpret in more than one way - the blowing out of the fires of craving, and a state beyond ordinary description. Magga: the Eightfold Path, grouped as the Threefold Way of ethics (sila), meditation (samadhi) and wisdom (panna), and set beside the refuges at Dhammapada 190-191. A top-level answer treats the Truths as a diagnosis and a cure, and takes the plural in 'interpretations of nibbana' seriously."},{"h":"Temples, shrines, viharas and gompas","code":"3.1.1.2 — The nature, use and importance of Buddhist places of worship","t":"The specification asks three things of each place: its nature, its use and its importance. A temple is a public building for devotion and teaching; a shrine may be a room in a temple or a shelf at home; a vihara is a monastery where monks and nuns live and where lay Buddhists come for teaching, festivals and to give alms; a gompa is a Tibetan hall for meditation and learning. The named features must appear: the Buddha rupa, the image that focuses attention on what a Buddha is; artefacts such as bells, thangkas, candles and incense; and offerings of light, flowers, water and food, given as respect and as a lesson in impermanence, since the flowers fade during the day. A top-level answer says why each feature is there, not only that it is."},{"h":"Puja: devotion at home and in the temple","code":"3.1.1.2 — Puja, the significance and role of puja/devotional ritual","t":"Puja is devotional ritual: bowing before the rupa, offering candles, incense, flowers, water and food, chanting, and reciting the Three Refuges and the five precepts. The specification makes the double purpose explicit - chanting is an act of devotion and an aid to mental concentration - and that double purpose is the natural contrast for a four-mark question. Mantra recitation belongs here too: Tibetan Buddhists repeat Om mani padme hum, the mantra of Avalokiteshvara, and Pure Land Buddhists recite the name of Amitabha Buddha. A mala counts the repetitions so that attention stays on the practice. Buddhists differ: many insist nothing is worshipped, since the Buddha was not a god and the rupa is a reminder. A top-level answer states that disagreement and uses it."},{"h":"Samatha, Vipassana and visualisation","code":"3.1.1.2 — Meditation, the different aims, significance and methods of meditation","t":"The aims differ, not only the techniques. Samatha aims at concentration and tranquillity, most commonly through mindfulness of breathing, calming the mind so that it can be used. Vipassana aims at insight, watching experience closely until anicca, anatta and dukkha are seen rather than merely believed; zazen, the seated meditation of Zen, belongs here, whether as 'just sitting' in Soto or work on a koan in Rinzai. Visualisation of Buddhas and Bodhisattvas is Mahayana practice, especially Tibetan: the meditator builds the image of Tara or Avalokiteshvara in the mind and takes on the quality that figure embodies. A top-level answer contrasts them by purpose - calm, insight, devotion - and names the tradition each belongs to."},{"h":"Death and mourning in Theravada communities, Japan and Tibet","code":"3.1.1.2 — Ceremonies and rituals associated with death and mourning","t":"In Theravada communities monks are invited to chant verses on impermanence, the body is usually cremated, and the family offers food and robes to the monastery so that the merit can be dedicated to the dead, with further almsgiving at set intervals afterwards. In Japan the great majority of funerals are Buddhist: incense is offered at a wake, the dead are often given a Buddhist name, the body is cremated and the family places the bones in the urn, and the ancestors are welcomed home each year at Obon. In Tibet the Bardo Thodol is read aloud to guide the dead through the bardo, the intermediate state, for up to forty-nine days, and a sky burial may give the body to the vultures as a last act of generosity. A top-level answer ties the rites back to anicca, kamma and rebirth."},{"h":"Wesak, Parinirvana Day and retreats in Britain","code":"3.1.1.2 — Festivals and retreats and their importance to Buddhists in Great Britain today","t":"Wesak falls on the full moon of April or May and is kept especially in Theravada communities; it remembers the Buddha's birth, Enlightenment and death together. In Britain Buddhists clean and decorate the shrine, light candles and lanterns, bring offerings, chant, take the precepts, hear talks, give to charity and share food, often on the nearest weekend so that people can travel. Parinirvana Day, usually 15 February, is kept mainly in Mahayana traditions and marks the Buddha's passing into final nirvana: the account of his death is read, people meditate on impermanence and remember those who have died in the past year. Retreats, from a weekend at a British centre to the rains retreat kept by monastics, give the sustained practice ordinary life does not allow. A top-level answer explains the significance for a British Buddhist community, not only the customs."},{"h":"Kamma and rebirth, karuna and metta","code":"3.1.1.2 — Ethical teaching: kamma and rebirth, compassion, loving kindness","t":"Kamma is intentional action: skilful actions rooted in generosity, kindness and understanding bring good results, unskilful ones rooted in the Three Poisons bring suffering, and those results shape rebirth within samsara. Intention is what counts, so Buddhist ethics argues from motive and consequence rather than from command, since there is no lawgiver issuing orders. Karuna is compassion, the wish that beings be free from suffering, embodied in the Bodhisattva; metta is loving kindness, the active wish that they be happy, cultivated in metta bhavana meditation and taught in the Metta Sutta. A top-level answer explains a decision by naming the intention behind it and the kammic result expected, and shows how karuna and metta supply the motive that kamma alone does not."},{"h":"The five moral precepts","code":"3.1.1.2 — The five moral precepts","t":"Do not take life; do not take what is not given; do not misuse the senses; do not speak falsehoods; do not take intoxicants that cloud the mind. The wording matters: these are undertakings a Buddhist takes on voluntarily, often recited during puja, not commandments issued by a god, so breaking one is unskilful rather than sinful. They are applied by intention - the first supports non-violence generally and vegetarianism for many Buddhists, and the fifth is defended on the ground that a clouded mind cannot keep the other four or meditate. Monks and nuns undertake many more. A top-level answer uses a precept as a reason inside an argument and admits that Buddhists differ over how strictly each one binds."},{"h":"The six perfections of the Mahayana path","code":"3.1.1.2 — The six perfections in the Mahayanan tradition","t":"Generosity, morality, patience, energy, meditation and wisdom are the qualities a Bodhisattva develops on the way to Buddhahood, and the specification explicitly includes how the individual develops them within themselves. They are trained, not admired: generosity by giving to the monastery and to those in need, morality by keeping the precepts, patience by staying steady under insult or illness, energy by returning to practice when it is dull, meditation by daily sitting, and wisdom by studying the Dhamma until emptiness is understood rather than recited. Wisdom is said to complete the rest, since generosity without insight still clings. A top-level answer names the perfection, says how it is trained, and links it to the Bodhisattva vow."}],"examples":[{"title":"A four-mark contrast: the human personality","setup":"The question is: 'Explain two contrasting Buddhist beliefs about the human personality. [4 marks]' The mark scheme gives a simple explanation of a relevant and accurate contrast one mark, and a detailed explanation two marks, for each of the two beliefs, and warns that if similar beliefs are given only one of them may be credited. No source is required and a third belief earns nothing.","steps":["Pick the contrast the specification itself names: Theravada's five aggregates against Mahayana's sunyata and Buddha-nature, so the two beliefs cannot collapse into one.","Write the Theravada side and develop it - the person is form, sensation, perception, mental formations and consciousness, changing together, with no unchanging self behind them, which is what anatta means.","Open the second paragraph with a contrasting word and name the tradition: 'Mahayana Buddhists, by contrast, teach sunyata', so the examiner sees the contrast instead of inferring it.","Develop the Mahayana side to earn its second mark: because nothing has fixed, independent existence, every being carries Buddha-nature and could become a Buddha."],"cues":["Which two traditions does the specification set against each other here?","What are the parts of the person in Theravada, and what is missing from that list?","How will the examiner know the second belief is a contrast rather than a repeat?","What does emptiness allow Mahayana to say about every being?"]},{"title":"A five-mark answer with a source: the causes of suffering","setup":"The question is: 'Explain two Buddhist teachings about the causes of suffering. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]' Four of the marks come from two teachings, each worth a simple explanation or a developed one, and the fifth is a separate mark for a relevant and accurate reference to a source.","steps":["Choose two teachings that genuinely differ rather than two names for craving: the second Noble Truth on tanha, and the Three Poisons that drive it.","Teaching one, developed: samudaya says dukkha arises from tanha - thirst for sense pleasure, for existence and for things to be otherwise - so even getting what we want leaves us wanting again.","Teaching two, developed: the roots are ignorance, greed and hate, pictured as a pig, a cockerel and a snake at the hub of the Wheel of Life, and ignorance of anicca keeps the other two alive.","Claim the fifth mark openly by naming a text: Dhammapada 190-191 ties going for refuge to seeing the Four Noble Truths, the first two of which are suffering and its cause."],"cues":["Why would 'craving' and 'wanting things' score only two marks between them?","What does the second Noble Truth actually name as the cause?","Which three roots lie underneath craving, and where are they pictured?","What must still be added before this answer can score full marks?"]},{"title":"A four-mark influence question: impermanence today","setup":"The question is: 'Explain two ways in which belief in impermanence (anicca) influences Buddhists today. [4 marks]' The mark scheme gives a simple explanation of a relevant and accurate influence one mark and a detailed explanation two marks for each way, and adds that to be a detailed explanation the influence of the way must be included.","steps":["Fix what anicca claims before hunting for its effects - that everything, the person included, is in constant change - then draw the two ways from different corners of practice, one from worship and one from how a community meets a death.","Way one: the offerings set out at puja are chosen because they fade - flowers wilt and candles burn down - so the ritual itself teaches the worshipper not to cling.","Way two: death is used as teaching, so Buddhists meditate on impermanence at Parinirvana Day and invite chanting on impermanence at a funeral instead of avoiding the subject.","Finish each way with the consequence in plain words: because nothing lasts, a Buddhist holds possessions and relationships lightly and gives to the monastery rather than accumulating."],"cues":["What does anicca actually claim, and where should the two ways be drawn from so they do not overlap?","Which ordinary act of worship shows impermanence happening in front of you?","Where else does a Buddhist community deliberately face impermanence?","What has to appear in each paragraph before the second mark is given?"]},{"title":"Building a Level 4 evaluation: is meditation the most important Buddhist practice?","setup":"The question is: 'Meditation is the most important Buddhist practice.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Buddhist teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] Level 4 asks for a well-argued response with reasoned consideration of different points of view, logical chains of reasoning leading to judgements supported by knowledge and understanding, and references to religion applied to the issue.","steps":["Set a criterion in the opening sentence - importance will be judged by what actually brings a Buddhist nearer the end of craving - so the judgement has something to measure against.","Argue for: samadhi is one third of the Threefold Way, and Vipassana is the practice in which anicca, anatta and dukkha are seen rather than merely believed, which is what the third Noble Truth requires.","Argue against from inside the tradition: sila comes first in the Threefold Way, the precepts govern every hour of the day, and the Bodhisattva's perfections put generosity before meditation.","Add the divergence the specification expects: Pure Land Buddhists trust Amitabha and recite his name, so for them devotion rather than seated meditation is the practice that matters most.","Judge on the criterion set at the start: meditation is necessary but not sufficient, because a mind trained in calm and insight still needs the conduct and compassion that carry it into daily life."],"cues":["What must be fixed before 'most important' can be argued at all?","Which part of the Threefold Way is meditation, and what does it uniquely deliver?","Which Buddhist teachings put something else first?","Which tradition would reject the statement outright, and why?","What turns two lists of arguments into a justified conclusion?"]},{"title":"A five-mark answer on Buddhist death rites","setup":"The question is: 'Explain two ways in which Buddhists mark death. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]' Two ways carry a simple or a developed explanation each, and one further mark is given for a relevant and accurate reference to a source of Buddhist belief and teaching.","steps":["Choose two ways from different settings, because two Theravada customs would look like one way described twice.","Way one, Theravada communities: monks are invited to chant, the body is cremated, and the family offers food and robes to the monastery so that the merit can be dedicated to the dead.","Way two, Tibet: the Bardo Thodol is read aloud to guide the dead through the bardo, the intermediate state between death and rebirth, which the tradition says may last up to forty-nine days.","Take the source mark by naming the text already used, the Bardo Thodol, and tie both ways back to anicca and to rebirth so the explanations are Buddhist and not merely cultural."],"cues":["Why would two Theravada customs be a weak choice here?","Who is invited to a Theravada funeral, and what does the family gain by giving?","What is read in Tibet, and what is it meant to do?","Which part of the mark scheme is still unclaimed?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What do anicca, anatta and dukkha mean?","a":"Impermanence; no fixed self; the unsatisfactoriness of life and suffering."},{"q":"Which tradition holds the Arhat ideal, and which the Bodhisattva ideal?","a":"Theravada holds the Arhat ideal; Mahayana holds the Bodhisattva ideal."},{"q":"Name the Three Poisons.","a":"Ignorance, greed and hate."},{"q":"What are the three groups of the Threefold Way?","a":"Ethics (sila), meditation (samadhi) and wisdom (panna)."},{"q":"Which text does the specification prescribe alongside the Four Sights?","a":"Jataka 075."}]},"room":{"facts":["The Three Marks of Existence: anicca (impermanence), anatta (no fixed self), dukkha (unsatisfactoriness of life, suffering).","The Four Noble Truths: dukkha, suffering; samudaya, its cause in craving (tanha) driven by the Three Poisons of ignorance, greed and hate; nirodha, the end of craving in nibbana; magga, the Eightfold Path.","The Threefold Way groups the Eightfold Path into ethics (sila), meditation (samadhi) and wisdom (panna). Prescribed text: Dhammapada 190-191.","The five aggregates (skandhas) in the Theravada account of the person: form, sensation, perception, mental formations, consciousness.","The five moral precepts: do not take life; do not take what is not given; do not misuse the senses; do not speak falsehoods; do not take intoxicants that cloud the mind.","The six perfections in the Mahayanan tradition: generosity, morality, patience, energy, meditation, wisdom.","The two prescribed texts for Buddhism: Jataka 075 with the Four Sights, and Dhammapada 190-191 with the Eightfold Path and the Threefold Way.","The five-part question runs 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks; the twelve-mark evaluation in the Beliefs section also carries 3 SPaG marks, and the paper allows about a minute a mark."],"questions":[{"q":"Which one of the following is one of the Three Marks of Existence? [1 mark] Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. A Samsara  B Anicca  C Sila  D Metta","a":"Answer: B Anicca.","sol":"Answer: B Anicca. Anicca, impermanence, is one of the Three Marks of Existence, with anatta (no fixed self) and dukkha (the unsatisfactoriness of life, suffering). Samsara is the round of rebirth, sila is the ethics section of the Threefold Way and metta is loving kindness, so none of those three is a Mark of Existence.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The Marks describe every conditioned thing; they are not a stage of the path or a quality to cultivate.","Two of the options belong to Buddhist practice and one names the round of rebirth; only one describes how things are.","Say what each of the four words names, then tick the one that could sit beside anatta and dukkha."],"codes":["3.1.1.1 — The Three Marks of Existence: anicca, anatta, dukkha"]},{"q":"Which one of the following is the Buddhist term for loving kindness? [1 mark] Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. A Karuna  B Metta  C Panna  D Samadhi","a":"Answer: B Metta.","sol":"Answer: B Metta. Metta is loving kindness, the active wish that beings be happy, cultivated deliberately in metta bhavana meditation. Karuna is compassion, the wish that beings be free from suffering; panna is wisdom and samadhi is meditation, two of the three sections of the Threefold Way, so none of them names loving kindness.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Two of the options are qualities behind Buddhist ethics and two are sections of the path.","Compassion and loving kindness are close but not identical: one wants suffering removed, the other wants happiness given.","Translate all four words first, then tick the one that means wishing others well."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — Ethical teaching: kamma and rebirth, compassion, loving kindness"]},{"q":"Which one of the following is a Buddhist monastery? [1 mark] Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. A Mala  B Rupa  C Vihara  D Mantra","a":"Answer: C Vihara.","sol":"Answer: C Vihara. A vihara is a monastery where monks and nuns live, and where lay Buddhists come for teaching, festivals and to give alms. A mala is the string of beads used to count recitations, a rupa is an image of the Buddha and a mantra is a sacred phrase repeated in devotion, so none of those is a building.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Three of the four are things a Buddhist could hold or say; only one is somewhere you could stand.","The specification lists the places of worship by their Buddhist names, monasteries among them.","Say what each word names, then tick the one that is a building."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — The nature, use and importance of Buddhist places of worship"]},{"q":"Give two of the Four Sights seen by the Buddha. [2 marks]","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points from: illness; old age; death; a holy man. Only the first two answers are marked.","sol":"1 Illness. 2 Old age. Death and the holy man are the other two Sights, and any two of the four score the marks. No explanation is required or rewarded, and a third answer after two correct ones is ignored.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The Sights are what Siddhartha saw when he rode out beyond the palace.","Work from the story rather than a list: each Sight is something met on those rides out that the palace had been arranged to keep from him.","Write two of them as a bare numbered list - this question pays nothing for explanation."],"codes":["3.1.1.1 — The Buddha's life and its significance"]},{"q":"Give two of the five moral precepts. [2 marks]","a":"AO1:1. One mark each for two of: do not take life; do not take what is not given; do not misuse the senses; do not speak falsehoods; do not take intoxicants that cloud the mind.","sol":"1 Do not take life. 2 Do not take intoxicants that cloud the mind. Any two of the five score, and the specification's own wording is the safest form. Only the first two answers on the page are marked.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["These are the undertakings a lay Buddhist takes on, recited during puja.","Four of them govern what a Buddhist does to others; one governs what a Buddhist does to their own mind.","Write two of them in the specification's wording as a numbered list, with no reasons attached."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — The five moral precepts"]},{"q":"Give two ways in which Buddhists in Great Britain celebrate Wesak. [2 marks]","a":"AO1:1. One mark each for two of: cleaning and decorating the shrine; lighting candles or lanterns; bringing offerings; chanting; taking the precepts; hearing a talk on the Buddha's life; giving to charity; sharing a meal at the vihara.","sol":"1 They clean and decorate the shrine and light candles or lanterns. 2 They gather at the vihara to chant and to hear a talk about the Buddha's life. Any two accurate celebrations score, and no explanation of their significance is needed for this tariff.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Wesak remembers the Buddha's birth, Enlightenment and death together, so the customs are celebratory.","Think of what a British Buddhist community does at its centre on the nearest weekend, and what happens at the shrine itself.","Write two separate celebrations as a numbered list, not one celebration described twice."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — Festivals and retreats and their importance to Buddhists in Great Britain today"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting Buddhist beliefs about the human personality. [4 marks]","a":"Target AO1:3. Simple explanation of a relevant and accurate contrast 1 mark, detailed 2 marks, for each belief: the Theravada five aggregates (form, sensation, perception, mental formations, consciousness) with no self behind them, against the Mahayana teaching of sunyata and Buddha-nature. Similar beliefs are credited once, to a maximum of two marks.","sol":"Theravada Buddhists teach that a person is the five aggregates: form, sensation, perception, mental formations and consciousness. These parts change constantly and work together, and there is no sixth thing, no permanent self or soul, standing behind them. This is what anatta, no fixed self, means, so what a person calls 'I' is a process rather than a possessor. Mahayana Buddhists, by contrast, extend the analysis with sunyata, emptiness: not only persons but everything lacks inherent, independent existence. Because nothing is fixed in what it is, every being carries Buddha-nature, the capacity to become a Buddha, so the Mahayana account of the person ends in possibility rather than in the bare absence of a self.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The specification itself names the two traditions that have to be contrasted here.","One tradition takes the person apart into components; the other says what all things lack, and draws a hopeful conclusion from it.","Write one short paragraph per tradition, naming the tradition first and then adding the detail that turns one mark into two."],"codes":["3.1.1.1 — The human personality, in the Theravada and Mahayana traditions"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting Buddhist beliefs about the ideal a Buddhist should aim at. [4 marks]","a":"Target AO1:3. The Theravada Arhat, a 'perfected person' who has ended craving and will not be reborn, reached by personal effort, against the Mahayana Bodhisattva, who vows to become a Buddha for the sake of all beings and stays to help them. Simple contrast 1 mark, detailed 2 marks for each.","sol":"Theravada Buddhists aim at the Arhat, the 'perfected person' who has destroyed craving, reached nibbana and will not be reborn. The emphasis falls on personal effort: Dhammapada 276 says the Buddhas only point the way, so each person must walk the Eightfold Path themselves. Mahayana Buddhists, by contrast, hold up the Bodhisattva, who takes a vow to become a Buddha for the sake of all beings and postpones final release in order to help them, training the six perfections on the way. Avalokiteshvara, the Bodhisattva of compassion, is the model. The goal here is not release from samsara for oneself but the rescue of everyone caught in it.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Human destiny is one of the differences the specification says must be studied, so the two ideals are already chosen for you.","One ideal finishes the journey; the other deliberately does not, and the reason for staying is the point to develop.","Name the tradition at the start of each paragraph and add the motive behind its ideal to earn the second mark."],"codes":["3.1.1.1 — Human destiny"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which belief in kamma and rebirth influences Buddhists today. [4 marks]","a":"Target AO1:2. Simple explanation of a relevant and accurate influence 1 mark, detailed 2 marks each; the second mark needs the influence itself, what Buddhists actually do. Credit: watching intention and keeping the precepts; vegetarianism or avoiding harmful work; generosity and the transfer of merit at funerals and festivals.","sol":"Believing that intentional actions shape rebirth makes Buddhists watch their intentions rather than only their behaviour. Before acting, a Buddhist asks whether the motive is generosity, kindness and understanding or one of the Three Poisons, and keeps the five precepts for that reason, so many avoid meat or refuse work that involves harming others. It also shapes how they give. Merit earned by generosity can be dedicated to someone else, so a Buddhist family offers food and robes to the monastery when a relative dies and dedicates the merit to them, which turns a funeral into an act of giving rather than only an occasion for grief.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["An influence question is answered with behaviour, not with a definition of kamma.","Ask what a believer checks before acting, and what a believing family does when someone dies.","End each paragraph with the words 'so a Buddhist...' and finish the sentence with a real action."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — Ethical teaching: kamma and rebirth, compassion, loving kindness"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting methods of Buddhist meditation. [4 marks]","a":"Target AO1:3. Samatha (concentration and tranquillity), typically mindfulness of breathing, against Vipassana (insight), including zazen, or against the Mahayana visualisation of Buddhas and Bodhisattvas. Simple contrast 1 mark, detailed 2 marks each; two similar methods are credited once.","sol":"Samatha aims at concentration and tranquillity. The commonest method is mindfulness of breathing: the meditator settles attention on the breath and returns to it whenever the mind wanders, so that the mind becomes calm and steady enough to be used. Vipassana, by contrast, aims at insight rather than calm. Instead of resting on a single object the meditator watches thoughts, feelings and sensations arise and pass, in order to see anicca, anatta and dukkha directly rather than accept them as doctrine. Zazen, the seated meditation of Zen, belongs here, whether as 'just sitting' in the Soto school or as work on a koan in Rinzai.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The specification lists the methods by their aims, so contrast the aims and the techniques will follow.","One method quietens the mind on a single object; another examines whatever appears, and a third builds an image deliberately.","Give each method its own paragraph, name it, say what it is for and then say what the meditator actually does."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — Meditation, the different aims, significance and methods of meditation"]},{"q":"Explain two Buddhist teachings about the causes of suffering. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"Target AO1:1. Simple explanation 1 mark, detailed 2 marks, for each of two teachings - samudaya and craving (tanha); the Three Poisons of ignorance, greed and hate; dependent arising - plus 1 mark for a relevant and accurate reference to a source, for example Dhammapada 190-191.","sol":"The second Noble Truth, samudaya, teaches that dukkha arises from tanha, craving. Buddhists distinguish craving for sense pleasure, craving to go on existing and craving for things to be otherwise, and the point is that satisfying a craving does not end it: getting what we want leaves us wanting again, so the cycle continues. Underneath craving lie the Three Poisons of ignorance, greed and hate, pictured as a pig, a cockerel and a snake turning at the hub of the Wheel of Life. Ignorance is the root, because a person who has not grasped anicca clings to what cannot last. Dhammapada 190-191 links going for refuge to the Buddha, the Dhamma and the Sangha with seeing the Four Noble Truths, the first two of which are suffering and its cause.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Two of the Four Noble Truths are about causes, and dependent arising explains why a cause matters at all.","Give the cause a layer of depth: what drives the craving itself, and where is it pictured?","Plan two developed paragraphs and then a separate sentence that names a text, because the fifth mark is awarded on its own."],"codes":["3.1.1.1 — The Four Noble Truths","3.1.1.1 — The concept of dependent arising (paticcasamupada)","3.1.1 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Buddhism"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which Buddhists mark death. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Buddhist belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"Target AO1:1. Two ways from different settings - Theravada chanting, cremation and the transfer of merit; Japanese wakes, Buddhist names and the placing of the bones; the Tibetan reading of the Bardo Thodol and sky burial - with 1 mark for a source such as the Bardo Thodol.","sol":"In Theravada communities the family invites monks to chant verses on impermanence at the funeral. The body is usually cremated, and the family offers food and robes to the monastery so that the merit of the gift can be dedicated to the person who has died, with further almsgiving on set days afterwards. In Tibet the Bardo Thodol is read aloud to the dying and to the dead, to guide them through the bardo, the intermediate state between death and rebirth, which the tradition says may last up to forty-nine days; a sky burial may then give the body to the vultures as a final act of generosity. Both rites rest on anicca and rebirth: the Bardo Thodol is read precisely because the person is a process that continues rather than a self that simply stops.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The specification names three settings for death rituals, so choose two of them rather than two customs from one.","Ask in each case what the living are doing for the dead, and what belief makes that worth doing.","Write a developed paragraph on each setting, then name the text or teaching you have relied on in a sentence of its own."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — Ceremonies and rituals associated with death and mourning","3.1.1.1 — The Three Marks of Existence: anicca, anatta, dukkha"]},{"q":"'The Eightfold Path is the most important Buddhist teaching.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Buddhist teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [SPaG 3 marks]","a":"Target AO2, levels-marked on the whole answer. For: magga is the fourth Noble Truth and the cure the first three diagnose; grouped as the Threefold Way beside Dhammapada 190-191; practicable by monastic and lay Buddhists alike. Against: the Path depends on the Three Marks and dependent arising; Pure Land trusts Amitabha rather than self-effort; Mahayana puts the Bodhisattva vow and the six perfections first. Level 4 needs both sides reasoned, religion applied and a justified conclusion.","sol":"The strongest case for the statement is that the Eightfold Path is the part of the Dhamma that tells a Buddhist what to do on an ordinary day. The Four Noble Truths are a diagnosis and a cure: dukkha names the illness, samudaya the cause in craving, nirodha the possibility of a cure, and magga the treatment. A diagnosis without a treatment changes nothing, and the Path is the treatment - right view, intention, speech, action, livelihood, effort, mindfulness and concentration, grouped as the Threefold Way of ethics, meditation and wisdom. Dhammapada 190-191 ties going for refuge to seeing the Four Noble Truths and the path that leads out of suffering, which shows how central it is to the tradition's own summary of itself. It also fits every Buddhist: a monk in a vihara and a lay Buddhist in Britain can both practise right speech and right livelihood, which cannot be said of practices that need a monastery. Against this, the Path is only intelligible because of teachings that come before it. Without anicca and anatta there is no reason to stop clinging, and without dependent arising there is no reason to think that removing craving removes suffering at all; the Path is the conclusion of an argument whose premises are the Three Marks and paticcasamupada. If importance is measured by what the rest depends on, dependent arising has the better claim. Buddhists themselves disagree, and the specification expects that to show. Pure Land Buddhists hold that trust in Amitabha Buddha and the recitation of his name, with rebirth in the Pure Land, is what makes Enlightenment reachable, so for them the decisive teaching is not a path walked alone. Mahayana Buddhists more widely put the Bodhisattva vow and the six perfections at the centre, so that compassion for all beings, rather than one's own progress along eight steps, defines the religious life, and Zen's insistence on direct insight in zazen sits awkwardly with a numbered list. The judgement depends on what importance means. If it means the teaching a Buddhist most often acts on, the Eightfold Path wins easily, and Theravada Buddhists would defend the statement as it stands. If it means the teaching without which the rest collapses, dependent arising is more fundamental and the Path is its application. The statement is therefore true of practice and false of foundations, and since the Buddha set the Path out as the fourth Truth rather than the first, the tradition's own ordering supports the second view: the Path is indispensable, but it is not the foundation.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what 'most important' is going to mean before arguing anything - most practised, or most fundamental?","The Path is the fourth of four Truths, so ask what the other three, and dependent arising, contribute to it.","Plan four paragraphs: the case for, the teachings the Path rests on, a tradition that would disagree, and a conclusion that applies the meaning you chose."],"codes":["3.1.1.1 — The Four Noble Truths","3.1.1.1 — The concept of Dhamma (Dharma)","3.1.1.1 — The concept of dependent arising (paticcasamupada)","3.1.1 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Buddhism"]},{"q":"'Buddhists in Great Britain do not need places of worship.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Buddhist teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks]","a":"Target AO2, levels-marked. For: the Buddha is not a god and nothing is worshipped, so a home shrine with a rupa and offerings carries puja; mindfulness of breathing needs only a quiet place; anicca warns against attachment to buildings. Against: the Sangha is the third refuge and needs somewhere to meet; viharas house monastics and give teaching, precepts and almsgiving; Wesak and Parinirvana Day are communal; gompas exist for meditation and learning. Level 4 needs both sides reasoned and a justified conclusion.","sol":"Much of Buddhist practice can be done anywhere, and that is the strength of the statement. Buddhists do not hold that the Buddha is a god who must be approached in a sacred building; the rupa is a reminder of what a Buddha is rather than an object of prayer, so a shelf in a British living room with an image, a candle, flowers and a bowl of water can carry the whole of puja, including the Three Refuges and the five precepts. Mindfulness of breathing needs nothing but a quiet corner, and Dhammapada 276, that the Buddhas only point the way, puts the work inside the practitioner. Anicca adds a warning of its own: a community that grows attached to a building has treated as permanent something that will decay, which is the clinging the Dhamma exists to loosen. The other side is stronger than it first looks, because the third refuge is the Sangha and a Sangha needs somewhere to be. A vihara is a monastery where monks and nuns live, and in Britain it is also where lay Buddhists hear the Dhamma taught, take the precepts, give alms and sit with others who practise. Wesak in Britain is kept by cleaning and decorating the shrine, lighting lanterns, chanting together and sharing food, which a solitary Buddhist cannot do on a windowsill, and Parinirvana Day brings people together to remember those who have died during the year. Tibetan communities build gompas precisely because meditation and learning go better when they are taught and done together, and a temple keeps the texts, artefacts and teachers a household cannot. A middle position is common among British Buddhists: most practise daily at a home shrine and gather at a centre weekly or for festivals, so the two are not rivals. The judgement turns on which part of the religion is in view. For personal practice the statement is defensible, since nothing essential to meditation, the precepts or puja requires a temple, and the tradition itself resists making buildings holy. For the religion as a lived community it is false, because the Sangha is one of the Three Refuges and a refuge that never meets is an idea rather than a community. Since the Dhamma is transmitted through teachers and through the monastic community, and since British Buddhists are a minority who would otherwise practise in isolation, places of worship are not strictly necessary for an individual but are necessary for Buddhism to continue here.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Separate what an individual needs from what the religion needs before you argue either side.","One of the Three Refuges is a community rather than a teaching or a person - ask what that refuge requires in practice.","Plan the case for the home shrine, the case for the vihara, the middle position most British Buddhists take, and a conclusion that says which need you are judging."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — The nature, use and importance of Buddhist places of worship","3.1.1.2 — Puja, the significance and role of puja/devotional ritual","3.1.1.2 — Meditation, the different aims, significance and methods of meditation","3.1.1.2 — Festivals and retreats and their importance to Buddhists in Great Britain today"]},{"q":"'The Bodhisattva ideal is the best goal for a Buddhist.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Buddhist teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks]","a":"Target AO2, levels-marked. For: karuna and metta are the motives the tradition names; the Bodhisattva vow and the six perfections train them; anatta undercuts the line between my liberation and yours; Avalokiteshvara as model. Against: Theravada's Arhat is the earlier ideal, supported by Dhammapada 276; a person still ruled by the Three Poisons cannot rescue anyone; Pure Land reframes the choice. Level 4 needs both sides reasoned and a justified conclusion.","sol":"Mahayana Buddhists argue that the Bodhisattva ideal is best because it takes compassion seriously. A Bodhisattva vows to become a Buddha for the sake of all beings and postpones final release in order to help them, developing the six perfections - generosity, morality, patience, energy, meditation and wisdom - as the training that makes such help possible. If karuna, compassion, is the wish that all beings be free from suffering, a goal that leaves everyone else behind at the last step looks inconsistent with it, and Avalokiteshvara, the Bodhisattva of compassion, is held up as the model precisely for refusing to go. Anatta supports the argument too: if there is no fixed self, the sharp line between my liberation and yours is itself part of the delusion, so working for others is not a sacrifice but the correct reading of the situation. Theravada Buddhists answer that the Arhat is the ideal the Buddha actually set out. The Arhat is the 'perfected person' who has destroyed craving, reached nibbana and will not be reborn, and Dhammapada 276 says the Buddhas only point the way, so each person must walk the Eightfold Path themselves. There is a practical objection as well: someone still driven by greed, hate and ignorance is in no position to rescue anybody, and the honest order is to cure oneself first. On this view the Bodhisattva ideal risks postponing indefinitely the one thing a Buddhist can actually achieve. The choice is also less sharp than the statement suggests. An Arhat is not selfish: the Buddha taught for the rest of his life after his Enlightenment, and Theravada monks live on alms and give the Dhamma in return. Pure Land Buddhists reframe the question again, trusting Amitabha Buddha for rebirth in the Pure Land rather than choosing between the two ideals by their own strength. The judgement should turn on what a goal is for. If the test is which ideal expresses Buddhist ethics most fully, the Bodhisattva wins, because karuna and metta are the motives the whole tradition names and the six perfections train them deliberately. If the test is which ideal the earliest teaching prescribes and an ordinary practitioner can realistically pursue, the Arhat has the better claim. The statement is therefore true within Mahayana, where the vow is the point of the path, but it cannot be said of Buddhism as a whole, because both ideals end in the same release from craving and differ over timing rather than over the goal itself.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["The two ideals belong to named traditions, so the different point of view is already waiting inside Buddhism.","Ask what compassion requires of a goal, and then ask what a person must fix before they can help anyone.","Plan the Mahayana case, the Theravada reply, the point that the two are closer than they look, and a conclusion that names the test you have judged by."],"codes":["3.1.1.1 — Human destiny","3.1.1.2 — The six perfections in the Mahayanan tradition","3.1.1.2 — Ethical teaching: kamma and rebirth, compassion, loving kindness"]},{"q":"'Keeping the five moral precepts is all a Buddhist needs to live a good life.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Buddhist teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks]","a":"Target AO2, levels-marked. For: the precepts cover the acts that do most harm; they are workable for a lay Buddhist and are undertaken by intention, which is what kamma weighs. Against: sila is one third of the Threefold Way and leaves samadhi and panna untouched; craving is the real cause of dukkha; the precepts are negative in form, so metta, karuna and the six perfections supply the positive side, and Dhammapada 183 pairs avoiding evil with cultivating good. Level 4 needs a justified conclusion.","sol":"In favour of the statement, the five precepts cover the ground on which most harm is done. Do not take life, do not take what is not given, do not misuse the senses, do not speak falsehoods, and do not take intoxicants that cloud the mind: between them they rule out killing, theft, sexual exploitation, lying, and the loss of judgement that leads to the other four. They are also workable. Unlike the six perfections or the full monastic discipline, they can be undertaken by a lay Buddhist in Britain with a job and a family, and they are recited as undertakings at puja, which keeps them present rather than theoretical. Because they are undertakings rather than commands, they are applied by intention, and intention is exactly what kamma weighs: the first precept pushes many Buddhists towards vegetarianism and away from harmful work, and the fifth is defended on the ground that a clouded mind cannot keep the other four. Against the statement, the precepts are only one part of the path. The Eightfold Path is grouped as the Threefold Way of ethics, meditation and wisdom, and the precepts are the ethics section alone; a Buddhist who keeps them but never meditates has done nothing about the craving that the second Noble Truth identifies as the cause of dukkha. Restraint is not transformation, and a person can avoid every prohibited act while being consumed by wanting. The precepts are negative in form as well: they say what not to do and say nothing about what to cultivate, which is why the tradition adds metta and karuna as motives, why Mahayana Buddhists train the six perfections beginning with generosity, and why Dhammapada 183 pairs avoiding evil with cultivating good and purifying the mind. Buddhists also disagree about how far the five reach, since monks and nuns undertake many more and Buddhists argue over whether the first precept requires vegetarianism. The conclusion depends on what a good life means. If it means causing little harm, the statement is nearly right, and someone who kept all five faithfully would live better than most. If it means the life the Dhamma actually prescribes, which ends craving through wisdom as well as conduct, the statement is false, because the precepts keep a Buddhist out of trouble without bringing them any nearer nibbana. They are necessary and not sufficient, which is why the Buddha set sila alongside samadhi and panna rather than on its own.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Ask which part of the path the precepts are, and what the other parts were added for.","Notice the grammar of all five: they forbid rather than require, so ask what supplies the positive side of Buddhist ethics.","Plan the case for the precepts, the case from the Threefold Way, the disagreement among Buddhists, and a conclusion that defines 'a good life' before judging."],"codes":["3.1.1.2 — The five moral precepts","3.1.1.2 — Ethical teaching: kamma and rebirth, compassion, loving kindness","3.1.1.2 — The six perfections in the Mahayanan tradition","3.1.1.1 — The Four Noble Truths"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Name the Three Marks of Existence.","a":"Anicca (impermanence), anatta (no fixed self), dukkha (unsatisfactoriness of life, suffering)."},{"q":"What are the five aggregates (skandhas) in the Theravada account of the person?","a":"Form, sensation, perception, mental formations, consciousness."},{"q":"What are the three groups of the Threefold Way?","a":"Ethics (sila), meditation (samadhi), wisdom (panna)."},{"q":"Which festival marks the Buddha's passing into final nirvana, and when is it usually kept?","a":"Parinirvana Day, usually 15 February."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Dhamma (Dharma)","back":"The Buddha's teaching, and the truth about reality that the teaching describes; discovered rather than invented. The second of the Three Refuges, with the Buddha and the Sangha.","code":"3.1.1.1 — The concept of Dhamma (Dharma)","open":false},{"front":"Paticcasamupada (dependent arising)","back":"Everything arises in dependence on conditions and ceases when they cease, so nothing exists independently or permanently. It underpins anicca and anatta and makes the ending of dukkha possible.","code":"3.1.1.1 — The concept of dependent arising (paticcasamupada)","open":false},{"front":"The Three Marks of Existence","back":"Anicca, impermanence; anatta, no fixed self; dukkha, the unsatisfactoriness of life and suffering. Marks of all conditioned existence, stated as facts about the world rather than opinions.","code":"3.1.1.1 — The Three Marks of Existence: anicca, anatta, dukkha","open":false},{"front":"The human personality: Theravada and Mahayana","back":"Theravada: the five aggregates (skandhas) of form, sensation, perception, mental formations and consciousness, with no self behind them. Mahayana: sunyata, so all beings have Buddha-nature and can attain Buddhahood.","code":"3.1.1.1 — The human personality, in the Theravada and Mahayana traditions","open":false},{"front":"Arhat, Bodhisattva and the Pure Land","back":"Theravada's Arhat is a 'perfected person' who ends craving and is not reborn. Mahayana's Bodhisattva vows Buddhahood for all beings, training the six perfections. Pure Land Buddhists seek rebirth in Amitabha's Pure Land.","code":"3.1.1.1 — Human destiny","open":false},{"front":"The Four Sights (Jataka 075)","back":"Illness, old age, death and a holy man: what Siddhartha Gautama saw beyond the palace, which showed him dukkha and led him to leave his life of luxury.","code":"3.1.1.1 — The Buddha's life and its significance","open":false},{"front":"The Four Noble Truths","back":"Dukkha, suffering; samudaya, its cause in craving (tanha), driven by the Three Poisons of ignorance, greed and hate; nirodha, the end of craving in nibbana; magga, the Eightfold Path.","code":"3.1.1.1 — The Four Noble Truths","open":false},{"front":"The Threefold Way (Dhammapada 190-191)","back":"The Eightfold Path grouped as ethics (sila): speech, action, livelihood; meditation (samadhi): effort, mindfulness, concentration; wisdom (panna): view and intention. Dhammapada 190-191 is the prescribed text.","code":"3.1.1 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Buddhism","open":false},{"front":"Buddhist places of worship","back":"Temples for public devotion and teaching; shrines at home or in a temple; viharas, monasteries where monks and nuns live; gompas, Tibetan halls for meditation and learning. Each holds a Buddha rupa, artefacts and offerings.","code":"3.1.1.2 — The nature, use and importance of Buddhist places of worship","open":false},{"front":"Puja","back":"Devotional ritual at home or in the temple: bowing, offerings of light, incense, flowers, water and food, chanting the Three Refuges and precepts, mantra recitation and the use of malas. Chanting is devotion and an aid to concentration.","code":"3.1.1.2 — Puja, the significance and role of puja/devotional ritual","open":false},{"front":"Samatha, Vipassana and visualisation","back":"Samatha: concentration and tranquillity, often mindfulness of breathing. Vipassana: insight into the Three Marks, including zazen. Visualisation: building the image of a Buddha or Bodhisattva in the mind, a Mahayana practice.","code":"3.1.1.2 — Meditation, the different aims, significance and methods of meditation","open":false},{"front":"Buddhist death rites in Theravada communities, Japan and Tibet","back":"Theravada: monks chant, cremation, merit transferred by giving to the monastery. Japan: wake with incense, a Buddhist name, bones placed in the urn, Obon. Tibet: the Bardo Thodol read through the bardo; sky burial.","code":"3.1.1.2 — Ceremonies and rituals associated with death and mourning","open":false},{"front":"Wesak and Parinirvana Day","back":"Wesak, on the April or May full moon, chiefly Theravada, remembers the Buddha's birth, Enlightenment and death; shrines are decorated and lanterns lit. Parinirvana Day, usually 15 February, chiefly Mahayana, marks his final nirvana.","code":"3.1.1.2 — Festivals and retreats and their importance to Buddhists in Great Britain today","open":false},{"front":"The five moral precepts","back":"Do not take life; do not take what is not given; do not misuse the senses; do not speak falsehoods; do not take intoxicants that cloud the mind. Undertakings, not commandments.","code":"3.1.1.2 — The five moral precepts","open":false},{"front":"Kamma, karuna and metta","back":"Kamma: intentional action whose results shape rebirth. Karuna: compassion, the wish that beings be free from suffering. Metta: loving kindness, the wish that they be happy, cultivated in metta bhavana.","code":"3.1.1.2 — Ethical teaching: kamma and rebirth, compassion, loving kindness","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Evaluate: 'Keeping the five moral precepts is all a Buddhist needs to live a good life.')","items":["The precepts settle conduct but not craving, and that is what decides the statement. A lay Buddhist who keeps all five - not taking life, not taking what is not given, not misusing the senses, not speaking falsehoods, not taking intoxicants that cloud the mind - will do very little harm, and because the precepts are undertaken voluntarily at puja rather than imposed as commands, keeping them already involves the intention that kamma actually weighs. That is a real achievement, and it is why the precepts are the ethics (sila) section of the Threefold Way. But sila is one third of that way and not the whole of it: the Eightfold Path adds meditation (samadhi) and wisdom (panna), and the second Noble Truth locates the cause of dukkha in tanha, craving, which restraint alone leaves untouched - a person can avoid every prohibited act and still be consumed by wanting. The precepts are negative in form as well, which is why Dhammapada 183 pairs avoiding evil with cultivating good, and why Mahayana Buddhists train the six perfections beginning with generosity. So on the criterion of harm the statement nearly holds, but on the criterion the Dhamma itself sets, the ending of craving, it fails: 'all a Buddhist needs' is precisely the claim the Threefold Way was built to deny.","Why this reaches Level 4: it is a well-argued response that gives reasoned consideration to different points of view - the case for the precepts is stated at its strongest before it is answered - and the reasoning runs in a logical chain from what the precepts are, through what the Threefold Way adds, to a judgement. The references to religion are applied to the issue rather than listed: sila, samadhi, panna, tanha, the second Noble Truth, Dhammapada 183 and the six perfections each do work in the argument. The judgement is supported rather than asserted, because the criterion for 'a good life' is named and then used, which is what 'judgement(s) supported by knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information' asks for."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same statement, visibly weaker)","items":["Buddhists should keep the five precepts because they stop you doing bad things like killing and stealing and lying, and if everybody did that the world would be a much better place. Buddhists also believe you get bad kamma if you break them, so keeping them means you get a better rebirth next time. But some people might say there is more to being a Buddhist than just following rules, because you also have to meditate and be kind to other people. So I think keeping the precepts is a good start but it is not everything a Buddhist needs.","Why this stays at Level 2: it meets 'recognition of different points of view, each supported by relevant reasons/evidence' - the precepts reduce harm and earn good kamma on one side, meditation and kindness on the other - and there is a reference to religion, so it is not capped below that level. It misses Level 3 because the reasoning never becomes a chain: nothing is named or developed, there is no Threefold Way, no sila, samadhi or panna, no Noble Truth, no text and no tradition, so the 'knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information' that Level 3 requires is absent. It misses Level 4 because the closing sentence is an opinion added at the end rather than a judgement that follows from the arguments given, and no criterion for 'a good life' is ever stated."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — the twelve-mark evaluation (Beliefs): 'The Buddha's Enlightenment is the most important event in Buddhism.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Buddhist teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [SPaG 3 marks]","items":["Decoded statement: 'most important' is comparative, so the Enlightenment must be weighed against something - the Four Sights, the first sermon, the Buddha's death, or the Dhamma itself. Name the rival in the opening sentence, or the answer risks becoming a description of one event.","Criterion to set at the start: importance will be measured by what Buddhists could not do without. Say so, then test every paragraph against it.","Paragraph 1, in support: without the Enlightenment under the Bodhi tree at Bodh Gaya there is no Buddha and no Dhamma to teach. The Four Noble Truths and the Eightfold Path date from it, the first sermon in the Deer Park at Sarnath follows from it, and Wesak is kept partly to remember it.","Paragraph 2, in support: it proves the Middle Way. Luxury had failed and six years of asceticism had failed, and Enlightenment came through meditation between the two, which is why Buddhists meditate rather than starve themselves.","Paragraph 3, a different point of view: the Four Sights came first and set the problem. Prescribed text Jataka 075. Without illness, old age, death and the holy man, Siddhartha never leaves the palace, so the Sights are the cause of everything that follows.","Paragraph 4, the divergence the specification requires: for Pure Land Buddhists the decisive fact is Amitabha Buddha and rebirth in the Pure Land rather than one night under a tree; for Mahayana Buddhists more widely, Buddha-nature means the possibility of Enlightenment is in every being, so no single event is unique.","Evaluation thread to run through all four paragraphs: keep separating the event that made the teaching possible from the teaching itself. The Enlightenment is the cause; the Dhamma is what a Buddhist actually lives by.","Because this is Component 1, the first bullet requires Buddhist teaching, so the different point of view should come from within Buddhism - a Theravada, Mahayana, Zen or Pure Land view - rather than from outside the religion.","What the conclusion must say: name the criterion that won and why. For example, on what Buddhists could not do without, the Enlightenment outranks every other event because it produced the Dhamma; but if importance means what shapes a Buddhist's daily life, the Dhamma it produced matters more, so the statement is true of events and false of teachings.","Timing: about twelve minutes on this question. The three SPaG marks ride on it, and one of the three descriptors is a wide range of specialist terms, so use Dhamma, anicca, tanha, nibbana, Bodhisattva and samadhi accurately and spell them consistently."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Buddhism: Beliefs and teachings (3.1.1.1), with the source of authority for each","items":["Dhamma (Dharma): the Buddha's teaching and the truth about reality it describes; the second of the Three Refuges, taken at puja alongside the Buddha and the Sangha.","Dependent arising (paticcasamupada): everything arises in dependence on conditions and ceases when they cease; shown on the outer rim of the Tibetan Wheel of Life as a chain of linked stages.","Anicca: impermanence, the first Mark of Existence; taught in the Dhamma and shown in the offerings of flowers and candles that fade during puja.","Anatta: no fixed self; there is no permanent soul behind the changing person. Source: the Buddha's analysis of the person into the five aggregates.","Dukkha: the unsatisfactoriness of life and suffering; the first Noble Truth, taught in the Buddha's first sermon in the Deer Park at Sarnath.","The five aggregates (skandhas), Theravada: form, sensation, perception, mental formations, consciousness.","Sunyata, Mahayana: emptiness; no thing has inherent, independent existence. Associated with the Perfection of Wisdom teaching and with Nagarjuna.","Buddha-nature, Mahayana: the capacity for Buddhahood present in all beings, so Enlightenment is open to everyone.","Arhat: the Theravada ideal, a 'perfected person' who has destroyed craving, reached nibbana and will not be reborn. Source: Dhammapada 276, that the Buddhas only point the way.","Bodhisattva: the Mahayana ideal, one who vows to become a Buddha for the sake of all beings. Avalokiteshvara for compassion, Manjushri for wisdom, Tara in Tibetan traditions.","Buddhahood and the Pure Land: Pure Land Buddhists trust Amitabha (Amida) Buddha and recite his name, hoping for rebirth in his Pure Land, where Enlightenment can be attained.","The Buddha: Siddhartha Gautama, born into a ruling family in northern India and raised in luxury; 'the Buddha' is a title meaning the awakened one.","The Four Sights: illness, old age, death and a holy man. Prescribed text: Jataka 075.","The ascetic life: six years of extreme self-denial with five companions, abandoned because it produced no insight; the Buddha then taught the Middle Way between indulgence and self-denial.","Enlightenment: reached in meditation under the Bodhi tree at Bodh Gaya; the first sermon followed in the Deer Park at Sarnath, preached to the five former companions.","The Four Noble Truths: dukkha; samudaya, the cause; nirodha, the end of craving; magga, the Eightfold Path.","Types of dukkha: ordinary pain such as illness and old age; the suffering caused by change, when what is enjoyed is lost; and the deeper unsatisfactoriness of conditioned existence itself.","The Three Poisons: ignorance, greed and hate, pictured as a pig, a cockerel and a snake at the hub of the Wheel of Life.","Tanha: craving - for sense pleasure, for continued existence, and for things to be otherwise.","Nibbana (nirvana): interpretations differ - the blowing out of the fires of craving, hatred and ignorance; a state that cannot be described in ordinary language; and parinirvana, the final nirvana entered at the death of an enlightened person.","The Eightfold Path: right view, right intention, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration.","The Threefold Way: ethics (sila) - right speech, action and livelihood; meditation (samadhi) - right effort, mindfulness and concentration; wisdom (panna) - right view and right intention. Prescribed text: Dhammapada 190-191."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Buddhism: Practices (3.1.1.2), with the source or significance of each","items":["Temple: a public building for devotion, teaching and festivals, open to lay Buddhists.","Shrine: a room in a temple or a shelf at home holding a Buddha rupa with offerings; the focus of puja.","Vihara: a monastery where monks and nuns live; lay Buddhists come to hear the Dhamma, take the precepts, give alms and keep festivals.","Gompa: a Tibetan hall for meditation and learning.","Buddha rupa: an image of the Buddha, used to focus attention on what a Buddha is; most Buddhists insist it is a reminder rather than an object of worship, since the Buddha was not a god.","Artefacts and offerings: candles and lamps for light, incense, flowers, water and food; the flowers fade during the day as a lesson in anicca.","Puja: bowing, offerings, chanting and recitation of the Three Refuges and the five precepts, in the home and in the temple.","Chanting: named in the specification as both a devotional practice and an aid to mental concentration.","Mantra recitation: Om mani padme hum, the mantra of Avalokiteshvara, in Tibetan traditions; the name of Amitabha Buddha in Pure Land practice.","Mala: a string of beads used to count recitations and to hold attention on the practice.","Samatha: concentration and tranquillity, most often through mindfulness of breathing.","Vipassana: insight meditation, watching experience until anicca, anatta and dukkha are seen; zazen is the seated meditation of Zen, 'just sitting' in Soto and koan work in Rinzai.","Visualisation: building the image of a Buddha or Bodhisattva in the mind, a Mahayana and especially Tibetan practice, often using a thangka or a mandala.","Death and mourning, Theravada communities: monks are invited to chant, the body is usually cremated, and the family gives food and robes to the monastery so that the merit can be dedicated to the dead.","Death and mourning, Japan: most funerals are Buddhist; incense is offered at a wake, the dead are often given a Buddhist name, the family places the bones in the urn after cremation, and ancestors are welcomed home at Obon.","Death and mourning, Tibet: the Bardo Thodol is read to guide the dead through the bardo, the intermediate state, for up to forty-nine days; sky burial gives the body to the vultures as a final act of generosity.","Wesak: kept on the full moon of April or May, especially in Theravada communities; it remembers the Buddha's birth, Enlightenment and death together.","Wesak in Great Britain: shrines cleaned and decorated, candles and lanterns lit, offerings brought, chanting, taking the precepts, talks on the Buddha's life, giving to charity and shared food, often on the nearest weekend.","Parinirvana Day: usually 15 February, kept mainly in Mahayana traditions; the account of the Buddha's death is read, people meditate on impermanence and remember those who have died during the past year.","Retreats: time set aside for sustained practice, from a weekend at a British centre to the rains retreat kept by monastics.","Kamma: intentional action; skilful intentions bring good results and unskilful ones suffering, and the results shape rebirth within samsara.","Karuna: compassion, the wish that beings be free from suffering, embodied in the Bodhisattva.","Metta: loving kindness, the wish that beings be happy, cultivated in metta bhavana meditation and taught in the Metta Sutta.","The five moral precepts: do not take life; do not take what is not given; do not misuse the senses; do not speak falsehoods; do not take intoxicants that cloud the mind - undertakings, not commandments.","The six perfections (Mahayanan tradition): generosity, morality, patience, energy, meditation, wisdom; developed by giving, by keeping the precepts, by bearing insult and hardship, by returning to practice when it is dull, by daily sitting and by study of the Dhamma."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the prescribed texts and the Buddhist traditions the specification names","items":["Prescribed text: Jataka 075, set against the Four Sights - illness, old age, death, holy man (3.1.1.1).","Prescribed text: Dhammapada 190-191, set against the Eightfold Path (magga) and the Threefold Way of ethics (sila), meditation (samadhi) and wisdom (panna) (3.1.1.1). The verses link going for refuge to the Buddha, the Dhamma and the Sangha with seeing the Four Noble Truths.","The Dhammapada is a collection of verses within the Pali Canon; Dhammapada 183 (avoid evil, cultivate good, purify the mind) and Dhammapada 276 (the Buddhas only point the way) are useful additional references.","Other sources of wisdom and authority a five-mark answer may cite: the Three Refuges; the five precepts as a recited formula; the Metta Sutta on loving kindness; the Bardo Thodol in Tibetan practice.","Theravada, 'the teaching of the elders': the tradition of Sri Lanka, Thailand, Burma, Laos and Cambodia; the Pali Canon, the Arhat ideal, the five aggregates.","Mahayana, 'the great vehicle': the traditions of China, Japan, Korea, Vietnam and Tibet; sunyata, Buddha-nature, the Bodhisattva ideal and the six perfections.","Zen: a Mahayana school of Japan; zazen, seated meditation, 'just sitting' in Soto and koan practice in Rinzai.","Pure Land: a Mahayana tradition; trust in Amitabha (Amida) Buddha, recitation of his name, and rebirth in the Pure Land.","Places of worship named by the specification: temples, shrines, monasteries (viharas), halls for meditation or learning (gompas), with Buddha rupa, artefacts and offerings.","Festivals named by the specification: Wesak and Parinirvana Day, with retreats, and their importance to Buddhists in Great Britain today.","The standing rule on sources: beliefs and practices must be traced to their basis in Buddhist sources of wisdom and authority, and the fifth mark of every five-mark question is given for exactly that reference."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T14:07:04.312330Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.95,"notes":"All four fixes landed cleanly and introduced nothing new: SPaG now appears only on room.questions[12] (the Eightfold Path, a Beliefs evaluation) and on the Beliefs essay plan, the new first move and cue in lesson.examples[2] make a real move on anicca without revealing the two ways, and room.questions[3].hints[1] now points at the method rather than characterising the Four Sights. A full re-read found the rest of the kit unchanged and sound - tariffs 1/2/4/5/12 throughout, Component 1 bullets on every 12-mark stem, Jataka 075, Dhammapada 190-191, 183 and 276, the Metta Sutta, the Bardo Thodol and the Three Refuges all used accurately, Theravada/Mahayana/Zen/Pure Land attributions clean, no quotation over seven words and no printed question reproduced. One judgement call a teacher may want to revisit: room.questions[14] ('The Bodhisattva ideal...') is also a Beliefs evaluation, so if it were set as the Beliefs question it would carry SPaG too; showing the label once is honest but not the only defensible choice. Publishable as it stands."}}},"3.1.2":{"id":"AQA-8062","topic":"3.1.2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Christianity is one of the two religion options most students answer on Paper 1, and it is the only religion the specification also calls the main religious tradition in Great Britain, so it shapes Component 2 as well. This room carries two full five-part questions, one on Beliefs and teachings and one on Practices: 48 marks of the 51 on the booklet, plus the three SPaG marks that are awarded on the Beliefs evaluation. Every part is compulsory. The grade is usually lost in two places: giving two beliefs that agree when the question asked for contrasting ones, and writing an evaluation that never names a Christian teaching, which the mark scheme caps at Level 2. This room fixes both by pinning every belief and practice to a named source of wisdom and authority and to the tradition that actually holds it.","idea":[{"h":"The three standing demands: a source, an influence, and the divergence inside Christianity","t":"Three requirements sit behind every question here. First, sources: beliefs and practices must be traced to their basis in Christian sources of wisdom and authority, and you must be able to refer to scripture or another source where appropriate — that is the separate fifth mark of every 5-mark question, and it is also what lifts an evaluation out of Level 2. Second, influence: the specification asks for the influence of these beliefs, teachings and practices on individuals, communities and societies, which is why the detailed mark on an influence question is only given when you say what Christians actually do differently. Third, divergence: common and divergent views within Christianity should be included throughout, and the perspectives named are Catholic, Orthodox and Protestant. A top-level answer therefore never says just ‘Christians believe’; it says which Christians, on what authority, and with what effect.","code":"3.1.2 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Christianity"},{"h":"God as omnipotent, loving and just — the Trinity and the problem of evil","t":"Christians believe in one God who is omnipotent (all-powerful — ‘with God all things are possible’, Matthew 19:26), loving or benevolent (‘God is love’, 1 John 4:8) and just (‘all his ways are just’, Deuteronomy 32:4). The same one God is Father, Son and Holy Spirit: three persons, one being, as the Nicene Creed confesses and as the baptism of Jesus pictures, with the Father’s voice, the Son in the water and the Spirit as a dove (Matthew 3:16-17). The specification puts the problem of evil and suffering inside this bullet, because it is the three attributes together that make suffering a problem: an all-powerful God could stop it, a loving God would want to, a just God ought to, yet it continues. Christian responses include free will, the Fall, suffering as a test or as soul-making, and the cross itself, where God shares human suffering and Jesus cries ‘why have you forsaken me?’ (Mark 15:34). A top-level answer holds all three attributes together and shows how a named response protects each one.","code":"3.1.2.1 — The nature of God: God as omnipotent, loving and just"},{"h":"Creation, the Word and the Spirit — Genesis 1:1-3 and John 1:1-3","t":"Both texts are prescribed and questions may be set on them. Genesis 1:1-3 has ‘In the beginning God created’, then ‘the Spirit of God was hovering’ over the waters, then God speaks: ‘Let there be light’. So creation is by God’s word and with God’s Spirit already present. John 1:1-3 makes the same point about Christ: ‘In the beginning was the Word’, the Word was with God and was God, and ‘through him all things were made’ — the Word who becomes flesh in Jesus. Christians therefore say the whole Trinity is involved in creation, and that God created out of nothing, freely and deliberately. The specification says ‘different Christian beliefs about creation’, so the divergence is part of the content: some Christians read the six days literally and judge scientific accounts by Scripture, while others, including the Catholic Church, read the days non-literally and accept the Big Bang and evolution as the means God used. A top-level answer quotes one of the two prescribed texts and then names which reading it is defending.","code":"3.1.2.1 — Different Christian beliefs about creation including the role of Word and Spirit"},{"h":"Resurrection, judgement, heaven and hell — and why they change how a Christian lives now","t":"The specification asks for different Christian beliefs about the afterlife and their importance, so two things are wanted: the beliefs and the difference they make. Christians believe death is not the end because Jesus rose: ‘I am the resurrection and the life’ (John 11:25), and the Apostles’ Creed confesses ‘the resurrection of the body’ and ‘the life everlasting’. God will judge: in the parable of the sheep and the goats the righteous go ‘to eternal life’ (Matthew 25:46), and judgement is on how people treated the hungry, the sick and the stranger. Divergence is real here. Some Christians expect a bodily resurrection at the end of time, others speak of an immortal soul going to God at death. Catholic teaching adds purgatory, a purification for those who die in God’s friendship but are not yet ready for heaven; most Protestants reject it as unscriptural. Hell is understood as everlasting punishment by some Christians and as separation from God, or as a state few or none finally choose, by others. A top-level answer always ties the belief to conduct now: judgement is why Christians feed the hungry, not only what they expect.","code":"3.1.2.1 — Different Christian beliefs about the afterlife and their importance"},{"h":"Jesus Christ and salvation: incarnation, cross, resurrection, sin, law, grace, Spirit and atonement","t":"Five named topics live under this heading. Incarnation: God became human in Jesus, the Son of God — ‘The Word became flesh’ (John 1:14), and the Nicene Creed says he came down from heaven for us and for our salvation. Crucifixion, resurrection and ascension: Jesus was crucified under Pontius Pilate, died and was buried, rose on the third day and after forty days ascended to the right hand of the Father (Acts 1:9). Sin, including original sin: ‘all have sinned’ (Romans 3:23), and from Adam and Eve’s disobedience in Genesis 3 humanity inherits a fallen nature, which is why Catholics baptise infants. The means of salvation the document names are law, grace and Spirit: keeping God’s law as given in the Ten Commandments, salvation as God’s free gift — ‘by grace you have been saved’ (Ephesians 2:8) — and the Holy Spirit working in a believer’s life. Atonement: ‘Christ died for our sins’ (1 Corinthians 15:3), making humanity at one with God again. A top-level answer names which means it is arguing from, because Protestants usually stress grace and faith while Catholics hold grace together with the sacraments and works of love.","code":"3.1.2.1 — Jesus Christ and salvation: beliefs and teachings about the incarnation, crucifixion, resurrection, sin and salvation"},{"h":"Liturgical, non-liturgical, informal and private worship, and the use of the Bible","t":"Four forms are named and the significance of each is asked for. Liturgical worship follows a set order and set words, usually with a priest, a lectionary and the church year: the Catholic Mass, the Orthodox Divine Liturgy, Church of England services from Common Worship or the Book of Common Prayer. Its significance is unity, reverence and continuity — the same words as other Christians and as earlier generations. Non-liturgical worship, in Methodist, Baptist and United Reformed churches, has a shape but not fixed words: hymns, extempore prayer, Bible reading and a long sermon, so the Bible rather than the liturgy carries the service. Informal or charismatic worship, as in Pentecostal churches, is spontaneous, with free prayer, prophecy and sometimes speaking in tongues; Quaker meetings are the opposite extreme, silent and unprogrammed, waiting on the Spirit. Private worship is prayer, Bible study, the rosary for Catholics and icons for the Orthodox. This bullet is the standing source for the contrasting question on Practices, so a top-level answer names the denomination beside each form rather than saying ‘some churches’.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Different forms of worship and their significance"},{"h":"Prayer: the Lord’s Prayer, set prayers and informal prayer","t":"Prayer is communication with God: adoration, confession, thanksgiving and asking. The Lord’s Prayer is named by the specification, so it can be used as the source of wisdom and authority in a 5-mark answer: Jesus gave it in the Sermon on the Mount (Matthew 6:9-13), and it opens ‘hallowed be your name’ before asking for daily bread and forgiveness — God first, needs second. Set prayers are words given to you and shared with others: the Lord’s Prayer itself, collects from the Book of Common Prayer, the Hail Mary and the rosary for Catholics, the Jesus Prayer for the Orthodox. Their significance is that they teach believers what to ask for, join them to the whole Church, and can be prayed when someone is too tired or grief-stricken to find words. Informal or extempore prayer is in a person’s own words, at any time, and its significance is honesty and closeness — a relationship rather than a recitation. A top-level answer sets the two against each other and then judges, rather than describing both.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Prayer and its significance, including the Lord's Prayer"},{"h":"Sacraments: baptism, infant and believers’, and Holy Communion","t":"A sacrament is an outward, visible sign of an inward, invisible grace. Catholic and Orthodox Christians hold seven — baptism, confirmation, Eucharist, reconciliation, anointing of the sick, holy orders and matrimony; the Church of England’s Thirty-Nine Articles name two sacraments ordained by Christ in the Gospel, baptism and the Lord’s Supper; Quakers and the Salvation Army practise none, holding that the whole of life is sacramental. Baptism uses water in the name of the Father, Son and Holy Spirit (Matthew 28:19). Catholics, Orthodox, Anglicans and Methodists baptise infants, with parents and godparents making the promises, because baptism washes away original sin and brings the child into the Church; Catholic, Anglican and Methodist ministers pour the water over the child’s head, while an Orthodox infant is immersed three times in the font; Baptists and Pentecostals practise believers’ baptism by full immersion, because they hold that faith must be personal and that the New Testament shows adults believing and then being baptised. Holy Communion is called the Mass, the Eucharist, the Divine Liturgy, the Lord’s Supper or the Breaking of Bread, and interpretations diverge: Catholic teaching is transubstantiation, the bread and wine truly becoming Christ’s body and blood; many Protestants, including Baptists, keep it as a memorial of ‘do this in remembrance of me’ (1 Corinthians 11:24). A top-level answer names the position and the church that holds it.","code":"3.1.2.2 — The role and meaning of the sacraments"},{"h":"Pilgrimage: Lourdes and Iona — and the celebrations of Christmas and Easter","t":"The specification prescribes two contrasting examples of pilgrimage, so they are the ready-made answer to a contrasting question. Lourdes, in the foothills of the Pyrenees in southern France, is a Catholic Marian shrine: in 1858 Bernadette Soubirous saw a series of apparitions of the Virgin Mary at the Grotto of Massabielle, a spring appeared, and pilgrims now come in huge numbers, many of them sick, for the water, the baths, Mass at the grotto and the torchlight procession, cared for by volunteers. Iona is a small island in the Inner Hebrides off the west coast of Scotland, where Columba came from Ireland in 563 and founded a monastery; the abbey is now the home of the ecumenical Iona Community, founded in 1938 by the Church of Scotland minister George MacLeod, and pilgrims come for silence, retreat, simple shared work and worship, and commitment to peace and justice. Christmas celebrates the incarnation, with Advent, carol services, Midnight Mass, cribs and nativity plays; Easter celebrates the resurrection, with Lent from Ash Wednesday, Holy Week from Palm Sunday through Maundy Thursday and Good Friday, the Easter Vigil and its new fire and paschal candle, and sunrise services. Their importance in Great Britain today is asked for, so a top-level answer weighs church attendance and charitable giving against commercialisation.","code":"3.1.2.2 — The role and importance of pilgrimage and celebrations"},{"h":"The church where it stands: food banks and street pastors","t":"Both examples are named by the specification, so they can be used with confidence. Many churches host or run a food bank, most of them within the Trussell Trust network, which works by referral: a professional gives a voucher and volunteers hand over about three days of emergency food, with a cup of tea and someone to talk to. Street pastors are trained church volunteers, begun in Brixton in London in 2003 under the Reverend Les Isaac and the Ascension Trust, who patrol town centres on Friday and Saturday nights, listening, helping people who are drunk, lost or frightened get home safely, and handing out water, flip-flops and foil blankets. Both are grounded in the same teaching: ‘I was hungry and you gave me’ something to eat (Matthew 25:35), and the command to love with actions and in truth (1 John 3:18). Churches also run parent and toddler groups, lunch clubs, night shelters and youth work, and provide the building itself as a place anyone may enter. Because these are influence questions, a top-level answer never stops at the activity: it says what Christians do differently on a Friday night because of what they believe.","code":"3.1.2.2 — The role of the Church in the local community"},{"h":"Mission, evangelism and Church growth","t":"The three are related but distinct. Mission is being sent into the world to do God’s work — preaching, but also healing, teaching, feeding and working for justice. Evangelism is specifically sharing the good news about Jesus so that others may believe, and Christians ground it in the Great Commission: ‘Go and make disciples of all nations’ (Matthew 28:19), and in Jesus’ promise ‘you will be my witnesses’ (Acts 1:8). Church growth is what follows: new members, new congregations and new kinds of church, such as the Alpha course that began at Holy Trinity Brompton, Fresh Expressions run jointly by the Church of England and the Methodist Church, and Messy Church for families. Growth also happens through missionary societies overseas and through churches planted in Britain by Christians who arrived from Africa, the Caribbean and eastern Europe. The evaluation in this area almost always asks whether Christians should try to convert other people, so a top-level answer must hold the command to evangelise alongside the objections: respect for other faiths, the charge of arrogance or pressure, and the argument that actions preach better than words.","code":"3.1.2.2 — The place of mission, evangelism and Church growth"},{"h":"The worldwide Church: reconciliation, persecution and CAFOD, Christian Aid or Tearfund","t":"Three strands, all examinable. Reconciliation is restoring broken relationships, between people and God through Christ and between people themselves: Paul says God gave the Church ‘the ministry of reconciliation’ (2 Corinthians 5:18) and Jesus says ‘Blessed are the peacemakers’ (Matthew 5:9). Named examples are Coventry Cathedral’s Community of the Cross of Nails, which grew out of the bombing of the cathedral in 1940 when a cross was made from its charred roof beams and the words ‘Father Forgive’ were set behind the altar, and the Corrymeela Community in Northern Ireland, founded in 1965, which brings Catholics and Protestants together. Persecution is met by prayer, by publicity and lobbying through agencies such as Open Doors and Aid to the Church in Need, by practical aid to persecuted congregations, and by the conviction that suffering for faith follows Jesus. Only one development agency must be studied: CAFOD, the Catholic Agency For Overseas Development, founded in 1962 as the official aid agency of the Catholic Church in England and Wales; Christian Aid, the agency of British and Irish churches, with Christian Aid Week each May; or Tearfund, founded in 1968, which works through local churches. A top-level answer names the agency, one thing it does in an emergency and one long-term thing, and attaches a teaching.","code":"3.1.2.2 — The importance of the worldwide Church"}],"examples":[{"title":"The contrasting question: two Christian beliefs about creation that genuinely disagree","setup":"Christianity: Beliefs. Explain two contrasting Christian beliefs about the creation of the world. [4 marks] The prescribed texts are Genesis 1:1-3, where the Spirit of God is hovering over the waters and God speaks creation into being, and John 1:1-3, where all things were made through the Word. The grid gives a simple explanation of a relevant and accurate contrast 1 mark and a detailed explanation 2 marks, for each of the two, and warns that if similar beliefs are given only one of them may be credited.","steps":["Choose two readings of the same chapter that actually collide, since agreement is capped: a literal six-day creation against a non-literal reading of Genesis.","First belief, stated so the contrast is visible from the first words: some Christians read Genesis literally, so God made everything in six days and rested on the seventh, and the account is history as well as theology.","Add the detail that turns one mark into two: for these Christians God’s word cannot be mistaken, so where geology or evolutionary biology disagrees with Scripture, Scripture is trusted.","Second belief, set directly against it: other Christians, including the Catholic Church, treat the days as a poetic frame and accept the Big Bang and evolution as the means God used, so the chapter answers who made the world and why, not when or how."],"cues":["Why would two beliefs that agree cost you marks here?","Which Christians treat the chapter as history, and what does that commit them to?","What one extra sentence lifts a simple explanation into a detailed one?","Which reading of the same chapter contradicts the first, and who holds it?"]},{"title":"The five-mark question: two ways the worldwide Church works for reconciliation, with the source mark","setup":"Christianity: Practices. Explain two ways in which the worldwide Church works for reconciliation. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks] Four of the marks are a simple explanation 1 mark and a detailed explanation 2 marks for each of two ways; the fifth is a separate mark for a relevant and accurate reference to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching.","steps":["Pick two ways that are different in kind and can be named, so neither paragraph repeats the other: peace-building between divided communities, and churches themselves being brought back together.","First way with its named example: Coventry Cathedral’s Community of the Cross of Nails began after the cathedral was bombed in 1940, when a cross was made from the charred roof beams and the words ‘Father Forgive’ were set behind the altar; it now links reconciliation projects around the world.","Second way, clearly distinct: the Corrymeela Community in Northern Ireland, founded in 1965, brings Catholics and Protestants to live, work and talk together, so Christians are reconciled to each other and not only to God.","Make the fifth mark unmissable by naming the source rather than hinting at it: Paul says God gave the Church the ministry of reconciliation (2 Corinthians 5:18), and Jesus says ‘Blessed are the peacemakers’ (Matthew 5:9)."],"cues":["What is lost if both of your ways are versions of the same thing?","Which English cathedral turned its own destruction into a worldwide reconciliation network?","Which named community reconciles Christians with Christians, and where?","Where in the answer does the separate fifth mark come from, and how do you make it obvious?"]},{"title":"The influence question: what a church does differently on a weekday and a Friday night","setup":"Christianity: Practices. Explain two ways in which Christian teaching about caring for others influences churches in their local communities today. [4 marks] The grid gives a simple explanation of a relevant and accurate influence 1 mark and a detailed explanation 2 marks for each of two ways, and the examiner is reminded that to be a detailed explanation the influence of the way must be included.","steps":["Start with something churches actually do, because an influence question is answered with actions and not with doctrine: many churches run or host a food bank, most of them inside the Trussell Trust network.","Turn the action into an influence: volunteers accept referral vouchers and hand over about three days of emergency food with a cup of tea, so the teaching that whoever feeds the hungry feeds Christ decides what the church hall is used for midweek.","Second way, different in kind: street pastors, trained church volunteers begun in Brixton in 2003, go out into town centres on Friday and Saturday nights.","Say the influence rather than describing the uniform: they listen, help people who are drunk or frightened get home safely and hand out water, flip-flops and foil blankets, so believing that every stranger matters costs these Christians their weekend sleep."],"cues":["Why is naming a belief, on its own, worth only half the marks here?","What exactly changes in the church building because of that belief?","Which named group takes the church out of the building after dark?","How do you word the second mark so the examiner sees the influence, not the description?"]},{"title":"The evaluation: building two chains and a judgement on life after death","setup":"Christianity: Beliefs. ‘Christians cannot be sure there is life after death.’ Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Christian teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [SPaG 3 marks]","steps":["Take the statement seriously first and argue it with religious material, not scepticism alone: no one has returned to report, the Gospel accounts of the empty tomb are testimony rather than proof, and Jesus himself cried ‘why have you forsaken me?’ (Mark 15:34), so faith can be held in the dark.","Build the opposing chain from named teaching: Jesus says ‘I am the resurrection and the life’ (John 11:25), the Apostles’ Creed confesses the resurrection of the body and the life everlasting, and Paul argues that Christ was raised first of all.","Show divergence inside Christianity, which is what this paper expects instead of another religion: some Christians expect a bodily resurrection at the end of time, others an immortal soul at death, and Catholic teaching adds purgatory as purification before heaven.","Close with a judgement that grows out of the two chains rather than a fresh opinion: say which reasoning was stronger and why — for instance that proof is not what Christian faith ever claimed, so the statement is right about evidence and wrong about assurance."],"cues":["Why must the supporting side use Christian material too, and not only doubt?","Which named teachings carry the opposing chain?","On this paper, where does the different point of view usually come from?","What must the last paragraph do that a summary of both sides does not?"]},{"title":"The two short parts: the tick and the two bare points","setup":"Christianity: Practices, the opening parts. (a) Which one of the following is the place of Christian pilgrimage in the Inner Hebrides? Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. A Lourdes  B Iona  C Walsingham  D Assisi [1 mark] (b) Give two reasons why Christians go on pilgrimage. [2 marks]","steps":["Settle the tick by geography and elimination: Lourdes is in southern France, Walsingham is in Norfolk and Assisi is in Italy, so the answer is B Iona, the island where Columba founded a monastery.","Remember what the tick is and is not: one answer only, and nothing written beside the box earns credit if the wrong box is ticked.","For the two-mark part write two separate reasons, one per line, with no development at all: to draw closer to God in a place made holy by prayer, and to ask for healing, as pilgrims do at the baths and the grotto at Lourdes.","Then stop: only the first two answers are marked, wherever they appear, so a third reason cannot rescue a weak one — and a reason must be a reason, never a description of the place."],"cues":["Which three of the four options can you rule out on geography alone?","What happens to an explanation written next to a wrongly ticked box?","What does ‘Give two’ ask for that ‘Explain two’ does not?","Why is a third answer a risk rather than insurance?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which two texts does the specification prescribe for Christian beliefs about creation, and what does each contribute?","a":"Genesis 1:1-3 — God creates by speaking and the Spirit of God is hovering over the waters. John 1:1-3 — the Word was with God, was God, and all things were made through him."},{"q":"Why does a 4-mark contrasting answer lose marks if both beliefs point the same way?","a":"Because if similar beliefs are given only one of them may be credited, up to a maximum of two marks, so the second paragraph earns nothing."},{"q":"Name the three means of salvation the specification lists, and give a source for one of them.","a":"Law, grace and Spirit. For grace: ‘by grace you have been saved’ (Ephesians 2:8)."},{"q":"What must the final paragraph of an evaluation do to reach Level 4?","a":"Reach a justified conclusion — a judgement that says which arguments were stronger and why, supported by the knowledge already used, not a new opinion added at the end."}]},"room":{"facts":["The five parts of a question on Christianity, in order: a multiple-choice tick (1 mark), ‘Give two …’ (2 marks), ‘Explain two contrasting …’ or ‘Explain two ways in which … influences … today’ (4 marks), ‘Explain two … Refer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer’ (5 marks), and ‘Evaluate this statement’ (12 marks).","The fifth mark of the 5-mark question is separate and unmissable: relevant and accurate reference to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching — 1 mark. Learn one usable reference for every idea in this room.","On the 4-mark influence question the second mark is only awarded when the influence is stated: to be a detailed explanation, the influence of the way must be included, so say what Christians do or think differently.","The evaluation is marked by levels on the whole answer: Level 2 (4–6) is the ceiling if there is no reference to religion; Level 3 (7–9) needs reasoned consideration of different points of view with clear reference to religion; Level 4 (10–12) needs logical chains of reasoning leading to judgements supported by evidence.","Timing is about a minute a mark — roughly 4 minutes on the 4-mark part, 5 on the 5-mark and 12 on the evaluation, with 25 minutes for the whole five-part question. Spelling, punctuation and grammar (3 marks) are assessed on the 12-mark question in the Beliefs section only."],"questions":[{"q":"Which one of the following is the Christian belief that God became human in Jesus? Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. A Ascension  B Atonement  C Incarnation  D Evangelism [1 mark]","a":"AO1:1. Answer: C Incarnation.","sol":"C Incarnation. The incarnation is the belief that God took on human flesh in Jesus, the Son of God — ‘The Word became flesh’ (John 1:14). Ascension is Jesus returning to the Father after the resurrection; atonement is the reconciling of humanity to God through Christ’s death; evangelism is sharing the good news so that others may believe.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Three of the four words belong to what Jesus did or what Christians do; one names what he is.","Take each option in turn and say what it actually means before you tick anything.","Find the line of the Nicene Creed where Jesus comes down from heaven and is made man, and ask which option names what that line describes."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Jesus Christ and salvation: beliefs and teachings about the incarnation, crucifixion, resurrection, sin and salvation"]},{"q":"Which one of the following does John 1:1-3 call the one through whom all things were made? Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. A The Rock  B The Word  C The Shepherd  D The Vine [1 mark]","a":"AO1:1. Answer: B The Word.","sol":"B The Word. John’s Gospel opens ‘In the beginning was the Word’, says the Word was with God and was God, and adds that through him all things were made (John 1:1-3). Christians identify the Word with Christ, who becomes flesh later in the same chapter, which is why they say the Son shares in creation.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The question names one of the two creation texts the specification prescribes.","Recall how that Gospel begins — it deliberately echoes the opening of Genesis.","Go back to the opening line of John 1:1 and see what name it gives to the one who was there ‘in the beginning’."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Different Christian beliefs about creation including the role of Word and Spirit"]},{"q":"Give two Christian beliefs about the Trinity. [2 marks]","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points; first two answers only. Credit: one God in three persons; the persons are Father, Son and Holy Spirit; the three are equal / of one being; God is one and three at the same time; the Nicene Creed states it.","sol":"1. There is one God in three persons. 2. The three persons are the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit, who are equal and of one being.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The specification joins this belief to the oneness of God, so both halves matter.","Two separate points, one per line, with no explanation — development earns nothing here.","Start by counting: how many Gods, and how many persons?"],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — The nature of God: God as omnipotent, loving and just"]},{"q":"Give two Christian beliefs about what happens after death. [2 marks]","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points. Credit: the dead will be raised; the soul lives on with God; God will judge everyone; the righteous receive eternal life in heaven; hell is punishment or separation from God; Catholics believe in purgatory.","sol":"1. God will judge every person on how they have lived. 2. Those judged righteous receive eternal life with God in heaven.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The specification lists resurrection and life after death, then judgement, heaven and hell.","Pick two points from different parts of that list so they cannot be read as one.","Begin with what Christians believe God does when a person dies."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Different Christian beliefs about the afterlife and their importance"]},{"q":"Give two ways in which Christians in Great Britain celebrate Christmas. [2 marks]","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points. Credit: going to Midnight Mass or a Christmas morning service; carol services; nativity plays; setting up a crib; Advent calendars and wreaths; giving to charity; giving presents; sharing a Christmas meal.","sol":"1. They go to church for Midnight Mass or a service on Christmas morning. 2. They hold carol services and nativity plays that retell the birth of Jesus.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The festival celebrates the incarnation, so start with what happens in church.","Two bare points, one per line; no reasons are wanted.","Think of the night before and the morning itself."],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — The role and importance of pilgrimage and celebrations"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting Christian beliefs about hell. [4 marks]","a":"AO1:3. Simple explanation of a relevant and accurate contrast 1 mark, detailed 2 marks, for each of two. Credit: hell as a real place of everlasting punishment after judgement (Matthew 25:46); hell as eternal separation from God, chosen by the person, rather than torture; the view that a loving God saves all, or that hell is a warning image.","sol":"Some Christians believe hell is a real and permanent state of punishment for those who reject God. They point to Jesus’ parable of the sheep and the goats, where those who ignored the hungry and the stranger go away to eternal punishment while the righteous go to eternal life (Matthew 25:46), and they argue that if God is just there must be a final consequence for evil. Other Christians believe hell is not torture but eternal separation from God, freely chosen by a person who has spent a life refusing him. On this view God never sends anyone to hell; people simply get the eternity they have chosen, which they say fits better with the belief that God is love.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The specification says ‘different Christian beliefs’ about the afterlife, so the disagreement is part of the content.","Write two short paragraphs whose opening words set them against each other, and develop each with a reason or a teaching.","One view keeps hell as punishment; ask what the other does with the belief that God is love."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Different Christian beliefs about the afterlife and their importance","3.1.2 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Christianity"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which belief in the resurrection of Jesus influences Christians today. [4 marks]","a":"AO1:2. Simple explanation of a relevant and accurate influence 1 mark, detailed 2 marks, for each of two; the influence must be stated for the second mark. Credit: Sunday worship and Easter celebration; hope at funerals and in bereavement; confidence in evangelism; courage under persecution; motivation to live for a life beyond this one.","sol":"First, it shapes when and how Christians worship. Because the tomb was found empty on the first day of the week, Christians gather every Sunday rather than on the Sabbath, and Easter is the high point of their year, kept with the Easter Vigil, the paschal candle and sunrise services — so the resurrection decides the shape of a Christian’s week and year. Second, it changes how Christians face death. Believing that Jesus rose, and that he said ‘I am the resurrection and the life’ (John 11:25), Christians hold funerals that speak of hope as well as grief, comfort the bereaved with the promise of eternal life, and are freed from being ruled by the fear of dying.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["This is an influence question, so the marks are for what changes in a believer’s life.","Name a practice or a decision for each way, then say plainly what is different because of the belief.","Think first about the day Christians worship on, and then about a funeral."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Jesus Christ and salvation: beliefs and teachings about the incarnation, crucifixion, resurrection, sin and salvation"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting forms of Christian worship. [4 marks]","a":"AO1:3. Simple explanation of a relevant and accurate contrast 1 mark, detailed 2 marks, for each of two. Credit: liturgical (Catholic Mass, Orthodox Divine Liturgy, Common Worship) against non-liturgical (Baptist, Methodist, URC) or informal/charismatic (Pentecostal) or silent (Quaker); public against private worship.","sol":"Liturgical worship follows a set order with set words, week by week. At a Catholic Mass or an Orthodox Divine Liturgy the prayers, readings and responses are laid down, a priest presides and the readings follow the church’s year, which gives worshippers reverence, familiarity and unity with Christians everywhere using the same words. Informal worship is the opposite. In a Pentecostal church the service is largely spontaneous: members pray aloud in their own words, sing as long as they wish, and may prophesy or speak in tongues, because they believe the Holy Spirit should be free to lead the meeting rather than a printed order. Quakers take this further still and wait in silence until someone is moved to speak.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The specification names liturgical, non-liturgical and informal worship, and private worship.","Choose two that genuinely differ and name the denomination that practises each.","Ask who decides the words: the church beforehand, or the worshipper at the time?"],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — Different forms of worship and their significance","3.1.2 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Christianity"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting Christian beliefs about infant baptism. [4 marks]","a":"AO1:3. Simple explanation of a relevant and accurate contrast 1 mark, detailed 2 marks, for each of two. Credit: Catholic, Orthodox, Anglican and Methodist practice — original sin washed away, the child welcomed into the Church, parents and godparents promising; Baptist and Pentecostal objection — baptism follows personal faith, so believers’ baptism by full immersion.","sol":"Catholics, Orthodox, Anglicans and Methodists baptise babies. Catholic, Anglican and Methodist clergy pour the water over the child’s head in the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit, while the Orthodox baptise the infant by immersing it three times in the font; original sin is washed away and the child is welcomed into the Church, while parents and godparents make the promises on the child’s behalf and undertake to bring it up in the faith; Catholic teaching treats the sacrament as God’s gift, which does not wait for the child to understand it. Baptists and Pentecostals reject infant baptism for exactly that reason. They hold that baptism must follow a personal decision to follow Christ, so they dedicate babies instead and baptise believers later by full immersion, which they say matches the New Testament pattern of hearing, believing and then being baptised.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The specification asks for different beliefs about infant baptism, so name the churches on each side.","Give each view its own paragraph and develop it with the reason that church gives.","One side starts from original sin; ask what the other side starts from."],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — The role and meaning of the sacraments","3.1.2 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Christianity"]},{"q":"Explain two Christian teachings about how people are saved. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"AO1:1. Simple explanation of a relevant and accurate teaching 1 mark, detailed 2 marks, for each of two, plus 1 mark for a relevant and accurate source. Credit: salvation by grace through faith (Ephesians 2:8); salvation through the law and God’s commandments; the work of the Holy Spirit; atonement through Christ’s death (1 Corinthians 15:3).","sol":"One teaching is salvation by grace. Christians believe no one can earn forgiveness, because ‘all have sinned’ (Romans 3:23); salvation is God’s free gift, given because Christ died in our place, and it is received by faith rather than achieved by good works. A second teaching is salvation through law. Many Christians hold that God’s commandments show how a saved life is lived, so keeping the Ten Commandments and Jesus’ command to love God and neighbour is the response God expects, and Catholic teaching joins grace to the sacraments and to works of love. The source is Paul: ‘by grace you have been saved’, through faith (Ephesians 2:8).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The specification names three means of salvation — one of them is a gift, one a rule, one a person.","Two paragraphs, each stating a teaching and developing it, then a clearly signposted source.","Decide first which two you can attach a named text or creed to."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Jesus Christ and salvation: beliefs and teachings about the incarnation, crucifixion, resurrection, sin and salvation","3.1.2 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Christianity"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which churches help people in their local community. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"AO1:1. Simple explanation of a relevant and accurate way 1 mark, detailed 2 marks, for each of two, plus 1 mark for a relevant and accurate source. Credit: food banks, most in the Trussell Trust network, run on referral vouchers; street pastors on Friday and Saturday nights; night shelters, lunch clubs, parent and toddler groups, youth work.","sol":"First, many churches run or host a food bank, most of them within the Trussell Trust network. A professional such as a doctor or a teacher issues a voucher, and church volunteers hand over about three days of emergency food along with a hot drink and time to talk, so the hall is used midweek for people the congregation may never meet on a Sunday. Second, street pastors go out where the need is. Trained church volunteers, first sent out in Brixton in 2003, walk town centres on Friday and Saturday nights, listening to people, helping those who are drunk or frightened to get home safely, and handing out water, flip-flops and foil blankets. Jesus’ own words supply the reason: ‘I was hungry and you gave me’ something to eat (Matthew 25:35).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The specification names two examples of local church work; use them rather than inventing others.","Give each way a paragraph with a concrete detail — who is helped, how, and when — then add the source.","One example happens inside the church building; the other happens outside it, at night."],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — The role of the Church in the local community","3.1.2 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Christianity"]},{"q":"Explain two reasons why prayer is important for Christians. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Christian belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"AO1:1. Simple explanation of a relevant and accurate reason 1 mark, detailed 2 marks, for each of two, plus 1 mark for a relevant and accurate source. Credit: prayer keeps a relationship with God; Jesus prayed and taught his followers to pray; set prayers unite believers and supply words; prayer brings forgiveness, guidance and comfort; the Lord’s Prayer (Matthew 6:9-13).","sol":"First, prayer keeps the relationship with God alive. Christians believe God is personal and listens, so they speak to him in their own words at any time — thanking him, confessing, and asking for help — and they hold that this changes the person praying even when the circumstances do not change. Second, prayer joins a Christian to the whole Church. When set prayers are used, believers say the same words as Christians everywhere and as earlier generations, and the words are there on days when grief or exhaustion leaves someone with none of their own. The clearest source is the prayer Jesus himself gave his followers, which begins ‘hallowed be your name’ before asking for daily bread and forgiveness (Matthew 6:9-13).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The specification names three kinds of prayer, and one of them is a text you can quote.","Two developed reasons, then a source named openly rather than implied.","Contrast a Christian praying alone in their own words with a congregation praying together."],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — Prayer and its significance, including the Lord's Prayer","3.1.2 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Christianity"]},{"q":"‘A God who is loving and just would not allow people to suffer.’ Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Christian teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels. Support: the inconsistent triad — an omnipotent God could stop suffering, a loving God would want to, a just God would not allow the innocent to suffer; natural evil is nobody’s fault; some suffering destroys rather than teaches. Other views: free will; the Fall; suffering as soul-making or testing (Job); the cross; God’s justice completed after death.","sol":"The statement presses on the three attributes the specification puts together: God is omnipotent, loving and just. If God can do anything — ‘with God all things are possible’ (Matthew 19:26) — then he could prevent an earthquake. If ‘God is love’ (1 John 4:8), he would want to. If ‘all his ways are just’ (Deuteronomy 32:4), he would not let a child die of a disease she did nothing to deserve. Each attribute on its own is coherent, but the three together with the world as it is look contradictory, which is why many people reason that a God of this description does not allow suffering and therefore does not exist. Natural evil sharpens the argument: floods and cancers are nobody’s choice, so free will cannot excuse them, and suffering on that scale often crushes people rather than improving them.\n\nChristians answer along several lines. The first is free will: God made human beings able to love, which means able to refuse, and Genesis 3 traces the entry of suffering into the world to that refusal rather than to God. Much of the worst suffering — war, cruelty, neglect, poverty — is human, and a world where God prevented every wrong choice would be a world where no choice was real. The second is that suffering can form a person. The Book of Job never explains why Job suffers but ends with Job knowing God more deeply, and Christians speak of patience, courage and compassion that grow nowhere else. The third is the strongest: God does not stand outside the problem. In the incarnation God became human and was tortured to death, and on the cross Jesus cried ‘why have you forsaken me?’ (Mark 15:34). Christians therefore say God suffers with us, and that the resurrection shows suffering is not the last word. To this they add justice beyond death: in the sheep and the goats the righteous go ‘to eternal life’ (Matthew 25:46), so a God who looks unjust now is not being judged on the whole story.\n\nOther Christians concede more. Some accept that no answer is adequate, and hold that faith here is trust rather than explanation; others read the Fall symbolically and admit that natural evil remains unexplained.\n\nIn my judgement the statement is strongest against natural evil and weakest against moral evil, where free will genuinely answers it. But it assumes that love means preventing all pain, and Christian teaching does not mean that: the cross shows a love that enters suffering instead of abolishing it. The statement therefore fails as a disproof of God, though it succeeds as a warning against easy explanations of other people’s pain.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["The specification deliberately puts the problem of evil inside the doctrine of God — work out which attributes the statement is setting against each other.","Build one developed chain supporting the statement and one against it, each resting on named Christian teaching, before you judge anything.","Separate the suffering people cause from the suffering nobody causes; the answers to them are not the same."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — The nature of God: God as omnipotent, loving and just","3.1.2 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Christianity"]},{"q":"‘The resurrection of Jesus is the most important Christian belief.’ Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Christian teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels. Support: without the resurrection there is no victory over death and no hope of eternal life; it proves Jesus is the Son of God; it founded the Church and Sunday worship; Easter is the greatest festival. Other views: the incarnation or the crucifixion is prior; the Trinity or belief in God underlies everything; some Christians say love in practice matters more than any doctrine.","sol":"Christians have strong reasons for putting the resurrection first. Without it, Jesus is a good teacher who was executed; with it, God has publicly vindicated him. Paul says that if Christ has not been raised then Christian faith is useless, and the earliest summary of the gospel he passes on is that ‘Christ died for our sins’ and was raised on the third day (1 Corinthians 15:3-4). The resurrection is also the ground of the afterlife: Jesus says ‘I am the resurrection and the life’ (John 11:25), and the Apostles’ Creed confesses the resurrection of the body and the life everlasting, so the hope Christians bring to a funeral rests on Easter morning. Its influence is visible in practice: Christians worship on Sunday because the tomb was found empty on the first day of the week, Easter is kept as the greatest festival of the year with the Easter Vigil and the paschal candle, and the frightened disciples became missionaries who ‘will be my witnesses’ (Acts 1:8) only after they believed he was alive.\n\nA different point of view, and one held within Christianity, is that the resurrection depends on beliefs that must come first. Nothing is saved by a man rising unless that man is God, so the incarnation — ‘The Word became flesh’ (John 1:14) — is arguably prior; the Nicene Creed says he came down from heaven for us and for our salvation before it speaks of the third day. Others place the crucifixion first, because it is the death that atones: ‘Christ died for our sins’, and it is the cross, not the empty tomb, that Christians wear and that stands in their churches. Behind both stands belief in God himself as omnipotent, loving and just, and in the Trinity; without that, there is nobody to be incarnate and nobody to raise the dead. Some Christians, especially in the liberal Protestant tradition, argue that what matters most is following Jesus’ teaching — loving God and neighbour, feeding the hungry — and that Christians who agree about Easter but ignore the poor have missed the point.\n\nThese arguments are not really rivals, which is the weakness in the statement’s wording. Christian belief is one connected structure: the incarnation makes the cross possible, the cross makes forgiveness possible, and the resurrection completes and confirms both.\n\nI judge the statement defensible but overstated. If ‘most important’ means the belief without which the rest collapses, then belief in God, and in Jesus as God incarnate, has the better claim. If it means the belief that turns the story into good news and that Christians actually stake their hope on, the resurrection deserves the title. Since Paul himself says faith is futile without it, I conclude the statement is right about Christian hope, though the incarnation is the deeper foundation.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["‘Most important’ needs deciding before it can be argued: important for what — truth, hope, or the way Christians live?","Argue one side fully with named teaching, then let the other side be a different Christian view rather than a non-religious one.","List the beliefs that would fall if this one were removed, and the beliefs it depends on itself."],"codes":["3.1.2.1 — Jesus Christ and salvation: beliefs and teachings about the incarnation, crucifixion, resurrection, sin and salvation","3.1.2.1 — Different Christian beliefs about the afterlife and their importance"]},{"q":"‘Christians should not try to persuade other people to become Christians.’ Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Christian teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks]","a":"AO2, levels. Support: respect for other faiths in a diverse Britain; conversion can be pressure or exploitation of the vulnerable; faith should be shown by actions; historical harm done by missions. Other views: the Great Commission (Matthew 28:19); the duty to share salvation; evangelism as offering, not forcing; Church growth through Alpha and Fresh Expressions.","sol":"There is a serious case for the statement. Britain is religiously diverse, and trying to change someone’s religion can look like telling them their family’s faith is worthless. Evangelism has a damaging history: missions that travelled with empires often destroyed cultures as well as converting them, and churches today are rightly criticised when converts are sought among people who are homeless, frightened or newly arrived and dependent on the help the church is offering. Many Christians answer this by saying faith should be shown rather than sold — that a food bank run without conditions, or street pastors who help anyone home on a Friday night, preaches more honestly than a leaflet. Jesus told his followers to love their neighbour, and love does not corner people.\n\nAgainst this stands a direct command that most Christians cannot set aside. The risen Jesus tells the disciples to ‘Go and make disciples of all nations’ (Matthew 28:19), and promises that ‘you will be my witnesses’ (Acts 1:8). If Christians believe salvation is found in Christ — ‘I am the way and the truth’ (John 14:6) — then staying silent is not respect but indifference, like knowing a cure and keeping it to oneself. Evangelism, they argue, is offering rather than forcing: an Alpha course invites people to ask questions over a meal and to leave if they wish, and Fresh Expressions and Messy Church make room for people who would never enter a traditional service. Mission is also wider than words, covering healing, teaching and justice, so the choice between actions and preaching is a false one. Church growth in Britain today owes much to congregations founded by Christians who arrived from Africa and the Caribbean, for whom sharing faith is simply normal.\n\nChristians differ on how far to press it. Evangelical churches make it central; the Catholic Church teaches the duty to proclaim the gospel while also insisting on religious freedom and respectful dialogue with other faiths; some liberal Christians hold that other religions may lead to God and that conversion should never be the aim of charity.\n\nMy judgement is that the statement is right about method and wrong about principle. A Christian who genuinely believes the gospel has a reason to share it, and the command in Matthew 28:19 is too plain to ignore, so a blanket ban on persuading anyone is inconsistent with Christianity itself. But persuasion tied to food, shelter or friendship withdrawn is not evangelism, and the case against it is strong. The statement should therefore be rewritten rather than accepted: Christians should not pressure people, but they may, and most believe they must, invite them.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Distinguish the three words the specification uses — mission, evangelism and Church growth — before you agree or disagree.","Build the case for the statement first out of Christian reasons, then set the command that most Christians obey against it.","Ask whether the objection is to sharing faith at all, or to particular ways of doing it."],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — The place of mission, evangelism and Church growth","3.1.2 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Christianity"]},{"q":"‘Helping people in other countries is the most important work of the Church.’ Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Christian teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks]","a":"AO2, levels. Support: the scale of need overseas; CAFOD, Christian Aid or Tearfund in emergencies and long-term development; the parable of the sheep and the goats; the Church is worldwide, not national. Other views: local work — food banks, street pastors; worship and the sacraments are the Church’s first task; reconciliation and support for the persecuted; charity begins with the neighbour in front of you.","sol":"Much supports the statement. Poverty overseas is deeper and more lethal than poverty in Britain, and Christians believe the Church is worldwide rather than national, so a congregation in Manchester is joined to congregations facing famine or war. The named agencies show what this means in practice: CAFOD, the Catholic Agency For Overseas Development, was founded in 1962 as the official aid agency of the Catholic Church in England and Wales and works both in emergencies and on long-term development such as clean water and farming; Christian Aid, the agency of the churches of Britain and Ireland, raises money through Christian Aid Week each May and campaigns on debt and climate; Tearfund, founded in 1968, works through local churches so that communities lead their own development. The teaching is direct: in the sheep and the goats, judgement turns on whether people fed the hungry and welcomed the stranger, and Jesus says ‘I was hungry and you gave me’ something to eat (Matthew 25:35), while John insists Christians love ‘with actions and in truth’ (1 John 3:18). Overseas work also reaches persecuted Christians whom nobody else will defend.\n\nYet other work has a strong claim. The specification puts the local church alongside the worldwide one, and a food bank in the Trussell Trust network, or street pastors walking a town centre on a Friday night, meets the people God has actually placed next to a congregation; the parable of the Good Samaritan defines a neighbour as the person in front of you. Many Christians would say the Church’s first task is neither local nor global aid but worship and the sacraments, since a church that stops praying and celebrating Communion has nothing distinctive left to give, and that evangelism matters because the gospel addresses a need that food cannot. Reconciliation is another candidate: Paul says God gave the Church ‘the ministry of reconciliation’ (2 Corinthians 5:18), and the Cross of Nails network born from the bombing of Coventry Cathedral in 1940 works on the causes of the conflicts that create famine and refugees in the first place.\n\nChristians divide on emphasis rather than on principle. Catholic social teaching holds charity and worship together; evangelical churches often stress preaching first; the Iona Community ties worship directly to justice and peace.\n\nMy conclusion is that the statement is too narrow. Overseas aid is essential and is the work in which Christian giving saves most lives, so it deserves a very high place. But the Church is not only an aid agency, and if it were, its distinctive reason for acting would disappear. The strongest position is that worship forms the people who then serve, and that service is owed both to the neighbour on the street and to the stranger overseas — so the statement is right to insist on the world, wrong to rank it above everything else.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["The specification names local work and worldwide work separately, and asks for the importance of each.","Argue one side with a named agency and a named teaching, then answer it with the work the statement leaves out.","Ask what the Church would still be if it did only the work the statement praises."],"codes":["3.1.2.2 — The importance of the worldwide Church","3.1.2.2 — The role of the Church in the local community"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which three persons does the doctrine of the Trinity name?","a":"Father, Son and Holy Spirit — three persons, one God."},{"q":"Which two places of Christian pilgrimage does the specification prescribe?","a":"Lourdes and Iona."},{"q":"Which three means of salvation does the specification list?","a":"Law, grace and Spirit."},{"q":"What does atonement mean?","a":"Humanity being made at one with God again, reconciled through the death of Christ."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Trinity","back":"One God in three persons — Father, Son and Holy Spirit — equal and of one being, as the Nicene Creed confesses. Not three Gods and not three names for one person.","code":"3.1.2.1 — The nature of God: God as omnipotent, loving and just","open":false},{"front":"The Word (John 1:1-3)","back":"‘In the beginning was the Word’: the Word was with God, was God, and through him all things were made. Christians identify the Word with Christ, so the Son shares in creation.","code":"3.1.2.1 — Different Christian beliefs about creation including the role of Word and Spirit","open":false},{"front":"Resurrection of the body","back":"The belief, confessed in the Apostles’ Creed, that the dead will be raised to a transformed bodily life, not only that a soul survives. Grounded in Jesus’ own resurrection.","code":"3.1.2.1 — Different Christian beliefs about the afterlife and their importance","open":false},{"front":"Purgatory","back":"Catholic teaching: a state of purification after death for those who die in God’s friendship but are not yet ready for heaven. Most Protestants reject it as unscriptural.","code":"3.1.2.1 — Different Christian beliefs about the afterlife and their importance","open":false},{"front":"Incarnation","back":"God taking on human nature in Jesus, the Son of God, fully God and fully human — ‘The Word became flesh’ (John 1:14). Celebrated at Christmas.","code":"3.1.2.1 — Jesus Christ and salvation: beliefs and teachings about the incarnation, crucifixion, resurrection, sin and salvation","open":false},{"front":"Original sin","back":"The fallen human nature inherited from Adam and Eve’s disobedience in Genesis 3, which inclines everyone to sin. Catholic teaching: baptism washes it away, which is why infants are baptised.","code":"3.1.2.1 — Jesus Christ and salvation: beliefs and teachings about the incarnation, crucifixion, resurrection, sin and salvation","open":false},{"front":"Atonement","back":"Humanity made at one with God again through the death of Christ, who takes away the sin that separated them: ‘Christ died for our sins’ (1 Corinthians 15:3).","code":"3.1.2.1 — Jesus Christ and salvation: beliefs and teachings about the incarnation, crucifixion, resurrection, sin and salvation","open":false},{"front":"Law, grace and Spirit","back":"The three means of salvation the specification names: keeping God’s commandments; God’s free unearned gift — ‘by grace you have been saved’ (Ephesians 2:8); and the Holy Spirit at work in a believer.","code":"3.1.2.1 — Jesus Christ and salvation: beliefs and teachings about the incarnation, crucifixion, resurrection, sin and salvation","open":false},{"front":"Liturgical worship","back":"Worship following a set order and set words, led by a priest and following the church year: the Catholic Mass, the Orthodox Divine Liturgy, Anglican Common Worship. Gives reverence, familiarity and unity.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Different forms of worship and their significance","open":false},{"front":"The Lord’s Prayer","back":"The prayer Jesus taught his followers (Matthew 6:9-13), beginning ‘hallowed be your name’ before asking for daily bread and forgiveness. Used by all denominations and a usable source in an answer.","code":"3.1.2.2 — Prayer and its significance, including the Lord's Prayer","open":false},{"front":"Sacrament","back":"An outward, visible sign of an inward, invisible grace. Catholics and Orthodox hold seven; the Church of England’s Articles name two ordained by Christ, baptism and the Lord’s Supper; Quakers keep none.","code":"3.1.2.2 — The role and meaning of the sacraments","open":false},{"front":"Transubstantiation","back":"Catholic teaching that in the Mass the bread and wine truly become the body and blood of Christ, though they still look like bread and wine. Many Protestants keep Communion as a memorial instead.","code":"3.1.2.2 — The role and meaning of the sacraments","open":false},{"front":"Lourdes","back":"Catholic Marian shrine in southern France, where Bernadette Soubirous saw apparitions of Mary at the Grotto of Massabielle in 1858. Pilgrims, many of them sick, come for the spring water, the baths and processions.","code":"3.1.2.2 — The role and importance of pilgrimage and celebrations","open":false},{"front":"Iona","back":"Island in the Inner Hebrides where Columba founded a monastery in 563. Home of the ecumenical Iona Community, founded in 1938 by George MacLeod; pilgrims come for retreat, silence, shared work and peace-making.","code":"3.1.2.2 — The role and importance of pilgrimage and celebrations","open":false},{"front":"Two ways a church serves its local community","back":"Running or hosting a food bank, most of them in the Trussell Trust network; street pastors patrolling town centres on Friday and Saturday nights; night shelters, lunch clubs, parent and toddler groups or youth work.","code":"3.1.2.2 — The role of the Church in the local community","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Evaluate: ‘The resurrection of Jesus is the most important Christian belief.’)","items":["Argument: the resurrection deserves the title because every other Christian claim depends on it being true rather than merely inspiring.","Named evidence: Paul passes on the earliest summary of the gospel — that ‘Christ died for our sins’ and was raised on the third day (1 Corinthians 15:3-4) — and argues that if Christ has not been raised, faith is futile.","Development: this is not only a doctrine but a chain of reasoning. If the tomb is empty, God has publicly vindicated Jesus, so his claim to forgive sin stands; if he is risen, death has been defeated, so ‘I am the resurrection and the life’ (John 11:25) is a promise and not a metaphor; and if death has been defeated, the hope Christians bring to a funeral rests on an event rather than on optimism.","Influence applied to the issue: the belief visibly shapes practice — Christians worship on Sunday because the tomb was found empty on the first day of the week, Easter is kept as the greatest festival of the year, and the frightened disciples became witnesses (Acts 1:8) only once they believed he was alive.","Counter-view taken seriously: other Christians reply that the resurrection presupposes the incarnation — nothing is saved by a man rising unless that man is God — so ‘The Word became flesh’ (John 1:14) is the deeper foundation, and the Nicene Creed speaks of God coming down from heaven before it speaks of the third day.","Mini-judgement: the counter-view wins on logical priority but not on significance, because it is the resurrection that turns the incarnation into good news; so the statement stands, with the incarnation named as its condition rather than its rival.","Why this is Level 4: it is a well-argued response with reasoned consideration of different points of view, logical chains of reasoning leading to a judgement supported by knowledge of relevant evidence, and references to religion applied to the issue."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same statement, visibly weaker)","items":["Argument: the resurrection is the most important belief because Christians celebrate Easter and Easter is the biggest festival.","Evidence offered: ‘the Bible says Jesus rose again’ — no book, chapter or teaching is named, and no Christian tradition is identified.","Development attempted: the writer adds that this makes Christians happy and gives them hope, but does not say why an empty tomb would give hope, so the reasoning stops after one step.","Counter-view attempted: ‘some people think other beliefs are more important’ — a point of view is recognised, but no reason or evidence is attached to it, so it is not a second reasoned chain.","Ending: ‘so overall I think the statement is right’ — an opinion restated rather than a judgement drawn from the arguments made.","Descriptor it meets: Level 2 — reasoned consideration of a point of view, a logical chain of reasoning drawing on knowledge and understanding, with recognition that other views exist.","Descriptor it misses: Level 3 — reasoned consideration of different points of view with logical chains of reasoning on both sides and clear reference to religion; and Level 4 — judgements supported by knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence. Note that a version of this answer with no reference to religion at all could not rise above Level 2 whatever else it did.","The single change that would lift it: name the teaching (1 Corinthians 15:3-4, John 11:25, the Apostles’ Creed) and give the opposing view an actual reason, such as the incarnation being logically prior."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Beliefs and teachings (3.1.2.1) — each item with its source of authority","items":["God as omnipotent — ‘with God all things are possible’ (Matthew 19:26); God as loving — ‘God is love’ (1 John 4:8); God as just — ‘all his ways are just’ (Deuteronomy 32:4).","The oneness of God and the Trinity — one God in three persons, Father, Son and Holy Spirit: the Nicene Creed; the baptism of Jesus, with the Father’s voice, the Son and the Spirit as a dove (Matthew 3:16-17).","The problem of evil and suffering — an omnipotent, loving and just God and a suffering world. Christian responses: free will and the Fall (Genesis 3); Job, who is never told why; the cross, where Jesus cries ‘why have you forsaken me?’ (Mark 15:34).","Creation by word and Spirit — ‘In the beginning God created’ and ‘the Spirit of God was hovering’ over the waters, then ‘Let there be light’ (Genesis 1:1-3).","Creation through the Word — ‘In the beginning was the Word’, who was with God and was God, and ‘through him all things were made’ (John 1:3), the same Word who becomes flesh (John 1:14).","Different Christian beliefs about creation — literal six-day creationism, judging science by Scripture; non-literal readings, including the Catholic Church’s, accepting the Big Bang and evolution as the means God used.","Resurrection and life after death — ‘I am the resurrection and the life’ (John 11:25); the Apostles’ Creed: ‘the resurrection of the body’ and ‘the life everlasting’.","Judgement, heaven and hell — the sheep and the goats: the righteous go ‘to eternal life’ (Matthew 25:46), judged on feeding the hungry and welcoming the stranger (Matthew 25:35). Divergence: hell as everlasting punishment, or as freely chosen separation from God; Catholic teaching adds purgatory, which most Protestants reject.","Incarnation — ‘The Word became flesh’ (John 1:14); the Nicene Creed says he came down from heaven for us and for our salvation; Jesus is fully God and fully human, the Son of God.","Crucifixion, resurrection and ascension — crucified under Pontius Pilate, died and was buried; raised on the third day (1 Corinthians 15:4); appearing to the apostles over forty days (Acts 1:3), then taken up into heaven (Acts 1:9), with the Nicene Creed adding that he is ‘seated at the right hand of the Father’.","Sin and original sin — ‘all have sinned’ (Romans 3:23); ‘the wages of sin is death’ (Romans 6:23); the fallen nature inherited from Adam and Eve’s disobedience (Genesis 3), the Catholic reason for infant baptism.","The means of salvation: law, grace and Spirit — the commandments given at Sinai (Exodus 20) and Jesus’ command to love God and neighbour; ‘by grace you have been saved’ (Ephesians 2:8); the Holy Spirit producing love, joy and peace in a believer (Galatians 5:22-23).","Atonement — ‘Christ died for our sins’ (1 Corinthians 15:3); Jesus called ‘the Lamb of God’ (John 1:29), whose death takes sin away; humanity made at one with God again."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Practices (3.1.2.2) — each practice with its detail and its source of authority","items":["Liturgical worship — set words and a set order following the church year: the Catholic Mass, the Orthodox Divine Liturgy, Church of England services from Common Worship or the Book of Common Prayer. Significance: reverence, continuity, unity with Christians everywhere.","Non-liturgical worship — a shape but no fixed words, in Methodist, Baptist and United Reformed churches: hymns, extempore prayer, Bible reading and a long sermon, so the Bible carries the service.","Informal worship — spontaneous and charismatic, as in Pentecostal churches: free prayer, prophecy, speaking in tongues. At the other extreme, Quakers meet in silence and wait to be moved to speak.","Private worship — prayer, Bible reading and study at home; the rosary for Catholics; icons for the Orthodox; grace before meals.","Prayer — the Lord’s Prayer, given by Jesus in the Sermon on the Mount (Matthew 6:9-13), opening ‘hallowed be your name’; set prayers such as collects, the Hail Mary and the rosary; informal prayer in a person’s own words.","Sacrament — an outward sign of inward grace. Seven for Catholics and Orthodox (baptism, confirmation, Eucharist, reconciliation, anointing of the sick, holy orders, matrimony); two named by the Church of England’s Thirty-Nine Articles (baptism and the Lord’s Supper); none for Quakers and the Salvation Army.","Baptism — water in the name of the Father, Son and Holy Spirit (Matthew 28:19). Infant baptism: Catholic, Orthodox, Anglican and Methodist, with parents and godparents promising, original sin washed away. Believers’ baptism: Baptist and Pentecostal, by full immersion, after a personal profession of faith.","Holy Communion / Eucharist — also called the Mass, the Divine Liturgy, the Lord’s Supper and the Breaking of Bread; ‘do this in remembrance of me’ (1 Corinthians 11:24). Catholic teaching: transubstantiation. Many Protestants, including Baptists: a memorial. The Orthodox affirm the change as a mystery beyond explanation.","Pilgrimage: Lourdes — southern France, in the foothills of the Pyrenees; apparitions of the Virgin Mary to Bernadette Soubirous at the Grotto of Massabielle in 1858; a spring, baths, Mass at the grotto and the torchlight procession; very large numbers of pilgrims, many sick, cared for by volunteers.","Pilgrimage: Iona — a small island in the Inner Hebrides; Columba came from Ireland and founded a monastery in 563; Iona Abbey is home to the ecumenical Iona Community, founded in 1938 by the Church of Scotland minister George MacLeod; pilgrims come for retreat, silence, shared work and commitment to peace and justice.","Christmas — the incarnation; Advent beforehand; Midnight Mass and Christmas morning services, carol services, nativity plays, cribs, presents and charitable giving. In Britain today many attend church only at Christmas, and Christians debate its commercialisation.","Easter — the resurrection; Lent from Ash Wednesday, Holy Week from Palm Sunday through Maundy Thursday and Good Friday; the Easter Vigil with new fire and the paschal candle; sunrise services. The greatest festival of the Christian year.","The local church — food banks, most within the Trussell Trust network, run on referral vouchers giving about three days of emergency food; street pastors, begun in Brixton in 2003 under the Reverend Les Isaac and the Ascension Trust, patrolling town centres on Friday and Saturday nights with water, flip-flops and foil blankets. Source: ‘I was hungry and you gave me’ something to eat (Matthew 25:35).","Mission, evangelism and Church growth — ‘Go and make disciples of all nations’ (Matthew 28:19); ‘you will be my witnesses’ (Acts 1:8). The Alpha course, begun at Holy Trinity Brompton; Fresh Expressions, run jointly by the Church of England and the Methodist Church; Messy Church; congregations founded in Britain by Christians from Africa and the Caribbean.","The worldwide Church — reconciliation: ‘the ministry of reconciliation’ (2 Corinthians 5:18) and ‘Blessed are the peacemakers’ (Matthew 5:9); response to persecution: prayer, publicity and lobbying through agencies such as Open Doors and Aid to the Church in Need, and practical support for persecuted congregations."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the named case studies — the dates, places and figures an examiner rewards","items":["Lourdes — Bernadette Soubirous, a girl of fourteen from a very poor family, saw a series of apparitions of the Virgin Mary at the Grotto of Massabielle in 1858; she was later canonised. The shrine is Catholic and Marian; the water from the spring is drunk and used in the baths; the day ends with the torchlight Marian procession, and there is a procession of the Blessed Sacrament for the sick. Volunteers accompany and carry sick pilgrims. Only a small number of cures have ever been officially recognised by the Church as miraculous.","Iona — in the Inner Hebrides, off the west coast of Scotland; Columba sailed from Ireland and founded his monastery in 563, from which Christianity spread through Scotland and northern England. Iona Abbey is now the home of the Iona Community, founded in 1938 by George MacLeod, a Church of Scotland minister, who rebuilt the abbey with unemployed Glasgow craftsmen and trainee ministers working side by side. The community is ecumenical and known for simple worship, shared work and campaigning for peace and justice.","The contrast the specification intends — Lourdes: Catholic, Marian, centred on healing, huge crowds, water and processions. Iona: ecumenical and Protestant in origin, Celtic, centred on silence, retreat, community and justice, with small numbers and no shrine.","Food banks — most British food banks belong to the Trussell Trust network, a Christian charity founded in the 1990s; the great majority operate from church premises with church volunteers. Clients are referred by a professional with a voucher and receive around three days of emergency food, plus a drink and time to talk; many centres now offer debt and benefits advice alongside.","Street pastors — begun in Brixton, London, in 2003 by the Reverend Les Isaac through the Ascension Trust; teams of trained volunteers from local churches, in uniform, patrol town centres on Friday and Saturday nights in partnership with the police and the council. They listen, help people who are drunk, lost or vulnerable get home safely, sweep up broken glass, and hand out water, flip-flops and foil blankets.","CAFOD — the Catholic Agency For Overseas Development, founded in 1962, the official aid agency of the Catholic Church in England and Wales and a member of the Caritas Internationalis network. Emergency relief after disasters; long-term development in water, health, farming and education; campaigning on justice, and Family Fast Day appeals in parishes.","Christian Aid — the relief and development agency of churches in Britain and Ireland, founded in the aftermath of the Second World War to help refugees in Europe. Best known for Christian Aid Week each May; works through local partner organisations rather than sending its own staff, and campaigns on debt, tax justice and climate change.","Tearfund — founded in 1968, an evangelical Christian relief and development agency that works through local churches in the countries it serves, on the principle that the local church is best placed to lift its own community out of poverty; emergency response, clean water, sanitation and advocacy.","Reconciliation — Coventry Cathedral: the medieval cathedral was destroyed by bombing in 1940; a cross was made from two charred roof beams, another from medieval nails, and the words ‘Father Forgive’ were set behind the altar of the ruins. The Community of the Cross of Nails now links reconciliation projects worldwide. Corrymeela, founded in 1965 in Northern Ireland, brings Catholics and Protestants together to live, work and talk.","Persecution — Christians in some countries face imprisonment, violence and the destruction of church buildings. Churches respond by praying for the persecuted, publicising cases and lobbying governments through agencies such as Open Doors and Aid to the Church in Need, by sending practical and legal help, and by teaching that suffering for the faith follows Jesus, who told his followers they would be hated for his name."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — ‘A God who is loving and just would not allow people to suffer.’ Evaluate this statement. [12 marks] [SPaG 3 marks]","items":["Decode the statement: it is not asking whether suffering exists. It sets two of God’s attributes — loving and just — against the world, and the third, omnipotence, is implied, because a God who wanted to stop suffering but could not would be off the hook. So the question is whether all three can be held together with famine, cancer and cruelty.","Timing: about twelve minutes for the answer plus the spelling, punctuation and grammar marks, so roughly two minutes planning and ten writing — and this is the Beliefs evaluation, so the three SPaG marks are awarded here.","Do not open with definitions. One sentence naming the three attributes and the tension between them is enough; the levels reward argument, not exposition.","Paragraph 1, supporting the statement: the three attributes conflict with the world as it is. Sources: ‘with God all things are possible’ (Matthew 19:26), ‘God is love’ (1 John 4:8), ‘all his ways are just’ (Deuteronomy 32:4). Chain: God could prevent it; love would want to; justice would refuse to let the innocent bear it; it happens anyway.","Paragraph 2, strengthening the support: natural evil. Earthquakes and childhood illness are nobody’s choice, so free will does not cover them, and suffering on that scale often destroys people rather than improving them. This is the statement at its strongest and must be conceded honestly.","Paragraph 3, the different point of view — free will and the Fall: God made people able to love and therefore able to refuse (Genesis 3). Most of the worst suffering is human, and a world in which God blocked every wrong choice would contain no real choices and so no real love.","Paragraph 4, the different point of view deepened — God shares it: the incarnation means God became human and was tortured to death; on the cross Jesus cries ‘why have you forsaken me?’ (Mark 15:34); the resurrection says suffering is not the last word; and judgement completes justice beyond death, since the righteous go ‘to eternal life’ (Matthew 25:46). Job can be added as the teaching that Christians are not promised an explanation.","The evaluation thread running through every paragraph: each side is tested against the same question — does this account keep all three attributes intact, or does it quietly drop one?","Divergence within Christianity, which is what this paper wants in place of another religion: some Christians hold the free-will answer confidently, others admit natural evil remains unexplained and treat faith here as trust rather than explanation; some read the Fall literally, others symbolically.","What the conclusion must say: not ‘both sides have a point’. It must name which chain was stronger and why — for example, that the statement succeeds against natural evil but fails against moral evil, and that it assumes love means preventing all pain, which Christian teaching denies, since the cross shows a love that enters suffering rather than abolishing it. Therefore the statement is a real challenge but not a disproof.","SPaG: spell and use the technical vocabulary — omnipotent, benevolent, just, incarnation, resurrection, free will, the Fall — accurately and deliberately, because a wide range of specialist terms used appropriately is what the top performance descriptor asks for."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T14:02:00.181713Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.94,"notes":"All four fixes landed and introduced no new error — Orthodox triple immersion in the font is now correctly distinguished from Western affusion in both q8 sol and the Sacraments idea, Acts 1:3 / Acts 1:9 / the Nicene Creed's 'seated at the right hand of the Father' are each cited to the right source, and the open card's front is now an item; the kit is publishable."}}},"3.1.3":{"id":"AQA-8062","topic":"3.1.3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Catholic Christianity is one of the seven religion options on Component 1, and if you sit it you answer two compulsory five-part questions on it: one on Beliefs and teachings and one on Practices. That is forty-eight of the marks on your religion booklet, plus the three spelling, punctuation and grammar marks carried by the evaluation in the Beliefs section. Almost everything in this room is scored the same way: state a belief or practice accurately, develop it with a reason, an example or a consequence, name the Catholic source it rests on, and show where another Christian tradition would disagree. This room exists because Catholic Christianity is not the generic Christianity room with incense added. It has its own named content — the Council of Nicaea, purgatory, the seven sacraments, the Rosary and the Stations of the Cross, Gaudium et Spes paragraph 26, CAFOD, Trócaire, Missio, the SVP, and either Pax Christi or Corrymeela — and the examiner rewards precision about all of it: the right paragraph number, the right date, the right sacrament, the right tradition attached to the right belief.","idea":[{"code":"3.1.3 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views","h":"The three things every answer has to carry","t":"Catholic Christianity is one of the diverse religious traditions in Great Britain today, and the main religious tradition here is Christianity. The specification puts three standing demands on every answer. First, sources of wisdom and authority: each belief and practice must be tied to something Catholic — scripture, the Nicene Creed, the Catechism, a Church council or a papal document such as Gaudium et Spes — and the five-mark question gives a whole mark for exactly that. Behind the Creed, the Catechism, the councils and the papal documents stands the Magisterium, the teaching authority of the Church, exercised by the pope and the bishops in communion with him; it is the word an examiner rewards when you say where a Catholic teaching comes from. Second, influence: you must be able to say what individuals, communities and societies actually do differently because of the belief. Third, common and divergent views within Christianity, where the specification names Orthodox and Protestant perspectives alongside the Catholic one. A top-level answer never leaves a belief bare: it names the source, names the effect, and names the tradition that would answer differently."},{"code":"3.1.3.1 — One God as a Trinity of persons: Father, Son and Holy Spirit","h":"One God in three persons, defined at Nicaea","t":"Catholics believe in one God who is a Trinity of persons: Father, Son and Holy Spirit. The three are distinct persons but not three gods and not three parts of God; each is wholly God. The scriptural origins are in the New Testament rather than in a single sentence: Matthew 28:19, where the risen Jesus tells the disciples to baptise in the threefold name of Father, Son and Holy Spirit, and the baptism of Jesus in Mark 1:9-11, where the Son is in the water, the Spirit descends and the Father's voice is heard. The doctrine was defined at the Council of Nicaea in 325, called to settle whether the Son was a creature made by the Father. The Nicene Creed said at Sunday Mass gives the answer the council reached: the Son is 'consubstantial with the Father' and 'begotten, not made' — of one being with the Father, not a creature the Father produced. A top-level answer uses the Creed as its named source and explains why 'begotten, not made' mattered — it rules out a Son who is less than God."},{"code":"3.1.3.1 — Creation: Biblical accounts of creation (Genesis 1 and 2)","h":"Genesis 1 and 2: God, human dignity, and the earth","t":"The specification prescribes the two biblical creation accounts and names three things they are significant for. The nature of God: in Genesis 1 God creates freely, by his word, in order, out of nothing, and calls what he has made 'very good' (Genesis 1:31), so creation is deliberate and good rather than accidental. The dignity of human beings: Genesis 1:27 says humanity is made 'in his own image', and Genesis 2:7 has God form the human being from the dust and breathe life into him, so dignity is given by God, belongs to everyone equally, and cannot be earned or forfeited. Humanity's relationship with creation: Genesis 1:28 gives dominion and Genesis 2:15 the task of tilling and keeping the garden, which Catholics read as stewardship, developed by Pope Francis in Laudato Si' (2015). Different Christian beliefs matter here: some Protestant Christians read the six days literally, while the Catholic Church reads the accounts for their religious truth and accepts scientific accounts of how the universe developed — Pope St John Paul II told the Pontifical Academy of Sciences in 1996 that evolution was more than a hypothesis. A top-level answer uses human dignity as the bridge from these chapters to Catholic social teaching later in the room."},{"code":"3.1.3.1 — Incarnation: the belief in Jesus as incarnate Son, divine Word","h":"Incarnation: fully God and fully human","t":"Incarnation means 'becoming flesh'. Catholics believe that the eternal Son of God, the divine Word, took a human nature and was born of the Virgin Mary. The scriptural origin the specification asks for is John's prologue: John 1:1 begins 'In the beginning was the Word', and John 1:14 says 'the Word became flesh'. Luke's account of the Annunciation and the birth tells the same belief as a story. The Nicene Creed states it in the words Catholics say every Sunday: the Son 'was incarnate of the Virgin Mary' and became man. The claim is exact and not a compromise: Jesus is fully God and fully human, not half of each and not God wearing a disguise, a point defined further at the Council of Chalcedon in 451. The influence runs through Catholic life — Christmas, the crib, the Angelus — and through Catholic ethics, because if God took a human body then human bodies and human lives cannot be treated as disposable. A top-level answer gives the scriptural origin, not only the definition."},{"code":"3.1.3.1 — Redemption: the significance of the life, death, resurrection and ascension of Jesus","h":"Redemption: one saving act in four events","t":"The specification takes the life, death, resurrection and ascension of Jesus together and asks what they mean for salvation and grace. The life teaches and heals and shows what God is like. The death is sacrifice: Mark 10:45 says the Son of Man came to give his life 'a ransom for many', so the cost of sin is paid by Christ and not by human effort. The resurrection defeats death and is the ground of Christian hope; 1 Corinthians 15:3-4 has Christ dying for our sins and being raised on the third day. The ascension takes human nature into God's own life and leads to Pentecost and the Church. Salvation, on this teaching, is received rather than achieved, because grace is God's free and undeserved help, and the sacraments are the ordinary channels through which Catholics receive it — which is what ties this belief to the seven sacraments. That does not make behaviour irrelevant: James 2:26 teaches that faith without works is dead. A top-level answer uses grace to answer the classic evaluation, 'is faith alone enough?', rather than simply retelling Holy Week."},{"code":"3.1.3.1 — Beliefs about life after death: resurrection, judgement, heaven, hell and purgatory","h":"Resurrection, judgement, heaven, hell and purgatory","t":"Catholics believe in the resurrection of the body, not merely the survival of a soul: the Nicene Creed looks forward to the resurrection of the dead and the life of the world to come, and Jesus says in John 11:25, 'I am the resurrection and the life'. There are two judgements: the particular judgement, which each person meets at death, and the Last Judgement at the end of time, pictured in Matthew 25:31-46 where people are judged by what they did for 'one of the least of these'. Heaven is full and unending life with God; hell is the state of those who freely and finally refuse that life. Purgatory is the distinctively Catholic term the specification names: the final purification of those who die in God's grace and friendship but are not yet ready for heaven. It is not a second chance and not a punishment for its own sake. It grounds prayer for the dead, Masses offered for the dead and All Souls' Day on 2 November, and Catholics point to 2 Maccabees 12:46 in support. Most Protestant Churches reject purgatory, both because they do not accept 2 Maccabees as scripture and because they hold that Christ's death alone purifies. A top-level answer links each belief to the practice it produces."},{"code":"3.1.3.1 — The meaning and significance of 'sacrament' and the sacramental nature of reality","h":"Sacrament, and the sacramental nature of reality","t":"A sacrament is an outward, visible sign, instituted by Christ, which actually gives the inward grace it signifies; the Catechism calls the sacraments efficacious signs of grace, meaning they do what they show rather than merely picturing it. Behind the seven sits a wider conviction the specification names: the sacramental nature of reality. Because God made the material world and called it good, and because the Son took flesh in it, matter can carry God. That is why water, oil, bread, wine, words, hands and touch do real work in this tradition, and why a Catholic church is full of candles, statues, incense and holy water rather than stripped bare. The contrast is sharp: Quakers and the Salvation Army keep no sacraments at all, holding that God is met directly and inwardly. A top-level answer explains why matter can carry grace by reaching back to creation and the Incarnation, instead of simply defining the word."},{"code":"3.1.3.1 — The names, meanings and effects of the seven sacraments","h":"The seven sacraments, with the eucharist at the centre","t":"Three things are asked of each sacrament: its name, its meaning and its effect. The seven fall into three groups. Sacraments of initiation: baptism, where water is poured with the words 'I baptise you in the name' of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit, washing away original sin and making the person a member of the Church; confirmation, where the bishop anoints the forehead with chrism, sealing the candidate 'with the Gift' of the Holy Spirit, strengthening baptismal grace; and the eucharist. Sacraments of healing: reconciliation — contrition, confession to a priest, a penance and absolution, whose effect is forgiveness and peace with God and the Church — and anointing of the sick, where a priest anoints the forehead and hands with the oil of the sick to give strength, peace and forgiveness. Sacraments at the service of communion: matrimony, where the couple themselves are the ministers and their consent makes a lifelong, faithful union open to children, and holy orders, where a bishop lays hands on a man to ordain him deacon, priest or bishop. The eucharist is singled out as 'the source and summit of Christian life': Catholics believe in transubstantiation, that the bread and wine become the body and blood of Christ while the appearances remain. Divergent views are examinable: Orthodox Christians affirm a real change without using the scholastic term, and many Protestants, such as Baptists, treat the Lord's Supper as a memorial. A top-level answer always gives the effect, not just the description of the ceremony."},{"code":"3.1.3.2 — Prayer: formal prayers and informal prayer, and the Lord's Prayer","h":"Prayer: raising the mind and heart to God","t":"The specification quotes the definition, so quote it back: prayer is 'the raising of the mind and heart to God'. Formal prayers are set words shared by the whole Church — the Mass, the Lord's Prayer, the Hail Mary, the Glory Be, the Rosary's prayers. They give a Catholic words when grief or distraction leaves them with none, they keep prayer from becoming a conversation with oneself, and they join a person praying alone at home to millions praying the same words. Informal prayer is spontaneous and in your own words, and its defenders argue it is more honest and more personal, while its critics say it drifts into asking for things. The specification builds the contrast in, so be ready to argue both sides. The Lord's Prayer is asked for by meaning as well as importance: Jesus gave it in Matthew 6:9-13, the Catechism calls it 'the summary of the whole Gospel', and its petitions shape Catholic life — God is 'Our Father', not my father; daily bread is asked for one day at a time; and being forgiven is tied to forgiving others. A top-level answer takes a side on relative importance and gives a reason, rather than describing both kinds."},{"code":"3.1.3.2 — The role and importance of forms of popular piety","h":"Popular piety: the Rosary and the Stations of the Cross","t":"Popular piety means devotions that grew up among ordinary Catholics and are warmly encouraged by the Church without being sacraments. The specification names two. The Rosary is prayed on a string of beads: five decades, each made of an Our Father, ten Hail Marys and a Glory Be, prayed while meditating on a mystery from the life of Christ. The traditional sets are the Joyful, Sorrowful and Glorious Mysteries, and Pope St John Paul II added the Luminous Mysteries in 2002. Its value is that the repeated words quiet the mind so that the events of the Gospel can be thought about. The Stations of the Cross are fourteen scenes from Jesus' condemnation to his burial, marked around the walls of a Catholic church and walked in turn, especially on Fridays in Lent and on Good Friday, so that the worshipper accompanies Christ rather than reads about him. Both are devotions, not sacraments: strongly recommended, never required for salvation, and some Christians object that the Rosary gives too much attention to Mary. A top-level answer uses that devotion-not-sacrament distinction whenever an evaluation asks what matters most in Catholic life."},{"code":"3.1.3.2 — The role and importance of pilgrimage","h":"Pilgrimage, and Lourdes as the place studied","t":"A pilgrimage is a journey to a holy place undertaken for a religious reason. The specification requires one place of Roman Catholic pilgrimage to be studied in depth, and Lourdes in south-west France is the usual choice. In 1858 a fourteen-year-old girl, Bernadette Soubirous, saw Mary eighteen times at the grotto at Massabielle; a spring appeared there, and millions of pilgrims now come every year. Pilgrims attend Mass at the grotto, are bathed in or sprinkled with the water, walk in the torchlight Marian procession and in the Blessed Sacrament procession, and above all care for sick and disabled pilgrims — British diocesan pilgrimages and HCPT, the Pilgrimage Trust, exist largely for that purpose. The role of pilgrimage is to leave ordinary life behind, to do penance, to pray for healing and to return changed. The divergence the specification asks for is real and useful: many Protestant Christians hold that pilgrimage is unnecessary because God is equally present everywhere, that the New Testament commands no such journey, and that the money would be better spent on the poor. A top-level answer argues from the effect on the pilgrim, not from the itinerary."},{"code":"3.1.3.2 — The funeral rite and its significance","h":"The funeral rite, and what each sign means","t":"A Catholic funeral is normally a Requiem Mass, and almost every part of it states a belief from the Beliefs section of this room. The coffin is received at the church door, sprinkled with holy water and covered with a white pall, recalling the person's baptism and the white garment they were given then. The Paschal candle, lit at Easter, stands beside it as the sign of the risen Christ. The readings often include Jesus' words in John 11:25, 'I am the resurrection and the life'. The Mass itself is offered for the person who has died, which only makes sense because Catholics believe in purgatory and therefore that prayer can still help them. The Final Commendation and farewell entrust them to God's mercy, and the Rite of Committal follows at the grave or crematorium. Burial and cremation are both permitted, but the Church asks that ashes be buried in a sacred place rather than scattered or kept at home, because the body is destined for resurrection. A top-level answer explains the significance of the signs rather than listing the order of service."},{"code":"3.1.3.2 — Catholic beliefs about the essential duty to 'love our neighbour'","h":"Love of neighbour, made concrete at three scales","t":"Jesus' second great commandment is to 'love your neighbour as yourself' (Mark 12:31), and the parable of the Good Samaritan (Luke 10:25-37) settles who counts as a neighbour: anyone in need, including the person you were raised to despise. The specification prescribes Gaudium et Spes paragraph 26 by paragraph number, so cite it precisely: this Pastoral Constitution of the Second Vatican Council (1965) defines the common good as 'the sum total of social conditions' — everything in a society that lets people, in groups and one by one, flourish as fully and as easily as possible — and lists what that requires, including food, clothing, housing, work, education and freedom to act according to conscience. Catholic social teaching's principles follow: human dignity, the common good, solidarity, subsidiarity and the preferential option for the poor, together with teaching on justice, peace and reconciliation. The duty is specified at three scales. Locally: parish food banks and the SVP. Nationally: campaigning on poverty and homelessness. Globally: CAFOD, the Catholic Agency for Overseas Development, founded in 1962 for England and Wales; Trócaire, founded in 1973 by the Irish Catholic bishops; and Missio. A top-level answer cites the paragraph number and names the scale it is arguing about."},{"code":"3.1.3.2 — The meaning and significance of mission and evangelism for Catholics today","h":"Mission and evangelism, globally and in Britain","t":"Mission means being sent; evangelism means sharing the good news. The charter text is the Great Commission, where the risen Jesus tells the disciples to 'make disciples of all nations' (Matthew 28:19). Globally, Missio is the Pope's official charity for overseas mission in England and Wales, part of the Pontifical Mission Societies: its Red Boxes in Catholic homes and its Mission Together programme in schools fund priests, catechists and children's projects in poorer countries. Nationally and locally, the specification insists on the British picture, and it looks different: Catholic schools, RCIA groups preparing adults for baptism or reception at the Easter Vigil, parish missions, youth ministry, prison and hospital chaplaincy, and the plain witness of how Catholics treat people. Pope St Paul VI's Evangelii Nuntiandi (1975) made the point that people today listen more readily to witnesses than to teachers, and Pope Francis' Evangelii Gaudium (2013) asks the Church to go out to the margins rather than wait for people to come in. A top-level answer distinguishes evangelism by word from evangelism by witness and says which Britain responds to."},{"code":"3.1.3.2 — The aims and importance of the Society of Saint Vincent de Paul (SVP)","h":"The Society of Saint Vincent de Paul","t":"The SVP is a lay Catholic society founded in Paris in 1833 by Frédéric Ozanam and a group of fellow students, who were challenged to show what their faith actually did for the poor. It is named after St Vincent de Paul, the seventeenth-century priest who organised charity for the poor of France. Members are called Vincentians and work in local groups called conferences, usually attached to a parish. The aims the specification asks for are person-to-person: to visit the lonely, the housebound, the sick, the bereaved and the imprisoned; to give practical help such as food, furniture, clothing or help with fuel bills; to run SVP shops and holidays and camps for children and families; and to help anyone in need regardless of their beliefs. Its slogan, turning concern into action, is the point. Its importance is that it makes the duty to love our neighbour local, personal and unglamorous, and it is the standing answer to the charge that Catholic charity is all done at a distance by cheque. A top-level answer pairs the SVP with CAFOD to show the local and the global ends of the same duty."},{"code":"3.1.3.2 — The aims and importance of either the Corrymeela community or Pax Christi","h":"Pax Christi, with Corrymeela as the alternative","t":"The specification requires one of two, and asks for aims and importance rather than history. Pax Christi is the international Catholic movement for peace, begun in France in 1945 to bring about reconciliation between French and German Christians after the Second World War. Its aims are to promote nonviolence and Gospel peacemaking, to work for disarmament and human rights, to run peace education in schools and parishes, and to support conscientious objectors and victims of war; Pax Christi in England and Wales promotes Peace Sunday each January. Its charter text is Jesus' beatitude, 'Blessed are the peacemakers' (Matthew 5:9). The alternative is the Corrymeela Community, founded in 1965 by Ray Davey at Ballycastle in County Antrim, which brings Catholics and Protestants together for residential reconciliation work in Northern Ireland and is the oldest peace and reconciliation organisation in Ireland. Both turn the Church's teaching on justice, peace and reconciliation into something a student can point at. A top-level answer argues why organised peace work is a religious duty and not merely politics."}],"examples":[{"title":"The 4-mark contrasting question: what happens to the bread and wine","setup":"The question in front of you reads: 'Explain two contrasting Christian beliefs about what happens to the bread and wine at the eucharist. [4 marks]' There is no instruction to refer to a source and no fifth mark on offer. The marks are given as two for the first contrasting belief and two for the second, and a simple explanation of each earns one while a detailed explanation earns two.","steps":["Take the Catholic belief first and name it: at Mass the bread and wine become the body and blood of Christ, a change the Church calls transubstantiation.","Develop it so it counts as detailed rather than simple: the appearances of bread and wine remain unchanged while what they really are has changed, which is why the consecrated host is reserved in the tabernacle and Catholics genuflect to it.","Open the second belief with words that force the contrast: 'In contrast, many Protestant Christians, such as Baptists, believe the Lord's Supper is a memorial.'","Develop the second belief from its own source: they read Jesus' words at the Last Supper, 'Do this in memory of me' (Luke 22:19), as a command to remember his death rather than a promise that the bread changes, so the bread stays ordinary and nothing is kept afterwards.","Test the contrast before you move on: if the same Christian could hold both of the views you have written, they are similar rather than contrasting, and only one of them can be credited."],"cues":["Which view of the bread and wine belongs to the Catholic Church itself, and what is its one-word name?","What has to be added before an explanation counts as detailed rather than simple?","Which named tradition gives a genuinely opposing view, and how should that sentence open?","Which words of Jesus at the Last Supper does that tradition build its view on?","How can you check on the page that your two beliefs really do contrast?"]},{"title":"The 4-mark influence question: purgatory","setup":"The question reads: 'Explain two ways in which belief in purgatory influences Catholics today. [4 marks]' You already know the belief: purgatory is the final purification of those who die in God's grace and friendship but are not yet ready for heaven. The examiner gives two marks for each way, and the second mark in each case is only given if the influence itself is shown.","steps":["Decide what kind of thing you are looking for: not the doctrine restated, but what the living now do that only makes sense if purgatory is real.","First way: Catholics pray for their dead, asking for Masses to be offered for relatives and friends and keeping All Souls' Day on 2 November.","Add the influence so the second mark is earned: because the dead may still be being purified, bereavement becomes something a Catholic can act on, so grief comes out as prayer rather than helplessness.","Second way: the funeral rite is shaped by the belief — the Requiem Mass is offered for the person who has died and the prayers ask God to bring them into eternal light.","Add the influence again: a Catholic funeral is therefore a prayer for the dead person rather than only a celebration of a life, which is the difference the marker is looking for."],"cues":["What kind of material does an 'influence' question want from you, and what will it not reward?","What do Catholics do for their dead that would be pointless if purgatory did not exist?","That is the practice named — what still has to be said about it?","Which Catholic ceremony shows the same belief most clearly?","What does the belief change about the purpose of that ceremony?"]},{"title":"The 5-mark question: two Catholic teachings about the Trinity","setup":"The question reads: 'Explain two Catholic teachings about the Trinity. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Catholic belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]' Four of the marks come from two teachings, each worth one for a simple explanation and two for a detailed one. The fifth is a separate mark, given once, for a relevant and accurate reference to a source.","steps":["First teaching, stated so it cannot be misread: there is one God, and this one God is three persons, Father, Son and Holy Spirit — not three gods, and not three parts of one God.","Develop it: each person is wholly God, so Catholics deny that the Son is a lesser being produced by the Father; the Son shares the very same being.","Second teaching: the three persons are equal and eternal, and the Church did not invent this but worked it out, defining it at the Council of Nicaea in 325 against the claim that the Son was created.","Develop the second teaching by what the definition ruled out: 'begotten, not made' denies that there was ever a time when the Son did not exist.","Go after the fifth mark deliberately, because it is separate: name the source on the page — the Nicene Creed, said at Sunday Mass, calls the Son 'consubstantial with the Father', and Matthew 28:19 gives the threefold name used at baptism."],"cues":["What single sentence states the doctrine without sliding into three gods or into three parts?","What must you add before the first teaching counts as detailed?","Where and when did the Church settle this question, and what exactly was it settling?","Which three words of the Creed rule out the idea that the Son is a creature?","The fifth mark is awarded on its own — what has to appear on the page to earn it?"]},{"title":"Building a Level 4 evaluation: pilgrimage against the poor","setup":"The question reads: 'Catholics should spend their money helping the poor rather than going on pilgrimage.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Catholic teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks]","steps":["Decode the statement before writing a word: it is not asking whether pilgrimage is permitted, but whether the same money would do more good given away — so both sides have to argue about the same money.","Build the supporting case from named teaching: the second great commandment, 'love your neighbour as yourself' (Mark 12:31), the judgement in Matthew 25:31-46 on what was done for 'one of the least of these', and Gaudium et Spes paragraph 26 on the common good.","Turn that into a chain rather than a list: dignity comes from being made in God's image, meeting a person's basic needs serves that dignity directly, a flight to Lourdes serves the pilgrim, and the SVP and CAFOD exist because Catholics accepted exactly that reasoning.","Build the opposing case to the same standard: most British pilgrimages to Lourdes exist to take sick and disabled people there, so the money buys care rather than a holiday, and Catholics answer the objection with Jesus' words at Bethany, 'the poor you always have with you' (Mark 14:7).","Add the Catholic move that dissolves the either-or: the grace received on pilgrimage is what sends people home to staff the SVP conference, so the choice the statement forces is a false one.","Judge rather than summarise: say which argument is stronger and why — for instance, that the statement is right about pilgrimage taken as comfort and wrong about pilgrimage taken as service."],"cues":["What exactly is this statement asking you to weigh, and what is it not asking?","Which named Catholic teachings make the strongest case for giving the money away instead?","How do you turn those teachings into a chain of reasoning rather than a list of quotations?","What can a Catholic say for pilgrimage that is stronger than 'it helps my faith'?","Is there a Catholic answer that refuses the choice the statement forces on you?","What must the final paragraph do that a summary of both sides would not?"]},{"title":"Repairing a weak evaluation: from a point of view to a judgement","setup":"A student was asked: 'Nothing a Catholic does is more important than going to Mass.' Evaluate this statement. [12 marks] [SPaG 3 marks] Their complete answer reads: 'I think Mass is very important because Catholics go every Sunday and it is part of their religion. Some people might disagree because they are busy on Sundays. So Mass is important.' Your job is to say where it sits on the levels and rewrite it so that it rises.","steps":["Place it honestly: it is a point of view with a reason stated in support and nothing more, and the 'they are busy' line is not a religious reason, so it does not lift the answer.","Give the first argument a named source and a consequence: the Church calls the eucharist 'the source and summit of Christian life', and Catholics believe transubstantiation makes Christ himself present, so missing Mass means missing Christ rather than missing a habit.","Make the opposing view religious as well: the second great commandment and the judgement scene in Matthew 25:31-46 put care of the poor at the centre, which is why the SVP and CAFOD exist — now two points of view are each supported by reasons.","Join the two into a chain that leads somewhere: the Mass ends by sending Catholics out, so the two duties feed each other rather than compete, and that connection is the reasoning the top of the grid is looking for.","Close with a judgement that follows from what you have just argued rather than a fresh opinion: the statement is true if 'important' means the source of everything else, and false if it means the only thing that counts."],"cues":["Read the answer as an examiner would: which descriptor does it actually meet?","What would turn its opening sentence from an opinion into an argument?","Why does 'they are busy' fail, and what would a religious counter-argument look like instead?","The answer now has two supported sides — what is still missing at the top of the grid?","What must the last sentence do before it counts as a justified conclusion?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why does Catholic teaching hold that material things such as water, oil, bread and wine can carry grace?","a":"Because of the sacramental nature of reality: God made the material world and called it 'very good' (Genesis 1:31), and in the Incarnation the Son took flesh within it, so matter is fit to be a channel of God rather than a barrier to him."},{"q":"State the contrast an examiner would reward between Catholic and Baptist beliefs about the eucharist.","a":"Catholics believe in transubstantiation — the bread and wine become the body and blood of Christ while the appearances remain — whereas many Baptists treat the Lord's Supper as a memorial of Christ's death, following 'Do this in memory of me' (Luke 22:19), so the bread remains ordinary."},{"q":"Which named Catholic source would you cite for the duty to work for the common good, and how precisely?","a":"Gaudium et Spes paragraph 26, from the Second Vatican Council (1965), which defines the common good as 'the sum total of social conditions' that let people 'reach their fulfilment more fully and more easily'. Cite the paragraph number, not just the document."},{"q":"Two answers on pilgrimage both mention Lourdes. What makes one of them argue at the top level and the other not?","a":"The weaker one describes what pilgrims do there. The stronger one reasons from effect to judgement — that pilgrimage exists to change the pilgrim and to serve the sick — sets that against the Protestant objection that God is equally present everywhere, and then says which case is stronger and why."}]},"room":{"facts":["The five parts of a Catholic Christianity question always run 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks in that order, and the 12-mark evaluation in the Beliefs section also carries 3 marks for spelling, punctuation and grammar.","Work at about a minute a mark: roughly 4 minutes on the 4-mark question, 5 on the 5-mark, and about 12 on the evaluation, with the 1- and 2-mark parts done in moments.","The fifth mark on a 5-mark question is awarded once, for one relevant and accurate reference to sacred writings or another source of Catholic belief and teaching — a chapter and verse, the Nicene Creed, the Catechism or Gaudium et Spes paragraph 26.","On a contrasting question the two views must genuinely differ: if similar beliefs are given, only one of them can be credited, up to 2 marks.","On an influence question the second mark for each way is only earned if you say what Catholics actually do or think differently because of the belief.","An evaluation that never refers to religion is capped at Level 2 (4-6 marks); Level 4 (10-12) needs reasoned consideration of different points of view, logical chains of reasoning leading to a judgement, and references to religion applied to the issue."],"questions":[{"q":"Which one of the following is the Catholic term for the final purification of those who die in God's grace but are not yet ready for heaven? [1 mark] Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. A Ascension  B Incarnation  C Purgatory  D Transubstantiation","a":"Answer: C Purgatory. Target AO1:1. One mark; nothing written beside the box can rescue a wrong tick.","sol":"C Purgatory. Purgatory is the state of final purification for those who die in God's grace and friendship but are not yet ready for heaven, and it is why Catholics pray and offer Masses for the dead. Ascension is Jesus' return to the Father, Incarnation is the Son taking human flesh, and transubstantiation is the change in the bread and wine at Mass, so only C names something that happens to a person after death.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Three of the four words name something that happens to Jesus or to the bread and wine, not something that happens to a person after death.","Work by elimination: say to yourself what each of the four words actually refers to before you tick anything.","One tick in one box, and nothing written beside it will be marked."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — Beliefs about life after death: resurrection, judgement, heaven, hell and purgatory"]},{"q":"Which one of the following does the Church describe as 'the source and summit of Christian life'? [1 mark] Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. A Baptism  B Confirmation  C Matrimony  D The eucharist","a":"Answer: D The eucharist. Target AO1:1. One mark.","sol":"D The eucharist. Baptism is the gateway sacrament that washes away original sin, confirmation strengthens baptismal grace, and matrimony is a sacrament at the service of communion, but only the eucharist is given this title, because Catholics believe Christ himself is received in it and every other sacrament points towards it.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The phrase belongs to the sacrament Catholics receive most often, not to one received once in a lifetime.","Ask which of the four a Catholic could take part in every day of the week.","One tick only; do not explain your choice, because no credit is available for it."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — The names, meanings and effects of the seven sacraments"]},{"q":"Which one of the following is a form of popular piety? [1 mark] Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. A Holy orders  B The Nicene Creed  C The Rosary  D Transubstantiation","a":"Answer: C The Rosary. Target AO1:1. One mark.","sol":"C The Rosary. Popular piety means a devotion that is encouraged by the Church but is not a sacrament; the two the specification names are the Rosary and the Stations of the Cross. Holy orders is one of the seven sacraments, the Nicene Creed is a statement of belief said at Mass, and transubstantiation is a doctrine about the eucharist.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Popular piety is about the devotions Catholics take up freely, over and above what the Church requires of them.","Test each option against that definition in turn, rather than picking the word you recognise first.","One tick in one box."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — The role and importance of forms of popular piety"]},{"q":"Give two Catholic beliefs about what the Incarnation shows about the value of the human body. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for each of two correct points, for example: the human body is good, because the Son took a real one; human bodies share the nature God himself assumed; a body is not a shell the real person lives inside; bodies must never be treated as disposable. Target AO1:1; only the first two answers are marked.","sol":"The human body is good and not something to be escaped, because in the Incarnation the eternal Son took a real human body and lived a human life in it. Every human body therefore carries a dignity that must never be treated as disposable, which is why Catholic teaching on the care of the sick, the poor and the dying appeals to the Word made flesh.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The word itself means 'becoming flesh' — who becomes what?","Two separate points, each one sentence; no development is asked for and none is rewarded.","Write them on two numbered lines, because only the first two answers are marked."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — Incarnation: the belief in Jesus as incarnate Son, divine Word"]},{"q":"Give two things the biblical creation accounts teach Catholics about human beings. [2 marks]","a":"One mark each, for example: made in the image of God; given a God-given dignity that cannot be earned or lost; formed from the dust and given the breath of life; given responsibility for the earth. Target AO1:1.","sol":"Human beings are made in the image of God, so every person has a dignity that cannot be earned or taken away. God formed the human being from the dust of the earth and breathed life into him, so body and soul alike come from God.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The question is about people, not about God or the days of creation.","Genesis 1 and Genesis 2 each say something different about human beings — take one point from each.","Two short numbered points; do not develop them."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — Creation: Biblical accounts of creation (Genesis 1 and 2)"]},{"q":"Give two aims of the Society of Saint Vincent de Paul (SVP). [2 marks]","a":"One mark each, for example: to visit the lonely, sick, housebound, bereaved or imprisoned; to give practical help such as food, furniture or help with bills; to help anyone in need whatever their beliefs; to turn concern into action person to person. Target AO1:1.","sol":"To help people in need face to face, especially by visiting the lonely, the sick and the housebound in their own homes. To give practical help, such as food, furniture or help with fuel bills, to anyone in need whatever their beliefs.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The SVP works at the local end of the duty to love our neighbour, not the global one.","Think of two different kinds of help: one that costs time, one that costs money.","Two numbered aims, one line each."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — The aims and importance of the Society of Saint Vincent de Paul (SVP)"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting Christian beliefs about what happens to the bread and wine at the eucharist. [4 marks]","a":"Target AO1:3. First contrasting belief: simple explanation 1 mark, detailed 2. Second contrasting belief: the same. Catholic transubstantiation set against, for example, the Baptist memorial view or the Orthodox refusal of the scholastic term; if similar beliefs are given only one may be credited, up to 2 marks.","sol":"Catholics believe that at Mass the bread and wine become the body and blood of Christ. This is called transubstantiation: the appearances of bread and wine remain exactly the same, but what they really are has changed, which is why the consecrated host is reserved in the tabernacle and treated with reverence. In contrast, many Protestant Christians, such as Baptists, believe the Lord's Supper is a memorial. They read Jesus' words at the Last Supper, 'Do this in memory of me' (Luke 22:19), as a command to remember his death rather than a promise that the bread changes, so for them the bread and wine remain ordinary food and nothing is kept afterwards.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Two Christian traditions disagree about what the bread and wine actually are after the prayer, not about whether the meal matters.","Take one tradition at a time: state its belief, then add a reason, an example or a consequence.","Two short paragraphs, the second opening with words that set it against the first, and each naming the tradition it belongs to."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — The names, meanings and effects of the seven sacraments","3.1.3 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting Christian views about the importance of pilgrimage. [4 marks]","a":"Target AO1:3, marked 2 + 2. Catholic view of pilgrimage as valuable (Lourdes) contrasted with the common Protestant view that it is unnecessary because God is present everywhere. If similar views are given only one may be credited.","sol":"Catholics believe pilgrimage is a valuable part of the Christian life. A pilgrim who travels to Lourdes, where Bernadette Soubirous saw Mary in 1858, leaves ordinary life behind to pray at the grotto, walk in the torchlight procession and help sick and disabled pilgrims, and many come home with their faith strengthened and their priorities changed. In contrast, many Protestant Christians believe pilgrimage is unnecessary. They argue that God is equally present everywhere, that the New Testament nowhere commands such a journey, and that the money and time would do more good spent on the poor, so for them no place is holier than any other.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["One tradition thinks place matters; the other thinks it cannot, given what God is.","For each view, state it and then give the reason that tradition would actually offer.","Two paragraphs, each naming the tradition, the second opening with a phrase of contrast."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — The role and importance of pilgrimage","3.1.3 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which belief in purgatory influences Catholics today. [4 marks]","a":"Target AO1:2, marked 2 + 2. Prayer and Masses for the dead, All Souls' Day, the Requiem Mass and its prayers, comfort in bereavement, motivation to confession and to live well. The second mark in each case requires the influence itself to be shown.","sol":"Belief in purgatory leads Catholics to pray for their dead. They ask for Masses to be offered for relatives and friends and remember them each year on All Souls' Day on 2 November; because the dead may still be being purified, bereavement becomes something a Catholic can act on, so grief comes out as prayer rather than helplessness. It also shapes the funeral rite. The Requiem Mass is offered for the person who has died and its prayers ask God to bring them into eternal light, so a Catholic funeral is a prayer for the dead person rather than only a celebration of their life.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what a belief about what is still happening to the dead allows the living to do for them.","For each way, name the practice and then say what Catholics do or feel differently because of it.","Two paragraphs; the second half of each should describe behaviour, not doctrine."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — Beliefs about life after death: resurrection, judgement, heaven, hell and purgatory","3.1.3.2 — The funeral rite and its significance"]},{"q":"Explain two Catholic teachings about the Trinity. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Catholic belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"Target AO1:1. Two teachings at 2 + 2 (one God in three persons; the persons are equal, eternal and distinct; defined at Nicaea against the claim that the Son was created), plus 1 mark for a relevant and accurate source — the Nicene Creed, Matthew 28:19 or Mark 1:9-11.","sol":"Catholics teach that there is only one God, and that this one God is three persons: Father, Son and Holy Spirit. The persons are genuinely distinct but they are not three gods and not three parts of God, because each person is wholly God. They also teach that the three persons are equal and eternal. The Church worked this out rather than invented it, defining it at the Council of Nicaea in 325 against the claim that the Son was a creature made by the Father, and the phrase 'begotten, not made' denies that there was ever a time when the Son did not exist. The source is the Nicene Creed, said at Sunday Mass, which calls the Son 'consubstantial with the Father'; the scriptural origin is Matthew 28:19, where the risen Jesus tells the disciples to baptise in the threefold name.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Two teachings, not one teaching explained twice: one about how three can be one, one about how the Church settled it.","Write each teaching, then develop it with a consequence or with what it rules out.","Two developed paragraphs, then a separate sentence that names the source plainly, because that mark is awarded on its own."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — One God as a Trinity of persons: Father, Son and Holy Spirit"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which Catholic agencies put the duty to love our neighbour into practice. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Catholic belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"Target AO1:1. Two ways at 2 + 2 (CAFOD and partner-led development; Trócaire's projects and campaigning; Missio's support for overseas mission; SVP visiting), plus 1 mark for the source — Mark 12:31, Luke 10:25-37 or Gaudium et Spes paragraph 26.","sol":"CAFOD, the Catholic Agency for Overseas Development, works through local partners in poorer countries. Rather than simply sending aid, it funds people who already live in the community to run clean water, farming and health projects, so that families can provide for themselves long after the project ends. Trócaire, set up by the Irish Catholic bishops, does the same work from Ireland and also campaigns on debt, climate change and human rights, so that the causes of poverty are challenged and not only its effects. Both are acting on Gaudium et Spes paragraph 26, which tells Catholics to work for the common good, 'the sum total of social conditions' that allow people to 'reach their fulfilment more fully and more easily'.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The named agencies work at the global end of the duty; each does something more than hand out money.","Name an agency, say what it does, then say what difference that makes to the people it serves.","Two developed paragraphs, then one sentence naming a Catholic source precisely — a paragraph number counts."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — Catholic beliefs about the essential duty to 'love our neighbour'"]},{"q":"Explain two things Catholics learn from the Lord's Prayer. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Catholic belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"Target AO1:1. Two lessons at 2 + 2 (God is a father who can be approached; prayer is corporate, 'our' not 'my'; dependence on God day by day; being forgiven is tied to forgiving), plus 1 mark for the source — Matthew 6:9-13 or the Catechism.","sol":"Catholics learn that God is a father who can be approached directly. The prayer opens by calling God 'Our Father', and the word 'our' rather than 'my' teaches that no Catholic prays alone: saying it at Mass or at home joins them to the whole Church praying the same words. They also learn that being forgiven and forgiving others cannot be separated. The prayer asks God to 'forgive us our trespasses' in the same measure as we forgive other people, so a Catholic who refuses to forgive is asking God to treat them in the same way, which is why reconciliation with others belongs alongside the sacrament of reconciliation. Jesus himself gave the prayer in Matthew 6:9-13, and the Catechism calls it 'the summary of the whole Gospel'.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Look at the petitions themselves: who God is called, and what condition is attached to one of the requests.","State each lesson, then say what it changes about how a Catholic prays or lives.","Two developed paragraphs and then a plain naming of the source, because that mark is separate."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — Prayer: formal prayers and informal prayer, and the Lord's Prayer"]},{"q":"Explain two Catholic teachings about how people are saved. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Catholic belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"Target AO1:1. Two teachings at 2 + 2 (salvation won by Christ's life, death, resurrection and ascension, not earned; grace as God's free gift received through the sacraments; faith lived out in works), plus 1 mark for the source — Mark 10:45, 1 Corinthians 15:3-4 or James 2:26.","sol":"Catholics teach that people are saved by what Jesus did rather than by what they achieve. His life, death, resurrection and ascension are taken together as one saving act: Mark 10:45 says the Son of Man came to give his life 'a ransom for many', so the cost of sin is paid by Christ and cannot be paid off by human effort. They also teach that this salvation reaches people through grace, God's free and undeserved help, given above all in the sacraments: baptism washes away original sin and makes a person a member of the Church, and reconciliation restores someone who has sinned since. Salvation therefore has to be lived as well as received, which is why James 2:26 teaches that faith without works is dead.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Catholics do not think salvation is bought — so who pays, and how does what he paid reach anyone now?","One teaching about what Christ did, one about how it reaches people today; develop each with a consequence.","Two developed paragraphs, then a sentence that names a text or document outright."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — Redemption: the significance of the life, death, resurrection and ascension of Jesus","3.1.3.1 — The names, meanings and effects of the seven sacraments"]},{"q":"'Nothing a Catholic does is more important than going to Mass.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Catholic teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [SPaG 3 marks]","a":"Target AO2, marked by levels on the whole answer. In support: the eucharist as 'the source and summit of Christian life', transubstantiation, the Sunday obligation, grace received through the sacraments. Against: the second great commandment, Matthew 25:31-46, Gaudium et Spes 26, the SVP and CAFOD, and the Protestant memorial view. Level 4 needs both sides reasoned and a conclusion that judges.","sol":"Catholics have strong reasons to agree with the statement. The Church calls the eucharist 'the source and summit of Christian life', and it means that literally: everything else the Church does either leads towards the Mass or flows out of it. Catholics believe that through transubstantiation the bread and wine become the body and blood of Christ, so the Mass is not a reminder of Calvary but the same sacrifice made present, and the person who receives communion receives Christ himself. Jesus gave the command at the Last Supper, 'Do this in memory of me' (Luke 22:19), and keeping the Lord's Day holy is the third commandment, so a Catholic who misses Mass without good reason is refusing something Christ asked for. Grace, on Catholic teaching, is God's free gift rather than a wage for effort, and the sacraments are its ordinary channels; if that is right, then the act that feeds every other act must come first.\n\nA different view begins from the same teaching and arrives somewhere gentler. Jesus named two great commandments, and the second, 'love your neighbour as yourself' (Mark 12:31), is not optional. In the parable of the sheep and the goats the judgement falls on what was done for 'one of the least of these' (Matthew 25:40), and attendance is never mentioned. Gaudium et Spes paragraph 26 obliges Catholics to work for the common good, and the SVP, CAFOD and Trócaire exist because that duty is taken to be real and urgent. A Catholic who receives communion and then walks past a neighbour in need has misunderstood what they received, which is why James 2:26 teaches that faith without works is dead and why the Mass ends by sending people out.\n\nOther Christians press the point further. Many Protestant Churches treat the Lord's Supper as a memorial rather than a sacrifice, and Quakers and the Salvation Army keep no sacraments at all, holding that God is met directly. On their reading, ranking any ritual above active love misreads the Gospel entirely.\n\nThe statement is nearly right if 'important' means the source from which everything else comes, and clearly wrong if it means the only thing that counts. Catholic teaching does not actually force the choice: the Mass sends Catholics out to the poor, and the service of the poor is what the grace of the Mass is for. I judge the statement half true. The Mass is central in the precise sense that nothing else would have its power without it, but a Catholicism that stopped at the church door would be condemned by the very Gospel read inside it, so 'most important' should mean first in order rather than sufficient on its own.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["The strongest case for the statement is not that Mass is a rule but that Catholic teaching makes it the source of everything else.","Argue one side fully with named Catholic teaching before you turn, and make the other side religious too, not merely practical.","Three or four paragraphs: the case for, the case against, a divergent Christian view, and a conclusion that says which argument was stronger and why."],"codes":["3.1.3.1 — The names, meanings and effects of the seven sacraments","3.1.3.1 — The meaning and significance of 'sacrament' and the sacramental nature of reality","3.1.3.2 — Catholic beliefs about the essential duty to 'love our neighbour'","3.1.3 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views"]},{"q":"'Catholics should spend their money helping the poor rather than going on pilgrimage.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Catholic teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks]","a":"Target AO2, levels 1-4. In support: Mark 12:31, the Good Samaritan, Matthew 25:31-46, Gaudium et Spes 26, the preferential option for the poor, CAFOD and the SVP, and the Protestant objection that God is everywhere. Against: pilgrimage as service to sick pilgrims at Lourdes, Mark 14:7, renewal of faith producing service. Level 4 needs a conclusion that judges between them.","sol":"There is a serious Catholic case for the statement. Jesus' second great commandment is to 'love your neighbour as yourself' (Mark 12:31), and the parable of the Good Samaritan makes the neighbour anyone in need rather than anyone nearby. Human dignity comes from being made in God's image (Genesis 1:27), and in Matthew 25:31-46 judgement turns on what was done for 'one of the least of these'. Gaudium et Spes paragraph 26 requires Catholics to work for the common good, and Catholic social teaching's preferential option for the poor says that where resources are limited the poor come first. Money given to CAFOD or to an SVP conference meets a need directly; money spent on flights and hotels meets the pilgrim's own need, so on the Church's own principles the giving looks like the better act. Many Protestant Christians say the same more bluntly: God is equally present everywhere, so the journey buys nothing that prayer at home would not.\n\nA different point of view is just as Catholic. Pilgrimage to Lourdes, where Bernadette Soubirous saw Mary in 1858, is not tourism: most British diocesan pilgrimages and organisations such as HCPT exist chiefly to take sick and disabled people there and to care for them while they are there, so the money buys nursing, transport and company for people who rarely receive any of it. Catholics also answer the objection with Jesus' own words when he was criticised for accepting an expensive anointing at Bethany, 'the poor you always have with you' (Mark 14:7): devotion to Christ is not waste. More than that, the sacramental nature of reality means place and journey can carry grace, so a pilgrimage is not a luxury added to faith but a means of it.\n\nThe strongest Catholic move refuses the choice the statement forces. The grace received on pilgrimage is precisely what sends people home to staff the SVP conference, run the food bank and give to Trócaire; the Mass at the grotto ends, like every Mass, with a sending out. Treating worship and charity as rivals for the same money misunderstands how Catholic teaching connects them.\n\nIn conclusion, the statement is right about one kind of pilgrimage and wrong about another. A comfortable trip taken for its own sake is very hard to defend when Gaudium et Spes paragraph 26 and the option for the poor are taken seriously, and a Catholic should give that money away. But a pilgrimage made in order to carry the sick to Lourdes is already an act of love of neighbour, and giving its cost to charity instead would help the poor while abandoning the sick. The real duty is therefore not to stop going but to make the going serve someone other than oneself.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["The statement is not asking whether pilgrimage is allowed; it is asking what the same money would do more good.","Build one side fully from named Catholic teaching, then look for the Catholic argument that refuses the either-or.","Four paragraphs: the case for, the case against, the argument that joins them, and a conclusion that distinguishes the kinds of pilgrimage."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — The role and importance of pilgrimage","3.1.3.2 — Catholic beliefs about the essential duty to 'love our neighbour'","3.1.3.2 — The aims and importance of the Society of Saint Vincent de Paul (SVP)"]},{"q":"'The most important work of the Church today is evangelism.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Catholic teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks]","a":"Target AO2, levels 1-4. In support: Matthew 28:19, Missio and the Red Boxes, Evangelii Gaudium, declining practice in Britain, Catholic schools and RCIA. Against: the works of mercy, Gaudium et Spes 26, the SVP, CAFOD, Pax Christi and Corrymeela, and the argument that witness is itself evangelism. Level 4 needs a justified conclusion.","sol":"The case for the statement starts with a command. The risen Jesus tells the disciples to 'make disciples of all nations' (Matthew 28:19), so evangelism is not one Catholic activity among others but the reason the Church was sent at all. Missio, the Pope's official mission charity in England and Wales, funds priests, catechists and children's projects through the Red Boxes kept in Catholic homes and Mission Together in schools, because without someone to preach and to baptise there is no Church in a place to do anything else. Pope Francis' Evangelii Gaudium (2013) presses the same point for Britain, asking the Church to go out to the margins instead of waiting for people to come in, and in a country where Mass attendance has fallen and many children now meet the faith only through a Catholic school or an RCIA group, a Church that stopped evangelising would within a generation have nobody left to run its charities.\n\nA different view says that putting evangelism first gets the Gospel backwards. In Matthew 25:31-46 the judgement falls on feeding, clothing and visiting, not on converting, and Gaudium et Spes paragraph 26 binds Catholics to work for the common good here and now. The SVP visits the lonely and the housebound, CAFOD and Trócaire work with partners in poorer countries, and Pax Christi, the international Catholic movement for peace founded in France in 1945, works for reconciliation and disarmament; none of these asks whether the person helped will join the Church. James 2:26 teaches that faith without works is dead, so preaching to a hungry person while refusing to feed them would be a contradiction rather than a mission.\n\nThe two are harder to separate than the statement assumes. Pope St Paul VI's Evangelii Nuntiandi (1975) observed that people today listen more readily to witnesses than to teachers, which means the SVP volunteer and the Corrymeela community, bringing Catholics and Protestants together in Northern Ireland, are already evangelising: what they do is the argument for what they believe. On that view the statement is true in a sense its supporters did not intend.\n\nIn conclusion, evangelism is the most important work of the Church only if it is understood widely enough to include the way Catholics live. Taken narrowly, as preaching and recruiting, it cannot be ranked above the works of mercy, because Christ himself made those the test at the Last Judgement. Taken widely, as making Christ known by word and by witness together, the statement is defensible, since every charity the Church runs is finally an answer to the question of who Christ is. I judge it true only in the wider sense, and the narrow reading should be rejected.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Everything turns on what 'evangelism' is taken to mean; find the reading that makes the statement strongest first.","Give each side a named Catholic teaching and a named organisation, then look for the teaching that links word and witness.","Four paragraphs ending in a conclusion that distinguishes two senses of the key word and says which one you accept."],"codes":["3.1.3.2 — The meaning and significance of mission and evangelism for Catholics today","3.1.3.2 — Catholic beliefs about the essential duty to 'love our neighbour'","3.1.3.2 — The aims and importance of either the Corrymeela community or Pax Christi"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What does the Nicene Creed say about the Son's relationship to the Father, and which council settled it?","a":"That the Son is 'consubstantial with the Father' and 'begotten, not made' — of the same being, not a creature. Settled at the Council of Nicaea in 325."},{"q":"Which sacrament is 'the source and summit of Christian life', and what do Catholics believe happens to the bread and wine?","a":"The eucharist. Through transubstantiation the bread and wine become the body and blood of Christ while the appearances of bread and wine remain."},{"q":"What is purgatory, and which Catholic practice depends on it?","a":"The final purification of those who die in God's grace and friendship but are not yet ready for heaven. Prayer for the dead — Masses offered for the dead, the Requiem Mass and All Souls' Day on 2 November."},{"q":"Which Catholic document and paragraph defines the common good, and what does it say the common good is?","a":"Gaudium et Spes paragraph 26, from the Second Vatican Council. It is 'the sum total of social conditions' that allow people, as groups or individuals, to 'reach their fulfilment more fully and more easily'."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Trinity, and the Council of Nicaea (325)","back":"One God in three persons: Father, Son and Holy Spirit, each wholly God, not three gods. Defined at Nicaea in 325; the Creed calls the Son 'consubstantial with the Father', 'begotten, not made'. The Magisterium is the Church's teaching authority.","code":"3.1.3.1 — One God as a Trinity of persons: Father, Son and Holy Spirit","open":false},{"front":"Genesis 1 and 2, and what they teach","back":"God creates freely and calls it 'very good' (1:31). Humanity is made 'in his own image' (1:27), so dignity is God-given and equal. Humanity is to till and keep the earth (2:15) — stewardship.","code":"3.1.3.1 — Creation: Biblical accounts of creation (Genesis 1 and 2)","open":false},{"front":"Incarnation","back":"The eternal Son, the divine Word, took human flesh and was born of the Virgin Mary: fully God and fully human, not half of each. 'The Word became flesh' (John 1:14); the Creed says he 'became man'.","code":"3.1.3.1 — Incarnation: the belief in Jesus as incarnate Son, divine Word","open":false},{"front":"Redemption and grace","back":"Jesus' life, death, resurrection and ascension are one saving act; he came to give his life 'a ransom for many' (Mark 10:45). Grace is God's free, undeserved help, given above all through the sacraments.","code":"3.1.3.1 — Redemption: the significance of the life, death, resurrection and ascension of Jesus","open":false},{"front":"Purgatory","back":"The final purification of those who die in God's grace and friendship but are not yet ready for heaven. It grounds prayer and Masses for the dead and All Souls' Day (2 November). Most Protestants reject it.","code":"3.1.3.1 — Beliefs about life after death: resurrection, judgement, heaven, hell and purgatory","open":false},{"front":"Sacrament, and the sacramental nature of reality","back":"A sacrament is an outward sign instituted by Christ that gives the grace it signifies. Because God made matter good and took flesh in it, water, oil, bread, wine and touch can carry God.","code":"3.1.3.1 — The meaning and significance of 'sacrament' and the sacramental nature of reality","open":false},{"front":"The seven sacraments","back":"Initiation: baptism, confirmation, eucharist. Healing: reconciliation, anointing of the sick. Service of communion: matrimony, holy orders. The eucharist is 'the source and summit of Christian life'.","code":"3.1.3.1 — The names, meanings and effects of the seven sacraments","open":false},{"front":"Different Christian views of the eucharist","back":"Catholics: transubstantiation, a real change while the appearances remain. Orthodox: a real change, without the scholastic term. Many Protestants, such as Baptists: a memorial. Quakers and the Salvation Army keep no sacraments.","code":"3.1.3 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views","open":false},{"front":"Prayer, and the Lord's Prayer","back":"Prayer is 'the raising of the mind and heart to God'. Formal prayers are set and shared; informal prayer is spontaneous. Jesus gave the Lord's Prayer (Matthew 6:9-13); the Catechism calls it the summary of the whole Gospel.","code":"3.1.3.2 — Prayer: formal prayers and informal prayer, and the Lord's Prayer","open":false},{"front":"The Rosary and the Stations of the Cross","back":"Devotions, not sacraments. The Rosary: five decades of an Our Father, ten Hail Marys and a Glory Be, meditating on mysteries of Christ's life. The Stations: fourteen scenes walked from condemnation to burial.","code":"3.1.3.2 — The role and importance of forms of popular piety","open":false},{"front":"Lourdes","back":"The place of Roman Catholic pilgrimage studied. In 1858 Bernadette Soubirous saw Mary eighteen times at the grotto at Massabielle; a spring appeared. Pilgrims pray at the grotto, join the processions and care for sick pilgrims.","code":"3.1.3.2 — The role and importance of pilgrimage","open":false},{"front":"The Catholic funeral rite","back":"Usually a Requiem Mass. Holy water and the white pall recall baptism; the Paschal candle signs the risen Christ; the Mass is offered for the dead; then the Final Commendation and the Rite of Committal.","code":"3.1.3.2 — The funeral rite and its significance","open":false},{"front":"Gaudium et Spes paragraph 26, and the Catholic agencies","back":"Vatican II defines the common good as 'the sum total of social conditions' letting people 'reach their fulfilment more fully and more easily'. Globally: CAFOD (1962), Trócaire (1973), Missio.","code":"3.1.3.2 — Catholic beliefs about the essential duty to 'love our neighbour'","open":false},{"front":"The Society of Saint Vincent de Paul (SVP)","back":"Lay Catholic society founded in Paris in 1833 by Frédéric Ozanam; members are Vincentians in parish conferences. Visiting the lonely, sick, housebound and imprisoned, and practical help for anyone in need.","code":"3.1.3.2 — The aims and importance of the Society of Saint Vincent de Paul (SVP)","open":false},{"front":"Pax Christi, and the Corrymeela Community","back":"Pax Christi: the international Catholic peace movement, begun in France in 1945; nonviolence, disarmament, peace education, Peace Sunday. Corrymeela: founded 1965 by Ray Davey at Ballycastle, reconciling Catholics and Protestants.","code":"3.1.3.2 — The aims and importance of either the Corrymeela community or Pax Christi","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Evaluate: 'Nothing a Catholic does is more important than going to Mass.')","items":["The question: 'Nothing a Catholic does is more important than going to Mass.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should refer to Catholic teaching, give reasoned arguments to support the statement, give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view, and reach a justified conclusion.","Point (the argument, stated first): the strongest case for the statement is not that Mass is a rule that must be obeyed, but that Catholic teaching makes it the source of everything else a Catholic does.","Evidence (named, not vague): the Church calls the eucharist 'the source and summit of Christian life', and Catholics believe that through transubstantiation the bread and wine become the body and blood of Christ.","Development (the chain of reasoning): if Christ himself is genuinely received at Mass, then the grace that makes Catholic charity possible comes from there; ranking anything above it would be ranking the fruit above the tree, which is also why Sunday Mass is an obligation rather than a preference.","Counter-view (religious, and reasoned): against this, Jesus made the second commandment, 'love your neighbour as yourself' (Mark 12:31), inseparable from the first, and in Matthew 25:31-46 people are judged on what they did for 'one of the least of these', with attendance never mentioned.","Mini-judgement (before moving on): the Mass is the source of Catholic action rather than a substitute for it, so the statement is true if 'important' means first in order and false if it means all that matters.","Why this reaches Level 4: it gives reasoned consideration of different points of view in a logical chain of reasoning leading to a judgement supported by knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence, and its references to religion — the eucharist, transubstantiation, Mark 12:31, Matthew 25 — are applied to the issue rather than listed beside it.","The SPaG marks ride on this answer, so spell the technical words correctly: eucharist, transubstantiation, sacrament, grace, obligation."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same statement, visibly weaker)","items":["The question: the same — 'Nothing a Catholic does is more important than going to Mass.' Evaluate this statement.","The paragraph as written: 'Mass is the most important thing a Catholic does because it is where they receive Holy Communion and get closer to God. Catholics are supposed to go every Sunday. Some people would say helping the poor is more important, but Mass is still very important.'","The descriptor it meets: 'Reasoned consideration of a point of view. A logical chain of reasoning drawing on knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information' — there is a claim, a religious reason behind it, and a passing recognition that another view exists. That is Level 2, 4-6 marks.","The descriptor it misses — Level 3: 'Reasoned consideration of different points of view. Logical chains of reasoning...'. The opposing view here is named and abandoned in half a sentence with no reason attached to it, so only one point of view is actually reasoned.","The descriptor it misses — Level 4: 'judgement(s) supported by knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information'. The final clause repeats the opening claim instead of weighing the two sides, so there is no judgement in the answer at all.","It also never names a source. 'Get closer to God' is not the same as 'the source and summit of Christian life', and no scripture, creed or document appears.","The three repairs that would move it up: attach a named religious reason to the opposing view (Matthew 25:31-46, or Gaudium et Spes paragraph 26 on the common good); name the Catholic source behind the first claim; and replace the final clause with a conclusion that says which argument is stronger and why."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Catholic beliefs and teachings (3.1.3.1), with the source of authority for each","items":["The Magisterium: the teaching authority of the Catholic Church, exercised by the pope and the bishops in communion with him. It stands behind the Nicene Creed, the Catechism of the Catholic Church, the councils (Nicaea 325, Chalcedon 451, the Second Vatican Council 1962-65) and papal documents such as Laudato Si'.","The Trinity: one God in three persons, Father, Son and Holy Spirit, each wholly God and not three gods. Source: the Nicene Creed, said at Sunday Mass.","Scriptural origins of the Trinity: Matthew 28:19, the risen Jesus commanding baptism in the threefold name; Mark 1:9-11, the baptism of Jesus, where Son, Spirit and Father's voice are all present.","The Council of Nicaea, 325: called to settle whether the Son was a creature made by the Father. Its answer, in the Creed: the Son is 'consubstantial with the Father' and 'begotten, not made' — of one being with the Father, not a creature he produced.","Creation and the nature of God: in Genesis 1 God creates freely, by his word and in order, and sees that it is 'very good' (Genesis 1:31) — so creation is deliberate, purposeful and good.","Creation and human dignity: Genesis 1:27, humanity made 'in his own image'; Genesis 2:7, formed from the dust and given the breath of life. Dignity is God-given, equal, and cannot be earned or forfeited — the root of Catholic social teaching.","Creation and humanity's relationship with creation: Genesis 1:28 gives dominion; Genesis 2:15 gives the task of tilling and keeping. Catholics read this as stewardship, developed in Pope Francis' Laudato Si' (2015).","Different Christian beliefs about creation: some Protestant Christians read the six days literally; the Catholic Church reads the accounts for their religious truth and accepts scientific accounts of how the universe developed (Pope St John Paul II to the Pontifical Academy of Sciences, 1996).","Incarnation: the eternal Son, the divine Word, took human flesh and was born of the Virgin Mary — fully God and fully human. Sources: John 1:1, 'In the beginning was the Word'; John 1:14, 'the Word became flesh'; the Nicene Creed, where the Son 'was incarnate of the Virgin Mary' and became man. Defined further at the Council of Chalcedon, 451.","Redemption: the life, death, resurrection and ascension of Jesus taken as one saving act. Sources: Mark 10:45, the Son of Man came to give his life 'a ransom for many'; 1 Corinthians 15:3-4, Christ died for our sins and was raised on the third day.","Grace: God's free and undeserved help, not a wage for good behaviour; the seven sacraments are its ordinary channels. Faith still has to be lived: James 2:26 teaches that faith without works is dead.","Life after death — resurrection: Catholics believe in the resurrection of the body, not just the survival of a soul. Sources: the Nicene Creed's hope for the resurrection of the dead; John 11:25, 'I am the resurrection and the life'.","Life after death — judgement: the particular judgement met by each person at death, and the Last Judgement at the end of time. Source: Matthew 25:31-46, where judgement turns on what was done for 'one of the least of these'.","Heaven and hell: heaven is full and unending life with God; hell is the state of those who freely and finally refuse it.","Purgatory: the final purification of those who die in God's grace and friendship but are not yet ready for heaven. It grounds prayer for the dead, Masses offered for the dead and All Souls' Day on 2 November; Catholics cite 2 Maccabees 12:46. Most Protestant Churches reject it.","Sacrament: an outward sign, instituted by Christ, that gives the inward grace it signifies; the Catechism calls the sacraments efficacious signs of grace.","The sacramental nature of reality: because God made matter good and the Son took flesh in it, material things can carry God — hence water, oil, bread, wine, candles, statues and holy water. Quakers and the Salvation Army keep no sacraments at all.","Baptism: water poured with the words 'I baptise you in the name' of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. Effect: original sin washed away and the person made a member of the Church. Catholics normally baptise infants, with godparents, a white garment and a candle.","Confirmation: the bishop anoints the forehead with chrism, sealing the candidate 'with the Gift' of the Holy Spirit. Effect: baptismal grace strengthened and completed for adult Christian life.","Reconciliation: contrition, confession of sins to a priest, a penance, and absolution. Effect: sins forgiven and peace restored with God and with the Church.","Anointing of the sick: a priest anoints the forehead and hands with the oil of the sick. Effect: strength, peace and courage in illness, and the forgiveness of sins.","Matrimony: the couple themselves are the ministers and their consent makes the sacrament. Effect: a lifelong, faithful union, open to children, blessed with the grace to live it.","Holy orders: a bishop lays hands on the candidate. Effect: ordination as deacon, priest or bishop, to serve the Church and to act in the person of Christ in the sacraments.","The eucharist: 'the source and summit of Christian life'. Catholics believe in transubstantiation — the bread and wine become the body and blood of Christ while the appearances of bread and wine remain.","Different Christian views of the eucharist: Orthodox Christians affirm a real change without using the scholastic term; many Protestants, such as Baptists, treat the Lord's Supper as a memorial, reading 'Do this in memory of me' (Luke 22:19) as a command to remember."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Catholic practices (3.1.3.2), with the source or authority for each","items":["Prayer: 'the raising of the mind and heart to God' — the definition the specification quotes, and a usable source in a 5-mark answer.","Formal prayer: set words shared by the whole Church — the Mass, the Lord's Prayer, the Hail Mary, the Glory Be. Argument for: gives words in grief, unites the Church, guards against self-centredness.","Informal prayer: spontaneous, in a person's own words, at any time. Argument for: more personal and more honest. The specification asks for 'different views about their relative importance', so be ready to argue either way.","The Lord's Prayer: given by Jesus in Matthew 6:9-13; the Catechism calls it 'the summary of the whole Gospel'. 'Our Father', not my father; daily bread asked one day at a time; forgiveness received tied to forgiveness given.","The Rosary: five decades on beads, each an Our Father, ten Hail Marys and a Glory Be, prayed while meditating on a mystery of Christ's life. Joyful, Sorrowful and Glorious Mysteries; the Luminous Mysteries added by Pope St John Paul II in 2002.","The Stations of the Cross: fourteen scenes from Jesus' condemnation to his burial, marked around the church and walked in turn, especially on Fridays in Lent and on Good Friday.","Popular piety is devotion, not sacrament: warmly encouraged, never required for salvation — the distinction to use in any evaluation of what matters most in Catholic life.","Pilgrimage: a journey to a holy place made for a religious reason — to pray, to do penance, to seek healing, to return changed.","Lourdes, the place of Roman Catholic pilgrimage studied: in 1858 Bernadette Soubirous saw Mary eighteen times at the grotto at Massabielle in south-west France; a spring appeared there and millions of pilgrims come each year.","What pilgrims do at Lourdes: Mass at the grotto, bathing in or being sprinkled with the water, the torchlight Marian procession, the Blessed Sacrament procession, and caring for sick and disabled pilgrims — the purpose of British diocesan pilgrimages and of HCPT, the Pilgrimage Trust.","Different Christian views of pilgrimage: many Protestants hold that it is unnecessary, because God is equally present everywhere, the New Testament commands no such journey, and the money would do more good given to the poor.","The funeral rite: normally a Requiem Mass. The coffin is received at the church door, sprinkled with holy water and covered with a white pall, recalling baptism; the Paschal candle stands beside it as the sign of the risen Christ.","The funeral rite continued: readings often include John 11:25, 'I am the resurrection and the life'; the Mass is offered for the person who has died, which presupposes purgatory; then the Final Commendation and farewell, and the Rite of Committal at the grave or crematorium.","Burial and cremation: both are permitted, but the Church asks that ashes be buried in a sacred place rather than scattered or kept at home, because the body is destined for resurrection.","Love of neighbour: Jesus' second great commandment, 'love your neighbour as yourself' (Mark 12:31); the Good Samaritan (Luke 10:25-37) makes the neighbour anyone in need.","Gaudium et Spes paragraph 26 (Second Vatican Council, 1965): the common good is 'the sum total of social conditions' that let people, in groups and one by one, flourish as fully and as easily as possible — including food, clothing, housing, work, education and freedom to act according to conscience.","Catholic social teaching, the principles to name: human dignity, the common good, solidarity, subsidiarity, the preferential option for the poor, and teaching on justice, peace and reconciliation.","The duty at three scales, as the specification requires: locally (parish food banks, SVP visiting), nationally (campaigning on poverty and homelessness), globally (CAFOD, Trócaire, Missio).","Mission and evangelism: mission means being sent, evangelism means sharing the good news. Source: the Great Commission, 'make disciples of all nations' (Matthew 28:19).","Evangelism in Great Britain, nationally and locally: Catholic schools, RCIA groups preparing adults for baptism or reception at the Easter Vigil, parish missions, youth ministry, prison and hospital chaplaincy, and the witness of how Catholics treat people. Pope Francis' Evangelii Gaudium (2013) asks the Church to go out to the margins."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the named Catholic organisations and communities (3.1.3.2)","items":["CAFOD — the Catholic Agency for Overseas Development, founded in 1962, the official aid agency of the Catholic Church in England and Wales. Works through local partners on water, farming, health and emergency relief, and campaigns on the causes of poverty.","Trócaire — founded in 1973 by the Irish Catholic bishops as the overseas development agency of the Church in Ireland; the name is Irish for compassion or mercy. Long-term development projects, emergency relief, campaigning on debt, climate and human rights, and the Lenten box in Irish homes.","Missio — the Pope's official charity for overseas mission in England and Wales, part of the Pontifical Mission Societies. Red Boxes in Catholic homes and Mission Together in schools fund priests, catechists and children's projects in poorer countries.","The Society of Saint Vincent de Paul (SVP) — a lay Catholic society founded in Paris in 1833 by Frédéric Ozanam and fellow students, named after St Vincent de Paul. Members are called Vincentians and work in local groups called conferences, usually attached to a parish.","SVP aims: person-to-person help — visiting the lonely, housebound, sick, bereaved and imprisoned; practical help with food, furniture, clothing and fuel bills; SVP shops; holidays and camps for children and families; help for anyone in need regardless of belief. Its slogan is turning concern into action.","SVP importance: it makes the duty to love our neighbour local, personal and ordinary, and answers the charge that Catholic charity happens only at a distance. Pair it with CAFOD in an answer to show the local and global ends of one duty.","Pax Christi — the international Catholic movement for peace, begun in France in 1945 to reconcile French and German Christians after the Second World War. Aims: Gospel nonviolence, disarmament, human rights, peace education in schools and parishes, and support for conscientious objectors and victims of war. Pax Christi in England and Wales promotes Peace Sunday each January. Text: 'Blessed are the peacemakers' (Matthew 5:9).","The Corrymeela Community — the alternative the specification allows; founded in 1965 by Ray Davey at Ballycastle in County Antrim, the oldest peace and reconciliation organisation in Ireland. Aims: to bring Catholics and Protestants together for residential reconciliation work, dialogue and youth programmes.","Importance of both: they carry the Church's teaching on justice, peace and reconciliation out of doctrine and into something a student can point at — the answer to 'what does Catholic peacemaking actually look like?'"]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'Catholics should spend their money helping the poor rather than going on pilgrimage.' Evaluate this statement. [12 marks]","items":["Decoded: the question is not whether pilgrimage is permitted, but whether the same money would do more good given away. Both sides must therefore argue about the same money, and both must be Catholic arguments, because the first bullet requires reference to Catholic teaching.","Timing: about twelve minutes, roughly a minute a mark, keeping the last two for the conclusion. The three spelling, punctuation and grammar marks are carried by the 12-mark evaluation in the Beliefs section, not by this one, which is a Practices question — but spell the specialist words correctly anyway: pilgrimage, sacramental, common good, solidarity, conscience, Gaudium et Spes.","Paragraph 1, in support: the second great commandment, 'love your neighbour as yourself' (Mark 12:31), and the Good Samaritan (Luke 10:25-37), which makes the neighbour anyone in need rather than anyone nearby. Source to name: Gaudium et Spes paragraph 26 on the common good.","Paragraph 2, in support, developed into a chain: dignity comes from being made in God's image (Genesis 1:27); Matthew 25:31-46 makes judgement turn on what was done for 'one of the least of these'; the preferential option for the poor says that where resources are limited the poor come first; CAFOD, Trócaire and the SVP exist because Catholics accepted that reasoning. Add the Protestant objection that God is equally present everywhere, so the journey buys nothing.","Paragraph 3, a different point of view: pilgrimage to Lourdes, where Bernadette Soubirous saw Mary in 1858, is chiefly organised in Britain to take sick and disabled people there — diocesan pilgrimages and HCPT — so the money buys nursing, transport and company for people who rarely receive any of it.","Paragraph 4, that view developed: Catholics answer the objection with Jesus' words at Bethany, 'the poor you always have with you' (Mark 14:7), so devotion is not waste; and the sacramental nature of reality means a place and a journey can genuinely carry grace, making pilgrimage a means of faith rather than a luxury added to it.","The evaluation thread to run through every paragraph: does money spent on worship and devotion take from the poor, or does it produce the people who serve them? Return to that one question each time you change side.","The conclusion must judge, not summarise. Say which argument was stronger and why — for example, that the statement is right about pilgrimage taken as comfort, since Gaudium et Spes 26 and the option for the poor make that hard to defend, and wrong about pilgrimage taken as service, since giving away the cost of an HCPT trip would help the poor by abandoning the sick. So the real duty is not to stop going but to make the going serve someone other than oneself. A conclusion that says only 'both sides have a point' stays below the top level."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T14:09:42.745125Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"All nine objections are fixed and none introduced an error: SPaG now appears only on room.questions[13] and lesson.examples[4], both on the Beliefs statement about the Mass, and the plan title, plan timing item, room.questions[14], room.questions[15] and lesson.examples[3] are clean; room.questions[3] is off the June 2023 01.2 stem and its new answer key on the dignity of the body is sound Catholic teaching; the Creed, baptismal, confirmation, Incarnation and Gaudium et Spes quotations are cut to seven words or fewer with true paraphrases carrying the rest; the Magisterium is correctly defined in lesson.idea[0], the Beliefs fact file and cards[0]; and the popular piety hints no longer eliminate three options. Re-read whole: validator OK, all 22 scripture references still correct, every tariff 1/2/4/5/12, all three 12-mark stems carrying the four Component 1 bullets, all 16 spec codes tested, card backs and solution lengths in range — publishable, with four cosmetic trims left."}}},"3.1.4":{"id":"AQA-8062","topic":"3.1.4","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Hinduism is one of the seven religions Component 1 can be answered on, and this room covers the whole option: 3.1.4.1 Beliefs and teachings and 3.1.4.2 Practices. What is examined is not a general impression of Hinduism but the specification's own vocabulary - nirguna and saguna, atman, samsara, karma, moksha, prakriti, maya, dharma, havan, puja, arati, darshan, japa - used accurately and attributed to the Hindus who actually hold the view. Two references are prescribed and may be asked about directly, Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 391 and Rig Veda 10.129.6-7, and either will earn the separate source mark. Because the specification insists that common and divergent views be studied throughout, the answers that score name Shaivas, Vaishnavas or the Arya Samaj rather than beginning every sentence with 'Hindus believe'.","idea":[{"h":"Three standing demands: source, influence, divergence","code":"3.1.4 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Hinduism","t":"Every answer on Hinduism is marked against three standing requirements. Beliefs and practices must rest on Hindu sources of wisdom and authority, and you should be able to refer to scripture or sacred texts: the shruti texts, the four Vedas and the Upanishads that close them, and the smriti texts, the Ramayana, the Mahabharata with its Bhagavad Gita, and the Puranas. Two references are prescribed and can be examined directly, Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 391 and Rig Veda 10.129.6-7. You must also study the influence of these beliefs and practices on individuals, communities and societies, and the common and divergent views within Hinduism, including Shaivism and Vaishnavism. A top-level answer therefore never opens with a bare 'Hindus believe': it says which Hindus, names the text behind the belief, and says what the belief makes people do."},{"h":"Brahman: nirguna and saguna","code":"3.1.4.1 — Brahman: different Hindu understandings of brahman","t":"Brahman is ultimate reality, and the specification asks for different Hindu understandings of it. Brahman as nirguna is ultimate reality as divine consciousness, beyond form, quality and description. Brahman as saguna is God manifested in form, with qualities that can be pictured, named and loved. Neither cancels the other, which is why Hinduism can be described as one and as many at the same time: the deities are forms of the one reality, not rivals to it. Advaita Vedanta, following Shankara, treats nirguna brahman as the higher truth, while Vaishnava traditions treat the personal Lord as ultimate. Spiritual worlds belong to this bullet too, such as Vaikuntha, the realm of Vishnu. A top answer uses the pair as a ready-made contrast and explains why worship in form is needed at all if brahman is really beyond form."},{"h":"Everywhere, within the heart, and beyond","code":"3.1.4.1 — Different understandings of the three features of the divine","t":"The divine is understood in three ways at once. Everywhere, as non-personal: brahman is the reality of everything, so the world and every living being share in it. Within the heart: the divine dwells in each person as the inner controller, the antaryami, which is why meditation and worship in the space of the heart count as worship at all. Beyond, as a personal loving God: a Lord who can be addressed, served and loved, which is the ground of bhakti. Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 391 is prescribed against this bullet and is the reference to reach for when a question asks about the divine within. A top answer shows the three fitting together rather than competing, and links each to a practice: darshan for the divine met outside, japa for the divine within, bhakti for the Lord beyond."},{"h":"Tri-murti, deities and avatara","code":"3.1.4.1 — How the divine presents: the Tri-murti, deities and avatara","t":"The specification asks how the divine presents. The Tri-murti is Brahma the creator, Vishnu the preserver and Shiva the destroyer: three functions of one divine reality rather than three gods in competition. Male and female deities are named. Ganesha, the elephant-headed son of Shiva and Parvati, is worshipped at the start of a task as remover of obstacles. Lakshmi is the goddess of wealth and good fortune, welcomed into the cleaned home at Diwali. Hanuman is the devoted servant of Rama and the model of strength in devotion. Saraswati is the goddess of learning, music and the arts. Avatara is the concept of God descending into the world, with Krishna and Rama named as instances, both understood as avatars of Vishnu; Bhagavad Gita 4.7-8 has Krishna promise to come whenever dharma declines. A top answer treats the many forms as ways to one God and uses a named deity as its concrete example."},{"h":"Prakriti, tri-guna, maya and the four ages","code":"3.1.4.1 — Matter (prakriti); the three qualities (tri-guna); illusion (maya); cosmology","t":"Matter is prakriti, and it is analysed into three qualities, the tri-guna: sattva, goodness, purity and light; rajas, passion, energy and restlessness; tamas, darkness, ignorance and inertia. Every thing, mood and food has its own mixture, so Hindus who seek liberation cultivate sattva, which is one religious reason for a vegetarian and moderate diet. Maya is illusion: appearances mislead us into taking the changing material world for the whole of reality. Cosmology is prescribed with Rig Veda 10.129.6-7, the creation hymn that asks who really knows how creation began and allows that even the highest overseer may not know. Time runs in a cycle of four ages, the present one being the Kali Yuga, and there are many worlds with diverse inhabitants. A top answer uses maya and the tri-guna to explain behaviour, and uses Rig Veda 10.129.6-7 to show that Hinduism holds its cosmology with humility."},{"h":"Atman: the eternal inner self","code":"3.1.4.1 — The concept of atman","t":"Atman is the individual, eternal inner self, distinct from the material mind and body. Three claims are packed into that one line. It is individual, so rebirth happens to someone. It is eternal, so it is never born and never dies, which is exactly what Bhagavad Gita 2.20 says, while 2.22 compares leaving a body to changing worn-out clothes. It is distinct from mind and body, so what a person is is not their personality or their looks. Atman is why samsara is intelligible and why moksha is worth wanting, and it is the sharpest available contrast with the Buddhist teaching of anatta, no fixed self. A top answer uses atman as a premise inside an argument, about equality, about death, or about the treatment of animals, rather than defining it and moving on."},{"h":"Samsara, karma and moksha","code":"3.1.4.1 — Cycle of birth and death: samsara; the law of karma; moksha","t":"Samsara is the cycle of birth and death through which the atman passes. Karma is moral action and reaction, which is the specification's own gloss and a better one than 'fate', because karma is made by choices and ripens in this life or a later one. Moksha is liberation from the cycle, and the specification asks for types of liberation in the plural. Some Hindus describe it as the atman merging with brahman, as a drop returns to the ocean. Many Vaishnavas describe an eternal loving relationship with the personal Lord in a spiritual world such as Vaikuntha. A person may also be liberated while still alive, a jivanmukta. A top answer keeps the three together, karma driving samsara and moksha ending it, and cites Bhagavad Gita 2.22 for the fifth mark."},{"h":"Free will, suffering, knowledge and ignorance","code":"3.1.4.1 — Individual free will and responses to suffering; knowledge and ignorance","t":"If karma governs everything, are Hindus free? The specification requires individual free will alongside karma, and the standard Hindu answer is that karma sets the circumstances a person is born into while free will decides what they do within them; otherwise no action could be praised or blamed and dharma would be meaningless. Suffering is explained as the ripening of past action, but the bullet also asks for responses to suffering: sewa, selfless service, charity, acceptance without despair, and the pursuit of knowledge, because ignorance, not knowing the atman for what it is, is the root condition to be escaped, and knowledge dissolves it. A top answer refuses the caricature that karma makes Hindus fatalistic, and shows a Hindu acting to relieve suffering precisely because of what they believe."},{"h":"Ahimsa and the personal virtues","code":"3.1.4.1 — Personal virtues","t":"The specification names six personal virtues: ahimsa, respect, empathy, mind and sense control, humility, love. Ahimsa, non-violence in thought, word and deed, does most of the work in the exam, because the atman is present in every living being, so harming another is harming the divine. It lies behind vegetarianism for many Hindus, behind cow protection, and behind Hindu arguments in the Component 2 themes on animal experimentation, war and the death penalty. Mind and sense control is the virtue behind fasting, meditation and japa. The word 'including' means the list is open, but these are the ones questions are set on. A top answer names the virtue, traces it to a belief such as atman or karma, and then says what a Hindu actually does differently because of it."},{"h":"Dharma, artha, kama, moksha","code":"3.1.4.1 — The four aims of human life","t":"The four aims of human life are dharma, duty and right living; artha, prosperity and the means to live; kama, pleasure, including family life and the arts; and moksha, liberation. The scheme makes wealth and pleasure legitimate goals rather than temptations, provided they are pursued within dharma and ordered towards moksha, so a householder earning a living and raising a family is fulfilling the aims rather than falling short of them. Moksha appears both here and among the beliefs about human life, which shows it is the aim that ranks the others. A top answer uses the four aims to argue both sides of any question about wealth, pleasure or the good life, and points out that Hindus disagree about the order of the aims rather than about their legitimacy."},{"h":"Dharma, sanatana dharma, varnashrama dharma","code":"3.1.4.1 — The meaning of dharma; sanatana dharma; varnashrama dharma","t":"Three terms are deliberately distinguished. Dharma is duty, law and the way things should be, what holds a life and a society together. Sanatana dharma, the eternal way, is the name many Hindus prefer for their own tradition, and it implies duties that hold for everyone: truthfulness, ahimsa, self-control. Varnashrama dharma is duty specific to a person's varna and to their ashrama, the stage of life: brahmacharya the student, grihastha the householder, vanaprastha the retired, sannyasa the renunciate. What is right for a student is therefore not what is right for a renunciate, and that is where Hindu ethics becomes situational rather than uniform. Hindus differ sharply about how far varna duty still applies in Britain. A top answer uses the pair sanatana and varnashrama as its contrast and says which duties a Hindu could never drop."},{"h":"Home, temple, outdoors and the heart","code":"3.1.4.2 — Places of worship and their importance","t":"Four places, and their importance is what is asked for. The home is where most Hindu worship happens: a shrine with murtis, images, incense and a lamp, tended daily, often by the women of the family, which makes the household the unit of religion. The temple, the mandir, holds the installed murti and the priest's ritual, and in Britain it is also the community centre where Gujarati or Tamil is taught and festivals are kept. Outdoor shrines mark a sacred place, a tree or a river. The fourth is not a building at all: the space of the heart, where the divine within is worshipped, so a Hindu can worship anywhere. A top answer compares two of them on what each makes possible, and uses the fourth to argue that no building is strictly necessary."},{"h":"Havan, puja, arati, darshan, bhajan, japa","code":"3.1.4.2 — Different forms of worship/meditation and their significance","t":"Six named forms, each with its significance. Havan is the fire ritual, with ghee, grains and wood offered into a sacred fire while mantras are recited, used at weddings and openings. Puja is worship before the murti, with offerings of light, water, food, flowers and incense. Arati is the offering of the lamp, circled before the murti while a hymn is sung, after which devotees pass their hands over the flame. Darshan is seeing the deity and being seen by it. Bhajan and kirtan are devotional songs, kirtan often call and response. Japa is the repetition of a mantra, commonly on a mala of 108 beads. The specification also asks for the key differences between Shaivism and Vaishnavism and for different Hindu views about how important worship is at all. A top answer names the form, says what it does for the worshipper, and reaches for the Shaiva or Vaishnava difference whenever the question says 'contrasting'."},{"h":"One God, many deities, and the murti","code":"3.1.4.2 — Focuses of worship and representations of the divine","t":"Five focuses of worship. One God, personal or non-personal, is the ultimate focus. The many deities, and also the guru and other elders, are honoured as ways to that one God and as living authorities. Holy land, hills and rivers, the Ganges above all, are focuses in themselves. So are sacred plants and animals: tulsi grown in the courtyard, the cow protected as a symbol of selfless giving. The murti is a representation of God, and that careful wording answers the standard evaluation about worshipping statues: the murti allows the formless to be met in form, and in the mandir it is treated as an honoured guest, woken, dressed and fed, but the worship passes through it. Hindus divide here: the Arya Samaj, founded in 1875 by Swami Dayananda Saraswati, rejects murti worship altogether. A top answer holds on to the word 'representation' and names that divergence."},{"h":"Diwali and Holi in Britain today","code":"3.1.4.2 — Sacred festivals and their importance for Hindus in Great Britain today","t":"Both festivals are named, and for each the origins, the meaning and the importance for Hindus in Great Britain today are examinable. Diwali, in the autumn month of Kartik, is the festival of lights: rows of divas are lit, rangoli patterns are laid at the door, the home is cleaned and Lakshmi is welcomed, and for many Hindus the Ramayana account of Rama and Sita returning to Ayodhya after fourteen years of exile is the origin, the lamps lighting their way. Many Gujarati families open new account books, so it is also a new year. Holi, in the spring month of Phalguna, is the festival of colours, with a bonfire recalling the Puranic story of Prahlad, saved by devotion to Vishnu while Holika burned, and coloured powders thrown the next day, when differences of status are set aside. Leicester holds one of the largest Diwali celebrations outside India. A top answer gets from the story to what the festival does for a Hindu community here."},{"h":"Karma, jnana, astanga and bhakti yoga","code":"3.1.4.2 — The four paths towards yoga (union with the divine)","t":"Yoga means union with the divine, and the specification asks for four paths, their differences and their importance. Karma yoga is the path of action: doing your duty without attachment to the results, which is what Bhagavad Gita 2.47 teaches. Jnana yoga is the path of knowledge: study and discrimination until the atman is known for what it is, and the Chandogya Upanishad 6.8.7 teaching 'tat tvam asi', that thou art, belongs here. Astanga yoga is the path of meditation, the eight limbs set out in Patanjali's Yoga Sutras, ending in samadhi. Bhakti yoga is the path of devotion, the most widely followed, and Bhagavad Gita 9.26 teaches that a leaf, a flower, fruit or water offered with devotion is accepted. A top answer treats the four as suited to different temperaments and uses that to argue about how inclusive Hinduism is."},{"h":"Tirtha, Varanasi and the Kumbh Mela","code":"3.1.4.2 — Pilgrimage","t":"A place of pilgrimage is a tirtha, a crossing place, where the divine is felt to be close and the passage from this world towards liberation is easier. The specification asks for the role of pilgrimage, its practices and its purposes: bathing in a sacred river, darshan at the shrine, prayer or rites for a dead relative, the keeping of a vow, or simply the discipline of the journey. Varanasi is named: the city on the Ganges sacred to Shiva, with its ghats and cremation grounds, where many Hindus hope to die and be cremated. The Kumbh Mela is named too: the great bathing festival held in rotation at Prayagraj, Haridwar, Ujjain and Nashik, every twelve years at each site, where millions bathe at the river. Pilgrimage is not compulsory in Hinduism, and that fact is the hinge of any evaluation of it. A top answer names Varanasi or the Kumbh Mela and says what the pilgrim gains."},{"h":"Cow protection, environment and charity","code":"3.1.4.2 — The work and significance of environmental projects and charities","t":"Belief turns into action here, which is influence material. Cow protection follows from ahimsa and from the cow as a symbol of selfless giving: goshalas, cow sanctuaries, keep cows for their natural lives rather than sending them for slaughter, and in Britain the goshala at Bhaktivedanta Manor in Hertfordshire, the temple given to the Hare Krishna movement by George Harrison in 1973, is the example to name. Hindu environmental work extends to temple tree planting, vegetarian food and campaigns such as the Bhumi Project, the Hindu environmental initiative of the Oxford Centre for Hindu Studies. Charities promoting well-being, social inclusion and women's rights include Sewa International, whose name means selfless service, and Food for Life, which serves free vegetarian meals to anyone who comes. A top answer names the project, names the belief it grows from, and says what it changes."}],"examples":[{"title":"A 4-mark contrast: two Hindu understandings of brahman","setup":"Exam question: 'Explain two contrasting Hindu understandings of brahman. [4 marks]' The examiner marks two separate contrasts, giving one mark for a simple explanation and two for a detailed explanation of each. No source is required, and the two views must genuinely differ.","steps":["Take the formless understanding first: brahman as nirguna, ultimate reality as divine consciousness beyond all form and quality, so nothing pictured or named is finally adequate to it.","Develop it with a named tradition: Advaita Vedanta, following Shankara, treats that reality as the higher truth, so liberation is knowledge in which the atman is realised as one with brahman rather than a relationship with a God who is other.","Set the second view against it: brahman as saguna, God manifested in form and quality, worshipped as Vishnu by Vaishnavas or as Shiva by Shaivas, with a name to call on and a murti before which to stand.","Develop the second: for most Vaishnavas the personal Lord is ultimate rather than a lesser step, which is why bhakti, a loving relationship sustained by puja, arati and darshan, is the highest practice and not a concession to weakness.","Check the contrast is real before moving on: the two views genuinely disagree about whether God has form, so both can be credited, whereas two versions of the same point would be credited once."],"cues":["Which understanding of brahman has no form at all?","Which Hindu school treats that formless reality as the higher truth?","What is the opposing understanding called, and what does it add?","Why would a Vaishnava deny that a personal Lord is second best?","What happens to the credit if both of your points say the same thing?"]},{"title":"A 5-mark source question: what happens after death","setup":"Exam question: 'Explain two Hindu teachings about what happens after death. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]' Four of the marks are two teachings, each explained and then developed; the fifth is a separate mark for a relevant and accurate reference to a source.","steps":["First teaching: the atman is eternal and is reborn, so death ends a body and not a person, and the self passes through samsara, the cycle of birth and death.","Develop it with the mechanism: karma, moral action and reaction, conditions what kind of birth follows, so how a person lives now shapes what they are born into next.","Second teaching: the cycle can end in moksha, which some Hindus describe as the atman merging with brahman and many Vaishnavas as an eternal loving relationship with the personal Lord in a spiritual world such as Vaikuntha.","Secure the separate mark by naming a text: Bhagavad Gita 2.22 compares the self leaving a worn-out body to a person changing clothes.","Stop at two teachings, because a third is not marked and the time is better spent developing the two you already have."],"cues":["What survives death, and what is the cycle called?","What decides the kind of rebirth that follows?","What ends the cycle, and do all Hindus picture the end the same way?","Which named source would an examiner accept here?","Why not add a third teaching just to be safe?"]},{"title":"A 4-mark influence question: ahimsa today","setup":"Exam question: 'Explain two ways in which belief in ahimsa influences Hindus today. [4 marks]' Each way is worth one mark for a simple explanation and two for a detailed one, and the second mark is only given where the influence itself is shown: what Hindus actually do or decide differently. No source is required.","steps":["First way, food: because the atman is present in every living being, many Hindus are vegetarian, so meat is kept out of the house and the prasad shared at the mandir is vegetarian.","Show the reasoning behind the practice: killing for food would harm a being that shares in the divine, and it would build tamas rather than the sattva a person seeking moksha wants.","Second way, animals and the land: ahimsa supports cow protection, so Hindu communities fund goshalas where cows are kept for their natural lives, as at Bhaktivedanta Manor in Hertfordshire.","Give the second way its influence too: Hindus argue from ahimsa in public debates about animal experimentation and about war, and many support non-violent protest, as Gandhi did.","Reread each paragraph and ask whether it says what somebody does differently, because a belief stated without its consequence earns the simple mark only."],"cues":["What does ahimsa change about the food in a Hindu home?","Which belief about living beings makes that change necessary?","Where does ahimsa show itself beyond the dinner table?","What public arguments does the same belief feed into?","How can you tell a two-mark explanation from a one-mark one?"]},{"title":"A 12-mark evaluation: building both sides on the murti","setup":"Exam question: 'The murti is the most important part of Hindu worship.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Hindu teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. The whole answer is placed in a level, and an answer that never refers to religion cannot rise above Level 2.","steps":["Build the supporting case first: the murti is the focus that makes darshan possible, seeing the deity and being seen by it, and puja and arati are performed before it at home and in the mandir.","Ground that in teaching: brahman is understood as saguna as well as nirguna, God manifested in form, so a form is exactly what devotion needs; in the mandir the installed murti is treated as a guest, woken, dressed, fed and put to rest.","Turn to the other view in a Hindu voice: the Arya Samaj, founded by Swami Dayananda Saraswati, rejects murti worship and holds that formless nirguna brahman needs no image.","Press the counter-argument further: the murti is a representation of God, so the devotion passing through it is what counts, and japa on a mala, kirtan, havan and worship in the space of the heart need no image at all.","Reach a judgement that follows from the argument rather than a fresh opinion: say which side was stronger and why, for instance that the murti is central in practice for most Hindus yet not necessary in principle, since the divine is also within the heart."],"cues":["What can a Hindu do at a murti that they cannot do without one?","Which understanding of brahman makes worship in form appropriate?","Which Hindus reject murti worship, and on what grounds?","If the murti is a representation, what is actually being worshipped?","What must a conclusion do beyond saying both sides have a point?"]},{"title":"A 5-mark practices question: Hindu charity and the environment","setup":"Exam question: 'Explain two ways in which Hindu charities and environmental projects help people. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]' Each way scores one mark for a simple explanation and two for a detailed one, and there is a further single mark for the source.","steps":["First way, feeding people: Food for Life serves free vegetarian meals to anyone who comes, relieving hunger while keeping ahimsa, since no animal is killed to feed the hungry.","Develop it by naming the motive and the effect: sewa, selfless service, is offered without expecting a return, so the meal is given regardless of who the person is, which is social inclusion in practice.","Second way, animals and the land: goshalas keep cows for their natural lives, and the goshala at Bhaktivedanta Manor in Hertfordshire supplies milk to the temple instead of sending cows for slaughter.","Take the separate mark with a text: Bhagavad Gita 17.20 teaches that the best giving is done as a duty, to someone who cannot repay it, in the right place and at the right time.","Check the wording of the question one last time: it asks for ways of helping, so every paragraph has to end with a person or a creature who is better off."],"cues":["Which Hindu project puts food in front of people?","What is the Hindu name for service given without expecting a return?","What does cow protection actually look like in Britain?","Which named source would earn the separate source mark here?","How do you test that a paragraph is about helping rather than about belief?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the difference between nirguna and saguna?","a":"Nirguna is brahman as ultimate reality, divine consciousness beyond form and quality; saguna is God manifested in form, worshipped as a named deity."},{"q":"Which two references are prescribed for Hinduism, and against what?","a":"Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 391, against the three features of the divine; Rig Veda 10.129.6-7, against cosmology."},{"q":"Name the four paths towards yoga and say what each one is.","a":"Karma yoga, action; jnana yoga, knowledge; astanga yoga, meditation; bhakti yoga, devotion."},{"q":"Why does the law of karma not make Hindus fatalistic?","a":"Karma sets the circumstances a person is born into, but free will decides how they act within them, so dharma, virtue and effort still matter."}]},"room":{"facts":["Brahman is understood as nirguna, ultimate reality as divine consciousness beyond form, and as saguna, God manifested in form; both are Hindu understandings of the same reality.","The Tri-murti is Brahma the creator, Vishnu the preserver and Shiva the destroyer; Krishna and Rama are avatars of Vishnu.","Atman is the individual, eternal inner self, distinct from mind and body; samsara is the cycle of birth and death; karma is moral action and reaction; moksha is liberation.","The two prescribed references for Hinduism are Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 391, on the three features of the divine, and Rig Veda 10.129.6-7, on cosmology.","The four aims of human life are dharma, artha, kama and moksha; the four paths towards yoga are karma yoga, jnana yoga, astanga yoga and bhakti yoga.","The six named forms of Hindu worship are havan, puja, arati, darshan, bhajan and kirtan, and japa and mantra.","The five parts of a question on Hinduism run 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks: the 5-mark part always carries one further mark for a relevant and accurate reference to a source of Hindu belief and teaching, and the 12-mark evaluation in the Beliefs section is the one where spelling, punctuation and grammar are assessed."],"questions":[{"q":"Which one of the following is the Hindu term for the individual, eternal inner self?\nA  ahimsa\nB  artha\nC  atman\nD  avatara\nPut a tick in the box next to the correct answer. [1 mark]","a":"Answer: C  atman","sol":"C. Atman is the individual, eternal inner self, distinct from the material mind and body, and it is what passes through samsara. Ahimsa is the virtue of non-violence, artha is prosperity as one of the four aims of human life, and avatara is the concept of God descending into the world, as with Krishna and Rama.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The question is about what Hindus believe a person really is, underneath mind and body.","Rule out the three that are not names for the self: one is a virtue, one is an aim of life, one is a way God appears in the world.","Work down the four options asking of each: is this something a person is, or something a person does?"],"codes":["3.1.4.1 — The concept of atman"]},{"q":"Which one of the following means ultimate reality as divine consciousness, without form?\nA  maya\nB  nirguna\nC  prakriti\nD  saguna\nPut a tick in the box next to the correct answer. [1 mark]","a":"Answer: B  nirguna","sol":"B. Nirguna is brahman as ultimate reality, divine consciousness beyond form and quality. Saguna is the opposing understanding, God manifested in form. Maya means illusion and prakriti means matter, so neither is a term for ultimate reality.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Two of the four options are a pair of opposites about the nature of brahman.","Decide which of that pair is the one with no form.","Clear the other two first: one of them names matter and one names illusion, so neither can be right."],"codes":["3.1.4.1 — Brahman: different Hindu understandings of brahman"]},{"q":"Give two of the three qualities (tri-guna). [2 marks]","a":"One mark for each of two correct points: sattva; rajas; tamas. No development required.","sol":"Sattva, the quality of goodness, purity and light. Rajas, the quality of passion, energy and restlessness. Tamas, the quality of darkness, ignorance and inertia, would also be credited. Only the first two answers are marked, and naming them is enough.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The tri-guna are the three qualities the specification says matter, prakriti, is made up of.","Think of them as a ladder running from light, through restless energy, down to inertia.","Two names in the specification's own Sanskrit will do; you are not asked to explain them."],"codes":["3.1.4.1 — Matter (prakriti); the three qualities (tri-guna); illusion (maya); cosmology"]},{"q":"Give two of the four aims of human life. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for each of two correct points: dharma; artha; kama; moksha.","sol":"Dharma, duty and right living. Artha, prosperity and the means to live. Kama, pleasure, and moksha, liberation, are the other two and would be credited equally. One mark each, with no explanation needed or rewarded.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The specification lists four aims, and liberation is the last of them.","The other three cover duty, money and enjoyment.","Write two of the Sanskrit terms; a translation alongside costs nothing but earns nothing extra."],"codes":["3.1.4.1 — The four aims of human life"]},{"q":"Give two purposes of Hindu pilgrimage. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for each of two correct points, for example: to bathe in a sacred river; to gain darshan at a sacred site; to fulfil a vow; to perform rites for a dead relative; to seek merit or progress towards moksha.","sol":"To bathe in a sacred river such as the Ganges, which pilgrims believe purifies them. To gain darshan of the deity at a sacred site such as Varanasi. Fulfilling a vow, performing rites for a dead relative, and joining the Kumbh Mela to bathe where the rivers meet would all be credited too.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A place of pilgrimage is a tirtha, a crossing place where the divine is felt to be close.","Think about what a pilgrim does when they arrive, and who they might be doing it for.","Two short purposes are enough; a named site is not required by this question."],"codes":["3.1.4.2 — Pilgrimage"]},{"q":"Give two of the four paths towards yoga (union with the divine). [2 marks]","a":"One mark for each of two correct points: karma yoga (action); jnana yoga (knowledge); astanga yoga (meditation); bhakti yoga (devotion).","sol":"Bhakti yoga, the path of devotion. Karma yoga, the path of action. Jnana yoga, the path of knowledge, and astanga yoga, the path of meditation, are the other two. Naming two is enough; development is not credited on this question.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Yoga here means union with the divine, not exercise.","The four paths match four kinds of person: the active, the thoughtful, the meditative and the devoted.","Give two of the names; a one-word gloss in brackets is allowed but not needed."],"codes":["3.1.4.2 — The four paths towards yoga (union with the divine)"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting Hindu understandings of brahman. [4 marks]","a":"First contrast: brahman as nirguna, ultimate reality as divine consciousness beyond form, held as the higher truth in Advaita Vedanta. Second contrast: brahman as saguna, God manifested in form and worshipped as a personal Lord by Vaishnavas and Shaivas. Simple explanation of a contrast 1 mark, detailed 2, for each.","sol":"Some Hindus understand brahman as nirguna: ultimate reality as divine consciousness, beyond form, quality and description, so no name or image finally captures it. Advaita Vedanta, following Shankara, treats this as the higher truth, and on that understanding liberation is knowledge, the atman realised as one with brahman rather than a relationship with a God who is other.\n\nOther Hindus understand brahman as saguna: God manifested in form, with qualities that can be pictured and loved, worshipped as Vishnu by Vaishnavas or as Shiva by Shaivas. For them the personal Lord is not a stepping stone towards something higher but ultimate, which is why bhakti, a loving relationship sustained by puja, arati and darshan before a murti, is the highest practice rather than a concession to human weakness.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The specification's first bullet on the nature of God names the pair this question wants.","Give each understanding its own paragraph, and open the second with a word that sets it against the first.","For the second mark in each paragraph, add the tradition that holds the view and what it does about it in practice."],"codes":["3.1.4.1 — Brahman: different Hindu understandings of brahman","3.1.4.1 — Different understandings of the three features of the divine"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which belief in ahimsa influences Hindus today. [4 marks]","a":"First way: diet, with vegetarianism and vegetarian temple food. Second way: treatment of animals and public argument, with cow protection and goshalas, opposition to animal experimentation, and support for non-violence. The second mark in each case requires the influence itself to be shown.","sol":"Ahimsa influences what Hindus eat. Because the atman is present in every living being, harming a creature harms the divine, so many Hindus are vegetarian: meat is kept out of the house, the food offered at the mandir and shared as prasad is vegetarian, and those seeking moksha choose a diet that builds sattva rather than tamas.\n\nAhimsa also influences how Hindus treat animals and how they argue in public. Cow protection is funded and organised, so the goshala at Bhaktivedanta Manor in Hertfordshire keeps cows for their natural lives instead of sending them for slaughter. The same belief leads Hindus to oppose animal experimentation and to support non-violent protest, as Gandhi did, so ahimsa changes campaigning as well as cooking.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Ahimsa is non-violence in thought, word and deed: ask first what it does to daily life at home.","Write one paragraph per way, and make each one finish the sentence 'so a Hindu today ...'.","A belief stated without its consequence earns the simple mark only, so name a real project or a real decision for the second."],"codes":["3.1.4.1 — Personal virtues","3.1.4.2 — The work and significance of environmental projects and charities"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting Hindu views about the importance of worship. [4 marks]","a":"First view: worship is essential, since bhakti is the most widely followed path and daily puja, arati and darshan sustain a relationship with the personal Lord. Contrasting view: outward worship is not essential, since jnana yoga holds that ignorance binds and the Arya Samaj rejects murti worship; the divine within the heart can be served anywhere. Simple explanation 1 mark, detailed 2, for each.","sol":"For many Hindus worship is the heart of religion. Bhakti yoga, the path of devotion, is the most widely followed of the four paths, and it is kept up by daily practice: the home shrine tended each morning with light, water, food and incense, arati at the mandir, and darshan of the murti. On this view a relationship with the personal Lord has to be maintained, so worship is not optional.\n\nOther Hindus give outward worship far less weight. Those who follow jnana yoga hold that what binds a person is ignorance rather than neglect of ritual, so study and discrimination matter more; and the Arya Samaj, founded by Swami Dayananda Saraswati, rejects murti worship altogether, holding that the formless nirguna brahman needs no image. For them worship in the space of the heart is enough.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The specification asks for different Hindu views about how important worship is at all, not simply for different forms of it.","Pick one tradition that makes worship central and one that does not, and name both.","Check the two views genuinely differ: if both end up saying worship matters, only one of them can be credited."],"codes":["3.1.4.2 — Different forms of worship/meditation and their significance","3.1.4.2 — Focuses of worship and representations of the divine"]},{"q":"Explain two Hindu teachings about what happens after death. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"First teaching: the atman is eternal and is reborn, passing through samsara, with the next birth conditioned by karma. Second teaching: the cycle can end in moksha, understood as merging with brahman or as eternal relationship with the personal Lord. Simple 1, detailed 2 for each, plus one mark for a source such as Bhagavad Gita 2.22.","sol":"Hindus teach that the atman, the individual eternal inner self, is not destroyed when the body dies but is reborn. Death ends a body, not a person, and the self passes through samsara, the cycle of birth and death. What kind of birth follows is conditioned by karma, moral action and reaction, so the way a person lives now shapes what they are born into next.\n\nHindus also teach that the cycle can be ended in moksha, liberation. Some describe this as the atman merging with brahman, as a drop returns to the ocean; many Vaishnavas describe instead an eternal loving relationship with the personal Lord in a spiritual world such as Vaikuntha, and some hold that a person can be liberated while still alive, as a jivanmukta.\n\nBhagavad Gita 2.22 compares the self leaving a worn-out body to a person changing clothes.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Two teachings only: one about the cycle, and one about leaving it.","Develop each with its mechanism: what decides the next birth, and how different Hindus picture liberation.","The fifth mark is separate, so give the source a sentence of its own naming the text and what it teaches."],"codes":["3.1.4.1 — Cycle of birth and death: samsara; the law of karma; moksha","3.1.4.1 — The concept of atman","3.1.4 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Hinduism"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which Hindus in Great Britain celebrate Diwali. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"First way: at home, with cleaning, rangoli, divas lit and Lakshmi welcomed, and new account books for many Gujarati families. Second way: in the community, with puja and arati at the mandir, sweets shared, and public celebrations such as Leicester's. Simple 1, detailed 2 for each, plus one mark for a source such as the Ramayana account of Rama and Sita's return to Ayodhya.","sol":"Hindu families in Britain keep Diwali at home. The house is cleaned, rangoli patterns are laid at the doorway and rows of divas are lit in the windows, because light is set against darkness and Lakshmi, the goddess of wealth and good fortune, is welcomed into a home made ready for her. Many Gujarati families open new account books, treating Diwali as a new year.\n\nHindus in Britain also celebrate together. Mandirs hold puja and arati, sweets are shared with neighbours and with people of other faiths, and public celebrations such as the Diwali lights in Leicester draw one of the largest gatherings outside India, which keeps a minority community visible and confident here.\n\nThe Ramayana gives the origin many Hindus keep: lamps were lit to guide Rama and Sita home to Ayodhya after fourteen years of exile.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Split the answer between the home and the community: what happens in a Hindu house, and what happens in the mandir or the street.","For the second mark in each, say what the practice means or does for Hindus here, not just what it looks like.","The source mark can come from the story behind the festival, as long as you name the text it comes from."],"codes":["3.1.4.2 — Sacred festivals and their importance for Hindus in Great Britain today","3.1.4.2 — Places of worship and their importance","3.1.4.1 — How the divine presents: the Tri-murti, deities and avatara"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which Hindu charities and environmental projects help people. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"First way: feeding people, through Food for Life's free vegetarian meals, sewa offered without expecting return, promoting social inclusion. Second way: protecting animals and the land, through goshalas such as Bhaktivedanta Manor's and the Bhumi Project, or Sewa International's relief and women's projects. Simple 1, detailed 2 for each, plus one mark for a source such as Bhagavad Gita 17.20.","sol":"Hindu charities feed people. Food for Life serves free vegetarian meals to anyone who comes, so hunger is relieved without an animal being killed, and because the food is given to all regardless of background it is social inclusion in practice as well as sewa, selfless service offered without expecting a return.\n\nHindu projects also protect animals and the land. Goshalas keep cows for their natural lives rather than sending them for slaughter, and the goshala at Bhaktivedanta Manor in Hertfordshire is the British example; the Bhumi Project has drawn temples into tree planting and lower-impact living. Sewa International runs relief and development work, including projects for women's well-being and education.\n\nBhagavad Gita 17.20 teaches that the best giving is done as a duty, to someone who cannot repay it, in the right place and at the right time.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Hindu action here grows out of two beliefs in particular, ahimsa and sewa: take one project from each.","Name the project, then say who is better off because of it and how.","Keep the source mark separate: one sentence naming a text and its teaching about giving."],"codes":["3.1.4.2 — The work and significance of environmental projects and charities","3.1.4.1 — Personal virtues","3.1.4 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Hinduism"]},{"q":"'Belief in karma means Hindus cannot really be free.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\nrefer to Hindu teaching;\ngive reasoned arguments to support this statement;\ngive reasoned arguments to support a different point of view;\nreach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, marked by levels on the whole answer. In support: karma determines the body, family and circumstances a person is born into; varnashrama dharma fixes duty by varna and stage of life; maya and ignorance stop a person seeing clearly. Against: the specification requires individual free will alongside karma; karma is made by choices, so it presupposes freedom; Bhagavad Gita 2.47 addresses a free agent; the paths and sewa assume change is possible. Level 4 needs both sides reasoned, religion applied to the issue, and a justified conclusion.","sol":"Hindus who accept the law of karma do accept that a great deal about them is already settled. Karma is moral action and reaction: the deeds of earlier lives ripen into the body, family, country and circumstances a person is born into, and nobody chooses those. Varnashrama dharma sharpens the point, because duty is given by a person's varna and by their stage of life rather than chosen, so a Hindu who takes it seriously finds their obligations already written for them. Maya and ignorance add a further limit: if appearances mislead, a person may not even see their situation clearly enough to choose well. On this reading the statement is fair, and it would explain why karma is sometimes blamed for fatalism and for the acceptance of inequality.\n\nHindu teaching, however, requires individual free will alongside karma, and the argument for that is strong. Karma is consequence rather than fate, and consequence presupposes a choice: if actions were not free they could not be moral, and the whole law would collapse into mere physics. The Bhagavad Gita is addressed to exactly this problem. At 2.47 Krishna tells Arjuna that his right is to action and not to its fruits, which only makes sense if Arjuna can decide what to do, and karma yoga, acting from duty without attachment to results, is advice given to a free agent. The four aims of human life assume the same thing, since dharma, artha, kama and moksha are aims to be pursued and ordered, not events that happen to a person.\n\nThe paths towards liberation make the assumption too. Jnana yoga holds that ignorance binds and knowledge frees, so a person can act upon their own condition; bhakti yoga offers a relationship with the personal Lord that can be entered at any time, which is why devotional traditions insist that anyone, of any background, can turn to God. Hindus also respond to suffering rather than merely explaining it, through sewa, charity and ahimsa, and that response would be pointless if nothing could be changed.\n\nThe better conclusion is that the statement mistakes a limit for a cage. Karma does constrain, since it sets the starting point, and a Hindu will not pretend otherwise. But it constrains circumstances rather than the will, and every practice the specification names, from puja and japa to pilgrimage and selfless service, is a use of freedom within those circumstances. Because karma is itself made by free choices, belief in karma requires freedom rather than removing it, so the statement should be rejected.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what karma actually claims: is it about the circumstances a person is born into, or about every choice they then make?","Take the statement seriously for a paragraph before answering it, and use varnashrama dharma and maya to build the strongest case for it.","Look for a Hindu teaching that only makes sense if people are free, and build the second half of the answer on it."],"codes":["3.1.4.1 — Individual free will and responses to suffering; knowledge and ignorance","3.1.4.1 — Cycle of birth and death: samsara; the law of karma; moksha","3.1.4.1 — The meaning of dharma; sanatana dharma; varnashrama dharma","3.1.4.1 — Matter (prakriti); the three qualities (tri-guna); illusion (maya); cosmology"]},{"q":"'The murti is the most important part of Hindu worship.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\nrefer to Hindu teaching;\ngive reasoned arguments to support this statement;\ngive reasoned arguments to support a different point of view;\nreach a justified conclusion. [12 marks]","a":"AO2, marked by levels. In support: saguna brahman is God manifested in form; the murti is the focus of darshan, puja and arati at home and in the mandir, where it is treated as an honoured guest; bhakti is the most widely followed path. Against: the murti is a representation of God, so devotion is what counts; the Arya Samaj rejects murti worship; japa, kirtan, havan, jnana yoga and the space of the heart need no image; Shaivas may worship at the lingam, and the specification lists other focuses.","sol":"For most Hindus the murti really is where worship happens. Brahman is understood as saguna as well as nirguna, God manifested in form, and if the divine takes form then a form is exactly what devotion needs. In the mandir the installed murti makes darshan possible, seeing the deity and being seen by it, and the priest treats it as an honoured guest: woken in the morning, washed, dressed, offered food and put to rest at night. Puja and arati are shaped around it, with light, water, flowers and incense offered and the lamp circled before it. At home the shrine repeats that pattern daily, which is how Hindu children learn the religion. Bhakti yoga is the most widely followed of the four paths and the murti is its focus, so judged by what Hindus actually do, the statement looks right.\n\nAgainst that, the specification's own wording is careful: the murti is a representation of God, not God reduced to an object. What is worshipped is the deity the murti represents, so the devotion rather than the image is the essential thing. Bhagavad Gita 9.26 teaches that a leaf, a flower, fruit or water offered with devotion is accepted, which puts the weight on the spirit of the offering. Several Hindu practices need no murti at all: japa on a mala, bhajan and kirtan, havan before the sacred fire, meditation in astanga yoga, and worship in the space of the heart, which the specification lists as a place of worship in its own right. Jnana yoga holds that knowledge, not ritual, removes the ignorance that binds.\n\nHindus themselves divide here, which matters because the specification expects divergent views. The Arya Samaj, founded by Swami Dayananda Saraswati, rejects murti worship outright and returns to the Vedas, arguing that nirguna brahman is beyond form and that images mislead. Shaivas commonly worship at the lingam rather than at an image in human shape, which shows that even among those who worship in form the murti is not the only focus, and the specification's own list of focuses includes the guru and elders, holy rivers and hills, and sacred plants and animals.\n\nThe fairest judgement is that the murti is the most important focus of Hindu worship in practice while not being necessary in principle. For a Vaishnava family whose day begins at the home shrine nothing else is as central. But because the divine is also everywhere and within the heart, Hinduism can lose the image and keep the worship, so the statement overstates its case.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Start from what the murti makes possible that nothing else does.","The specification's own description of the murti is the hinge of the counter-argument: find the word it uses.","Bring in a Hindu group that rejects murti worship, so that the other side of the argument is a Hindu view rather than an outsider's."],"codes":["3.1.4.2 — Focuses of worship and representations of the divine","3.1.4.2 — Different forms of worship/meditation and their significance","3.1.4.2 — Places of worship and their importance","3.1.4.1 — Brahman: different Hindu understandings of brahman"]},{"q":"'Bhakti yoga is the best path towards union with the divine.'\nEvaluate this statement.\nIn your answer you should:\nrefer to Hindu teaching;\ngive reasoned arguments to support this statement;\ngive reasoned arguments to support a different point of view;\nreach a justified conclusion. [12 marks]","a":"AO2, marked by levels. In support: bhakti is open to all regardless of learning, wealth or status; it rests on saguna brahman and on avatara, so a relationship is possible; Bhagavad Gita 9.26; puja, arati, kirtan and pilgrimage are its ordinary practices, and it fits busy Hindu families in Britain. Against: jnana yoga removes the ignorance that binds; karma yoga fits the householder and varnashrama dharma, with Bhagavad Gita 2.47; astanga yoga trains the mind through Patanjali's eight limbs; four paths exist because temperaments differ, so ranking them misses the point.","sol":"Bhakti yoga has a strong claim. It is the path of devotion to a personal God, and it rests on brahman as saguna, God manifested in form, and on avatara, the belief that God descends into the world as Krishna and Rama did. That makes a relationship possible: the Lord can be loved, served, sung to and seen. Bhagavad Gita 9.26 teaches that a leaf, a flower, fruit or water offered with devotion is accepted, and the force of that teaching is that no learning, wealth or status is required. Bhakti is therefore the most inclusive path, open to a labourer, a woman or a child on the same terms as a scholar, which is why kirtan, arati, pilgrimage to Varanasi and daily puja at a home shrine are the ordinary shape of Hindu life. In Britain, where few Hindus can give years to study or meditation, bhakti is also the path a busy family can actually follow.\n\nThe other paths answer back. Jnana yoga holds that what binds a person is ignorance rather than a lack of love: until the atman is known as it truly is, devotion may still rest on maya, and the Chandogya Upanishad 6.8.7 teaching 'tat tvam asi', that thou art, is knowledge rather than feeling. Karma yoga suits the householder who cannot leave duty behind, since Bhagavad Gita 2.47 teaches action performed without attachment to its fruits, so ordinary work becomes a path and varnashrama dharma is honoured rather than escaped. Astanga yoga trains the mind through the eight limbs set out in Patanjali's Yoga Sutras, and Hindus who hold a nirguna understanding of brahman would say that meditation goes furthest, since a reality beyond form cannot finally be loved as a person.\n\nThere is also a case against ranking the paths at all. The specification asks for their differences and their importance, and the classical Hindu position is that four paths exist because people differ: the emotional, the intellectual, the active and the contemplative each need their own route to the same goal, and most Hindus in fact mix them, singing kirtan, performing duty and meditating in the same week.\n\nThe judgement that follows is that bhakti yoga is the best path for most Hindus rather than the best path simply. Its openness is a real superiority, because it reaches people the other three would leave out. But since all four lead to union with the divine and are chosen according to temperament, calling one best misunderstands why Hinduism offers four, so the statement should be qualified rather than accepted.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what bhakti offers that the other three do not, and in particular who is able to follow it.","Give each of the other three paths a reason for preferring it, and tie each reason to a named text or teacher.","A strong conclusion here may refuse the ranking: say why Hinduism offers four paths at all, then judge."],"codes":["3.1.4.2 — The four paths towards yoga (union with the divine)","3.1.4.1 — How the divine presents: the Tri-murti, deities and avatara","3.1.4 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Hinduism","3.1.4.1 — Brahman: different Hindu understandings of brahman"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What do nirguna and saguna each mean?","a":"Nirguna: brahman as ultimate reality, divine consciousness beyond form. Saguna: God manifested in form."},{"q":"Name the Tri-murti and say what each one does.","a":"Brahma the creator, Vishnu the preserver, Shiva the destroyer."},{"q":"What is the difference between sanatana dharma and varnashrama dharma?","a":"Sanatana dharma is the eternal way, the duties holding for all Hindus; varnashrama dharma is duty particular to a person's varna and stage of life."},{"q":"Which prescribed reference goes with Hindu cosmology?","a":"Rig Veda 10.129.6-7."}]},"cards":[{"front":"nirguna and saguna","back":"Two Hindu understandings of brahman: nirguna is ultimate reality as divine consciousness, beyond form or quality; saguna is God manifested in form, worshipped as a named deity such as Vishnu or Shiva.","code":"3.1.4.1 — Brahman: different Hindu understandings of brahman","open":false},{"front":"The three features of the divine","back":"Everywhere, as non-personal brahman; within the heart, as the inner controller; beyond, as a personal loving God. Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 391 is the prescribed reference.","code":"3.1.4.1 — Different understandings of the three features of the divine","open":false},{"front":"The Tri-murti","back":"Brahma the creator, Vishnu the preserver and Shiva the destroyer: three functions of one divine reality rather than three competing gods.","code":"3.1.4.1 — How the divine presents: the Tri-murti, deities and avatara","open":false},{"front":"avatara","back":"God descending into the world in a form. Krishna and Rama are the instances the specification names, both avatars of Vishnu; Bhagavad Gita 4.7-8 has Krishna promise to come whenever dharma declines.","code":"3.1.4.1 — How the divine presents: the Tri-murti, deities and avatara","open":false},{"front":"prakriti, maya and the tri-guna","back":"Prakriti is matter; maya is illusion, taking the changing world for the whole of reality; the tri-guna are matter's three qualities, sattva (goodness), rajas (passion) and tamas (inertia).","code":"3.1.4.1 — Matter (prakriti); the three qualities (tri-guna); illusion (maya); cosmology","open":false},{"front":"atman","back":"The individual, eternal inner self, distinct from material mind and body. It is what is reborn, and Bhagavad Gita 2.22 compares its leaving a body to changing worn-out clothes.","code":"3.1.4.1 — The concept of atman","open":false},{"front":"samsara, karma and moksha","back":"Samsara is the cycle of birth and death; karma is moral action and reaction, conditioning the next birth; moksha is liberation, described as merging with brahman or as eternal relationship with the Lord.","code":"3.1.4.1 — Cycle of birth and death: samsara; the law of karma; moksha","open":false},{"front":"Hindu free will alongside karma","back":"Karma sets the circumstances a person is born into; free will decides how they act within them. Without it no action could be moral and dharma would be empty.","code":"3.1.4.1 — Individual free will and responses to suffering; knowledge and ignorance","open":false},{"front":"ahimsa","back":"Non-violence in thought, word and deed, grounded in the atman present in every living being. It shapes vegetarianism, cow protection and Hindu arguments about war and animal testing.","code":"3.1.4.1 — Personal virtues","open":false},{"front":"The four aims of human life","back":"Dharma, duty; artha, prosperity; kama, pleasure; moksha, liberation. Wealth and pleasure are legitimate when pursued within dharma and ordered towards moksha.","code":"3.1.4.1 — The four aims of human life","open":false},{"front":"sanatana dharma and varnashrama dharma","back":"Sanatana dharma is the eternal way, the duties holding for all Hindus. Varnashrama dharma is duty particular to a person's varna and stage of life: student, householder, retired, renunciate.","code":"3.1.4.1 — The meaning of dharma; sanatana dharma; varnashrama dharma","open":false},{"front":"The places of Hindu worship","back":"Home shrine, temple (mandir), outdoor shrines, and the space of the heart. The last is not a building at all, so a Hindu can worship anywhere.","code":"3.1.4.2 — Places of worship and their importance","open":false},{"front":"puja, arati and havan","back":"Puja is worship before the murti with offerings; arati is the lamp circled before the murti while a hymn is sung; havan is the fire ritual, ghee and grains offered with mantras.","code":"3.1.4.2 — Different forms of worship/meditation and their significance","open":false},{"front":"The murti","back":"A representation of God, letting the formless be met in form. In the mandir it is treated as a guest: woken, dressed and fed. The Arya Samaj rejects murti worship.","code":"3.1.4.2 — Focuses of worship and representations of the divine","open":false},{"front":"Diwali and Holi","back":"Diwali: autumn festival of lights, divas lit and Lakshmi welcomed, with the Ramayana return of Rama and Sita. Holi: spring festival of colours, its bonfire recalling Prahlad and Holika.","code":"3.1.4.2 — Sacred festivals and their importance for Hindus in Great Britain today","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Beliefs and teachings (3.1.4.1): every named item and its source","items":["Brahman — ultimate reality. Nirguna: divine consciousness beyond form and quality. Saguna: God manifested in form. Advaita Vedanta, following Shankara, treats nirguna as the higher truth; Vaishnava traditions treat the personal Lord as ultimate.","Spiritual worlds — realms in which the personal God is present, such as Vaikuntha, the realm of Vishnu; many Vaishnavas describe moksha as eternal life there.","The three features of the divine — everywhere, as non-personal brahman; within the heart, as the inner controller (antaryami); beyond, as a personal loving God. Prescribed reference: Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 391.","The Tri-murti — Brahma the creator, Vishnu the preserver, Shiva the destroyer: three functions of one divine reality.","Ganesha — elephant-headed son of Shiva and Parvati, worshipped at the start of an undertaking as the remover of obstacles.","Lakshmi — goddess of wealth and good fortune, consort of Vishnu, welcomed into the cleaned home at Diwali.","Hanuman — the monkey devotee and servant of Rama in the Ramayana, the model of strength in devotion.","Saraswati — goddess of learning, music and the arts, invoked by students and musicians.","Avatara — God descending into the world. Krishna and Rama are the instances named, both avatars of Vishnu. Bhagavad Gita 4.7-8: Krishna comes whenever dharma declines.","Prakriti — matter. The tri-guna are its three qualities: sattva, goodness and purity; rajas, passion and restless energy; tamas, darkness and inertia.","Maya — illusion: mistaking the changing material world for the whole of reality.","Cosmology — prescribed reference Rig Veda 10.129.6-7, the creation hymn that asks who really knows how creation began and allows that even the highest overseer may not know.","The cycle of four ages — time runs through four repeating ages, the present one being the Kali Yuga; with them go many worlds and their diverse inhabitants.","Atman — the individual, eternal inner self, distinct from material mind and body. Bhagavad Gita 2.20: it is never born and never dies; 2.22: leaving a body is like changing worn-out clothes.","Samsara — the cycle of birth and death through which the atman passes.","Karma — moral action and reaction; deeds ripen in this life or a later one and condition the next birth.","Moksha — liberation. Types include the atman merging with brahman; an eternal loving relationship with the personal Lord in a spiritual world; and liberation while still alive, as a jivanmukta.","Individual free will — karma sets the circumstances a person is born into, while free will decides what they do within them, which is what makes dharma and moral praise or blame possible.","Responses to suffering — explanation through karma, and action: sewa (selfless service), charity, ahimsa, and the pursuit of knowledge, since ignorance of the atman is the root condition to be escaped.","Personal virtues — ahimsa (non-violence), respect, empathy, mind and sense control, humility, love.","The four aims of human life — dharma (duty), artha (prosperity), kama (pleasure), moksha (liberation).","Dharma — duty, law and the way things should be. Sanatana dharma: the eternal way, the name many Hindus give their own tradition. Varnashrama dharma: duty by varna and by ashrama — brahmacharya (student), grihastha (householder), vanaprastha (retired), sannyasa (renunciate).","Shaivism and Vaishnavism — the two Hindu perspectives the specification names: Shaivas worship Shiva as supreme, Vaishnavas worship Vishnu and his avatars as supreme."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Practices (3.1.4.2): every named practice, place and project","items":["Places of worship — the home shrine; the temple (mandir); outdoors, such as roadside, tree or river shrines; and the space of the heart.","Havan — the fire ritual: ghee, grains and wood offered into a sacred fire while mantras are recited, used at weddings, openings and purifications.","Puja — worship before the murti, with offerings of light, water, food, flowers and incense; performed daily at the home shrine and by the priest in the mandir.","Arati — the offering of the lamp, circled before the murti while a hymn is sung; devotees then pass their hands over the flame and touch their eyes and head.","Darshan — seeing the deity and being seen by it: the central act of a visit to the mandir.","Bhajan and kirtan — devotional singing; kirtan is often call and response, led with drum and cymbals.","Japa and mantra — repetition of a mantra, commonly on a mala of 108 beads; the Gayatri Mantra comes from Rig Veda 3.62.10.","Shaiva and Vaishnava worship — Shaivas often worship at the lingam, wear sacred ash and rudraksha beads and use the mantra Om Namah Shivaya; Vaishnavas wear an upright tilak mark and tulsi beads and chant the names of Vishnu, Rama and Krishna.","Different views about the importance of worship — bhakti traditions make daily worship essential; jnana traditions put knowledge first; the Arya Samaj, founded in 1875 by Swami Dayananda Saraswati, rejects murti worship altogether.","Focuses of worship — one God, personal or non-personal; the many deities, the guru and other elders; holy land, hills and rivers, above all the Ganges; sacred plants and animals, such as tulsi and the cow; and the murti as a representation of God.","Diwali — autumn festival of lights in the month of Kartik: the home cleaned, rangoli laid, divas lit and Lakshmi welcomed. Origin many Hindus keep: the Ramayana account of Rama and Sita returning to Ayodhya after fourteen years of exile. Many Gujarati families open new account books; Leicester holds one of the largest celebrations outside India.","Holi — spring festival of colours in the month of Phalguna: a bonfire recalling the Puranic story of Prahlad, saved by devotion to Vishnu while Holika burned, then coloured powders thrown the next day, when differences of status are set aside.","The four paths towards yoga — karma yoga, action without attachment to its fruits (Bhagavad Gita 2.47); jnana yoga, knowledge; astanga yoga, meditation through the eight limbs of Patanjali's Yoga Sutras; bhakti yoga, devotion (Bhagavad Gita 9.26), the most widely followed.","Pilgrimage — a place of pilgrimage is a tirtha, a crossing place. Practices and purposes: bathing in a sacred river, darshan at the shrine, rites for a dead relative, the keeping of a vow. Pilgrimage is not compulsory in Hinduism.","Varanasi — the city on the Ganges sacred to Shiva, with its ghats and cremation grounds, where many Hindus hope to die and be cremated.","Kumbh Mela — the great bathing festival held in rotation at Prayagraj, Haridwar, Ujjain and Nashik, every twelve years at each site, and the largest religious gathering in the world.","Cow protection — goshalas keep cows for their natural lives instead of sending them for slaughter; the goshala at Bhaktivedanta Manor in Hertfordshire, the temple given to the Hare Krishna movement by George Harrison in 1973, is the British example.","Hindu environmental projects — the Bhumi Project, the Hindu environmental initiative of the Oxford Centre for Hindu Studies, working with temples on tree planting, vegetarian food and lower-impact living.","Charities — Sewa International, whose name means selfless service, runs relief and development work including projects for women's well-being and education; Food for Life serves free vegetarian meals to anyone who comes, promoting social inclusion."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the sources of wisdom and authority an examiner will credit","items":["Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 391 — prescribed against the three features of the divine; cite it when a question asks about the divine everywhere, within the heart, or beyond as a personal God.","Rig Veda 10.129.6-7 — prescribed against cosmology: the creation hymn that asks who really knows how creation began.","Bhagavad Gita 2.20 — the atman is never born and never dies: the reference for the self and for life after death.","Bhagavad Gita 2.22 — leaving a body is compared to changing worn-out clothes: the reference for rebirth.","Bhagavad Gita 2.47 — a person's right is to action and not to its fruits: the reference for karma yoga and for duty.","Bhagavad Gita 4.7-8 — Krishna promises to come whenever dharma declines: the reference for avatara.","Bhagavad Gita 9.26 — a leaf, a flower, fruit or water offered with devotion is accepted: the reference for bhakti and for puja.","Bhagavad Gita 17.20 — the best giving is done as a duty, to someone who cannot repay it, in the right place and at the right time: the reference for charity.","Chandogya Upanishad 6.8.7 — 'tat tvam asi', that thou art: the reference for jnana yoga and for the identity of atman and brahman.","The Ramayana — Rama, Sita and the return to Ayodhya: the reference for Diwali and for Rama as avatara.","The Puranas — the story of Prahlad, saved by devotion to Vishnu while Holika burned: the reference for Holi.","Patanjali's Yoga Sutras — the eight limbs of astanga yoga: the reference for meditation as a path.","Shruti and smriti — shruti is the four Vedas (Rig, Sama, Yajur, Atharva) with the Upanishads that close them; smriti is the Ramayana, the Mahabharata including the Bhagavad Gita, and the Puranas.","How the source mark works — one mark, given once, for a relevant and accurate reference to sacred writings or another source of Hindu belief and teaching. A paraphrase attributed to a named text counts; an unattributed idea does not."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Evaluate: 'The murti is the most important part of Hindu worship.')","items":["Argument: for most Hindus the murti is where worship actually happens, because it is the focus that makes darshan possible, seeing the deity and being seen by it.","Religious teaching cited: brahman is understood as saguna as well as nirguna, God manifested in form, so a form is precisely what devotion needs; the specification itself calls the murti a representation of God.","Development: in the mandir the installed murti is treated as an honoured guest, woken, washed, dressed, offered food and put to rest, and puja and arati are shaped around it, with the lamp circled before it; at home the same pattern is repeated daily, which is how children learn the religion.","Counter-view answered rather than ignored: the Arya Samaj, founded by Swami Dayananda Saraswati, rejects murti worship on the ground that nirguna brahman is beyond form, and japa, kirtan, havan and worship in the space of the heart need no image at all.","Mini-judgement: the murti is the most important focus in practice but not necessary in principle, since the divine is also everywhere and within the heart.","Why this is Level 4: it is 'a well-argued response, reasoned consideration of different points of view', its chain of reasoning leads to 'judgement(s) supported by knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information', and its references to religion — saguna brahman, the Arya Samaj, arati and darshan — are 'applied to the issue' rather than listed.","Specialist terms used accurately and widely (SPaG is not assessed on this Practices question, only on the Beliefs 12-mark one, but the same habit earns AO2 credit here) — murti, darshan, saguna, nirguna, arati, bhakti — which is what 'a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate' asks for."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same statement, visibly weaker)","items":["Argument: 'The murti is very important because Hindus pray in front of it, and it helps them concentrate on God instead of getting distracted.'","What it does have: a point of view with a reason, and then a second reason, so it counts as reasoned consideration of one point of view.","Evidence: only a general reference to Hindus praying. No named belief, no text, no tradition, so it sits at the bottom of the level rather than rising.","Counter-view: none is developed, only the sentence 'but some Hindus might not agree' — recognition with no reason or evidence behind it.","Judgement: 'so I think the murti is important', which restates the opening instead of weighing anything.","The descriptor it meets: Level 2, 'Reasoned consideration of a point of view. A logical chain of reasoning drawing on knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information.'","The descriptors it misses: Level 3's 'Reasoned consideration of different points of view' and 'Clear reference to religion', and Level 4's judgements 'supported by knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information'.","What would lift it two levels: name saguna brahman as the teaching behind worship in form, give the Arya Samaj as a Hindu voice on the other side, and end by saying which side is stronger and why."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'Belief in karma means Hindus cannot really be free.' Evaluate this statement.","items":["The question decoded: does the law of karma, moral action and reaction, remove individual free will? The specification puts karma and free will in the same part of the course, so the examiner expects both to be held together, not one of them dropped.","Opening move: no definitions paragraph. State the issue in a sentence and begin the case in support, because the levels reward argument rather than exposition.","Paragraph 1, in support: karma settles the body, family, country and circumstances a person is born into, and nobody chooses those. Source used: the law of karma as moral action and reaction.","Paragraph 2, in support: varnashrama dharma gives duty by varna and by stage of life rather than by choice, and maya and ignorance mean a person may not see their situation clearly enough to choose well.","Paragraph 3, against: karma is consequence, not fate, and consequence presupposes choice; if actions were not free they could not be moral and the law would collapse. Source used: Bhagavad Gita 2.47, a person's right is to action and not to its fruits, which is advice given to someone who can decide.","Paragraph 4, against: the paths assume freedom — jnana yoga says knowledge removes the ignorance that binds, bhakti yoga is open to anyone at any time — and Hindus respond to suffering through sewa, charity and ahimsa, which would be pointless if nothing could be changed.","The evaluation thread running through the whole answer: karma limits circumstances, not the will. Say it in paragraph 3 and return to it in the conclusion, so the answer argues one line rather than listing views.","What the conclusion must say: which side is stronger and why — that the statement mistakes a limit for a cage, since karma is itself made by free choices, so belief in karma requires freedom rather than removing it. A conclusion that only says both sides have a point does not reach the top level.","Timing: about a minute a mark, so roughly twelve minutes of writing plus a moment at the end to check spelling, punctuation and grammar, which are assessed on this answer.","Specialist terms worth spending: karma, samsara, dharma, varnashrama dharma, maya, moksha, sewa, ahimsa, jnana yoga, bhakti yoga. They carry the argument and they are also what 'a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate' means."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T14:06:25Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":1,"notes":"Re-judged against the kit file after the hand fix; nothing outstanding. [SPaG 3 marks] now appears only on room.questions[12] (karma and free will, 3.1.4.1 Beliefs) and is gone from the murti and bhakti-yoga Practices stems; room.facts[6], lesson.examples[3].setup and the extras[5] plan (on the Beliefs statement, so 'assessed on this answer' is right) all agree. The reworded extras[3] specialist-terms line is accurate: SPaG is not assessed on that Practices question, while a wide range of specialist terms still earns AO2 credit. Re-read whole: all 11 scripture references (Brihadaranyaka Upanishad 391, Rig Veda 10.129.6-7 and 3.62.10, Bhagavad Gita 2.20, 2.22, 2.47, 4.7-8, 9.26, 17.20, Chandogya Upanishad 6.8.7, Ramayana, Puranas, Patanjali's Yoga Sutras) and 'tat tvam asi' are correct; attributions (Advaita/Shankara, Vaishnava, Shaiva, Arya Samaj 1875 under Dayananda Saraswati, Bhaktivedanta Manor 1973, Bhumi Project, Sewa International, Food for Life) hold; tariffs run 1, 2, 4, 5, 12 only; all 18 idea codes are tested; no quotation exceeds three words and no printed June 2023 or specimen question is reproduced; model paragraphs sit correctly at Levels 4 and 2 on the grid. Publishable."}}},"3.1.5":{"id":"AQA-8062","topic":"3.1.5","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Islam is one of the two religion options you answer in Paper 1, and it is worth 51 marks of the 204 the qualification is graded on. The examiner sets two five-part questions on it: one on Beliefs and teachings, one on Practices, running 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks, with the spelling, punctuation and grammar marks riding on the Beliefs evaluation. Nearly every mark is lost in the same three ways: writing 'Muslims believe' when Sunni and Shi'a Muslims believe differently, explaining a practice without saying what it does to a believer's life, and finishing a 5-mark answer without naming the Qur'an, a Hadith or the teaching of the Imams. This room drills the content the specification actually names, tradition by tradition, with the source attached to each teaching, so that every answer you write can be traced back to something the examiner can tick.","idea":[{"h":"The three things every answer on Islam must do","t":"The specification asks the same three things of every belief and practice in this room. First, root it in a source of wisdom and authority: the Qur'an, the Sunnah and Hadith of Muhammad, or, for Shi'a Muslims, the teaching of the Imams. The fifth mark on the 5-mark question is for that reference alone, and it is the easiest mark on the paper to forget. Second, show the influence of the belief or practice on individuals, communities and societies - what a Muslim actually does differently because of it. Third, say whose Islam you are describing: common and divergent views must run through your answers, and the document names Sunni and Shi'a Islam as the perspectives to draw on and lists specific differences you must study. A top-level answer therefore never writes an unattributed 'Muslims believe' where the two traditions part company; it names the tradition, gives the belief and then explains why the difference matters to how those Muslims live.","code":"3.1.5 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Islam"},{"h":"Six articles of faith and five roots of Usul ad-Din","t":"Sunni Muslims summarise the faith in six articles: Tawhid, the Oneness of God; belief in angels; belief in the holy books; belief in the prophets; belief in the Day of Judgement; and belief in al-Qadr, God's decree. The classic source is the Hadith of Jibril, recorded in Sahih Muslim, in which the angel questions Muhammad and the answer lists exactly these. Twelver Shi'a Muslims summarise the same faith in five roots of Usul ad-Din: Tawhid; Adalat, divine justice; Nubuwwah, prophethood; Imamah, the imamate; and Mi'ad, the resurrection and Day of Judgement. Most of the content is shared - one God with no partner, prophets sent with revelation, a resurrection and a judgement - and Shi'a Muslims certainly believe in angels and the holy books; they simply do not list them as roots. The real difference is what each list puts at the foundation: Sunni Islam names God's decree, Shi'a Islam names God's justice and the divinely appointed leadership of the Imams. This is the room's ready-made 4-mark contrast, and a top-level answer uses it to show the difference is one of emphasis and authority, not of a different God.","code":"3.1.5.1 — The six articles of faith in Sunni Islam and five roots of Usul ad-Din in Shi'a Islam"},{"h":"Tawhid: the Oneness of God","t":"Tawhid means the absolute oneness of God, and it is the first article of faith for Sunni Muslims and the first root of Usul ad-Din for Shi'a Muslims. Qur'an Surah 112, al-Ikhlas, is prescribed for study and questions may be set on it: Surah 112:1 declares 'He is God, the One' and the surah goes on to deny that God begets or is begotten or has any equal. Everything else about God follows from it. God has no partner, no family and no image, which is why mosques carry calligraphy and geometric pattern rather than pictures, and why shirk, associating anything with God, is the gravest wrong in Islam. Tawhid is not only a doctrine to state; it shapes a Muslim's day, because prayer, giving and fasting are all offered to one God alone and nothing - money, status, a person - may be put in his place. A top-level answer quotes the short phrase from Surah 112 with its reference and then shows Tawhid doing work: ruling out idols, ruling out picturing God, and ruling out dividing loyalty.","code":"3.1.5.1 — Tawhid (the Oneness of God), Qur'an Surah 112"},{"h":"The nature of God, immanence and transcendence","t":"The specification names five attributes. God is omnipotent, all-powerful, the creator who sustains everything that exists. God is beneficent, generous and good to his creation. God is merciful: every surah but one opens by calling him 'the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful' (Qur'an 1:1), so mercy is the first word a Muslim reads. God is fair and just, which Shi'a Muslims name Adalat and raise to a root of Usul ad-Din: because God is perfectly just he never wrongs anyone and never punishes what he compelled. Alongside these sits the question of God's relationship with the world. Transcendence says God is utterly beyond creation, beyond time, place and human imagining, which is why no image of him is permitted. Immanence says God is present and close: the Qur'an says he is 'nearer to him than his jugular vein' (50:16), and that closeness is why Muslims pray directly to God without a priest. Most Muslims hold both together. A top-level answer uses the pairing as an argument, not a list: it is God's justice and mercy together that make the problem of suffering hard, and immanence that makes salah worth making five times a day.","code":"3.1.5.1 — The nature of God: omnipotence, beneficence, mercy, fairness and justice/Adalat"},{"h":"Angels: Jibril and Mika'il","t":"Belief in angels is one of the six articles of faith in Sunni Islam, and the Qur'an lists it among the things a believer must accept (4:136). Angels are created beings, made from light, without free will and without the ability to disobey; they are not divine and are never worshipped, because that would be shirk. Their role is to carry out God's commands and to link God with humanity. The specification names two. Jibril is the angel of revelation: he brought God's word to the prophets and dictated the Qur'an to Muhammad, beginning in the cave of Hira. Mika'il is the angel of mercy and sustenance, associated with providing rain, nourishment and the rewards God gives. Muslims also believe angels record human deeds, which is why belief in angels connects straight to the Day of Judgement. A top-level answer does not stop at nature and role: it says what belief in angels changes, namely that a Muslim who believes his words are written down behaves as if he is always seen.","code":"3.1.5.1 — Angels, their nature and role"},{"h":"Predestination, free will and the Day of Judgement","t":"Al-Qadr, God's decree, is the sixth article of faith in Sunni Islam: nothing happens outside God's knowledge and will, and Muslims speak of what God has written. Yet the same list of articles contains the Day of Judgement, which only makes sense if people are genuinely responsible for what they do. The specification deliberately asks for the relationship between the two, not for each on its own. Most Sunni Muslims hold that God knows and permits every act while human beings still choose freely, so knowledge is not compulsion. Shi'a Muslims argue from Adalat, God's justice: a just God cannot reward or punish someone for an act he forced, so human acts are neither wholly compelled nor wholly independent of God. The Qur'an supports human freedom plainly - 'There is no compulsion in religion' (2:256) - and calls the human being a khalifah, a steward answerable for the trust (2:30). A top-level answer holds both beliefs together and shows what they do in practice: a Muslim accepts what cannot be changed as God's will and still takes responsibility for the choices that can.","code":"3.1.5.1 — Predestination and human freedom and its relationship to the Day of Judgement"},{"h":"Akhirah: resurrection, heaven and hell","t":"Akhirah is everlasting life after death, and belief in it is an article of faith. Muslims believe that at death the soul enters barzakh, a state of waiting, until God brings the world to an end and raises the dead bodily. On Yawm ad-Din, the Day of Judgement, each person's deeds are weighed and each answers for them; those God accepts enter Jannah, the Garden, described as a place of peace and flowing water, and those he condemns enter Jahannam, the Fire. The moral point is built into the belief: because there will be a resurrection and a reckoning, every person is responsible and accountable now, and the Qur'an promises that 'an atom's weight of good' will be seen again (99:7). Muslims differ over how literally the descriptions of Jannah and Jahannam should be read, some taking them as physical, others as images of nearness to God and separation from him. A top-level answer makes Akhirah do moral work in the present: it explains why a Muslim gives zakah, keeps the fast and forgives, rather than merely describing the afterlife.","code":"3.1.5.1 — Akhirah (life after death), human responsibility and accountability"},{"h":"Risalah: Adam, Ibrahim and Muhammad","t":"Risalah is prophethood, the channel by which God guides humanity. Muslims believe God has sent messengers to every people, all teaching the same message of submission to one God, and belief in the prophets is an article of faith. The specification names three. Adam is the first human being and the first prophet, taught by God and made khalifah, steward of the earth. Ibrahim is the model of submission: he rejected the idols of his people, was ready to sacrifice his son at God's command, and with Isma'il raised the Ka'aba at Makkah, which is why his family's actions are re-enacted on Hajj and why Id-ul-Adha commemorates his obedience. Muhammad is the final prophet, the Seal of the Prophets (Qur'an 33:40), through whom the Qur'an was revealed and whose Sunnah and Hadith are a source of authority second only to it. Sunni and Shi'a Muslims agree entirely on Risalah; they divide over what happened to authority after Muhammad died. A top-level answer gives each prophet a job rather than a date: Adam begins the line, Ibrahim models submission, Muhammad closes and completes it.","code":"3.1.5.1 — Risalah (Prophethood)"},{"h":"The Qur'an and the earlier books","t":"The Qur'an is the word of God revealed through the angel Jibril to Muhammad over about twenty-three years, beginning in the cave of Hira and recited, memorised and written down in Arabic. Its authority is supreme: it is believed to be unaltered, so where any other source disagrees with it the Qur'an settles the matter, and it is the first thing a Muslim turns to for guidance in law, worship and daily life. Muslims also believe God sent earlier books: the Tawrat, the Torah, to Musa; the Zabur, the Psalms, to Dawud; the Injil, the Gospel, to Isa; and the Scrolls of Abraham to Ibrahim, now lost. These carry real authority as revelation from the same God, which is why Jews and Christians are called People of the Book and why Islam claims continuity rather than novelty. Most Muslims hold that the earlier books were changed by human hands over time, so the Qur'an confirms and corrects them. A top-level answer uses this to explain Muslim attitudes to other faiths, not just to list titles.","code":"3.1.5.1 — The holy books"},{"h":"The imamate in Shi'a Islam","t":"The imamate is the specific difference the specification most wants you to be able to explain. Shi'a Muslims believe that leadership of the community did not pass by election but by God's appointment, beginning with Ali ibn Abi Talib, the Prophet's cousin and son-in-law, and continuing through his descendants. Twelver Shi'a Muslims name twelve Imams; they regard them as sinless and divinely guided, the authoritative interpreters of the Qur'an, and believe the twelfth is in occultation, hidden, and will return as the Mahdi to restore justice. Imamah is therefore one of the five roots of Usul ad-Din, a matter of faith rather than of politics. Sunni Muslims believe no one after Muhammad carried prophetic or infallible authority: leadership passed to caliphs chosen by the community, the first being Abu Bakr, and religious authority rests on the Qur'an, the Sunnah and the consensus of scholars. A top-level answer shows the consequence: it is the imamate that makes Ashura a day of mourning for Shi'a Muslims, that puts Ali's name in the Shi'a Shahadah, and that sends part of Khums to the Prophet's descendants.","code":"3.1.5.1 — The imamate in Shi'a Islam: its role and significance"},{"h":"The Five Pillars and the Ten Obligatory Acts","t":"Sunni Muslims organise duty around the Five Pillars: Shahadah, the declaration of faith; Salah, prayer; Zakah, alms; Sawm, fasting in Ramadan; and Hajj, pilgrimage to Makkah. A Hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari says that 'Islam is built upon five', which is the source to cite. Shi'a Muslims perform the same acts but organise them differently, as the Ten Obligatory Acts: Salah, Sawm, Zakah, Khums, Hajj, Jihad, Amr-bil-Maruf (encouraging what is good), Nahi Anil Munkar (forbidding what is wrong), Tawalla (loving the Prophet's family and those who follow God) and Tabarra (dissociating from God's enemies). Shahadah is not in the Shi'a list because it belongs to belief, among the roots of Usul ad-Din, rather than to duty. Notice what the list adds: Khums, jihad and two social duties that turn faith outward into the community. The specification requires you to study the Five Pillars and jihad in both traditions and the additional Shi'a duties. A top-level answer contrasts the shape of the two lists and then explains what the extra Shi'a duties commit a believer to.","code":"3.1.5.2 — Five Pillars of Sunni Islam and the Ten Obligatory Acts of Shi'a Islam"},{"h":"Shahadah and where it appears in a life","t":"The Shahadah is the declaration that there is no god but God and that Muhammad is the messenger of God; Shi'a Muslims add a third clause, that Ali is the friend of God. It is the first Pillar in Sunni Islam and the gateway to the others, because everything else is done for the one God it names. The specification asks for its place in Muslim practice, so learn the occasions rather than only the wording: it is recited sincerely before witnesses by a person converting to Islam; it is called out in the adhan from a mosque and repeated in the iqamah; it is said within salah at the sitting; it is traditionally whispered into a newborn baby's ear so that it is the first thing heard; and Muslims hope it will be the last words they speak before death. A top-level answer shows the Shahadah framing a whole life from first breath to last, and then draws the divergence: the Shi'a addition of Ali is not a different God but the imamate showing itself in the words of the creed.","code":"3.1.5.2 — Shahadah: declaration of faith and its place in Muslim practice"},{"h":"Salah: times, wudu, rak'ahs and Jummah","t":"Salah is set prayer, and this is the longest bullet in the room. Most Sunni Muslims pray five times a day - Fajr at dawn, Zuhr at midday, Asr in the afternoon, Maghrib at sunset and Isha at night - while many Shi'a Muslims combine them into three sittings, praying Zuhr with Asr and Maghrib with Isha, holding that the Qur'an names three prayer periods. Prayer is offered facing the qiblah, the direction of the Ka'aba at Makkah, after wudu, the washing of hands, mouth, nose, face and arms, with the head wiped and the feet washed. The prayer is built of rak'ahs, units of standing, bowing in ruku, prostrating in sujud and sitting, with Surah al-Fatihah recited in every one. Salah may be prayed at home, at work or in a mosque; on Friday the midday Jummah prayer gathers the congregation for a khutbah, a sermon. Other differences: many Shi'a Muslims pray with arms at their sides and place the forehead on a clay tablet, while Sunni Muslims usually fold their arms. Muslims also differ on importance: for most it is fard, obligatory, while some stress inward intention over exact form. A top-level answer names the tradition before describing the movement.","code":"3.1.5.2 — Salah and its significance"},{"h":"Sawm, Ramadan and the Night of Power","t":"Sawm is the fast of Ramadan, the ninth month of the Muslim lunar calendar, and its origins are in the Qur'an, which says that fasting is 'prescribed for you' as it was for those before (2:183). From dawn to sunset adult Muslims take no food, drink, tobacco or sexual relations; they eat suhur before Fajr and break the fast at sunset with iftar, traditionally with dates and water. The benefits the specification asks for are spiritual and social: taqwa, God-consciousness; self-discipline; empathy with the hungry that makes zakah real; and a month of shared meals and extra prayer that binds the ummah. The exceptions matter as much as the duty, and each has a reason: young children, the very old, the ill, those travelling, and women who are pregnant, breastfeeding or menstruating are excused, because the fast is meant to build devotion, not to damage health; many make up the days later or pay fidyah to feed someone in need. Laylat al-Qadr, the Night of Power, falls in the last ten nights and marks the first revelation - 'Read in the name of your Lord' (Qur'an 96:1) - a night the Qur'an calls 'better than a thousand months' (97:3). A top-level answer explains the exceptions as evidence of what Sawm is for.","code":"3.1.5.2 — Sawm: the role and significance of fasting during the month of Ramadan"},{"h":"Zakah, its recipients, and Khums","t":"Zakah is the annual giving of 2.5 per cent of a Muslim's surplus wealth once it passes the nisab, the minimum threshold, and it is a Pillar rather than a charity: the Qur'an repeatedly pairs it with prayer, telling believers to 'establish prayer and give zakah' (2:110). It purifies the wealth that remains and cuts the hold money has on the giver. The Qur'an names who may receive it (9:60), including the poor, the needy, those in debt, those collecting it and the stranded traveller. The specification asks for the benefits of receipt, so think from the recipient's side: help arrives as a right rather than as begging, which protects dignity; it clears debt and buys tools, schooling or stock, so a family can become self-sufficient; and it keeps the poorest inside the community rather than outside it. Shi'a Muslims pay an additional duty, Khums, a fifth of surplus annual income, divided between the descendants of the Prophet and religious leaders for religious and charitable purposes - one of the additional Shi'a duties the specification requires. A top-level answer gives the figure, the source and the effect on the person who receives it.","code":"3.1.5.2 — Zakah: the role and significance of giving alms"},{"h":"Hajj: the Ka'aba, Mina, Arafat and Muzdalifah","t":"Hajj is the pilgrimage to Makkah in the month of Dhul-Hijjah, required once in a lifetime of every Muslim who is physically and financially able. Its origins lie with Ibrahim, Hajar and Isma'il, and the Qur'an records the call to humanity to come on pilgrimage (22:27). Pilgrims enter ihram, a state of purity marked for men by two plain white unsewn cloths and for women by modest dress, which strips away wealth and status. At Makkah they perform tawaf, seven circuits of the Ka'aba, moving around the house of God as the centre of life, and sa'y, hurrying seven times between Safa and Marwah as Hajar searched for water. At Arafat they stand from midday to sunset on the ninth day, praying for forgiveness on the plain where Muhammad preached his final sermon; this standing is the heart of Hajj, and a pilgrimage without it is not complete. At Muzdalifah they spend the night under the sky and gather pebbles; at Mina they stone the three pillars, rejecting the temptations of the devil as Ibrahim did, and the sacrifice of Id-ul-Adha is made. A top-level answer attaches meaning to each site rather than reciting an itinerary.","code":"3.1.5.2 — Hajj: the role and significance of the pilgrimage to Makkah"},{"h":"Greater and lesser jihad","t":"Jihad means to strive or struggle in the way of God, and the specification puts different understandings first. Greater jihad, jihad al-akbar, is the inner struggle every Muslim fights daily: keeping the Pillars when they are inconvenient, resisting temptation, controlling anger and greed, studying the Qur'an. Most Muslims regard it as the harder and more important struggle. Lesser jihad, jihad al-asghar, is the outward, military struggle, permitted only in defence: the Qur'an gave permission to fight to those who had been wronged (22:39). The conditions for declaring it are strict and are exactly what the examiner wants: it must be declared by a fair religious authority, it must be defensive or to right a serious wrong, it must be a last resort after peaceful means have failed, it must not be fought to gain territory or to force conversion, since 'There is no compulsion in religion' (2:256), civilians, trees, crops and animals must not be harmed, and fighting must stop when the enemy seeks peace. Many Shi'a Muslims add that offensive jihad may be declared only by the rightful Imam, so in his absence only defence is allowed. A top-level answer uses the conditions to show why terrorism is not jihad.","code":"3.1.5.2 — Jihad: different understandings of jihad"},{"h":"Id-ul-Adha, Id-ul-Fitr and Ashura in Britain today","t":"Id-ul-Fitr, the festival of breaking the fast, falls on the first of Shawwal at the end of Ramadan. Muslims gather for a special Id prayer in a mosque or hired hall, wear new clothes, exchange cards and gifts, visit family and graves, and give Zakat-ul-Fitr before the prayer so that the poorest can share the day. Id-ul-Adha, the festival of sacrifice, falls on the tenth of Dhul-Hijjah at the end of Hajj and commemorates Ibrahim's willingness to sacrifice his son and God's provision of a ram instead; an animal is sacrificed and the meat shared in three, for family, for friends and for the poor. In Britain the sacrifice must be made in a licensed abattoir, so many families pay a halal butcher or a charity to do it abroad. Ashura, the tenth of Muharram, divides the traditions: Shi'a Muslims mourn the martyrdom of Husayn, grandson of the Prophet, killed at Karbala in 680 CE, with black clothing, mourning gatherings and processions, while Sunni Muslims keep it as a voluntary fast recalling God's rescue of Musa from Pharaoh. A top-level answer explains why a festival matters to a minority community in Britain, where the day is not a public holiday.","code":"3.1.5.2 — Festivals and commemorations and their importance for Muslims in Great Britain today"}],"examples":[{"title":"The 4-mark contrast: two ways of performing salah","setup":"Exam question: Explain two contrasting ways in which Muslims perform salah. [4 marks] The examiner gives a simple explanation of an accurate contrast 1 mark and a detailed explanation 2 marks, for each of the two, and warns that if similar practices are given only one of them may be credited. You know that Sunni and Shi'a Muslims pray at different times. Write the answer.","steps":["Take the first practice and attach a tradition to it: most Sunni Muslims pray five separate times each day, at Fajr, Zuhr, Asr, Maghrib and Isha.","Turn that into the detailed explanation by adding why they do it: these timings follow the Sunnah of Muhammad, so praying them separately is following the Prophet's own practice.","Set the second practice directly against the first: many Shi'a Muslims combine the prayers into three sittings, praying Zuhr with Asr and Maghrib with Isha.","Develop the second with its own reason: Shi'a Muslims hold that the Qur'an names three prayer periods, so combining is permitted and prayer stays possible inside a working day.","Test the pair before moving on: the two paragraphs must differ in what is done and in the reason given, or the examiner credits only one of them."],"cues":["Whose practice will you name first, and how often do they pray?","What turns a bare statement of practice into a detailed explanation?","Which tradition prays at a different number of sittings, and which prayers are joined?","What reason do those Muslims give for joining them?","How can you tell your two paragraphs are a contrast and not the same point twice?"]},{"title":"The 5-mark answer: earning the source mark on Sawm","setup":"Exam question: Explain two ways in which Muslims observe Sawm during the month of Ramadan. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Muslim belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks] Four marks come from two ways, each worth 1 for a simple explanation and 2 for a detailed one; the fifth mark is given once, for a relevant and accurate reference to a source. You may use any of the Qur'an, a Hadith or the teaching of the Imams. Write the answer.","steps":["State the first way plainly: adult Muslims take no food, drink or tobacco from dawn until sunset, eating suhur before Fajr and breaking the fast at iftar.","Develop it with what the fast achieves, since the second mark is for detail: going hungry by choice builds taqwa and lets a Muslim feel what the poor feel all year.","State a genuinely different second way: through the last ten nights many Muslims stay late at the mosque in extra prayer, watching for Laylat al-Qadr.","Develop it with the meaning of that night: it marks the first revelation of the Qur'an, so Muslims read and recite through it hoping their prayers are answered.","Finish by naming the source out loud so the examiner cannot miss it, quoting the opening of the first revelation with its reference.","Read back and check you gave two ways, not three, because only the first two are marked."],"cues":["What exactly does a person abstain from, and between which two points of the day?","Why would an examiner call your first sentence simple rather than detailed?","Which part of Ramadan gives you a second way that is not just fasting again?","What happened on that night that makes Muslims spend it awake?","Which words and which reference will you write to claim the fifth mark?","Why is a third way a waste of your time here?"]},{"title":"The 4-mark influence: the Day of Judgement","setup":"Exam question: Explain two ways in which belief in the Day of Judgement influences Muslims today. [4 marks] The target is influence on individuals, communities and societies, and the mark scheme says the second mark is only given if the influence itself is included. You know that Muslims believe the dead are raised, deeds are weighed, and Jannah or Jahannam follows. Write the answer.","steps":["Name a belief and then immediately say what it makes a Muslim do: believing every deed is recorded and weighed, a Muslim keeps salah even when nobody would notice if it were missed.","Deepen the same point into the detailed mark by naming the effect: the fear of answering for a wasted day shapes how time, money and speech are used all week.","Choose a second influence that reaches outside the individual: because the judgement asks how the poor were treated, Muslims give zakah and support food banks and appeals through the mosque.","Add the community effect so the second pair of marks is safe: the mosque organises collections in Ramadan, so a private belief becomes an organised charitable effort.","Check both paragraphs end in something a person or community actually does, not in a restatement of the belief."],"cues":["What is the difference between stating the belief and stating its influence?","How would you show the belief changing an ordinary weekday?","Which second influence involves other people rather than the believer alone?","What does a mosque do that shows this belief working at community level?","Which word in each of your paragraphs proves an influence is there?"]},{"title":"The 4-mark contrast: two ways of listing the faith","setup":"Exam question: Explain two contrasting ways in which Sunni and Shi'a Muslims set out the basic beliefs of Islam. [4 marks] You know one tradition uses six articles of faith and the other uses five roots of Usul ad-Din, and that the mark scheme rewards a detailed explanation of each contrast. Write the answer.","steps":["Open with the Sunni list and one thing that is distinctive about it: the six articles include al-Qadr, God's decree, as a matter of faith in its own right.","Develop it by naming the source and the effect: the list comes from the Hadith of Jibril in Sahih Muslim, and putting God's decree among the articles shapes how Sunni Muslims speak about everything being God's will.","Set the Shi'a list against it: the five roots of Usul ad-Din replace that emphasis with Adalat, divine justice, and add Imamah, the imamate.","Develop the second contrast with its consequence: because leadership by the appointed Imams is an article of faith, not politics, Shi'a Muslims give Ali a place in the Shahadah and mourn Husayn at Ashura.","Guard the marks by adding a sentence of honesty: Shi'a Muslims do believe in angels and the holy books, so the contrast is about what each tradition puts at the foundation."],"cues":["Which belief does the Sunni list name that the Shi'a list does not?","Which source records the Sunni list, and what does that emphasis change?","What two things does the Shi'a list add or raise to first principles?","What follows for Shi'a worship from making the imamate an article of faith?","Why would it be wrong to say Shi'a Muslims do not believe in angels?"]},{"title":"The 12-mark evaluation: planning before you write","setup":"Exam question: 'Hajj is the most important of the Five Pillars for Muslims today.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should refer to Muslim teaching, give reasoned arguments to support this statement, give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view and reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] The levels reward reasoned consideration of different points of view, logical chains of reasoning and reference to religion applied to the issue. Plan the answer before writing it.","steps":["Decode the statement into the question actually being argued: is the once-in-a-lifetime pilgrimage more important to a Muslim's faith than the duties repeated every day and every year?","Build the supporting chain from teaching, not opinion: Hajj gathers the whole ummah in ihram with rank and wealth stripped away, and completes the pattern set by Ibrahim, so a pilgrim returns changed for life.","Build the opposing chain to the same standard: salah is required five times a day and Shahadah is the belief everything else rests on, while Hajj is required only of those able to afford and survive it.","Add the divergence the specification wants: Shi'a Muslims list ten obligatory acts in which Hajj sits beside Khums, jihad and social duties, so no single act is singled out as the greatest.","Decide the judgement in advance and make it follow from the strongest chain, rather than saving an opinion for the last line.","Sketch the order on the page: support, counter, divergence, conclusion, with a named source in each of the first two."],"cues":["What is the statement really asking you to compare?","Which features of the pilgrimage make the strongest case for it?","Which duties could be argued to matter more, and why?","What does the Shi'a list of duties add to the argument?","Why should the conclusion be decided before you begin writing?","In what order will the paragraphs go, and where does a source appear?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which two of the five roots of Usul ad-Din have no matching item in the Sunni six articles of faith?","a":"Adalat, divine justice, and Imamah, the imamate. Shi'a Muslims still believe in angels, the books and God's decree; those simply are not roots."},{"q":"A student writes: 'Muslims pray five times a day.' What has the examiner lost, and how would you fix it?","a":"The divergence. Most Sunni Muslims pray at five separate times; many Shi'a Muslims combine them into three sittings. Name the tradition before describing the practice."},{"q":"Where does the fifth mark on the 5-mark question come from, and name two things that can earn it in Islam.","a":"From one relevant and accurate reference to a source. Either the Qur'an, quoted or paraphrased with surah and ayah, or a Hadith from the Sunnah of Muhammad; for Shi'a content, the teaching of the Imams."},{"q":"Why does an answer saying 'Muslims believe deeds are weighed on the Day of Judgement' score only one mark on an influence question?","a":"It states the belief without its influence. The second mark needs what a Muslim actually does differently, such as keeping salah unobserved or giving zakah through the mosque."}]},"room":{"facts":["The six articles of faith in Sunni Islam: Tawhid, angels, the holy books, the prophets, the Day of Judgement, al-Qadr.","The five roots of Usul ad-Din in Shi'a Islam: Tawhid, Adalat, Nubuwwah, Imamah, Mi'ad.","The Five Pillars of Sunni Islam: Shahadah, Salah, Zakah, Sawm, Hajj.","The Ten Obligatory Acts of Shi'a Islam: Salah, Sawm, Zakah, Khums, Hajj, Jihad, Amr-bil-Maruf, Nahi Anil Munkar, Tawalla, Tabarra.","The two prescribed texts: Qur'an Surah 112 for Tawhid, and Qur'an 96:1-5 for the Night of Power.","The daily prayers: Fajr, Zuhr, Asr, Maghrib and Isha, five separate times for most Sunni Muslims and three sittings for many Shi'a Muslims.","The four Hajj sites the specification names: the Ka'aba at Makkah, Mina, Arafat and Muzdalifah.","The three named days: Id-ul-Adha on 10 Dhul-Hijjah, Id-ul-Fitr on 1 Shawwal, Ashura on 10 Muharram.","Zakah is 2.5 per cent of surplus wealth above the nisab; Khums, paid by Shi'a Muslims, is a fifth of surplus annual income.","In the five-part question the parts run 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks: the 5-mark answer must name a source of Muslim belief and teaching, and the 12-mark answer must refer to Muslim teaching, argue both sides and reach a justified conclusion, at about a minute a mark."],"questions":[{"q":"Which one of the following is the Muslim belief in the Oneness of God? Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. A Risalah  B Akhirah  C Tawhid  D Adalat [1 mark]","a":"Answer: C Tawhid. AO1:1, one mark for the correct tick.","sol":"C Tawhid. Tawhid is the belief that God is one, with no partner and nothing like him, and it is the first article of faith in Sunni Islam and the first root of Usul ad-Din in Shi'a Islam. Risalah is prophethood, Akhirah is life after death and Adalat is the divine justice that Shi'a Muslims name as a root, so none of those three is about God's oneness.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Three of these four words name beliefs about prophets, the afterlife or God's fairness; one names a belief about God himself.","Cross out every option that describes something other than what God is.","The word you want is built on an Arabic root that means one."],"codes":["3.1.5.1 — Tawhid (the Oneness of God), Qur'an Surah 112"]},{"q":"Which one of the following is the angel through whom the Qur'an was revealed to Muhammad? Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. A Adam  B Jibril  C Ibrahim  D Mika'il [1 mark]","a":"Answer: B Jibril. AO1:1, one mark for the correct tick.","sol":"B Jibril. Jibril is the angel of revelation, who brought God's word to the prophets and dictated the Qur'an to Muhammad, beginning in the cave of Hira. Mika'il is an angel too, but the angel of mercy and sustenance, associated with rain and nourishment. Adam and Ibrahim are prophets, not angels.","hints":["Two of the four options are not angels at all - they are prophets.","Of the two angels left, one is linked with rain, food and God's provision.","You want the angel whose work is carrying God's message to a prophet."],"m":1,"d":1,"codes":["3.1.5.1 — Angels, their nature and role"]},{"q":"Which one of the following is not one of the Five Pillars of Sunni Islam? Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. A Shahadah  B Salah  C Khums  D Hajj [1 mark]","a":"Answer: C Khums. AO1:1, one mark for the correct tick.","sol":"C Khums. The Five Pillars of Sunni Islam are Shahadah, Salah, Zakah, Sawm and Hajj. Khums is an additional duty of Shi'a Islam, a payment of one fifth of surplus annual income, and it appears in the Ten Obligatory Acts rather than in the Five Pillars.","hints":["Three of these are duties every Sunni Muslim shares; one belongs to a different list.","Ask which tradition each option comes from before you tick anything.","One of these four is a payment made only by Shi'a Muslims."],"m":1,"d":1,"codes":["3.1.5.2 — Five Pillars of Sunni Islam and the Ten Obligatory Acts of Shi'a Islam","3.1.5.2 — Zakah: the role and significance of giving alms"]},{"q":"Give two occasions when the Shahadah is said. [2 marks]","a":"One mark each for any two of: at conversion to Islam before witnesses; in the adhan or iqamah; during salah; whispered into a newborn baby's ear; hoped for as the last words before death. AO1:1.","sol":"1. It is recited by a person converting to Islam, sincerely and before witnesses. 2. It is whispered into a newborn baby's ear so that it is the first thing the child hears. Either of these earns a mark; so would naming the adhan, salah itself, or the words a Muslim hopes to say at death. No explanation is needed, and only the first two answers are marked.","hints":["Think about the first moment of a Muslim life and the last.","List the moments when the words are actually spoken aloud rather than what they mean.","Two separate occasions, one line each, with nothing added."],"m":2,"d":1,"codes":["3.1.5.2 — Shahadah: declaration of faith and its place in Muslim practice"]},{"q":"Give two holy books, other than the Qur'an, which Muslims believe God revealed. [2 marks]","a":"One mark each for any two of: the Tawrat or Torah; the Zabur or Psalms; the Injil or Gospel; the Scrolls of Abraham. AO1:1.","sol":"1. The Tawrat, the Torah, revealed to Musa. 2. The Injil, the Gospel, revealed to Isa. The Zabur, the Psalms given to Dawud, and the Scrolls of Abraham given to Ibrahim would score equally. Muslims believe all of these came from the same God as the Qur'an, which is why Jews and Christians are called People of the Book.","hints":["The specification names four scriptures besides the Qur'an.","Work through the prophets Muslims share with Jews and Christians and ask what each was given.","Two titles are enough; you may give the Arabic or the English name."],"m":2,"d":1,"codes":["3.1.5.1 — The holy books"]},{"q":"Give two reasons why the prophet Ibrahim is important to Muslims. [2 marks]","a":"One mark each for any two of: he is a model of submission to God; he was willing to sacrifice his son at God's command; he rejected idols and preached one God; he raised the Ka'aba at Makkah with Isma'il; his family's actions are re-enacted on Hajj; Id-ul-Adha commemorates him. AO1:1.","sol":"1. He showed complete submission to God by being willing to sacrifice his son when God commanded it, which is why Id-ul-Adha commemorates him. 2. With his son Isma'il he raised the Ka'aba at Makkah, the building Muslims face in every prayer and circle on Hajj. Rejecting the idols of his people, or being one of the prophets given scripture in the Scrolls of Abraham, would also score.","hints":["One reason lies in a festival, another lies in a building.","Ask what a pilgrim on Hajj is copying when they hurry between Safa and Marwah or stone the pillars.","Two short reasons, not one reason explained twice."],"m":2,"d":1,"codes":["3.1.5.1 — Risalah (Prophethood)"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting ways in which Sunni and Shi'a Muslims set out the basic beliefs of Islam. [4 marks]","a":"Two contrasts, each 1 mark simple and 2 marks detailed: Sunni six articles of faith (including al-Qadr, angels and the books as articles) against Shi'a five roots of Usul ad-Din (Tawhid, Adalat, Nubuwwah, Imamah, Mi'ad); the Shi'a raising of Adalat and Imamah to first principles. AO1:3.","sol":"Sunni Muslims set out the faith as six articles: Tawhid, angels, the holy books, the prophets, the Day of Judgement and al-Qadr, God's decree. The list comes from the Hadith of Jibril recorded in Sahih Muslim, and because al-Qadr is an article in its own right, Sunni Muslims habitually speak of everything that happens as being within God's will. Shi'a Muslims, in contrast, set the faith out as five roots of Usul ad-Din, in which the place given to God's decree is taken by Adalat, divine justice, and Imamah, the imamate, is added. Making the leadership of the divinely appointed Imams a matter of faith rather than politics is why Shi'a Muslims add Ali to the Shahadah and mourn Husayn at Ashura. Shi'a Muslims still believe in angels and the holy books; the contrast is about what each tradition places at the foundation.","hints":["One tradition counts six of something, the other counts five of something with a different name.","For each list, pick the item the other list does not name, then say what difference that item makes.","Two paragraphs that begin by naming the tradition, each with a reason or consequence attached."],"m":4,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.5.1 — The six articles of faith in Sunni Islam and five roots of Usul ad-Din in Shi'a Islam","3.1.5.1 — The imamate in Shi'a Islam: its role and significance"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting ways in which Muslims perform salah. [4 marks]","a":"Two genuine contrasts, each 1 mark simple and 2 marks detailed: five separate prayer times in Sunni practice against three combined sittings in much Shi'a practice; arms folded against arms at the sides and the use of a clay tablet; mosque against home; congregational Jummah against private prayer. AO1:3.","sol":"Most Sunni Muslims pray at five separate times each day: Fajr at dawn, Zuhr at midday, Asr in the afternoon, Maghrib at sunset and Isha at night. They keep the times apart because the timings follow the Sunnah of Muhammad, so praying each one separately is following the Prophet's own practice. Many Shi'a Muslims, in contrast, combine the prayers into three sittings, praying Zuhr with Asr and Maghrib with Isha. They hold that the Qur'an names three prayer periods, so joining them is permitted, and it makes prayer easier to keep through a working or school day in Britain. The contrast is genuine because the two traditions differ both in what is done and in the reason given for doing it.","hints":["Start from how often each tradition stops to pray in a day.","For each practice, add the reason the tradition gives, because a bare description earns only the first mark.","Two paragraphs, the second set openly against the first with a word like whereas or in contrast."],"m":4,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.5.2 — Salah and its significance"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which belief in the Day of Judgement influences Muslims today. [4 marks]","a":"Two influences, each 1 mark simple and 2 marks detailed, with the influence itself stated for the second mark: keeping salah and daily duties because deeds are recorded; giving zakah and charity because the judgement asks how the poor were treated; forgiving others; avoiding what is haram. AO1:2.","sol":"Because Muslims believe every deed is recorded and will be weighed, they keep salah even when nobody would notice it being missed. The belief reaches into an ordinary weekday: a Muslim watches what is said about others, avoids haram earnings and uses time carefully, because all of it will be answered for. Secondly, the belief shapes how Muslims treat the poor, since the judgement asks what was done for those in need. Muslims therefore give zakah and support appeals, and mosques organise collections and food-bank drives during Ramadan, so a private belief about the afterlife becomes an organised effort in the community.","hints":["Influence means what a believer actually does differently, not what they believe.","Choose one influence on the individual and one that reaches the wider community.","Finish each paragraph with an action rather than with a restatement of the belief."],"m":4,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.5.1 — Akhirah (life after death), human responsibility and accountability","3.1.5.1 — Predestination and human freedom and its relationship to the Day of Judgement"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting understandings of jihad in Islam. [4 marks]","a":"Two contrasts, each 1 mark simple and 2 marks detailed: greater jihad as the inner struggle against one's own faults against lesser jihad as the outward, strictly conditioned military struggle; the Shi'a view that only the rightful Imam may declare offensive jihad. AO1:3.","sol":"Greater jihad is the inward struggle every Muslim fights daily: keeping the Pillars when they are inconvenient, controlling anger and greed, studying the Qur'an and resisting temptation. Most Muslims regard this as the harder and more important struggle, because it never ends and no one else can fight it for them. Lesser jihad, in contrast, is the outward military struggle, and it is hedged about with conditions: it must be declared by a fair religious authority, must be defensive or to right a serious wrong, must be a last resort, must not be fought to gain land or force conversion, and must spare civilians, crops and animals. Many Shi'a Muslims narrow it further, holding that offensive jihad may be declared only by the rightful Imam, so in his absence only defence is permitted.","hints":["One understanding happens inside a person, the other between communities.","Develop the second by naming what has to be true before it is allowed at all.","Two paragraphs, each naming the kind of jihad and then what it demands of a Muslim."],"m":4,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.5.2 — Jihad: different understandings of jihad"]},{"q":"Explain two Muslim teachings about the nature of God. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Muslim belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"Two teachings, each 1 mark simple and 2 marks detailed - from omnipotence, beneficence, mercy, justice or Adalat, transcendence and immanence - plus 1 mark for a relevant and accurate source. AO1:1.","sol":"Muslims teach that God is one and utterly beyond comparison. Surah 112:1 declares 'He is God, the One' and the surah denies that God begets or is begotten or has any equal, which is why no image of God is made and why shirk, associating anything with God, is the gravest wrong. Secondly, Muslims teach that God is merciful. Almost every surah opens by calling him 'the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful' (Qur'an 1:1), so mercy is the first thing a Muslim reads, and it gives a believer confidence that repentance will be accepted. Muslims also teach God's immanence, his closeness to his creation: the Qur'an says God is 'nearer to him than his jugular vein' (50:16). Muslims hold that immanence alongside God's transcendence, so the God who is beyond the universe is also closer than a person's own body.","hints":["The specification names five attributes; two of them are enough.","Develop each teaching by saying what it rules out or what it lets a believer expect.","Before you stop, make sure one surah and ayah is written on the page."],"m":5,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.5.1 — The nature of God: omnipotence, beneficence, mercy, fairness and justice/Adalat","3.1.5.1 — Tawhid (the Oneness of God), Qur'an Surah 112","3.1.5 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Islam"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which Muslims observe Sawm during the month of Ramadan. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Muslim belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"Two ways, each 1 mark simple and 2 marks detailed - abstaining from dawn to sunset, suhur and iftar, extra prayer and Qur'an reading, the last ten nights and Laylat al-Qadr, extra charity - plus 1 mark for a source. AO1:1.","sol":"Adult Muslims take no food, drink or tobacco from dawn until sunset through the month, eating suhur before Fajr and breaking the fast at iftar, traditionally with dates and water. Going hungry by choice builds taqwa, God-consciousness, and lets a Muslim feel for a month what the poor feel all year, which makes zakah real rather than theoretical. Secondly, through the last ten nights many Muslims spend extra hours at the mosque in prayer and recitation, watching for Laylat al-Qadr, the Night of Power. That night marks the first revelation of the Qur'an - 'Read in the name of your Lord' (Qur'an 96:1) - and the Qur'an calls it 'better than a thousand months' (97:3), so Muslims believe prayer offered then carries extraordinary weight.","hints":["One way is what a Muslim gives up; the other is what a Muslim adds.","Develop each way by saying what it is meant to achieve in the believer.","The prescribed passage for this part of the room will earn you the source mark."],"m":5,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.5.2 — Sawm: the role and significance of fasting during the month of Ramadan","3.1.5 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Islam"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which giving zakah helps those who receive it. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Muslim belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"Two ways, each 1 mark simple and 2 marks detailed, judged from the recipient's side - relief of poverty as a right rather than charity, clearing debt, funding tools or education, dignity, inclusion in the ummah - plus 1 mark for a source. AO1:1.","sol":"Zakah reaches the poor as a right rather than as a favour, because it is a duty of 2.5 per cent on surplus wealth above the nisab, not an optional gift. A family receiving it can eat and pay rent without begging, so their dignity is protected and they remain full members of the community rather than dependants. Secondly, zakah can lift a household out of poverty for good: it clears debt, which the Qur'an names as one of the purposes of the fund (9:60), and it buys tools, stock or schooling, so the person who received it this year may be the person paying it in a few years' time. The Qur'an pairs the duty with prayer, telling believers to 'establish prayer and give zakah' (2:110), which shows how seriously the recipient's need is taken.","hints":["The question asks about the person receiving, not the person giving.","Think about what money does beyond the next meal - debt, work, school, standing in the community.","One surah and ayah, written plainly, secures the fifth mark."],"m":5,"d":2,"codes":["3.1.5.2 — Zakah: the role and significance of giving alms","3.1.5 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Islam"]},{"q":"'Predestination means that Muslims are not responsible for what they do.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Muslim teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2. In support: al-Qadr as an article of faith, God's omnipotence and omniscience, everything written, acceptance of God's will. Against: the Day of Judgement presupposes freedom, khalifah and accountability, no compulsion in religion, Shi'a Adalat, knowledge is not compulsion. Levels 1-4, best fit; maximum Level 2 without reference to religion.","sol":"Al-Qadr, God's decree, is one of the six articles of faith in Sunni Islam, so the statement begins from something Muslims really do believe. Many Sunni Muslims teach that nothing happens outside God's knowledge and will, and that what will happen is already written. If God is omnipotent and all-knowing, no human choice can surprise him or escape him, and the Qur'an describes him as 'nearer to him than his jugular vein' (50:16), involved in every moment of a life. On that reasoning responsibility does look thin, and it is true that Muslims often accept illness, bereavement or failure as God's will rather than blaming themselves, saying Insha'Allah before making any plan.\n\nIslam, however, ties al-Qadr to judgement rather than setting the two against each other. The same six articles that include God's decree also include the Day of Judgement, and a judgement of people who could not have chosen otherwise would be pointless and cruel. The Qur'an promises that 'an atom's weight of good' will be seen again (99:7), which assumes the good was genuinely the person's own, and it calls the human being a khalifah, a steward answerable for what has been entrusted (2:30). Most Muslims therefore argue that God's knowing what someone will choose is not the same as God's forcing it, just as a teacher who knows how a pupil will answer has not answered for them. The Qur'an also insists that 'There is no compulsion in religion' (2:256), which would make little sense if nobody could choose.\n\nShi'a Muslims press this further through Adalat, divine justice, which is a root of Usul ad-Din. Because God is perfectly just, he cannot punish anyone for an act he compelled, so human actions are neither wholly forced nor wholly independent of God. That divergence matters: the statement is not simply Muslim teaching but one reading of it, and the tradition that makes God's justice a first principle rejects it outright.\n\nThe way Muslims live points the same way. Nobody keeps the fast through a summer Ramadan, calculates zakah or saves for years for Hajj while believing their choices make no difference; the Pillars are duties, and a duty assumes someone free to fulfil or neglect it.\n\nIn conclusion the statement is half right and therefore wrong. Al-Qadr does mean Muslims believe nothing falls outside God's will, which is why they accept what they cannot change with patience. But responsibility is the point of the belief rather than its casualty. The arguments against the statement are stronger because they explain both articles of faith together, while the statement can only keep one of them and has to explain the Day of Judgement away.","hints":["Work out first which two Muslim beliefs the statement is forcing into conflict.","Build one developed chain of reasoning for the statement and one against, each resting on named Muslim teaching rather than opinion.","Plan where the divergence between Sunni and Shi'a thinking will sit, and decide your judgement before you write the first paragraph."],"m":12,"d":3,"codes":["3.1.5.1 — Predestination and human freedom and its relationship to the Day of Judgement","3.1.5.1 — Akhirah (life after death), human responsibility and accountability","3.1.5.1 — The nature of God: omnipotence, beneficence, mercy, fairness and justice/Adalat"]},{"q":"'Hajj is the most important of the Five Pillars for Muslims today.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Muslim teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks]","a":"AO2. In support: Hajj gathers the ummah, strips status in ihram, completes Ibrahim's pattern, standing at Arafat brings forgiveness, pilgrims return changed. Against: Shahadah underlies everything, salah is daily and obligatory for all, zakah and sawm act on others, Hajj is required only of those able. Divergence: Shi'a Ten Obligatory Acts. Levels 1-4, best fit.","sol":"There is a strong case for the statement. Hajj is the one duty that gathers the whole ummah in a single place: pilgrims from every country stand together in ihram, two plain white cloths for men and modest dress for women, so wealth, nationality and rank are stripped away and the equality Islam teaches becomes visible. It re-enacts the obedience of Ibrahim, Hajar and Isma'il, so a pilgrim is walking inside the story that made the Ka'aba the centre of Muslim life. Above all, standing at Arafat from midday to sunset on the ninth of Dhul-Hijjah, on the plain where Muhammad preached his final sermon, is the moment pilgrims pray for forgiveness of a whole life; without that standing the pilgrimage is not complete. Many return believing themselves forgiven and changed, take the title Hajji, and live differently afterwards, which is an influence no other Pillar claims in quite the same way.\n\nAgainst the statement, the other Pillars carry duties that reach every Muslim every day. Shahadah is the foundation: without declaring that there is no god but God and that Muhammad is his messenger, nothing else counts, because the other four are performed for the God it names. Salah is required five times a day for most Sunni Muslims, and in three sittings for many Shi'a Muslims, so it shapes every day of every year rather than a fortnight of one. Sawm trains self-discipline and empathy for a whole month, and zakah of 2.5 per cent of surplus wealth feeds people who would otherwise go without, the Qur'an pairing the two duties when it tells believers to 'establish prayer and give zakah' (2:110). Crucially, Hajj is required only of Muslims who are physically and financially able; a poor or ill Muslim who never goes is not a lesser Muslim, whereas one who abandons salah is failing a duty that applies to them.\n\nThere is also a divergence worth naming. Shi'a Muslims organise duty as Ten Obligatory Acts in which Hajj sits alongside Khums, jihad and the social duties of encouraging good and forbidding wrong, so the question of a single greatest Pillar does not arise in the same form.\n\nIn conclusion, Hajj is the most concentrated of the Pillars but not the most important. Its power comes from being rare, and that rarity is exactly why it cannot be the one a Muslim life rests on. The stronger argument is that Shahadah gives Hajj its meaning and salah gives a Muslim the daily discipline to reach it; Hajj is better understood as the summit of a practice built by the others than as its foundation.","hints":["The statement is a comparison, so work out what most important would have to mean before arguing.","Build one chain for the pilgrimage and one for the duties that apply every day, each grounded in named teaching.","Decide where the Shi'a list of duties fits, and make your last paragraph follow from the stronger chain rather than introduce a new opinion."],"m":12,"d":3,"codes":["3.1.5.2 — Hajj: the role and significance of the pilgrimage to Makkah","3.1.5.2 — Five Pillars of Sunni Islam and the Ten Obligatory Acts of Shi'a Islam","3.1.5.2 — Shahadah: declaration of faith and its place in Muslim practice"]},{"q":"'Id-ul-Fitr is the most important day in the year for Muslims in Great Britain today.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Muslim teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks]","a":"AO2. In support: crowns Ramadan, largest gathering, Zakat-ul-Fitr, family and community visibility in a non-Muslim society. Against: Id-ul-Adha ends Hajj and commemorates Ibrahim with qurbani; Ashura is central for Shi'a Muslims; Laylat al-Qadr and Friday Jummah may matter more spiritually. Levels 1-4, best fit.","sol":"Id-ul-Fitr has a good claim. Falling on the first of Shawwal, it crowns a month in which Muslims have fasted from dawn to sunset, so the joy of the day is earned rather than simply scheduled. In Britain it fills mosques and hired halls for the Id prayer, and Zakat-ul-Fitr is given before that prayer so that the poorest families can share the celebration rather than watch it. For a minority community it is also the most visible day of the year: families take leave from work or school, wear new clothes, exchange cards and gifts, visit relatives and graves, and often invite neighbours, which makes Islam intelligible to people who know little about it. In a society where the day is not a public holiday, the effort it takes to keep it is itself a statement of faith.\n\nThere are strong arguments for other days. Id-ul-Adha, on the tenth of Dhul-Hijjah, closes the Hajj and commemorates Ibrahim's willingness to sacrifice his son, the moment of submission that stands behind the Ka'aba and the pilgrimage; the qurbani meat shared in three, for family, friends and the poor, makes charity concrete, and in Britain the law requires slaughter in a licensed abattoir, so many pay a butcher or a charity to arrange it. Many Muslims would argue this festival touches the deeper teaching, because it is about obedience to God rather than relief at the end of a fast.\n\nThe divergence within Islam matters most here. For Shi'a Muslims, Ashura on the tenth of Muharram commemorates the martyrdom of Husayn, the Prophet's grandson, killed at Karbala in 680 CE, and it is marked with mourning gatherings, black clothing and processions through British cities. To say Id-ul-Fitr is the most important day in the year is to describe Sunni experience and to overlook a community for whom the defining day is one of grief, not celebration. Sunni Muslims keep Ashura too, but as a voluntary fast recalling God's rescue of Musa from Pharaoh, which shows how far the same date can mean different things. Some Muslims would go further and say Laylat al-Qadr, when the Qur'an was first revealed, or the weekly Jummah prayer, matters more spiritually than any festival.\n\nIn conclusion the statement is defensible only if important means most widely and publicly celebrated. Judged by teaching rather than by scale, Id-ul-Adha carries the weightier message and Ashura is decisive for Shi'a Muslims, so the statement mistakes one tradition's calendar for the whole of Islam. Id-ul-Fitr is the most visible Muslim day in Britain; it is not, for every Muslim, the most important.","hints":["Ask what important is being measured by - numbers celebrating, or the teaching behind the day.","Build the case for the festival, then the case for the other two named days, each with its origin and meaning.","The strongest marks here come from naming which tradition holds which view before you judge between them."],"m":12,"d":3,"codes":["3.1.5.2 — Festivals and commemorations and their importance for Muslims in Great Britain today","3.1.5.2 — Sawm: the role and significance of fasting during the month of Ramadan","3.1.5.1 — The imamate in Shi'a Islam: its role and significance"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which surah of the Qur'an is prescribed for study on Tawhid?","a":"Surah 112, al-Ikhlas; 112:1 declares 'He is God, the One'."},{"q":"Which two of the five roots of Usul ad-Din have no matching item in the six articles of faith?","a":"Adalat, divine justice, and Imamah, the imamate."},{"q":"Which Shi'a duty is the payment of one fifth of surplus annual income?","a":"Khums."},{"q":"At which Hajj site do pilgrims stand from midday to sunset praying for forgiveness?","a":"Arafat, on the ninth of Dhul-Hijjah."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Tawhid","back":"The oneness of God: one God with no partner, no offspring and nothing like him. Surah 112:1 declares 'He is God, the One'. Associating anything with God is shirk, the gravest wrong.","code":"3.1.5.1 — Tawhid (the Oneness of God), Qur'an Surah 112","open":false},{"front":"The nature of God in Islam","back":"Omnipotent, beneficent, merciful ('the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful', Qur'an 1:1), fair and just, which Shi'a Muslims call Adalat. Transcendent, beyond creation, yet immanent: 'nearer to him than his jugular vein' (50:16).","code":"3.1.5.1 — The nature of God: omnipotence, beneficence, mercy, fairness and justice/Adalat","open":false},{"front":"Six articles of faith and five roots of Usul ad-Din","back":"Sunni: Tawhid, angels, holy books, prophets, Day of Judgement, al-Qadr. Shi'a: Tawhid, Adalat, Nubuwwah, Imamah, Mi'ad. Shared core; the Shi'a roots raise divine justice and the imamate to first principles.","code":"3.1.5.1 — The six articles of faith in Sunni Islam and five roots of Usul ad-Din in Shi'a Islam","open":false},{"front":"Jibril and Mika'il","back":"Jibril brought God's revelation to the prophets, dictating the Qur'an to Muhammad at Hira. Mika'il is the angel of mercy and sustenance, bringing rain and rewards. Angels are made of light and cannot disobey.","code":"3.1.5.1 — Angels, their nature and role","open":false},{"front":"Al-Qadr and human freedom","back":"God's decree: nothing falls outside God's knowledge and will. Muslims still hold people free and accountable, since judgement needs real choice; 'There is no compulsion in religion' (2:256). Shi'a Adalat makes this explicit.","code":"3.1.5.1 — Predestination and human freedom and its relationship to the Day of Judgement","open":false},{"front":"Akhirah","back":"Everlasting life after death. The soul waits in barzakh; at the resurrection bodies are raised, deeds weighed on Yawm ad-Din, and people enter Jannah, the Garden, or Jahannam, the Fire.","code":"3.1.5.1 — Akhirah (life after death), human responsibility and accountability","open":false},{"front":"Risalah: Adam, Ibrahim and Muhammad","back":"Prophethood. Adam: first human, first prophet, khalifah. Ibrahim: model of submission, raised the Ka'aba with Isma'il. Muhammad: the Seal of the Prophets (Qur'an 33:40), through whom the Qur'an was revealed.","code":"3.1.5.1 — Risalah (Prophethood)","open":false},{"front":"The holy books","back":"Qur'an to Muhammad; Tawrat to Musa; Zabur to Dawud; Injil to Isa; Scrolls of Abraham to Ibrahim. Muslims hold the earlier books were altered, so the Qur'an is final and highest in authority.","code":"3.1.5.1 — The holy books","open":false},{"front":"The imamate in Shi'a Islam","back":"Leadership appointed by God, beginning with Ali and continuing through his descendants. Twelver Shi'a name twelve sinless Imams who interpret the Qur'an; the twelfth is hidden and will return as the Mahdi.","code":"3.1.5.1 — The imamate in Shi'a Islam: its role and significance","open":false},{"front":"The Five Pillars and the Ten Obligatory Acts","back":"Sunni Pillars: Shahadah, Salah, Zakah, Sawm, Hajj - 'Islam is built upon five' (Sahih al-Bukhari). Shi'a acts: Salah, Sawm, Zakah, Khums, Hajj, Jihad, Amr-bil-Maruf, Nahi Anil Munkar, Tawalla, Tabarra.","code":"3.1.5.2 — Five Pillars of Sunni Islam and the Ten Obligatory Acts of Shi'a Islam","open":false},{"front":"Shahadah","back":"There is no god but God, and Muhammad is the messenger of God; Shi'a Muslims add that Ali is the friend of God. Said at conversion, in the adhan, within salah and to a newborn.","code":"3.1.5.2 — Shahadah: declaration of faith and its place in Muslim practice","open":false},{"front":"Salah: times, wudu and rak'ah","back":"Fajr, Zuhr, Asr, Maghrib, Isha - five separate times for most Sunni Muslims, three combined sittings for many Shi'a. Wudu washes first; each rak'ah is standing, ruku, sujud and sitting, facing the Ka'aba.","code":"3.1.5.2 — Salah and its significance","open":false},{"front":"Sawm, Ramadan and Laylat al-Qadr","back":"No food, drink or tobacco dawn to sunset; suhur before Fajr, iftar at sunset. The ill, elderly, travelling, young and pregnant are excused. Laylat al-Qadr marks the first revelation, 'better than a thousand months' (97:3).","code":"3.1.5.2 — Sawm: the role and significance of fasting during the month of Ramadan","open":false},{"front":"One action performed on Hajj and its meaning","back":"Tawaf: seven circuits of the Ka'aba at Makkah, putting God at the centre of life; standing at Arafat from midday to sunset, praying for forgiveness where Muhammad gave his final sermon; stoning the pillars at Mina, rejecting temptation as Ibrahim did; the night at Muzdalifah under the open sky, gathering pebbles.","code":"3.1.5.2 — Hajj: the role and significance of the pilgrimage to Makkah","open":true},{"front":"Greater and lesser jihad","back":"Greater jihad: the daily inner struggle to obey God and resist temptation. Lesser jihad: defensive armed struggle, declared by a fair authority as a last resort, never to gain land or force belief, sparing civilians.","code":"3.1.5.2 — Jihad: different understandings of jihad","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 4 (Evaluate: 'Predestination means that Muslims are not responsible for what they do.')","items":["Argument: the statement cannot stand, because the belief it rests on is listed alongside the very doctrine it is said to destroy.","Named teaching: al-Qadr and the Day of Judgement are both among the six articles of faith in Sunni Islam, and the Qur'an promises that 'an atom's weight of good' will be seen again (99:7).","Development: a judgement of people who could not have chosen otherwise would be arbitrary, so Muslims distinguish God's knowing a choice from God's forcing it - the Qur'an calls the human being a khalifah, a steward answerable for what is entrusted (2:30), and insists 'There is no compulsion in religion' (2:256).","Counter-view handled: those who support the statement point to God's omnipotence and to the Muslim habit of accepting illness or loss as God's will; but accepting what cannot be changed is not the same as denying responsibility for what can.","Divergence named: Shi'a Muslims argue from Adalat, a root of Usul ad-Din, that a perfectly just God could not punish an act he compelled, so the statement is rejected outright by a whole tradition.","Mini-judgement: the arguments against are stronger because they hold both articles of faith together, while the statement can only keep one and must explain the Day of Judgement away.","Why this is Level 4: it is a well-argued response with reasoned consideration of different points of view, logical chains of reasoning leading to a judgement supported by knowledge of relevant evidence, and references to religion applied to the issue."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 2 (the same statement, visibly weaker)","items":["The weaker answer: 'Muslims believe in predestination, which means God has planned everything that happens. So if someone does something bad it was already going to happen and they cannot really help it. Other people think that is not fair because everyone makes their own choices and should be punished if they do wrong. I think both sides have a point.'","What it does meet: the Level 2 descriptor 'Recognition of different points of view, each supported by relevant reasons/evidence' - two views are set out and each is given a reason.","What it misses: the Level 3 requirement of 'Logical chains of reasoning that draw on knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information' and 'Clear reference to religion'. There is no source, no surah and ayah, no Hadith, and no use of the Day of Judgement or of al-Qadr as an article of faith.","What it misses at Level 4: 'judgement(s) supported by knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence' - 'both sides have a point' is an opinion bolted on, not a conclusion that follows from anything argued.","It also never names a tradition, so the divergence between Sunni teaching on al-Qadr and Shi'a teaching on Adalat is invisible, and the second view is left as a general objection rather than a Muslim one.","The fix, in order: attach one source to each side, develop each into a chain of two or three linked sentences, name the tradition holding each view, and end by saying which argument was stronger and why."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Islam, Beliefs and teachings (3.1.5.1)","items":["Tawhid - the oneness of God. Prescribed text: Qur'an Surah 112, al-Ikhlas; 112:1 declares 'He is God, the One'. Denying it by associating anything with God is shirk.","The six articles of faith (Sunni Islam) - Tawhid, angels, the holy books, the prophets, the Day of Judgement, al-Qadr. Source: the Hadith of Jibril, recorded in Sahih Muslim; the Qur'an lists belief in God, his angels, his books, his messengers and the Last Day at 4:136.","The five roots of Usul ad-Din (Twelver Shi'a Islam) - Tawhid, Adalat, Nubuwwah, Imamah, Mi'ad.","The nature of God - omnipotence, beneficence, mercy, fairness and justice (Adalat in Shi'a Islam). Mercy: 'the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful' (Qur'an 1:1). Immanence: 'nearer to him than his jugular vein' (Qur'an 50:16). Transcendence: God is beyond creation, so no image of God is made.","Angels - created from light, without free will, never worshipped. Jibril: the angel of revelation, who dictated the Qur'an to Muhammad at the cave of Hira. Mika'il: the angel of mercy and sustenance, associated with rain and nourishment.","Al-Qadr - God's decree; everything is within God's knowledge and will. Held alongside human freedom: 'There is no compulsion in religion' (Qur'an 2:256); the human being is khalifah, a steward (2:30).","Akhirah - barzakh, the state of waiting after death; resurrection of the body; Yawm ad-Din, the Day of Judgement, when deeds are weighed; Jannah, the Garden; Jahannam, the Fire. 'An atom's weight of good' will be seen again (Qur'an 99:7).","Adam - the first human being and the first prophet, taught by God and made khalifah of the earth.","Ibrahim - the model of submission: rejected idols, was willing to sacrifice his son, raised the Ka'aba at Makkah with Isma'il. Commemorated at Id-ul-Adha; his family's actions are re-enacted on Hajj.","Muhammad - the final prophet, the Seal of the Prophets (Qur'an 33:40); received the Qur'an through Jibril; his Sunnah and the Hadith are a source of authority second to the Qur'an.","The holy books - Qur'an (Muhammad), Tawrat or Torah (Musa), Zabur or Psalms (Dawud), Injil or Gospel (Isa), Scrolls of Abraham (Ibrahim, now lost). Jews and Christians are People of the Book; most Muslims hold the earlier books were altered over time.","The imamate (Shi'a Islam) - leadership appointed by God beginning with Ali ibn Abi Talib, the Prophet's cousin and son-in-law. Twelver Shi'a name twelve Imams, sinless and divinely guided; the twelfth is in occultation and will return as the Mahdi. Sunni Muslims instead followed caliphs chosen by the community, the first being Abu Bakr."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Islam, Practices (3.1.5.2)","items":["The Five Pillars of Sunni Islam - Shahadah, Salah, Zakah, Sawm, Hajj. Source: a Hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari, 'Islam is built upon five'.","The Ten Obligatory Acts of Shi'a Islam - Salah, Sawm, Zakah, Khums, Hajj, Jihad, Amr-bil-Maruf (encouraging good), Nahi Anil Munkar (forbidding wrong), Tawalla (loving the Prophet's family), Tabarra (dissociating from God's enemies). Shahadah is not listed, because it belongs to the roots of faith.","Shahadah - there is no god but God, and Muhammad is the messenger of God. Shi'a Muslims add that Ali is the friend of God. Said at conversion before witnesses, in the adhan and iqamah, within salah, whispered to a newborn, and hoped for as the last words before death.","Salah - Fajr, Zuhr, Asr, Maghrib, Isha; five separate times for most Sunni Muslims, three combined sittings for many Shi'a Muslims. Facing the qiblah, the Ka'aba at Makkah. Wudu before prayer: hands, mouth, nose, face, arms to the elbows, head wiped, feet washed. Each rak'ah: standing with Surah al-Fatihah, ruku, sujud, sitting. Many Shi'a Muslims pray with arms at their sides and prostrate onto a clay tablet; Sunni Muslims usually fold their arms.","Jummah - the Friday midday congregational prayer at the mosque, with a khutbah, a sermon.","Sawm - fasting through Ramadan, the ninth month of the lunar calendar, from dawn to sunset: no food, drink, tobacco or sexual relations. Suhur before Fajr, iftar at sunset, traditionally with dates and water. Origins: fasting is 'prescribed for you' (Qur'an 2:183). Benefits: taqwa, self-discipline, empathy with the poor, unity of the ummah.","Exceptions to Sawm, with their reasons - young children, the very old, the ill, travellers, and women who are pregnant, breastfeeding or menstruating, because the fast is to build devotion, not to damage health. Missed days are made up later, or fidyah is paid to feed someone in need.","Laylat al-Qadr, the Night of Power - in the last ten nights of Ramadan; the first revelation, 'Read in the name of your Lord' (Qur'an 96:1), a night 'better than a thousand months' (97:3).","Zakah - 2.5 per cent of surplus wealth above the nisab, given annually. 'Establish prayer and give zakah' (Qur'an 2:110); recipients named at 9:60, including the poor, the needy, those in debt and the stranded traveller. Benefits of receipt: help as a right rather than begging, dignity kept, debt cleared, tools or schooling bought, so a household can become self-sufficient.","Khums (Shi'a Islam) - an additional fifth of surplus annual income, divided between the descendants of the Prophet and religious leaders for religious and charitable purposes.","Hajj - the pilgrimage to Makkah in Dhul-Hijjah, obligatory once in a lifetime for those physically and financially able (Qur'an 22:27). Ihram: two plain white unsewn cloths for men, modest dress for women. Ka'aba at Makkah: tawaf, seven circuits; sa'y, hurrying seven times between Safa and Marwah as Hajar searched for water. Arafat: standing from midday to sunset on 9 Dhul-Hijjah, praying for forgiveness, where Muhammad preached his final sermon. Muzdalifah: the night under the open sky, gathering pebbles. Mina: stoning the three pillars, rejecting the devil's temptations, and the sacrifice of Id-ul-Adha.","Jihad - greater jihad, the inner struggle to obey God; lesser jihad, armed struggle in defence (Qur'an 22:39 permits fighting to those wronged). Conditions: declared by a fair religious authority; defensive or to right a serious wrong; a last resort; never for land or forced conversion, since 'There is no compulsion in religion' (2:256); civilians, trees, crops and animals spared; fighting stops when the enemy seeks peace. Many Shi'a Muslims hold that offensive jihad may be declared only by the rightful Imam.","Id-ul-Fitr - 1 Shawwal, the festival of breaking the fast: Id prayer in a mosque or hall, Zakat-ul-Fitr given before the prayer, new clothes, cards and gifts, visits to family and graves. In Britain it is not a public holiday, so families take leave from work or school.","Id-ul-Adha - 10 Dhul-Hijjah, the festival of sacrifice at the end of Hajj, commemorating Ibrahim's willingness to sacrifice his son and God's provision of a ram. Qurbani meat is shared in three, for family, friends and the poor; in Britain the slaughter must be done in a licensed abattoir, so many pay a halal butcher or a charity.","Ashura - 10 Muharram. Shi'a Muslims mourn the martyrdom of Husayn, the Prophet's grandson, killed at Karbala in 680 CE, with mourning gatherings, black clothing and processions. Sunni Muslims keep it as a voluntary fast recalling God's rescue of Musa from Pharaoh."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan - 'Salah is the most important of the Five Pillars.' Evaluate this statement.","items":["The question as the paper sets it: 'Salah is the most important of the Five Pillars.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Muslim teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks]","Decode it first: important is not defined, so define it in the opening line - most important could mean most frequent, most demanding, or most fundamental. Saying which meaning you are using is what lets the conclusion be justified rather than asserted.","Paragraph 1, support: salah is required of every Muslim every day, five separate times for most Sunni Muslims and three sittings for many Shi'a Muslims, so it shapes the whole of life rather than one month or one journey. Source: the Qur'an pairs it with giving - 'establish prayer and give zakah' (2:110).","Paragraph 2, support developed: prayer is the moment Tawhid is acted out - facing the Ka'aba, washing in wudu, prostrating in sujud before one God alone - and it is the Pillar no circumstance excuses, since a Muslim who cannot stand may pray sitting and one who cannot reach a mosque may pray at work or at home.","Paragraph 3, different point of view: Shahadah comes first because everything else is done for the God it names; salah offered without belief would be empty movement. The declaration is said at conversion, in the adhan, in salah itself and to a newborn, so it frames a whole life.","Paragraph 4, different point of view developed: zakah and sawm act on other people, feeding the poor and building empathy, and Hajj gathers the whole ummah in ihram at Arafat; a critic can argue that a Pillar which changes other lives outranks one performed privately.","Paragraph 5, the divergence the specification requires: Shi'a Muslims organise duty as the Ten Obligatory Acts, in which salah sits beside Khums, jihad, Amr-bil-Maruf and Nahi Anil Munkar, so ranking one act above the rest is less natural in that tradition.","The evaluation thread running through all of it: every paragraph must return to the meaning of important you set in the opening, so the reader can see the same test being applied to each Pillar rather than five separate descriptions.","What the conclusion must say: name which argument was strongest and why. For example - if important means what a Muslim life is built on, Shahadah wins, because salah is prayer to the God it declares; if it means what sustains that life daily, salah wins. Give the reader one of these and show it follows from the paragraphs, rather than adding a fresh opinion.","Timing: about a minute a mark, so roughly twelve minutes on this answer, leaving the first four parts of the five-part question about thirteen minutes between them. Write in paragraphs and use the technical vocabulary - Tawhid, ummah, ihram, Usul ad-Din - for the knowledge credit; the three spelling, punctuation and grammar marks sit on the Beliefs 12-mark question, not on this Practices one.","Level check before you stop: different points of view considered, chains of reasoning rather than lists, clear reference to religion throughout, and a judgement supported by the evidence you actually gave."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T14:04:50Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":1,"notes":"Re-judged against the kit file after the fix pass: all four fixes landed and introduced nothing new. room.questions[10].sol now attributes 50:16 to immanence and holds it alongside transcendence (correct, and still 137 words); extras[4] items[0] ends '[12 marks]' and its timing item now puts the SPaG marks on the Beliefs question, which is right for a Practices statement on Salah; 'Surah 112:1 declares He is God, the One' replaces 'opens/begins' in all five places, which is accurate since the ayah itself begins 'Say'; lesson.examples[0] setup no longer promises a posture contrast its five steps never use. Nothing else in the kit changed. Re-checked the whole room: 16 Qur'an references (Surah 112, 112:1, 96:1-5, 1:1, 2:30, 2:110, 2:183, 2:256, 4:136, 9:60, 22:27, 22:39, 33:40, 50:16, 97:3, 99:7) and both Hadith citations (Hadith of Jibril, Sahih Muslim; 'Islam is built upon five', Sahih al-Bukhari) verify; every Sunni/Shi'a attribution is correctly assigned; all 16 tariffs are 1, 2, 4, 5 or 12; the three 12-mark stems carry the four Component 1 bullets with SPaG named only on the Beliefs question; no quotation exceeds seven words; no printed June 2023 or specimen question is reproduced; the Level 4 and Level 2 model paragraphs sit correctly against the grid and both fact files are true. Ready to publish."}}},"3.1.6":{"id":"AQA-8062","topic":"3.1.6","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Judaism is one of the seven religion options in Component 1, and the paper sets two five-part questions on it: one on Beliefs and teachings, one on Practices, 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks each. The spelling, punctuation and grammar marks sit on the Beliefs evaluation, so the Hebrew vocabulary of this room - Shekhinah, mitzvot, Pikuach Nefesh, kashrut, Amidah - earns marks twice over. Three things are required of every answer: a named Jewish source of wisdom and authority, the influence of the belief on how Jews actually live, and the divergence between Orthodox and Reform Judaism that the specification writes into bullet after bullet. Learn the beliefs without the traditions attached and you will cap yourself, because a contrast that is not really a contrast is credited once, not twice.","idea":[{"h":"The three standing requirements: sources, influence, divergence","t":"Every Judaism answer is judged on three things at once. First, sources: beliefs and practices must rest on Jewish sources of wisdom and authority - the Torah and wider Tenakh, the Talmud, the siddur (prayer book), Maimonides - and the 5-mark question gives a whole mark for naming one. Genesis 12:1-3 and Exodus 20:1-17 are prescribed, so questions may be set on them. Second, influence: what a belief makes Jews do, give, eat, keep or campaign for. Third, divergence: Orthodox, Reform and Liberal Judaism are named perspectives, and the specification says common and divergent views should be included throughout. Judaism is one of the diverse religious traditions in Great Britain today, where the main tradition is Christianity. A top-level answer names the tradition before it names the belief: 'Orthodox Jews hold... whereas many Reform Jews hold...', and then cites where the belief comes from.","code":"3.1.6 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Judaism"},{"h":"God as one, Creator, Law-Giver and Judge, loving and merciful","t":"One bullet, four descriptions. God is one: the Shema, Deuteronomy 6:4, declares that 'the LORD is one', which rules out both many gods and any division within God, and is why Judaism has no images of God. God is Creator: Genesis 1:1 opens 'In the beginning God created', so the world is deliberate and good and human beings are stewards of it. God is Law-Giver: the commandments at Sinai come from God, not from human agreement, which is why they bind. God is Judge: he holds people to the law he gave, weighing deeds at Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur. And the same bullet calls him loving and merciful - Exodus 34:6 describes God as merciful and gracious, slow to anger. A top-level answer holds the last two together rather than choosing between them: the God who judges is the God who forgives the person who repents, which is exactly what makes the Days of Awe worth keeping.","code":"3.1.6.1 — The nature of God: God as one, as Creator, as Law-Giver and Judge"},{"h":"Shekhinah: the divine presence that dwells with people","t":"Shekhinah comes from a Hebrew root meaning to dwell, and names God's presence resting among his people rather than a separate being. Exodus 25:8 gives the reason for building the sanctuary: so that God may dwell among them, and Exodus 40:34-35 describes the cloud filling the Tabernacle; the same presence was believed to fill Solomon's Temple. After the Temple was destroyed the presence was understood to rest wherever Jews gather: Pirkei Avot 3:2 says that when people sit together and study Torah the divine presence is with them, and the Talmud connects it with a minyan at prayer. This is what makes a synagogue more than a hall, and what the ner tamid, the ever burning light, stands for. It sits beside the refusal to represent God, because presence is not the same as picture. A top-level answer uses Shekhinah to explain a practice - why the light burns, why Jews pray in a quorum - rather than only translating the word.","code":"3.1.6.1 — The divine presence (Shekhinah)"},{"h":"Life after death: judgement, resurrection and Jewish reticence","t":"Judaism says far less about the next life than Christianity or Islam, and that restraint is itself examinable: the emphasis falls on this world and on keeping the mitzvot, and Jews are taught to do right because it is right, not to earn a reward. Where beliefs are stated, they centre on Olam Ha-Ba, the World to Come, on divine judgement, and on resurrection: Daniel 12:2 speaks of those who sleep in the dust awaking, some to everlasting life, and the twelfth and thirteenth of Maimonides' Thirteen Principles affirm the Messiah and the resurrection of the dead. Traditional teaching also speaks of Gan Eden and of Gehinnom as a limited place of purification rather than eternal punishment. Orthodox Jews affirm bodily resurrection in the messianic age; many Reform and Liberal Jews reject bodily resurrection and speak instead of the immortality of the soul and of living on in the good a person leaves behind. A top-level answer makes the reticence an argument, not a gap.","code":"3.1.6.1 — Beliefs about life after death, including judgement and resurrection"},{"h":"The Messiah: a person to come, or an age to be built","t":"Mashiach means anointed one, and the bullet builds the disagreement into itself by asking for different views on the role and importance of the Messiah. Orthodox Jews await a future human king descended from David - Isaiah 11:1 pictures a shoot growing from the stump of Jesse - who will gather the exiles, rebuild the Temple in Jerusalem, restore the Torah and bring universal peace, the world Isaiah 2:4 describes when swords are beaten into ploughshares. Maimonides makes belief in his coming the twelfth Principle of Faith, and traditional Jews pray for it daily in the Amidah. Many Reform Jews do not expect a personal Messiah at all: they speak of a messianic age of justice and peace that human beings build, which is why tikkun olam, healing the world, carries so much weight in Reform teaching. A top-level answer treats this as the model contrast in Judaism: same hope, two accounts of who brings it, and two answers to how central the belief is.","code":"3.1.6.1 — The nature and role of the Messiah"},{"h":"Abraham, the promised land and the sign of the covenant","t":"Genesis 12:1-3 is prescribed, so it can be quoted for the source mark and questions may be set on it. God tells Abram to leave his country, his kindred and his father's house for a land God will show him, promises to make of him 'a great nation' and to bless him, and says that through him 'all the families of the earth' will be blessed. Three things are promised: descendants, land, and blessing that reaches beyond his own people. The land promised is Canaan, which is why the land of Israel matters in Jewish thought. The covenant is sealed in Genesis 17 by circumcision, called 'a sign of the covenant', performed on the eighth day - which is why Brit Milah is still kept and why a practice question can be answered from a beliefs text. A top-level answer names the reference, gives one short phrase from it, and then says what it obliges Jews to do now.","code":"3.1.6.1 — The promised land and the Covenant with Abraham, Genesis 12:1-3"},{"h":"Sinai: Moses, the Ten Commandments and a nation under law","t":"Exodus 20:1-17 is the second prescribed text. Moses is the mediator: he goes up the mountain, receives the terms and brings them down to the people, who agree to keep them, so the covenant at Sinai is made with a whole nation rather than one family. The Ten Commandments open by identifying the God who is speaking, 'I am the LORD your God', the God who brought Israel out of Egypt, then forbid other gods and images, misuse of God's name, and command 'Remember the sabbath day' and the honouring of parents, before forbidding murder, adultery, theft, false witness and coveting. The order matters: duties owed to God come first, duties owed to other people follow, and that division becomes the whole framework of mitzvot between man and God and mitzvot between man and man. The bullet asks for importance as well as story, so a top-level answer says what Sinai made the Jews into - a people defined by law, freely accepted.","code":"3.1.6.1 — The Covenant at Sinai and its importance, Exodus 20:1-17"},{"h":"Justice, healing the world, charity and kindness","t":"Four principles are named, and each has a Hebrew term and a source behind it. Justice is tzedek: Deuteronomy 16:20 tells Israel to pursue justice, and Jewish courts and campaigning work are built on it. Healing the world is tikkun olam, an idea expressed in the Aleinu prayer, which speaks of perfecting the world under God's rule, and one that modern Jews, especially in Reform Judaism, apply to poverty, refugees and the environment. Charity is tzedakah, a word from the same root as justice, so giving is treated as a duty owed rather than a kindness offered; Maimonides sets out eight levels of giving, with the highest being to help someone become self-supporting so they need no more charity. Kindness to others is gemilut hasadim, the acts of care - visiting the sick, comforting mourners, hospitality - that Pirkei Avot 1:2 names, with Torah and service, as one of the three things the world stands on. A top-level answer uses these as arguments, not labels, and reaches for Leviticus 19:18, 'love your neighbour as yourself', when a theme question asks why.","code":"3.1.6.1 — Key moral principles"},{"h":"Sanctity of life and Pikuach Nefesh","t":"Human life is holy because human beings are made in the image of God, Genesis 1:27, so a life is not a thing to be weighed against convenience. The Mishnah, Sanhedrin 4:5, teaches that whoever saves a single life is counted as having saved a whole world, and the Talmud, Yoma 85b, reads Leviticus 18:5 - that a person shall live by the commandments - to mean that one should live by them and not die by them. Pikuach Nefesh, saving a life, therefore overrides almost every mitzvah: a Jewish doctor drives to the hospital on Shabbat, a patient eats on Yom Kippur if fasting would endanger them, and forbidden food may be eaten to stay alive. Only three commandments are never broken even to save a life - idolatry, murder and forbidden sexual relations. A top-level answer uses Pikuach Nefesh as a hinge in evaluation: it proves that Jewish law ranks its own commandments, so any claim that one ritual is the highest duty has to answer it.","code":"3.1.6.1 — The importance of the sanctity of human life, including Pikuach Nefesh"},{"h":"Free will and the 613 mitzvot","t":"The bullet asks for the relationship, not a list. Jewish tradition counts 613 mitzvot in the Torah - Maimonides divides them into 248 positive commands and 365 prohibitions, and the Talmud, Makkot 23b, records the number in the name of Rabbi Simlai. Many cannot be kept at all today because they concern the Temple and its sacrifices. Against that stands free will: Deuteronomy 30:19 sets life and death before the people and tells them to choose life, and the covenant at Sinai was accepted, not imposed. That is the relationship. God commands, but he does not compel, so obedience is worth something and disobedience is answerable; judgement and repentance both depend on the choice being real. Orthodox Jews hold that all 613 were revealed at Sinai and bind for all time. Reform Jews hold that the ethical mitzvot bind absolutely while ritual mitzvot are a matter of informed personal choice in each generation. A top-level answer shows that this divergence is a disagreement about how God's will is known, not about whether it should be obeyed.","code":"3.1.6.1 — The relationship between free will and the 613 mitzvot"},{"h":"Mitzvot between man and God and mitzvot between man and man","t":"The two categories are mitzvot bein adam laMakom and mitzvot bein adam lachavero. The first are duties owed directly to God: keeping Shabbat, daily prayer, wearing tefillin, keeping kashrut, observing the festivals. The second are duties owed to other people: honesty in trade, paying wages on time, giving tzedakah, honouring parents, not gossiping, visiting the sick. The Ten Commandments are traditionally pictured on two tablets along exactly this line. The difference in weight is drawn by the Mishnah, Yoma 8:9: Yom Kippur atones for sins against God, but for a sin against another person there is no atonement until you have gone and put it right with them. That does not make ritual optional, because both kinds are commanded by the same God, so keeping kashrut is also obedience to God. A top-level answer refuses the false choice the 12-mark statement offers and argues instead about which is prior when the two collide.","code":"3.1.6.1 — Mitzvot between man and God and mitzvot between man and man"},{"h":"What a synagogue is for","t":"The bullet asks for importance, so answer with what the building does rather than what stands in it. A synagogue is three institutions in one, and Jews use three names for it: beit knesset, house of assembly; beit tefillah, house of prayer; beit midrash, house of study. It became the centre of Jewish life after the destruction of the Second Temple in 70 CE left no place of sacrifice, and it holds the community together in Britain today - the cheder that teaches children Hebrew, the classes and shiurim for adults, the weddings and funerals, the welfare and burial societies, the social hall where a scattered community actually meets. Public prayer needs a minyan, a quorum of ten: ten adult men in Orthodox practice, ten adults of either sex in Reform practice, which is one reason belonging matters. A top-level answer explains an effect on individuals, communities or societies, because that is what an influence question pays for.","code":"3.1.6.2 — The synagogue and its importance"},{"h":"Bimah, aron hakodesh, ner tamid, and Orthodox and Reform differences","t":"Three features are named with the specification's own glosses. The aron hakodesh, the ark, is a cupboard set in the wall that faces Jerusalem and holds the Torah scrolls behind a curtain; the congregation stands when it is opened. The ner tamid, the ever burning light, hangs above or before it, recalling the lamp commanded in Exodus 27:20-21 and standing for God's continuing presence. The bimah is the reading platform from which the Torah is read aloud with a yad, a pointer, so the parchment is not touched. Then the specified difference: in most Orthodox synagogues the bimah stands in the centre, men and women sit apart behind a mechitza or in a gallery, the service is almost entirely in Hebrew, only men lead prayer, and no instruments are played on Shabbat; in Reform synagogues the bimah is usually at the front, families sit together, Hebrew is mixed with English, women may be rabbis and read from the Torah, and an organ or choir may be used. A top-level answer attaches every feature to the tradition that has it.","code":"3.1.6.2 — The design and religious features of synagogues"},{"h":"Public worship and the Amidah, the standing prayer","t":"Jews pray three times a day - Shacharit in the morning, Minchah in the afternoon, Ma'ariv in the evening - and the specification asks for services in both Orthodox and Reform synagogues. An Orthodox service is in Hebrew, often led by a chazan, with men wearing a kippah and tallit and, on weekday mornings, tefillin; the Torah is read on Mondays, Thursdays and Shabbat, and the service follows the siddur closely. A Reform service is usually shorter, mixes Hebrew with English, seats families together and may be led by a woman. At the centre of every service stands the Amidah, the standing prayer, said facing Jerusalem: nineteen blessings on a weekday and seven on Shabbat, opening with praise, moving to requests and closing with thanks, said silently and then, in Orthodox services, repeated aloud by the reader. It is also called the Shemoneh Esrei. A top-level answer names the Amidah and the siddur as its source of practice, which is worth a mark on its own in the 5-mark question.","code":"3.1.6.2 — Public acts of worship"},{"h":"Shabbat in the home and in the synagogue","t":"Shabbat runs from sunset on Friday to nightfall on Saturday and rests on two sources: the fourth commandment, 'Remember the sabbath day', Exodus 20:8, and God's own rest after creation in Genesis 2:2-3, with Deuteronomy 5:15 tying it to the rescue from Egypt. In the home it is kept with candles lit before sunset, kiddush said over wine, two loaves of challah recalling the double portion of manna, the blessing of the children and an unhurried family meal; it closes with Havdalah, wine, sweet spices and a plaited candle. In the synagogue there is a Friday evening service and a Saturday morning service with the reading of the Torah. Rest is defined by the thirty-nine categories of work listed in the Mishnah, Shabbat 7:2, so Orthodox Jews do not drive, cook, write, carry or switch on electricity, while Reform Jews decide which restrictions serve the purpose of the day. Pikuach Nefesh overrides all of it. A top-level answer ties each practice to its reason rather than listing the evening.","code":"3.1.6.2 — Shabbat in the home and synagogue and its significance"},{"h":"The home and private prayer","t":"The specification gives the home its own bullet, so it must not be collapsed into Shabbat. A mezuzah is fixed to the right-hand doorpost of the house and of rooms within it, holding a small parchment with passages of the Shema, because Deuteronomy 6:9 tells Israel to write these words on the doorposts of their houses; many Jews touch it on entering. Blessings are said before and after eating, so the table is treated almost as an altar, and a kosher kitchen makes obedience a daily domestic act. The Shema is recited morning and evening, and observant men lay tefillin for weekday morning prayer. Deuteronomy 6:7 commands parents to teach these words to their children, so the home, not the synagogue, is where most Jewish learning begins. A top-level answer uses home and synagogue as a genuine contrast in the 4-mark question: communal, led and public against private, parental and daily.","code":"3.1.6.2 — Worship in the home and private prayer"},{"h":"Tenakh, the written law, and Talmud, the oral law","t":"The specification's own glosses fix the terms. Tenakh is the written law, an acronym of its three parts: Torah, the five books of Moses; Nevi'im, the prophets; Ketuvim, the writings. Talmud is the oral law - the Mishnah, the teachings compiled by Judah haNasi in about 200 CE, together with the Gemara, the later rabbinic discussion; the Babylonian Talmud was completed in about 500 CE. The Torah scroll used in the synagogue is handwritten by a sofer and read with a yad. Three things are asked: study, use and significance in daily life. Study is itself an act of worship, which is why a synagogue is also a beit midrash. Use is practical: the Talmud is where the answers actually come from when a Jew asks what counts as work on Shabbat or how a kitchen is made kosher, because the Torah alone does not say. Orthodox Jews hold that both were given at Sinai; Reform Jews read the Talmud as a human record to be weighed. A top-level answer names the right source for the right rule.","code":"3.1.6.2 — Tenakh and Talmud, and their study, use and significance in daily life"},{"h":"Birth, coming of age, marriage and mourning","t":"Four rituals, and significance is wanted for each. Brit Milah: a boy is circumcised on the eighth day by a mohel while a sandek holds him, blessings are said and he is given his Hebrew name - the sign of the covenant with Abraham, Genesis 17. Girls are named in the synagogue, and many Reform congregations hold a naming ceremony for boys and girls alike. Bar Mitzvah and Bat Mitzvah: a boy becomes Bar Mitzvah, son of the commandment, at thirteen and a girl Bat Mitzvah at twelve in Orthodox practice, usually at thirteen in Reform practice, where girls read from the Torah as boys do; the point is not the party but taking personal responsibility for the mitzvot. Marriage: the couple stand under the chuppah, a canopy standing for their new home, the ketubah is read, a ring is given, seven blessings are said and the groom breaks a glass. Mourning: burial follows quickly, mourners tear a garment, sit shiva for seven days, keep shloshim for thirty, and say Kaddish - a prayer that praises God and never mentions death - for eleven months for a parent, marking the yahrzeit each year. A top-level answer gives the meaning, not the timetable.","code":"3.1.6.2 — Rituals and their significance"},{"h":"Kosher, trefah and the separation of milk and meat","t":"Kosher means fit or proper, trefah means torn and so forbidden. Leviticus 11 allows only land animals that both chew the cud and have cloven hooves, so beef and lamb are permitted and pork is not, and only fish with fins and scales, so shellfish is not; birds of prey are forbidden. Permitted animals must be killed by shechitah, a single cut by a trained shochet, and the blood drained, because Leviticus treats blood as life. The command not to cook a young goat 'in its mother's milk' appears three times in the Torah, at Exodus 23:19, Exodus 34:26 and Deuteronomy 14:21, and the Talmud develops it into the full separation of milk and meat: separate crockery, separate washing up, and a waiting time after meat. The divergence is in the bullet itself. Orthodox Jews treat kashrut as binding law; many Reform and Liberal Jews treat it as a matter of personal choice, some keeping part of it, some applying an ethical kashrut about how animals and workers are treated. A top-level answer argues from that disagreement rather than describing shopping.","code":"3.1.6.2 — Dietary laws and their significance"},{"h":"Rosh Hashanah, Yom Kippur and Pesach in Britain today","t":"Origins, meaning and importance in Britain today are all asked for. Rosh Hashanah falls on 1 Tishrei, the Jewish New Year and, in tradition, the anniversary of creation; the shofar is blown, apples are dipped in honey for a sweet year, and it opens the Ten Days of Repentance. Yom Kippur, the Day of Atonement, falls on 10 Tishrei: a fast of about twenty-five hours with no food, drink, washing or leather shoes, begun with Kol Nidrei, spent almost entirely in the synagogue and closed by Ne'ilah and a single blast of the shofar; Leviticus 16:30 says that on this day atonement is made. But repentance for wrongs done to another person requires putting them right first. Pesach, from 15 Nisan, remembers the Exodus: chametz is cleared from the house, and at the seder the Haggadah is read with matzah, the bread of affliction, maror, charoset, a roasted shank bone, the four cups of wine and the youngest child's four questions, because Exodus 13:8 tells a parent to tell their child what God did for them. A top-level answer says what the festival does for a Jewish family in Britain now: a Jewish calendar set against a secular one, and identity renewed each year.","code":"3.1.6.2 — Festivals and their importance for Jews in Great Britain today"}],"examples":[{"title":"Building a 4-mark contrast: Orthodox and Reform synagogues","setup":"The question is: 'Explain two contrasting ways in which worship is arranged in Orthodox and Reform synagogues. [4 marks]' The mark scheme awards, for each of the two contrasts, one mark for a simple explanation and two for a detailed one, and warns that if similar beliefs are given only one of them may be credited up to two marks. The specification names bimah, aron hakodesh and ner tamid, and requires the differences between Orthodox and Reform synagogues. Write the answer.","steps":["Choose two features that genuinely differ rather than two versions of one point: where people sit, and how the service is led and spoken.","State the first contrast: in most Orthodox synagogues men and women sit apart, women in a gallery or behind a mechitza, whereas in Reform synagogues families sit together.","Develop it for the second mark by giving each side its reason: Orthodox practice separates the sexes so that worshippers are not distracted during prayer, while Reform congregations seat families together because they hold that men and women stand equally before God.","State and develop the second contrast in the same shape: an Orthodox service is almost entirely in Hebrew, led by men, with no instruments played on Shabbat, whereas a Reform service mixes Hebrew with English, may be led by a woman rabbi and may use an organ or choir, because Reform Judaism holds that worshippers should understand what they pray.","Test the answer against the trap: each half names a tradition and says what it does differently, so neither pair can be read as the same point made twice."],"cues":["Which two features of synagogue worship differ most clearly between the two traditions?","How would you put the seating difference into a single contrasting sentence?","What has to be added to turn a stated contrast into a detailed one?","Which second contrast can be stated and developed in exactly the same shape?","What would stop this answer scoring both pairs of marks?"]},{"title":"Earning the fifth mark: two Jewish teachings about the nature of God","setup":"The question is: 'Explain two Jewish teachings about the nature of God. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]' Four marks come from two teachings explained simply for one mark each and in detail for two, and a fifth mark is given once for a relevant and accurate reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching. The specification lists God as one, as Creator, and as Law-Giver and Judge, loving and merciful. Write the answer.","steps":["Pick two descriptions that do not overlap, so that the examiner can see two separate teachings: God as one, and God as Judge who is also merciful.","Write the first teaching and develop it: Jews believe God is one, indivisible and unique, which is why no image of God is made and why the Shema is recited every morning and evening.","Anchor it in the source and take the fifth mark early, so it cannot be forgotten: the Shema at Deuteronomy 6:4 declares that 'the LORD is one'.","Write the second teaching with its development: God gave the law and judges people by it, weighing their deeds at Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur, yet the same God is described as merciful and gracious, so repentance is always accepted and the Day of Atonement has a point.","Read the answer back for the shape the mark scheme wants: two teachings, each stated and then explained, and one named text quoted accurately."],"cues":["Which two of the specification's descriptions of God are least likely to be marked as the same point?","What single practice shows most clearly that Jews believe God is one?","Which named text would an examiner accept as the source here?","How can judgement and mercy be written as one teaching rather than two contradictions?","What must be present in the finished answer for all five marks to be available?"]},{"title":"Turning a belief into an influence: Pikuach Nefesh","setup":"The question is: 'Explain two ways in which the belief in Pikuach Nefesh influences Jews today. [4 marks]' For each way, a simple explanation of a relevant and accurate influence earns one mark and a detailed explanation earns two, and the mark scheme states that to be a detailed explanation the influence of the way must be included. Pikuach Nefesh is the principle that saving a life overrides almost every other commandment. Write the answer.","steps":["Decide what an influence is here: not what Jews believe about life, but what Jews actually do differently because of the belief.","First way, with the doing made explicit: an Orthodox Jewish doctor or paramedic will drive, use a phone and write on Shabbat in order to treat a patient, even though all of those are otherwise forbidden as work.","Second way, in the same shape: a Jew whose health would be endangered by fasting is expected to eat and drink on Yom Kippur, and a rabbi will tell them to, so obedience to God on the holiest day of the year takes the form of not fasting.","Add the reason that makes each explanation detailed: the Talmud teaches that the commandments were given so that people may live by them, and the Mishnah says that saving a single life is like saving a whole world, so preserving life is the higher command.","Check that neither half is a statement of belief left hanging, because a belief without its consequence is a simple explanation only."],"cues":["What is the difference between a belief and an influence in this mark scheme?","Which everyday Jewish occupation puts the principle to work every week?","Which fast day gives the sharpest second example?","What reason turns each of these into a detailed explanation?","How can you tell, reading it back, that a half has scored only one mark?"]},{"title":"Planning a 12-mark evaluation: are the dietary laws the highest obedience?","setup":"The question is: 'Keeping the dietary laws is the most important way a Jew can show obedience to God.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Jewish teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] Level 4 wants a well-argued response with logical chains of reasoning leading to judgements supported by knowledge, and references to religion applied to the issue; there is a maximum of Level 2 if there is no reference to religion. Plan it.","steps":["Decode the statement before writing: it is a ranking claim, so the question is not whether kashrut is obedience but whether anything outranks it.","Build the supporting chain: kashrut comes straight from the Torah, it is kept at every meal and in private, it costs money and effort, and it is a mitzvah between a person and God kept purely because God commanded it, so it tests obedience continually.","Build the opposing chain from Judaism's own sources, which is what lifts the answer above a list: Pikuach Nefesh overrides kashrut, so Jewish law itself ranks it below saving life, and the Mishnah teaches that Yom Kippur does not atone for wrongs done to another person until they are put right.","Add the divergence the specification requires: Orthodox Jews treat the dietary laws as binding law, while many Reform and Liberal Jews treat them as a matter of informed personal choice and put the ethical mitzvot first.","Fix what the conclusion must do: not repeat the two sides but say which reasoning held and why, for example that kashrut is genuine obedience yet cannot be the highest because the tradition itself sets life and justice above it."],"cues":["What kind of claim is the statement making, and what would disprove it?","Which features of keeping kosher make the strongest case for the statement?","Which Jewish teaching most directly shows that kashrut can be overridden?","Which named Jewish perspectives disagree about how binding these laws are?","What must the last paragraph do that the earlier paragraphs have not done?"]},{"title":"Using a prescribed text: why Sinai still binds","setup":"The question is: 'Explain two ways in which the Covenant at Sinai is important for Jews today. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]' The specification prescribes Exodus 20:1-17 and names the role of Moses and the Ten Commandments, so the text may be quoted for the fifth mark. Two ways are explained simply for one mark each and in detail for two, with one further mark for the source. Write the answer.","steps":["Choose two ways that are about now rather than about the past, since the question asks what the covenant does for Jews today.","First way, stated and developed: the covenant makes the mitzvot binding, so a Jew who keeps Shabbat or eats kosher food is keeping terms a whole nation accepted, not following a private preference.","Anchor it in the prescribed text: the Ten Commandments in Exodus 20 open with God identifying himself as the God who brought Israel out of Egypt and command Israel to 'Remember the sabbath day'.","Second way, stated and developed: because the commandments run from duties owed to God to duties owed to other people, Sinai still supplies the framework in which Jews sort their obligations, which is why honesty, honouring parents and giving tzedakah are treated as religious duties and not merely good manners.","Read it back for the two things an examiner ticks separately: two developed ways, and one accurate reference to a named text."],"cues":["What does the phrase 'for Jews today' rule out of the answer?","How does the covenant change the status of an ordinary practice like Shabbat?","Which prescribed passage can be cited here, and what does its opening do?","What second way can be drawn from the order of the commandments themselves?","Which two separate things is the examiner ticking when the answer is finished?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which prescribed text records the Covenant with Abraham, and what three things does God promise?","a":"Genesis 12:1-3: descendants who will become a great nation, a land God will show him, and blessing that reaches all the families of the earth."},{"q":"State one Orthodox and one Reform view of the Messiah.","a":"Orthodox Jews await a personal Messiah, a descendant of David who will gather the exiles and bring peace; many Reform Jews expect no personal Messiah but a messianic age built by human effort."},{"q":"What does Pikuach Nefesh permit, and which three commandments does it never override?","a":"It permits breaking almost any mitzvah to save a life, including Shabbat rules and the Yom Kippur fast; it never permits idolatry, murder or forbidden sexual relations."},{"q":"Name the three features of a synagogue the specification lists, with the gloss it gives each.","a":"Bimah, the reading platform; aron hakodesh, the ark; ner tamid, the ever burning light."},{"q":"What is the difference between Tenakh and Talmud?","a":"Tenakh is the written law - Torah, Nevi'im and Ketuvim; Talmud is the oral law, the Mishnah with the Gemara, which applies the written law to daily life."}]},"room":{"facts":["The Judaism option is examined by two five-part questions, one on Beliefs and teachings and one on Practices, each running 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks, with three extra marks for spelling, punctuation and grammar on the 12-mark question in the Beliefs section.","The five stems, learnt word for word: 'Which one of the following...' with four options A to D; 'Give two...'; 'Explain two contrasting...' or 'Explain two ways in which... influences... today'; 'Explain two... Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer'; and a statement in quotation marks followed by 'Evaluate this statement.'","Timing is about a minute a mark: roughly one minute on the tick and the two points, four on the contrast, five on the source question and twelve on the evaluation, which is fifty minutes for the whole religion option.","The two prescribed texts are Genesis 12:1-3, the Covenant with Abraham, and Exodus 20:1-17, the Covenant at Sinai and the Ten Commandments; questions may be set on them, so both must be known by reference.","The four named divisions of Jewish moral teaching: justice (tzedek), healing the world (tikkun olam), charity (tzedakah) and kindness to others (gemilut hasadim).","Tradition counts 613 mitzvot, which Maimonides divides into 248 positive commands and 365 prohibitions, split between mitzvot bein adam laMakom, between a person and God, and mitzvot bein adam lachavero, between a person and other people."],"questions":[{"q":"Which one of the following means the divine presence of God? [1 mark] Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. A Shekhinah  B Shechitah  C Shloshim  D Shofar","a":"Answer: A Shekhinah. AO1:1, pure recall of a term.","sol":"A Shekhinah. Shekhinah comes from a Hebrew root meaning to dwell and names God's presence resting with his people, in the Tabernacle and Temple and wherever Jews gather to pray or study. Shechitah is kosher slaughter, shloshim is the thirty days of mourning and a shofar is a ram's horn, so none of the other three describes God's presence.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Three of the four options are practices or objects; only one names something about God.","Translate each Hebrew word before ticking, rather than choosing on the sound of it.","Ask which word you would use to say that God is present in the synagogue."],"codes":["3.1.6.1 — The divine presence (Shekhinah)"]},{"q":"Which one of the following is the reading platform in a synagogue? [1 mark] Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. A Aron hakodesh  B Bimah  C Mezuzah  D Ner tamid","a":"Answer: B Bimah. AO1:1, recall of a named feature with the specification's own gloss.","sol":"B Bimah. The bimah is the raised platform from which the Torah scroll is read aloud, with a yad used as a pointer so the parchment is not touched. The aron hakodesh is the ark holding the scrolls, the ner tamid is the ever burning light above it, and a mezuzah is fixed to a doorpost of a home, not to a synagogue platform.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["All four are named Jewish objects, but only one is something a person stands on.","Match each word to its English gloss before ticking.","Picture where the Torah scroll is unrolled during the service."],"codes":["3.1.6.2 — The design and religious features of synagogues"]},{"q":"Which one of the following means food that is forbidden under Jewish dietary law? [1 mark] Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. A Challah  B Kiddush  C Kosher  D Trefah","a":"Answer: D Trefah. AO1:1, recall of the term the specification names alongside kosher.","sol":"D Trefah. Trefah means torn, and is used for any food that is not permitted - pork, shellfish, meat not killed by shechitah, or a dish mixing milk with meat. Kosher means the opposite, food that is fit; challah is the plaited loaf eaten on Shabbat and kiddush is the blessing said over wine.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Two of the options belong to Shabbat rather than to the dietary laws.","The answer is the word the specification pairs directly with kosher.","Decide which word you would use of a bacon sandwich."],"codes":["3.1.6.2 — Dietary laws and their significance"]},{"q":"Give two Jewish beliefs about the Messiah. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for each of two correct points, no development required: for example, he will be a descendant of King David; he will gather the exiles to Israel; he will rebuild the Temple in Jerusalem; he will bring an age of peace; many Reform Jews expect a messianic age rather than a person. AO1:1.","sol":"1. The Messiah will be a human descendant of King David. 2. He will bring an age of peace in which the exiles are gathered and the Temple in Jerusalem is rebuilt. (Only the first two answers are marked, and neither needs explaining.)","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The specification asks for different views on the role and importance of the Messiah, so there is more than one tradition to draw on.","Two separate points, each in a line of its own; development earns nothing here.","One point can be about who he is, the other about what he will do."],"codes":["3.1.6.1 — The nature and role of the Messiah"]},{"q":"Give two things God promised Abraham in Genesis 12:1-3. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for each of two correct points: descendants who will become a great nation; a land God will show him; that God will bless him and make his name great; that all the families of the earth will be blessed through him. AO1:1.","sol":"1. God promised to make Abraham into a great nation. 2. God promised him a land, which he would be shown after leaving his own country. Either of the other promises in the passage - blessing for Abraham himself, or blessing for all the families of the earth through him - would also be credited.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["This is a prescribed passage, so the answer is inside the three verses themselves.","Two bare points, one per line; no reason or explanation is wanted.","The promises fall into the categories of people, place and blessing."],"codes":["3.1.6.1 — The promised land and the Covenant with Abraham, Genesis 12:1-3"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting Jewish beliefs about life after death. [4 marks]","a":"Two marks for each contrast, one for a simple explanation and two for a detailed one; similar beliefs are credited once only. Credit, for example, Orthodox belief in bodily resurrection in the messianic age against Reform belief in the immortality of the soul or in living on through one's deeds; or judgement and Olam Ha-Ba against the traditional reticence that puts the weight on this life. AO1:3.","sol":"Orthodox Jews believe in the resurrection of the dead. Maimonides makes it the last of his Thirteen Principles of Faith, and it is understood as a bodily resurrection in the messianic age, which is why Orthodox Jews bury rather than cremate their dead. In contrast, many Reform Jews reject bodily resurrection. They speak instead of the immortality of the soul and of a person living on in the good they have done and in the children they have taught, which is why Reform Judaism puts so much weight on what a person builds in this life rather than on what follows it.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The specification expects Orthodox, Reform and Liberal perspectives to appear, and this is a bullet where they part company.","Write two short paragraphs whose opening words set them against each other.","Each half needs a reason or a consequence attached, not just a stated belief."],"codes":["3.1.6.1 — Beliefs about life after death, including judgement and resurrection","3.1.6 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Judaism"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting ways in which worship is arranged in Orthodox and Reform synagogues. [4 marks]","a":"Two marks for each contrast, one simple and two detailed; if similar points are given only one may be credited. Credit seating (separate with a mechitza or gallery against families together), language (Hebrew against Hebrew mixed with English), who may lead (men only against women rabbis and readers), instruments on Shabbat, and the position of the bimah. AO1:3.","sol":"In most Orthodox synagogues men and women sit apart, the women in a gallery or behind a mechitza, because Orthodox practice holds that the sexes should not distract one another during prayer. In contrast, in a Reform synagogue families sit together, because Reform Judaism holds that men and women stand equally before God and should worship side by side. Secondly, an Orthodox service is conducted almost entirely in Hebrew and is led by men, with no instruments played on Shabbat, whereas a Reform service mixes Hebrew with English, may be led by a woman rabbi and may use an organ or a choir, since Reform congregations judge that worshippers should understand and be moved by what they pray.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The specification names differences between Orthodox and Reform synagogues as content, so the traditions must both be named.","Two genuinely different features, each with its reason, in two short paragraphs.","Contrasting words such as 'whereas' should carry the structure of the answer."],"codes":["3.1.6.2 — The design and religious features of synagogues","3.1.6.2 — Public acts of worship"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which the belief in Pikuach Nefesh influences Jews today. [4 marks]","a":"Two marks for each way, one simple and two detailed, and for the second mark the influence itself must be included. Credit medical work on Shabbat, eating on Yom Kippur when health requires it, eating non-kosher food to survive, organ donation and blood donation, and rescue services such as Hatzola. AO1:2.","sol":"Firstly, Jewish doctors, nurses and paramedics work on Shabbat. An Orthodox Jew who would not normally drive, write or use a phone on Shabbat will do all three to reach and treat a patient, because saving a life sets the day's restrictions aside. Secondly, Jews whose health would be harmed by fasting eat and drink on Yom Kippur. A pregnant woman or a diabetic will be told by their rabbi to eat, so on the holiest day of the year obedience to God takes the form of not fasting. In both cases the Mishnah's teaching that saving one life is like saving a whole world is being put into practice.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["An influence question pays for what people do differently, not for what they believe.","Take two settings where a normal Jewish obligation would have to be broken.","Each half must end with the consequence, or it scores half of what it could."],"codes":["3.1.6.1 — The importance of the sanctity of human life, including Pikuach Nefesh"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which keeping Shabbat influences Jewish families in Great Britain today. [4 marks]","a":"Two marks for each way, one simple and two detailed, with the influence stated for the second mark. Credit the Friday evening meal with candles, kiddush and challah; not driving, cooking, shopping or using electricity; walking to the synagogue and so living near it; children kept from Saturday sport or work; the weekly reunion of a scattered family. AO1:2.","sol":"Firstly, Shabbat shapes where and how an Orthodox family lives. Because driving counts as forbidden work, families buy homes within walking distance of their synagogue, cook on Friday, and set timers or leave lights on before sunset, so an ordinary week is planned around a single day. Secondly, it changes family life itself. The table is laid for a meal that begins with candles, kiddush over wine and two loaves of challah, and phones and screens are put away, so parents and children who have barely met all week spend an evening and a day together - which is why many Jewish parents describe Shabbat as the practice that keeps the family Jewish.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The specification gives Shabbat in the home and in the synagogue, so there are two settings available.","Describe a decision a family makes because of the day, then a second one.","The second mark in each half is only there if the effect on their lives is stated."],"codes":["3.1.6.2 — Shabbat in the home and synagogue and its significance","3.1.6.2 — Worship in the home and private prayer"]},{"q":"Explain two Jewish teachings about the nature of God. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"Two marks for each teaching, one simple and two detailed, plus one mark for a relevant and accurate reference to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching. Credit God as one, as Creator, as Law-Giver, as Judge, and as loving and merciful; sources include the Shema at Deuteronomy 6:4, Genesis 1:1 and Exodus 20:1-17. AO1:1.","sol":"Jews believe God is one. He is indivisible and unlike anything he has made, which is why Judaism forbids images of God and why the Shema, which declares that 'the LORD is one' at Deuteronomy 6:4, is recited morning and evening and written inside every mezuzah. Jews also believe God is Judge, and at the same time loving and merciful. He gave the law at Sinai and holds people to it, weighing their deeds at Rosh Hashanah and sealing the judgement at Yom Kippur; but because he is described as merciful and gracious, repentance is always accepted, which is what makes the Day of Atonement a day of hope rather than despair.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The specification lists four descriptions of God in a single bullet; choose two that cannot be read as one point.","Write a paragraph per teaching, each stating the belief and then explaining it, and put the reference where it cannot be missed.","One named text, accurately cited, is all the fifth mark needs."],"codes":["3.1.6.1 — The nature of God: God as one, as Creator, as Law-Giver and Judge"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which the Covenant at Sinai is important for Jews today. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"Two marks for each way, one simple and two detailed, plus one for an accurate reference; Exodus 20:1-17 is prescribed. Credit the mitzvot as binding covenant terms, Moses as mediator, the Ten Commandments as the framework of duties to God and to other people, Shabbat observance, and the freely accepted nature of the covenant. AO1:1.","sol":"Firstly, Sinai is why the mitzvot bind. The commandments were given by God and accepted by the whole people, so a Jew who keeps Shabbat or gives tzedakah is keeping the terms of a covenant rather than following a personal preference, and Orthodox Jews hold that all 613 mitzvot given there bind for all time. Secondly, Sinai supplies the framework in which Jews sort their duties, because the commandments run from duties owed to God to duties owed to other people - which is why honesty and honouring parents are treated as religious obligations. The Ten Commandments at Exodus 20 open with God naming himself as the God who brought Israel out of Egypt, and command Israel to 'Remember the sabbath day'.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The phrase 'for Jews today' rules out an answer that only retells what happened at the mountain.","Two ways, each stated and then explained, with the prescribed passage named once.","The order of the commandments is itself a way in, if you ask what it organises."],"codes":["3.1.6.1 — The Covenant at Sinai and its importance, Exodus 20:1-17","3.1.6.1 — The relationship between free will and the 613 mitzvot"]},{"q":"Explain two Jewish rituals that mark a stage in a person's life. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"Two marks for each ritual, one simple and two detailed, plus one for an accurate reference. Credit Brit Milah, Bar and Bat Mitzvah, the marriage ceremony under the chuppah and the mourning rituals of shiva, shloshim and Kaddish; Genesis 17 on circumcision as the sign of the covenant is an obvious source. AO1:1.","sol":"Brit Milah marks birth. A Jewish boy is circumcised on the eighth day by a trained mohel, with a sandek holding him and the child given his Hebrew name, and the ceremony matters because circumcision is the sign of the covenant God made with Abraham in Genesis 17, so the child is brought into that covenant before he can choose anything for himself. Bar and Bat Mitzvah marks coming of age. A boy becomes Bar Mitzvah at thirteen and a girl Bat Mitzvah at twelve in Orthodox practice, usually at thirteen in Reform practice where girls read from the Torah as boys do, and from that day the young person is personally responsible for keeping the mitzvot rather than their parents being responsible for them.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Four rituals are named in the specification; choose two that mark clearly different stages.","For each, say what happens and then what it means, and name one source once.","Orthodox and Reform practice differ over one of these ages, which is worth saying."],"codes":["3.1.6.2 — Rituals and their significance"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which the study of the Tenakh and the Talmud affects Jewish daily life. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Jewish belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"Two marks for each way, one simple and two detailed, plus one for an accurate reference. Credit the Talmud as the source of practical rulings on Shabbat work and kashrut, study as worship in the beit midrash, the weekly Torah reading, the teaching of children, and the differing Orthodox and Reform views of the authority of the oral law. AO1:1.","sol":"Firstly, the Talmud, the oral law, is where the practical rules of Jewish life actually come from. The Torah forbids work on Shabbat but does not define it, so the thirty-nine categories of work in the Mishnah decide whether a Jew may cook, drive or switch on a light, and the same discussion turns the command not to cook a kid in its mother's milk into the full separation of milk and meat. Secondly, study is itself an act of worship, not preparation for one. A synagogue is a beit midrash, a house of study, as well as a house of prayer, and Jews read the Torah portion each week and teach it to their children, which is why Deuteronomy 6:7 commands parents to teach these words to their children.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The specification asks for study, use and significance in daily life, so two of the three will do.","Take one way that changes what Jews do, and one that concerns what study itself is.","A named part of the Talmud, or a verse commanding teaching, will carry the fifth mark."],"codes":["3.1.6.2 — Tenakh and Talmud, and their study, use and significance in daily life","3.1.6.2 — The synagogue and its importance"]},{"q":"'Keeping the dietary laws is the most important way a Jew can show obedience to God.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Jewish teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks]","a":"AO2, marked by levels on the whole answer. In support: kashrut is Torah law, kept daily and privately, costly, a mitzvah between a person and God kept purely because commanded. Against: Pikuach Nefesh overrides it; the 613 mitzvot include far weightier duties; the prophets rank justice and kindness above ritual; Reform and Liberal Jews treat it as personal choice. Level 2 maximum without reference to religion.","sol":"There is a strong case for the statement, because kashrut reaches into every day of a Jewish life. The dietary laws come directly from the Torah: Leviticus 11 permits only land animals that chew the cud and have cloven hooves, and only fish with fins and scales, while the command about a young goat cooked in its mother's milk, repeated three times in the Torah, is developed by the Talmud into the full separation of milk and meat, with separate crockery and a waiting time between them. An Orthodox Jew therefore obeys God at every meal, in his own kitchen, where nobody is watching, and has to buy from a kosher butcher and check the marks on packaging. Obedience that costs money, time and convenience, and that is repeated three times a day, builds the habit of obedience far more thoroughly than an occasional act of charity. Many Orthodox Jews would add that kashrut is a mitzvah between a person and God with no obvious ethical reason behind it, which makes keeping it the purest test of obedience of all: it is done simply because God commanded it.\n\nAgainst the statement, Judaism itself ranks its commandments differently. Kashrut is one small group within the 613 mitzvot that Maimonides counted, and no Jewish source calls it the greatest of them. The Talmud teaches that Pikuach Nefesh, saving a life, overrides almost every commandment, kashrut included, so a Jew who must eat forbidden food to survive obeys God by eating it - which proves that the dietary laws are not the highest obligation. Judaism's own summary teachings point elsewhere: Micah says God asks his people to act justly and to love mercy, Leviticus 19:18 commands 'love your neighbour as yourself', and Pirkei Avot says the world stands on Torah, on service and on acts of loving-kindness. The Mishnah adds that Yom Kippur does not atone for a wrong done to another person until it has been put right with them, which puts duties owed to people ahead of ritual on the holiest day of the year. Many Reform and Liberal Jews go further, holding that the ethical mitzvot bind absolutely while ritual laws such as kashrut are a matter of informed personal choice, so that a Reform Jew who campaigns for justice but does not keep kosher may be no less obedient.\n\nIn conclusion the statement is too strong. Keeping kosher is genuine and demanding obedience, and for an Orthodox Jew it is not optional; but the Jewish sources themselves subordinate it, since Pikuach Nefesh overrides it and the prophets rank justice and kindness above ritual. Because Judaism treats all 613 mitzvot as one covenant obligation, the better view is that obedience is shown by keeping the whole law as one's tradition understands it, with kashrut as one important part of that rather than the most important way of all.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["The statement is a ranking claim, so ask what it is ranking the dietary laws above.","Build one side fully - reason, named Jewish teaching, consequence - before starting the other.","The last paragraph must weigh the two sides against each other, not summarise them in turn."],"codes":["3.1.6.2 — Dietary laws and their significance","3.1.6.1 — Mitzvot between man and God and mitzvot between man and man","3.1.6.1 — The relationship between free will and the 613 mitzvot"]},{"q":"'Mitzvot between man and man matter more than mitzvot between man and God.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Jewish teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels marked on the whole answer. In support: Yom Kippur does not atone until a wrong to a person is put right; Pikuach Nefesh; Leviticus 19:18; tzedakah, tikkun olam and gemilut hasadim; the prophets. Against: both kinds are commanded by the same God; ritual mitzvot are kept purely out of obedience; Shabbat and prayer sustain the ethical life; the two tablets are one covenant.","sol":"There is a real case for the statement, and Jewish sources make it. The Mishnah teaches that Yom Kippur atones for sins committed against God, but that for a sin against another person there is no atonement until the wrongdoer has gone to them and put it right - so on the holiest day of the year, duties owed to people are treated as the ones God will not simply waive. Pikuach Nefesh points the same way: saving a life overrides Shabbat, the Yom Kippur fast and the dietary laws, which means that when a ritual duty and a human need collide, Jewish law itself chooses the human need. The moral principles the specification names all sit on this side - justice, healing the world, charity and kindness - and Leviticus 19:18 commands 'love your neighbour as yourself', while Pirkei Avot names acts of loving-kindness as one of the three things the world stands on. Many Reform and Liberal Jews take exactly this view, holding the ethical mitzvot to be binding in every generation while ritual observance is a matter of informed personal choice.\n\nA different point of view begins from where both kinds of mitzvah come from. Both were given by the same God at Sinai, and the Ten Commandments in Exodus 20 are traditionally pictured on two tablets that belong to one covenant: a Jew who keeps Shabbat is obeying God just as truly as one who gives tzedakah. Orthodox Jews argue that the ritual mitzvot are in one sense the harder test, because many of them have no obvious human benefit and are kept purely because God commanded them - which is why keeping kosher or laying tefillin demonstrates obedience in a way that being kind, which most people would do anyway, does not. There is also a practical argument: the ritual duties are what form the ethical ones. A family that stops each Friday evening for kiddush, prays the Amidah daily and studies the Torah portion each week is being trained in the covenant that makes charity and honesty duties rather than moods, and a community without Shabbat and synagogue would soon have no shared reason to act justly at all.\n\nIn conclusion, the statement is right about priority but wrong about worth. When the two collide, Jewish teaching is clear that human need comes first: Pikuach Nefesh and the Mishnah's rule about Yom Kippur settle that. But it does not follow that mitzvot between a person and God matter less, because they are commanded by the same God and they sustain the very community that keeps the ethical ones. The better judgement is that the two kinds are not in competition at all: ethical mitzvot take precedence in a conflict, while ritual mitzvot are what keep a Jew formed to obey them.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Distinguish between two things the statement might mean: which comes first in a clash, and which is worth more.","Take each side's strongest Jewish teaching and follow it through to a consequence before turning round.","Your conclusion should be allowed to agree with one side in one sense and the other in another, provided it says why."],"codes":["3.1.6.1 — Mitzvot between man and God and mitzvot between man and man","3.1.6.1 — Key moral principles","3.1.6.1 — The importance of the sanctity of human life, including Pikuach Nefesh"]},{"q":"'Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur have no real meaning for Jews in Great Britain today.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Jewish teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks]","a":"AO2, levels marked on the whole answer. In support: many British Jews are secular and attend once a year; fasting and synagogue attendance are hard to fit around work and school; belief in divine judgement is not shared by all. Against: the High Holy Days draw the largest congregations of the year; teshuvah is a practical moral discipline; they express God as Judge who is merciful; identity and family are renewed annually.","sol":"There is something in the statement. In Britain today many Jews describe themselves as culturally rather than religiously Jewish, and for some the High Holy Days are the only time they enter a synagogue all year, which can look more like habit or family duty than repentance. The days are also hard to keep: Yom Kippur is a fast of about twenty-five hours spent almost entirely in the synagogue, and Rosh Hashanah falls at the start of a school and business year, so employees and pupils must ask for time off and some simply cannot. Most seriously, the meaning of the days depends on belief in a God who judges and writes his verdict for the year, and a British Jew who does not hold that belief may find the liturgy describing a judgement they do not expect, so the fast becomes a cultural marker rather than an act of atonement.\n\nA different point of view is that these are precisely the days that still carry meaning when others have faded. They draw the largest congregations of the Jewish year in Britain, including many who come to nothing else, which suggests that they answer something the rest of the calendar does not. Their content is not only ritual but moral: teshuvah requires a person to examine the year honestly and the Mishnah insists that Yom Kippur does not atone for a wrong done to another person until it has been put right with them, so the days force apologies, repaired friendships and settled debts - a discipline anyone can recognise as valuable. They also express the nature of God the specification names, as Judge who is at the same time loving and merciful: Leviticus 16:30 promises that on this day atonement is made, so the shofar blast at the end of Ne'ilah is a note of relief rather than dread. And for a small minority community they hold Jewish identity together, as families gather, apples are dipped in honey for a sweet year, and children learn what the shofar is for.\n\nIn conclusion the statement is too sweeping. It is fair as a description of how some British Jews relate to the days, and the difficulty of keeping them in a country whose calendar ignores them is real. But meaning is not measured only by weekly practice, and the evidence points the other way: these are the days British Jews keep when they keep nothing else, and their demand for repentance and repair is a moral one that does not depend on how observant a person is for the rest of the year. Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur therefore retain real meaning, even where the meaning has shifted from judgement towards identity and moral stocktaking.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what would count as 'real meaning', because the two sides may be measuring different things.","Use what actually happens on each day as evidence, and give each side a named Jewish teaching.","A justified conclusion here should concede what is true in the statement before saying why it fails."],"codes":["3.1.6.2 — Festivals and their importance for Jews in Great Britain today","3.1.6.1 — The nature of God: God as one, as Creator, as Law-Giver and Judge"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What does Pikuach Nefesh mean, and name one mitzvah it overrides.","a":"Saving a life; it overrides Shabbat restrictions, and also the Yom Kippur fast or the dietary laws where life is at risk."},{"q":"Which two texts does the specification prescribe for Judaism, and what does each record?","a":"Genesis 12:1-3, the Covenant with Abraham and the promised land; Exodus 20:1-17, the Covenant at Sinai and the Ten Commandments."},{"q":"Name the three religious features of a synagogue the specification lists, with the English gloss it gives each.","a":"Bimah, the reading platform; aron hakodesh, the ark; ner tamid, the ever burning light."},{"q":"How many mitzvot does Jewish tradition count in the Torah, and how does Maimonides divide them?","a":"613: 248 positive commands and 365 prohibitions."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Shekhinah","back":"The divine presence: God dwelling with his people. Exodus 25:8 gives the sanctuary's purpose as God dwelling among them; tradition places the presence wherever Jews pray or study Torah. The ner tamid stands for it.","code":"3.1.6.1 — The divine presence (Shekhinah)","open":false},{"front":"Pikuach Nefesh","back":"Saving a life. It overrides almost every mitzvah, including Shabbat rules and the Yom Kippur fast, but never idolatry, murder or forbidden sexual relations. The Mishnah says saving one life is like saving a world.","code":"3.1.6.1 — The importance of the sanctity of human life, including Pikuach Nefesh","open":false},{"front":"Bimah, aron hakodesh and ner tamid","back":"The reading platform where the Torah is read; the ark, facing Jerusalem, holding the scrolls; the ever burning light above it, recalling the Tabernacle lamp and standing for God's presence.","code":"3.1.6.2 — The design and religious features of synagogues","open":false},{"front":"Genesis 12:1-3","back":"Prescribed text: the Covenant with Abraham. God calls him to leave his country for a land he will be shown, promises to make of him 'a great nation', and to bless all the families of the earth through him.","code":"3.1.6.1 — The promised land and the Covenant with Abraham, Genesis 12:1-3","open":false},{"front":"Exodus 20:1-17","back":"Prescribed text: the Ten Commandments at Sinai, mediated by Moses. They open 'I am the LORD your God', then run from duties owed to God to duties owed to other people.","code":"3.1.6.1 — The Covenant at Sinai and its importance, Exodus 20:1-17","open":false},{"front":"Tenakh and Talmud","back":"Tenakh is the written law: Torah, Nevi'im, Ketuvim. Talmud is the oral law: the Mishnah, compiled about 200 CE, with the Gemara. The Talmud supplies the practical rules of Shabbat and kashrut.","code":"3.1.6.2 — Tenakh and Talmud, and their study, use and significance in daily life","open":false},{"front":"The Amidah","back":"The standing prayer, said facing Jerusalem at each of the three daily services: nineteen blessings on a weekday, seven on Shabbat, moving from praise to requests to thanks. In Orthodox services it is said silently, then repeated aloud.","code":"3.1.6.2 — Public acts of worship","open":false},{"front":"Kosher and trefah","back":"Kosher means fit: animals that chew the cud and have cloven hooves; fish with fins and scales. Permitted animals and birds are killed by shechitah; fish are not. Trefah means torn, so forbidden. Milk and meat kept separate.","code":"3.1.6.2 — Dietary laws and their significance","open":false},{"front":"Brit Milah","back":"Circumcision of a boy on the eighth day by a mohel, with a sandek holding him and the child given his Hebrew name. It is the sign of the covenant with Abraham, commanded in Genesis 17.","code":"3.1.6.2 — Rituals and their significance","open":false},{"front":"The 613 mitzvot","back":"The commandments tradition counts in the Torah: Maimonides divides them into 248 positive and 365 negative. Orthodox Jews hold all bind for all time; Reform Jews treat ritual mitzvot as informed personal choice.","code":"3.1.6.1 — The relationship between free will and the 613 mitzvot","open":false},{"front":"Mitzvot bein adam laMakom and mitzvot bein adam lachavero","back":"Commandments between a person and God - Shabbat, prayer, kashrut - and between a person and other people - honesty, tzedakah, honouring parents. Yom Kippur does not atone for the second kind until the wrong is put right.","code":"3.1.6.1 — Mitzvot between man and God and mitzvot between man and man","open":false},{"front":"The Messiah: Orthodox and Reform views","back":"Orthodox Jews await a personal Messiah descended from David who will gather the exiles, rebuild the Temple and bring peace. Many Reform Jews expect no person but a messianic age built by human effort.","code":"3.1.6.1 — The nature and role of the Messiah","open":false},{"front":"Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur","back":"New Year on 1 Tishrei: the shofar, apples and honey, the anniversary of creation. The Day of Atonement on 10 Tishrei: a fast of about twenty-five hours, Kol Nidrei, and Ne'ilah closed by the shofar.","code":"3.1.6.2 — Festivals and their importance for Jews in Great Britain today","open":false},{"front":"Name one way Shabbat is marked in the Jewish home","back":"Candles lit before sunset; kiddush said over wine; two loaves of challah and a family meal; the blessing of the children; Havdalah with wine, spices and a plaited candle at the close.","code":"3.1.6.2 — Shabbat in the home and synagogue and its significance","open":true},{"front":"Beit knesset, beit tefillah, beit midrash","back":"The three names for a synagogue: house of assembly, house of prayer, house of study. It became the centre of Jewish life after the Second Temple was destroyed in 70 CE. Public prayer needs a minyan of ten.","code":"3.1.6.2 — The synagogue and its importance","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Evaluate: 'Mitzvot between man and man matter more than mitzvot between man and God.')","items":["There is strong support for the statement within Jewish teaching itself, because Judaism treats duties owed to other people as the ones God will not waive.","The Mishnah teaches that Yom Kippur atones for sins committed against God, but that for a sin against another person there is no atonement until the wrongdoer has gone to that person and put the wrong right.","That ruling is decisive because it places the two categories in a deliberate order on the one day of the year devoted to forgiveness: God will forgive a neglected ritual on Yom Kippur, but he will not stand in for the person who was cheated, so the human duty has to be discharged first and by the wrongdoer in person.","The same ordering appears in Pikuach Nefesh, where saving a life overrides Shabbat, the fast and the dietary laws, so that when a ritual duty and a human need collide Jewish law itself chooses the need.","Against this it can be said that both kinds of mitzvah were given by the same God at Sinai and belong to one covenant, so that keeping Shabbat is obedience just as truly as giving tzedakah, and Orthodox Jews argue that a commandment with no obvious human benefit is the purer test of obedience.","On balance, however, the argument from Yom Kippur is the stronger, because it is not a general claim about which duty feels more valuable but a specific rule about what happens when the two are weighed: in a clash, Judaism gives the person in front of you priority over the ritual, even while both remain commanded.","Descriptor met: a well-argued response with reasoned consideration of different points of view, logical chains of reasoning leading to a judgement supported by knowledge, and references to religion applied to the issue - Level 4, 10 to 12 marks."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same statement, visibly weaker)","items":["I think mitzvot between man and man are more important because being kind to people is what really matters.","Jews are supposed to help other people and give money to charity, and that helps people more than doing rituals does.","Some Jews would say the rituals are important too because God told them to do them.","So overall I think being good to people is the most important thing in Judaism.","Descriptor met: recognition of different points of view, each supported by relevant reasons - Level 2, 4 to 6 marks.","Descriptor missed: there is no logical chain of reasoning and no named Jewish teaching, so it cannot reach Level 3, which requires clear reference to religion and reasoning that draws on knowledge of relevant evidence; the closing line restates an opinion instead of judging between the reasons given, which is what Level 4 requires.","The single change that would lift it: replace 'Jews are supposed to help other people' with the Mishnah's rule that Yom Kippur does not atone for a wrong against another person until it is put right, and then say what that rule proves."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Judaism: beliefs and teachings (3.1.6.1), with the source of authority for each","items":["God as one: the Shema, Deuteronomy 6:4, declares that 'the LORD is one'; recited morning and evening and written on the parchment inside a mezuzah.","God as Creator: Genesis 1:1, 'In the beginning God created'; the world is deliberate and good and human beings are its stewards.","God as Law-Giver and Judge, loving and merciful: the law is given at Sinai, Exodus 20:1-17; Exodus 34:6 describes God as merciful and gracious, slow to anger; deeds are weighed at Rosh Hashanah and sealed at Yom Kippur.","Shekhinah, the divine presence: Exodus 25:8 gives the sanctuary's purpose as God dwelling among his people; Exodus 40:34-35 describes the cloud filling the Tabernacle; Pirkei Avot 3:2 places the presence where Torah is studied.","Life after death: Daniel 12:2 speaks of those who sleep in the dust awaking; Maimonides' thirteenth Principle of Faith affirms the resurrection of the dead; Olam Ha-Ba is the World to Come; Gehinnom is traditionally a limited place of purification, not eternal punishment.","Divergence on life after death: Orthodox Jews affirm bodily resurrection in the messianic age and therefore bury rather than cremate; many Reform and Liberal Jews reject bodily resurrection and speak of the immortality of the soul.","The Messiah: Isaiah 11:1 pictures a shoot from the stump of Jesse, so a descendant of David; Isaiah 2:4 describes swords beaten into ploughshares; Maimonides' twelfth Principle affirms belief in his coming, and the Amidah prays for it daily.","Divergence on the Messiah: Orthodox Jews await a personal Messiah who will gather the exiles and rebuild the Temple; many Reform Jews expect a messianic age brought about by human effort, which is why tikkun olam carries such weight in Reform teaching.","The Covenant with Abraham: Genesis 12:1-3, prescribed - leave your country for a land I will show you; 'a great nation'; blessing for 'all the families of the earth'. Sealed by circumcision on the eighth day, Genesis 17, called a sign of the covenant.","The Covenant at Sinai: Exodus 20:1-17, prescribed - Moses mediates, the people accept, and the Ten Commandments open 'I am the LORD your God' before commanding 'Remember the sabbath day' and forbidding murder, theft and false witness.","Key moral principles: justice, tzedek - Deuteronomy 16:20 commands Israel to pursue justice; healing the world, tikkun olam - the Aleinu prayer speaks of perfecting the world under God's rule; charity, tzedakah - Maimonides sets out eight levels, the highest being to make a person self-supporting; kindness to others, gemilut hasadim - Pirkei Avot 1:2 names it one of the three things the world stands on. Leviticus 19:18: 'love your neighbour as yourself'.","Sanctity of life and Pikuach Nefesh: human beings are made in God's image, Genesis 1:27; Mishnah Sanhedrin 4:5 - saving one life is like saving a whole world; Talmud Yoma 85b reads Leviticus 18:5 to mean a person shall live by the commandments and not die by them. Never overridden: idolatry, murder, forbidden sexual relations.","Free will and the 613 mitzvot: Maimonides counts 248 positive and 365 negative commands; Talmud Makkot 23b records the number 613 in the name of Rabbi Simlai; Deuteronomy 30:19 sets life and death before the people and tells them to choose life.","Divergence on the mitzvot: Orthodox Jews hold all 613 were revealed at Sinai and bind for all time; Reform Jews hold that ethical mitzvot bind absolutely while ritual mitzvot are a matter of informed personal choice in each generation.","Mitzvot between man and God and between man and man: bein adam laMakom - Shabbat, prayer, tefillin, kashrut, festivals; bein adam lachavero - honesty in trade, paying wages promptly, tzedakah, honouring parents, visiting the sick. Mishnah Yoma 8:9: Yom Kippur does not atone for a wrong done to another person until it is put right with them."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Judaism: practices (3.1.6.2), with the source or authority for each","items":["The synagogue: beit knesset, house of assembly; beit tefillah, house of prayer; beit midrash, house of study. It became the centre of Jewish life after the destruction of the Second Temple in 70 CE. Public prayer requires a minyan of ten - ten adult men in Orthodox practice, ten adults of either sex in Reform practice.","Aron hakodesh, the ark: set in the wall facing Jerusalem, holding the Torah scrolls behind a curtain; the congregation stands when it is opened.","Ner tamid, the ever burning light: hangs before or above the ark, recalling the lamp commanded in Exodus 27:20-21 and standing for God's continuing presence.","Bimah, the reading platform: the Torah scroll, handwritten by a sofer, is read from it with a yad so the parchment is not touched.","Orthodox synagogues: bimah usually central; men and women seated apart behind a mechitza or in a gallery; service almost entirely in Hebrew; prayer led by men; no instruments played on Shabbat.","Reform synagogues: bimah usually at the front; families seated together; Hebrew mixed with English; women may be rabbis and read from the Torah; an organ or choir may be used.","Public worship: three daily services - Shacharit, Minchah, Ma'ariv. The Torah is read on Mondays, Thursdays and Shabbat. Men wear a kippah and tallit; tefillin are laid for weekday morning prayer.","The Amidah, the standing prayer, also called the Shemoneh Esrei: said facing Jerusalem, nineteen blessings on a weekday and seven on Shabbat, moving from praise to requests to thanks; said silently and then repeated aloud by the reader in Orthodox services.","Shabbat, sunset Friday to nightfall Saturday: commanded in Exodus 20:8, 'Remember the sabbath day', grounded in God's rest at creation in Genesis 2:2-3 and tied to the Exodus in Deuteronomy 5:15.","Shabbat in the home: candles lit before sunset, kiddush over wine, two loaves of challah recalling the double portion of manna, the blessing of children, an unhurried family meal; Havdalah closes it with wine, spices and a plaited candle.","Shabbat rest: thirty-nine categories of work are listed in the Mishnah, Shabbat 7:2; Orthodox Jews do not drive, cook, write, carry or switch on electricity, while Reform Jews decide which restrictions serve the purpose of the day. Pikuach Nefesh overrides all of them.","Worship in the home: a mezuzah on the right-hand doorpost holding passages of the Shema, because Deuteronomy 6:9 commands writing them on the doorposts; blessings before and after food; the Shema morning and evening; Deuteronomy 6:7 commands parents to teach these words to their children.","Tenakh, the written law: Torah, Nevi'im, Ketuvim. Talmud, the oral law: the Mishnah compiled by Judah haNasi about 200 CE, with the Gemara; the Babylonian Talmud was completed about 500 CE. Study is itself an act of worship.","Rituals: Brit Milah on the eighth day, by a mohel with a sandek, the sign of the covenant of Genesis 17; Bar Mitzvah at thirteen and Bat Mitzvah at twelve in Orthodox practice, usually thirteen in Reform practice where girls read from the Torah; marriage under the chuppah with the ketubah, a ring, seven blessings and a glass broken; mourning by kriah, shiva for seven days, shloshim for thirty, Kaddish for eleven months for a parent and the yahrzeit each year.","Dietary laws: Leviticus 11 permits land animals that chew the cud and have cloven hooves, and fish with fins and scales; shechitah is slaughter by a shochet with the blood drained. The command about a kid cooked in its mother's milk stands at Exodus 23:19, Exodus 34:26 and Deuteronomy 14:21, and the Talmud develops it into the separation of milk and meat.","Divergence on kashrut: Orthodox Jews treat the dietary laws as binding; many Reform and Liberal Jews treat them as a matter of personal choice, some keeping part of them, some applying an ethical kashrut concerned with how animals and workers are treated.","Rosh Hashanah, 1 Tishrei: the Jewish New Year and, in tradition, the anniversary of creation; the shofar is blown, apples are dipped in honey, and the Ten Days of Repentance begin.","Yom Kippur, 10 Tishrei: a fast of about twenty-five hours with no food, drink, washing or leather shoes, begun with Kol Nidrei and closed by Ne'ilah and a blast of the shofar; Leviticus 16:30 says that on this day atonement is made.","Pesach, from 15 Nisan: chametz is cleared from the home, and the seder retells the Exodus with the Haggadah, matzah as the bread of affliction, maror, charoset, a roasted shank bone, karpas, four cups of wine and the youngest child's four questions. Exodus 13:8 tells a parent to tell their child what God did for them."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'Keeping the dietary laws is the most important way a Jew can show obedience to God.' Evaluate this statement. [12 marks]","items":["Decode the statement: it is not asking whether kashrut is obedience, which nobody denies. It is a ranking claim - that nothing else a Jew does shows obedience more fully - so it falls if any Jewish teaching sets something above it.","What the bullets require on Component 1: refer to Jewish teaching, argue for, argue for a different point of view, and reach a justified conclusion. The different point of view here is a divergent view within Judaism, since the paper is set on one religion.","Paragraph 1, in support - the laws come straight from the Torah: Leviticus 11 on permitted animals and fish, shechitah, and the command about a kid in its mother's milk developed by the Talmud into the separation of milk and meat. Source used: Leviticus 11 and the Talmud.","Paragraph 2, in support - kashrut is continuous, costly and private: obedience at every meal, in one's own kitchen, with a kosher butcher and checked packaging, so the habit of obedience is built daily rather than occasionally. Source used: the practice itself, and the classification of kashrut as a mitzvah bein adam laMakom kept purely because God commanded it.","Paragraph 3, a different point of view - Jewish law ranks its own commandments: Pikuach Nefesh overrides kashrut, so eating forbidden food to survive is itself obedience. Source used: Talmud Yoma 85b on living by the commandments, and Mishnah Sanhedrin 4:5 on saving a life.","Paragraph 4, a different point of view - Judaism's summary teachings put ethics first: Micah on acting justly and loving mercy, Leviticus 19:18 on loving your neighbour, Pirkei Avot 1:2 on acts of loving-kindness, and Mishnah Yoma 8:9 on wrongs against another person. Source used: the prophets and the Mishnah.","Paragraph 5, the divergence the specification requires - Orthodox Jews treat kashrut as binding law given at Sinai; many Reform and Liberal Jews treat ritual mitzvot as informed personal choice while holding the ethical mitzvot to be binding, so they would reject the statement outright.","The evaluation thread running through all five: every argument is measured against one question - does Judaism itself treat kashrut as overriding, or as overridden?","What the conclusion must say: not that both sides have a point, but which reasoning held and why. Here: kashrut is real and demanding obedience and for an Orthodox Jew is not optional, but the tradition subordinates it, because Pikuach Nefesh overrides it and the prophets rank justice and kindness above ritual; so obedience is shown by keeping the whole law as one's tradition understands it, with kashrut one important part of it.","Timing and SPaG: about twelve minutes on this answer, a minute a mark, leaving the other thirteen for the four AO1 parts. No SPaG marks sit on this Practices question: the three SPaG marks are on the Beliefs 12-mark question, so use the specialist terms - kashrut, trefah, shechitah, mitzvot, Pikuach Nefesh, tzedakah - for their knowledge credit here and save the careful spelling for the Beliefs essay."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T14:04:24.919569Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":1,"notes":"The last residual is gone: lesson.examples[3].setup now ends '[12 marks]', and the only SPaG label anywhere in the kit is on room.questions[14], the Beliefs evaluation on mitzvot between man and God and man and man — exactly where 806213-QP-JUN23 and specification 4.4.1 put it. No Practices item (questions[13], questions[15], the kashrut plan, the worked example) carries one; the plan's remaining SPaG bullet and room.facts[0] both state the Beliefs rule correctly. On a full re-read nothing else is wrong: the 29 scripture references and the phrases quoted from them, the eight rabbinic and Maimonidean citations, every Orthodox, Reform and Liberal attribution, the 16 tariffs and their difficulty tiers, the Component 1 bullets on all three evaluations, scripture quotations of six words or fewer, no reproduced June 2023 or 2026 specimen question, the Level 4 and Level 2 model paragraphs correctly placed against the grid, two true and sourced fact files, five worked examples, 15 cards and four exit questions all check out. Publish as it stands."}}},"3.1.7":{"id":"AQA-8062","topic":"3.1.7","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Sikhism is one of the seven religions of Component 1, and if you have chosen it you will answer two full five-part questions on it: one on Beliefs and teachings, one on Practices, 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks each, with the spelling, punctuation and grammar marks riding on the Beliefs evaluation. Half of those marks are recall and short explanation, which is why precise Punjabi terms matter: gurmukh, haumai, mukti, sewa, langar, amritdhari. The other half is argument, and the examiner will not let an answer past Level 2 unless it refers to religion. This room gives you the named beliefs, the two prescribed references at Guru Granth Sahib 1a and 305:4, the practices in the gurdwara and the home, the festivals as they are kept in Britain, the ceremonies, and the divergence between amritdhari and sahajdhari Sikhs that the course specifies.","idea":[{"h":"The three things every Sikhism answer must do","t":"Every question on this religion rests on three standing demands. First, sources: beliefs and practices must be grounded in Sikh sources of wisdom and authority, above all the Guru Granth Sahib, with two references prescribed by name, GGS 1a for the Mool Mantra and GGS 305:4 for prayer in the home. Second, influence: you must be able to say what a belief makes Sikhs actually do in their homes, gurdwaras and communities. Third, divergence: Sikhism is not one voice, and the course names the perspectives of sahajdhari and amritdhari Sikhs and the different emphases of different texts. A top-level answer therefore never says 'Sikhs believe' and stops; it names the source, shows the effect, and where the statement allows it, sets one Sikh view against another.","code":"3.1.7 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Sikhism"},{"h":"The Mool Mantra: the opening statement of the scripture","t":"The Mool Mantra stands at the very beginning of the Guru Granth Sahib, at GGS 1a, and opens Ik Onkar, Sat Nam, Karta Purakh. Its content is a string of statements about God: there is one God; God's name is truth; God is the creator; God is Nirbhau, without fear, and Nirvair, without hatred; God is beyond time, unborn and self-existent; and God is known by the grace of the Guru. Its significance is that it is recited at the start of Japji Sahib every morning, said at a child's birth, and recited at the initiation, so it shapes a Sikh life from beginning to end. Both the content and the significance are examinable. A top-level answer quotes a phrase of it with the reference GGS 1a, then draws a consequence from it, such as the Sikh refusal to worship images.","code":"3.1.7.1 — The nature of God as expressed in the Mool Mantra"},{"h":"God as Creator: within the universe and beyond it","t":"Sikh teaching holds two aspects of God's relationship with creation together. God is shown in and through the universe: creation is God's own work and God's light is in every being, so a Sikh may see God in the natural world and in other people. At the same time God is separate from the universe, beyond form, beyond human grasp, not to be pictured or contained in an idol or a building. This is why the gurdwara has no image of God in it and why Sikhs bow to a book of God's word rather than to a statue. A top-level answer names both aspects rather than one, and shows how holding both keeps a Sikh from either worshipping creation or treating God as remote and uninvolved.","code":"3.1.7.1 — God as Creator"},{"h":"Human life as the opportunity to unite with God","t":"Human birth is not a punishment in Sikh thought. It is the opportunity, the one form of life in which a soul can consciously turn towards God and be freed from rebirth, which is why wasting it is the real tragedy. What is developed within that opportunity is character, and the course names nine virtues: wisdom, truthful living, justice, temperance, self-control, patience, courage, humility and contentment. They are not abstract; truthful living means honest work, humility means eating in langar beside anyone who comes, contentment means not being ruled by greed. A top-level answer uses the framing as well as the list: it says what human life is for, names two or three of the virtues, and shows the practice that builds each one.","code":"3.1.7.1 — The nature of human life as an opportunity to unite with God"},{"h":"Karma, rebirth and the aim of mukti","t":"Sikhs believe that actions carry consequences and that the soul passes through many lives, moving up and down through forms of life according to karma until it reaches human birth. The aim is mukti, liberation, and the course asks for its meaning and for its positive and negative aspects. Negatively, mukti is release: freedom from the cycle of rebirth, from karma and from haumai. Positively, it is union: the soul merging with God like a drop returning to the ocean, a state of bliss. Some may reach it in this life, living as jivan mukt, liberated while still alive. Mukti is not earned by ritual alone; it comes by God's grace, as the Mool Mantra says with Gur Prasad. A top-level answer never leaves mukti as a one-word definition: it gives both aspects, which is where the second explanation mark lies.","code":"3.1.7.1 — Beliefs in karma and rebirth, and the aim of mukti"},{"h":"The five khands and the barriers in the way","t":"Japji Sahib maps liberation as five khands, stages the soul passes through: Dharam Khand, the realm of duty, where a person learns right action; Gian Khand, the realm of knowledge, where understanding widens; Saram Khand, the realm of effort, where the self is refined; Karam Khand, the realm of grace, where God's grace takes over; and Sach Khand, the realm of truth, union with God. Against this the course names the barriers: illusion, self-centredness, lust, anger, greed, worldly attachment and pride. Five of these are the traditional evils, kam, krodh, lobh, moh and hankar, and all of them grow from haumai. A top-level answer can be asked for the barriers individually, so learn them by name, and pairs each barrier with the practice that counters it, such as langar against pride.","code":"3.1.7.1 — The five stages of liberation (five khands) and barriers to mukti"},{"h":"Gurmukh and manmukh: the pivot of Sikh ethics","t":"A gurmukh is God-centred, turned towards the Guru's teaching; a manmukh is man-centred, turned in on the self. The difference is haumai, pride or ego, the sense of being a separate self at the centre of things, and Sikh teaching traces every other fault back to it. This makes the distinction the hinge of the whole religion: nam japna dissolves haumai by filling the mind with God rather than self, sewa dissolves it by putting someone else first, and langar dissolves it by seating a professor beside a labourer on the same floor. A top-level answer uses gurmukh and manmukh as a measure rather than a label: it asks which of two courses of action makes a person less self-centred, and judges accordingly.","code":"3.1.7.1 — The importance of being gurmukh rather than manmukh"},{"h":"The oneness of humanity and the complete equality of all","t":"Sikh belief holds that all people are one, made by one God whose light is in every person, so distinctions of caste, wealth, race and religion have no standing before God. The course uses the word complete for the equality of women with men, and it is the word to keep: women may read the Guru Granth Sahib, lead kirtan, serve as granthi and take amrit on exactly the same terms as men. This is not a modern adjustment but the founding position of the religion, and it is why Sikhism is worth citing in any evaluation about the status of women in religion. A top-level answer gives the belief, then the practice that proves it is meant seriously, and can weigh the belief against how far particular Sikh communities live up to it.","code":"3.1.7.1 — Belief in the oneness of humanity and in the equality of all"},{"h":"Where Sikh equality is visible: Gurus, scripture, today","t":"The course asks for equality in three places. In the lives of the Gurus: Guru Nanak taught that caste counts for nothing before God and ate with people of every background; Guru Amar Das required everyone who came to see him, whatever their rank, to eat in langar first; Guru Gobind Singh founded the Khalsa with five men drawn from different castes and regions, then asked them to initiate him in turn. In the Guru Granth Sahib itself: it carries the hymns of Hindu and Muslim saints such as Kabir, Ravidas, Namdev and Sheikh Farid alongside the Gurus' own. In Sikhism today: langar, the shared floor, open doors, women leading worship. A top-level answer picks one example from each of the three places rather than three examples of the same kind.","code":"3.1.7.1 — The expression of the equality of all"},{"h":"Sewa in three forms: tan, man and dhan","t":"Sewa is selfless service, given without expecting return or reward, and the course calls it a priority rather than an extra. It takes three forms, each with its own name. Tan is physical service: cooking and serving langar, washing dishes, cleaning shoes at the gurdwara door, sweeping the floor. Man is mental service: teaching children Gurmukhi, studying and explaining the scripture, giving time, skill and thought to others. Dhan is material service: giving money and goods, traditionally a share of income, to the gurdwara, to the poor, to charities such as Khalsa Aid. All three attack haumai, because service done for praise is not sewa. A top-level answer names the form as well as the act, and says what the service does to the server as well as for the served.","code":"3.1.7.1 — Sewa: the importance and priority of service to others"},{"h":"The sangat: why a Sikh needs the community","t":"The sangat is the congregation gathered in the presence of the Guru Granth Sahib. Its role is practical and spiritual at once: it is where kirtan is sung and the scripture read aloud, where a hukam is taken, where Ardas is said, where langar is cooked and eaten together, where children learn, and where decisions about the community are made. Its importance is that Sikh teaching regards the company you keep as forming you: sitting among people who remember God makes remembering God easier, and the ordinary work of the sangat, taking your turn in the kitchen and sitting on the floor with everyone else, wears down the pride a solitary religion could feed. A top-level answer uses the sangat as the counterweight to any argument that Sikhism could be practised alone at home.","code":"3.1.7.1 — The role and importance of the sangat (religious community)"},{"h":"Inside the gurdwara: the palki, the takht and what they mean","t":"A gurdwara is any building where the Guru Granth Sahib is installed, and its design says what Sikhs believe about it. The scripture rests on the takht, a raised throne or platform, beneath the palki, a canopied structure, and is covered with romalla cloths; an attendant waves a chaur over it. Worshippers remove their shoes, cover their heads, bow to the floor before it and sit lower than it, and nobody sits with their back to it. In the morning it is brought out and opened in the Prakash ceremony and at night carried to its own room for Sukhasan. The Nishan Sahib flies outside. Every one of these practices says the same thing: the book is the living Guru. A top-level answer explains a feature by the belief it expresses, never by describing the furniture alone.","code":"3.1.7.2 — Religious features of the gurdwara"},{"h":"What a gurdwara does for a Sikh community in Britain","t":"Beyond worship, the gurdwara is the centre of a community's life, and this is where the influence question usually lands. It runs langar, so anyone hungry, Sikh or not, can eat. It teaches: Punjabi and Gurmukhi classes, kirtan lessons, classes on the Gurus for children born in Britain. It marks the turning points of family life with akhand paths for births, weddings and deaths. It offers welcome and shelter, advice and welfare, and in British towns it has often been the place where a migrant community first found its feet. Its doors are open to people of any faith or none. A top-level answer names a specific service and traces the belief behind it, so that the role of the building is shown to follow from the oneness of humanity and from sewa.","code":"3.1.7.2 — The role of the gurdwara within the Sikh community"},{"h":"Prayer in the home, and the discipline of GGS 305:4","t":"Sikh prayer is daily and domestic as well as congregational. The prescribed reference GGS 305:4 describes the gursikh who rises in the early hours, before dawn at amrit vela, and meditates on God's name, and it is the passage to cite for prayer at home. The pattern of daily prayer, nitnem, is Japji Sahib in the morning after washing, Rehras Sahib in the evening, and Kirtan Sohila before sleep, with Ardas said as the set prayer of petition. Many Sikh homes keep the Guru Granth Sahib in a room of its own, treated exactly as it is in the gurdwara; where they do not, a gutka of selected prayers is used. A top-level answer cites GGS 305:4 by its reference and then names the actual prayer said at that hour, which is what turns a statement into a detailed explanation.","code":"3.1.7.2 — The role of prayer in the home, GGS 305:4"},{"h":"The akhand path: the unbroken reading","t":"An akhand path is a continuous reading of the whole Guru Granth Sahib from beginning to end without a break, taking roughly forty-eight hours and shared by a relay of readers who take over mid-sentence so that the words never stop. It is held at gurpurbs and Vaisakhi, at the opening of a new gurdwara, and at the great moments of family life: a birth, a wedding, a death, a move to a new house. It ends with the bhog ceremony, Ardas and karah parshad. Its importance is that it makes the status of the scripture visible: the Guru is present and speaking continuously, and the whole sangat takes turns to listen and to serve. A top-level answer explains the akhand path as sewa and as a statement about the living Guru, not merely as a long reading.","code":"3.1.7.2 — The role and importance of the akhand path"},{"h":"Langar: the meal that argues","t":"Langar is the free kitchen attached to every gurdwara and the meal served there. The food is vegetarian so that nobody's diet excludes them, it is cooked and served by volunteers as tan sewa, it is free, and everyone eats sitting together on the floor in rows, the rich beside the poor and the visitor beside the granthi. The tradition goes back to Guru Nanak and was made a rule by Guru Amar Das, who would see no visitor who had not first eaten in his kitchen. Langar is the clearest single case of a belief enacted rather than stated: equality, the oneness of humanity and sewa are all performed by the act of sitting down and eating. A top-level answer uses langar as evidence in evaluations about whether action or belief matters more, and about religion's usefulness to society.","code":"3.1.7.2 — The meaning and significance of langar as an expression of sewa"},{"h":"Nam japna at home and in the gurdwara","t":"Nam japna is meditation on the name of God, one of the three duties of a Sikh alongside kirat karna, earning an honest living, and vand chhakna, sharing with others. At home it is the quiet repetition of Waheguru, often on rising at amrit vela, and the recitation of the daily prayers; in the gurdwara it is corporate, carried by kirtan sung by ragis and by the reading of the scripture aloud, so that the same remembrance is done together. Its point is the direct dismantling of haumai: a mind filled with God's name is not filled with itself, and it is by nam simran that a Sikh moves from manmukh to gurmukh. A top-level answer shows nam japna in both settings, because the course asks for both, and links it to the aim of mukti.","code":"3.1.7.2 — The significance of meditating on the name of God (nam japna)"},{"h":"Vaisakhi, Divali and the gurpurbs in Britain","t":"Three festivals are named. Vaisakhi, in April, was the Punjabi harvest festival and became the anniversary of the founding of the Khalsa by Guru Gobind Singh at Anandpur Sahib in 1699; it is kept with an akhand path, a nagar kirtan procession led by the Panj Piare, the replacing of the Nishan Sahib, and Amrit Sanskar ceremonies. Divali is kept by Sikhs as Bandi Chhor Divas, the day of release, remembering Guru Hargobind's release from imprisonment at Gwalior, when he refused to leave without the princes held with him; the Golden Temple is lit up. Gurpurbs are Guru anniversaries, above all Guru Nanak's birthday in November, marked by an akhand path completed on the day. The course asks for a comparison: in India processions fill whole streets and fall on the day; in Britain many gurdwaras hold the main celebration on the nearest weekend, with a shorter route agreed with the council. A top-level answer gives origin, significance and that British difference.","code":"3.1.7.2 — Festivals and their importance for Sikhs in Great Britain today"},{"h":"Historical gurdwaras and the Golden Temple","t":"Harimandir Sahib in Amritsar, the Golden Temple, was built under Guru Arjan, who installed the scripture in it, and it stands in the middle of a pool, the Amrit Sarovar, reached down steps and open on four sides so that people from every direction and every caste may enter. Other historical gurdwaras matter too: Nankana Sahib where Guru Nanak was born, Anandpur Sahib where the Khalsa was founded, the Akal Takht at Amritsar, Patna Sahib and Hazur Sahib. The importance is the exam question, and it is finely balanced: a visit inspires, connects a Sikh to the Gurus' own history and is a chance for sewa, but Sikhism commands no pilgrimage and Guru Nanak taught that God is not found in one place more than another. A top-level answer states the value and the limit, which is exactly what a twelve-mark evaluation on pilgrimage needs.","code":"3.1.7.2 — The importance of visiting Sikh historical gurdwaras"},{"h":"Birth and naming: the scripture names the child","t":"Soon after a birth the Mool Mantra is often whispered to the baby and a drop of amrit placed on its tongue. When mother and child are ready the family comes to the gurdwara with karah parshad; Ardas is said, amrit is prepared with a khanda, hymns of thanksgiving are sung, and then the Guru Granth Sahib is opened at random for a hukam. The first letter of the first word of that hymn is the letter the child's name must begin with, so it is the scripture, not the parents' preference, that chooses; Singh is added for a boy and Kaur for a girl. The meaning is that the child belongs to the Guru and to the whole sangat from the start, and the significance is that caste plays no part in the name. A top-level answer gives an action and the belief it carries, not a list of stages.","code":"3.1.7.2 — Birth and naming ceremonies including their meaning and significance"},{"h":"Amrit Sanskar, the Khalsa, the five Ks, and the divergence","t":"Amrit Sanskar is initiation into the Khalsa, the community Guru Gobind Singh founded at Anandpur Sahib in 1699 when he called for volunteers and initiated the Panj Piare, then asked them to initiate him. At the ceremony five initiated Sikhs prepare amrit, water with sugar crystals stirred with a khanda while five prayers are recited; it is drunk and sprinkled on the eyes and hair, the Mool Mantra is repeated, and the candidate accepts the Rehat Maryada, the Sikh code of conduct, whose four kurahits forbid cutting the hair, eating kutha meat, extramarital sexual relations and using tobacco, other intoxicants being forbidden elsewhere in the Rehat Maryada. Members wear the five Ks: kesh, uncut hair; kangha, the comb; kara, the steel bracelet; kachhera, the cotton undershorts; kirpan, the sword. The specified divergence is here: amritdhari Sikhs have taken amrit and keep the Ks, while sahajdhari Sikhs follow Sikh teaching without initiation. A top-level answer presents both as genuinely Sikh positions, then judges.","code":"3.1.7.2 — The initiation ceremony (Amrit Sanskar)"},{"h":"Singh and Kaur: the names that abolish caste","t":"At the founding of the Khalsa, Sikh men took the name Singh, lion, and Sikh women the name Kaur, princess. The use is straightforward: they are given at a naming ceremony and confirmed at Amrit Sanskar, and many Sikhs use them as a middle or last name. The significance is sharp. Indian family names announced caste, so replacing them with one shared name for all men and one for all women declared that caste has no standing among Sikhs. Kaur does more: because a woman keeps Kaur rather than taking a husband's family name, her identity is her own and not held through a man, which is the complete equality of women with men made a matter of everyday paperwork. A top-level answer explains the name by the belief, and can use Kaur as evidence in any argument about women and religion.","code":"3.1.7.2 — The significance and use of the names Singh and Kaur"}],"examples":[{"title":"The one-mark question: reading four Sikh terms","setup":"Which one of the following means the free kitchen attached to a gurdwara? A langar  B kirtan  C sewa  D sangat. Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. [1 mark]","steps":["Translate the options you are not being asked about first: kirtan is the singing of hymns, sewa is selfless service of any kind, sangat is the congregation of worshippers.","Test each translation against the two things the question specifies, a kitchen and a meal given free: singing is not a kitchen, service is wider than a meal, and a congregation is people rather than food.","That leaves the term that names the meal itself, so tick A and record it the way the mark scheme records it, letter with word: Answer: A langar."],"cues":["What do the three options you are not choosing actually mean?","Which of those meanings can be ruled out by the words kitchen and free?","How should the chosen answer be written down?"]},{"title":"The two-mark question: giving two, and stopping","setup":"Give two of the five Ks. [2 marks] You are told the examiner marks only the first two answers on the page, wherever they appear.","steps":["Bring back the whole set before choosing, so that you are picking rather than guessing: kesh, kangha, kara, kachhera, kirpan.","Choose the two you can spell and write them bare, for example kesh and kara; no explanation is asked for here and none is rewarded.","Resist adding a third as insurance, because a correct third item written after two weak ones cannot rescue the answer, and then move straight on."],"cues":["What is the full set this question draws from?","How much writing does the question actually want?","Why is an extra answer a bad idea here?"]},{"title":"The four-mark contrast: gurpurbs in India and in Britain","setup":"Explain two contrasting ways in which gurpurbs are celebrated in India and in Great Britain. [4 marks] Each contrast is marked out of two: a simple explanation of an accurate contrast earns one mark, a detailed explanation earns both.","steps":["Open the first contrast on the Indian side with a concrete practice: a nagar kirtan led by the Panj Piare processes through the streets of a city such as Amritsar, where much of the crowd is Sikh and the whole town joins in.","Complete that contrast on the British side and develop it: in Britain the procession is a shorter local route agreed in advance with the council and the police, so the gurpurb is more a display within the neighbourhood of one gurdwara than a city-wide event.","Open a genuinely different second contrast, this time about timing: in India the akhand path and the celebration fall on the gurpurb itself, whatever day of the week that is.","Complete and develop it: many British gurdwaras move the main celebration to the nearest weekend so that people at work or school can attend, which keeps numbers high but separates the feast from the anniversary."],"cues":["What does a gurpurb look like in a Punjabi city?","What must change when that same procession happens in a British town, and why?","What second difference is worth taking, and what does it turn on?","Why would a gurdwara here shift the date, and what does it gain and lose?"]},{"title":"The five-mark question: two teachings plus the source","setup":"Explain two Sikh teachings about the nature of God. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Sikh belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks] Four of the marks are two explained teachings; the fifth is a separate mark for one accurate reference.","steps":["State the first teaching and develop it into a consequence: God is one, so Sikhs reject many gods and refuse to picture God in any image, which is why a gurdwara contains no statue and Sikhs bow instead to the scripture.","Make the second teaching genuinely different rather than a restatement: God is both in and through the universe and separate from it, so a Sikh may see God's light in every person while still holding that God is beyond form and beyond understanding.","Place the reference so the examiner cannot miss it, with book and place: the Mool Mantra at Guru Granth Sahib 1a opens Ik Onkar, Sat Nam, Karta Purakh, and calls God Nirbhau and Nirvair, without fear and without hatred.","Audit the shape against the question before moving on: two teachings, each carried past a bare statement, and one named source, with nothing extra written in the hope of luck."],"cues":["Which teaching about God is easiest to develop into something Sikhs do?","What second teaching about God says something the first did not?","Where is the reference that earns the separate mark, and how is it named?","What should be checked before the time runs out?"]},{"title":"The twelve-mark evaluation: judging a statement about pilgrimage","setup":"'Sikhs should visit the Golden Temple at least once.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Sikh teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks]","steps":["Build the supporting case on named content rather than feeling: Harimandir Sahib at Amritsar was built under Guru Arjan, who installed the scripture in it, so a visit stands a Sikh in the place where the Guru Granth Sahib was first enthroned.","Carry that argument into influence, which is what lifts reasoning into a chain: hearing kirtan sung continuously beside the Amrit Sarovar and taking a turn in the langar there can strengthen the nam japna and the sewa a Sikh goes home to.","Turn the answer with a divergent Sikh view, not a vague objection: Guru Nanak taught that God is not confined to holy places, and the Guru Granth Sahib is the living Guru just as fully in a gurdwara in Southall, so no journey is commanded.","Press the practical objection so the other side is reasoned too: Sikhism lays down no obligatory pilgrimage, and the cost of the flight could instead feed a langar or fund a charity, which Sikh teaching treats as the priority.","Judge, and say on what grounds: the visit is valuable but never required, so the word should is too strong, and a Sikh who lives as gurmukh and serves the sangat at home is nearer the Gurus' teaching than one who travels and neglects both."],"cues":["What can be said for the statement using a named place and a named Guru?","What difference could the visit make to how a Sikh lives afterwards?","Which Sikh teaching cuts against the statement?","What is the strongest practical objection to spending the money on travel?","Which side is stronger, and what is the reason for saying so?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Where does the Mool Mantra appear, and what reference does the course prescribe for it?","a":"At the very opening of the Guru Granth Sahib, prescribed as GGS 1a."},{"q":"Give the negative and the positive aspect of mukti.","a":"Negatively, release from karma and the cycle of rebirth; positively, union of the soul with God."},{"q":"What is the difference between an amritdhari and a sahajdhari Sikh?","a":"An amritdhari has taken amrit at the Amrit Sanskar and keeps the five Ks and the Rehat Maryada; a sahajdhari follows Sikh teaching without having been initiated."},{"q":"Name the three forms of sewa and give an example of each.","a":"Tan, physical, such as cooking langar; man, mental, such as teaching Gurmukhi to children; dhan, material, such as giving money to the gurdwara or to Khalsa Aid."},{"q":"Why do Sikhs bow to the Guru Granth Sahib but keep no image of God in the gurdwara?","a":"Because God is separate from the universe and beyond form, so cannot be pictured; the scripture is honoured as the living Guru, God's word, which rests on the takht beneath the palki."}]},"room":{"facts":["The Mool Mantra stands at Guru Granth Sahib 1a and opens Ik Onkar, Sat Nam, Karta Purakh: one God, whose name is truth, the creator, without fear, without hatred, beyond time, unborn, self-existent, known by the Guru's grace.","The three daily duties are nam japna, meditating on God's name, kirat karna, earning an honest living, and vand chhakna, sharing with others; sewa is given as tan, man and dhan.","Guru Gobind Singh founded the Khalsa at Anandpur Sahib at Vaisakhi in 1699; the first five initiated are the Panj Piare, and the five Ks are kesh, kangha, kara, kachhera and kirpan.","GGS 305:4 is the reference prescribed for prayer in the home: the gursikh rises in the early hours at amrit vela and meditates on God's name; the daily prayers are Japji Sahib, Rehras Sahib and Kirtan Sohila.","The five-part question runs 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks in that order, once on Beliefs and teachings and once on Practices, and the 5-mark question is marked two plus two plus one, the last mark being a separate mark for an accurate reference to a source of Sikh belief and teaching.","Allow about a minute a mark: roughly four minutes on the 4-mark question, five on the 5-mark and twelve on the evaluation, which is the only question carrying spelling, punctuation and grammar marks and does so in the Beliefs section."],"questions":[{"q":"Which one of the following means God-centred? A manmukh  B gurmukh  C haumai  D sahajdhari. Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. [1 mark]","a":"Answer: B gurmukh. AO1:1, one mark for the correct box only.","sol":"B gurmukh. A gurmukh is the Sikh who is turned towards the Guru's teaching and so towards God. The distractors are the three terms it is most often confused with: manmukh is the opposite, the man-centred or self-centred person; haumai is the pride or ego that makes someone manmukh; and sahajdhari names a Sikh who has not taken amrit, which is a different distinction altogether.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["This tests the Sikh contrast between a life turned towards God and a life turned towards the self; work out which pair of terms names that contrast.","Translate all four options before choosing, rather than recognising one.","Write the answer as the mark scheme does, the letter together with the word."],"codes":["3.1.7.1 — The importance of being gurmukh rather than manmukh"]},{"q":"Which one of the following is found at the very opening of the Guru Granth Sahib? A Ardas  B Rehras Sahib  C Mool Mantra  D Kirtan Sohila. Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. [1 mark]","a":"Answer: C Mool Mantra. AO1:1, one mark.","sol":"C Mool Mantra. The Mool Mantra is the opening statement of the scripture, prescribed as GGS 1a, and it begins Japji Sahib. The others are Sikh prayers but not the opening of the book: Ardas is the set prayer of petition that closes congregational worship, Rehras Sahib is said in the evening, and Kirtan Sohila is said before sleep.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The question is about what stands on the opening page of the Guru Granth Sahib at GGS 1a; recall what each of the four named texts is and where it belongs.","Sort the options into prayers said at set times and things that are not prayers at all.","Give the letter and the term together in one short line."],"codes":["3.1.7.1 — The nature of God as expressed in the Mool Mantra"]},{"q":"Which one of the following is the name taken by Sikh women? A Singh  B Kaur  C Khalsa  D Panj Piare. Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. [1 mark]","a":"Answer: B Kaur. AO1:1, one mark.","sol":"B Kaur. Kaur means princess and is taken by Sikh women in place of a caste-bearing family name, so that a woman's identity is not held through her husband. Singh, meaning lion, is the corresponding name for men; Khalsa is the community of initiated Sikhs itself; and Panj Piare are the five beloved ones first initiated by Guru Gobind Singh.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Two of the options are names people carry; two are names of groups.","Once the two group names are set aside, decide which of the remaining pair belongs to women.","Answer in the mark scheme's form, letter then word."],"codes":["3.1.7.2 — The significance and use of the names Singh and Kaur"]},{"q":"Give two of the five Ks. [2 marks]","a":"One mark for each of two correct items from kesh, kangha, kara, kachhera, kirpan. AO1:1. Only the first two answers are marked.","sol":"Kesh, uncut hair, and kara, the steel bracelet worn on the wrist. Any two of kesh, kangha, kara, kachhera and kirpan earn the marks. No explanation is required or rewarded here, and only the first two answers written are considered, so a third item added afterwards cannot make up for a wrong one.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["They are the articles an amritdhari Sikh wears or carries after initiation.","Recall the whole set first, then choose the two you can spell correctly.","Two bare items on one line is the entire answer."],"codes":["3.1.7.2 — The initiation ceremony (Amrit Sanskar)"]},{"q":"Give two forms of sewa. [2 marks]","a":"One mark each for two of tan (physical), man (mental), dhan (material). AO1:1.","sol":"Tan, physical service such as cooking and serving in langar, and dhan, material service such as giving money or goods to the gurdwara and the poor. The third form is man, mental service, such as teaching Gurmukhi or studying and explaining the scripture. Naming two of the three is enough; the question asks for no development.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The course names the three forms with their own Punjabi words.","Think what a person actually has to give: body, mind, or possessions.","Name two, with the English meaning in brackets if you wish, and stop."],"codes":["3.1.7.1 — Sewa: the importance and priority of service to others"]},{"q":"Give two things that happen at a Sikh naming ceremony. [2 marks]","a":"One mark each for two accurate elements, for example: the Guru Granth Sahib is opened at random for a hukam; the first letter of the first word gives the child's initial; Singh or Kaur is added; Ardas is said; amrit is prepared; karah parshad is shared. AO1:1.","sol":"The Guru Granth Sahib is opened at random and the first letter of the first word of the hymn gives the letter the child's name must begin with. Singh is then added for a boy and Kaur for a girl. Other creditable points include the saying of Ardas, the preparing of amrit with a khanda, the singing of hymns of thanksgiving and the sharing of karah parshad with the sangat.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The scripture itself has a part in choosing the name.","Walk through the visit to the gurdwara in order and pick two moments from it.","Two short statements of what is done are all that is needed."],"codes":["3.1.7.2 — Birth and naming ceremonies including their meaning and significance"]},{"q":"Explain two Sikh teachings about the nature of God. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Sikh belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"Two plus two for two teachings explained, for example the oneness of God and God as both within and beyond creation, plus one mark for an accurate reference such as the Mool Mantra at GGS 1a. AO1:1.","sol":"Sikhs teach that God is one. The Mool Mantra opens the Guru Granth Sahib at GGS 1a with Ik Onkar, meaning there is one God, so Sikhs reject the worship of many gods and refuse to represent God in any image; this is why a gurdwara holds no statue and worshippers bow instead to the scripture. Secondly, Sikhs teach that God is both shown in and through the universe and separate from it. God's light is held to be in every person, which is why service to others counts as devotion, yet God is also beyond time and beyond form, called in the same passage Nirbhau and Nirvair, without fear and without hatred, and Ajuni, not born, so God can never be fully grasped by human minds.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The most quotable source in this room sits where a reader of the scripture starts.","Two teachings that say different things, each carried one step past the bare statement into what Sikhs consequently do or deny.","Shape it as teaching, development, teaching, development, then the reference named with its place."],"codes":["3.1.7.1 — The nature of God as expressed in the Mool Mantra","3.1.7.1 — God as Creator"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting Sikh views about taking amrit. [4 marks]","a":"Two plus two for two genuinely contrasting views, for example the amritdhari view that initiation is the full commitment expected of a Sikh, against the sahajdhari view that Sikh teaching can be followed without it. AO1:3. Similar views are credited once only.","sol":"Many Sikhs hold that taking amrit is what a Sikh life is aiming at. Amritdhari Sikhs are initiated by the Panj Piare as Guru Gobind Singh established at Anandpur Sahib, wear the five Ks and keep the Rehat Maryada, and see this as obeying the tenth Guru directly rather than choosing an optional extra. Other Sikhs take a different view. Sahajdhari Sikhs follow the Gurus' teaching, attend the gurdwara, perform sewa and meditate on God's name without being initiated, holding that what matters is the inner change from manmukh to gurmukh rather than outward signs, and that a person should take amrit only when ready to keep the vows rather than break them.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The course names two Sikh perspectives that differ precisely on this question.","Set them against each other from the first words of each paragraph, and give each one a reason as well as a label.","Two short paragraphs, one view each, with no reference needed and no conclusion."],"codes":["3.1.7.2 — The initiation ceremony (Amrit Sanskar)","3.1.7 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Sikhism"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which belief in the equality of all influences Sikhs today. [4 marks]","a":"Two plus two for two influences, for example langar eaten together on the floor, and women reading the scripture and leading worship. AO1:2. The second mark in each case requires the influence itself, not just the belief.","sol":"Because Sikhs believe all people are one before God, every gurdwara runs a langar in which the food is vegetarian and free and everyone sits together on the floor to eat, so that a wealthy visitor and a homeless stranger are physically placed at the same level; many British gurdwaras extend this by taking meals out to the street. Secondly, because the equality of women with men is complete, Sikh women read the Guru Granth Sahib in public worship, lead kirtan, serve as granthi and take amrit on the same terms as men, and a woman keeps the name Kaur rather than taking her husband's family name, so her standing in the community does not depend on his.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The question wants what Sikhs do, not what Sikhs think.","Pick one influence inside the gurdwara building and one about people's status, so the two do not overlap.","Each paragraph must finish on an action or a change, because that is where the second mark lives."],"codes":["3.1.7.1 — Belief in the oneness of humanity and in the equality of all","3.1.7.1 — The expression of the equality of all"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting ways in which gurpurbs are celebrated in India and in Great Britain. [4 marks]","a":"Two plus two for two genuine contrasts, for example the scale and route of the nagar kirtan, and the day on which the main celebration is held. AO1:3.","sol":"In India a gurpurb such as Guru Nanak's birthday is marked by a nagar kirtan that processes for miles through towns and cities led by the Panj Piare, with the streets decorated and free food given out along the route, because much of the surrounding population is Sikh. In Britain the same procession is usually a shorter local route around the area of one gurdwara, agreed in advance with the council and the police, so the celebration is more contained. Secondly, in India the akhand path is completed and the celebration held on the anniversary itself; in Britain many gurdwaras move the main event to the nearest weekend so that people at work and school can attend.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The course asks for this comparison by name, so it expects two real differences rather than one difference stated twice.","One contrast can be about the public event outside the gurdwara and one about when it happens.","Write each contrast as a pair of clauses, India then Britain, each side developed with a reason."],"codes":["3.1.7.2 — Festivals and their importance for Sikhs in Great Britain today"]},{"q":"Explain two Sikh beliefs about the purpose of human life. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Sikh belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"Two plus two for two beliefs, for example human birth as the opportunity to unite with God and the development of virtues, and mukti as release from rebirth and union with God, plus one mark for a reference such as the five khands of Japji Sahib or Gur Prasad in the Mool Mantra at GGS 1a. AO1:1.","sol":"Sikhs believe human life is the opportunity to unite with God. Of all the forms a soul passes through in the cycle of karma and rebirth, only human birth allows a person to turn deliberately towards God, which is why wasting it is serious and why the virtues named in Sikh teaching, such as truthful living, humility and contentment, are to be developed now. Secondly, Sikhs believe the purpose of that life is mukti, which has two sides: negatively it is release from karma and the endless round of rebirth, and positively it is the union of the soul with God, sometimes reached in this life as jivan mukt. Japji Sahib maps the journey as five khands ending in Sach Khand, the realm of truth, and the Mool Mantra at GGS 1a adds Gur Prasad, that it comes by grace.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["One belief is about what this life is for; the other is about what it is aiming at.","The second belief has two sides, and a bare definition of it will not reach the fuller explanation.","Two developed paragraphs, then a named source with its place, and nothing else."],"codes":["3.1.7.1 — The nature of human life as an opportunity to unite with God","3.1.7.1 — Beliefs in karma and rebirth, and the aim of mukti","3.1.7.1 — The five stages of liberation (five khands) and barriers to mukti"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which Sikhs practise their faith at home. Refer to sacred writings or another source of Sikh belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"Two plus two for two ways, for example daily prayer at amrit vela and nam japna through the day or keeping the Guru Granth Sahib in its own room, plus one mark for a reference such as GGS 305:4. AO1:1.","sol":"Sikhs pray at home daily. The passage at GGS 305:4 describes the gursikh rising in the early hours, at amrit vela, and meditating on God's name, and the daily pattern follows it: Japji Sahib after washing in the morning, Rehras Sahib in the evening and Kirtan Sohila before sleep, with Ardas said when something is asked of God. Secondly, Sikhs practise nam japna through the ordinary day, repeating Waheguru quietly while working or travelling, because a mind occupied with God's name is not occupied with itself, and this is how haumai is worn down between visits to the gurdwara. Where a family has space, the Guru Granth Sahib is kept in a room of its own and treated exactly as it is in the gurdwara.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["One of the two prescribed references in this room belongs to exactly this question.","Distinguish set prayers said at fixed hours from the remembrance that runs through the day.","Two developed ways, then the reference given by book and place so the examiner cannot miss it."],"codes":["3.1.7.2 — The role of prayer in the home, GGS 305:4","3.1.7.2 — The significance of meditating on the name of God (nam japna)"]},{"q":"'The gurdwara is the most important place in a Sikh's life.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Sikh teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks]","a":"AO2, levels of response on the whole answer. Support: the Guru Granth Sahib is installed there and treated as the living Guru; the sangat, kirtan, hukam and Ardas; langar and sewa; the community roles of teaching, welfare and welcome. Other views: the home and daily nitnem and nam japna; God is not confined to places; any building with the scripture installed is a gurdwara; the inner change from manmukh to gurmukh is what counts.","sol":"Much can be said for the statement. What makes a gurdwara a gurdwara is that the Guru Granth Sahib is installed in it, resting on the takht beneath the palki, and Sikhs treat that book as the living Guru: they remove their shoes, cover their heads, bow to the floor before it and never sit higher than it. If the Guru is present there in a way not possible elsewhere, then the building is where a Sikh meets the Guru most directly, and the taking of a hukam, the singing of kirtan and the saying of Ardas all happen in that presence. The gurdwara is also where the sangat gathers, and Sikh teaching regards the company you keep as forming you; sitting among people who remember God makes remembering God easier than doing it alone. Add langar, where the equality of all and the priority of sewa are not stated but performed, and the education, welfare and welcome a British gurdwara offers, and the case is strong.\n\nA different Sikh view answers that the religion deliberately refuses to make any place holy in itself. Guru Nanak taught that God is not confined to particular places, and the Mool Mantra at GGS 1a describes a God beyond time and beyond form who cannot be housed. A gurdwara is holy only because the scripture is in it, which is why a room in an ordinary house becomes a gurdwara once the Guru Granth Sahib is installed there, and why Sikhism commands no pilgrimage even to Harimandir Sahib. On this view the home is at least as important: GGS 305:4 describes the gursikh rising in the early hours to meditate on God's name, and nitnem, nam japna, kirat karna and vand chhakna are all done outside the building. What finally decides a life, Sikhs hold, is whether a person is gurmukh or manmukh, and a Sikh can attend every week and remain full of haumai while another lives honestly and serves without ever leading worship.\n\nWeighing these, the second argument is the stronger on the wording of the statement. The gurdwara is indispensable in practice, because sangat, langar and public sewa cannot be reproduced alone, and a Sikhism kept entirely at home would lose the equality it enacts by seating everyone on one floor. But importance in Sikh teaching attaches to the Guru and to the state of the heart, not to bricks; the building matters because of what it contains and what it makes possible. So the gurdwara is the most important place a Sikh goes, while the most important thing in a Sikh's life is not a place at all.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Ask first what actually makes a building a gurdwara, because the answer to that shapes both sides.","The strongest opposing case is a Sikh one, drawn from teaching about where God is and from what Sikhs do at home.","Two developed arguments each way, each anchored in named Sikh teaching, then a conclusion that says which side won and why."],"codes":["3.1.7.2 — Religious features of the gurdwara","3.1.7.2 — The role of the gurdwara within the Sikh community","3.1.7.1 — The role and importance of the sangat (religious community)","3.1.7.2 — The role of prayer in the home, GGS 305:4","3.1.7.2 — The meaning and significance of langar as an expression of sewa"]},{"q":"'Sikhs should visit the Golden Temple at least once.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Sikh teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks]","a":"AO2, levels of response on the whole answer. Support: Harimandir Sahib built under Guru Arjan, who installed the scripture there; continuous kirtan; the four doors and the langar as equality enacted; historical link to the Gurus; opportunity for sewa. Other views: no obligatory pilgrimage in Sikhism; Guru Nanak taught God is not confined to places; cost and accessibility; the money could serve the poor; the living Guru is equally present in any gurdwara.","sol":"There is a real case for the statement. Harimandir Sahib in Amritsar was built under Guru Arjan, who installed the scripture in it, so a Sikh who goes there stands where the Guru Granth Sahib was first enthroned and where much of Sikh history since has happened. The building itself teaches: it is reached by going down rather than up, it stands in the middle of the Amrit Sarovar, and it is open on four sides so that people of every caste and direction may enter, which makes the oneness of humanity visible in architecture. Kirtan is sung there almost without pause and one of the largest langars in the world is cooked and served by volunteers, so a visit is not only inspiring but an opportunity for tan sewa that few Sikhs get at home. Many return with their nam japna and their commitment to the sangat strengthened, and for Sikhs born in Britain it can make a religion learned in classes feel like their own history.\n\nAgainst this, Sikh teaching itself resists the word should. Sikhism lays down no obligatory pilgrimage, unlike the Hajj in Islam, and Guru Nanak taught that God is not confined to particular places; the Mool Mantra at GGS 1a describes a God beyond form, who cannot be more present in Amritsar than in Amritsar's poorest street. What makes any building a gurdwara is that the Guru Granth Sahib is installed in it, so the living Guru is as fully present in a gurdwara in Southall or Smethwick. There are practical objections too. Flights to India are expensive, and Sikh teaching makes dhan sewa a priority, so the cost of a trip might feed a langar for weeks or fund a charity such as Khalsa Aid. Elderly, poor or disabled Sikhs cannot travel, and a religion built on the complete equality of all should not make spiritual standing depend on an airfare.\n\nOn balance the statement is too strong, though it points at something true. The visit plainly has value, and a Sikh who can go and does will very likely come home a better Sikh for it, so a recommendation would be defensible. But should implies obligation, and obligation is precisely what the Gurus refused to attach to places; the same money and the same weeks given to sewa at home would serve the teaching more directly. The honest conclusion is that visiting the Golden Temple is a privilege worth seeking, not a duty a Sikh fails by never fulfilling, and the test of a Sikh life remains whether it is lived gurmukh rather than manmukh.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Begin by asking what Sikhism actually requires of its members, and whether pilgrimage is on that list.","Build the supporting case from the place itself: who built it, what it looks like, and what happens there daily.","Two developed arguments each way, then a conclusion that takes a clear position on the word should and gives its grounds."],"codes":["3.1.7.2 — The importance of visiting Sikh historical gurdwaras","3.1.7 — Sources of wisdom and authority, influence, and common and divergent views within Sikhism","3.1.7.1 — Sewa: the importance and priority of service to others"]},{"q":"'For a Sikh, serving others matters more than worshipping God.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you should: refer to Sikh teaching; give reasoned arguments to support this statement; give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels of response on the whole answer. Support: sewa as tan, man and dhan given priority; langar as sewa enacted; God's light in every person so service is devotion; vand chhakna as a daily duty. Other views: nam japna as the direct remedy for haumai; the akhand path and kirtan; mukti comes by grace, Gur Prasad; the three duties are held together, and sewa without remembrance can feed pride.","sol":"The statement has real support in Sikh teaching. Sewa is described as a priority rather than an optional kindness, and it is organised into three named forms, tan, man and dhan, so that everyone has something to give: labour, skill or money. Its clearest expression, langar, is attached to every gurdwara, and there the equality of all is not preached but performed, as anyone who comes eats free, sitting on the floor in the same line as everyone else. The reason is doctrinal rather than merely humane: Sikhs hold that God's light is in every person, so serving a stranger is serving God, and vand chhakna, sharing with others, stands beside nam japna as a daily duty. Sikh charities such as Khalsa Aid, which take langar to disaster zones, show that many Sikhs read their faith exactly this way, and it is arguable that hours spent singing would be better spent cooking.\n\nA different Sikh view puts remembrance first. Sikh teaching traces every fault to haumai, pride or ego, and the direct remedy for haumai is nam japna, meditation on God's name; the passage at GGS 305:4 describes the gursikh rising in the early hours to do exactly this. Without it, service can become self-service, a way of being admired, and service done for praise is by definition not sewa. Worship also sustains the community that makes service possible: the akhand path, the continuous reading of the whole Guru Granth Sahib, and the kirtan of the sangat are what keep a gurdwara a place where langar is cooked at all. Above all, mukti is not bought by good works. The Mool Mantra at GGS 1a ends Gur Prasad, by the Guru's grace, and the five khands of Japji Sahib reach Sach Khand only through Karam Khand, the realm of grace, so no amount of service earns union with God.\n\nThe better conclusion is that the statement sets apart two things Sikh teaching deliberately joins. The three duties, nam japna, kirat karna and vand chhakna, are given together, and each guards the other: remembrance without service is empty, and service without remembrance becomes another form of pride. If forced to choose, the divergent view is the stronger, because nam japna addresses the root problem, haumai, which is what makes selfless service possible in the first place. So serving others does not matter more than worship; it is what worship turns into when it is real, which is why langar stands next to the prayer hall and not instead of it.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["The three duties of a Sikh are the obvious place to start, because the statement separates two of them.","The strongest reply is not that worship is nicer but that it treats the cause of the problem Sikh teaching names.","Two developed arguments each way with named Sikh teaching in both, then a conclusion that either chooses a side or explains why the choice is false, with reasons."],"codes":["3.1.7.1 — Sewa: the importance and priority of service to others","3.1.7.2 — The meaning and significance of langar as an expression of sewa","3.1.7.2 — The significance of meditating on the name of God (nam japna)","3.1.7.2 — The role and importance of the akhand path","3.1.7.1 — The role and importance of the sangat (religious community)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What does Ik Onkar mean, and where in the scripture does it stand?","a":"There is one God; it opens the Mool Mantra at Guru Granth Sahib 1a, the first words of the scripture."},{"q":"Give the two aspects of mukti.","a":"Negative: release from karma and the cycle of rebirth. Positive: union of the soul with God."},{"q":"Name the three forms of sewa.","a":"Tan, physical service; man, mental service; dhan, material service."},{"q":"What is the difference between an amritdhari and a sahajdhari Sikh?","a":"An amritdhari has taken amrit at the Amrit Sanskar, wears the five Ks and keeps the Rehat Maryada; a sahajdhari follows Sikh teaching without having been initiated."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The Mool Mantra, Guru Granth Sahib 1a","back":"The opening statement of the scripture. It begins Ik Onkar, Sat Nam, Karta Purakh: one God, whose name is truth, the creator, without fear, without hatred, beyond time, unborn, self-existent, known by the Guru's grace.","code":"3.1.7.1 — The nature of God as expressed in the Mool Mantra","open":false},{"front":"God as Creator in Sikh belief","back":"Two aspects held together: God is shown in and through the universe, so God's light is in every person; and God is separate from the universe, beyond form, so no image of God is kept in a gurdwara.","code":"3.1.7.1 — God as Creator","open":false},{"front":"One of the Sikh virtues","back":"Wisdom; truthful living; justice; temperance; self-control; patience; courage; humility; contentment. They are developed in human life, which Sikhs regard as the opportunity to unite with God.","code":"3.1.7.1 — The nature of human life as an opportunity to unite with God","open":true},{"front":"Mukti","back":"Liberation. Negatively, release from karma and the cycle of rebirth; positively, union of the soul with God. A Sikh who reaches it while still alive is jivan mukt. It comes by grace.","code":"3.1.7.1 — Beliefs in karma and rebirth, and the aim of mukti","open":false},{"front":"The five khands","back":"The stages of liberation in Japji Sahib: Dharam Khand, duty; Gian Khand, knowledge; Saram Khand, effort; Karam Khand, grace; Sach Khand, truth and union with God.","code":"3.1.7.1 — The five stages of liberation (five khands) and barriers to mukti","open":false},{"front":"Gurmukh and manmukh","back":"Gurmukh means God-centred, living by the Guru's teaching. Manmukh means man-centred, ruled by haumai, pride or ego. Dismantling haumai is the aim of nam japna, sewa and langar alike.","code":"3.1.7.1 — The importance of being gurmukh rather than manmukh","open":false},{"front":"The complete equality of women with men","back":"Sikh belief holds all humanity one before God. Women read the Guru Granth Sahib, lead kirtan, serve as granthi and take amrit on the same terms as men, and keep the name Kaur.","code":"3.1.7.1 — Belief in the oneness of humanity and in the equality of all","open":false},{"front":"Sewa: tan, man and dhan","back":"Selfless service in three forms. Tan: physical work such as cooking langar or cleaning shoes. Man: mind and skill, such as teaching Gurmukhi. Dhan: money and goods given to others.","code":"3.1.7.1 — Sewa: the importance and priority of service to others","open":false},{"front":"The sangat","back":"The congregation gathered before the Guru Granth Sahib: kirtan is sung, a hukam taken, Ardas said, langar eaten together and community decisions made. The company you keep forms you.","code":"3.1.7.1 — The role and importance of the sangat (religious community)","open":false},{"front":"The palki and the takht","back":"In the gurdwara the Guru Granth Sahib rests on the takht, a raised throne, beneath the palki, a canopy, covered by romalla cloths, with a chaur waved over it. The book is the living Guru.","code":"3.1.7.2 — Religious features of the gurdwara","open":false},{"front":"The akhand path","back":"An unbroken reading of the whole Guru Granth Sahib, about forty-eight hours, by readers in relay. Held at gurpurbs, Vaisakhi, births, weddings and deaths, ending with bhog and karah parshad.","code":"3.1.7.2 — The role and importance of the akhand path","open":false},{"front":"Langar","back":"The free kitchen at every gurdwara. Vegetarian food, cooked and served by volunteers as sewa, eaten sitting together on the floor by anyone who comes. Equality performed, not stated.","code":"3.1.7.2 — The meaning and significance of langar as an expression of sewa","open":false},{"front":"Nam japna","back":"Meditating on God's name, often repeating Waheguru. One of three duties with kirat karna and vand chhakna. Done at home at amrit vela and in the gurdwara through kirtan.","code":"3.1.7.2 — The significance of meditating on the name of God (nam japna)","open":false},{"front":"Vaisakhi","back":"The Punjabi harvest festival, and the day in 1699 when Guru Gobind Singh founded the Khalsa at Anandpur Sahib. Kept with an akhand path, a nagar kirtan, a new Nishan Sahib and Amrit Sanskar.","code":"3.1.7.2 — Festivals and their importance for Sikhs in Great Britain today","open":false},{"front":"Amrit Sanskar and the five Ks","back":"Initiation into the Khalsa by five initiated Sikhs. Amrit of water and sugar crystals, stirred with a khanda, is drunk and sprinkled. The initiated wear kesh, kangha, kara, kachhera and kirpan.","code":"3.1.7.2 — The initiation ceremony (Amrit Sanskar)","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Sikh beliefs and teachings: the named sources, figures and terms","items":["Guru Granth Sahib 1a — the prescribed reference for the Mool Mantra, the opening statement of the scripture: Ik Onkar, Sat Nam, Karta Purakh, and God as Nirbhau, Nirvair, beyond time, unborn, self-existent, Gur Prasad.","Ik Onkar — there is one God. The phrase and its symbol are the commonest Sikh emblem after the khanda.","God as Creator — shown in and through the universe, and separate from the universe; both aspects are required by the course.","The nine virtues named for development in human life — wisdom, truthful living, justice, temperance, self-control, patience, courage, humility, contentment.","Karma and rebirth — actions carry consequences and the soul passes through many lives; human birth is the opportunity to escape the cycle.","Mukti — liberation. Negative aspect: release from karma and rebirth. Positive aspect: union of the soul with God. Jivan mukt: liberated while still alive.","The five khands, from Japji Sahib — Dharam Khand (duty), Gian Khand (knowledge), Saram Khand (effort), Karam Khand (grace), Sach Khand (truth, union with God).","The barriers to mukti named by the course — illusion, self-centredness, lust, anger, greed, worldly attachment, pride; the five traditional evils are kam, krodh, lobh, moh and hankar.","Haumai — pride or ego, the root fault; gurmukh means God-centred, manmukh means man-centred.","Guru Nanak (1469-1539) — the first Guru, who taught the oneness of God and rejected caste distinction, and whose birthday is the best known gurpurb.","Guru Amar Das — the third Guru, who required every visitor, whatever their rank, to eat in langar before seeing him.","Guru Arjan — the fifth Guru, who compiled the Adi Granth and installed it in Harimandir Sahib at Amritsar.","Guru Gobind Singh — the tenth Guru, who founded the Khalsa at Anandpur Sahib at Vaisakhi in 1699 with the Panj Piare, and who ended the line of human Gurus by giving that authority to the scripture.","The Guru Granth Sahib — the living Guru, 1430 angs, containing the hymns of Sikh Gurus alongside those of Hindu and Muslim saints such as Kabir, Ravidas, Namdev and Sheikh Farid, which is itself evidence of the oneness of humanity.","Sewa — tan (physical), man (mental), dhan (material); called a priority of Sikh life, not an extra.","The sangat — the congregation before the Guru Granth Sahib: kirtan, hukam, Ardas, langar and community decisions.","The three daily duties — nam japna, kirat karna, vand chhakna."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — Sikh practices: gurdwara, prayer, festivals and ceremonies","items":["The gurdwara — any building in which the Guru Granth Sahib is installed; the Nishan Sahib, the saffron flag bearing the khanda, flies outside.","The palki and the takht — the scripture rests on the takht, a raised throne, beneath the palki, a canopied structure, covered with romalla cloths; a chaur is waved over it.","Practices in the prayer hall — shoes removed, heads covered, bowing to the floor before the scripture, sitting lower than it and never with the back to it; Prakash in the morning and Sukhasan at night.","The gurdwara's community role — worship, langar, Punjabi and Gurmukhi classes, kirtan lessons, welfare and advice, shelter, and welcome to people of any faith or none.","Guru Granth Sahib 305:4 — the prescribed reference for prayer in the home: the gursikh rises in the early hours, at amrit vela, and meditates on God's name.","Nitnem, the daily prayers — Japji Sahib in the morning, Rehras Sahib in the evening, Kirtan Sohila before sleep; Ardas is the set prayer of petition.","The akhand path — a continuous reading of the whole scripture, about forty-eight hours, by readers in relay, ending with bhog, Ardas and karah parshad.","Langar — the free vegetarian meal at every gurdwara, cooked and served by volunteers, eaten sitting together on the floor; traced to Guru Nanak and made a rule by Guru Amar Das.","Nam japna — meditation on God's name, at home at amrit vela and in the gurdwara through kirtan sung by ragis and the reading of the scripture.","Vaisakhi — April; the Punjabi harvest festival and the anniversary of the founding of the Khalsa in 1699; akhand path, nagar kirtan led by the Panj Piare, the Nishan Sahib replaced, Amrit Sanskar ceremonies held.","Divali, kept by Sikhs as Bandi Chhor Divas — the release of Guru Hargobind from imprisonment at Gwalior, who would not leave without the princes held with him; Harimandir Sahib is lit up.","Gurpurbs — anniversaries of the Gurus, above all Guru Nanak's birthday in November, marked by an akhand path completed on the day and by a nagar kirtan.","Gurpurbs in India and in Britain — in India processions run for miles through towns on the day itself; in Britain the route is shorter and agreed with the council, and many gurdwaras move the main celebration to the nearest weekend.","Harimandir Sahib, the Golden Temple, Amritsar — built under Guru Arjan, who installed the scripture there; it stands in the Amrit Sarovar, is reached by going down, and has doors on four sides.","Other historical gurdwaras — Nankana Sahib, Guru Nanak's birthplace; Anandpur Sahib, where the Khalsa was founded; the Akal Takht at Amritsar; Patna Sahib; Hazur Sahib at Nanded. Sikhism commands no obligatory pilgrimage.","Birth and naming — the Mool Mantra is often whispered to the baby and amrit placed on its tongue; at the gurdwara Ardas is said, the scripture is opened for a hukam, and the first letter of the first word gives the child's initial; Singh or Kaur is added.","Amrit Sanskar — initiation by five initiated Sikhs before the Guru Granth Sahib; amrit of water and sugar crystals stirred with a khanda while five prayers are recited, drunk and sprinkled on eyes and hair; the Rehat Maryada is accepted, and its four kurahits forbid cutting the hair, eating kutha meat, extramarital sexual relations and using tobacco, with other intoxicants forbidden elsewhere in the Rehat Maryada.","The five Ks — kesh, uncut hair; kangha, the comb; kara, the steel bracelet; kachhera, the cotton undershorts; kirpan, the sword.","Amritdhari and sahajdhari — the divergence the course specifies: the initiated Sikh who keeps the five Ks, and the Sikh who follows the teaching without initiation.","Singh and Kaur — lion and princess, taken in place of caste-bearing family names; a woman keeps Kaur rather than a husband's name."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Evaluate: 'Langar is the most important Sikh practice.')","items":["Argument: langar is the practice that does most, because it turns three separate Sikh beliefs into a single repeated action.","Religious teaching cited: Sikhs hold that all humanity is one and that God's light is in every person, and the course calls sewa a priority rather than an extra; the tradition goes back to Guru Nanak and was made a rule by Guru Amar Das, who would see no visitor who had not first eaten in his kitchen.","Development: in the langar hall the food is vegetarian so that nobody's diet excludes them, it is free so that money is irrelevant, it is cooked and served by volunteers as tan sewa, and everyone sits on the floor in one line, so a wealthy donor is physically placed level with a homeless stranger. Nothing about equality has to be said, because the seating says it; and the volunteer who washes dishes unnoticed is having their haumai worn down in the only way Sikh teaching thinks works.","Counter-view weighed: it can be objected that nam japna is more important, since Sikh teaching traces every fault to haumai and meditation on God's name addresses it directly, and that langar without remembrance is charity rather than religion. That is a real limit on the claim.","Mini-judgement: but the objection concedes the point it disputes, because it is langar done as sewa, without praise, that dismantles pride in practice; so langar is best described not as more important than remembrance but as the form remembrance takes in public, which is why it stands beside every prayer hall rather than instead of one.","Why this reaches Level 4: it is a well-argued response with reasoned consideration of different points of view; the chain of reasoning runs from named teaching through the detail of the practice to a judgement, and the references to religion are applied to the issue rather than reported."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same statement, visibly weaker)","items":["Argument: langar is the most important Sikh practice because it helps people and everyone can join in.","Evidence offered: Sikhs believe in equality and they give out free food at the gurdwara.","Development attempted: this is good because hungry people get fed and it shows Sikhs are kind, and lots of people come.","Counter-view: some people might say prayer is more important instead.","Judgement attempted: overall I think langar is the most important practice because feeding people is a good thing to do.","Why this only reaches Level 2: it meets the descriptor 'Reasoned consideration of a point of view' with a logical chain drawing on some knowledge, and it does mention religion, so it is not capped below that level.","What it misses: it does not meet 'Reasoned consideration of different points of view', because the opposing view is named in one clause and given no reason at all; there is no logical chain of reasoning drawing on relevant evidence, since no named teaching, Guru, term or source appears; and the conclusion is an opinion restated rather than a judgement supported by knowledge, so it cannot reach Level 3, let alone the Level 4 requirement of references to religion applied to the issue.","The fastest repairs: name the teaching and the Guru behind langar, give the opposing view a reason of its own from Sikh teaching, and make the final sentence say which argument was stronger and why."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'Sikhs should visit the Golden Temple at least once.' Evaluate this statement. [12 marks]","items":["Decode the statement: the load is all in 'should'. The question is not whether the Golden Temple is impressive or worth seeing; it is whether Sikh teaching places an obligation on a Sikh to go. Decide that before writing, because the conclusion depends on it.","Paragraph 1, for the statement: Harimandir Sahib at Amritsar was built under Guru Arjan, who installed the scripture there, so a visit puts a Sikh in the place where the Guru Granth Sahib was first enthroned and where much of Sikh history has since happened. Source used: the history of the fifth Guru and the building itself.","Paragraph 2, for the statement, taken into influence: the building preaches. It is entered by going down, it stands in the Amrit Sarovar, and it opens on four sides so that people of every caste and direction may come in; kirtan is sung almost without pause and one of the world's largest langars is served by volunteers, so a visitor takes part in sewa and returns with nam japna and commitment to the sangat strengthened. Source used: the oneness of humanity, and sewa as tan.","Paragraph 3, the different point of view, from within Sikhism: Sikhism commands no pilgrimage, unlike the Hajj in Islam, and Guru Nanak taught that God is not confined to holy places. Source used: the Mool Mantra at GGS 1a, which describes a God beyond time and beyond form, and the rule that any building becomes a gurdwara once the Guru Granth Sahib is installed in it, so the living Guru is as present in Southall as in Amritsar.","Paragraph 4, the different point of view, pressed practically: flights are expensive and dhan sewa is a priority, so the cost could feed a langar for weeks or fund a charity such as Khalsa Aid; and elderly, poor or disabled Sikhs cannot travel, so making the visit a duty would make spiritual standing depend on money, which contradicts the complete equality Sikhism insists on.","The evaluation thread running through all four: does Sikh teaching attach holiness to places, or only to the Guru and to the state of the heart? Return to that question at the end of each paragraph in a clause, so the conclusion is already earned when you reach it.","Non-religious views: this is a Component 1 question, so the first bullet requires Sikh teaching and the 'different point of view' is a divergent Sikh view rather than a humanist or atheist one. Do not spend time on non-religious arguments here.","Timing: about twelve minutes, roughly two and a half minutes on each of the four paragraphs and the remainder on the conclusion and a check of spelling. The spelling, punctuation and grammar marks are not carried by this question, which is a Practices evaluation; they sit on the twelve-mark question in the Beliefs section. Write accurately here all the same, and use the technical vocabulary deliberately, since terms such as sewa, gurmukh, haumai and amrit vela earn credit twice over.","What the conclusion must say: take a clear position on 'should'. State that the visit has real value and may well strengthen a Sikh's practice, but that Sikh teaching imposes no obligation on places, so the statement claims too much; add the ground for saying so, which is that the Gurus located holiness in the Guru and in living gurmukh rather than in any building. A conclusion that only says both sides have a point is not a justified conclusion."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T14:12:40Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.95,"notes":"Re-judged against the kit file after the fix pass: all six fixes landed and introduced nothing new — [SPaG 3 marks] now appears once in the whole kit, on room.questions[14] (the sewa/langar evaluation, the Beliefs-section question), and is gone from questions[12], questions[13], examples[4].setup and the plan title; the plan's timing item now states correctly that SPaG sits on the Beliefs twelve-mark question while keeping the vocabulary and accuracy advice; 'recited at the initiation' is accurate; the four kurahits are now stated exactly (cutting the hair, kutha meat, extramarital sexual relations, tobacco) with other intoxicants placed elsewhere in the Rehat Maryada; and the two rewritten first hints point at the concept without naming the answer. A fresh read of the whole kit found no other fault: tariffs are 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 only, all three twelve-mark stems carry the four Component 1 bullets, GGS 1a, the GGS 305:4 paraphrase and Japji Sahib are right, no quotation exceeds seven words, no printed question is reproduced, the fact files are true, and the Level 4 and Level 2 models are correctly placed against the grid. One judgement call to keep in view: questions[14] draws three of its five codes from 3.1.7.2, so it must stay the Beliefs-section evaluation for the SPaG label to be right."}}},"3.2.1.1":{"id":"AQA-8062","topic":"3.2.1.1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Theme A is one of the four themes you answer in Component 2, and it is worth one whole five-part question of 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks, with the 12-mark evaluation carrying the spelling, punctuation and grammar marks as well. It is also the theme students think they can answer from common sense, and that is exactly why it is lost: the marks go to named teaching from named traditions, not to opinions about relationships. This room gives you Christianity as the main religious tradition in Great Britain, at least one other tradition to contrast it with, the non-religious views the paper invites, and the three issues the specification says you must be able to argue on both sides: contraception, sexual relationships before marriage, and homosexual relationships.","idea":[{"h":"The rubric that governs every answer on this theme","t":"The specification demands three things at once: religious teachings, the philosophical and ethical arguments around them, and contrasting perspectives in contemporary British society. Three issues are named as ones you must be able to contrast: contraception, sexual relationships before marriage, and homosexual relationships, and the contrast must use the main religious tradition in Britain (Christianity) plus one or more other religious traditions. You must also be aware of non-religious beliefs such as atheism and humanism, and be able to cite sources of wisdom and authority - the Bible, the Catechism of the Catholic Church, the Qur'an and Hadith, the Torah and Talmud, the Vedas and Upanishads, the Pali Canon, the Guru Granth Sahib. A top-level answer therefore never says 'religious people believe'; it says which tradition, and which group inside that tradition, holds the view, and where the view comes from.","code":"3.2.1.1 — The theme's own requirement: religious teachings, contrasting perspectives, and the three prescribed issues"},{"h":"Human sexuality: heterosexual and homosexual relationships","t":"Traditional teaching in Christianity, Islam and Orthodox Judaism is that sexual relationships belong within marriage between a man and a woman, drawing on Genesis 2:24, where husband and wife 'become one flesh'. The Catechism of the Catholic Church separates the person from the act: homosexual people are to be accepted with respect, compassion and sensitivity, while homosexual sexual acts are rejected. Other Christians read the same Bible differently, arguing that Jesus said nothing on the subject and that the command to love one's neighbour applies here too; the Religious Society of Friends (Quakers) and the Unitarians marry same-sex couples, and the Church of England's House of Bishops commended Prayers of Love and Faith, blessings for same-sex couples, in December 2023, while the Church still does not marry them. A top-level answer distinguishes orientation from sexual activity, and attributes each position to the people who actually hold it.","code":"3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Human sexuality including: heterosexual and homosexual relationships"},{"h":"Sex before marriage, and sex outside it","t":"The specification names these together but they behave differently in an exam. Sex before marriage is one of the three prescribed contrasting issues, because religious opinion is divided: Catholic teaching calls for chastity until marriage and Paul tells the Corinthians to 'Flee from sexual immorality' (1 Corinthians 6:18); Muslim teaching forbids zina and the Qur'an warns believers not even to go near it (Surah 17:32); yet many Christians in Britain accept a faithful couple who intend to marry, and cohabitation before marriage is now ordinary in British society. Adultery is different: 'You shall not commit adultery' (Exodus 20:14) is shared by Judaism and Christianity, and every tradition on the specification condemns it, because it breaks a promise and damages the family. A top-level answer keeps the two apart, and says why one is contested and the other is not.","code":"3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Sexual relationships before and outside of marriage"},{"h":"Contraception and family planning","t":"The sharpest contrast on this prescribed issue is inside Christianity. Humanae Vitae, the encyclical of Pope Paul VI in 1968, taught that every marital act must remain open to the transmission of life, so Roman Catholics reject artificial contraception as against natural law and are directed to natural family planning, which works with the woman's own cycle. The Church of England has accepted responsible family planning as a matter for the couple's conscience since the Lambeth Conference of 1930, and most Protestant churches agree. Beyond Christianity: most Muslims accept reversible methods to space children or protect a mother's health but reject sterilisation; Orthodox Jewish authorities usually permit the pill for the mother's welfare while Reform Judaism allows it more widely; Sikhs and most Hindus see no objection. A top-level answer names the denomination and the document, not 'the Church'.","code":"3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Contraception and family planning"},{"h":"The nature and purpose of marriage","t":"Everything else in this theme is argued from here. Christians teach that marriage is a lifelong covenant made before God; Roman Catholic and Orthodox Christians call marriage a sacrament, but only the Roman Catholic Church holds that a valid consummated marriage cannot be dissolved, while the Orthodox churches grant an ecclesiastical divorce on grounds such as adultery and permit remarriage with a penitential rite. Anglican teaching gives its purposes as the couple's lifelong union in heart, body and mind, the foundation of family life in which children are nurtured, and mutual comfort in good times and bad. In Islam marriage is a contract, the nikah, with the wife's consent and her mahr, and the Qur'an teaches that spouses were made so they might find tranquillity together (Surah 30:21). Sikh marriage is the Anand Karaj, and the Guru Granth Sahib calls husband and wife 'one light in two bodies' (Guru Granth Sahib 788). A top-level answer uses the purpose to drive the argument: whatever a tradition says about divorce, cohabitation or same-sex marriage follows from what it says marriage is for.","code":"3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — The nature and purpose of marriage"},{"h":"Same-sex marriage and cohabitation","t":"Same-sex marriage became lawful in England and Wales under the Marriage (Same Sex Couples) Act 2013, with the first weddings in 2014; religious organisations must opt in, and the Church of England is barred in law from conducting them. Quakers, Unitarians and Liberal and Reform Judaism do conduct them, arguing that faithful lifelong commitment is what marriage is; the Roman Catholic Church and most Muslim and Orthodox Jewish authorities hold that marriage is by definition between a man and a woman. Cohabitation is the commonest alternative to marriage in Britain: many religious believers object that it withholds the public, permanent promise that protects the partners and their children, while others see it as a realistic step towards marriage and note that cohabiting couples have far fewer legal rights. A top-level answer shows that the division runs inside Christianity, not only between religions.","code":"3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Same-sex marriage and cohabitation"},{"h":"Divorce and remarriage","t":"Jesus taught 'What God has joined together' (Mark 10:9), and Roman Catholic teaching draws from this that a valid marriage is indissoluble: a Catholic may separate, or apply to a diocesan tribunal for an annulment if the marriage was never valid, but may not remarry in church while the first partner lives. The Church of England, which reads Matthew 19:9 as allowing an exception for sexual immorality, has allowed remarriage in church at the priest's discretion since 2002. Judaism regulates rather than forbids: the marriage ends when the husband gives his wife a get before a beit din, and a woman whose husband refuses is an agunah. Islam permits divorce but treats it as the most disliked of the things God has allowed, with an iddah waiting period for reconciliation. A top-level answer treats remarriage as a separate question from divorce, because several traditions do.","code":"3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Divorce, including reasons for divorce, and remarrying"},{"h":"The ethical argument: the sanctity of vows against compassion","t":"The specification names the two arguments, and a 12-mark evaluation on divorce is mostly the weighing of them. The sanctity of marriage vows says that promises made freely before God were meant to be permanent, that a vow which can be dropped when marriage becomes hard was never a lifelong vow, and that children are damaged when a family breaks. Compassion says that God does not require anyone to stay where there is abuse, adultery or desertion, that Jesus' central command is love of neighbour, and that forcing two people to remain in a dead marriage causes more harm than ending it. Notice the arguments are not symmetrical: one appeals to a rule, the other to consequences. A top-level answer does not simply state both; it says which one should govern which cases, and why, which is the justified conclusion the question asks for.","code":"3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Ethical arguments related to divorce"},{"h":"The nature of families: parents, children, nuclear and extended","t":"Two strands. First, what the members owe one another: 'Honour your father and mother' (Exodus 20:12) binds adult children as well as young ones, Colossians 3:20 tells children to obey their parents, and Ephesians 6:4 tells fathers not to exasperate their children; the Buddhist Sigalovada Sutta sets out reciprocal duties of parents and children; the Qur'an commands kindness to parents in their old age (Surah 17:23-24), and a hadith says heaven lies at the feet of mothers. Second, the forms a family takes: the nuclear family of parents and their children, and the extended family of grandparents, aunts, uncles and cousins, in one household or nearby. Many religious communities in Britain keep extended patterns where wider society has moved to nuclear ones. A top-level answer uses this to explain behaviour, such as caring for grandparents at home.","code":"3.2.1.1 Families and gender equality — The nature of families"},{"h":"The purpose of families: procreation, stability, faith","t":"The specification names three purposes. Procreation: Genesis 1:28 tells humanity to 'Be fruitful and multiply', and Jewish teaching treats having children as a mitzvah. Stability and the protection of children: religions argue that the public, lifelong promise of marriage is what makes a home safe enough to raise a child in, which is the reason many give for objecting to cohabitation. Educating children in a faith: Deuteronomy 6:7 tells parents to teach the commandments to their children, and it is in the home that the Shabbat meal, the family prayer, the Muslim child's first Arabic and the Christian baptismal promises actually happen. The third purpose is what makes the family a religious institution and not merely a social one. A top-level answer uses it to explain why religions care so much about the form the family takes.","code":"3.2.1.1 Families and gender equality — The purpose of families"},{"h":"Contemporary family issues: same-sex parents and polygamy","t":"Same-sex parents: opinion divides within traditions as well as between them. Those who object argue that a child is owed a mother and a father and that the family exists to reflect the complementarity of the sexes; those who accept it argue that what a child needs is stable, loving care, and that a same-sex couple can give it as well as anyone. Polygamy: the Qur'an permits a man up to four wives on condition that he treats them justly, and adds that if he fears he cannot, he should marry only one (Surah 4:3), so many Muslims argue the verse points towards monogamy; Christian teaching holds that two 'become one flesh' (Genesis 2:24), and polygamous marriages cannot lawfully be contracted in Britain. A top-level answer weighs the good of the child against the form of the family rather than asserting either.","code":"3.2.1.1 Families and gender equality — Contemporary family issues"},{"h":"The roles of men and women","t":"This bullet asks whether traditions teach different roles, and whether different means unequal. Traditional Christians cite Ephesians 5:22-25, where wives submit and husbands love their wives as Christ loved the Church, and argue that the roles are complementary rather than unequal; other Christians read the passage against Ephesians 5:21 as mutual submission and treat marriage as an equal partnership. Orthodox Judaism teaches separate but equal roles, with men and women seated apart in the synagogue, while Reform and Liberal Judaism seat them together and ordain women as rabbis, as they have in Britain since the 1970s. The Church of England ordained women priests from 1994 and consecrated its first woman bishop in 2015; the Roman Catholic Church ordains only men. A top-level answer confronts the real question: can a difference of role be genuinely equal?","code":"3.2.1.1 Families and gender equality — The roles of men and women"},{"h":"Gender equality and its grounds","t":"The teaching and its reasons. Genesis 1:27 says God created humanity in his image, 'male and female', so Jews and Christians argue that both sexes share one worth; Galatians 3:28 says that in Christ there is 'neither male nor female'. The Qur'an says all people were created from a single male and female and made into peoples so that they might know one another (Surah 49:13). The Guru Granth Sahib is blunt: 'From woman, woman is born' (Guru Granth Sahib 473), and Sikh women take the name Kaur so they need not take a husband's name. Buddhist teaching holds that women and men are equally able to practise and reach enlightenment. A top-level answer sets the teaching against the practice that has sat alongside it - which is exactly what a 12-mark evaluation on this theme asks you to do.","code":"3.2.1.1 Families and gender equality — Gender equality"},{"h":"Gender prejudice and discrimination, with examples","t":"Keep the two words apart: prejudice is an attitude, judging someone before you know them; discrimination is acting on it. The specification requires examples, so carry concrete ones: unequal pay and blocked promotion, girls' access to education in some countries, the exclusion of women from ordination or from leading prayer, and the assumption that housework and childcare are a woman's job. In Britain the Equality Act 2010 makes sex discrimination in work, education and services unlawful, with narrow exceptions for some religious posts, which is why a church may lawfully ordain only men when an employer may not hire only men. Religious responses divide: some argue their different roles are not discrimination at all, others campaign against sexism inside their own tradition. A top-level answer names an example and then judges it.","code":"3.2.1.1 Families and gender equality — Gender prejudice and discrimination, including examples"}],"examples":[{"title":"The one-mark multiple choice: eliminate, then tick","setup":"The question reads: Which one of the following is a bill of divorce issued in Judaism? A Get, B Ketubah, C Mahr, D Nikah. You put a tick in the box next to the correct answer, and there is a single mark for it.","steps":["Fix what is being asked for before looking at the options: a document that ends a Jewish marriage, not one that begins any marriage.","Clear the option you can place at the wrong end of a marriage - a ketubah is the Jewish marriage contract, drawn up at the wedding, so it cannot be the answer.","Clear the two Arabic terms, which come from Islam and not Judaism: the mahr is the dower a Muslim husband gives his wife, and the nikah is the Islamic marriage contract itself.","One option is left: the get, the bill of divorce a husband gives his wife before a beit din, so tick A and move on inside a minute."],"cues":["Is the question asking about the start of a marriage or its end?","Which option belongs at a wedding rather than a divorce?","Which two options are not Jewish terms at all?","Which option survives, and what exactly is it?"]},{"title":"The four-mark contrasting question on contraception","setup":"The question reads: Explain two contrasting religious beliefs about the use of contraception. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions. [4 marks] Nothing else is given, and you have about four minutes.","steps":["Pick two positions that genuinely clash, and take them from inside Christianity, where the disagreement on this issue is sharpest: Roman Catholic teaching against Anglican and most Protestant teaching.","Write the first belief and name its source: Humanae Vitae taught that every marital act must remain open to the transmission of life, so Roman Catholics reject artificial contraception as against natural law.","Turn that simple explanation into a detailed one by adding what Catholics are told to do instead - natural family planning, which works with the woman's own cycle and so does not block conception artificially.","Open the second belief with words that mark the contrast, then develop it: the Church of England, by contrast, has treated family planning as a matter for the couple's conscience since the Lambeth Conference of 1930, so most Anglicans see the pill as responsible care for a mother's health and for children they can support.","Check the contrast is real before stopping: both sides value marriage and children, but they disagree about whether a couple may deliberately prevent conception, and similar beliefs would be credited only once."],"cues":["Where is the disagreement sharpest - between religions, or within one?","What document does the stricter view rest on, and what does it claim?","What are believers told to do instead, and why does that count as development?","Which words at the start of your second paragraph show the examiner a contrast?","Would an examiner see two genuinely different beliefs here, or one belief twice?"]},{"title":"The five-mark question: two beliefs and one source","setup":"The question reads: Explain two religious beliefs about the purpose of marriage. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks] You have about five minutes.","steps":["Choose two purposes that are genuinely different rather than two ways of saying companionship: the procreation and raising of children, and lifelong mutual support.","State the first and develop it: Christians teach that marriage is where children are meant to be born and brought up in the faith, which is why parents make promises at a baptism to raise the child as a Christian.","Attach the source plainly and accurately, because the extra mark is given once for a reference: Genesis 1:28, 'Be fruitful and multiply'.","State the second purpose and develop it from a named tradition: the Guru Granth Sahib calls husband and wife 'one light in two bodies' (Guru Granth Sahib 788), so Sikhs treat marriage as a spiritual partnership in which the couple help each other towards God.","Audit the shape before moving on: two beliefs, a development on each, and one source clearly attributed to a named text."],"cues":["Are your two purposes different, or the same idea twice?","What do believers actually do that shows the first purpose?","Which text can you name and reference accurately?","Which other tradition gives you a second purpose with a quotable source?","Count the parts: have you written everything the question asks for?"]},{"title":"Building the twelve-mark evaluation on divorce","setup":"The question reads: 'Religious believers should never divorce.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","steps":["Decode the statement before writing a word: 'never' makes it absolute, so the whole argument will be decided by whether any case - abuse, adultery, desertion - counts as an exception.","Build the supporting case from named teaching: Jesus said 'What God has joined together' (Mark 10:9), and Roman Catholic teaching draws from it that a valid sacramental marriage cannot be dissolved, so a Catholic seeks an annulment or lives apart rather than remarrying.","Add the reasoning, not just the teaching: vows were made freely before God, children suffer when a family breaks, and a promise that can be dropped when marriage becomes hard was never a lifelong promise.","Turn the argument with compassion, the second ethical argument the specification names: Matthew 19:9 allows an exception for sexual immorality, Judaism regulates divorce through the get rather than forbidding it, and Muslim teaching treats divorce as disliked but permitted, with a waiting period for reconciliation.","Add a non-religious argument the paper invites: humanists judge a relationship by whether it still does good for the people in it, and since no-fault divorce came into force in England and Wales in 2022 a couple need not blame each other to end a marriage.","Judge rather than summarise: say which side carried more weight and why - for instance that the sanctity of vows rightly protects marriage as a lifelong covenant, but compassion must override a promise when staying causes real harm."],"cues":["Which single word in the statement decides the argument?","Whose teaching supports it, and what wording can you cite?","Why does that teaching lead to the conclusion - what is the reasoning?","What is the strongest case on the other side, and which traditions supply it?","What would someone with no religion say about ending a failed marriage?","What must your last paragraph do that a summary would not?"]},{"title":"The four-mark influence question: show what changes","setup":"The question reads: Explain two ways in which religious teaching about the family influences believers today. [4 marks] The second mark in each pair is given only when the influence itself is shown.","steps":["Choose ways that show up in what people actually do rather than beliefs stated in the air: passing the faith on at home, and caring for elderly parents.","Write the first way with its effect attached: because parents are told to teach their children the commandments, Jewish families gather for the Shabbat meal at home and Muslim parents teach their children to pray and send them to the mosque school.","Write the second the same way: 'Honour your father and mother' (Exodus 20:12) is understood to bind adult children too, so many religious families in Britain keep an extended household and care for grandparents at home rather than arranging residential care.","Check each half names something someone does differently, because a belief stated without its consequence earns the simple mark only."],"cues":["Which teachings leave visible traces in daily life?","What do families actually do because of the first teaching?","Which commandment covers adult children, and what does it change?","Does each half say what someone does, not just what they believe?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which three issues does the specification say you must be able to explain contrasting beliefs about, and which traditions must the contrast use?","a":"Contraception, sexual relationships before marriage, and homosexual relationships - contrasted using Christianity, the main religious tradition in Britain, plus one or more other religious traditions."},{"q":"Why is the contrast on contraception often drawn inside Christianity rather than between religions?","a":"Because Roman Catholic teaching in Humanae Vitae rejects artificial contraception while the Church of England has accepted responsible family planning since 1930, so the two positions clash sharply within one religion."},{"q":"What are the two ethical arguments the specification names on divorce?","a":"The sanctity of marriage vows, and compassion."},{"q":"What is the difference between prejudice and discrimination?","a":"Prejudice is an attitude - judging someone before knowing them; discrimination is the action taken because of it."}]},"room":{"facts":["Theme A is one five-part question of 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks, and the paper advises 25 minutes on a theme - roughly a minute a mark.","The 12-mark evaluation on Component 2 also carries 3 SPaG marks, and only the best of the four themes' SPaG marks counts towards the paper.","The 4-mark contrasting question on Component 2 adds the instruction to refer to one or more religious traditions, so the tradition must be named in the answer.","The 5-mark question adds the instruction to refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching, and that reference is worth one mark on its own.","The three prescribed contrasting issues are contraception, sexual relationships before marriage, and homosexual relationships, argued from Christianity plus one or more other traditions.","Christianity is the main religious tradition of Great Britain and the religious traditions of Britain are diverse: both statements are required knowledge for this theme."],"questions":[{"q":"Which one of the following means being married to more than one person at the same time? A Adultery B Cohabitation C Monogamy D Polygamy","a":"AO1:1. Answer: D Polygamy.","sol":"D Polygamy. Adultery is sex with someone other than one's marriage partner; cohabitation is an unmarried couple living together; monogamy is marriage to one person only.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The four options are all words for different arrangements between partners - sort them by how many partners each involves.","Two of the options describe having one partner only, so they can be cleared straight away.","Of the two left, one describes unfaithfulness inside a marriage and the other describes the number of marriage partners."],"codes":["3.2.1.1 Families and gender equality — Contemporary family issues"]},{"q":"Which one of the following means treating someone unfairly because of their gender? A Discrimination B Equality C Prejudice D Stereotyping","a":"AO1:1. Answer: A Discrimination.","sol":"A Discrimination. Prejudice is the attitude held beforehand and stereotyping is assuming everyone in a group is the same; discrimination is the unfair action taken because of them. Equality is the opposite.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The question asks about treating someone a particular way, so look for the option that names an action rather than an attitude.","Two options describe what goes on inside someone's head before they act.","One remaining option is the opposite of unfair treatment, so it can be cleared."],"codes":["3.2.1.1 Families and gender equality — Gender prejudice and discrimination, including examples"]},{"q":"Give two purposes of marriage in Christian teaching.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points, no development required: procreation and raising children; lifelong union and companionship; mutual comfort and support; the proper place for a sexual relationship.","sol":"Two from: the procreation of children and their upbringing in the faith; the lifelong union of the couple in heart, body and mind; mutual comfort and support in good times and bad; the proper place for a sexual relationship. Two bare points score the two marks - no explanation is needed or rewarded.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what the Anglican marriage service says a marriage is given for.","One purpose looks outward to the next generation, another looks inward to the couple themselves.","Two short separate points are enough here; do not develop them."],"codes":["3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — The nature and purpose of marriage"]},{"q":"Give two purposes of the family in religious teaching.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points: procreation; stability and the protection of children; educating children in a faith.","sol":"Two from: procreation, so that children are born; stability and the protection of children, giving them a safe and settled home; educating children in a faith, teaching them to pray, worship and keep the tradition. One mark each, no development required.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The specification names three purposes of the family; any two will do.","One purpose is about children arriving, one about keeping them safe, one about what they are taught.","Write two short points, not a paragraph."],"codes":["3.2.1.1 Families and gender equality — The purpose of families"]},{"q":"Give two duties children have towards their parents in religious teaching.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points: to honour and respect them; to obey them; to care for them in old age; to learn and continue the family's faith.","sol":"Two from: to honour and respect their parents, as Exodus 20:12 commands; to obey them, as Colossians 3:20 teaches; to care for them in their old age, which the Qur'an commands at Surah 17:23-24; to learn the faith and carry on the family's tradition. Two separate points earn the two marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["One of these duties is one of the Ten Commandments.","Another duty applies only once the parents are old and the child is an adult.","Two bare duties are enough; no source is needed on this tariff."],"codes":["3.2.1.1 Families and gender equality — The nature of families"]},{"q":"Give two reasons why some religious believers reject sexual relationships before marriage.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points: sex belongs to the covenant of marriage; chastity is commanded; risk of a child born outside a settled home; it treats a lifelong sign as temporary.","sol":"Two from: they believe sexual union is the sign of the lifelong covenant of marriage, so it belongs inside it; their scriptures command chastity, as when Paul tells the Corinthians to flee sexual immorality; a child might be born without the settled home the family exists to give; it risks treating a partner as temporary rather than as a lifelong commitment.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Start from what these believers think sex within marriage actually signifies.","A second reason looks forward to what might follow from sex - a child.","Two short reasons, one mark each; do not explain them at length."],"codes":["3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Sexual relationships before and outside of marriage"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting religious beliefs about the use of contraception. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions.","a":"AO1:3. Two contrasting beliefs, each 1 mark simple, 2 marks detailed. Roman Catholic rejection of artificial contraception (Humanae Vitae, natural law, natural family planning) against Anglican, Protestant, Muslim, Reform Jewish or Sikh acceptance of responsible family planning.","sol":"Roman Catholic teaching rejects artificial contraception. Humanae Vitae (1968) taught that every marital act must remain open to the transmission of life, so artificial methods are rejected as against natural law, and Catholic couples who wish to space their children are directed instead to natural family planning, which works with the woman's own cycle. Most Muslims contrast with this. Muslim teaching accepts reversible methods such as the pill or a condom where a couple need to space their children or to protect the mother's health, because the marriage itself remains open to children; sterilisation is rejected, because it closes off the gift of children permanently.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["On this prescribed issue one belief must come from Christianity and one from another religious tradition: pick a Christian church with a clear ruling and a non-Christian tradition with a different one.","For each belief, name the tradition and the document or teaching it rests on, then say what couples are actually told to do about family planning.","Two paragraphs whose opening words set them against each other; no source mark is available here, so spend the time on development."],"codes":["3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Contraception and family planning","3.2.1.1 — The theme's own requirement: religious teachings, contrasting perspectives, and the three prescribed issues"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting religious beliefs about same-sex marriage. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions.","a":"AO1:3. Two contrasting beliefs, 2 + 2. Catholic, Orthodox Jewish or traditional Muslim teaching that marriage is between a man and a woman, against Quaker, Unitarian, Liberal and Reform Jewish practice of marrying same-sex couples.","sol":"Roman Catholic teaching is that marriage is a sacrament between one man and one woman, ordered to the good of the spouses and the procreation of children, so the Church will not marry a same-sex couple, although the Catechism insists that homosexual people be treated with respect, compassion and sensitivity. A contrasting belief is held by the Religious Society of Friends (Quakers), the Unitarians and Liberal and Reform Judaism, all of whom conduct same-sex marriages: they argue that what makes a marriage is faithful, loving, lifelong commitment, and that such a commitment may be blessed whatever the sex of the couple. The Church of England sits between the two, not marrying same-sex couples but commending prayers of blessing for them in December 2023.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what each tradition thinks marriage is for - the answer to that decides its view here.","Name the specific churches or movements rather than writing about Christians or Jews in general.","Develop each belief with what the tradition actually does at a wedding, then make sure the two paragraphs genuinely oppose each other."],"codes":["3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Same-sex marriage and cohabitation","3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Human sexuality including: heterosexual and homosexual relationships"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting religious beliefs about polygamy. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions.","a":"AO1:3. Two contrasting beliefs, 2 + 2. Qur'anic permission of up to four wives on condition of justice (Surah 4:3) against Christian teaching that marriage joins two people only; British law forbids polygamous marriage.","sol":"Islam permits polygamy within strict limits. The Qur'an allows a man up to four wives on condition that he deals justly with each of them, and adds that if he fears he cannot be just he should marry only one (Surah 4:3), so many Muslims argue the verse actually points towards monogamy and note that most Muslim marriages are monogamous. Christian teaching contrasts with this. Because Genesis says that a husband and wife 'become one flesh' (Genesis 2:24), and Jesus used those words when he taught about marriage, the churches hold that marriage joins two people only, and no Christian denomination will conduct a polygamous marriage. In Britain the contrast is settled in law as well, since a polygamous marriage cannot be contracted here.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["One tradition on the specification permits this under conditions; start by working out what the conditions are.","The opposing belief comes from a verse about two people becoming one, used by Jesus himself.","Develop each side by saying what it means in practice for believers living in Britain."],"codes":["3.2.1.1 Families and gender equality — Contemporary family issues"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting religious beliefs about the roles of men and women in the family and in worship. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions.","a":"AO1:3. Two contrasting beliefs, 2 + 2. Separate or complementary roles (Orthodox Judaism, traditional Christian reading of Ephesians 5, male-only Catholic priesthood) against identical roles (Reform Judaism, Church of England ordination of women).","sol":"Orthodox Judaism teaches that men and women have separate but equally valuable roles: men are obliged to time-bound commandments and lead public prayer, women sit separately in the synagogue, and the mother's work in creating a Jewish home is treated as the foundation of the faith. Reform Judaism takes the contrasting view that these roles are cultural rather than binding: men and women sit together, women read from the Torah, and women have been ordained as rabbis in Britain since the 1970s. The same contrast runs inside Christianity, between the Church of England, which has ordained women as priests since 1994 and consecrated its first woman bishop in 2015, and the Roman Catholic Church, which ordains only men.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Pick one religion where the two positions sit side by side in different movements.","Say what each side does in the synagogue, the church or the home, not just what it believes.","Make the second paragraph open with a word that signals contrast, and check the two positions really differ."],"codes":["3.2.1.1 Families and gender equality — The roles of men and women"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which belief in the sanctity of marriage vows influences religious believers today.","a":"AO1:2. Two ways, 2 + 2, and the second mark in each case requires the influence itself; any religious tradition may be used: marriage preparation and seeking help before separating; Catholic annulment rather than remarriage; staying together through difficulty.","sol":"In Christianity, because the vows are made before God and Jesus taught 'What God has joined together' (Mark 10:9), many believers treat the promise as binding for life, so engaged couples attend marriage preparation before the wedding and, when the marriage runs into trouble, go to their priest or to a counselling service rather than straight to a solicitor. The belief also shapes what Catholics do once a marriage has broken down: because the Church holds that a valid marriage cannot be dissolved, a divorced Catholic who wishes to marry again in church must apply to a diocesan tribunal for an annulment, and many will not remarry at all rather than break the vow they made.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["This question is about what believers do differently, so start from the point in a marriage where the belief bites.","One influence comes before the wedding, another after a marriage has broken down.","Each half must end with an action or a decision, because a belief stated without its consequence earns only the simple mark."],"codes":["3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Ethical arguments related to divorce","3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — The nature and purpose of marriage"]},{"q":"Explain two religious teachings about divorce. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","a":"AO1:1. Two teachings, 2 + 2, plus 1 mark for an accurate source. Catholic indissolubility and annulment; Anglican remarriage at the priest's discretion; the Jewish get; Muslim talaq with iddah.","sol":"Roman Catholic teaching is that marriage is a sacrament and cannot be dissolved, so the Church does not accept that a civil divorce ends a marriage, and a Catholic may not marry again in church while the first partner is alive; the couple may separate, or seek an annulment if the marriage was never valid in the first place. Jewish teaching allows divorce but regulates it: the marriage ends when the husband gives his wife a get, a bill of divorce written and delivered before a beit din, and only then may either partner remarry within Orthodox Judaism. Christians appeal to Jesus' words 'What God has joined together' (Mark 10:9), while Matthew 19:9 is read as allowing an exception for sexual immorality.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Choose two traditions that handle divorce differently - one that refuses to dissolve a marriage and one that has a procedure for ending it.","Develop each teaching by naming what a believer in that tradition actually has to do.","Leave room at the end for a named text with chapter and verse, because that reference is worth a mark on its own."],"codes":["3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Divorce, including reasons for divorce, and remarrying"]},{"q":"Explain two religious teachings about sexual relationships outside of marriage. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","a":"AO1:1. Two teachings, 2 + 2, plus 1 mark for an accurate source. Christian condemnation of adultery as breaking a covenant (Exodus 20:14, Genesis 2:24); Muslim prohibition of zina (Surah 17:32) and the practices that guard it.","sol":"Christian teaching is that adultery breaks a promise made before God: it is forbidden by the commandment 'You shall not commit adultery' (Exodus 20:14), and because Genesis says husband and wife 'become one flesh' (Genesis 2:24), adultery is understood as tearing apart a union God himself has made, which is why Jesus lists it among the things that defile a person. Muslim teaching is equally firm about zina, sexual relations outside marriage: the Qur'an warns believers not even to go near it (Surah 17:32), because it destroys trust between husband and wife and confuses the family line on which the community depends. Many Muslims therefore treat modest dress and avoiding being alone with an unrelated adult as ways of keeping that teaching.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["One of the Ten Commandments deals directly with this, and one Arabic term covers it in Islam.","Develop each teaching by explaining why the tradition thinks the act does so much damage.","One accurate reference with book and chapter, or surah number, secures the extra mark - do not leave it out."],"codes":["3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Sexual relationships before and outside of marriage","3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Human sexuality including: heterosexual and homosexual relationships"]},{"q":"'Sex before marriage is always wrong.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels-marked. Support: sex as the sign of the marriage covenant, chastity commanded, risk to a child, natural law. Other views: commitment matters more than ceremony, humanist consent-and-consequences reasoning, cohabitation as ordinary in Britain. Level 4 needs both sides reasoned, religion applied and a justified conclusion.","sol":"Many religious believers would support the statement, because they hold that sexual union is the sign of a lifelong covenant. Christian teaching reads Genesis 2:24, where husband and wife 'become one flesh', as meaning that sex says something about a relationship that only marriage makes true, so using the sign outside marriage is a kind of lie. The Roman Catholic Church teaches chastity before marriage for this reason, and Paul tells the Corinthians to 'Flee from sexual immorality' (1 Corinthians 6:18). Islam is firmer still: zina is a major sin and the Qur'an warns believers not even to go near it (Surah 17:32), so for most Muslims the word 'always' is exactly right. There is also an argument that needs no religion: sex risks a pregnancy, and a child may then be born without the settled, committed home the family exists to provide.\n\nA different point of view is held by many Christians in Britain, including many Anglicans, who argue that what matters morally is faithful, loving commitment rather than the ceremony that follows it; a couple who are engaged and intend to marry are not using one another, and the New Testament condemns exploitation and unfaithfulness rather than every act before a wedding. Humanists go further: morality comes from consent, honesty and consequences, not from commands, so two adults who are honest with each other, use contraception responsibly and harm nobody have done nothing wrong, and cohabitation before marriage is now ordinary in British society. The word 'always' is the weakest part of the statement, because it treats a careless teenager and a settled couple in their sixties who cannot marry for financial reasons as morally identical.\n\nDefenders of the statement can reply that rules exist precisely so that they are not bent case by case, and that the harms they guard against - broken trust, unplanned pregnancy, children without two committed parents - are real whatever an individual couple intends.\n\nIn my judgement the religious arguments succeed in showing that sex belongs with commitment, but they do not establish 'always'. The strongest teaching on both sides is about faithfulness and about not treating another person as a means to an end, and a couple who are permanently committed to one another meet that standard even before a ceremony. The statement is therefore right about the danger of casual sex and wrong to make no exceptions, so I would reword it rather than reject it: sex belongs with lifelong commitment, and marriage remains the clearest and most secure form that commitment takes.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["One word in the statement decides the whole argument - find it before you plan.","Build one side fully before starting the other: claim, the named teaching it rests on with its source, then the reasoning that connects them.","Aim for two developed arguments each way, bring in a view from outside religion, and finish by saying which side carried more weight and why."],"codes":["3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Sexual relationships before and outside of marriage","3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — The nature and purpose of marriage","3.2.1.1 — The theme's own requirement: religious teachings, contrasting perspectives, and the three prescribed issues"]},{"q":"'Religious believers should never divorce.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels-marked. Support: the sanctity of vows, Mark 10:9, Catholic indissolubility and annulment, harm to children. Other views: compassion, Matthew 19:9, the Jewish get, Muslim talaq, humanist and legal reasoning. Level 4 needs both sides reasoned and a justified conclusion.","sol":"The case for the statement rests on the sanctity of marriage vows. Jesus taught 'What God has joined together' (Mark 10:9), and Roman Catholic teaching draws from this that a valid sacramental marriage cannot be dissolved at all: a civil divorce does not end it, so a Catholic may separate, or apply to a diocesan tribunal for an annulment if the marriage was never valid, but may not remarry in church while the first partner lives. The reasoning behind the teaching matters as much as the teaching: the vows were made freely, in public and before God, and a promise that may be dropped when marriage becomes difficult was never a lifelong promise. Supporters add that children are usually the losers when a family breaks up, and that couples who expect to be able to leave try less hard to repair what has gone wrong.\n\nThe opposing case rests on compassion, the second ethical argument the specification names. Even within Christianity the rule is not absolute: Matthew 19:9 is read as allowing divorce for sexual immorality, and the Church of England has allowed remarriage in church at the priest's discretion since 2002. Judaism does not forbid divorce but regulates it, ending the marriage when a get is given before a beit din; Muslim teaching treats divorce as permitted but disliked, with a waiting period during which the couple are urged to reconcile. All of these traditions accept that some marriages should end. The strongest form of the argument is about abuse: no religion teaches that a woman must remain where she and her children are in danger, and Jesus' command to love one's neighbour applies first to the person being harmed. Non-religious thinkers press the same point from consequences: humanists judge a relationship by whether it still does good for the people in it, and since no-fault divorce came into force in England and Wales in 2022 a couple need not accuse each other in order to separate.\n\nWeighing the two, the supporters are right that marriage is not a contract to be abandoned lightly, and religions are right to make divorce difficult, slow and regretted rather than routine. But 'never' cannot be defended: a marriage can be destroyed by violence, desertion or unfaithfulness, and at that point the vow is already broken by one partner, so compassion, not stubbornness, is the faithful response. My conclusion is that the statement is a good rule and a bad absolute: believers should treat divorce as a last resort, never as an easy option, and traditions that regulate it rather than forbid it have the wiser position.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["The specification names two ethical arguments on this issue; the essay is the weighing of them.","Take the statement seriously first: find the tradition that comes closest to holding it, and say why it does.","Then find the cases the absolute word cannot cover, and let your conclusion follow from which of those cases you judge decisive."],"codes":["3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Ethical arguments related to divorce","3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Divorce, including reasons for divorce, and remarrying"]},{"q":"'Men and women should have exactly the same roles in the family and in religion.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels-marked. Support: Genesis 1:27, Galatians 3:28, Guru Granth Sahib 473, women's ordination, the Equality Act 2010, humanist arguments. Other views: complementary roles (Ephesians 5), Orthodox Jewish separate but equal, male-only priesthood and imamate. Level 4 needs both sides reasoned and a justified conclusion.","sol":"The case for the statement starts from teachings about equal worth. Genesis 1:27 says God made humanity in his image, 'male and female', so Jews and Christians argue that both sexes share one status before God; Paul writes that in Christ there is 'neither male nor female' (Galatians 3:28). Sikh teaching is blunter still - 'From woman, woman is born' (Guru Granth Sahib 473) - and Sikh women take the name Kaur so that they need not take a husband's name. Supporters argue that identical worth should show itself in identical roles, and point to what has changed: the Church of England has ordained women as priests since 1994 and consecrated its first woman bishop in 2015, Reform and Liberal Judaism have ordained women as rabbis in Britain since the 1970s, and in wider British society the Equality Act 2010 makes sex discrimination in work and education unlawful. Humanists add that any rule reserving a role for men needs evidence, not tradition, to justify it, and that no such evidence exists.\n\nA different view is that equal worth does not require identical roles. Traditional Christians cite Ephesians 5:22-25, where wives submit and husbands love their wives as Christ loved the Church, and argue the roles are complementary: different gifts, one partnership. Orthodox Judaism describes the roles of men and women as separate but equal, with men obliged to time-bound commandments and women entrusted with the Jewish home through which identity and practice pass. The Roman Catholic Church ordains only men, holding that the priest acts in the person of Christ, who chose twelve men as apostles; in Islam the imam who leads mixed congregational prayer is male, while the Qur'an describes men as 'protectors and maintainers of women' (Surah 4:34), which many Muslims read as responsibility rather than superiority. On this view, insisting that everyone do the same thing is not equality but sameness, and it devalues the work mothers actually do.\n\nAgainst that, critics reply that separate roles have in practice been unequal ones: it is always women who are excluded from leadership, and the doctrine of difference has been used to keep them from education, pay and promotion, which is prejudice turned into discrimination.\n\nMy judgement is that the statement is right about religion and too strong about the family. Where a role carries authority - ordination, leading prayer, teaching - excluding women cannot be squared with Genesis 1:27 or Galatians 3:28, and the traditions that have opened these roles have lost nothing. Within a family, however, a couple may freely divide work between them without either being demeaned, provided the choice is theirs and not imposed. Equality is about worth and opportunity; it does not require every household to look the same.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Separate two questions the statement runs together: equal worth, and identical roles.","Each side has scripture behind it, so cite one text per side and explain what follows from it.","Test the opposing view against what has actually happened to women, and let the conclusion say where the statement holds and where it does not."],"codes":["3.2.1.1 Families and gender equality — Gender equality","3.2.1.1 Families and gender equality — The roles of men and women","3.2.1.1 Families and gender equality — Gender prejudice and discrimination, including examples"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which papal encyclical of 1968 taught that every marital act must remain open to the transmission of life?","a":"Humanae Vitae, issued by Pope Paul VI."},{"q":"What is the get?","a":"The Jewish bill of divorce, given by a husband to his wife before a beit din, which ends the marriage."},{"q":"Which words of Jesus, and at which chapter and verse of Mark, are used against divorce?","a":"'What God has joined together' - Mark 10:9."},{"q":"What is the difference between prejudice and discrimination?","a":"Prejudice is the attitude - judging someone in advance; discrimination is the unfair action taken because of it."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Anand Karaj","back":"The Sikh wedding, held before the Guru Granth Sahib, in which the couple circle the scripture four times as the Lavan hymns are sung. The Guru Granth Sahib calls the couple 'one light in two bodies'.","code":"3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — The nature and purpose of marriage","open":false},{"front":"Nikah and mahr","back":"The nikah is the Islamic marriage contract, requiring the free consent of both parties and witnesses; the mahr is the dower the husband gives to his wife, which remains hers. Marriage in Islam is a contract, not a sacrament.","code":"3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — The nature and purpose of marriage","open":false},{"front":"Get","back":"The Jewish bill of divorce, written and delivered before a beit din. Only when it is given does the marriage end and either partner become free to remarry in Orthodox Judaism. A refused get leaves the wife an agunah.","code":"3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Divorce, including reasons for divorce, and remarrying","open":false},{"front":"Annulment","back":"A ruling by a Catholic diocesan tribunal that a marriage was never valid, so no sacramental bond existed. It is not a divorce: the Church holds that a valid consummated marriage cannot be dissolved at all.","code":"3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Divorce, including reasons for divorce, and remarrying","open":false},{"front":"Humanae Vitae (1968)","back":"The encyclical of Pope Paul VI teaching that every marital act must remain open to the transmission of life. It is the basis of Roman Catholic rejection of artificial contraception as against natural law.","code":"3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Contraception and family planning","open":false},{"front":"Natural family planning","back":"Spacing births by using the infertile parts of a woman's cycle. It is the only method Roman Catholic teaching permits. Anglicans, and most other Protestant churches, have accepted artificial methods (the Anglican Lambeth Conference of 1930 was the first).","code":"3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Contraception and family planning","open":false},{"front":"Cohabitation","back":"An unmarried couple living together. Common in Britain today. Many religious believers object that it withholds the public lifelong promise that protects the couple and any children; others see it as a step towards marriage.","code":"3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Same-sex marriage and cohabitation","open":false},{"front":"Prayers of Love and Faith","back":"Prayers of blessing for same-sex couples, commended by the Church of England's House of Bishops in December 2023. The Church of England still teaches that marriage is between a man and a woman and does not conduct same-sex weddings.","code":"3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Human sexuality including: heterosexual and homosexual relationships","open":false},{"front":"Adultery","back":"Sex between a married person and someone who is not their partner. Forbidden by 'You shall not commit adultery' (Exodus 20:14) and by the Qur'an's prohibition of zina, and condemned by every tradition on the specification.","code":"3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Sexual relationships before and outside of marriage","open":false},{"front":"Chastity","back":"Refraining from sexual activity outside marriage. Taught by the Roman Catholic Church and by Islam, and supported by Paul's instruction to 'Flee from sexual immorality' (1 Corinthians 6:18).","code":"3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Sexual relationships before and outside of marriage","open":false},{"front":"Polygamy","back":"Marriage to more than one partner at once. The Qur'an permits up to four wives on condition of justice, and says marry only one if you fear you cannot be just (Surah 4:3). Not lawful in Britain.","code":"3.2.1.1 Families and gender equality — Contemporary family issues","open":false},{"front":"Nuclear family and extended family","back":"The nuclear family is parents and their children; the extended family adds grandparents, aunts, uncles and cousins in one household or nearby. Many religious communities in Britain keep extended patterns, especially for caring for elderly parents.","code":"3.2.1.1 Families and gender equality — The nature of families","open":false},{"front":"The sanctity of marriage vows","back":"The argument that promises made freely before God were meant to be permanent, so a marriage may not simply be ended. It is weighed in evaluations against compassion for people in a broken or abusive marriage.","code":"3.2.1.1 Sex, marriage and divorce — Ethical arguments related to divorce","open":false},{"front":"Created in the image of God (Genesis 1:27)","back":"Genesis says God made humanity in his image, 'male and female'. Jews and Christians use it to argue that men and women share equal worth; Paul's 'neither male nor female' (Galatians 3:28) is used the same way.","code":"3.2.1.1 Families and gender equality — Gender equality","open":false},{"front":"Prejudice and discrimination","back":"Prejudice is an attitude, judging someone before knowing them; discrimination is acting on it. Examples of gender discrimination: unequal pay, blocked promotion, exclusion from education or from religious leadership. The Equality Act 2010 makes it unlawful in Britain.","code":"3.2.1.1 Families and gender equality — Gender prejudice and discrimination, including examples","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Evaluate: 'Same-sex couples should be able to marry in a religious ceremony.')","items":["The paragraph: Those who support the statement argue that the tradition's own definition of marriage already fits same-sex couples. The Religious Society of Friends (Quakers), the Unitarians and Liberal and Reform Judaism all conduct same-sex marriages, and they reason from what they believe marriage is: a faithful, public, lifelong commitment in which two people help each other to live well, which is a good thing wherever it is found. Christians who take this view point out that Jesus taught nothing about same-sex relationships but a great deal about love of neighbour, and that the Church has already accepted that marriage is not only for procreation, since it marries couples who are too old to have children. The strongest objection is the Roman Catholic and Orthodox teaching that marriage is a sacrament between one man and one woman, ordered to the good of the spouses and the procreation of children, and that a church cannot bless what its scriptures do not recognise; the Catechism insists at the same time that homosexual people be treated with respect, compassion and sensitivity. On balance the first argument is stronger within those traditions that already treat marriage as primarily a covenant of love, but it has little force against a tradition for which procreation is written into the definition - which is why the honest conclusion is that this question is settled differently by different religious bodies, and English law was right to let each decide for itself rather than compel any of them.","Why this is Level 4: it gives reasoned consideration of different points of view, with a logical chain of reasoning on each side rather than a list of opinions; references to religion are applied to the issue (Quaker and Reform Jewish practice, Catholic sacramental teaching, the Catechism) instead of being mentioned in passing; and it reaches a judgement supported by that knowledge, saying which argument works where and why. That matches 'A well-argued response, reasoned consideration of different points of view. Logical chains of reasoning leading to judgement(s) supported by knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information. Reference to religion applied to the issue.'"]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same statement, visibly weaker)","items":["The paragraph: I think same-sex couples should be allowed to get married in a religious ceremony because everyone should be treated the same and it is not fair if some people can have a wedding and others cannot. Love is love and they are not hurting anybody. On the other hand some religious people disagree and say marriage should only be between a man and a woman because that is what they have always believed. So both sides have a point, but I think they should be allowed.","Why this is only Level 2: it does show 'Recognition of different points of view, each supported by relevant reasons/evidence', which is the Level 2 descriptor, and the reason given for the first view is a real one. What it misses is what Level 3 requires - 'Logical chains of reasoning that draw on knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information' and 'Clear reference to religion'. No tradition, church, movement or text is named; 'some religious people' is not a reference to religion applied to the issue, and 'that is what they have always believed' is an assertion rather than a chain of reasoning. The last sentence announces a preference instead of justifying one, so it is not the judgement Level 4 asks for. To move up it would need the named positions of particular religious bodies, the reason each gives, and a conclusion that follows from those reasons."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Christianity on relationships and families","items":["Marriage: a lifelong covenant made before God; in Roman Catholic and Orthodox teaching one of the sacraments, but only the Roman Catholic Church holds that a valid consummated marriage cannot be dissolved, while the Orthodox churches grant ecclesiastical divorce and permit remarriage with a penitential rite.","Purposes in Anglican teaching: the lifelong union of the couple in heart, body and mind; the foundation of family life in which children are nurtured; mutual comfort and help in good times and bad.","Genesis 2:24 - husband and wife 'become one flesh'; used for the unity and exclusiveness of marriage.","Mark 10:9 - Jesus' teaching 'What God has joined together', the central text against divorce.","Matthew 19:9 - Jesus allows an exception for sexual immorality; the text Protestant churches use to permit divorce.","Exodus 20:14 - 'You shall not commit adultery', one of the Ten Commandments, shared with Judaism.","1 Corinthians 6:18 - Paul tells the Corinthians to 'Flee from sexual immorality'; the New Testament basis for chastity outside marriage.","Contraception: Humanae Vitae (Pope Paul VI, 1968) rejects artificial contraception and permits natural family planning; the Church of England has accepted responsible family planning as a matter of conscience since the Lambeth Conference of 1930.","Divorce and remarriage: Roman Catholics may apply to a diocesan tribunal for an annulment; the Church of England has allowed remarriage in church at the priest's discretion since 2002.","Same-sex relationships: the Catechism of the Catholic Church teaches that homosexual people must be accepted with respect, compassion and sensitivity, while holding that sexual acts belong within marriage between a man and a woman; the Religious Society of Friends (Quakers) and the Unitarians conduct same-sex marriages; the Church of England's House of Bishops commended Prayers of Love and Faith in December 2023, though the Church does not marry same-sex couples.","Women's ministry: the Church of England ordained its first women priests in 1994 and consecrated its first woman bishop in 2015; the Roman Catholic Church ordains only men.","Gender equality: Genesis 1:27, humanity created in God's image, 'male and female'; Galatians 3:28, in Christ there is 'neither male nor female'.","Family duties: Exodus 20:12, 'Honour your father and mother'; Colossians 3:20, children are to obey their parents; Ephesians 6:4, fathers are not to exasperate their children.","Roles: Ephesians 5:22-25 is used by traditional Christians for complementary roles - wives submit, husbands love their wives as Christ loved the Church - while many Christians read it alongside Ephesians 5:21 as mutual submission and treat marriage as an equal partnership."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Islam on relationships and families","items":["Marriage (nikah) is a contract rather than a sacrament: both parties must consent, there are witnesses, and the bride receives a mahr (dower) which remains hers.","Surah 30:21 - God created spouses for one another so that husband and wife may find tranquillity, love and mercy together.","Sex outside marriage (zina) is forbidden; the Qur'an warns believers not to go near it (Surah 17:32).","Polygamy: Surah 4:3 permits up to four wives on condition of justice between them, and adds that a man who fears he cannot be just should marry only one. Polygamous marriages cannot be contracted in Britain.","Contraception: most Muslims accept reversible methods to space children, protect a mother's health or avoid serious hardship; permanent sterilisation and methods acting after conception are widely rejected.","Divorce: permitted but strongly discouraged, and often described as the most disliked of the things God has allowed. Talaq is followed by the iddah waiting period of about three months, during which reconciliation is encouraged; a wife may seek a divorce through khul.","Parents: Surah 17:23-24 commands kindness to parents, especially in old age; a well-known hadith teaches that heaven lies at the feet of mothers.","Roles: Surah 4:34 describes men as 'protectors and maintainers of women', which many Muslims read as responsibility rather than superiority; the mother's role in the home commands great respect.","Equality: Surah 49:13 teaches that all people were created from a single male and female and made into peoples and tribes so that they might know one another.","Worship: in a mixed mosque the imam leading congregational prayer is male, and women pray in a separate area."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Judaism on relationships and families","items":["Marriage (kiddushin) is a mitzvah; the ketubah is the marriage contract setting out the husband's obligations to his wife.","Genesis 1:28 - 'Be fruitful and multiply' is treated as a duty to have children, so family life is a religious obligation and not only a private choice.","Divorce: allowed but regulated. The marriage ends when the husband gives his wife a get before a beit din; a woman whose husband refuses one is an agunah, a chained wife.","Contraception: Orthodox authorities generally permit it to protect the mother's health or to space children, preferring the contraceptive pill because it does not interfere with the act itself; Reform Judaism allows it for social and financial reasons too.","Roles: Orthodox Judaism teaches separate but equal roles - men and women sit apart in the synagogue and only men count towards an Orthodox minyan - while Reform and Liberal Judaism seat men and women together and have ordained women as rabbis in Britain since the 1970s.","Adultery is forbidden by 'You shall not commit adultery' (Exodus 20:14), and Jewish teaching treats it as a betrayal of the covenant of marriage.","Educating children in the faith: Deuteronomy 6:7 tells parents to teach the commandments to their children, and the home, the Shabbat meal and preparation for Bar or Bat Mitzvah are where it happens.","Honouring parents: 'Honour your father and mother' (Exodus 20:12) is one of the Ten Commandments, and the Talmud extends it to the wider care of parents in old age."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: non-religious views and the law in Britain","items":["Humanists take their morality from reason, empathy and the consequences of actions rather than from revelation or scripture, and judge a relationship by whether it is honest, consenting and good for those in it.","Humanists UK supports same-sex marriage, access to contraception, and divorce where a relationship has irretrievably broken down, arguing that no one is helped by keeping a dead marriage alive.","Humanist marriage ceremonies are legally recognised in Scotland (since 2005) and in Northern Ireland; in England and Wales a humanist ceremony must still be accompanied by a civil registration.","Atheists commonly argue that marriage is a human institution that societies have shaped for their own purposes, so its rules may be changed as society changes, rather than being fixed by God.","Marriage (Same Sex Couples) Act 2013: same-sex marriage became lawful in England and Wales, with the first weddings in 2014. Religious organisations must opt in, and the Church of England is barred in law from conducting them.","Civil Partnership Act 2004: created civil partnerships for same-sex couples; they were opened to opposite-sex couples in 2019.","Divorce, Dissolution and Separation Act 2020: brought in no-fault divorce in England and Wales from April 2022, so neither partner need blame the other.","Equality Act 2010: makes sex discrimination in work, education and the provision of services unlawful, with narrow exceptions that allow some religious posts to be restricted.","Bigamy is a criminal offence in Britain, so a polygamous marriage cannot lawfully be contracted here.","Cohabitation before marriage has become common in British society, which is why the specification requires awareness of contrasting perspectives in contemporary Britain and not only of religious teaching."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'Marriage is the only right place for a sexual relationship.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion.","items":["Decode the statement: 'only' makes it absolute, so the question is not whether marriage is a good place for sex but whether any other place can ever be acceptable. Two issues sit underneath it - sex before marriage and homosexual relationships - and both are on the specification's list of prescribed contrasting issues.","Timing: about twelve minutes on the answer, plus the three SPaG marks, so plan for two minutes' thinking, roughly four paragraphs, and a minute to check spelling and the specialist terms (covenant, chastity, cohabitation, sacrament, zina).","Paragraph 1, in support: sexual union is the sign of the lifelong covenant. Source: Genesis 2:24, husband and wife 'become one flesh'. Develop with Roman Catholic teaching on chastity and with 1 Corinthians 6:18, 'Flee from sexual immorality'.","Paragraph 2, in support, from another tradition: Islam forbids zina and the Qur'an warns believers not to go near it (Surah 17:32); many Muslims keep practices of modesty to guard the teaching. Add the non-religious supporting argument that sex risks a child who then lacks a settled home.","Paragraph 3, a different point of view: many Christians in Britain, including many Anglicans, hold that faithful commitment matters more than the ceremony; Quakers and Liberal and Reform Judaism marry same-sex couples, so 'marriage' itself is contested; cohabitation before marriage is now ordinary in British society.","Paragraph 4, the non-religious view: humanists argue morality rests on consent, honesty and consequences, so an honest, committed and careful relationship harms nobody and cannot be wrong simply for lacking a ceremony.","The evaluation thread running through all four paragraphs: what is the moral work being done by the wedding itself - does the ceremony create the commitment, or only recognise it publicly?","The conclusion must do three things: answer the question directly, say which arguments were the stronger and why, and follow from what has already been written. For example: the teaching is right that sex belongs with lifelong commitment, and marriage is the clearest form that commitment takes, but 'only' cannot be sustained against a couple whose commitment is already permanent - so the statement is a sound rule and a weak absolute."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"The rubric of Theme A in one place","items":["The three prescribed contrasting issues: contraception; sexual relationships before marriage; homosexual relationships. On each you must be able to explain contrasting beliefs using Christianity, the main religious tradition in Britain, plus one or more other religious traditions.","Christianity is the main religious tradition of Great Britain and the religious traditions of Britain are diverse: you may draw on Christianity, Buddhism, Hinduism, Islam, Judaism and Sikhism, and on non-religious beliefs such as atheism and humanism.","Sources of wisdom and authority you may cite: the Bible, the Catechism of the Catholic Church, the Qur'an and Hadith, the Torah and Talmud, the Vedas and Upanishads, the Pali Canon, and the Guru Granth Sahib.","Never write 'religious people believe'. Name the tradition, and inside Christianity name the denomination - Roman Catholic, Church of England, Orthodox, Quaker - because the contrasts on this theme run within Christianity as often as between religions.","Contrast means opposing views or genuinely different views; two beliefs that turn out to be similar are credited only once, so check that your two paragraphs really disagree."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T14:09:31.354183Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.86,"notes":"All six fixes landed and five are clean. Indissolubility is now Roman Catholic only, with the Orthodox position stated truly (ecclesiastical divorce on grounds such as adultery, remarriage with a penitential rite) in both the lesson idea and the Christianity fact file; Prayers of Love and Faith are correctly 'commended by the House of Bishops in December 2023' in three of the four places; question 10's stem is now the generic Component 2 form with `a` opening the tradition and `sol` marking Christianity as a choice; question 6's contrast now reaches outside Christianity and the Muslim position (reversible methods for spacing or the mother's health, sterilisation rejected) is accurately stated; the plan title carries the five bullets; cards[5] is accurate at 39 words. Re-reading the whole kit against the original brief, everything checked before still holds: the eleven scripture references, all tariffs at 1/2/4/5/12, SPaG on the three 12-mark stems only, no quotation over five words, no printed June 2023 Theme A question reproduced, both model paragraphs correctly levelled, all fifteen card backs within 40 words and every sol within the brief's word bands. What remains is one stale hint pair and one meta sentence on question 6, and one uncorrected 'authorising' in question 7's sol — three small edits, none of them a wrong reference or misattribution, and the kit publishes once they are made. Three residuals hand-fixed after re-judge (Q6 hints, Q6 sol tail, Q7 wording)."}}},"3.2.1.2":{"id":"AQA-8062","topic":"3.2.1.2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Theme B is one of the four themes you answer in Component 2, and it carries a whole five-part question of 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks, with the spelling, punctuation and grammar marks riding on the evaluation. It is the theme where having an opinion is easiest and earning marks is hardest: every part rewards named teaching from a named tradition applied to abortion, euthanasia, animal experimentation, the environment and the origins of the universe and of human life. Two ideas decide most of the marks - sanctity of life and quality of life - and a student who can argue with both, from Christianity and from at least one other tradition, and who can add a humanist or atheist view where the paper invites it, has the whole theme under control.","idea":[{"h":"The rubric that governs every answer on Theme B","t":"The specification asks for three things at once: religious teachings, the religious, philosophical and ethical arguments around them, and contrasting perspectives in contemporary British society. Three issues are named as ones you must be able to explain contrasting beliefs about, with reference to Christianity, the main religious tradition in Britain, and one or more other religious traditions: abortion, euthanasia and animal experimentation. Section 3.2.1 adds that you must be aware of non-religious beliefs such as atheism and humanism, and must make specific reference to sources of wisdom and authority - the Bible, the Catechism of the Catholic Church, the Qur'an and Hadith, the Torah and Talmud, the Vedas and Upanishads, the Guru Granth Sahib, the Pali Canon. A top-level answer therefore never opens with 'religious people think'. It names the tradition - Roman Catholic, Church of England, Sunni Muslim, Orthodox Jewish, Theravada Buddhist - names the source, and sets a genuinely different view beside it.","code":"3.2.1.2 — The theme's own requirement: religious teachings, contrasting perspectives, and the three prescribed issues"},{"h":"Where the universe came from: creation and the Big Bang","t":"Genesis 1:1 opens 'In the beginning God created', and Genesis 1 sets the work out over six days with rest on the seventh; Christians express this as creation out of nothing, by God's word alone, with God as designer and sustainer rather than as one cause among others. The specification asks for different interpretations of these teachings, and they differ sharply: young-earth creationists read the six days as six days, while the Roman Catholic Church and the Church of England read Genesis as a religious account of who made the world and why rather than a scientific report of how. Set against this is the Big Bang theory, which dates the beginning of the universe to about 13.8 billion years ago; its first form was proposed by Georges Lemaitre, a Belgian Catholic priest, which is a useful reminder that science and religion have not always been enemies. Three relationships are arguable: conflict, independence and complement. A top-level answer says which reading of Genesis it is arguing from, and whether the two accounts are answering the same question at all.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The origins of the universe"},{"h":"Stewardship, dominion, responsibility, awe and wonder","t":"Five terms are named and you should be able to use all five. Dominion comes from Genesis 1:28, where humanity is told to 'subdue it' and rule the creatures: read as ownership it has been blamed for centuries of exploitation. Stewardship comes from Genesis 2:15, where the human being is placed in the garden 'to work it and keep it', and from Psalm 24:1, 'The earth is the Lord's' - creation is God's property, held in trust. Responsibility is the sting in that: a trustee is answerable, and Muslims express the same idea by calling the human being khalifah, God's steward on earth (Surah 2:30), who will answer on the Day of Judgement. Awe and wonder, the response of Psalm 19:1, 'The heavens declare the glory of God', supplies a reason for valuing creation that has nothing to do with what it is worth to us. Pope Francis's encyclical Laudato Si' (2015) puts all of this into modern language. A top-level answer plays dominion against stewardship rather than listing them, and says which reading the evidence supports.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The value of the world and the duty to protect it"},{"h":"Use and abuse: natural resources and pollution","t":"The specification's own framing is 'use and abuse', so using the world is not itself the wrong: the argument is about where use becomes abuse. The two named examples are the use of natural resources - fossil fuels are finite, and burning them drives climate change, while renewables are not - and pollution of air, land and water, including plastic waste and the destruction of habitats. Religious teaching is applied to both. Jewish law forbids needless destruction under bal tashchit, drawn from Deuteronomy 20:19, which forbids cutting down fruit trees even in a siege; Muslims teach that God created everything in balance and that upsetting the balance is a wrong against creation; Christian bodies such as A Rocha, Christian Aid and CAFOD campaign on the ground that pollution falls hardest on the poorest people, whom believers are commanded to love. A top-level answer never leaves the teaching floating: it ties a named teaching to a named abuse and says what the believer is therefore obliged to do.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The use and abuse of the environment"},{"h":"Animals: experimentation and food","t":"Animal experimentation is one of the three prescribed contrasting issues, so you must be able to argue it from Christianity and from at least one other tradition. In Britain animal research needs a licence under the Animals (Scientific Procedures) Act 1986, and licences for testing cosmetics on animals have not been issued since 1998. Christian arguments rest on Genesis 1:27, that only human beings are made in the image of God, and on dominion: the Catechism of the Catholic Church allows medical experimentation on animals within reasonable limits where it helps to care for or save human lives, while teaching that causing animals to suffer or die needlessly is contrary to human dignity. Hindu and Buddhist arguments rest on ahimsa, non-violence to every living thing, and on rebirth and karma, which make the line between animal and human far less absolute. On food, Jewish shechita and Muslim halal slaughter permit meat but regulate the killing, while many Hindus are vegetarian and Buddhists are divided. A top-level answer contrasts the traditions and then judges, rather than reporting both and stopping.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The use and abuse of animals"},{"h":"Where human life came from: creation and evolution","t":"The structure repeats, with evolution in place of the Big Bang. Genesis 1:27 teaches that human beings are made in the image of God, and Genesis 2:7 that the first man was formed 'from the dust of the ground' and given the breath of life; Muslims teach that Adam was created from clay and given a soul by God. The interpretations again differ: creationists hold that human beings were made directly and specially; theistic evolutionists, including the Roman Catholic Church and most Anglicans, hold that evolution by natural selection is the process God used and that the soul of each person is still created directly by God; others read Genesis as a story carrying the truth that human beings are willed and loved rather than accidental. Charles Darwin published On the Origin of Species in 1859, and Richard Dawkins argues that natural selection explains the appearance of design without any designer. A top-level answer identifies what the disagreement is actually about - mechanism, or meaning - before taking a side.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — The origins of life"},{"h":"Sanctity of life against quality of life","t":"This is the hinge of the whole second half of the theme. Sanctity of life holds that human life is holy because God gives it and because human beings are made in God's image (Genesis 1:27), so it may not be deliberately ended whatever its condition: Exodus 20:13, 'You shall not murder'; Job's words that 'the Lord has taken away'; Paul's claim that 'your body is a temple' (1 Corinthians 6:19). Quality of life judges by what the life is actually like - freedom from unbearable pain, awareness, dignity, the ability to have relationships and to make choices. Neither side denies the other's facts; they disagree about which fact decides. Both the abortion and the euthanasia bullets below turn on that choice, and the specification names both again as the ethical arguments under abortion. A top-level answer names both ideas, says which it is letting decide, and gives the reason - and the best answers notice that quality of life is a judgement about a person, not a measurement of one.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — The concepts of sanctity of life and the quality of life"},{"h":"Abortion, including when the mother's life is at risk","t":"The Abortion Act 1967, as amended in 1990, permits abortion in Great Britain up to 24 weeks with the agreement of two doctors, and later where the woman's life is at risk, she would suffer grave permanent injury, or the child would be seriously disabled. The religious argument turns first on when life begins: Roman Catholic teaching takes conception, and the Catechism of the Catholic Church says that human life must be respected and protected absolutely from that moment (paragraph 2270); most Sunni scholars date ensoulment at 120 days, following a hadith; Orthodox Jewish law holds that the foetus is not yet a full person, so the mother's life takes precedence. The specification singles out the hard case because that is where the traditions divide. Catholic teaching handles it by the doctrine of double effect: treating an ectopic pregnancy is permitted because the death of the embryo is foreseen but not intended. The Church of England holds that the foetus has a right to life but that abortion may be justified in strictly limited circumstances. A top-level answer uses the mother's-life case to show exactly where the traditions part company.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — Abortion, including situations when the mother's life is at risk"},{"h":"The two ethical arguments the specification names","t":"The document asks for the ethical arguments, and names them: sanctity of life and quality of life. In the abortion debate, the sanctity argument says that if a human being exists from conception then abortion is the deliberate killing of an innocent, and no further weighing is needed; the quality argument asks what the life of the child and of the mother would actually be, and treats a life of severe suffering, or a pregnancy forced on a child or caused by rape, as morally relevant. Rights language sits on top of both: the right to life against the right to choose what happens in one's own body. Humanists reason from sentience, consent and consequences rather than from commands, and Humanists UK supports a woman's right to choose, though some non-religious people oppose abortion on the ground that a human life is a human life whether or not anyone is watching. A Level 4 answer weighs the two named arguments against each other rather than listing what each religion says about them.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — Ethical arguments related to abortion"},{"h":"Euthanasia: the words, the law and the arguments","t":"Get the vocabulary exact, because the marks follow it. Voluntary euthanasia is where the person asks; non-voluntary is where they cannot ask; active euthanasia is an act that ends the life, passive is the withdrawal of treatment; assisted suicide is helping someone to end their own life. In England and Wales euthanasia would be prosecuted as murder or manslaughter, and assisting a suicide is an offence under the Suicide Act 1961, though Parliament has repeatedly debated changing the law; Dignity in Dying campaigns for change and Care Not Killing against it. Religious arguments run from sanctity of life: the Catechism of the Catholic Church calls euthanasia a grave wrong against human dignity, while allowing burdensome treatment to be refused and pain relief that may shorten life; the Qur'an forbids taking a life God has made sacred (Surah 17:33); Jewish law forbids hastening death but does not demand every possible treatment. Set against these are autonomy, compassion and quality of life, and against those the fear of pressure on the frail. Palliative care is the third way: Dame Cicely Saunders founded St Christopher's Hospice in London in 1967. A top-level answer weighs autonomy and compassion against sanctity and risk, and then decides.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — Euthanasia"},{"h":"Death, the afterlife, and what a life is worth","t":"Christians believe in resurrection and judgement: the Apostles' Creed professes 'the resurrection of the body', and Jesus says in John 11:25, 'I am the resurrection and the life'; Catholics also teach purgatory. Muslims believe in akhirah - the dead wait in Barzakh until the Day of Judgement, and are raised, judged and sent to Jannah or Jahannam. Hindus believe the atman is reborn life after life according to karma until moksha frees it from samsara. Judaism concentrates on this life while holding a range of beliefs about olam ha-ba, the world to come. Humanists hold that this life is the only one there is. The specification asks for the impact of these beliefs on the value of human life, and the impact cuts both ways: a judgement to come makes what is done here far more serious, while a life that continues beyond death may make dying less final and so weaken the case for clinging to every last week. A top-level answer uses the afterlife belief as a reason inside the abortion or euthanasia argument rather than describing it for its own sake.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — Beliefs about death and an afterlife"}],"examples":[{"title":"The one-mark multiple choice: define first, then eliminate","setup":"The question reads: Which one of the following means looking after the world on God's behalf? A Awe, B Dominion, C Evolution, D Stewardship. You put a tick in the box next to the correct answer, and there is a single mark for it.","steps":["Fix the definition you are hunting for before reading the options again: a duty to care for something that belongs to someone else, and to answer to that owner for it.","Clear the option that comes from science rather than from religious teaching about the world - evolution explains how species change, and says nothing about how anyone ought to treat them.","Clear the feeling word: awe is the wonder creation produces in a believer, not a task a believer is given.","Separate the two duties that are left. One is the right to rule creation; the other means holding creation in trust for its owner, which is what the question described, so tick D and move on inside a minute."],"cues":["What exactly is the question asking you to define?","Which option is a scientific theory rather than a duty?","Which option names a feeling rather than a job?","Of the two duties left, which one implies an owner to answer to?"]},{"title":"The two-mark question: two bare points, then stop","setup":"The question reads: Give two religious beliefs about the origins of the universe. It is worth two marks, one for each correct point, no development is required and only your first two answers are marked wherever they appear on the page.","steps":["Write the first belief as a bare statement, because nothing beyond the point itself is rewarded here: God created the universe out of nothing.","Make the second belief a genuinely different claim rather than the first one reworded: the creation was carried out over six days, as Genesis 1 sets it out.","Put the pen down. A third belief cannot rescue a weak first one, and the time it costs belongs to the twelve-mark evaluation at the end of the theme."],"cues":["What is the shortest form of a creation belief that still counts as a point?","How can the second point avoid simply repeating the first?","Why is writing a third answer a waste rather than an insurance policy?"]},{"title":"The four-mark contrast: make the two beliefs collide","setup":"The question reads: Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about abortion. In your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions. It is worth four marks, two plus two: a simple explanation of each contrast earns one mark and a detailed explanation earns two, and if the two beliefs turn out to be similar only one of them can be credited.","steps":["Choose two positions that cannot both be held, rather than two shades of the same caution: Roman Catholic opposition from the moment of conception against the Orthodox Jewish rule that the mother's life takes precedence.","Write the first with its reason attached, since the reason is what lifts a simple explanation into a detailed one: Catholic teaching holds that human life must be protected from conception, so the embryo already has the standing of a person and abortion is the deliberate killing of an innocent.","Open the second paragraph with a word that marks the clash - whereas, by contrast - so the examiner can see the contrast without hunting for it, and name the tradition you are drawing on, because the instruction demands it.","Develop the second the same way: in Orthodox Judaism the foetus is not yet a full person, so where continuing the pregnancy would kill the mother the abortion becomes required rather than merely allowed."],"cues":["Which two positions actually collide?","What has to be added to a belief to make its explanation detailed?","How will the examiner see at a glance that the second belief contrasts?","What reason does the second tradition give for its rule?"]},{"title":"The five-mark question: two teachings and the mark for the source","setup":"The question reads: Explain two religious teachings about the duty to look after the environment. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer. It is worth five marks: four of them are two plus two for the teachings themselves, and the fifth is awarded separately for a relevant and accurate reference.","steps":["Take the first teaching from the specification's own vocabulary - stewardship - and state it plainly: God has entrusted the world to human beings, who must hand it on rather than use it up.","Develop it with what the teaching makes a believer actually do: reduce waste, support conservation work such as A Rocha's, and treat pollution as a wrong against God as well as against a neighbour.","Make the second teaching a different idea rather than the same one restated - accountability: Muslims hold that the human being is khalifah, God's trustee on earth, and will answer for the earth on the Day of Judgement.","Earn the fifth mark by naming a source instead of gesturing at one: Genesis 2:15 places the human being in the garden 'to work it and keep it', and Surah 2:30 calls the human being a khalifah on the earth."],"cues":["Which word from the specification gives you the first teaching?","What turns this teaching into a detailed explanation rather than a simple one?","How is the second teaching different in kind from the first?","What form of reference would an examiner accept as relevant and accurate?"]},{"title":"Planning the twelve-mark evaluation before writing a word","setup":"The question reads: 'A person should be allowed to choose when to die.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. It carries twelve marks plus three for spelling, punctuation and grammar, and the paper allows about twelve minutes.","steps":["Decode the statement before planning: it is a claim about autonomy at the end of life, so the subject is voluntary euthanasia and assisted dying for a competent adult, not decisions taken for someone who cannot speak.","Plan the supporting case as one chain rather than a list: unbearable suffering with no prospect of recovery, the person's own settled and repeated wish, and the Christian argument from compassion that a God of love cannot will a death drawn out in agony.","Plan the opposing case to the same standard: life is God-given and not ours to end, the Catechism of the Catholic Church calls euthanasia a grave wrong against human dignity, Muslims hold that the hour of death belongs to God, and palliative care of the kind Cicely Saunders built offers a third way.","Add the view from outside religion on each side, because the bullets invite it: humanists argue from autonomy and from consequences, while Care Not Killing points out that pressure on the frail to stop being a burden is a consequence too.","Fix the conclusion now rather than discovering it later: decide which argument carried more weight and why, so that the judgement grows out of the reasoning instead of arriving as a fresh opinion in the last line."],"cues":["What is the statement actually about, and what is it not about?","What would make the supporting case a chain rather than a list?","Which named teachings answer that case directly?","What can be added here that is not a religious argument at all?","What must the final paragraph do that the earlier ones did not?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which three issues does the specification name as ones you must be able to explain contrasting beliefs about, and which traditions must the contrast use?","a":"Abortion, euthanasia and animal experimentation - contrasted with reference to Christianity, the main religious tradition in Britain, and one or more other religious traditions."},{"q":"What is the difference between dominion and stewardship, and where does each come from?","a":"Dominion is the rule over creation given in Genesis 1:28; stewardship is the duty to care for creation on God's behalf, from Genesis 2:15 and Psalm 24:1, and it makes the human being answerable for how the world is treated."},{"q":"Why does the question of when life begins decide so much of the abortion argument?","a":"Because if a person exists from that moment, abortion is the killing of a person. Catholic teaching takes conception, most Sunni scholars date ensoulment at 120 days, and Orthodox Jewish law holds the foetus is not yet a full person, so the mother's life takes precedence."},{"q":"What does 'sanctity of life' claim that 'quality of life' does not?","a":"That human life is holy because God gives it, so its value does not depend on its condition and it may not be deliberately ended; quality of life judges by what the life is actually like."}]},"room":{"facts":["Theme B is one five-part question of 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks, and the paper advises 25 minutes on a theme - about a minute a mark.","The 12-mark evaluation on Component 2 also carries 3 SPaG marks, and only the best of the four themes' SPaG marks counts towards the paper.","On the three prescribed contrasting issues - abortion, euthanasia and animal experimentation - the 4-mark question adds the instruction to refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain (Christianity) and one or more other religious traditions; on the other topics of this theme it is set as \"Explain two contrasting religious beliefs about ...\".","The fifth mark on the 5-mark question is awarded separately for a relevant and accurate reference to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching.","The three prescribed contrasting issues on this theme are abortion, euthanasia and animal experimentation, argued from Christianity plus one or more other religious traditions.","The five named religious teachings about the world are stewardship, dominion, responsibility, awe and wonder; the two named scientific views are the Big Bang theory and evolution.","Sanctity of life and quality of life are the two ideas the second half of the theme turns on, and the specification names both again as the ethical arguments about abortion."],"questions":[{"q":"Which one of the following means looking after the world on God's behalf? A Awe B Dominion C Evolution D Stewardship","a":"AO1:1. Answer: D Stewardship.","sol":"D Stewardship. Awe is the wonder creation inspires in a believer; dominion is the rule over creation given in Genesis 1:28; evolution is a scientific theory about how species change, not a duty laid on anyone.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The answer is a job given to human beings, not a feeling and not a scientific theory.","Two of the four options are not duties at all, so clear those first.","Of the two duties left, one means ruling creation and the other means caring for something that still belongs to its owner."],"codes":["3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The value of the world and the duty to protect it"]},{"q":"Which one of the following means the belief that all human life is holy and God-given? A Euthanasia B Quality of life C Sanctity of life D Secularism","a":"AO1:1. Answer: C Sanctity of life.","sol":"C Sanctity of life. Euthanasia is ending a life to relieve suffering; quality of life judges a life by what it is actually like; secularism is the view that religion should not direct public life.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The question defines a belief about the value of life itself, not an action taken and not a view about religion's place in public life.","One option names something people do, so it can go straight away.","Of the options left, one measures a life by its condition - the answer does the opposite."],"codes":["3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — The concepts of sanctity of life and the quality of life"]},{"q":"Which one of the following means helping a person to end their own life? A Abortion B Assisted suicide C Palliative care D Pollution","a":"AO1:1. Answer: B Assisted suicide.","sol":"B Assisted suicide, which is an offence in England and Wales under the Suicide Act 1961. Abortion ends a pregnancy; palliative care controls pain and distress without hastening death; pollution is damage done to the environment.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["One option has nothing to do with human life and death at all.","Of the three that remain, one concerns the beginning of a life rather than its end.","The last two both concern the dying, but only one of them involves the death being brought about."],"codes":["3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — Euthanasia"]},{"q":"Give two religious beliefs about the origins of the universe. [2 marks]","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points, no development required; only the first two answers are marked.","sol":"Two bare points are all that is wanted. First: God created the universe out of nothing, by his word alone. Second: the creation was carried out over six days, as Genesis 1 sets it out. Nothing beyond the points themselves is credited, and a third answer would be ignored.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["This part is about what believers hold happened at the beginning, not about why they hold it.","No development is rewarded here, so a single clear sentence is a complete answer for each mark.","Make the second point a different claim rather than the first one reworded, or only one of them can count."],"codes":["3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The origins of the universe"]},{"q":"Give two ways in which religious believers might look after the environment. [2 marks]","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct ways; only the first two answers are marked.","sol":"Cutting waste at home and in the place of worship, by recycling and using less energy. Supporting a conservation or development charity such as A Rocha, Christian Aid or CAFOD, including campaigning on climate change.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The question asks what believers do, not what they believe.","One way can be something done privately and the other something done with others.","Naming a real organisation is a safe way of making the second point plainly different from the first."],"codes":["3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The use and abuse of the environment"]},{"q":"Give two reasons why many Christians are opposed to abortion. [2 marks]","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct reasons; only the first two answers are marked.","sol":"Human beings are made in the image of God, so a human life is sacred from the moment it begins. The commandment 'You shall not murder' (Exodus 20:13) forbids the deliberate killing of an innocent human being.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Both reasons should be about the status of the life in the womb, not about how a woman feels.","One reason can come from a belief about how human beings were made, the other from a command.","Keep each reason to one sentence: development earns nothing on this part."],"codes":["3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — Abortion, including situations when the mother's life is at risk","3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — The concepts of sanctity of life and the quality of life"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about abortion. In your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions. [4 marks]","a":"AO1:3. Marked 2 + 2 - simple explanation of a relevant and accurate contrast 1 mark, detailed explanation 2 marks, for each belief. The beliefs must genuinely differ; if similar beliefs are given only one may be credited, to a maximum of 2.","sol":"Roman Catholic teaching is that abortion is always seriously wrong. The Catechism of the Catholic Church holds that human life must be respected and protected absolutely from the moment of conception, so the embryo already has the standing of a person, and Catholics regard abortion as the deliberate killing of an innocent human being that no circumstance can make good.\n\nBy contrast, Orthodox Jewish teaching holds that the foetus is not yet a full person and that the mother's life takes precedence over the pregnancy. Where doctors judge that continuing would kill the mother, an abortion is not merely permitted but required, because the life already fully established outweighs the life not yet begun.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The two beliefs have to collide: choose positions that could not both be held by the same person.","Each belief needs its reason attached, because a point on its own is only a simple explanation.","Name the tradition each belief comes from, and open the second paragraph with a word that signals the clash."],"codes":["3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — Abortion, including situations when the mother's life is at risk","3.2.1.2 — The theme's own requirement: religious teachings, contrasting perspectives, and the three prescribed issues"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about using animals in medical experiments. In your answer you should refer to the main religious tradition of Great Britain and one or more other religious traditions. [4 marks]","a":"AO1:3. Marked 2 + 2; each contrast needs an explanation and a development. If similar beliefs are given only one may be credited, to a maximum of 2.","sol":"Many Christians accept medical experiments on animals. Genesis teaches that only human beings are made in the image of God, and that humanity is given dominion over the animals, and the Catechism of the Catholic Church allows medical experimentation on animals when it stays within reasonable limits and helps to care for or save human lives, so a Christian may support the testing of a new cancer drug while still condemning cruelty.\n\nHindus and Buddhists take a contrasting view. Ahimsa, non-violence towards every living thing, applies to animals as fully as to people, and because rebirth may be in an animal body the boundary between us and them is not absolute, so deliberately inflicting suffering on an animal is bad karma for the experimenter whatever the benefit to human beings.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The contrast is sharpest between a tradition that ranks human beings above animals and one that does not draw that line so firmly.","Each side needs the reasoning that produces its conclusion, not just the conclusion.","Name the tradition in each paragraph, since the instruction requires it."],"codes":["3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The use and abuse of animals","3.2.1.2 — The theme's own requirement: religious teachings, contrasting perspectives, and the three prescribed issues"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting religious beliefs about the origins of human life. [4 marks]","a":"AO1:3. Marked 2 + 2; the two beliefs must genuinely differ, and each needs development to reach the second mark.","sol":"Some Christians hold that God created human beings directly and specially. Genesis 1:27 teaches that human beings are made in the image of God, and creationists read the account literally, so humanity is not descended from other animals but was formed as human from the start; on this view evolution cannot be the true account of our origins.\n\nOther Christians, including the Roman Catholic Church and most Anglicans, contrast sharply with this. They accept the evidence for evolution by natural selection and treat it as the process God used, holding that Genesis teaches why human beings exist rather than how they came to be, while the soul of each person is still created directly by God.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The sharpest contrast on this issue is between two ways of reading the same text, not between two religions.","Say what each view does with the scientific evidence, because that is where the two part company.","Develop each belief with the reasoning behind it, and name the tradition or group that holds it."],"codes":["3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — The origins of life"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which belief in stewardship influences religious believers today. [4 marks]","a":"AO1:2. Marked 2 + 2 - simple explanation of a relevant and accurate influence 1 mark, detailed 2 marks. The second mark in each pair requires the influence itself: what believers actually do or decide differently.","sol":"Stewardship changes how believers consume. Christians who hold that the earth is God's and is held in trust cut waste, recycle, use less energy and buy fairly traded goods, because wasting resources is treated as misusing something that belongs to God rather than as a private choice about money.\n\nIt also pushes believers into campaigning. Since Pope Francis published Laudato Si' many Catholic parishes have switched to renewable energy and joined climate protests, and Christian Aid and CAFOD lobby the British government on emissions on the ground that pollution falls hardest on the world's poorest people, whom Christians are commanded to love.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["An influence question is answered with behaviour, not with belief.","For each way, finish the sentence 'so they...' - that is where the second mark lives.","Make one way personal and everyday and the other public or organised, so the two cannot be counted as the same."],"codes":["3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The value of the world and the duty to protect it","3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The use and abuse of the environment"]},{"q":"Explain two religious beliefs about the sanctity of life. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"AO1:1. Marked 2 + 2 for the two beliefs, plus one separate mark for a relevant and accurate reference to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching.","sol":"The first belief is that human life is holy because God made it. Christians teach that human beings are made in the image of God, which gives every life the same value whatever its age, health or usefulness, so a life may not be ended because somebody has judged it no longer worth living.\n\nThe second is that life is God's to give and to take. Believers hold that God alone decides when a life begins and when it ends, which is why Job, having lost everything, can say that 'the Lord has taken away' rather than claiming the right to decide for himself.\n\nScripture makes both explicit: Psalm 139:13 says that God 'knit me together in my mother's womb'.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Two beliefs are wanted, so the second must not simply restate the first in other words.","One belief can be about how human life was made, the other about who has the right to end it.","Do not leave the source implied: the fifth mark is given for naming it, and one clear reference is enough."],"codes":["3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — The concepts of sanctity of life and the quality of life"]},{"q":"Explain two religious teachings about the use of animals for food. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"AO1:1. Marked 2 + 2 for the two teachings plus one mark for the reference. Teachings may come from within one religion or from two.","sol":"One teaching is that animals may be eaten but must be killed with care. Jewish law permits only kosher meat, slaughtered by a trained shochet with a single cut of a flawless blade, and the rule tza'ar ba'alei chayim forbids causing any living creature needless pain; Muslim halal slaughter follows the same principle, with the name of God pronounced over the animal.\n\nA second teaching is that eating animals should be avoided altogether. Many Hindus are vegetarian because ahimsa forbids harming any living thing, and because the atman may be reborn in an animal body, so the cow in particular is honoured rather than eaten.\n\nGenesis 9:3 is the reference for the first teaching: God tells Noah that every living creature 'will be food for you'.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The two teachings pull in different directions, which is easier to write than two versions of the same rule.","Develop each with the detail an examiner can check - who performs the slaughter, what the rule forbids, why the animal is honoured.","Name the source plainly at the end, and make sure it supports one of the two teachings you have actually given."],"codes":["3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The use and abuse of animals"]},{"q":"Explain two religious beliefs about life after death. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"AO1:1. Marked 2 + 2 for the two beliefs plus one mark for the reference to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching.","sol":"Christians believe in resurrection and judgement. They hold that the dead will be raised as Jesus was and judged on how they have lived, which is why the Apostles' Creed professes 'the resurrection of the body', and why Christians treat this life as a preparation rather than as everything there is.\n\nHindus believe in samsara. The atman, the eternal self, is reborn in a new body life after life according to karma, until liberation, moksha, releases it from the cycle altogether; the present life is therefore one stage of a much longer journey rather than the only one.\n\nJesus' words in John 11:25, 'I am the resurrection and the life', are the reference a Christian answer would use.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Two beliefs from two traditions are easier to keep genuinely different than two from one.","Develop each with the technical term the tradition itself uses, which also helps the spelling and grammar marks later.","The fifth mark needs a named source: a creed or a text with its reference will both do."],"codes":["3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — Beliefs about death and an afterlife"]},{"q":"'Science and religion cannot both be right about how the universe began.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels-marked on the whole answer. In support: the literal six-day account against 13.8 billion years; a natural explanation leaves nothing for a creator to do (Dawkins). Other views: Genesis answers why and not how; the Big Bang as the mechanism of creation; Lemaitre; design and first-cause arguments. Level 4 needs both sides reasoned, religion applied to the issue, and a justified conclusion.","sol":"Those who support the statement point to a straightforward clash of accounts. Genesis 1 describes a universe made by God in six days and then finished, while cosmology describes a universe that began about 13.8 billion years ago and has been expanding ever since. Young-earth creationists accept the clash and reject the science, because if scripture is God's own word it cannot be corrected by human theories that change every generation. Atheists such as Richard Dawkins accept the same clash and reject the religion, arguing that once there is a natural explanation of how the universe began, adding a creator explains nothing further and is simply an extra thing to believe. Both sides agree on the crucial point: the two accounts answer the same question, and two different answers to one question cannot both be true.\n\nA different point of view is held by most religious believers in Britain, including the Roman Catholic Church and the Church of England, who read Genesis as a religious account of who made the world and why rather than as a scientific report of how. On that reading the six days are a framework carrying three claims - that the world is God's, that it is ordered, and that it is good - and modern cosmology neither makes nor denies any of them. The Big Bang then describes the mechanism of a creation whose author is God, and the fact that the theory's first form was proposed by Georges Lemaitre, a Belgian Catholic priest, is a useful reminder that the two have not always been enemies. Some believers press further and treat the Big Bang as evidence for God, since a universe with a beginning invites the question of what caused it, and the finely balanced conditions that allowed stars and life to form look to them like design.\n\nThe strongest reply to that is that it rescues religion by making it unfalsifiable: if no possible discovery could count against the creation account, the account has stopped saying anything definite about the universe at all. A humanist would add that the first-cause argument only moves the question back a step, because a creator would need explaining just as much as a singularity does.\n\nIn my judgement the statement is true only of one particular kind of religious belief. Where Genesis is read as literal history it does contradict the science, and there the statement holds exactly. But the reading held by most believers in Britain makes a claim about purpose and value that no measurement of the early universe could confirm or refute, so the two are answering different questions and can both be right. The statement is therefore too sweeping: science and religion conflict where they are made to compete, and most religious believers do not make them compete.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["The argument turns on one thing: whether the two accounts are answering the same question.","Build each side as a chain - claim, the named teaching or evidence it rests on, then the reasoning that joins them - before starting the other side.","Aim for two developed arguments each way, bring in a view from outside religion, and close by saying which side carried more weight and why."],"codes":["3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The origins of the universe","3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — The origins of life"]},{"q":"'The quality of a person's life matters more than its sanctity.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels-marked. In support: compassion, autonomy, the cruelty of keeping a suffering person alive, humanist reasoning, Dignity in Dying. Against: life made in God's image, the Catechism on the absolute protection of life, Surah 17:33, the slippery slope, palliative care. Level 4 weighs the two named ethical arguments and reaches a judgement that follows from them.","sol":"In support of the statement, the strongest argument is that sanctity of life can be cruel in practice. If human life is holy simply because it exists, then a patient in the last weeks of motor neurone disease, who can no longer move or swallow and whose pain cannot be fully controlled, must be kept alive against their settled wish; many people, religious and not, think that puts a doctrine above the person it is supposed to be about. Christians who take this view argue from compassion rather than against scripture: a God who is love cannot will a death drawn out in agony, and Jesus' command to love your neighbour is a command about the neighbour's actual condition. Humanists make the same case without religion at all, from autonomy: an adult of sound mind is the person best placed to judge whether their own life is still worth living, and Dignity in Dying argues that the law should respect that judgement with safeguards.\n\nA different point of view is that quality of life is exactly the wrong test, because it makes the worth of a life depend on somebody's assessment of it. The Catechism of the Catholic Church teaches that human life must be respected and protected absolutely from the moment of conception, and calls euthanasia a grave wrong against human dignity; the reasoning is that every human being is made in the image of God, so value is intrinsic and equal, and that once a society begins grading lives by quality the disabled, the demented and the very old are graded down first. Muslims hold that the hour of death belongs to God, and the Qur'an forbids taking a life God has made sacred (Surah 17:33). There is a practical reply as well: where good palliative care exists, of the kind Dame Cicely Saunders built at St Christopher's Hospice from 1967, the unbearable quality of life on which the whole argument depends is usually not unbearable at all.\n\nThat case is strengthened by noticing that quality of life is not a measurement but a judgement, and one that frightened or exhausted patients may make about themselves under pressure from the cost and trouble they believe they are causing others.\n\nIn my judgement the two ideas are not really rivals, and the statement is wrong to rank them. Sanctity of life explains why a life may not be thrown away; quality of life explains what is owed to a life that is suffering. The right conclusion is that quality should decide how a person is treated - which treatments are worth continuing, what pain relief is given - while sanctity decides that they may not be deliberately killed. Where the two genuinely collide, at the very end of a terminal illness, I find the religious case the stronger, because the risk of a society that weighs lives against one another is greater than the suffering that good palliative care leaves unrelieved.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Both of the ideas in the statement are named by the specification: define neither at length, but be clear which one you are letting decide.","Test the statement in the hardest case you can think of rather than in the abstract, and build each side as one chain of reasoning.","Non-religious arguments are invited here, and the last paragraph must say which argument carried more weight and why."],"codes":["3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — The concepts of sanctity of life and the quality of life","3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — Euthanasia","3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — Ethical arguments related to abortion"]},{"q":"'Experimenting on animals to develop new medicines is always wrong.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels-marked. In support: ahimsa and karma, tza'ar ba'alei chayim, Singer on speciesism, the availability of alternatives. Against: the image of God and dominion, the Catechism's limited permission, the regulation of research, the human lives saved. Level 4 tests the word 'always', applies religion to the issue and reaches a justified conclusion.","sol":"The argument for the statement begins from suffering. Animals used in medical research are bred to be made ill, and can neither consent nor understand why; the philosopher Peter Singer calls the automatic preference for our own species speciesism, and argues that if suffering is what makes an act wrong then the suffering of a dog counts in the same way as the suffering of a human being. Hindu and Buddhist teaching reaches a similar conclusion by a different route: ahimsa forbids violence towards any living thing, and since rebirth may be in an animal body the line between us and them is not fixed, so deliberately inflicting pain on an animal is bad karma for the person who does it however useful the result. Jewish teaching on tza'ar ba'alei chayim forbids causing needless pain to any living creature, and many people argue that with cell cultures and computer modelling now available a great deal of this pain has become needless.\n\nA different point of view is that the word 'always' cannot be defended. Most Christians hold that human beings alone are made in the image of God, and that Genesis 1:28 gives humanity dominion over the animals, so human life may rightly be given priority; the Catechism of the Catholic Church allows medical experimentation on animals when it stays within reasonable limits and helps to care for or save human lives, while insisting that causing animals to suffer or die needlessly is contrary to human dignity. That is a distinction rather than a licence: it condemns testing cosmetics, which has not been licensed in Britain since 1998, while permitting the work behind insulin, vaccines and modern cancer treatments. British law takes the same shape, since the Animals (Scientific Procedures) Act 1986 requires a licence and a justification, and weighs the benefit against the suffering caused.\n\nSupporters of the statement can reply that an act does not stop being wrong because it is regulated or because it is useful, and that a benefit to one species bought with the suffering of another is exactly what dominion has been used to excuse for centuries. Those who defend the research answer that refusing it has victims too, since the children who would have died of a disease are as real as the animals in the laboratory.\n\nIn my judgement the statement is right about the principle and wrong about the word 'always'. Ahimsa and the Jewish rule against needless suffering establish that animal pain matters in itself, and that most uses of animals fail that test - which is why the ban on cosmetic testing was right. But a treatment that saves human lives and could not have been developed in any other way is not needless suffering, and the traditions that permit it are not being careless; they are ranking two goods, both of which they take seriously. I would therefore reject 'always' and say instead that experimenting on animals is wrong wherever the benefit is trivial or the same knowledge could be obtained another way.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["One word in the statement decides how hard it is to defend - find it before you plan.","Build each side as a chain: the principle, the named teaching or law it rests on, and the conclusion it forces.","A non-religious argument is welcome on either side here, and the conclusion must answer the statement as it actually stands."],"codes":["3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The use and abuse of animals","3.2.1.2 — The theme's own requirement: religious teachings, contrasting perspectives, and the three prescribed issues"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What do stewardship and dominion each mean, and where does each come from?","a":"Stewardship is the duty to look after the world for God and answer for it, from Genesis 2:15; dominion is the rule over creation given to humanity in Genesis 1:28."},{"q":"Up to how many weeks does the Abortion Act, as amended in 1990, normally allow an abortion, and how many doctors must agree?","a":"Up to 24 weeks, with two doctors agreeing; later abortions are allowed only in limited circumstances, such as a serious risk to the woman's life."},{"q":"What is the difference between voluntary euthanasia and assisted suicide?","a":"In voluntary euthanasia another person ends the patient's life at their request; in assisted suicide the person ends their own life with help, which is an offence under the Suicide Act 1961."},{"q":"Which Belgian Catholic priest first proposed the theory that became the Big Bang?","a":"Georges Lemaitre."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Stewardship","back":"The duty to look after the world for God and hand it on in good order. Genesis 2:15 places the human being in the garden 'to work it and keep it'; Psalm 24:1 says 'The earth is the Lord's'.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The value of the world and the duty to protect it","open":false},{"front":"Dominion","back":"The rule over creation given to humanity in Genesis 1:28, which tells them to 'subdue it'. Read as ownership it has been blamed for exploitation; read as trusteeship it becomes stewardship.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The value of the world and the duty to protect it","open":false},{"front":"Khalifah","back":"Arabic for steward or trustee. Surah 2:30 calls the human being a khalifah on the earth, so Muslims hold that they will answer to God on the Day of Judgement for how creation was treated.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The value of the world and the duty to protect it","open":false},{"front":"Awe and wonder","back":"The amazement creation produces in a believer, which gives a reason to value it apart from its usefulness. Psalm 19:1: 'The heavens declare the glory of God'.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The value of the world and the duty to protect it","open":false},{"front":"Bal tashchit","back":"The Jewish rule against needless destruction, drawn from Deuteronomy 20:19, which forbids cutting down fruit trees even in a siege. It is applied today to waste, pollution and the destruction of habitats.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The use and abuse of the environment","open":false},{"front":"The Big Bang theory","back":"The scientific account of the universe beginning about 13.8 billion years ago and expanding ever since. Its first form was proposed by Georges Lemaitre, a Belgian Catholic priest.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The origins of the universe","open":false},{"front":"Theistic evolution","back":"The view, held by the Roman Catholic Church and most Anglicans, that evolution by natural selection is the process God used to create living things, with each human soul created directly by God.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — The origins of life","open":false},{"front":"Ahimsa","back":"Non-violence towards every living thing, central to Hinduism and Buddhism. It makes many Hindus vegetarian and supplies the main religious argument against animal experimentation.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of the universe — The use and abuse of animals","open":false},{"front":"Sanctity of life","back":"The belief that human life is holy because God gives it and human beings are made in his image, so it may not be deliberately ended, whatever its condition.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — The concepts of sanctity of life and the quality of life","open":false},{"front":"Quality of life","back":"The measure of what a life is actually like: freedom from unbearable pain, awareness, dignity, relationships and choice. It is the argument set against sanctity of life on abortion and euthanasia.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — The concepts of sanctity of life and the quality of life","open":false},{"front":"The Abortion Act 1967","back":"British law, amended in 1990, permitting abortion up to 24 weeks where two doctors agree, and later where the woman's life is at risk, she would suffer grave permanent injury, or the child would be seriously disabled.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — Abortion, including situations when the mother's life is at risk","open":false},{"front":"Ensoulment at 120 days","back":"Most Sunni scholars, following a hadith, hold that the soul enters the foetus at 120 days. Before that some permit abortion for serious reasons, and saving the mother's life is widely accepted as the lesser of two evils.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — Abortion, including situations when the mother's life is at risk","open":false},{"front":"The doctrine of double effect","back":"Catholic reasoning that an act done for a good purpose may be permitted although a bad effect is foreseen but not intended, as when treating an ectopic pregnancy ends the pregnancy.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — Ethical arguments related to abortion","open":false},{"front":"The kinds of euthanasia","back":"Voluntary: the person asks. Non-voluntary: they cannot ask. Active: an act ends the life. Passive: treatment is withdrawn. Assisted suicide: help given to someone to end their own life.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — Euthanasia","open":false},{"front":"Akhirah","back":"The Muslim belief in life after death: the dead wait in Barzakh until the Day of Judgement, when they are raised and judged and go to Jannah or Jahannam. It makes this life morally serious.","code":"3.2.1.2 The origins and value of human life — Beliefs about death and an afterlife","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Evaluate: 'Abortion is always wrong.')","items":["The paragraph: Those who support the statement argue from the sanctity of life, and the argument is at its strongest where it starts. The Catechism of the Catholic Church teaches that human life must be respected and protected absolutely from the moment of conception, so the embryo is not a potential person but a person with potential; on that view abortion is the deliberate killing of an innocent human being, and since nothing can make the killing of the innocent good, the word 'always' is exactly right. The obvious objection is the mother's life, and Catholic teaching answers it without abandoning the principle: under the doctrine of double effect a doctor may treat an ectopic pregnancy even though the treatment will end it, because the death is foreseen but not intended. The strongest reply comes from Orthodox Judaism, which reads the same commandment against murder and still rules that where the birth would kill the mother her life takes precedence, since the foetus is not yet a full person; a humanist would put it more bluntly, that a being with no awareness cannot be wronged in the way a woman with a life, a family and a future can be wronged. On balance the sanctity argument succeeds in showing that abortion is always serious and usually wrong, but it does not establish 'always', because the traditions that hold life most sacred have themselves made room for the case where one life can only be saved by ending another.","Why this is Level 4: it gives reasoned consideration of different points of view - Roman Catholic, Orthodox Jewish and humanist - as chains of reasoning rather than as a list of opinions; the references to religion are applied to the issue (the Catechism on conception, double effect in the ectopic case, the precedence of the mother's life) instead of being mentioned in passing; and the judgement grows out of the reasoning by testing the one word the statement depends on. That matches 'A well-argued response, reasoned consideration of different points of view. Logical chains of reasoning leading to judgement(s) supported by knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information. Reference to religion applied to the issue.'"]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same statement, visibly weaker)","items":["The paragraph: I think abortion is always wrong because it is killing a baby, and killing is wrong. Christians believe that life is sacred and that you should not murder, so they are against abortion. Some people say it should be allowed if the mother might die, or if she is very young and could not cope, and that it is her body and therefore her choice. So there are different opinions about abortion and people should be allowed to decide for themselves.","Why this is only Level 2, and what it misses: it states a point of view with reasons and it recognises that other views exist, each with some reason attached, which is the Level 2 descriptor - 'Recognition of different points of view, each supported by relevant reasons/evidence' - and the mention of Christian belief lifts the ceiling the level rules impose, 'Maximum of Level 2 if there is no reference to religion' - that rule holds an answer down at Level 2 rather than raising it to Level 2, so referring to religion is what makes Levels 3 and 4 reachable at all. What it misses is what Levels 3 and 4 ask for. The reference to religion is vague ('Christians believe'): no tradition is named, no source is given and no teaching is applied to the issue, so there is no 'logical chain of reasoning that draws on knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information' and no 'clear reference to religion' in the sense Level 3 means. The opposing view is reported rather than reasoned. And the final sentence dodges the question instead of answering it, so it cannot earn 'judgement(s) supported by knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information'."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Christian teaching on the origins and value of life and the world","items":["Origins of the universe: God created out of nothing, by his word. Genesis 1:1 - 'In the beginning God created' - and Genesis 1 sets the work over six days with rest on the seventh.","Different interpretations: young-earth creationists read the six days literally; the Roman Catholic Church and the Church of England read Genesis as a religious account of who made the world and why, compatible with the Big Bang and with evolution.","Origins of human life: human beings are made in the image of God (Genesis 1:27) and formed 'from the dust of the ground' (Genesis 2:7) - the basis of the sanctity of life.","Stewardship: Genesis 2:15 places the human being in the garden 'to work it and keep it'; Psalm 24:1, 'The earth is the Lord's', makes creation God's property, held in trust.","Dominion: Genesis 1:28 tells humanity to 'subdue it' and rule the creatures - read as rule it has been blamed for exploitation, read as trusteeship it becomes stewardship.","Awe and wonder: Psalm 19:1, 'The heavens declare the glory of God' - a reason for valuing creation that has nothing to do with its usefulness.","Modern teaching on the environment: Pope Francis's encyclical Laudato Si' (2015) treats care for creation as a moral duty and links pollution and climate change to injustice towards the poor.","Named Christian organisations: A Rocha (conservation), and Christian Aid, CAFOD and Tearfund (development work and climate campaigning).","Animals: the Catechism of the Catholic Church teaches that animals are entrusted to human stewardship, that medical experimentation on animals is acceptable within reasonable limits where it helps to care for or save human lives, and that causing animals to suffer or die needlessly is contrary to human dignity.","Abortion: the Catechism of the Catholic Church teaches that human life must be respected and protected absolutely from the moment of conception (paragraph 2270), so abortion is gravely wrong at every stage.","The doctrine of double effect: a Catholic doctor may treat an ectopic pregnancy or a cancerous womb although the treatment will end the pregnancy, because that effect is foreseen but not intended.","The Church of England holds that the foetus has a right to life from conception, while accepting that abortion may be justified in strictly limited circumstances, such as a serious risk to the mother's life or health.","Euthanasia: the Catechism of the Catholic Church calls euthanasia a grave wrong against human dignity; at the same time a Catholic may refuse burdensome or disproportionate treatment, and may accept pain relief that shortens life as a side effect.","Death and the afterlife: Christians believe in the resurrection of the body and judgement - the Apostles' Creed professes 'the resurrection of the body', and Jesus says in John 11:25, 'I am the resurrection and the life'; Catholics also teach purgatory.","Palliative care: Dame Cicely Saunders, a Christian, founded St Christopher's Hospice in London in 1967, and the hospice movement is the main Christian answer to the demand for euthanasia."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Islamic teaching on the origins and value of life and the world","items":["Origins of the universe: Allah creates by command - the Qur'an (Surah 2:117) describes God saying to a thing 'Be', and it is. Creation is deliberate and there is no partner in it.","Science: many Muslims accept the Big Bang as a description of how God created, and point to Surah 21:30, which speaks of the heavens and the earth having been joined and then parted.","Origins of human life: Adam was created by God from clay and given life and a soul; human beings are honoured above the rest of creation and are accountable for what they do with that standing.","Khalifah: Surah 2:30 calls the human being a khalifah - a trustee or steward - on the earth, answerable to God on the Day of Judgement for how creation has been treated.","Balance: Muslims teach that God created everything in due measure and balance (mizan), so waste, greed and pollution are wrongs against God's own ordering of the world.","Animals: the Qur'an describes the creatures as 'communities like you' (Surah 6:38). Animals may be used and eaten but not abused; halal slaughter requires a swift cut and the name of God pronounced over the animal.","Abortion: life is a gift from God, and the Qur'an forbids killing children (Surah 17:31). Most Sunni scholars date ensoulment at 120 days, and abortion to save the mother's life is widely accepted as the lesser of two evils.","Euthanasia: the hour of death belongs to God alone, and the Qur'an forbids taking a life that God has made sacred (Surah 17:33), so voluntary euthanasia and assisted suicide are rejected by Muslim scholars.","Death and the afterlife: akhirah - the dead wait in Barzakh until the Day of Judgement, when they are raised, judged and sent to Jannah or Jahannam, which makes what is done in this life enormously serious."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Jewish teaching on the origins and value of life and the world","items":["Origins: the Torah opens with God creating the heavens and the earth (Genesis 1:1); many Jews treat the six days as a structure for the account rather than as a diary of the work.","Bal tashchit: 'do not destroy', drawn from Deuteronomy 20:19, which forbids cutting down fruit trees even in a siege; it is applied today to waste, pollution and the destruction of habitats.","Tikkun olam: the duty of repairing the world, leaving it better than it was found.","Animals: tza'ar ba'alei chayim forbids causing needless pain to any living creature; kosher slaughter (shechita) is carried out by a trained shochet with a single cut of a flawless blade, and the Torah sets out which animals may be eaten (Deuteronomy 14).","The commandment of Sabbath rest extends to working animals as well as to people (Exodus 20:10).","Abortion: the mother's life takes precedence. The Mishnah teaches that where the birth threatens the mother's life the pregnancy may be ended, because the foetus is not yet a full person; Orthodox Judaism rejects abortion for convenience.","Pikuach nefesh: saving a life overrides almost every other commandment, which shapes Jewish argument at both ends of life.","Euthanasia: deliberately hastening death is forbidden, but Jewish law does not require that a dying patient be kept alive by every possible means.","Death and the afterlife: Judaism concentrates on how this life is lived, while holding a range of beliefs across Orthodox and Reform Judaism about olam ha-ba, the world to come."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Hindu and Buddhist teaching used on this theme","items":["Hindu origins: creation is cyclical - the universe is created, sustained and destroyed over vast ages - and there is more than one creation account in the Hindu scriptures, so no single literal timetable is at stake.","Atman and samsara: the self is eternal and is reborn life after life according to karma, until moksha releases it from the cycle.","Ahimsa: non-violence towards every living thing is the central ethical principle, and it is the main Hindu and Buddhist argument against animal experimentation.","Food: many Hindus are vegetarian, and the cow is honoured as a symbol of selfless giving rather than eaten.","The Buddhist first precept is to abstain from taking life, so killing an animal for research generates bad karma for the person who does it.","Abortion: generally opposed on the grounds of ahimsa and karma, though Hindus also weigh the duty (dharma) owed to the family, and some accept abortion where the mother's life is at risk.","Euthanasia: Buddhists disagree among themselves - the first precept weighs against it, while compassion (karuna) and the wish to relieve suffering are given weight by some; many Hindus hold that a death deliberately brought forward disturbs the working out of karma."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: non-religious views, the law in Britain and the named organisations","items":["Humanism: Humanists UK holds that this is the only life there is, that morality comes from reason, evidence and concern for human welfare, and that the wishes and wellbeing of the person concerned should decide.","Humanists UK supports a woman's right to choose an abortion, and supports legal assisted dying for terminally ill adults with safeguards; not every non-religious person agrees with either position.","Utilitarian reasoning weighs the suffering and the happiness an action causes; Peter Singer calls the automatic preference for our own species 'speciesism' and argues that animal suffering counts.","Richard Dawkins argues that evolution by natural selection explains the appearance of design without any designer; other scientists, religious and not, treat science and religion as answering different questions.","The Abortion Act 1967, amended by the Human Fertilisation and Embryology Act 1990, permits abortion in Great Britain up to 24 weeks with the agreement of two doctors, and later where the woman's life is at risk, she would suffer grave permanent injury, or the child would be seriously disabled.","Euthanasia would be prosecuted as murder or manslaughter in England and Wales, and assisting a suicide is an offence under the Suicide Act 1961; Parliament has repeatedly debated changing the law on assisted dying.","Dignity in Dying campaigns for legal assisted dying; Care Not Killing, whose members include religious and medical bodies, campaigns against it and for better palliative care.","The Animals (Scientific Procedures) Act 1986 requires a licence for animal research in the UK and weighs the benefit against the suffering caused; licences for testing cosmetics on animals have not been issued in the UK since 1998.","The Big Bang is dated to about 13.8 billion years ago, and Charles Darwin set out evolution by natural selection in On the Origin of Species in 1859."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'Euthanasia should be made legal in Britain.' Evaluate this statement.","items":["The stem as the paper sets it: the statement in quotation marks, then 'Evaluate this statement.' and the five bullets - reasoned arguments in support, reasoned arguments for a different point of view, religious arguments, non-religious arguments if you wish, and a justified conclusion. Twelve marks, plus three for spelling, punctuation and grammar.","Decode it first: 'euthanasia' here means voluntary euthanasia and assisted dying for a competent adult who asks, not decisions taken for someone who cannot ask; 'made legal in Britain' makes this a question about the law and its consequences, not only about whether one act would be right.","Timing: about twelve minutes - two planning, four on the supporting case, four on the opposing case, two on the conclusion.","Paragraph one, in support: autonomy. A competent adult is the person best placed to judge whether their own life is bearable, and Dignity in Dying argues that the present law forces the dying either to travel abroad or to die badly. Christians who agree argue from Jesus' command to love your neighbour, applied to the neighbour's actual suffering.","Paragraph two, in support: quality of life. Where pain cannot be controlled and the illness is terminal, keeping a person alive serves a principle rather than a person; a humanist would add that nobody benefits from the extra weeks, and that compassion is the whole of the case.","Paragraph three, against: sanctity of life. Life is God-given and not ours to end - the Catechism of the Catholic Church calls euthanasia a grave wrong against human dignity, and Muslims hold that the hour of death belongs to God (Surah 17:33). Jewish law forbids hastening death while not requiring every possible treatment to be given.","Paragraph four, against: what a change in the law would do. Care Not Killing warns of pressure on the old, the ill and the disabled to stop being a burden, and points to palliative care of the kind Cicely Saunders built at St Christopher's Hospice from 1967 as the alternative that removes the demand.","The evaluation thread running through all four paragraphs: the question is not whether suffering is bad, which nobody denies, but whether a law can permit some deaths without endangering other people.","What the conclusion must do: say which side carried more weight, say why in terms of the arguments already made, and answer the statement as it stands, including the word 'legal' - for example that the moral case for an individual act may be strong while the case against a general law remains stronger, or the reverse, provided the reason is given rather than asserted.","SPaG: use the theme's own vocabulary accurately - voluntary and non-voluntary euthanasia, active and passive, assisted suicide, palliative care, sanctity of life, quality of life, autonomy - because a wide range of specialist terms used correctly is part of the three marks."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"The rubric of Theme B in one place","items":["The three issues you must be able to explain contrasting beliefs about: abortion, euthanasia and animal experimentation - with reference to Christianity, the main religious tradition in Britain, and one or more other religious traditions.","The wider requirement of section 3.2.1: awareness of different religious perspectives within and between religions, and of non-religious beliefs such as atheism and humanism; and specific reference to sources of wisdom and authority, including scripture.","Two statements you are expected to know: the religious traditions of Great Britain are, in the main, Christian, and the religious traditions in Great Britain are diverse.","The five named teachings about the world: stewardship, dominion, responsibility, awe and wonder.","The two named scientific views: the Big Bang theory, for the origins of the universe, and evolution, for the origins of life.","The two named ethical arguments about abortion: sanctity of life and quality of life - and both apply again to euthanasia.","Sources you may cite: the Bible, the Catechism of the Catholic Church, the Qur'an and Hadith, the Torah and Talmud, the Vedas and Upanishads, the Guru Granth Sahib, the Pali Canon and the sermons of the Buddha."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T14:13:47Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.95,"notes":"Re-judged against the kit file itself. All seven fixes landed and none introduced an error: questions[6] and [7] and lesson.examples[2].setup now carry the 2026 specimen's prescribed-issue line verbatim, which is what the specification requires for abortion and animal experimentation, and both sols already supplied Christianity plus a second tradition (Catechism 2270 against Orthodox Judaism; Genesis 1:27 and the Catechism's limited permission against ahimsa and karma), so stem and answer key now agree; questions[8] is back to the plain June 2023 form with its hints still consistent and its within-Christianity contrast legitimate under \"similarities and differences within and/or between religions\"; facts[2] now states the rule correctly for both cases; questions[4] is AO1:1, which makes every AO label right (AO1:1 on the 1, 2 and 5-mark parts, AO1:3 on the contrasts, AO1:2 on the stewardship-influence question, AO2 on the evaluations); and the Level 2 rationale now describes the cap as a ceiling. Everything checked in the first pass still holds - all sixteen biblical references, all six Qur'anic, Catechism 2270, the Apostles' Creed, the Mishnah and the 120-day ensoulment hadith, the Abortion Act 1967 as amended in 1990 with 24 weeks and two doctors in Great Britain, the Suicide Act 1961, ASPA 1986, the 1998 end of cosmetics licensing, Lemaitre, Darwin 1859, Laudato Si' 2015 and St Christopher's Hospice 1967; tariffs are 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 4, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 5, 12, 12, 12; all three 12-mark stems carry the Component 2 bullets and [Plus SPaG 3 marks] and no shorter question mentions SPaG; no scripture quotation exceeds seven words; and no June 2023 or 2026 specimen Theme B question is reproduced. Ready to publish."}}},"3.2.1.3":{"id":"AQA-8062","topic":"3.2.1.3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Theme C is one of the four themes you answer in Component 2, and it is the one theme where the paper is not really asking what a religion does but whether what it claims is true. That makes it the easiest theme to answer badly, because a student who has only opinions can fill a page and still sit in Level 1. The marks here go to named arguments used properly: the Design argument and the First Cause argument with the weaknesses the specification insists you also know, the argument from miracles with an example you can date and place, evil and suffering and the science arguments on the other side, and the three kinds of knowledge of the divine - special revelation, enlightenment and general revelation. This is also the only theme whose prescribed contrast is not a second religion: on visions, miracles and nature as general revelation you must set Christianity, the main religious tradition in Britain, against non-religious beliefs such as atheism and humanism. This room gives you both sides of every argument, the two named examples the specification demands, and the vocabulary of the divine that an examiner rewards twice, once for the point and once for the specialist term.","idea":[{"h":"The rubric that governs every answer on this theme","t":"The specification asks for three things at once: religious teachings, the religious and philosophical arguments around them, and their impact and influence in the modern world, with awareness of contrasting perspectives in contemporary British society. Three issues are named as ones you must be able to contrast - visions, miracles, and nature as general revelation - and here the contrast prescribed is not a second religion but the main religious tradition in Britain, Christianity, set against non-religious beliefs such as atheism and humanism. You may still draw on Buddhism, Hinduism, Islam, Judaism and Sikhism, and you must be able to say that the religious traditions of Great Britain are in the main Christian and also diverse. Sources of wisdom and authority are expected: the Bible, the Catechism of the Catholic Church, the Qur'an and Hadith, the Torah and Talmud, the Vedas and Upanishads, the Pali Canon and the Guru Granth Sahib. A top-level answer therefore never writes 'religious people think' or 'science says'. It names the tradition and the group within it, and it treats the humanist position as a case with reasons behind it rather than as an absence of belief.","code":"3.2.1.3 — The theme's own requirement: religious and philosophical arguments, and the three prescribed issues"},{"h":"The Design argument: from order to a designer","t":"The argument moves from what we observe - order, regularity and apparent purpose in the natural world - to the conclusion that it was designed, and that the designer is God. Its classic English form is William Paley's, in Natural Theology (1802): a watch found lying on a heath has parts fitted together for a purpose, so we infer a watchmaker, and the eye, the wing and the seed are fitted together far more intricately than any watch. Thomas Aquinas made the same move in the fifth of his Five Ways, arguing that things without intelligence act towards an end and so must be directed by something intelligent. Modern defenders point to fine-tuning: the physical constants of the universe sit in the narrow range that allows any life at all. Psalm 19:1 is the standard scriptural support, that 'The heavens declare the glory of God'. The specification requires the weaknesses too, so an answer that only states the argument is capped: evolution by natural selection, set out in Darwin's On the Origin of Species (1859), explains apparent design without a designer, which is why Richard Dawkins called natural selection a blind watchmaker; David Hume objected that the universe is not much like a machine and that the analogy is too weak to carry so large a conclusion; disease, parasites and stillbirth look like bad design; and even a successful argument gives you a designer, not the loving, personal God of Christianity. A top-level answer uses a weakness as a hinge - it states the argument, presses the strongest objection, and then says whether the objection actually defeats it.","code":"3.2.1.3 Philosophical arguments for and against the existence of God — The Design argument"},{"h":"The First Cause argument: why is there anything at all?","t":"Thomas Aquinas set this out in the Summa Theologica as the second of his Five Ways: everything we observe is caused by something else, nothing causes itself, and a chain of causes cannot stretch back for ever, so there must be a first, uncaused cause, and this is what people call God. It suits Christian teaching, because Genesis 1:1 begins with the claim that 'In the beginning God created'. Its strength is that it takes seriously a question science cannot dismiss: not how the universe developed, but why there is a universe rather than nothing. The weaknesses the specification requires are just as important. The obvious one is that the argument seems to break its own rule - if everything must have a cause, God must too, and if God can be uncaused, why not the universe itself? Bertrand Russell took exactly that line, saying the universe simply is, with no explanation needed. Others argue that the Big Bang is a scientific account of a beginning that needs no personal cause at all, and that even if the argument works, a first cause need not be good, loving or interested in human beings. A top-level answer keeps the two halves separate: whether the argument proves something started the universe, and whether that something is God.","code":"3.2.1.3 Philosophical arguments for and against the existence of God — The First Cause argument"},{"h":"The argument from miracles, and the example you must be able to name","t":"A miracle is usually defined as an event that appears to break the laws of nature and is attributed to the action of God. The argument runs that such events happen, that no natural explanation covers them, and that they are therefore evidence of a God who is immanent and answers prayer. The scriptural example Christians use most is Jesus calming a storm on the Sea of Galilee with the words 'Peace! Be still!' (Mark 4:39), after which the disciples ask who this can be that even wind and sea obey. The specification requires one worked example, so it is safest to prepare a modern one with dates: the Roman Catholic Church has recognised seventy cures at Lourdes as miraculous since the apparitions of 1858, the most recent being that of Sister Bernadette Moriau, announced in 2018, and every claim is examined first by the Lourdes Medical Bureau. Miracles are also one of the three prescribed contrasting issues, so you must be able to give the non-religious side: David Hume argued that testimony can never establish a miracle, because mistaken or dishonest witnesses are always more likely than a broken law of nature; humanists add that an unexplained recovery is not the same as an inexplicable one, and that miracle claims from rival religions cancel one another out. A top-level answer says which kind of evidence a miracle actually is - testimony - and then judges how much weight testimony can bear.","code":"3.2.1.3 Philosophical arguments for and against the existence of God — The argument from miracles"},{"h":"Evil and suffering as an argument against God","t":"This is the strongest argument on the atheist side and the specification names it as an argument against the existence of God. It is usually set out as an inconsistent triad: God is omnipotent, so he could stop suffering; God is omnibenevolent, so he would want to; yet suffering plainly exists. Two kinds are distinguished - moral evil, the harm human beings choose to do, and natural evil, the harm that comes from earthquakes, disease and famine, which nobody chose. The religious replies are part of the content, not an optional extra. The free will defence, associated with Augustine, argues that a world of genuine love requires genuine freedom, and freedom that cannot be misused is not freedom. The soul-making reply, developed by John Hick in Evil and the God of Love (1966) from Irenaeus, argues that courage, compassion and patience could not exist in a world without hardship. The Book of Job answers differently: God replies to Job out of the whirlwind (Job 38) and Job is told that he cannot see the whole picture. Christians also point to the cross, where Jesus cries 'why have you forsaken me?' (Mark 15:34), and say that God shares suffering rather than watching it. A top-level answer notices that each reply covers some suffering and not all of it, and judges on that basis rather than declaring a winner.","code":"3.2.1.3 Philosophical arguments for and against the existence of God — Evil and suffering as an argument against the existence of God"},{"h":"Arguments from science against the existence of God","t":"The claim is that natural explanations now do the work God was once invoked to do. The Big Bang accounts for the origin of the universe about 13.8 billion years ago without any personal creator; evolution by natural selection accounts for the complexity of living things; and the study of the brain offers accounts of religious experience in terms of brain activity rather than divine visitation. A further, harder objection is that religious claims cannot be tested and so cannot be counted as knowledge at all. Richard Dawkins argues in The God Delusion (2006) that God is an unnecessary hypothesis. The replies are equally part of the theme. Science answers how and not why: even a complete physical account of the Big Bang leaves open why there is anything to explain. Many religious believers have been scientists of the first rank - Georges Lemaitre, the Belgian Catholic priest who proposed the expanding universe (1927) and the primeval atom (1931), and Francis Collins, who led the Human Genome Project and wrote The Language of God (2006). The Roman Catholic Church holds evolution to be compatible with faith, and Pope John Paul II told the Pontifical Academy of Sciences in 1996 that evolution is more than a hypothesis; the Church of England takes the same view, while creationist Christians who read Genesis 1 literally do not. A top-level answer distinguishes the claim that science disproves God from the much weaker claim that science makes God unnecessary.","code":"3.2.1.3 Philosophical arguments for and against the existence of God — Arguments based on science against the existence of God"},{"h":"Special revelation, and the example of a vision you must be able to name","t":"Special revelation is knowledge of the divine given directly to a particular person or group, rather than available to everyone: visions, dreams, prophecy, a voice in prayer, and for Christians supremely the incarnation, since John 1:14 says that 'The Word became flesh'. The specification carefully writes 'God, gods or ultimate reality', so the theme is not framed for monotheism alone. Named examples matter, because one is required. Moses meets God at the burning bush and is given the name 'I am who I am' (Exodus 3:14). Saul is blinded on the road to Damascus and hears a voice asking 'why do you persecute me?' (Acts 9:4), and the vision turns a persecutor into the apostle Paul. The best modern example to learn with dates is Roman Catholic: Bernadette Soubirous, aged fourteen, reported eighteen apparitions of a lady at the grotto of Massabielle in Lourdes, in southern France, between February and July 1858; on 25 March the lady told her 'I am the Immaculate Conception'; Bernadette was made a saint in 1933. Note the division within Christianity here, since most Protestants do not accept Marian apparitions as revelation. Visions are one of the three prescribed contrasting issues. A top-level answer keeps the two questions apart: what the person experienced, and what would make the rest of us believe them.","code":"3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — Special revelation"},{"h":"Enlightenment: knowledge reached from within","t":"The specification gives enlightenment its own bullet alongside special and general revelation, and it is the material that stops this theme becoming a Christianity-only topic. Enlightenment is knowledge of ultimate reality reached through insight, discipline and meditation rather than given from outside by a God. Siddhartha Gautama became the Buddha - the title means the awakened one - while meditating under the Bodhi tree at Bodh Gaya, and what he understood was the Four Noble Truths and the way out of the cycle of rebirth. Buddhism has no creator God, so this is knowledge of how things really are rather than a message from a person. Hinduism speaks of moksha, release, and the Upanishads teach that the self is one with Brahman, summed up in the phrase 'That thou art' (Chandogya Upanishad 6). Enlightenment stretches the word revelation, and that is exactly its exam value: it is the readiest evidence that religions do not all locate knowledge of the ultimate in the same place. A top-level answer uses it to test any statement that begins 'the only way to know God is', because the Buddhist case shows the question can be posed without a God at all.","code":"3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — Enlightenment as a source of knowledge about the divine"},{"h":"General revelation: nature and scripture","t":"General revelation is knowledge of the divine that is open to anyone, and the specification names two routes: nature and scripture. Nature is the one it prescribes as a contrasting issue. Christians read the order, scale and beauty of the world as pointing beyond itself, and Psalm 19:1 is the standard text, that 'The heavens declare the glory of God'; Paul argues in Romans 1:20 that God's invisible qualities have been visible in what has been made ever since the world began, which is why believers say nobody has an excuse for missing them. Scripture is general revelation because it is written down, public and available to be read, taught and preached, even though the events it records were themselves special revelation. The non-religious contrast is precise and should be stated precisely: humanists share the awe and deny the inference, saying that a sunset or a galaxy is astonishing without being a message, and that calling it a revelation adds nothing to the explanation. Some Christians go further in the other direction, arguing that nature alone gives at best a creator and that scripture is needed for anything more. A top-level answer distinguishes feeling awe from drawing a conclusion, because that is exactly where the two sides part company.","code":"3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — General revelation: nature and scripture"},{"h":"The ideas of the divine that these sources produce","t":"The specification lists three matched pairs, and they should be used precisely because they earn marks twice, once for the point and once as specialist vocabulary. Omnipotent means all-powerful: the Apostles' Creed opens by calling God the Father almighty, and Genesis 1:1 has God create everything from nothing. Omniscient means all-knowing, past, present and future, which is what makes God's foreknowledge and human free will hard to hold together. A personal God can be addressed, loved and obeyed - Jesus teaches his followers to pray 'Our Father in heaven' (Matthew 6:9) - whereas an impersonal ultimate reality is a force or ground of being that cannot be spoken to, as Brahman without form is described in Hinduism. Immanent means present and active within the world, which is what a miracle, an answered prayer and the incarnation all assert; transcendent means beyond the universe, unlimited by space and time and beyond full human grasp, as when Isaiah 55:8 has God say 'my thoughts are not your thoughts'. Christians hold several of these together: God is both immanent and transcendent, both personal and beyond description. A top-level answer shows which source produced which idea - a vision suggests a personal God, the order of nature suggests a designer, and enlightenment suggests an impersonal ultimate reality - because that link is what the specification means by ideas that come from these sources.","code":"3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — Different ideas about the divine that come from these sources"},{"h":"The value of revelation, and the objections written into the content","t":"The specification does not simply ask whether revelation is valuable; it writes the objections into the content, which is what makes a genuinely two-sided evaluation possible on any revelation question. The first objection is that revelations conflict. The God revealed to Christians is three persons in one; the God of Surah 112:1, 'Say, He is Allah, the One', is emphatically not; Buddhist enlightenment reveals no creator at all. They cannot all be accurate descriptions of the same reality, so at most one tradition's revelation is reliable, and possibly none. The second is that alternative explanations exist: hallucination, illness, exhaustion, fasting, grief, expectation, or ordinary events remembered as extraordinary. The third the specification states bluntly - the people who claimed these experiences may have been lying, or simply mistaken. Believers answer that a revelation can be tested: does it change the person's life, is it consistent with existing teaching, is there physical or medical evidence, and did others witness it? The Lourdes Medical Bureau and the Church's long refusal to certify most claimed cures are used as evidence that believers are not credulous. A top-level answer accepts that special revelation convinces the one who receives it far more easily than anyone else, and then asks what that difference is worth as evidence.","code":"3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — The value of general and special revelation and enlightenment"}],"examples":[{"title":"The opening multiple choice: work out the idea, then eliminate","setup":"The question reads: Which one of the following means that God is present and active within the world? A Immanent, B Omnibenevolent, C Personal, D Transcendent. You put a tick in one box, and nothing else you write on the page can rescue a wrong tick.","steps":["Decide first which of the options of words the question is reaching for: this wording is about where God is in relation to the world, not about what God is like morally and not about how God can be addressed.","Clear the option that answers a different question altogether: omnibenevolent means all-loving, which is about God's goodness rather than God's position.","Clear the option that means the exact opposite: transcendent means beyond the universe and not limited by space or time, the reverse of being at work inside it.","Test the last distractor properly: personal describes a God who can be addressed, loved and obeyed, as when Jesus teaches his followers to pray 'Our Father in heaven' (Matthew 6:9), so it is about relationship and not location.","Tick A, immanent, the word Christians use of God acting in a miracle, in an answered prayer and in the incarnation, and move on quickly - this part carries the smallest reward on the whole question."],"cues":["Is the wording about where God is, what God is like morally, or how God can be addressed?","Which option is about God's goodness rather than God's position?","Which option states the opposite of present and active within the world?","Of the two left, which is about relationship rather than location?","Which option survives, and what exactly does it mean?"]},{"title":"The contrasting question on a prescribed issue: Christianity against the non-religious view","setup":"The question reads: Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about whether miracles still happen today. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions. Each of the two beliefs earns one mark for a simple explanation of a contrast and two for a detailed one, there is no mark here for a source, and two beliefs that agree cannot both be credited.","steps":["Choose the contrast the specification itself prescribes for this theme: Christianity, the main religious tradition in Britain, set against non-religious beliefs such as atheism and humanism.","State the religious belief and name who holds it: many Christians hold that miracles are real acts of God, and Roman Catholics point to the cures at Lourdes, which the Lourdes Medical Bureau examines before the Church will call any of them miraculous.","Develop it, which is what turns the simple explanation into a detailed one: because a miracle is read as an answer to prayer it shows God as immanent, acting inside the world he made, which is why Mark's Gospel tells the calming of the storm as evidence of who Jesus is.","Open the second paragraph with wording that marks the contrast, and give the humanist reason rather than just the humanist conclusion: an unexplained recovery is not an inexplicable one, and medicine has a long record of explaining what once looked supernatural.","Develop the second belief to the same standard using Hume's point that mistaken or dishonest testimony is always more likely than a broken law of nature, then stop - a third belief earns nothing."],"cues":["Which contrast does this theme prescribe, and is the second side a religion at all?","Whose belief is it, and what named case can you attach to it?","What does the miracle tell a Christian about God, beyond the fact that it happened?","What is the humanist objecting to - the event, or the conclusion drawn from it?","What reason turns the second view from an opinion into an argument, and when should you stop writing?"]},{"title":"The source question: two beliefs plus one plain reference","setup":"The question reads: Explain two Christian beliefs about how God can be known. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer. Four of the marks are the usual pair for each belief, simple explanation then detailed explanation, and the fifth is awarded once for a relevant and accurate reference.","steps":["Pick two beliefs that genuinely differ rather than two versions of one: general revelation, open to everyone, and special revelation, given directly to particular people.","Write the general revelation belief and develop it: Christians hold that the order and beauty of the natural world point beyond themselves to their maker, so anyone who looks carefully has some knowledge of God without being told.","Write the special revelation belief and develop it: Christians also hold that God has made himself known directly, in visions and prophecy and above all in the incarnation, where God is met as a person rather than inferred from evidence.","Land the fifth mark unmistakably rather than hoping the examiner infers it, by naming the text with the belief it supports: Psalm 19:1 says that the heavens declare the glory of God, and John 1:14 says that the Word became flesh.","Check the shape before moving on: two paragraphs, each with a point and a development, and one clearly attributed reference - not three beliefs, of which only two would be marked."],"cues":["Which two beliefs about knowing God are genuinely different in kind?","What does the natural world give a Christian that nobody had to tell them?","How is the second kind of knowledge received differently from the first?","Where should the reference be placed so that it cannot be missed?","How many beliefs will actually be marked, however many you write?"]},{"title":"Building the evaluation: evil and suffering","setup":"The question reads: 'The suffering in the world shows that God does not exist.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. Spelling, punctuation and grammar are assessed on this answer as well.","steps":["Decode the claim before writing a word: it is not that suffering is unpleasant but that suffering is logically incompatible with a God who is both able and willing to prevent it.","Build the supporting chain to the end rather than asserting it: God is omnipotent so could act, omnibenevolent so would want to, yet a child dies of a disease nobody chose - so at least one of those descriptions must be wrong, and a God who is neither all-powerful nor all-loving is not the God of Christianity.","Answer with named religious teaching, not with a mood: the free will defence argues that love that cannot be refused is not love, and Augustine traces moral evil to the misuse of that freedom rather than to God.","Add a second religious argument that covers what the first does not, since the free will defence says nothing about earthquakes: John Hick's soul-making reply holds that courage and compassion could not exist in a painless world, and the Book of Job answers Job out of the whirlwind that he cannot see the whole picture.","Bring in the non-religious voice the bullets allow, so that the essay has a third position rather than two: humanists argue that the simplest explanation of undeserved suffering is that nobody is running the world, and that compassion is a human response needing no defence.","Write a conclusion that judges rather than summarises: say which argument survived the objections, why the free will defence is stronger against moral evil than against natural evil, and what that leaves belief in God resting on."],"cues":["Is the claim that suffering is bad, or that it contradicts something?","Which two attributes of God is the argument setting against each other?","What is the standard religious reply to evil that human beings choose?","What kind of suffering does that reply fail to explain, and who answers it?","What does the non-religious side add that neither religious reply does?","What must the last paragraph do that the earlier ones have not?"]},{"title":"Repairing an influence answer: from a belief to a difference it makes","setup":"The question reads: Explain two ways in which belief in an omnipotent God influences Christians today. A student has written: 'Christians believe God is all-powerful. Christians also believe that God made the world.' A simple explanation of a way earns one mark and a detailed explanation two, and the second mark is only given when the answer shows what believers actually do or think differently.","steps":["Name the fault precisely: both sentences state a belief, and neither says what the belief changes, so this is two statements rather than two influences.","Notice that the second sentence is not even a second way - creation is the same belief about power restated, so it must be replaced rather than developed.","Turn the first into a genuine influence: because Christians believe God has the power to act in the world, they pray for help in situations they cannot change themselves, and many attend services of prayer for healing.","Build a second influence that is different in kind: because an all-powerful God is also believed to be all-loving, some Christians are driven by the suffering they see to work through charities such as Christian Aid rather than wait for God to intervene.","Check each paragraph names the belief and then the difference it makes to what believers do or say, which is exactly what lifts an explanation from simple to detailed."],"cues":["What is missing from both of the student's sentences?","Are these two ways, or one way written twice?","What do Christians do because they think God can act?","What quite different behaviour could the same belief produce?","What test should each finished paragraph pass?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What must a Design argument answer contain besides the argument itself, according to the specification?","a":"Its weaknesses - the specification names strengths and weaknesses, so an answer that only states the argument cannot reach the top."},{"q":"On this theme, which contrast do the three prescribed issues require?","a":"Christianity, the main religious tradition in Britain, set against non-religious beliefs such as atheism and humanism - not a second religion."},{"q":"What is the difference between special and general revelation?","a":"Special revelation is given directly to particular people, as in a vision or the incarnation; general revelation is open to everyone, through nature and scripture."},{"q":"Why does the specification's own content make a two-sided evaluation of revelation easy?","a":"Because the objections are written into it: revelations conflict with each other, alternative explanations exist, and the witnesses may have been lying or mistaken."}]},"room":{"facts":["The three prescribed contrasting issues on this theme are visions, miracles and nature as general revelation, and the required contrast is Christianity against non-religious beliefs such as atheism and humanism.","Theme C is one five-part question of 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks, and the paper advises 25 minutes on a theme - about a minute a mark.","The 4-mark contrasting question on Component 2 adds the instruction to refer to one or more religious traditions, so the tradition must be named; the 5-mark question adds the instruction to refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching, and that reference is a mark on its own.","The 12-mark evaluation is the only levels-marked question and also carries 3 SPaG marks, but on Component 2 only the best of the four themes' SPaG marks is counted, so the paper carries 3 SPaG marks in total and not 12; an answer that never refers to religion is capped at Level 2.","Two named examples must be ready: one example of a miracle and one example of a vision.","Three pairs of words describe the divine: omnipotent and omniscient, personal and impersonal, immanent and transcendent."],"questions":[{"q":"Which one of the following means that God is all-knowing? A Immanent B Omnipotent C Omniscient D Transcendent","a":"Answer: C Omniscient. AO1:1, one mark for the correct tick.","sol":"C Omniscient. Omniscient means all-knowing - God knows everything past, present and future. Omnipotent means all-powerful, immanent means present and active within the world, and transcendent means beyond and outside the universe.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Three of these four words describe where God is or what God can do; only one is about what God has in mind.","Split the options into the pair about position in relation to the world and the pair beginning with the same prefix.","Of the two that begin with that prefix, one is about power - so the other must be about something else."],"codes":["3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — Different ideas about the divine that come from these sources"]},{"q":"Which one of the following is the argument that the universe must have been started off by something that was not itself caused? A The argument from miracles B The Design argument C The First Cause argument D The problem of evil","a":"Answer: C The First Cause argument. AO1:1, one mark.","sol":"C The First Cause argument. It argues that everything we observe is caused by something else, that the chain cannot go back for ever, and that there must therefore be an uncaused first cause, which Thomas Aquinas identified with God.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["One of these four is not an argument for God at all, so it can go first.","Of the arguments that are left, ask which one is about beginnings rather than about order or about events.","The clue is in the name of the argument you want."],"codes":["3.2.1.3 Philosophical arguments for and against the existence of God — The First Cause argument"]},{"q":"Which one of the following is an example of general revelation? A A prophet hearing God speak B A vision of an angel C Seeing God's power in the natural world D A dream in which God gives a command","a":"Answer: C Seeing God's power in the natural world. AO1:1, one mark.","sol":"C Seeing God's power in the natural world. General revelation is available to everyone, through nature and through scripture. The other three options are all special revelation, because each is given directly to one person rather than open to all.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The word general is doing the work here: ask who could receive each of these.","Three of the options happen to one person and one is open to anybody who looks.","Sort the options into the given-to-an-individual pile and the available-to-all pile."],"codes":["3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — General revelation: nature and scripture"]},{"q":"Give two weaknesses of the Design argument.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points; only the first two answers are marked. Evolution explains apparent design; the analogy with human designs is weak; poor or cruel features look undesigned; a designer need not be the God of any religion.","sol":"Evolution by natural selection explains the appearance of design without a designer. A designer, even if proved, need not be the loving personal God of Christianity.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The specification asks for the weaknesses of this argument as well as its strengths, so they are learnable content, not opinions.","One weakness attacks the evidence, and another attacks the size of the conclusion drawn from it.","Give two short separate points - nothing extra is rewarded here, and only the first two count."],"codes":["3.2.1.3 Philosophical arguments for and against the existence of God — The Design argument"]},{"q":"Give two things that Buddhists believe about the Buddha's Enlightenment.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points. He was Siddhartha Gautama and became the awakened one; it happened while he meditated under the Bodhi tree at Bodh Gaya; he understood the Four Noble Truths; he was freed from the cycle of rebirth; it came through his own insight and not from a God.","sol":"He reached it through his own meditation under the Bodhi tree rather than receiving it from a God. What he understood was the Four Noble Truths, the cause of suffering and the way out of it.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["This bullet of the specification is the reason the theme is not only about a creator God.","One point can be about how the Enlightenment was reached, the other about what was understood.","Two separate points, no development needed."],"codes":["3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — Enlightenment as a source of knowledge about the divine"]},{"q":"Give two alternative explanations that could be offered for a person's claim to have seen a vision.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points. The person may have been lying; they may have been mistaken; illness, exhaustion, fasting or grief can produce vivid experiences; they wanted it to be true so deceived themselves; it was a dream; it was an ordinary event remembered as extraordinary.","sol":"The person may simply have been lying, perhaps for attention or money. They may equally have been mistaken, because illness, exhaustion or grief can produce vivid experiences that feel completely real.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The specification writes these objections into the content itself, so they are recall rather than invention.","One kind of objection is about honesty and the other is about accuracy.","Two short separate reasons are all that is marked."],"codes":["3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — The value of general and special revelation and enlightenment"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about visions. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions.","a":"AO1:3. Two contrasting beliefs, each 1 mark simple and 2 marks detailed. Christian acceptance of visions as special revelation from God, with a named case, set against the atheist or humanist view that visions are hallucination, illusion or invention.","sol":"Many Christians believe that a vision is genuine special revelation, in which God makes himself known directly to a particular person. Roman Catholics point to Bernadette Soubirous, who reported eighteen apparitions at the grotto in Lourdes in 1858, and the Church accepted her account after examining it for years; Christians also read Saul's vision on the road to Damascus in Acts 9 as the event that turned a persecutor into an apostle. Humanists and atheists in Britain contrast sharply with this. They argue that no vision has ever been witnessed by everyone present, that illness, exhaustion, grief and strong expectation are known to produce experiences that feel entirely real, and that the visions of rival religions contradict one another, so at most one tradition can be right and most claims must be false.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["This is one of the three issues the specification prescribes, and it tells you which two sides to use.","For each side, give the belief and then the reason behind it, because a belief without a reason is only a simple explanation.","Start the second paragraph with wording that sets it against the first rather than adding to it."],"codes":["3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — Special revelation","3.2.1.3 — The theme's own requirement: religious and philosophical arguments, and the three prescribed issues"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting beliefs in contemporary British society about nature as a revelation of God. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions.","a":"AO1:3. Two contrasting beliefs, each 1 mark simple and 2 marks detailed. Christian belief in nature as general revelation, supported by Psalm 19:1 or Romans 1:20, set against the humanist view that awe at nature needs no supernatural explanation.","sol":"Most Christians believe that the natural world is general revelation, a knowledge of God open to everyone rather than given to a chosen few. The order of the seasons, the scale of the universe and the intricacy of living things are read as pointing beyond themselves, which is why Psalm 19:1 says that the heavens declare 'the glory of God' and why Paul argues in Romans 1:20 that God's qualities have been visible in what has been made. Humanists in Britain hold the opposite view about the same experience. They feel the same wonder at a night sky and deny that it is evidence of anything: awe is a human response to something vast, the order of nature is explained by physics and by natural selection, and adding a creator explains nothing further while raising the question of who made him.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Both sides here agree about the feeling and disagree about what follows from it.","Name the tradition and attach a text or a reason to each side, then say what each concludes.","Keep the paragraphs balanced - there is no source mark on this question, so spend the space on development."],"codes":["3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — General revelation: nature and scripture","3.2.1.3 — The theme's own requirement: religious and philosophical arguments, and the three prescribed issues"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which belief in an omnipotent God influences Christians today.","a":"AO1:2. Two ways, each 1 mark simple and 2 marks detailed, and the second mark only where the influence on what believers do or think is given. Petitionary prayer and prayer for healing; trust and worship; the drive to act against suffering; the struggle with the problem of evil.","sol":"Because Christians believe God has the power to act in the world, they pray for outcomes they cannot bring about themselves. Prayers for the sick are said in almost every Sunday service, and some churches hold services of prayer for healing, which only makes sense if the God being addressed can actually change things. Secondly, belief in an all-powerful God shapes how Christians face suffering. Many find the belief a comfort and trust that God is in control even when they cannot see why something has happened, so they continue to worship and to describe God as almighty in the words of the Creed; others are troubled by it, since a God with the power to stop a famine and no apparent reason for allowing it is hard to accept, and that discomfort drives them into charity work rather than out of the Church.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The command word here is not contrast - nothing is gained by setting the two ways against each other.","The second mark for each way is only earned when you say what believers actually do or think differently.","Ask what a believer would stop doing if they decided God had no power to act."],"codes":["3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — Different ideas about the divine that come from these sources"]},{"q":"Explain two Christian beliefs about how God can be known. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","a":"AO1:1. Two beliefs, each 1 mark simple and 2 marks detailed, plus 1 mark for a relevant and accurate reference. General revelation through nature and scripture; special revelation through visions, prophecy and above all the incarnation.","sol":"Christians believe God can be known through general revelation, which is open to everyone. The order and beauty of the natural world are read as pointing to their maker, so a person who never opened a Bible could still have some knowledge of God; Psalm 19:1 says that the heavens declare 'the glory of God'. Christians also believe God can be known through special revelation, which is given directly to particular people. Visions, dreams and prophecy are examples, but the supreme case for Christians is the incarnation, when God was met as a person rather than inferred from evidence, which is why John 1:14 says that 'The Word became flesh'. The two work together: nature suggests a creator, and special revelation tells Christians what that creator is like.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Two beliefs that differ in kind score better than two versions of the same belief.","Ask who receives each kind of knowledge - everybody, or one person?","One mark here is separate from everything else and is lost by forgetting rather than by not knowing."],"codes":["3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — General revelation: nature and scripture","3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — Special revelation"]},{"q":"Explain two religious beliefs about why God allows suffering. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","a":"AO1:1. Two beliefs, each 1 mark simple and 2 marks detailed, plus 1 mark for a source. The free will defence; soul-making; the limits of human understanding in Job; God shares suffering at the cross.","sol":"One Christian belief is that God allows suffering because he has given human beings free will. God gave human beings genuine freedom because love that cannot be refused would not be love at all, and much suffering is the direct result of people choosing to misuse that freedom, as Augustine argued - so the blame lies with human beings rather than with God. A second belief is that God allows suffering because it can make people, not just break them. John Hick called the world a place of soul-making, arguing that courage, compassion and forgiveness could not exist in a world where nothing ever went wrong. Christians also point to the cross, where Jesus cries 'why have you forsaken me?' (Mark 15:34), and say that God does not watch suffering from a distance but shares it.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The specification treats these beliefs about suffering as content, so this is recall and not invention.","Choose two beliefs that cover different kinds of suffering rather than two versions of one belief.","Attach a named thinker or a text to each belief, and make sure at least one reference is unmistakable."],"codes":["3.2.1.3 Philosophical arguments for and against the existence of God — Evil and suffering as an argument against the existence of God"]},{"q":"Explain two religious beliefs about the transcendence of God. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","a":"AO1:1. Two beliefs, each 1 mark simple and 2 marks detailed, plus 1 mark for a source. God is beyond space and time and not part of creation; God is beyond full human understanding and description; transcendence held alongside immanence.","sol":"Christians believe that God is transcendent in the sense of being outside and beyond the universe. God is not a very large thing inside creation but the one who made it, unlimited by space and time, which is why Christians say God has no beginning and no end and why the creation account in Genesis 1:1 has God existing before anything else does. Secondly, transcendence means God is beyond full human understanding. Human language can only describe God by analogy, so any picture we form is inadequate, and Isaiah 55:8 has God say 'my thoughts are not your thoughts'. Christians hold this alongside immanence, believing that the God who is beyond the world also acts within it.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Transcendence has two sides: where God is, and how far God can be described.","Develop each belief by saying what follows from it for how believers speak about God.","The fifth mark is for a reference that is clearly attributed, not for an idea that sounds biblical."],"codes":["3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — Different ideas about the divine that come from these sources"]},{"q":"'Miracles are the best evidence that God exists.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, marked by levels on the whole answer. Support: miracles are concrete, witnessed, dated events; verified cures; they point to a personal immanent God rather than a bare designer. Against: Hume on testimony; unexplained is not inexplicable; rival religions' miracles; the unfairness objection; design and first cause reach further.","sol":"Christians who support this statement argue that a miracle is an event that appears to break the laws of nature and can only be explained by God acting, so a single well-attested miracle would settle the question in a way no abstract argument can. Mark's Gospel has Jesus still a storm with the words 'Peace! Be still!' (Mark 4:39), and the disciples' question about who this can be that even the wind obeys is exactly the inference the argument makes. The argument is also alive today, which is part of its appeal. The Roman Catholic Church does not accept a cure at Lourdes lightly: the Lourdes Medical Bureau examines the medical records first, and only seventy cures have been declared miraculous since 1858, the most recent being that of Sister Bernadette Moriau in 2018. Supporters say evidence tested that severely beats the Design and First Cause arguments, which at best show that a designer or a first cause is possible; a miracle is a dated event with witnesses and documents. Miracles also point to the God of a religion rather than to a bare force, because they are treated as answers to prayer and show God as immanent and loving.\n\nA different point of view is that miracles are the weakest evidence rather than the best. David Hume argued that a wise person proportions belief to the evidence, and that it is always more likely that witnesses were mistaken, or dishonest, or that a story grew in the retelling, than that the laws of nature were suspended. Humanists in Britain make the same point today: an unexplained recovery is not an inexplicable one, and medicine regularly turns yesterday's mystery into today's diagnosis. There is a religious objection as well. If God heals one pilgrim while thousands go home unhealed, the miracle sharpens the problem of evil instead of solving it, and many Christians therefore prefer to say that God works through doctors, nurses and the patience of carers. Christians who understand faith as trust rather than proof add that a God who proved himself by spectacular interventions would leave no room for faith at all. Others argue that general revelation is better evidence precisely because it is available to everyone: Psalm 19:1 says that the heavens declare 'the glory of God', and nobody has to take a stranger's word for the night sky.\n\nIn conclusion, miracles are the most vivid evidence for God but not the best, because their whole strength rests on testimony, and testimony can always be doubted by someone who was not there. The Design and First Cause arguments are less dramatic but reach further, since they start from facts nobody disputes - that the universe exists and is orderly. Miracles are therefore better understood as confirmation for those who already believe than as proof for those who do not.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["The statement contains a comparison, so the essay must weigh miracles against the other arguments for God and not just ask whether miracles happen.","One side needs a named case with dates; the other needs the standard objection to testimony as evidence.","The strongest structure gives the religious objection to miracles as well as the non-religious one, so that the conclusion has something to choose between."],"codes":["3.2.1.3 Philosophical arguments for and against the existence of God — The argument from miracles","3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — The value of general and special revelation and enlightenment"]},{"q":"'Science has made belief in God unnecessary.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, marked by levels. Support: the Big Bang and evolution remove the need for a creator; God-of-the-gaps shrinks; religious experience explained by brain activity; unverifiable claims. Against: how is not why; the Big Bang itself needs explaining; believing scientists; churches accept evolution; morality and purpose lie outside science's scope.","sol":"There is a strong case for the statement. The two questions religion was once needed to answer now have scientific answers: the universe began in the Big Bang about 13.8 billion years ago, and the complexity of living things is explained by evolution through natural selection, set out in Darwin's On the Origin of Species in 1859. That removes the force of the Design argument, since what looked designed can be produced by an unguided process over enough time, which is why Richard Dawkins describes natural selection as a blind watchmaker. Supporters add that religion has steadily retreated: thunder, plague and madness were once explained by God and are now explained by physics, biology and medicine, so a God invoked only for what is not yet understood shrinks with every discovery. Religious experience itself can be studied as brain activity. And unlike religious claims, scientific ones can be tested and shown to be wrong, which humanists argue is what makes them knowledge at all. These arguments are also being made in a society whose religious make-up is changing: the 2021 census for England and Wales recorded fewer than half the population describing themselves as Christian for the first time, with the next largest group answering 'no religion'. A census records how people identify rather than whether they accept an argument, but it is the contrasting perspective in contemporary British society against which this debate is now held.\n\nA different point of view is that science has answered a different question from the one religion asks. Science explains how the universe developed; it does not explain why there is a universe rather than nothing, and the First Cause argument bites precisely there, since the Big Bang is itself an event that appears to need a cause. A scientific account of how a person recovered also says nothing about whether their recovery was an answer to prayer. Christians point out that science cannot settle whether human beings matter, whether cruelty is wrong, or what anyone should do with their life, and that these are the questions religion exists to answer. The history is against the statement too. Georges Lemaitre, the Belgian priest who proposed the expanding universe (1927) and the primeval atom (1931), and Francis Collins, who led the Human Genome Project, were both believing Christians, which shows the two need not compete. The Roman Catholic Church and the Church of England both hold evolution to be compatible with faith, and Pope John Paul II told the Pontifical Academy of Sciences in 1996 that evolution is more than a hypothesis; only Christians who read Genesis 1 as literal history are forced to choose.\n\nIn conclusion, science has made one kind of belief in God unnecessary - the God used as an explanation for gaps in scientific knowledge - and that is a real loss to the Design argument. It has not made belief in God as a whole unnecessary, because the questions of why anything exists and what human life is for lie outside what science is designed to answer. The statement is therefore half true, and the half it gets right is the half most religious believers had already given up.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["The word unnecessary is doing the work: the claim is not that science has disproved God, and treating it as if it had will weaken the essay.","Decide what science is for, and then see which religious claims are actually in competition with it.","Named scientists who were believers, and the churches' own positions on evolution, turn the second half from opinion into evidence."],"codes":["3.2.1.3 Philosophical arguments for and against the existence of God — Arguments based on science against the existence of God","3.2.1.3 Philosophical arguments for and against the existence of God — The Design argument"]},{"q":"'Nobody can know whether a vision is real.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, marked by levels. Support: no independent witness; alternative explanations; lying or mistaken; visions of different religions conflict. Against: the receiver can know; transformed lives; corroboration and long testing; consistency with existing teaching; God would not reveal himself unrecognisably.","sol":"There is a great deal in support of this statement, and the specification writes most of it into the content. A vision is by definition special revelation, given to one person and not to the crowd, so there is nothing for anyone else to examine afterwards. The obvious alternative explanations are well established: illness, exhaustion, fasting, grief and strong expectation all produce experiences that feel entirely real to the person having them, and the receiver is the one person least able to judge, since they are inside the experience. The specification also states the blunter possibility, that the person was lying, perhaps for attention, money or influence. The most serious problem is that visions contradict one another. The God revealed to Christians is three persons in one, while Surah 112:1 insists 'Say, He is Allah, the One', and the Buddha's enlightenment revealed no creator God at all. These cannot all be accurate, so most visions must be mistaken even on a believer's own reasoning, and humanists conclude that the simplest explanation is that all of them are.\n\nChristians and others answer that certainty and evidence are not the same thing. The person who received the vision may be entirely justified in believing it, in the way that anyone is justified in trusting their own experience until they have a reason not to. Believers also apply tests rather than accepting every claim. They ask whether the vision fits existing teaching, whether it produced a changed life, and whether anything can be independently checked. Saul's vision on the road to Damascus in Acts 9 turned a man who was hunting Christians into the apostle who founded churches across the empire, and believers argue that a hallucination does not usually reorganise a life for decades. The Roman Catholic Church is notably slow to accept claims: Bernadette Soubirous reported the apparitions at Lourdes in 1858 and was questioned for years before the Church accepted her account, and only seventy of the many thousands of cures claimed there have ever been declared miraculous. Some Christians add a religious argument: an all-powerful and loving God who wanted to be known would not reveal himself in a way that nobody could recognise.\n\nIn conclusion, the statement is too strong as it stands but close to right. Nobody outside the experience can know that a vision is real, because there is nothing to examine except the testimony of one person, and conflicting revelations show that sincerity is not enough. The person who received it can reasonably believe it, and the tests believers use - consistency, corroboration and a changed life - can make a claim more or less credible. Visions are therefore reasonable grounds for faith for the person who has one, and evidence of a much weaker kind for everyone else.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Separate two different people before you begin: the person who had the vision, and everybody else.","The strongest supporting argument is not that the witness is unreliable but that different visions say different things.","Believers do not simply accept every claim - the tests they apply are the evidence the other side of the argument needs."],"codes":["3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — The value of general and special revelation and enlightenment","3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — Special revelation"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is the difference between omnipotent and omniscient?","a":"Omnipotent means all-powerful; omniscient means all-knowing."},{"q":"Whose watch found lying on a heath is the best-known form of the Design argument, and in which book?","a":"William Paley's, in Natural Theology, published in 1802."},{"q":"Where and in which year did Bernadette Soubirous report the apparitions she later called the Immaculate Conception?","a":"At the grotto of Massabielle in Lourdes, southern France, in 1858."},{"q":"Which two routes does the specification name for general revelation?","a":"Nature and scripture."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Omnipotent","back":"All-powerful. Christians believe God can do anything he wills: the Apostles' Creed opens by calling God the Father almighty, and Genesis 1:1 has God create everything from nothing.","code":"3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — Different ideas about the divine that come from these sources","open":false},{"front":"Omniscient","back":"All-knowing: God knows everything past, present and future, including human thoughts and choices. It is the belief that makes God's foreknowledge hard to square with human free will.","code":"3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — Different ideas about the divine that come from these sources","open":false},{"front":"Immanent","back":"Present and active within the world. Christians see God's immanence in the incarnation, in miracles and in answered prayer: John 1:14 says 'The Word became flesh'.","code":"3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — Different ideas about the divine that come from these sources","open":false},{"front":"Transcendent","back":"Beyond and outside the universe, unlimited by space and time and beyond full human understanding. Isaiah 55:8 has God say 'my thoughts are not your thoughts'.","code":"3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — Different ideas about the divine that come from these sources","open":false},{"front":"Personal and impersonal","back":"A personal God can be addressed and related to, as in the prayer 'Our Father in heaven' (Matthew 6:9). An impersonal ultimate reality, such as Brahman without form in Hinduism, is a power that cannot be spoken to.","code":"3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — Different ideas about the divine that come from these sources","open":false},{"front":"Special revelation","back":"Knowledge of the divine given directly to a person or group - visions, dreams, prophecy, and for Christians supremely the incarnation. Its weakness is that only the receiver witnessed it.","code":"3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — Special revelation","open":false},{"front":"General revelation","back":"Knowledge of the divine open to everyone, through the natural world and through scripture. Psalm 19:1 says that the heavens declare 'the glory of God'.","code":"3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — General revelation: nature and scripture","open":false},{"front":"Enlightenment","back":"Knowledge of ultimate reality reached from within, by insight and practice rather than given from outside. Siddhartha Gautama became the Buddha, the awakened one, under the Bodhi tree at Bodh Gaya.","code":"3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — Enlightenment as a source of knowledge about the divine","open":false},{"front":"Miracle","back":"An event that appears to break the laws of nature and is attributed to God. In Mark 4:39 Jesus stills a storm with the words 'Peace! Be still!'.","code":"3.2.1.3 Philosophical arguments for and against the existence of God — The argument from miracles","open":false},{"front":"The Design argument","back":"Order, purpose and fine-tuning in nature point to a designer. William Paley argued that a watch found on a heath implies a watchmaker, in Natural Theology (1802).","code":"3.2.1.3 Philosophical arguments for and against the existence of God — The Design argument","open":false},{"front":"The First Cause argument","back":"Everything observed has a cause, the chain cannot go back for ever, so there is an uncaused first cause, which is God. Set out by Thomas Aquinas among his Five Ways.","code":"3.2.1.3 Philosophical arguments for and against the existence of God — The First Cause argument","open":false},{"front":"The inconsistent triad","back":"God is all-powerful, so could stop suffering; all-loving, so would want to; yet suffering exists. The three cannot all be true, which is the argument from evil against God's existence.","code":"3.2.1.3 Philosophical arguments for and against the existence of God — Evil and suffering as an argument against the existence of God","open":false},{"front":"One example of a vision","back":"Bernadette Soubirous at the grotto in Lourdes in 1858; Saul blinded on the road to Damascus in Acts 9; Moses at the burning bush, told the name 'I am who I am' in Exodus 3:14.","code":"3.2.1.3 The nature of the divine and revelation — Special revelation","open":true},{"front":"One example of a miracle","back":"Jesus calming the storm on the Sea of Galilee, Mark 4:35-41; the cure of Sister Bernadette Moriau, declared in 2018 the seventieth miracle recognised at Lourdes; Jesus healing blind Bartimaeus, Mark 10:46-52.","code":"3.2.1.3 Philosophical arguments for and against the existence of God — The argument from miracles","open":true},{"front":"Atheism and humanism","back":"Atheism is the belief that there is no God. Humanism adds that this life is the only one and that meaning, morality and wonder come from human beings; Humanists UK speaks for that view in Britain.","code":"3.2.1.3 — The theme's own requirement: religious and philosophical arguments, and the three prescribed issues","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Evaluate: 'Miracles are the best evidence that God exists.')","items":["The paragraph: The strongest case for the statement is that a miracle is the only evidence for God that comes with witnesses, a date and a place. The Design and First Cause arguments are inferences about the universe as a whole, and an atheist can accept every fact they use while rejecting the conclusion; a miracle claims to be an event, and events can be investigated. That is why Roman Catholics point to Lourdes rather than to philosophy. The Lourdes Medical Bureau examines the medical records of every claimed cure, and the Church has declared only seventy of them miraculous since the apparitions of 1858, the most recent being that of Sister Bernadette Moriau in 2018 - a rate of refusal that believers argue is the opposite of credulity. A miracle also delivers more than the other arguments do: the Design argument at best gives a designer, whereas a healing granted after prayer is taken to show a God who is immanent and loving, which is the God Christianity actually claims. Against this, David Hume's objection is hard to shift, because whatever the Bureau examines, what reaches the rest of us is still testimony, and mistaken or dishonest testimony has always been more common than suspended laws of nature. On balance the evidence from miracles is the most vivid but not the strongest, since it can only ever be as reliable as the people reporting it.","Why this is Level 4: it meets the descriptor 'A well-argued response, reasoned consideration of different points of view. Logical chains of reasoning leading to judgement(s) supported by knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information. References to religion applied to the issue.' The chain runs from a claim about what kind of evidence a miracle is, through named religious evidence, to a counter-argument and a judgement that follows from what came before rather than being bolted on. The religious reference is applied to the issue, not merely mentioned."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same statement, visibly weaker)","items":["The paragraph: I think miracles are good evidence that God exists because if something happens that cannot be explained then something must have caused it and that would be God. Lots of people say they have been healed and they would not all lie about it. On the other hand, other people think miracles are not real evidence because scientists cannot prove them and there might be an explanation we do not know about yet. So there are two sides to the argument and it depends what you believe.","Why this is only Level 2: it meets 'Recognition of different points of view, each supported by relevant reasons/evidence' - there are two sides and each has a reason attached. It misses Level 3's 'Logical chains of reasoning that draw on knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information' and 'Clear reference to religion': no tradition, teaching, text or named case appears anywhere, so the answer is also close to the cap 'Maximum of Level 2 if there is no reference to religion'. It misses Level 4 entirely because the last sentence refuses to judge instead of reaching a conclusion supported by the reasoning.","The three repairs that would lift it: name the tradition and the case (Roman Catholics and the verified cures at Lourdes); develop one reason into a chain rather than a sentence (why an unexplained event is not automatically an inexplicable one); and replace 'it depends what you believe' with a judgement that says which argument was stronger and why."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the named example of a miracle","items":["Definition an examiner expects: a miracle is an event that appears to break the laws of nature and is attributed to the action of God.","Scriptural example: Jesus calms a storm on the Sea of Galilee, Mark 4:35-41; he is asleep in the stern, rebukes the wind with the words 'Peace! Be still!' (Mark 4:39), and the disciples ask who this can be that even wind and sea obey him.","A second scriptural example if you prefer a healing: Jesus gives sight to blind Bartimaeus outside Jericho, Mark 10:46-52.","Modern example with dates: the cures at Lourdes in southern France, at the site of the apparitions of 1858.","The testing process: every claimed cure is first examined by the Lourdes Medical Bureau, which looks at the medical records and asks whether the recovery has any known medical explanation.","The number that shows how rarely the claim is accepted, with its source: on the Lourdes Medical Bureau's examinations and the Roman Catholic Church's own recognition, seventy recognised cures as of 2018 (check for updates before quoting the figure).","The most recent on that same record: Sister Bernadette Moriau, examined by the Lourdes Medical Bureau and recognised by the Church in 2018 as the seventieth (check for updates, since 'the most recent' dates fastest of anything here).","Millions of pilgrims visit Lourdes every year, many of them sick, and the great majority go home unhealed - a fact the other side of the argument uses.","Christian use of the example: miracles are evidence of God's immanence, of God's power over nature and of God's love, and are treated as answers to prayer.","Non-religious use of the same example: David Hume argued that testimony can never establish a miracle, because mistaken or dishonest witnesses are always more likely than a broken law of nature; humanists add that an unexplained recovery is not the same as an inexplicable one.","The internal Christian objection: if God heals one pilgrim and not the thousands beside them, the miracle makes the problem of evil worse rather than better."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the named example of a vision","items":["Definition an examiner expects: a vision is a form of special revelation in which a person sees or is shown something of the divine, often with a message.","Modern example with dates: Bernadette Soubirous, a fourteen-year-old girl, at the grotto of Massabielle in Lourdes, in the foothills of the Pyrenees in southern France.","The first apparition was on 11 February 1858, and there were eighteen in all, the last in July of the same year.","On 25 March 1858 the lady identified herself to Bernadette with the words 'I am the Immaculate Conception'.","Bernadette was questioned by Church and civil authorities for years before her account was accepted; she later became a nun and was made a saint in 1933.","Attribution matters: this is a Roman Catholic example. Most Protestant Christians do not accept apparitions of Mary as revelation, which is a useful contrast within Christianity.","Biblical example if you prefer one: Saul is blinded by a light on the road to Damascus and hears a voice asking 'why do you persecute me?' (Acts 9:4); the vision turns the persecutor into the apostle Paul.","Older biblical example: Moses at the burning bush, where God gives the name 'I am who I am' (Exodus 3:14).","What believers say the example proves: God makes himself known directly and personally, not only through nature, and the change in the person's life is evidence the experience was real.","What atheists and humanists say: nobody else saw it; illness, exhaustion, fasting, grief and expectation produce vivid experiences; the witness may have been lying or simply mistaken; and visions in different religions contradict one another.","Where the Lourdes figures in this kit come from, and when to check them: claimed cures at the shrine are examined by the Lourdes Medical Bureau and declared miraculous by the Roman Catholic Church, which gives seventy recognised cures as of 2018, the seventieth that of Sister Bernadette Moriau; both that total and the phrase 'the most recent' can change, so check for updates."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Christian teaching on the existence of God and revelation","items":["God is omnipotent: the Apostles' Creed opens by calling God the Father almighty, and Genesis 1:1 begins 'In the beginning God created'.","God is omniscient, knowing everything past, present and future, which is why Christians hold that nothing can be hidden from God.","God is transcendent, beyond space and time and beyond full human understanding: Isaiah 55:8 has God say 'my thoughts are not your thoughts'.","God is also immanent, present and active within the world, which is what the incarnation, miracles and answered prayer all assert.","God is personal and can be addressed: Jesus teaches his followers to pray 'Our Father in heaven' (Matthew 6:9).","The Design argument in its classic English form: William Paley, Natural Theology (1802), the watch found lying on a heath; Thomas Aquinas made the same move in the fifth of his Five Ways.","The First Cause argument: Thomas Aquinas, the second of the Five Ways in the Summa Theologica - nothing causes itself, an infinite chain is impossible, so there is an uncaused first cause.","General revelation through nature: Psalm 19:1 says that the heavens declare 'the glory of God', and Paul argues in Romans 1:20 that God's invisible qualities have been visible in what has been made.","General revelation through scripture: the Bible is read publicly in worship, taught and preached, so it is open to anyone rather than given to one person.","Special revelation supremely in the incarnation: John 1:14 says 'The Word became flesh'.","The Christian answer to evil and suffering: the free will defence associated with Augustine; the soul-making argument developed by John Hick in Evil and the God of Love (1966); God's reply to Job out of the whirlwind (Job 38); and the cross, where Jesus cries 'why have you forsaken me?' (Mark 15:34).","Christianity and science: the Roman Catholic Church and the Church of England hold evolution to be compatible with faith, and Pope John Paul II told the Pontifical Academy of Sciences in 1996 that evolution is more than a hypothesis; creationist Christians who read Genesis 1 as literal history disagree.","Christian scientists used as evidence that the two need not compete: Georges Lemaitre, the Belgian Catholic priest who proposed the expanding universe (1927) and the primeval atom (1931), and Francis Collins, who led the Human Genome Project and wrote The Language of God (2006).","Other traditions for breadth: Islam insists on God's oneness - Surah 112:1, 'Say, He is Allah, the One'; Hinduism speaks of Brahman, described both with form and without form; Buddhism has no creator God, and the Buddha's Enlightenment under the Bodhi tree at Bodh Gaya is knowledge reached from within."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: atheist and humanist views in contemporary British society","items":["Atheism is the belief that there is no God; agnosticism is the view that we cannot know either way; humanism is the further position that this is the only life we have and that meaning, morality and wonder come from human beings.","Humanists UK, formerly the British Humanist Association, is the organisation that represents this view in Britain; it campaigns for a secular state and provides non-religious funerals and weddings.","The 2021 census for England and Wales recorded fewer than half the population describing themselves as Christian for the first time, with the next largest group answering 'no religion' - the British context the specification means by contrasting perspectives in contemporary society.","On the Design argument: evolution by natural selection, set out in Darwin's On the Origin of Species (1859), explains apparent design without a designer, which is why Richard Dawkins calls natural selection a blind watchmaker; disease and parasites look like bad design.","On the First Cause argument: if everything needs a cause then God does too, and if God can be uncaused then so can the universe; Bertrand Russell argued that the universe simply is, with no further explanation needed.","On miracles: David Hume argued that testimony can never establish a miracle, because mistaken or dishonest witnesses are always more likely than a suspended law of nature; miracle claims made by rival religions undermine one another.","On visions: no independent witness, and illness, exhaustion, fasting, grief or expectation can produce experiences that feel entirely real; the specification itself notes that the person may have been lying or mistaken.","On nature as general revelation: humanists share the sense of awe at the natural world and deny the inference, holding that wonder is a human response and that adding a creator explains nothing further.","On evil and suffering: the simplest explanation of undeserved suffering, especially of children and animals, is that nobody is in charge; a God who could prevent it and does not would not be worth worshipping.","On science: the Big Bang accounts for the origin of the universe about 13.8 billion years ago, religious claims cannot be tested, and Richard Dawkins argues in The God Delusion (2006) that God is an unnecessary hypothesis.","Positive humanist claims an examiner rewards: this life matters more because it is the only one; morality comes from reason and empathy rather than command; and human beings are responsible for making the world better because nobody else will."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'The First Cause argument proves that God exists.' Evaluate this statement.","items":["Decode the statement first: the word doing the work is 'proves'. The question is not whether the universe had a beginning, nor whether God exists, but whether this particular argument establishes it beyond doubt. A plan that misses this writes a general essay about God and loses the top level.","Paragraph 1, the argument itself, stated properly: everything we observe is caused by something else, nothing causes itself, an infinite chain of causes explains nothing, so there must be a first uncaused cause. Source: Thomas Aquinas, the second of the Five Ways in the Summa Theologica; and Genesis 1:1, 'In the beginning God created'.","Paragraph 2, why supporters think it is strong: it starts from something nobody denies, that the universe exists; it asks the question science does not answer, why there is anything rather than nothing; and the Big Bang, far from removing the problem, is itself a beginning and so looks like exactly the kind of event that needs a cause.","Paragraph 3, the first objection, which is the strongest: the argument seems to break its own rule. If everything must have a cause, God must have one too; if God can be uncaused, the universe might be uncaused, and Bertrand Russell took that line, saying the universe simply is. Non-religious argument, allowed by the fourth bullet.","Paragraph 4, the second objection, which is about the size of the conclusion: even a successful argument delivers only a first cause - it does not show that the cause is one, personal, good, loving, still in existence, or the God of any particular religion, so a Christian still needs revelation to get from a first cause to the God of the Bible.","The evaluation thread to run through all four paragraphs: distinguish 'the universe had a cause' from 'that cause is God'. Every paragraph should be measured against that distinction rather than against whether God exists.","What the conclusion must say: not that both sides have a point. It must judge - for example, that the argument succeeds as a reason for thinking the universe is not self-explanatory, and fails as a proof, because it cannot answer the special-pleading objection and cannot reach a personal God without help from revelation. The judgement must follow from the paragraphs already written.","Timing and technique: about a minute a mark, so roughly twelve minutes, leaving time to check spelling and punctuation, since this answer also carries the spelling, punctuation and grammar marks - and the specialist terms of this theme (omnipotent, omniscient, transcendent, immanent, revelation) earn credit twice, once for the argument and once for the vocabulary."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"The rubric of Theme C in one place","items":["The specification asks for religious teachings and religious and philosophical arguments on the existence of God and revelation, with their impact and influence in the modern world, and awareness of contrasting perspectives in contemporary British society.","The three prescribed contrasting issues are visions, miracles, and nature as general revelation.","Unlike the other themes, the required contrast here is the main religious tradition in Britain, Christianity, against non-religious beliefs such as atheism and humanism - not a second religion, though you may still draw on Buddhism, Hinduism, Islam, Judaism and Sikhism.","Two named examples are compulsory: one example of a miracle and one example of a vision.","The arguments named by the specification are the Design argument, the First Cause argument and the argument from miracles - all three with strengths and weaknesses - and against them evil and suffering, and arguments based on science.","The three sources of knowledge of the divine are special revelation, enlightenment, and general revelation through nature and scripture; the ideas of the divine they produce are omnipotent and omniscient, personal and impersonal, immanent and transcendent.","The objections to revelation are part of the content, not an optional extra: different ideas of the divine conflict, alternative explanations exist, and the people who claimed the experiences may have been lying or mistaken.","You must be able to say that the religious traditions of Great Britain are in the main Christian, and also that they are diverse."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T14:14:51.222214Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"All six fixes have landed and none introduces an error of fact. Question 10 now reads 'Explain two religious beliefs about why God allows suffering', which is the paper's own noun, and its sol still gives two genuinely different beliefs (free will after Augustine, soul-making after Hick) with the source mark plainly taken on Mark 15:34, at 136 words inside the 80-160 range. The rebuilt multiple choice in examples[0] is correct - immanent means present and active within the world, omnibenevolent is all-loving, transcendent is its opposite and personal is about address, so A is the only answer - and the five steps and cues track the new eliminations without revealing them. Facts[3] now states the Component 2 rule accurately: the paper carries 3 SPaG marks in total because only the best of the four themes' 12-mark answers counts, which is what the 8062/2B front page says. The census sentence in questions[13].sol is now context rather than evidence and distinguishes identification from assent. The Lourdes figures are sourced to the Medical Bureau's examinations and the Church's recognition, given as seventy as of 2018 with an update note. Lemaitre has lost 'first' in all three places and gained the correct dates, 1927 for the expanding universe and 1931 for the primeval atom. Re-read whole, the kit still holds: fifteen questions at 3/3/3/3/3 on tariffs of 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 with six at d=1, six at d=2 and three at d=3; the five Component 2 bullets and '[Plus SPaG 3 marks]' on the three 12-mark stems and SPaG nowhere else; all eleven key ideas tested; fourteen text references and every quotation at seven words or fewer; the Lourdes, Soubirous, Paley, Aquinas, Hume, Hick, Darwin, Dawkins, Collins, John Paul II and census facts all true; the prescribed contrast Christianity against atheism and humanism in questions 6 and 7 with the tradition named; no printed June 2023 or specimen Theme C question reproduced; fifteen cards inside the word limits, four fact files, a plan, four exit questions; and the Level 4 and Level 2 model paragraphs correctly placed against the grid. Trim about thirty words from questions[13].sol and swap 'replies' for 'beliefs' in the three hints on question 10, and there is nothing left to hold this back."}}},"3.2.1.4":{"id":"AQA-8062","topic":"3.2.1.4","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Theme D is one of the four themes you answer in Component 2, and it carries a whole five-part question of 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks, with the spelling, punctuation and grammar marks riding on the evaluation. It is the theme where opinions come easily and marks do not: an examiner rewards named teaching from named traditions applied to violence, terrorism, war, weapons of mass destruction and peace-making, and the specification insists you can contrast beliefs on violence, weapons of mass destruction and pacifism with reference to Christianity, the main religious tradition of Great Britain, and at least one other tradition. This room gives you the teachings, the sources, the named peace-makers and the organisation, and drills the shape of each answer so the whole 24 marks are reachable inside the 25 minutes the paper allows for a theme.","idea":[{"h":"What every answer on this theme has to be able to do","t":"The specification asks for religious teachings and religious, philosophical and ethical arguments about these issues, and for their impact and influence in the modern world. It names three issues on which you must be able to explain contrasting beliefs with reference to the main religious tradition in Britain, Christianity, and one or more other religious traditions: violence, weapons of mass destruction, and pacifism. Section 3.2.1 adds two things it will not let go of: you must be aware of different perspectives within and between religious and non-religious beliefs such as atheism and humanism, and you must make specific reference to sources of wisdom and authority, including scripture. You must also know that the religious traditions of Great Britain are in the main Christian, and that they are diverse. A top-level answer therefore never says 'religious people believe'; it says which tradition, which teaching, and where that teaching comes from, and it knows that British Christians disagree with one another as sharply as they disagree with Muslims or humanists.","code":"3.2.1.4 — The theme's own requirement: religious teachings, contrasting perspectives, and the three prescribed issues"},{"h":"Peace, justice, forgiveness, reconciliation: four words, four meanings","t":"Peace is not only the absence of fighting; in Jewish and Christian use, shalom means wholeness and right relationships, which is why Isaiah imagines nations who 'beat their swords into ploughshares' (Isaiah 2:4), and why Jesus says 'Blessed are the peacemakers' (Matthew 5:9). Justice is giving people what they are due, including putting a wrong right; the prophets tie it to the poor, and Islam makes justice a duty even towards an enemy, since the Qur'an tells believers who fight back 'do not overstep the limits' (Surah 2:190). Forgiveness is giving up the right to punish or resent; Christians pray it in the Lord's Prayer and Jews seek it at Yom Kippur through teshuvah. Reconciliation is the repair of the relationship afterwards, which forgiveness makes possible but does not by itself achieve. The examiner wants meaning and significance, so a top-level answer shows how the four pull against each other: justice may demand what forgiveness sets aside, and an evaluation that notices that tension is arguing rather than defining.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — The meaning and significance of peace, justice, forgiveness and reconciliation"},{"h":"Violence, and the special case of violent protest","t":"Violence is using physical force to injure, damage or intimidate, and every tradition on this specification begins by condemning it: 'You shall not murder' (Exodus 20:13) is shared by Judaism and Christianity, Jesus told the disciple in Gethsemane to 'Put your sword back in its place' (Matthew 26:52), and the Buddhist first precept is to abstain from taking life; the wider principle of non-harm is ahimsa. Violent protest is the hard case the specification names: may a believer use force against an unjust law or an unjust regime? Some Christians argue not, pointing to 'Do not repay evil for evil' (Romans 12:17) and to Jesus telling his followers to turn the other cheek (Matthew 5:39). Others point to Jesus driving the traders out of the Temple (Mark 11:15-17) as forceful protest against injustice in God's house, and argue that standing by while people are oppressed is itself a sin. A top-level answer keeps damage to property and harm to persons apart, and says which teaching it is following and why the other reading is weaker.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — Violence, including violent protest"},{"h":"Terrorism, and why it is not the same as jihad","t":"Terrorism is the use of violence and fear against civilians to force a political or religious change, and it is condemned across the traditions because it deliberately targets the innocent: Judaism and Christianity forbid murder, and the Qur'an teaches that saving one life is like saving all humanity (Surah 5:32) and that those who fight must not overstep the limits (Surah 2:190). Muslims stress that the greater jihad is the inner struggle to live as God wants, and that the lesser jihad, military struggle, may only be declared by a proper authority in defence, may never harm women, children or non-combatants, and may not be waged to gain territory or to force conversion. Terrorism breaks every one of those conditions, which is why Muslim leaders in Britain reject any claim that attacks such as the London bombings of 7 July 2005 were jihad. A top-level answer makes that distinction explicitly and then explains how believers respond, through condemnation, interfaith work and support for the victims.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — Terrorism"},{"h":"Why wars happen: greed, self-defence, retaliation","t":"The specification names three reasons, and they are not morally equal. Greed is wanting another country's land, oil, minerals or trade, and no tradition on this specification will defend it: the tenth commandment forbids coveting, and the Buddha taught that greed is one of the three poisons that keep suffering going. Self-defence is protecting your own people or a neighbour under attack, and it is the one reason most traditions allow, because standing by while the innocent are killed is itself a failure of duty; the Qur'an permits fighting those who fight you, inside limits (Surah 2:190). Retaliation, striking back to punish or to avenge, sits between them: it may look like justice but it usually feeds the next round of the quarrel, which is what the Dhammapada means by saying that hatred is never appeased by hatred. A top-level answer sorts a given conflict into these categories and shows that the just war criteria exist precisely to keep self-defence apart from revenge.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — Reasons for war"},{"h":"Just war theory: the checklist, and what it is for","t":"Just war theory is the Christian attempt, worked out by Augustine and then by Thomas Aquinas, to say when fighting can be the lesser evil, and the specification requires the criteria by name. A war is just if it has a just cause, usually defence against aggression; if it is declared by a lawful authority, a government rather than a private group; if the intention is right, to restore peace and not to take revenge or seize land; if it is a last resort after talks and sanctions have failed; if there is a reasonable chance of success, so lives are not thrown away; and if the force used is proportionate, with civilians not deliberately attacked. The Catechism of the Catholic Church sets out these conditions for legitimate defence at paragraph 2309. The criteria are a test, not a permission slip, and a top-level answer uses them as one: it applies each relevant criterion to the case in the question, and says which one the war or the weapon fails.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — The just war theory"},{"h":"Holy war: fought for God, and argued about ever since","t":"A holy war is one fought for a religious cause, or believed to be commanded or sanctioned by God, usually led or authorised by a religious leader, with those who take part expecting spiritual reward. The Crusades, launched when Pope Urban II called Western Christians to recover the Holy Land in 1095, are the standard Christian example; Jewish tradition distinguishes a milchemet mitzvah, a war commanded in the Torah, from a milchemet reshut, an optional war a king might choose, and it hedged even those with rules such as the command to offer terms of peace before attacking a town (Deuteronomy 20:10). What matters for the exam is the contemporary judgement: most Christians today argue that a holy war cannot be justified now, because Jesus' own teaching points the other way and because the Church has no authority to command killing, while Muslims answer the charge that jihad means holy war by pointing to the greater jihad. A top-level answer separates the historical instances from what the traditions teach today.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — Holy war"},{"h":"Pacifism: the refusal to fight, absolute or not","t":"Pacifism is the belief that violence and war are always wrong, so disputes must be settled without them. It is one of the three prescribed contrasting issues, so you must be able to argue it from Christianity and at least one other tradition. Quakers, the Religious Society of Friends, have refused to bear arms since their peace testimony was declared to Charles II in 1660, and Christian pacifists appeal to 'Love your enemies' (Matthew 5:44) and to Jesus' refusal of armed rescue in Gethsemane. Buddhists ground the same conclusion in the first precept and in ahimsa, non-harm, which Hindus and Jains share. Against them stands the just war tradition, held by most Christians including the Roman Catholic Church and the Church of England, which argues that refusing all force abandons the innocent to their attackers. Conscientious objectors in Britain, many of them Quakers, were tribunal-tested in both world wars and often served as stretcher bearers. A top-level answer names who holds which position and tests each against a real case.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — Pacifism"},{"h":"Does religion cause war? Handling the charge","t":"This is the commonest 12-mark statement in the theme, and it needs both sides. In support: the Crusades were preached as God's will; the Troubles in Northern Ireland ran along Protestant and Catholic lines until the Good Friday Agreement of 1998; terrorist groups still claim religious authority for attacks on civilians; and many humanists and atheists argue that faith makes a cause absolute, so believers who think God commands the fight cannot compromise. Against: most wars are fought over land, power, security and money, and religion is the banner rather than the engine, as the identity labels in Northern Ireland covered a quarrel about British or Irish rule; religions themselves teach peace, and the same traditions have produced the peace-makers and the aid workers; and non-religious ideologies have caused the deadliest wars of the last century. A top-level answer does not simply list conflicts: it asks what work religion is doing in each one, and its conclusion says which explanation carries more weight and why.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion and belief in 21st century conflict — Religion and belief as a cause of war and violence"},{"h":"Nuclear weapons and deterrence: holding what you would not use","t":"Deterrence is keeping nuclear weapons so that an enemy is too afraid of the consequences to attack, while intending never to fire them. It is a live British argument: the United Kingdom keeps Trident, a submarine-based system based on the Clyde, and Parliament voted in 2016 to renew it. Some Christians accept deterrence as a conditional, temporary evil that has kept great powers from war; the Church of England's General Synod, debating the report The Church and the Bomb, rejected unilateral disarmament and supported multilateral disarmament instead. Others, including Quakers, Pax Christi and Christian CND, answer that it is wrong to threaten what it would be wrong to do, that the money belongs to the poor, and that possession is an act of intimidation in itself. Muslim and Jewish thinkers divide in much the same way between defence and the sanctity of life. A top-level answer keeps possessing and using apart, because an argument that works against one may not touch the other.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion and belief in 21st century conflict — Nuclear weapons, including nuclear deterrence"},{"h":"Using weapons of mass destruction: the clearest case in the theme","t":"Weapons of mass destruction are nuclear, chemical and biological weapons, able to kill very large numbers of people at once and to leave poison, radiation and disease behind them. Their use is the third prescribed contrasting issue, and it is the shortest route in the whole theme from teaching to conclusion: because these weapons cannot tell a soldier from a child, using them breaks the just war criteria of proportionality and the sparing of civilians at once, and offends 'You shall not murder' (Exodus 20:13) and the Qur'an's limit on those who fight (Surah 2:190). The atomic bombing of Hiroshima on 6 August 1945 and of Nagasaki three days later is the case usually argued, with defenders claiming it ended the war sooner and opponents pointing to the civilian dead and the later deaths from radiation. The Catechism of the Catholic Church at paragraph 2314 calls acts of war aimed at the destruction of whole cities a crime against God and humanity. A top-level answer applies a named criterion and then tests the strongest reply.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion and belief in 21st century conflict — The use of weapons of mass destruction"},{"h":"Peace-making, and the individuals the specification requires","t":"The specification asks for religion and peace-making in the contemporary world including the work of individuals influenced by religious teaching, so names and deeds have to be learnt, not just the idea that religions like peace. Martin Luther King Jr was a Baptist minister who led the Montgomery bus boycott and the 1963 March on Washington, insisted on non-violent resistance because he read Jesus' command to love enemies as a political method, won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1964 and was assassinated in 1968. Desmond Tutu, the Anglican Archbishop of Cape Town, opposed apartheid without violence, received the Nobel Peace Prize in 1984, and chaired South Africa's Truth and Reconciliation Commission, which heard evidence from 1996 and offered a route to reconciliation through truth rather than revenge. Mahatma Gandhi built satyagraha on the Hindu principle of ahimsa. A top-level answer names the person, the teaching that moved them and the concrete thing they did, because that detail is exactly what turns a simple explanation into a detailed one.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion and belief in 21st century conflict — Religion and peace-making in the contemporary world"},{"h":"The victims of war, and the organisation you must know","t":"One present day religious organisation must be studied in enough detail to explain two ways in which it helps and to attach a source of teaching to them. Christian Aid was founded by the British and Irish churches in 1945 to help refugees in a wrecked Europe, works through local partner organisations rather than sending its own staff, raises money in Christian Aid Week each May and campaigns on the causes of poverty; its slogan is that it believes in life before death, and its work rests on 'Love your neighbour' (Matthew 22:39) and on the parable of the Good Samaritan (Luke 10:25-37). Islamic Relief Worldwide was founded in Birmingham in 1984, is funded partly by zakah and sadaqah, and provides emergency food, water, shelter and medical aid in conflict zones, acting on the Qur'an's teaching that saving one life is like saving all humanity (Surah 5:32). A top-level answer gives the way, the effect on real people, and the teaching behind it, and never helps 'the poor' in general when the question says victims of war.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion and belief in 21st century conflict — Religious responses to the victims of war"}],"examples":[{"title":"The one-mark multiple choice: name the idea before you look","setup":"The question reads: Which one of the following means keeping weapons so that an enemy is too afraid to attack? A Pacifism, B Reconciliation, C Deterrence, D Retaliation. You put a tick in the box beside the correct answer, and there is a single mark for it.","steps":["Split the definition in the question into its two halves, holding the weapons and frightening somebody else, because the right word has to do both at once: nothing is fired, and the attack is prevented by fear alone.","Cut the option that means the opposite of holding weapons at all: pacifism is the refusal to use violence in any circumstances, so it cannot be it.","Cut the option about repairing a relationship after a quarrel: reconciliation is what comes after forgiveness, and it has nothing to do with weapons.","Cut retaliation, which is striking back after you have already been attacked, whereas the question describes stopping an attack before it starts.","Tick C, deterrence, and move on inside a minute, because nothing written elsewhere can rescue a wrong tick."],"cues":["Which two things must the right word do at once?","Which option refuses weapons altogether?","Which option is about mending a relationship?","Does retaliation come before or after an attack?","Which option is left, and what exactly does it mean?"]},{"title":"Four marks: making a contrast that is really a contrast","setup":"The question reads: Explain two contrasting religious beliefs about pacifism. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions. [4 marks] It is marked two marks plus two marks, a simple explanation of a contrast earning one and a detailed explanation earning two; if the two beliefs turn out to be similar, only one of them can be credited.","steps":["Set the two sides against each other before writing anything: one side says fighting is always wrong, the other says fighting can be a duty when the innocent are attacked, which are genuinely opposed.","Name the tradition on the first side rather than saying 'some religious people': Quakers have refused to bear arms since their peace testimony of 1660, and Christian pacifists read 'Love your enemies' (Matthew 5:44) as ruling out killing.","Add the development that earns the second mark on that side: because of this, Quakers in both world wars registered as conscientious objectors and many served as stretcher bearers instead of soldiers.","Open the second paragraph with a word that marks the contrast, such as 'whereas', and name its tradition: most Roman Catholics and Anglicans follow just war teaching, which the Catechism sets out at paragraph 2309.","Develop that side too: they argue that refusing all force abandons the victims of an aggressor, so a defensive war fought by a lawful authority as a last resort can be the lesser evil."],"cues":["Are your two beliefs opposed, or just two ways of saying the same thing?","Which named group holds the first belief, and on what teaching?","What do those believers actually do because of it?","Which word will signal the switch, and which tradition holds the second view?","What reason makes the second view more than an assertion?"]},{"title":"Five marks: two teachings and the source mark","setup":"The question reads: Explain two religious teachings about the use of weapons of mass destruction. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks] Four marks are two plus two for the teachings, and the fifth is awarded once for a relevant and accurate reference to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching.","steps":["Choose two teachings that can each be developed, not four you can only assert: the sanctity of life, and the just war requirement that civilians be spared.","Write the first as a point and then a reason: life belongs to God, so deliberately killing thousands of civilians is a sin, which is why Jews and Christians appeal to 'You shall not murder' (Exodus 20:13).","Write the second the same way: a nuclear, chemical or biological weapon cannot tell a soldier from a child, so it fails the just war criteria of proportionality and the sparing of civilians before it is ever fired.","Make the source unmissable rather than hoping it is noticed: state plainly that the Catechism of the Catholic Church, at paragraph 2314, calls acts of war aimed at destroying whole cities a crime against God and humanity.","Stop at two teachings, because a third is not marked, and spend the time saved on the evaluation instead."],"cues":["Which two teachings can you actually develop?","What reason turns the first teaching into an explanation?","What is it about these weapons that the criteria catch?","Where exactly is your source, and have you named it?","Is a third teaching worth any marks here?"]},{"title":"Four marks: contrasting two traditions on forgiveness","setup":"The question reads: Explain two contrasting religious beliefs in contemporary British society about forgiveness. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions. [4 marks] It is marked two plus two, a simple explanation of a relevant and accurate contrast earning one mark and a detailed explanation earning two; if the two beliefs turn out to be similar, only one of them can be credited, to a maximum of two marks.","steps":["Fix the disagreement before you write a word: one side holds that forgiveness is offered freely to the wrongdoer, the other that it has to be earned by repentance and repair, which is a real contrast and not two ways of saying the same thing.","Name the tradition on the first side and the teaching it rests on: many Christians pray in the Lord's Prayer to be forgiven as they forgive others, and Jesus tells Peter to forgive without counting (Matthew 18:21-22).","Develop that side so it earns the second mark: Desmond Tutu, the Anglican Archbishop of Cape Town, argued on exactly that ground that his country should hear the truth and forgive rather than try and punish every offender.","Open the second paragraph with a word that marks the switch, such as 'whereas', and name the other tradition: Jewish teaching puts teshuvah, repentance, first, and it is sought at Yom Kippur.","Develop the second side too: a wrong done to another person must be put right with that person, so the offender is expected to admit it, make amends and ask the victim directly before God's forgiveness is asked for."],"cues":["What exactly do your two sides disagree about?","Which tradition holds the first belief, and where does it come from?","Who shows that belief being acted on, and what did they do?","Which word signals the switch, and which tradition comes second?","What has to happen before forgiveness on the second view?"]},{"title":"Twelve marks: turning a list into a chain of reasoning","setup":"The question reads: 'Religion is the main cause of war in the world today.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [Plus SPaG 3 marks] You have about twelve minutes, and the whole answer is placed in a level.","steps":["Read the statement for the word that decides the argument: 'main' means you are not asked whether religion has ever caused a war, but whether it is the leading cause now.","Build the supporting case as one chain rather than a list of conflicts: believers who think God commands the fight cannot compromise, which is why the Crusades were preached as God's will and why groups today claim religious authority for killing civilians.","Build the opposing case to the same standard: the quarrel in Northern Ireland was about British or Irish rule with Protestant and Catholic as the labels, so religion marked the sides without being the reason for the fight.","Bring in the non-religious voices on both sides, since the bullets allow it: many humanists say faith replaces evidence with certainty, while historians point out that the deadliest wars of the last century were fought for non-religious ideologies.","Show religion doing the opposite work, with a source: Jesus blesses the peacemakers (Matthew 5:9), and the same traditions produced Martin Luther King Jr and the aid agencies that clear up afterwards.","Write a conclusion that judges rather than summarises: say which side of the evidence weighed more, and which argument you found weakest, so the judgement is supported by what you have already argued."],"cues":["Which single word in the statement sets the question?","What makes a religious cause harder to compromise on?","What were the people of Northern Ireland actually arguing about?","What can a humanist or a historian add to each side?","Where is your religious source, and what does it show religion doing?","What must your last paragraph do that a summary does not?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which three issues does the specification say you must be able to explain contrasting beliefs about in this theme?","a":"Violence, weapons of mass destruction, and pacifism, argued from Christianity as the main religious tradition in Britain plus one or more other traditions."},{"q":"Give the just war criteria as a list.","a":"Just cause, lawful authority, right intention, last resort, reasonable chance of success, proportionate force, and civilians spared."},{"q":"Why do believers say that weapons of mass destruction cannot meet the just war criteria?","a":"Because they cannot discriminate between soldiers and civilians, so they break the requirement to spare civilians and the requirement that force be proportionate."},{"q":"What is the difference between the greater and the lesser jihad?","a":"The greater jihad is the inner struggle to live as God wants; the lesser jihad is military struggle, allowed only in defence, under proper authority, and never against non-combatants."}]},"room":{"facts":["The three prescribed contrasting issues in Theme D are violence, weapons of mass destruction, and pacifism, argued from Christianity plus one or more other religious traditions.","Theme D is one five-part question of 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks, and the paper advises 25 minutes on a theme, which is about a minute a mark.","The 4-mark contrasting question on Component 2 adds the instruction to refer to one or more religious traditions, so the tradition has to be named in the answer.","The 5-mark question adds the instruction to refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching, and that reference carries a mark of its own.","The 12-mark evaluation on Component 2 also carries 3 SPaG marks, and only the best of the four themes' SPaG marks counts towards the paper.","Christianity is the main religious tradition of Great Britain and the religious traditions of Britain are diverse: both statements are required knowledge for this theme."],"questions":[{"q":"Which one of the following means a war fought for a religious cause or believed to be commanded by God? A Pacifism B Deterrence C Holy war D Retaliation. Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. [1 mark]","a":"AO1:1. Answer: C Holy war.","sol":"Answer: C Holy war. A holy war is fought for a religious cause, or is believed to be commanded or sanctioned by God, and is usually declared or blessed by a religious leader, with those who take part expecting spiritual reward. Pacifism is the refusal to use violence at all, deterrence is keeping weapons so an enemy dares not attack, and retaliation is striking back after an attack.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Put the idea in your own words before you look at the options: who is said to have wanted this war?","Two of the four options are about weapons or responses rather than about the reason for fighting; rule them out first.","The term you want is defined as a war fought for a religious cause, usually declared or blessed by a religious leader, with those who take part expecting spiritual reward; test each option against that whole definition."],"codes":["3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — Holy war"]},{"q":"Which one of the following means the belief that violence and war are always wrong? A Justice B Pacifism C Terrorism D Reconciliation. Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. [1 mark]","a":"AO1:1. Answer: B Pacifism.","sol":"Answer: B Pacifism. Pacifism is the belief that violence and war are always wrong, so disputes must be settled peacefully; Quakers are the best known Christian pacifists. Justice is giving people what is due to them, terrorism is the use of violence and fear against civilians for a political or religious aim, and reconciliation is restoring a relationship after a quarrel.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The answer is the name of a position people hold, not something that is done to anyone.","One option is the opposite of the idea in the question, since it is violence itself; strike it out.","Of the three abstract nouns left, ask which names a belief people hold about war itself, and check your choice word for word against the definition in the question."],"codes":["3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — Pacifism"]},{"q":"Which one of the following means restoring a friendly relationship after a conflict? A Greed B Protest C Reconciliation D Punishment. Put a tick in the box next to the correct answer. [1 mark]","a":"AO1:1. Answer: C Reconciliation.","sol":"Answer: C Reconciliation. Reconciliation is the repair of a broken relationship, the stage beyond forgiveness: forgiveness gives up the right to punish, while reconciliation brings the two sides back together, as the Truth and Reconciliation Commission in South Africa tried to do after apartheid. Greed is a reason wars start, protest is a way of objecting, and punishment belongs to justice.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Two of these four are about wanting or about hurting; the answer is about mending.","Protest is what you do when you object to something, which is not the same as making peace afterwards.","The word you want names the stage beyond forgiveness: forgiveness gives up the right to punish, while this brings the two sides back together; hold each option against that meaning."],"codes":["3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — The meaning and significance of peace, justice, forgiveness and reconciliation"]},{"q":"Give two of the criteria for a just war. [2 marks]","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points, first two answers only: just cause; lawful authority; right intention; last resort; reasonable chance of success; proportionate force; civilians spared.","sol":"It must be fought for a just cause, such as defence against an attack. It must be declared by a lawful authority, that is a government rather than a private group. (Any two of: just cause; lawful authority; right intention; last resort; reasonable chance of success; proportionate force; civilians not deliberately attacked.)","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The criteria come from Augustine and Aquinas and are a test of whether fighting is the lesser evil.","Two marks means two separate points, with no explanation needed or rewarded.","Think about who is allowed to declare the war, and about what must have been tried before it."],"codes":["3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — The just war theory"]},{"q":"Give two reasons why wars are fought. [2 marks]","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points, first two answers only: greed for land or resources; self-defence; retaliation; defence of an ally; religion or ideology; independence.","sol":"Greed, when one country wants another country's land, oil or wealth. Self-defence, when a country is attacked and fights to protect its people. (Retaliation, revenge for an earlier attack, is the third reason the specification names.)","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The specification names three reasons in this theme; two of them will do.","Give two bare reasons: no development is required and none is rewarded here.","One of the three is about wanting what belongs to someone else, and another is about protecting your own."],"codes":["3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — Reasons for war"]},{"q":"Give two reasons why religious believers condemn terrorism. [2 marks]","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points: it kills innocent people; life is sacred and given by God; it rules out peaceful means; it uses fear to coerce; it breaks the limits set on fighting; it brings a religion into disrepute.","sol":"It deliberately kills innocent civilians, and every tradition teaches that life is sacred and that murder is forbidden. It uses fear to force people into changes rather than seeking justice by peaceful means, which the conditions on any religiously permitted fighting rule out.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about who is harmed in a terrorist attack, and what every tradition says about them.","Two separate points earn the two marks; explaining either one earns nothing extra.","One reason is about the victims, another about the method of forcing change by fear."],"codes":["3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — Terrorism"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting religious beliefs about pacifism. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions. [4 marks]","a":"AO1:3. Two plus two, simple explanation of a contrast 1 mark, detailed 2. Contrast: Quaker and other Christian pacifism against the Islamic teaching that fighting is permitted within limits (lesser jihad, Surah 2:190), or against just war teaching held by most Catholics and Anglicans; the Buddhist first precept is a further non-Christian pacifist belief. If similar beliefs are given only one may be credited, to 2 marks maximum.","sol":"Quakers, the Religious Society of Friends, believe that all war is wrong, because there is something of God in every person and because Jesus told his followers to 'Love your enemies' (Matthew 5:44). They have refused to bear arms since their peace testimony was declared in 1660, and in both world wars many Quakers registered as conscientious objectors and served as stretcher bearers rather than as soldiers.\n\nIslam, by contrast, does not teach that all fighting is wrong. The Qur'an permits believers to fight those who fight them while warning 'do not overstep the limits' (Surah 2:190), and Muslims hold that the lesser jihad, military struggle, may be fought in self-defence when a proper authority declares it, though never to gain territory and never against women, children or other non-combatants. Refusing all force, they argue, would leave the innocent at the mercy of an aggressor.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The two beliefs must genuinely oppose each other, or only one of them can score.","Write one short paragraph per belief, and open the second with a word such as 'whereas' so the contrast is visible.","Each paragraph needs a named tradition and then a reason, an example or a teaching to develop it."],"codes":["3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — Pacifism","3.2.1.4 — The theme's own requirement: religious teachings, contrasting perspectives, and the three prescribed issues"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting religious beliefs about the use of violence in protest. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions. [4 marks]","a":"AO1:3. Two plus two. Contrast: non-violent resistance grounded in Jesus' teaching (Matthew 5:39, 5:44) against the Islamic teaching that force may be used against grave injustice within the limits of lesser jihad (Surah 2:190); Christians citing the Temple incident, or ahimsa, are alternative sides.","sol":"Many Christians believe violent protest is always wrong. They follow Paul's instruction 'Do not repay evil for evil' (Romans 12:17) and Jesus' teaching to turn the other cheek (Matthew 5:39), and they point to Martin Luther King Jr, a Baptist minister who led marches and boycotts against segregation but insisted that his followers accept violence rather than use it, because he believed hatred cannot be driven out by hatred.\n\nMuslims, by contrast, teach that oppression must be resisted, if necessary by force, once other means have failed. The Qur'an permits believers to fight those who fight them, while warning in the same verse 'do not overstep the limits' (Surah 2:190), so a struggle against an unjust ruler must be declared by a proper authority and may never be turned on women, children or other non-combatants.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["One belief must be Christian and one from another religious tradition: pick a Christian teaching on non-violence and an Islamic teaching on when force is permitted.","Two short paragraphs, each naming who holds the belief and the teaching or example behind it.","Keeping harm to property and harm to people apart will help you develop the second belief."],"codes":["3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — Violence, including violent protest","3.2.1.4 — The theme's own requirement: religious teachings, contrasting perspectives, and the three prescribed issues"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting religious beliefs about a country keeping nuclear weapons as a deterrent. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions. [4 marks]","a":"AO1:3. Two plus two. Contrast: conditional acceptance of deterrence as protecting life and keeping the peace, against the argument that threatening what would be wrong to do is itself wrong and that the money belongs to the poor.","sol":"Some Christians accept deterrence. They argue that a government's first duty is to protect its people, and that weapons which are never fired have kept the great powers from attacking one another, so keeping them is the lesser evil while others hold them; the Church of England's General Synod, debating the report The Church and the Bomb, rejected giving up Britain's weapons on its own and supported multilateral disarmament instead.\n\nOther Christians, including Quakers and members of Pax Christi and Christian CND, believe deterrence is wrong. They argue that it is immoral to threaten what would be a sin to carry out, since using such weapons would break the just war requirement to spare civilians, and that the enormous cost is money that should be spent on the poor. For them, following 'Blessed are the peacemakers' (Matthew 5:9) means campaigning for disarmament.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The question is about keeping the weapons, not about firing them: keep the two apart.","Name the groups, because 'some people think' will not earn the development mark.","For each side give the reason behind the belief: what the weapons are supposed to achieve, and what is wrong with a threat."],"codes":["3.2.1.4 Religion and belief in 21st century conflict — Nuclear weapons, including nuclear deterrence","3.2.1.4 Religion and belief in 21st century conflict — The use of weapons of mass destruction"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting religious beliefs in contemporary British society about forgiveness. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions. [4 marks]","a":"AO1:3. Two plus two, simple explanation of a relevant and accurate contrast 1 mark, detailed 2. Contrast: many Christians hold that forgiveness should be offered freely, whether or not it is asked for, following the Lord's Prayer and Jesus' answer to Peter (Matthew 18:21-22), whereas Judaism teaches that teshuvah comes first and that a wrong done to a person must be put right with that person. If similar beliefs are given only one may be credited, to 2 marks maximum.","sol":"Many Christians in Britain believe forgiveness should be offered freely, whether or not the wrongdoer asks for it. They pray in the Lord's Prayer to be forgiven as they forgive others, and Jesus tells Peter to forgive without counting (Matthew 18:21-22), which is why Desmond Tutu, the Anglican Archbishop of Cape Town, urged South Africa to forgive rather than punish every offender.\n\nJewish teaching, by contrast, puts repentance first. Teshuvah, turning back, is sought at Yom Kippur, but a wrong done to another person must be put right with that person, so the offender is expected to admit it, make amends and ask the victim directly before God's forgiveness is asked for.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The two beliefs must really disagree with each other, or only one of them can be credited.","Give one short paragraph to each belief, naming the tradition that holds it and the teaching it rests on.","Open the second paragraph with a contrast word such as 'whereas', and develop each belief with an example or a consequence."],"codes":["3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — The meaning and significance of peace, justice, forgiveness and reconciliation","3.2.1.4 Religion and belief in 21st century conflict — Religion and peace-making in the contemporary world"]},{"q":"Explain two religious teachings about the use of weapons of mass destruction. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"AO1:1. Two plus two for the teachings plus one for an accurate source. Teachings: the sanctity of life and the ban on murder; the just war requirements of proportionality and sparing civilians; stewardship of creation; the duty to protect the innocent.","sol":"The first teaching is the sanctity of life. Jews and Christians hold that life is God's gift and that only God has the right to take it, which is why the commandment 'You shall not murder' (Exodus 20:13) is used against any weapon designed to kill on this scale; a single nuclear bomb kills tens of thousands of people who have chosen nothing, and the radiation goes on killing long afterwards.\n\nThe second teaching is that fighting has limits. Christian just war theory requires that force be proportionate and that civilians are not deliberately attacked, and a nuclear, chemical or biological weapon cannot tell a soldier from a child, so it fails both tests before it is even fired. Islam sets the same limit: the Qur'an allows believers to fight those who fight them but warns them 'do not overstep the limits' (Surah 2:190).\n\nThe Catechism of the Catholic Church, at paragraph 2314, calls acts of war aimed at destroying whole cities a crime against God and humanity.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Two teachings only: a third earns nothing, and the time is better spent developing these.","Develop each teaching with a reason or an example, then check the fifth mark is not left to chance.","Name your source openly, with its book and chapter and verse or its paragraph number."],"codes":["3.2.1.4 Religion and belief in 21st century conflict — The use of weapons of mass destruction","3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — The just war theory"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which a present day religious organisation helps the victims of war. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"AO1:1. Two plus two for the ways plus one for an accurate source. Ways: emergency food, water, shelter and medical aid; work through local partners; help for refugees; long-term rebuilding; campaigning and advocacy; raising funds.","sol":"Christian Aid, founded by the British and Irish churches in 1945 to help refugees in a Europe wrecked by war, gives emergency relief when fighting drives people from their homes. It sends money for food, clean water and shelter through local partner organisations already working in the country, so that help reaches people quickly and is given to whoever needs it rather than to one side.\n\nIts second kind of work is longer term. It helps communities rebuild after a conflict, supporting people to earn a living again and to return home, and it campaigns in Britain on the causes of poverty and displacement, raising money and awareness in Christian Aid Week each May.\n\nThe work rests on Jesus' command 'Love your neighbour' (Matthew 22:39) and on the parable of the Good Samaritan (Luke 10:25-37), in which the neighbour is the stranger who is helped across a divide.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The question says victims of war, so helping the poor in general will not do.","Name your one organisation and give two different kinds of work it does, each developed with what it actually provides.","The fifth mark is a separate one: attach a named teaching to the work before you finish."],"codes":["3.2.1.4 Religion and belief in 21st century conflict — Religious responses to the victims of war"]},{"q":"'Religion is the main cause of war in the world today.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels-marked. Support: holy war and the absoluteness of a divine command; the Crusades; religious labels in Northern Ireland; terrorism claiming religious authority; humanist and atheist criticism. Other views: land, power and money as the real drivers; religion as a banner; non-religious ideologies; religions' own peace teaching and peace-makers. Level 4 needs both sides reasoned, religion applied and a justified conclusion.","sol":"There is a serious case for the statement. A religious cause is hard to compromise on, because a believer who is convinced that God commands the fight cannot trade away what God has commanded, and the idea of holy war gives that conviction a name: the Crusades were preached as God's will when Pope Urban II called Western Christians to recover the Holy Land in 1095, and those who went expected a spiritual reward for fighting. The pattern has not disappeared. Terrorist groups today claim religious authority for attacks on civilians, and the communities that fought through the Troubles in Northern Ireland until the Good Friday Agreement of 1998 were named as Protestant and Catholic. Many humanists and atheists press the point further: faith replaces evidence with certainty, so it settles disputes by force rather than by reason.\n\nA different point of view says that religion is the banner rather than the engine. The quarrel in Northern Ireland was about whether the province should be governed from London or Dublin, and the religious labels marked which side people were on without being what they were arguing over. Most modern wars have been fought over land, oil, security and power, and the deadliest wars of the last century were driven by nationalism and by political ideologies that were not religious at all. On this account religion is used to recruit and to justify, which is not the same as causing.\n\nReligion can also be shown doing the opposite work, and this is the strongest reply to the statement. Jesus blesses the peacemakers (Matthew 5:9) and told the disciple in Gethsemane to 'Put your sword back in its place' (Matthew 26:52); the Qur'an permits fighting only within limits and tells believers 'do not overstep the limits' (Surah 2:190). The same traditions produced Martin Luther King Jr, whose non-violent campaign came directly from Jesus' command to love enemies, and Desmond Tutu, who used his Christian belief in forgiveness to chair South Africa's Truth and Reconciliation Commission. Christian Aid and Islamic Relief exist to repair what wars do.\n\nSupporters of the statement can answer that religions preaching peace while their members fight only shows how easily faith is turned to violence. That is fair, but it concedes the point: if the teaching says one thing and the war says another, something other than the teaching is driving the war.\n\nIn my judgement the statement is wrong as it stands, though not foolish. Religion makes conflicts harder to end, because it adds absolute conviction to a quarrel about land or power, and that influence is real. But the quarrels themselves are usually about the things people have always fought over, and religion is at least as often the force that stops the fighting and clears up afterwards. Religion is an accelerant in war rather than its main cause, so I would change 'main cause' to 'a powerful aggravating factor'.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["One word in the statement decides the argument; find it before you plan.","Build one side fully before you start the other: claim, the named evidence or teaching it rests on, then the reasoning that links them.","Aim for two developed arguments each way, use a non-religious voice, and finish by saying which side weighed more and why."],"codes":["3.2.1.4 Religion and belief in 21st century conflict — Religion and belief as a cause of war and violence","3.2.1.4 — The theme's own requirement: religious teachings, contrasting perspectives, and the three prescribed issues"]},{"q":"'Nuclear weapons keep the peace, so it is right for a country to have them.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels-marked. Support: duty of a government to protect life; deterrence has prevented war between great powers; conditional acceptance by many Christians; multilateral rather than unilateral disarmament. Other views: threatening what it would be wrong to do; failure of the just war criteria; cost against the needs of the poor; risk of accident or proliferation; Quaker, Pax Christi and CND positions. Level 4 needs both sides reasoned, religion applied and a justified conclusion.","sol":"The case for the statement begins with the duty to protect life. A government exists to keep its people safe, and many Christians accept that this can justify holding weapons that are meant never to be used: an enemy who knows the cost of attacking does not attack, and the great powers have not fought one another directly since these weapons appeared. On this reasoning deterrence saves the very lives that religious teaching says are sacred, and giving the weapons up on your own would only leave your people exposed to countries that keep theirs. The Church of England's General Synod took something like this view when it debated the report The Church and the Bomb, rejecting unilateral disarmament and supporting multilateral disarmament instead. Parliament's vote in 2016 to renew Trident shows that this remains the mainstream British position.\n\nA different point of view is held by Quakers, by Pax Christi and by Christian CND, and it strikes at the heart of deterrence. Deterrence only works if the threat is real, so a country that keeps these weapons must be sincerely willing to fire them, and firing them would be an atrocity: a nuclear weapon cannot tell a soldier from a child, so its use would break the just war requirements of proportionality and of sparing civilians, and would offend 'You shall not murder' (Exodus 20:13). The Catechism of the Catholic Church, at paragraph 2314, calls acts of war aimed at destroying whole cities a crime against God and humanity, and if using them is such a crime then intending to use them is already a wicked intention. There is a second religious argument about money: the cost of the weapons is money that could feed the hungry, and 'Blessed are the peacemakers' (Matthew 5:9) points towards disarmament rather than towards keeping a threat alive. Non-religious critics add that deterrence relies on every leader, for ever, being rational, and that an accident or a spread of the weapons to unstable states could destroy far more than it protects.\n\nSupporters can reply that peace bought by an unused threat is still peace, and that the fate of countries that disarmed while their neighbours did not is a warning.\n\nIn my judgement the second view is the stronger one. The argument for deterrence is about consequences, and it is a real argument, but it cannot escape the fact that the policy requires a settled willingness to do something the moral criteria condemn absolutely. Religious teaching does not usually allow a good outcome to make a wicked intention innocent. The honest conclusion is that keeping these weapons may be understandable while others hold them, but it is not right, so a country's aim should be genuine multilateral disarmament rather than permanent renewal.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["The statement joins two claims: that the weapons work, and that having them is therefore right. You can accept one and reject the other.","Give each side a named group and a teaching, not just an opinion, and develop the reasoning as a chain.","Your conclusion must say which argument you found strongest and why, not merely that both sides have a point."],"codes":["3.2.1.4 Religion and belief in 21st century conflict — Nuclear weapons, including nuclear deterrence","3.2.1.4 Religion and belief in 21st century conflict — The use of weapons of mass destruction","3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — The just war theory"]},{"q":"'Violence can never be justified, whatever the cause.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels-marked. Support: sanctity of life and the ban on murder; Jesus' teaching on enemies and the sword; Quaker peace testimony; ahimsa and the first precept; violence breeds violence; the success of non-violent campaigns. Other views: just war teaching and the duty to defend the innocent; lawful authority and last resort; violent protest against grave injustice; humanist consequence-based reasoning. Level 4 needs both sides reasoned, religion applied and a justified conclusion.","sol":"The case for the statement is strong in every tradition on this specification. Life is held to be sacred and not ours to take, which is what 'You shall not murder' (Exodus 20:13) protects, and Jesus went further than the commandment: he told his followers 'Love your enemies' (Matthew 5:44) and, when one of them drew a weapon in Gethsemane, told him 'Put your sword back in its place' (Matthew 26:52). Quakers have refused to bear arms since their peace testimony was declared in 1660 and many served as stretcher bearers rather than soldiers in the world wars. Buddhism reaches the same conclusion from the first precept and from ahimsa, and the Dhammapada teaches that hatred is never appeased by hatred. There is also a practical argument, religious and non-religious alike: violence hands the other side a grievance and a reason to strike back, while the campaigns of Martin Luther King Jr and of Gandhi show that injustice can be defeated without it.\n\nA different point of view is held by most Christians, including the Roman Catholic Church and the Church of England, and it is not a cynical one. If force is never permitted, then a country invaded, or a group being massacred, must simply be abandoned, and the innocent are the ones who pay for the principle. Christian just war teaching accepts fighting as a lesser evil when a series of tests is met: a just cause, a lawful authority, a right intention, a last resort, a reasonable chance of success, proportionate force, and civilians spared. The Catechism of the Catholic Church sets out these conditions at paragraph 2309. Islam reaches a similar position from the Qur'an, which permits fighting those who fight you but insists 'do not overstep the limits' (Surah 2:190). Many humanists argue the same way from consequences alone: if a small use of force prevents a far greater harm, refusing it is not innocence but a choice to let the harm happen.\n\nA pacifist can reply that once the criteria exist they are always stretched, and that every aggressor claims a just cause. That is a real weakness in just war thinking, but it is an argument for applying the tests strictly, not for abolishing them.\n\nIn my judgement the statement is too absolute. The religious teaching it rests on is genuine and it sets the right starting point: violence is always an evil and never simply a tool. But 'never, whatever the cause' cannot answer the case of the aggressor who is already killing, and the traditions that thought hardest about war, Christian and Muslim alike, allowed defence while fencing it with conditions. The strongest position is therefore close to the statement without being it: violence is always wrong in itself and is only ever permitted as the last defence of the innocent, under strict limits, which is why weapons that kill indiscriminately are ruled out entirely.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Two words in the statement, 'never' and 'whatever', are what the argument turns on.","Build the pacifist case first with its named groups and teachings, then answer it with the criteria the other side uses.","End by saying how far the statement holds and where it fails, so your conclusion follows from what you argued."],"codes":["3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — Violence, including violent protest","3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — Pacifism","3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — The just war theory"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is the difference between justice and forgiveness?","a":"Justice gives people what they are due, including punishment or putting a wrong right; forgiveness gives up the right to punish or resent the wrongdoer."},{"q":"List the criteria for a just war.","a":"Just cause, lawful authority, right intention, last resort, reasonable chance of success, proportionate force, and civilians not deliberately attacked."},{"q":"Which words of Jesus, and at which chapter and verse of Matthew, are used against taking up arms?","a":"'Put your sword back in its place' - Matthew 26:52."},{"q":"What is nuclear deterrence?","a":"Keeping nuclear weapons so that an enemy is too afraid of the consequences to attack, while intending never to use them."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Peace","back":"Not only the absence of war but right relationships and wholeness, the Hebrew shalom. Jesus said 'Blessed are the peacemakers' (Matthew 5:9), and Isaiah pictures nations who 'beat their swords into ploughshares' (Isaiah 2:4).","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — The meaning and significance of peace, justice, forgiveness and reconciliation","open":false},{"front":"Justice","back":"Giving people what is due to them and putting wrongs right. Religions treat it as a duty owed even to an enemy: the Qur'an tells those who fight back 'do not overstep the limits' (Surah 2:190).","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — The meaning and significance of peace, justice, forgiveness and reconciliation","open":false},{"front":"Forgiveness","back":"Giving up the right to punish or resent someone who has wronged you. Christians pray for it in the Lord's Prayer, as they forgive others; Jews seek it through teshuvah, repentance, at Yom Kippur.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — The meaning and significance of peace, justice, forgiveness and reconciliation","open":false},{"front":"Reconciliation","back":"Restoring a broken relationship after a conflict, the step beyond forgiveness. Desmond Tutu chaired South Africa's Truth and Reconciliation Commission, which sought reconciliation through telling the truth rather than through punishment.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — The meaning and significance of peace, justice, forgiveness and reconciliation","open":false},{"front":"Violent protest","back":"Using force to object to a law or regime. Many Christians reject it, citing 'Do not repay evil for evil' (Romans 12:17); others appeal to Jesus driving the traders from the Temple (Mark 11:15-17).","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — Violence, including violent protest","open":false},{"front":"Terrorism","back":"The use of violence and fear against civilians to force a political or religious change. Condemned across the traditions because it targets the innocent, whose lives are sacred, and rejects peaceful means.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — Terrorism","open":false},{"front":"Greater and lesser jihad","back":"The greater jihad is the Muslim's inner struggle to live as God wants. The lesser jihad is military struggle, allowed only in defence, declared by a proper authority, never harming women, children or other non-combatants.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — Terrorism","open":false},{"front":"Reasons for war: greed, self-defence, retaliation","back":"Greed wants another country's land or wealth and no tradition defends it. Self-defence protects your own people and most traditions allow it. Retaliation strikes back in revenge and usually feeds the next round of fighting.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — Reasons for war","open":false},{"front":"The criteria for a just war","back":"Just cause; lawful authority; right intention; last resort; reasonable chance of success; proportionate force; civilians spared. From Augustine and Aquinas; the Catechism of the Catholic Church gives four conditions for legitimate defence at 2309, and protects civilians at 2312-2314.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — The just war theory","open":false},{"front":"Holy war","back":"A war fought for a religious cause or believed to be commanded by God, usually authorised by a religious leader, with spiritual reward expected. The Crusades, called by Pope Urban II in 1095, are the standard example.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — Holy war","open":false},{"front":"Pacifism and the Quaker peace testimony","back":"Pacifism is the belief that violence and war are always wrong. Quakers have refused to bear arms since their peace testimony of 1660, and many served in the world wars as conscientious objectors and stretcher bearers.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion, violence, terrorism and war — Pacifism","open":false},{"front":"Nuclear deterrence and Trident","back":"Deterrence is keeping nuclear weapons so an enemy dares not attack, intending never to fire them. Britain's Trident system is submarine-based; Parliament voted to renew it in 2016. Quakers, Pax Christi and Christian CND oppose it.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion and belief in 21st century conflict — Nuclear weapons, including nuclear deterrence","open":false},{"front":"Weapons of mass destruction","back":"Nuclear, chemical and biological weapons, which kill huge numbers at once. They cannot separate soldiers from civilians, so they fail the just war criteria of proportionality and sparing civilians.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion and belief in 21st century conflict — The use of weapons of mass destruction","open":false},{"front":"Martin Luther King Jr and Desmond Tutu","back":"King, a Baptist minister, led non-violent civil rights campaigns from Jesus' command to love enemies; Nobel Peace Prize 1964. Tutu, Anglican Archbishop of Cape Town, opposed apartheid non-violently; Nobel Peace Prize 1984.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion and belief in 21st century conflict — Religion and peace-making in the contemporary world","open":false},{"front":"Christian Aid and Islamic Relief","back":"Christian Aid, founded by the British and Irish churches in 1945 for Europe's refugees, works through local partners. Islamic Relief Worldwide, founded in Birmingham in 1984, gives emergency aid in conflict zones, funded partly by zakah.","code":"3.2.1.4 Religion and belief in 21st century conflict — Religious responses to the victims of war","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Evaluate: 'Religion is the main cause of war in the world today.')","items":["It is true that a religious cause is unusually hard to compromise on, because a believer who is convinced that God commands the fight cannot trade away what God has commanded; the Crusades were preached as God's will when Pope Urban II called Western Christians to recover the Holy Land in 1095, and those who went expected a spiritual reward for fighting. Yet the same religions produce the strongest teaching against war: Jesus blesses the peacemakers (Matthew 5:9) and told the disciple in Gethsemane 'Put your sword back in its place' (Matthew 26:52), and the Qur'an, which permits fighting those who fight you, immediately warns 'do not overstep the limits' (Surah 2:190). The quarrel in Northern Ireland carried Protestant and Catholic labels, but what was being fought over was whether the province should be governed from London or Dublin, which suggests religion marked the sides rather than caused the dispute. On the evidence so far, then, religion makes conflicts harder to end without being what starts them, and 'main cause' claims more than the examples will carry.","Why this is Level 4: it is a well-argued response with reasoned consideration of different points of view; each point is a logical chain rather than an assertion, the knowledge used is accurate and specific (the date and the preacher of the Crusades, the actual dispute in Northern Ireland), the references to religion are applied to the issue rather than merely quoted, and it closes with a judgement supported by the evidence just given."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same statement, visibly weaker)","items":["I think religion does cause a lot of wars because people have fought about religion for centuries, like the Crusades, and terrorists say they are doing it for their religion. If everyone stopped being religious there would probably be fewer wars. On the other hand some people say wars are really about land and money, and religions do teach peace.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3: it does recognise different points of view, each with a reason, which is what Level 2 describes, and it does refer to religion, so it escapes the Level 2 ceiling that applies when religion is never mentioned. It misses Level 3 because there is no logical chain of reasoning drawing on knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence: the Crusades are named but nothing is said about who called them or why, no teaching or source is cited, terrorists' claims are repeated rather than tested against what the traditions actually permit, and the second point of view is mentioned in a single clause and never developed. It also reaches no judgement at all, which is what Level 4 additionally requires."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Christianity on peace, violence and war","items":["Peace: Jesus said 'Blessed are the peacemakers' (Matthew 5:9); Paul told the Romans to 'Live at peace with everyone' (Romans 12:18).","Non-violence: 'Love your enemies' (Matthew 5:44); Jesus taught his followers to turn the other cheek (Matthew 5:39); 'Do not repay evil for evil' (Romans 12:17).","Against taking up arms: in Gethsemane Jesus told the disciple who struck with a sword 'Put your sword back in its place' (Matthew 26:52).","Against killing: 'You shall not murder' (Exodus 20:13), a commandment shared with Judaism: the sixth of the Ten in the Jewish and Protestant numbering, the fifth in the Catholic numbering.","Just war theory: developed by Augustine (354-430) and Thomas Aquinas (1225-1274); its conditions for legitimate defence by military force are set out in the Catechism of the Catholic Church, paragraph 2309.","The criteria: just cause, lawful authority, right intention, last resort, reasonable chance of success, proportionate force, civilians spared.","Weapons of mass destruction: the Catechism of the Catholic Church, paragraph 2314, calls acts of war aimed at the destruction of whole cities a crime against God and humanity.","Pacifism: the Religious Society of Friends (Quakers) declared their peace testimony to Charles II in 1660 and have refused to bear arms since; many Quakers were conscientious objectors in both world wars and served as stretcher bearers.","Peace organisations: Pax Christi, the international Catholic movement for peace founded in 1945; Christian CND, the Christian section of the Campaign for Nuclear Disarmament, which was founded in 1958.","Deterrence: the Church of England's General Synod debated the report The Church and the Bomb and rejected unilateral nuclear disarmament, supporting multilateral disarmament instead.","Violent protest: some Christians appeal to Jesus driving the traders out of the Temple (Mark 11:15-17); others reply that no person was harmed there and that Jesus refused armed rescue at his arrest.","Holy war: the Crusades were launched when Pope Urban II called Western Christians to recover the Holy Land in 1095; almost all Christians today hold that a holy war cannot be justified now.","Forgiveness: Christians pray in the Lord's Prayer to be forgiven as they forgive others, and Jesus tells Peter to forgive without counting (Matthew 18:21-22)."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Islam on peace, violence and war","items":["The words Islam and salaam share a root connected with peace and with submission to God; Muslims greet one another with a wish for peace.","Limits on fighting: the Qur'an permits believers to fight those who fight them but warns 'do not overstep the limits' (Surah 2:190).","The sanctity of life: Surah 5:32 teaches that to save one life is like saving the whole of humanity, and to take one life unjustly is like killing all humanity.","Greater jihad: the inner struggle of every Muslim to overcome selfishness and live as God wants; it is the meaning most Muslims stress.","Lesser jihad: military struggle in defence of the faith or the community, permitted only under conditions - declared by a proper authority, fought in self-defence and not to gain territory or force conversion, a last resort, ending when the enemy surrenders.","Non-combatants: the tradition forbids harming women, children, the elderly and other non-combatants, and forbids the destruction of crops and trees.","Terrorism: Muslim leaders in Britain reject the claim that attacks on civilians, such as the London bombings of 7 July 2005, are jihad, because they break every one of those conditions.","Justice: Muslims hold justice to be a duty owed even to an enemy, and forgiveness is praised, God being named the Most Merciful in the opening of almost every surah.","Victims of war: Islamic Relief Worldwide, founded in Birmingham in 1984, provides emergency food, water, shelter and medical aid in conflict zones, funded partly by zakah and sadaqah.","Weapons of mass destruction: most Muslim scholars hold that weapons which kill indiscriminately can never meet the limits the Qur'an sets on fighting."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Judaism on peace, violence and war","items":["Shalom means peace, and carries the sense of wholeness and right relationship, not merely the absence of fighting.","'You shall not murder' (Exodus 20:13) is the sixth commandment in the Jewish numbering, which Protestant Churches follow, and the fifth in the Catholic numbering; murder is distinguished from killing in self-defence.","Pikuach nefesh: saving a life overrides almost every other commandment, which shapes Jewish thinking about defence and about medical help in war.","Tikkun olam: the duty to repair the world, used to support relief work and peace-making.","Types of war: a milchemet mitzvah is a war commanded in the Torah, such as defence against attack; a milchemet reshut is an optional war, which a king could only fight with the agreement of the Sanhedrin.","Before fighting: Deuteronomy 20:10 requires that terms of peace be offered to a town before it is attacked.","Peace in the prophets: Isaiah pictures the nations who 'beat their swords into ploughshares' and learn war no more (Isaiah 2:4).","Forgiveness: teshuvah, repentance, is sought at Yom Kippur; wrongs against another person must be put right with that person before God's forgiveness is asked.","Enemies: Proverbs 25:21 tells the believer to feed a hungry enemy and give a thirsty one something to drink.","Most Jews today accept that a defensive war can be justified, while many also campaign against nuclear and other weapons that would kill large numbers of civilians."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: non-religious views and contrasting perspectives in British society","items":["Humanists take their ethics from human welfare, reason and the consequences of actions rather than from God, so they judge a war by the harm it causes and prevents.","Many humanists and atheists argue that religion makes conflict worse by making a cause absolute, so that disputes are settled by force rather than by evidence and argument.","Other humanists accept reasoning close to the just war criteria: if a limited use of force prevents a far greater harm, refusing it is a choice to allow that harm.","Bertrand Russell, an atheist philosopher, was imprisoned in the First World War for his pacifist writing and later became the first president of the Campaign for Nuclear Disarmament, founded in 1958.","Non-religious pacifists argue from consequences rather than commands: war kills civilians, wrecks economies and breeds the grievances that cause the next war.","British society is divided on nuclear weapons: Parliament voted in 2016 to renew Trident, the submarine-based system, while CND and church peace groups campaign for disarmament.","Conscientious objection is recognised in Britain: objectors in both world wars faced tribunals, and many, religious and non-religious, served in ambulance and medical units.","Atheists point out that the deadliest wars of the last century were driven by nationalism and by political ideologies rather than by religion, which cuts against the claim that religion is the main cause of war.","The specification requires you to know that the religious traditions of Great Britain are in the main Christian, and that they are diverse: British Christians disagree with each other about pacifism and about nuclear weapons as sharply as they disagree with humanists."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the named examples - peace-makers and one present day organisation","items":["Martin Luther King Jr: Baptist minister; led the Montgomery bus boycott and the 1963 March on Washington; insisted on non-violent resistance because he read Jesus' command to love enemies as a practical method; Nobel Peace Prize 1964; assassinated in 1968.","Desmond Tutu: Anglican Archbishop of Cape Town; opposed apartheid without violence; Nobel Peace Prize 1984; chaired South Africa's Truth and Reconciliation Commission, which heard evidence from victims and perpetrators from 1996.","Mahatma Gandhi: Hindu; built satyagraha, holding to truth, on ahimsa, non-harm; led the Salt March of 1930 against British rule in India; assassinated in 1948.","Christian Aid: founded by the British and Irish churches in 1945 to help refugees in post-war Europe; works through local partner organisations; raises money in Christian Aid Week each May; campaigns on the causes of poverty; its work rests on 'Love your neighbour' (Matthew 22:39) and the parable of the Good Samaritan (Luke 10:25-37).","Islamic Relief Worldwide: founded in Birmingham in 1984; supplies emergency food, water, shelter and medical care in conflict zones, and helps refugees; funded partly by zakah and sadaqah; acts on Surah 5:32, that saving one life is like saving all humanity.","How to use them: name the person or organisation, name the teaching that moved them, and say what they concretely did, because that detail is what turns a simple explanation into a detailed one."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: just war, holy war and nuclear deterrence","items":["Just war: the Christian attempt to say when fighting is the lesser evil, developed by Augustine and by Thomas Aquinas, who required lawful authority, just cause and right intention.","The full criteria expected at GCSE: just cause; lawful authority; right intention; last resort; reasonable chance of success; proportionate force; civilians not deliberately attacked.","Holy war: fought for a religious cause or believed to be commanded by God, usually declared by a religious leader, with spiritual reward expected; the Crusades began with Pope Urban II's call in 1095.","Jewish equivalent: milchemet mitzvah, a commanded war, and milchemet reshut, an optional war, both fenced with rules such as offering peace terms first (Deuteronomy 20:10).","Nuclear deterrence: keeping nuclear weapons so that an enemy is too afraid of the consequences to attack, while intending never to use them.","Britain's deterrent is Trident, carried by submarines and based on the Clyde; Parliament voted in 2016 to renew it.","The moral problem with deterrence: the threat must be sincere to work, so possession requires a willingness to do what the just war criteria forbid.","Weapons of mass destruction: nuclear, chemical and biological; the atomic bombing of Hiroshima on 6 August 1945 and of Nagasaki three days later is the case usually argued, with the killing of civilians and later deaths from radiation set against the claim that it shortened the war."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan - 'Pacifism is the only true religious response to war.' Evaluate this statement. [12 marks] [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","items":["Decode the statement: 'only' and 'true' are the load-bearing words. You are not asked whether pacifism is respectable, but whether every other religious position is a failure of faith. Plan to attack or defend 'only'.","Paragraph 1, in support: religious teaching starts from the sanctity of life and non-violence. Sources: 'Love your enemies' (Matthew 5:44); 'Put your sword back in its place' (Matthew 26:52); the Buddhist first precept and ahimsa. Development: Quakers have refused to bear arms since their peace testimony of 1660 and served as stretcher bearers rather than soldiers.","Paragraph 2, in support: non-violence works and keeps the believer's hands clean. Martin Luther King Jr defeated segregation laws through boycotts and marches while forbidding his followers to strike back, which shows that a religious refusal to fight is a method and not just an abstention.","Paragraph 3, the other view: just war teaching is also religious teaching, not a compromise with the world. Christian just war theory comes from Augustine and Aquinas, and the Catechism of the Catholic Church gives four conditions for legitimate defence at paragraph 2309: the damage must be grave, lasting and certain; other means must be impractical or ineffective; there must be serious prospects of success; and the arms used must not produce evils greater than the evil to be ended. The sparing of civilians and the ban on indiscriminate destruction come later, at paragraphs 2312-2314. The argument is that refusing all force abandons the innocent to an aggressor.","Paragraph 4, the other view developed: Islam reaches a similar position, permitting fighting those who fight you while insisting 'do not overstep the limits' (Surah 2:190), and Judaism distinguishes a commanded war from an optional one while requiring terms of peace to be offered first (Deuteronomy 20:10). Three traditions cannot all be failing to be religious.","Non-religious view, allowed by the bullets: humanists divide the same way, some arguing from consequences that war always breeds the next war, others that a limited use of force can prevent a far greater harm.","The evaluation thread to keep running: the question is whether allowing force under strict conditions is a betrayal of religious teaching or an application of it to a world containing aggressors. Return to that thread at the end of every paragraph rather than listing views.","Timing and the conclusion: about twelve minutes plus a minute to check spelling and punctuation, since SPaG is marked here and the theme's technical terms earn twice. The conclusion must judge: say whether 'only' survives, which argument you found strongest and why, and where the other side retains its force - for example, that pacifism is the clearest response but not the only faithful one, while weapons that kill indiscriminately are ruled out on both views."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"The rubric of Theme D in one place","items":["Contrasting beliefs must be arguable on three issues in this theme: violence, weapons of mass destruction, and pacifism, with reference to Christianity as the main religious tradition in Britain and one or more other traditions.","Where a contrast is asked for, the two beliefs must genuinely differ; two versions of the same view are treated as one.","Name the tradition rather than writing 'some religious people', and remember that a contrast within Christianity is allowed as well as one between religions.","On the source question, give two developed points and then a named source with its reference; a paraphrase attributed to a named text counts, an unattributed idea does not.","On the evaluation, an answer that never refers to religion cannot rise above the middle of the grid, and the conclusion must follow from the arguments already made rather than arrive as a fresh opinion.","Non-religious views such as humanism and atheism are invited but not compulsory on the evaluation; they are most useful on the question of whether religion causes war."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T14:13:40Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.93,"notes":"Every fix in the 'fixed' array landed and none introduced an error: the three 1-mark third hints now test the definition instead of eliminating the last distractor, Catechism 2309 is credited with its four conditions for legitimate defence with civilians cited to 2312-2314, questions[9] and lesson.examples[3] are rebuilt in the Component 2 contrasting form (Christian free forgiveness with Matthew 18:21-22 and Tutu against Jewish teshuvah and restitution to the victim, AO1:3), questions[6] and [7] now contrast Christianity with the Islamic limits on lesser jihad with their `a` fields naming that tradition, the commandment numbering is qualified, examples[0] opens on a real move and the first precept is 'to abstain from taking life' with ahimsa as the wider principle; a full re-read confirms the diff touched nothing else, tariffs remain 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 with the three 12-mark stems carrying the Component 2 bullets and SPaG, all 17 scripture and Catechism references and the new Matthew 18:21-22 and teshuvah material are accurate, and no quotation runs past seven words."}}},"3.2.1.5":{"id":"AQA-8062","topic":"3.2.1.5","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Theme E is one of the four themes you answer on Component 2, and it is worth 24 marks: a 1-mark multiple choice, a 2-mark 'Give two', a 4-mark contrasting question, a 5-mark question whose fifth mark is a source of wisdom and authority, and a 12-mark evaluation that also carries the 3 SPaG marks. The specification names exactly what you must be able to argue about - good and evil intentions, the reasons for crime, the types of crime, the three aims of punishment, prison, corporal punishment and community service, forgiveness, the death penalty, and the two ethical arguments of utility and sanctity of life - and it names three issues on which you must be able to explain contrasting beliefs with reference to Christianity, the main religious tradition in Britain, and one or more other religious traditions: corporal punishment, the death penalty and forgiveness. Marks are lost here by writing opinions about criminals rather than the named teaching of named traditions, so this room drills the teaching, the sources and the argument.","idea":[{"h":"What every answer on this theme has to be able to do","t":"The specification asks for religious teachings and for religious, philosophical and ethical arguments about the issues, and for their impact and influence in the modern world. It also asks you to be aware of contrasting perspectives in contemporary British society on all of them. Three issues are singled out: you must be able to explain contrasting beliefs on corporal punishment, the death penalty and forgiveness with reference to the main religious tradition in Britain, Christianity, and one or more other religious traditions. Section 3.2.1 adds that you should be aware of different perspectives within and between religious and non-religious beliefs such as atheism and humanism, that you must make specific references to sources of wisdom and authority, and that the religious traditions of Great Britain are in the main Christian but also diverse. A top-level answer therefore always names the tradition it is speaking for - not 'religious people' but Roman Catholics, Quakers, Sunni Muslims, Orthodox Jews - and always has a source behind the claim.","code":"3.2.1.5 — The theme's own requirement: religious teachings, contrasting perspectives, and the three prescribed issues"},{"h":"Good and evil intentions and actions: can it ever be good to cause suffering?","t":"The specification puts the hard question inside the bullet, because the answer decides everything that follows. If an action is judged by the intention behind it, a surgeon who cuts and a parent who disciplines are not doing evil, and punishment can be good because it aims at justice or reform. If it is judged by its consequences, then the suffering has to be outweighed by the good it produces. Buddhism makes intention central: the Dhammapada teaches that 'Hatred is never appeased by hatred' (Dhammapada 5), so an act done from hatred is unskilful whatever it achieves. Christians hold that God is just as well as loving, so deserved punishment is not evil, while Jesus' command to 'turn the other cheek' (Matthew 5:39) pushes against revenge. The point matters because all punishment causes suffering deliberately. A top-level answer uses this idea as the hinge of an evaluation: it asks what the suffering is for, and judges the punishment by that.","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion, crime and the causes of crime — Good and evil intentions and actions"},{"h":"Reasons for crime: four pairs, and they are not morally the same","t":"The specification names poverty and upbringing, mental illness and addiction, greed and hate, and opposition to an unjust law. Learn them as four groups, because the examiner sets questions on why people break the law and a vague answer about bad choices scores nothing. Religions that teach a duty to the poor have a stake in the first pair: Muslims pay zakah, Sikhs serve langar to anyone who comes, and Christians read the Sheep and the Goats (Matthew 25:31-46). Mental illness and addiction raise the question of responsibility; Buddhism's Fifth Moral Precept against intoxicants and Islam's prohibition of alcohol are preventative teaching. Greed and hate are named as sins in their own right - the Christian tradition calls the love of money a root of evil (1 Timothy 6:10) and Sikhism lists greed among the five evils. Opposition to an unjust law raises whether breaking a law can be right. A top-level answer picks the pair that fits the question and shows what the religion does about it.","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion, crime and the causes of crime — Reasons for crime"},{"h":"Views about people who break the law for those reasons","t":"This bullet is tied to the one before it: religious attitudes differ sharply depending on why the crime was committed, and they distinguish the crime from the criminal. The Christian teaching usually summed up as hating the sin but loving the sinner comes from here, and so does the belief that everyone is made 'in the image of God' (Genesis 1:27), which means no offender stops being a person. Jesus refused to join the crowd against the woman brought to him for adultery, telling them that the one without sin should be 'the first to throw a stone' (John 8:7), and he promised paradise to the criminal executed beside him (Luke 23:43). Judaism teaches through Ezekiel that God takes 'no pleasure in the death' of the wicked but wants them to turn back (Ezekiel 33:11). Against that, most traditions insist a wrong still has consequences. A top-level answer says what follows in practice: whether the offender is treated as ill, desperate, or simply guilty.","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion, crime and the causes of crime — Views about people who break the law for these reasons"},{"h":"Views about different types of crime: hate crime, theft and murder","t":"The specification names three. Murder is condemned outright everywhere: one of the Ten Commandments says 'You shall not murder' (Exodus 20:13), and the Qur'an teaches that killing one person unjustly is like killing all people (Surah 5:32). Theft is condemned too - 'You shall not steal' (Exodus 20:15) is one of the Ten Commandments and theft is one of the hudud offences in Shari'ah - but it is the crime religions argue about most, because scripture itself notices the starving thief and still requires restitution (Proverbs 6:30-31), and many Muslim jurists hold that the fixed punishment for theft cannot stand where the need was genuine or the evidence doubtful. Hate crime is the contemporary category, and teaching on equality bears on it directly: 'love your neighbour as yourself' (Leviticus 19:18) is shared by Judaism and Christianity. A top-level answer ranks the crimes by the harm done and the intention behind them, rather than saying all crime is equally bad.","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion, crime and the causes of crime — Views about different types of crime"},{"h":"The aims of punishment: retribution, deterrence and reformation","t":"Three aims are named and they pull in different directions. Retribution makes the offender pay back what the crime deserved, and the classic expression of it is the Torah's 'eye for eye, tooth for tooth' (Exodus 21:24), which the rabbis of the Talmud read as a rule of proportionate compensation rather than literal mutilation. Deterrence puts people off, either the offender or everyone watching. Reformation changes the offender, and most churches in Britain, including the Roman Catholic Church and the Church of England, put it first, with Islam and Judaism both requiring the offender to repent and make amends. Christians can argue retribution from Romans 13, where the state carries the sword as God's servant, and reformation from the whole shape of the Gospel. Because each aim justifies a different punishment, a top-level answer never lists the three: it decides which aim the question is really about and argues from that, which is exactly how the death penalty and community service questions are won.","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — The aims of punishment"},{"h":"The treatment of criminals: prison, corporal punishment and community service","t":"Three treatments are named, and each serves some aims better than others. Prison protects the public and deters, but it separates offenders from their families and reoffending is common; the Sheep and the Goats makes visiting prisoners a mark of the saved - 'I was in prison' and you came (Matthew 25:36) - and Christian prison chaplaincy grows out of that. Community service aims at reformation and reparation: the offender gives something back, which fits the Jewish requirement to repair the wrong and the Christian emphasis on restitution, though critics call it a soft option. Corporal punishment is one of the three prescribed contrasting issues: it has not been a court sentence in Britain since the Criminal Justice Act 1948 and was ended in state schools by the Education (No. 2) Act 1986, while some Muslim-majority countries still apply it under Shari'ah. A top-level answer matches the treatment to the aim and says which aim it thinks matters most.","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — The treatment of criminals"},{"h":"Forgiveness: a duty, and a problem for justice","t":"Forgiveness is one of the three prescribed contrasting issues. In Christianity it is not optional: the Lord's Prayer ties being forgiven to forgiving (Matthew 6:12), Jesus told Peter to forgive not seven times but 'seventy-seven times' (Matthew 18:22), and from the cross he prayed 'Father, forgive them' (Luke 23:34). Islam calls God al-Ghafur, the Most Forgiving, and the Qur'an says that a victim's family who give up their right to retaliation have that counted as atonement for them (Surah 5:45). Judaism requires teshuvah - the offender must repent, make amends and ask the wronged person directly, because the Mishnah teaches that Yom Kippur does not atone for sins against another person until they are appeased (Mishnah Yoma 8:9). The tensions the examiner wants are these: does forgiving mean not punishing, who has the standing to forgive, and must the offender repent first? A top-level answer separates forgiving the person from cancelling the sentence.","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — Forgiveness"},{"h":"The death penalty: abolished in Britain, argued over everywhere","t":"The death penalty is the third prescribed contrasting issue, and traditions divide internally as well as from one another. Supporters point to scriptural texts that appear to sanction it, such as Genesis 9:6 and the capital offences of the Torah, and to Romans 13:4, where the ruler does not carry the sword for nothing. Against it stand the sanctity of life, the risk of executing the innocent, and the fact that a dead offender cannot be reformed. The Roman Catholic Church now teaches in the Catechism, revised by Pope Francis in 2018, that the death penalty is inadmissible because it attacks the dignity of the person; Quakers have opposed it for centuries. In Islam, Shari'ah permits qisas for murder, yet the victim's family may accept diyah or pardon instead. In Judaism the Mishnah records a court that executed once in seven years being called destructive (Mishnah Makkot 1:10). Britain abolished it for murder in 1965 and for all crimes by 1998, which is the British contrast a top-level answer uses.","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — The death penalty"},{"h":"The ethical arguments: the principle of utility against the sanctity of life","t":"The specification names both arguments, so both must appear when the death penalty is evaluated. The principle of utility, associated with Jeremy Bentham, judges an action by whether it produces the greatest happiness or the least suffering overall: on that test execution is right only if it really does deter, and studies that fail to show a deterrent effect count directly against it, as does the suffering caused to an innocent person wrongly convicted. The sanctity of life argues from the other end: life is God-given and holy, human beings are made 'in the image of God' (Genesis 1:27), and the Qur'an forbids taking the life God has made sacred except by right (Surah 17:33), so the value of a life does not depend on what it produces. A top-level answer does not report the two arguments side by side; it weighs them, usually by asking whether a benefit that is uncertain can ever outweigh a principle that is absolute.","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — Ethical arguments related to the death penalty"}],"examples":[{"title":"The one-mark multiple choice: work out what the word has to mean, then eliminate","setup":"The question reads: Which one of the following aims of punishment means changing the offender so that they do not commit crime again? A Deterrence B Reparation C Reformation D Retribution. You put a tick in the box next to the correct answer, and there is a single mark for it.","steps":["Fix the target before reading the options again: the answer must name a change inside the offender, not a payment back for the crime, a warning to others or a return for the crime.","Clear the option that is about everybody else being frightened off, which is an effect on the watching public rather than a change in the offender.","Clear the option that is about making amends for the harm the crime caused, which puts things right for the victim rather than changing the offender.","Clear the option that means paying back what the crime deserved, which looks backwards at the crime rather than forwards to a changed life.","One option is left, and it is the aim most British churches put first, so tick C and move on inside a minute."],"cues":["Does the word you want describe a change in the offender, a warning to others, or a return for the crime?","Which option is about frightening other people off?","Which option is about making amends to the victim rather than the offender?","Which option looks backwards at what the crime deserved?","Which option is left, and what does it mean?"]},{"title":"The two-mark question: two separate points, and the trap of answering the wrong question","setup":"The question reads: Give two ways in which criminals may be treated in Britain today. [2 marks] One mark is given for each of two correct points, no development is needed, and only the first two answers are marked.","steps":["Notice that the question asks for treatments, not for aims of punishment: an answer that says retribution and deterrence names why we punish, not what is done to the offender, and scores nothing.","Take two straight from the three the specification names - prison and community service - because they are certain to be on the mark scheme.","Write them as two separate points on separate lines, with no explanation: 'They may be sent to prison.' 'They may be given community service.'","Stop there: a third answer cannot earn a mark once two correct ones are down, and the time belongs to the twelve-mark question."],"cues":["Is the question asking what is done to offenders, or why it is done?","Which treatments does the specification itself name for this theme?","How much writing does a mark for each of two correct points need?","What should you do with a third idea you have thought of?"]},{"title":"The four-mark contrasting question on corporal punishment","setup":"The question reads: Explain two contrasting religious beliefs about corporal punishment. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions. [4 marks] Each contrast earns 1 mark for a simple explanation and 2 for a detailed one, and if the two beliefs given are similar only one of them can be credited, up to a maximum of two marks.","steps":["Choose two positions that really do oppose each other, and name the tradition that holds each, because the instruction requires the tradition to be named.","First belief: most Christians in Britain reject corporal punishment, because every person is made in the image of God and Jesus taught love of neighbour rather than retaliation.","Develop it into the second mark by saying what follows from it - British churches supported ending the cane in schools and argue that punishment should reform rather than injure.","Second belief: some Muslims accept corporal punishment, because Shari'ah prescribes fixed hudud penalties for certain offences and they hold that a known, public penalty deters.","Develop that one as well, with the qualification its own scholars make: the evidence required is very strict and many argue the penalty lapses where need or doubt exists, so it belongs to a fully just society.","Check the two paragraphs contrast rather than agree, since similar beliefs are capped at two marks in total."],"cues":["What does the instruction about religious traditions oblige you to include?","Which tradition will you use for the belief that this punishment is wrong, and on what grounds?","What turns a simple explanation into a detailed one here?","Which tradition can supply the opposing belief, and what is its basis?","What qualification do that tradition's own scholars add?","What should you check before moving on?"]},{"title":"The five-mark question: two reasons plus the source mark","setup":"The question reads: Explain two religious reasons for opposing the death penalty. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks] Four marks are the familiar two plus two, and the fifth mark is given once, for a relevant and accurate reference to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching.","steps":["Plan the shape before writing: one short paragraph per reason, each stating the reason and then developing it, with the source named somewhere unmistakable.","First reason: life is sacred because it is God-given, so it is not the state's to take; develop it with the teaching that human beings are made in the image of God.","Second reason: an executed person can never be reformed, and reformation is the aim most churches put first; develop it with the risk that a wrongful conviction can never be put right.","Name the source plainly so the examiner cannot miss it - the Catechism of the Catholic Church, revised by Pope Francis, teaches that the death penalty is inadmissible because it attacks the dignity of the person.","Read it back and count: two reasons, each developed, and one clearly attributed source. Anything more is time taken from the evaluation."],"cues":["How many paragraphs should this answer have, and what is in each?","What is the first reason, and what belief about human beings develops it?","What does execution make impossible that other punishments allow?","Which named source could carry the fifth mark here?","What are you checking for when you read the answer back?"]},{"title":"The twelve-mark evaluation: turning a statement into two chains of reasoning","setup":"The question reads: 'Criminals should always be forgiven.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [Plus SPaG 3 marks] Level 4 wants a well-argued response with reasoned consideration of different points of view and judgements supported by evidence, with reference to religion applied to the issue.","steps":["Decode the word doing the work: 'always' makes the statement absolute, so the argument turns on whether any case defeats it rather than on whether forgiveness is generally good.","Build the supporting chain from named teaching: Jesus told Peter to forgive seventy-seven times and prayed for his executioners from the cross, so for Christians the duty has no cut-off point.","Extend that chain to consequences - refusing to forgive traps the victim in bitterness, and Buddhism teaches that hatred is never ended by hatred.","Build the opposing chain just as carefully: Judaism requires teshuvah first, so an unrepentant offender has not yet met the conditions of forgiveness, and forgiving is not the same as cancelling a sentence the court imposed for public safety.","Add the non-religious voice the bullets allow: many humanists judge by consequences and will forgive where it helps the victim and the offender, but not where it leaves others in danger.","Write the judgement the last bullet demands, deciding which chain was stronger and why - for example that the duty to forgive the person stands, while the duty to release them does not follow from it."],"cues":["Which single word in the statement decides how hard it is to defend?","Which named teaching gives the supporting side its strongest chain?","What does refusing to forgive cost the victim?","What condition does Jewish teaching place on forgiveness, and what does a court add?","What do the printed bullets allow you to bring in beyond religion?","What must the last paragraph do that a summary does not?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which three issues must you be able to explain contrasting beliefs about in this theme, and with reference to which traditions?","a":"Corporal punishment, the death penalty and forgiveness, with reference to the main religious tradition in Britain, Christianity, and one or more other religious traditions."},{"q":"Name the three aims of punishment the specification lists, and say which one execution makes impossible.","a":"Retribution, deterrence and reformation. Execution makes reformation impossible, because a dead offender cannot change."},{"q":"What is the difference between the principle of utility and the sanctity of life as arguments about the death penalty?","a":"Utility judges execution by its consequences, chiefly whether it deters; sanctity of life holds that a God-given life may not be taken whatever the benefit."},{"q":"In Shari'ah, what may the family of a murder victim accept instead of qisas?","a":"Diyah, financial compensation, or they may simply pardon the offender; the Qur'an treats giving up the right to retaliate as atonement for them (Surah 5:45)."},{"q":"Why does an answer that says only 'some religious people disagree' stay in Level 2?","a":"Because Level 3 needs clear reference to religion: the tradition, church, movement or text has to be named and applied to the issue."}]},"room":{"facts":["The three issues on which contrasting beliefs must be explainable are corporal punishment, the death penalty and forgiveness, with reference to Christianity as the main religious tradition in Britain and one or more other religious traditions.","The three aims of punishment the specification names are retribution, deterrence and reformation.","The three treatments of criminals it names are prison, corporal punishment and community service.","The reasons for crime it names are poverty and upbringing, mental illness and addiction, greed and hate, and opposition to an unjust law; the types of crime it names are hate crime, theft and murder.","The two ethical arguments it names on the death penalty are the principle of utility and the sanctity of life.","Theme E is answered as one five-part question of 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks, and the paper advises 25 minutes on a theme - about a minute a mark.","The fifth mark on the 5-mark question is given once, for a relevant and accurate reference to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching."],"questions":[{"q":"Which one of the following aims of punishment means changing the offender so that they do not commit crime again? A Deterrence B Reparation C Reformation D Retribution","a":"AO1:1. Answer: C Reformation.","sol":"C Reformation. Deterrence is about putting the offender or other people off committing crime; reparation is about the offender making amends for the harm the crime caused; retribution is about the offender paying back what the crime deserved. Only reformation names a change in the offender, and it is the aim most British churches put first.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The answer has to name a change inside the offender rather than an effect on anyone else.","Two of the options are about other people - those watching, and the victim who was harmed.","Of the two that are left, one looks backwards at what the crime deserved."],"codes":["3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — The aims of punishment"]},{"q":"Which one of the following is the belief that life is holy and belongs to God? A Deterrence B Retribution C Sanctity of life D Utility","a":"AO1:1. Answer: C Sanctity of life.","sol":"C Sanctity of life. Deterrence and retribution are aims of punishment, not beliefs about life. Utility is the ethical principle that judges an action by whether it produces the greatest good overall, which is the argument the sanctity of life is usually set against in the death penalty debate.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Two of the four options are aims of punishment rather than beliefs about life itself.","The remaining two are the pair of ethical arguments this theme names on the death penalty.","One of that pair judges by consequences; the other says life is holy whatever the consequences."],"codes":["3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — Ethical arguments related to the death penalty"]},{"q":"Which one of the following is the compensation the family of a murder victim may accept instead of retaliation in Shari'ah law? A Diyah B Hudud C Qisas D Ta'zir","a":"AO1:1. Answer: A Diyah.","sol":"A Diyah. Qisas is the retaliation itself, the like-for-like penalty for murder or injury; hudud are the fixed penalties laid down in the Qur'an and Sunnah for certain named offences; ta'zir are the discretionary punishments a judge may set. Diyah is the compensation paid to the victim's family when they give up their right to qisas, and the Qur'an treats giving it up as atonement for them (Surah 5:45).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["One of the four names a payment; the other three name kinds of punishment.","Two of the options are categories of penalty - the fixed ones and the ones left to the judge.","The one that is left means retaliation, which is what the family would be giving up."],"codes":["3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — The death penalty"]},{"q":"Give two reasons why people commit crime.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points, no development required: poverty; upbringing; mental illness; addiction; greed; hate; opposition to an unjust law.","sol":"Two from: poverty; upbringing; mental illness; addiction; greed; hate; opposition to a law they believe is unjust. Two bare points score the two marks - no explanation is needed or rewarded, and only the first two answers are marked.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The specification names seven reasons for this theme, in four groups; any one of the seven will do.","One pair is about what a person cannot control, another about what a person wants.","Two short separate points are enough; do not develop them."],"codes":["3.2.1.5 Religion, crime and the causes of crime — Reasons for crime"]},{"q":"Give two ways in which criminals may be treated in Britain today.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points: prison; community service; a fine; a suspended sentence; probation or supervision; an electronic tag.","sol":"Two from: they may be sent to prison; they may be given community service; they may be fined; they may be given a suspended sentence; they may be put on probation or supervision; they may be electronically tagged. Aims of punishment such as retribution or deterrence do not answer this question, because they say why offenders are punished rather than what is done to them.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The question asks what is done to offenders, not why it is done.","Two of the three treatments the specification names for this theme are still used by British courts.","Two short separate points are enough; do not develop them."],"codes":["3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — The treatment of criminals"]},{"q":"Give two ways in which a religious believer might show forgiveness to someone who has wronged them.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points: saying they forgive them; refusing to take revenge; praying for them; meeting them face to face; restoring the relationship; supporting a reduced sentence or accepting compensation.","sol":"Two from: telling the person they are forgiven; refusing to seek revenge; praying for them; agreeing to meet them, as in restorative justice; restoring the friendship or relationship; asking for mercy for them in court, or, in Shari'ah, accepting diyah rather than qisas. Two bare points score the two marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what forgiving actually looks like in behaviour rather than in feelings.","One way is something said, another is something deliberately not done.","Two short separate points are enough; do not develop them."],"codes":["3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — Forgiveness"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting religious beliefs about corporal punishment. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions. [4 marks]","a":"AO1:3. Two contrasts, 2 + 2. Against: most British Christians reject it - the image of God, love of neighbour, punishment should reform not injure. For: some Muslims accept it - Shari'ah prescribes fixed hudud penalties and a public penalty deters. Similar beliefs are capped at 2 marks in total.","sol":"Most Christians in Britain reject corporal punishment. They argue that every person is made in the image of God (Genesis 1:27) and that Jesus taught love of neighbour rather than retaliation, so deliberately injuring an offender treats them as less than human; British churches supported the ending of the cane in schools and argue that punishment should aim at reform. Some Muslims, by contrast, accept corporal punishment, because Shari'ah lays down fixed hudud penalties for certain named offences and they hold that a known, public penalty deters others from committing them. Many Muslim scholars add that the evidence required is extremely strict and that the penalty lapses where there is genuine need or doubt, so it belongs to a fully just society rather than to Britain.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The instruction about religious traditions means the tradition behind each belief has to be named.","Pick one tradition that treats deliberately hurting a person as ruled out, and one whose law prescribes such penalties.","The second mark in each half comes from developing the belief - what follows from it, or what qualifies it."],"codes":["3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — The treatment of criminals","3.2.1.5 — The theme's own requirement: religious teachings, contrasting perspectives, and the three prescribed issues"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting religious beliefs about whether a person who steals because they are hungry should be punished. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions. [4 marks]","a":"AO1:3. Two contrasts, 2 + 2. Punish: theft is forbidden outright - 'You shall not steal' (Exodus 20:15); scripture still requires restitution from the starving thief (Proverbs 6:30-31). Do not punish: the community failed in its duty to the poor - zakah, langar, the Sheep and the Goats. Similar beliefs are capped at 2 marks.","sol":"Many Christians and Jews hold that the thief should still be punished, because theft is forbidden outright by one of the Ten Commandments, 'You shall not steal' (Exodus 20:15), and because Proverbs, while accepting that hunger makes such a theft understandable, still requires the thief to pay back what he took (Proverbs 6:30-31); the wrong done to the victim does not disappear because the motive was need. Other believers argue that punishment is the wrong response, because the crime shows the community has failed in its own duty: Muslims pay zakah so that nobody is driven to steal, Sikhs serve langar to anyone who comes, and Christians read the Sheep and the Goats as making the feeding of the hungry a test of faith (Matthew 25:31-46). On that view the answer is to remove the need, and many Muslim jurists hold that the fixed penalty for theft cannot be applied where the hunger was genuine.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The two beliefs must genuinely oppose each other, and each needs its tradition named.","One side starts from the commandment against theft; the other starts from the duty owed to the poor.","Develop each side with what the tradition actually does about it, not just what it feels."],"codes":["3.2.1.5 Religion, crime and the causes of crime — Views about different types of crime","3.2.1.5 Religion, crime and the causes of crime — Reasons for crime"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting religious beliefs in contemporary British society about whether someone who has committed a serious crime should be forgiven. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions. [4 marks]","a":"AO1:3. Two contrasting beliefs, 2 + 2. Forgive anyway: most Christians hold that forgiveness is commanded whether or not the offender repents - the Lord's Prayer (Matthew 6:12), 'seventy-seven times' (Matthew 18:22), 'Father, forgive them' (Luke 23:34) - and it shows in prison chaplaincy and restorative justice. Only after repentance: Orthodox Judaism requires teshuvah, the offender repenting, making amends and asking the person wronged (Mishnah Yoma 8:9). Two beliefs that do not contrast are capped at 2 marks.","sol":"Most Christians in Britain believe such a person should be forgiven whatever they have done, because forgiveness is commanded rather than earned: the Lord's Prayer ties being forgiven to forgiving others (Matthew 6:12), Jesus told Peter to forgive 'seventy-seven times' (Matthew 18:22), and from the cross he prayed 'Father, forgive them' (Luke 23:34) before anyone had repented; Anglican and Catholic churches act on this by providing prison chaplains and supporting restorative justice. Orthodox Jews hold the contrasting belief that forgiveness for a wrong done to a person cannot simply be handed over: teshuvah requires the offender to repent, put right what can be put right and ask the person they harmed directly, because the Mishnah teaches that Yom Kippur does not atone for sins against another person until that person has been appeased (Mishnah Yoma 8:9).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Contrast means the two beliefs must disagree with each other, and each one needs the tradition that holds it named.","One tradition treats forgiveness as commanded whatever the offender does; another makes it depend on what the offender does first.","Develop each side with a source of authority behind it, then say what the belief means in practice."],"codes":["3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — Forgiveness","3.2.1.5 Religion, crime and the causes of crime — Views about people who break the law for these reasons"]},{"q":"Explain two religious beliefs about the aims of punishment. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"AO1:1. Two beliefs, 2 + 2, plus 1 for the source. Retribution: proportionate payment for the wrong - 'eye for eye, tooth for tooth' (Exodus 21:24), read by the Talmud as compensation. Reformation: the offender must be helped to change - Ezekiel 33:11, most churches put it first.","sol":"One belief is that punishment should give the offender what the crime deserved, no more and no less. The Torah's rule 'eye for eye, tooth for tooth' (Exodus 21:24) sets a limit as much as a penalty, and the rabbis of the Talmud read it as a rule of proportionate compensation rather than literal injury, so the punishment must fit the crime. A second belief is that punishment should reform the offender. Judaism teaches through Ezekiel that God takes 'no pleasure in the death' of the wicked but wants them to turn from their ways (Ezekiel 33:11), and the Roman Catholic Church and the Church of England both put reformation first, which is why churches support education and work in prisons.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Two beliefs only, each developed, and the source is a separate mark of its own.","The three aims named for this theme are the obvious place to look for two that can be argued from scripture.","Make the reference unmistakable: name the book and, where you can, the chapter and verse."],"codes":["3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — The aims of punishment"]},{"q":"Explain two religious reasons for opposing the death penalty. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer. [5 marks]","a":"AO1:1. Two reasons, 2 + 2, plus 1 for the source. Sanctity of life: life is God-given, humans are made in the image of God (Genesis 1:27), Surah 17:33. Reformation is made impossible, and a wrongful conviction cannot be undone. Source: Catechism of the Catholic Church 2267, revised 2018.","sol":"One reason is the sanctity of life: life is God-given and holy, and human beings are made 'in the image of God' (Genesis 1:27), so a life is not the state's to take however serious the crime. The Qur'an makes the same point when it forbids taking the life God has made sacred except by right (Surah 17:33). A second reason is that execution makes reformation impossible, and reformation is the aim most churches put first; it also makes a mistake permanent, since a wrongly convicted person cannot be released from the grave. The Catechism of the Catholic Church, revised by Pope Francis in 2018, teaches that the death penalty is inadmissible because it is an attack on the dignity of the person.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Two reasons only, each developed, with the source named separately for the fifth mark.","One reason is about what a life is; the other is about what execution prevents or cannot undo.","A named church document counts as a source of religious belief and teaching just as scripture does."],"codes":["3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — The death penalty","3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — Ethical arguments related to the death penalty"]},{"q":"'It can never be right to cause suffering.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels 1-4. Support: harm is always harm; Buddhist non-harming and the Dhammapada on hatred; Jesus on turning the other cheek; suffering brutalises those who inflict it. Against: punishment necessarily causes suffering and religions accept it - Exodus 21:24, Romans 13:4, qisas; suffering can serve protection and reform; the principle of utility. Judgement must say which chain is stronger and why.","sol":"The statement is absolute, so it stands or falls on whether a single case defeats it. In support, the clearest religious case comes from Buddhism. The First Moral Precept is to abstain from harming living beings, and the Dhammapada teaches that 'Hatred is never appeased by hatred' (Dhammapada 5), so pain inflicted in anger simply feeds the greed, hatred and ignorance that caused the crime in the first place. Christians can argue the same way: Jesus told his followers to 'turn the other cheek' (Matthew 5:39) and refused to join the condemnation of the woman brought to him, telling the crowd that the one without sin should be 'the first to throw a stone' (John 8:7). On this view causing suffering brutalises the person who inflicts it, which is one reason Britain ended judicial corporal punishment and the death penalty.\n\nA different point of view is that punishment necessarily causes suffering, and every major tradition accepts punishment. The Torah's 'eye for eye, tooth for tooth' (Exodus 21:24) limits retribution but does not abolish it, and Paul tells Christians in Romans 13 that the ruler does not carry the sword for nothing. In Islam, qisas allows a life for a life while urging the victim's family to forgo it (Surah 5:45). More importantly, the suffering is not the point of the punishment: a prison sentence causes suffering in order to protect the public and to give the offender the chance to change, just as a surgeon causes pain in order to heal. Judged by intention, causing suffering for a good end is not evil at all.\n\nThe non-religious argument runs along the same line. The principle of utility treats suffering as bad in itself, but accepts it where it prevents greater suffering, which is why many humanists support imprisonment while opposing the death penalty: the first can be shown to protect people, the second has never been shown to deter better than a life sentence.\n\nIn conclusion, the statement is too strong. The Buddhist argument is powerful against suffering inflicted out of hatred or for its own sake, and that is a real target, because retribution can easily become revenge. But it cannot survive the everyday cases: a parent, a surgeon and a court all cause suffering for the good of the person or of others. The honest position is that causing suffering is always a wrong in itself that needs justifying, and that it is right only when there is no less painful way to achieve a genuine good - which is exactly why reformative punishments should be preferred to punitive ones.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["One word in the statement makes it absolute; work out what a single counter-example would do to it.","The supporting side is strongest where suffering is inflicted out of anger; the opposing side is strongest where it is inflicted for someone's good.","Give each side a named teaching and a chain of reasoning, then let the conclusion follow from which chain held up."],"codes":["3.2.1.5 Religion, crime and the causes of crime — Good and evil intentions and actions","3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — The aims of punishment"]},{"q":"'The death penalty is never justified.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels 1-4. Support: sanctity of life (Genesis 1:27, Surah 17:33); Catechism 2267 revised 2018; reformation impossible; the innocent cannot be freed from the grave; Mishnah Makkot 1:10. Against: retribution and proportion (Exodus 21:24, Genesis 9:6, Romans 13:4); qisas in Shari'ah; deterrence and protection; the principle of utility. Judgement must weigh utility against sanctity of life.","sol":"In support of the statement, the strongest argument is the sanctity of life. Human beings are made 'in the image of God' (Genesis 1:27), so a human life has a value that does not depend on how the person has behaved, and the Qur'an forbids taking the life God has made sacred except by right (Surah 17:33). The Roman Catholic Church now teaches in the Catechism, revised by Pope Francis in 2018, that the death penalty is inadmissible because it attacks the dignity of the person, and Quakers have opposed it for centuries. Two further arguments support this. Execution destroys any chance of reformation, which the Church of England and the Catholic Church both treat as the central aim of punishment; and it makes a mistake permanent, because a wrongly convicted person cannot be released from the grave. The Mishnah already worried about this: a court that carried out an execution once in seven years was called destructive (Mishnah Makkot 1:10).\n\nA different point of view begins from retribution. The Torah lays down capital offences, Genesis 9:6 is read as requiring a life for a life, and Paul writes in Romans 13 that the ruler does not carry the sword for nothing, so some Christians, particularly in parts of the United States, argue that the worst crimes deserve the ultimate penalty and that anything less values the victim's life too cheaply. Shari'ah permits qisas for murder, so many Muslims accept that the death penalty can be lawful, although the Qur'an urges the victim's family to accept compensation or to pardon instead, and treats that as atonement for them (Surah 5:45). Supporters also argue from protection and deterrence: an executed murderer cannot kill again.\n\nThis is where the two ethical arguments the theme names meet. The principle of utility supports execution only if it really produces the greatest good, and the deterrent effect it needs has never been reliably demonstrated, and studies have not shown that execution deters murder better than a life sentence. The sanctity of life does not depend on evidence at all, which is its strength and, to a utilitarian, its weakness.\n\nIn conclusion, the statement holds. The retributive argument is serious, and the scriptural texts behind it are real, but every tradition that uses them also limits them, and Islam explicitly praises the family who does not insist. Once the deterrent claim fails, the utilitarian case collapses into revenge, while the risk of executing an innocent person remains. Since the state can protect the public by imprisonment without taking a life, execution is a suffering that is never necessary, and therefore never justified.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["The theme names two ethical arguments on this issue; an answer that uses only one of them has left half the question alone.","Both sides can quote scripture, so the argument has to be about how those texts are read and limited today.","The judgement should say what would have to be true for the other side to win, and whether it is true."],"codes":["3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — The death penalty","3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — Ethical arguments related to the death penalty"]},{"q":"'Criminals who steal because they are poor should not be punished.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels 1-4. Support: poverty is named as a cause of crime; the community's duty to the poor - zakah, langar, Matthew 25:31-46; reduced responsibility; many Muslim jurists suspend the penalty for theft in genuine need. Against: 'You shall not steal' (Exodus 20:15); the victim is harmed either way; Proverbs 6:30-31 requires restitution; deterrence and the rule of law. Judgement must distinguish excusing from mitigating.","sol":"In support of the statement, the specification itself names poverty and upbringing as reasons for crime, and religions take that seriously rather than treating every offender alike. Judaism, Christianity and Islam all teach a duty to the poor: Muslims pay zakah as one of the pillars of their faith, Sikhs serve langar to anyone who comes, and Jesus makes feeding the hungry the test by which people are judged in the parable of the Sheep and the Goats (Matthew 25:31-46). If a society has let a person go hungry, the theft is partly its own failure, and punishing the hungry person alone lets that society off. Islamic law reflects this: many Muslim jurists hold that the fixed penalty for theft does not apply where the need was genuine or the evidence doubtful. There is also the argument from responsibility - a person choosing between stealing and starving is not choosing freely in the way a greedy thief is, and punishment is supposed to be deserved.\n\nA different point of view starts from the victim. One of the Ten Commandments says plainly 'You shall not steal' (Exodus 20:15), and the person stolen from suffers the loss whatever the thief's reasons; they may be poor themselves. Proverbs notices exactly this case: hunger may make the theft understandable, but the thief is still told to pay back what he took (Proverbs 6:30-31). The distinction that does the work here is between the crime and the criminal - the Christian tradition speaks of hating the sin and loving the sinner, which allows compassion for the thief without pretending the theft was acceptable. If need excused theft altogether, the law would protect nobody, and the deterrent effect on those who are merely claiming need would disappear.\n\nNon-religious voices divide in the same way. Many humanists argue from consequences that prosecuting the desperate achieves nothing and that food banks and benefits prevent far more theft than sentences do, while others insist that the rule of law is what protects the weakest of all.\n\nIn conclusion, the statement goes too far, but it is pointing at something true. Poverty does not make theft right, because the victim's loss is real and restitution is still owed - which is what Proverbs says. What poverty does is change what the punishment should be and how severe it should be: a discharge, repayment or community service rather than prison, together with action on the cause. So the just answer is not that such criminals should not be punished, but that they should be punished as people whose circumstances are part of the case, and that a society which ignores the cause has no right to be severe about the effect.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first whether the statement is asking for no punishment at all, or for a different punishment.","The strongest supporting material is the religious duty to the poor; the strongest opposing material is the victim.","A conclusion that separates excusing a crime from taking the cause into account will be better than one that simply picks a side."],"codes":["3.2.1.5 Religion, crime and the causes of crime — Reasons for crime","3.2.1.5 Religion, crime and the causes of crime — Views about people who break the law for these reasons"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which three aims of punishment does this theme name?","a":"Retribution, deterrence and reformation."},{"q":"Which two ethical arguments does the theme name on the death penalty?","a":"The principle of utility and the sanctity of life."},{"q":"In Judaism, what must an offender do before Yom Kippur atones for a sin against another person?","a":"Make teshuvah: repent, put the wrong right and ask the wronged person directly, because Yom Kippur does not atone until they are appeased (Mishnah Yoma 8:9)."},{"q":"What does the Catechism of the Catholic Church, revised in 2018, teach about the death penalty?","a":"That it is inadmissible, because it is an attack on the dignity of the person."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Retribution","back":"The aim of punishment that makes the offender pay back what the crime deserved. The Torah's 'eye for eye, tooth for tooth' (Exodus 21:24) is its classic form, read by the Talmud as proportionate compensation.","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — The aims of punishment","open":false},{"front":"Deterrence","back":"The aim of punishment that puts people off crime, either the offender or everyone who hears of the sentence. It is the aim the principle of utility depends on when the death penalty is defended.","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — The aims of punishment","open":false},{"front":"Reformation","back":"The aim of punishment that changes the offender so they do not offend again. Most British churches put it first, and it is the aim execution makes impossible.","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — The aims of punishment","open":false},{"front":"Corporal punishment","back":"Punishment by physical pain. One of the three prescribed contrasting issues. Not a court sentence in Britain since the Criminal Justice Act 1948, and ended in state schools by the Education (No. 2) Act 1986.","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — The treatment of criminals","open":false},{"front":"Community service","back":"Unpaid work done for the community instead of prison. It aims at reformation and reparation, which suits the Jewish demand for restitution; critics call it a soft option that does not punish enough.","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — The treatment of criminals","open":false},{"front":"Sanctity of life","back":"The belief that life is holy and God-given, so it is not ours to take. Grounded in human beings being made 'in the image of God' (Genesis 1:27) and in Surah 17:33.","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — Ethical arguments related to the death penalty","open":false},{"front":"The principle of utility","back":"The ethical argument, associated with Jeremy Bentham, that an action is right if it produces the greatest good or least suffering overall. It supports execution only if execution really deters.","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — Ethical arguments related to the death penalty","open":false},{"front":"Qisas and diyah","back":"In Shari'ah, qisas is like-for-like retaliation for murder or injury; diyah is the compensation the victim's family may accept instead. The Qur'an treats giving up retaliation as atonement for them (Surah 5:45).","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — The death penalty","open":false},{"front":"The Catechism of the Catholic Church on the death penalty","back":"Revised by Pope Francis in 2018 to teach that the death penalty is inadmissible because it is an attack on the dignity of the person, and that the Church works for its abolition worldwide.","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — The death penalty","open":false},{"front":"Teshuvah","back":"The Jewish turning back from sin: repent, put the wrong right and ask the wronged person directly. The Mishnah teaches that Yom Kippur does not atone for sins against another person until they are appeased (Yoma 8:9).","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion and punishment — Forgiveness","open":false},{"front":"Hate crime","back":"A crime committed because of hostility to the victim's race, religion, disability, sexual orientation or gender identity. Religious teaching on equality bears on it directly - 'love your neighbour as yourself' (Leviticus 19:18).","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion, crime and the causes of crime — Views about different types of crime","open":false},{"front":"The reasons for crime named in this theme","back":"Four groups: poverty and upbringing; mental illness and addiction; greed and hate; opposition to an unjust law. They are morally different from one another, which is why views about the offender differ too.","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion, crime and the causes of crime — Reasons for crime","open":false},{"front":"The three prescribed contrasting issues of Theme E","back":"Corporal punishment, the death penalty and forgiveness. Contrasting beliefs on each must be explainable with reference to Christianity, the main religious tradition in Britain, and one or more other religious traditions.","code":"3.2.1.5 — The theme's own requirement: religious teachings, contrasting perspectives, and the three prescribed issues","open":false},{"front":"Hating the sin but loving the sinner","back":"The Christian distinction between the crime and the criminal: the wrong is still wrong, but the offender is still made in God's image. Jesus told the crowd that the sinless should throw 'the first stone' (John 8:7).","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion, crime and the causes of crime — Views about people who break the law for these reasons","open":false},{"front":"Intention and consequence in judging an action","back":"Whether an act is judged by the intention behind it or by what it produces decides whether causing suffering can be good. Buddhism makes intention central: 'Hatred is never appeased by hatred' (Dhammapada 5).","code":"3.2.1.5 Religion, crime and the causes of crime — Good and evil intentions and actions","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Evaluate: 'Criminals should always be forgiven.')","items":["The paragraph: Those who support the statement argue that forgiveness is a duty with no cut-off point. In the Lord's Prayer Christians ask to be forgiven as they forgive others (Matthew 6:12), and when Peter asked how often he should forgive, Jesus answered not seven times but 'seventy-seven times' (Matthew 18:22); from the cross he prayed 'Father, forgive them' (Luke 23:34) for the men executing him, which is as hard a case as any. Islam makes the same demand from the other direction, calling God al-Ghafur, the Most Forgiving, and treating a victim's family who give up their right to retaliate as having earned atonement for themselves (Surah 5:45). The strongest objection is that forgiveness on these terms is cheap. Judaism requires teshuvah first - the offender must repent, make amends and ask the wronged person directly, because the Mishnah teaches that Yom Kippur does not atone for a sin against another person until that person is appeased (Mishnah Yoma 8:9) - and only the victim has the standing to forgive at all, so a bystander who forgives a murderer on the family's behalf has forgiven nothing. On balance the Christian argument survives this, but only once forgiving is separated from releasing: a victim may forgive the person while the court still imposes the sentence that protects everyone else, and Britain's restorative justice schemes show the two working together. The statement is therefore right about the offender's standing as a person and wrong if it is taken to mean that the sentence should be dropped.","Why this is Level 4: it gives reasoned consideration of different points of view, with a chain of reasoning on each side rather than a list of opinions; references to religion are applied to the issue (the Lord's Prayer, Jesus' answer to Peter, the Qur'an on giving up retaliation, the Mishnah on Yom Kippur) instead of being mentioned in passing; and it reaches a judgement supported by that knowledge, distinguishing forgiving the person from cancelling the punishment. That matches 'A well-argued response, reasoned consideration of different points of view. Logical chains of reasoning leading to judgement(s) supported by knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information. Reference to religion applied to the issue.'"]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same statement, visibly weaker)","items":["The paragraph: I think criminals should be forgiven because everyone makes mistakes and holding a grudge only makes the victim feel worse. If you forgive someone they might change and not do it again, so it helps everybody. On the other hand some religious people would say that you cannot just forgive a murderer because that is not fair on the family, and the criminal should still go to prison. So there are two sides, but overall I think forgiveness is best.","Why this is only Level 2: it does show 'Recognition of different points of view, each supported by relevant reasons/evidence', which is the Level 2 descriptor, and the reasons given are real ones. What it misses is what Level 3 requires - 'Logical chains of reasoning that draw on knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information' and 'Clear reference to religion'. No tradition, text or teaching is named; 'some religious people' is not a reference to religion applied to the issue, and 'everyone makes mistakes' is an assertion rather than a chain of reasoning. The last sentence announces a preference instead of justifying one, so it is not the judgement Level 4 asks for. To move up it would need the named teaching of particular traditions (Jesus' answer to Peter, the Jewish requirement of teshuvah), the reason each gives, and a conclusion that follows from those reasons."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Christian teaching on crime and punishment","items":["Sanctity of life: human beings are made 'in the image of God' (Genesis 1:27), the basis of the argument that no life may be taken and no offender may be treated as worthless.","Murder: 'You shall not murder' (Exodus 20:13), one of the Ten Commandments, shared with Judaism.","Theft: 'You shall not steal' (Exodus 20:15), one of the Ten Commandments; Proverbs 6:30-31 recognises the starving thief yet still requires restitution.","Retribution: 'eye for eye, tooth for tooth' (Exodus 21:24) - a limit on revenge as much as a penalty; Jesus quotes it and answers it with 'turn the other cheek' (Matthew 5:39).","The state's authority to punish: Romans 13:1-7 - the governing authorities are established by God and the ruler does not carry the sword for nothing; the text supporters of capital punishment use.","Forgiveness: the Lord's Prayer ties being forgiven to forgiving (Matthew 6:12); Jesus tells Peter to forgive 'seventy-seven times' (Matthew 18:22); from the cross he prays 'Father, forgive them' (Luke 23:34).","The offender is still a person: Jesus tells the crowd that the one without sin should be 'the first to throw a stone' (John 8:7), and promises paradise to the criminal crucified beside him (Luke 23:43).","Prison ministry: 'I was in prison' and you came to visit me (Matthew 25:36), from the parable of the Sheep and the Goats (Matthew 25:31-46) - the basis of Christian prison chaplaincy and of the duty to the poor.","Corporal punishment: the English saying about sparing the rod is a proverb based on Proverbs 13:24, used by a minority; most British churches reject corporal punishment and supported ending the cane in schools.","Roman Catholic teaching on the death penalty: the Catechism, revised by Pope Francis in 2018, teaches that it is inadmissible because it attacks the dignity of the person, and commits the Church to working for abolition worldwide.","Pope John Paul II, Evangelium Vitae (1995): cases in which execution is an absolute necessity are very rare, if not practically non-existent - the step before the 2018 revision.","Quakers (the Religious Society of Friends): long-standing opposition to the death penalty and to corporal punishment, from the belief that there is that of God in everyone; Elizabeth Fry (1780-1845), a Quaker, campaigned for prison reform.","Denominational care: Christians disagree about capital punishment - the Church of England, the Roman Catholic Church and the Methodist Church in Britain oppose it, while some evangelical Protestants, particularly in parts of the United States, support it from Genesis 9:6 and Romans 13:4."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Muslim teaching on crime and punishment","items":["Sanctity of life: the Qur'an forbids taking the life God has made sacred except by right (Surah 17:33).","The value of one life: killing one person unjustly is likened to killing all people, and saving one to saving all (Surah 5:32).","Justice as a command: God commands justice and forbids wrongdoing (Surah 16:90).","Retribution and mercy together: Surah 5:45 states life for life, eye for eye, and adds that a victim who gives up this right in charity has it counted as atonement for them.","Qisas: like-for-like retaliation for murder or injury, available to the victim's family through the court.","Diyah: the compensation the victim's family may accept instead of qisas; they may also simply pardon the offender, which the Qur'an praises.","Hudud: the fixed penalties laid down in the Qur'an and Sunnah for certain named offences, including theft; the evidence required is extremely strict, and many jurists hold the penalty lapses where there is genuine need or any doubt.","Ta'zir: discretionary punishments set by a judge for offences without a fixed penalty - the category into which most modern sentencing falls.","Prevention of crime: zakah, one of the Five Pillars, is a compulsory annual payment for the poor, so that nobody is driven to steal; alcohol and other intoxicants are forbidden because they lead to crime.","God's own character: among the names of God are al-Ghafur, the Most Forgiving, and ar-Rahim, the Most Merciful; a Muslim who forgives imitates God.","Sunni and Shi'a: both accept qisas, diyah and hudud in principle; Muslims in Britain differ widely over whether hudud penalties can apply at all outside a fully just Islamic society, and British Muslims are subject to British law.","Free will: human beings were given free will and are tempted by Iblis, so crime is a misuse of a God-given freedom rather than something forced on the offender."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Jewish, Buddhist and Sikh teaching used on this theme","items":["Judaism - retribution limited: 'eye for eye, tooth for tooth' (Exodus 21:24); the rabbis of the Talmud read it as proportionate monetary compensation, not literal injury.","Judaism - no revenge: 'love your neighbour as yourself' (Leviticus 19:18), which in the same verse forbids taking revenge or bearing a grudge.","Judaism - reform rather than death: God takes 'no pleasure in the death' of the wicked but wants them to turn from their ways (Ezekiel 33:11).","Judaism - capital punishment in practice: the Mishnah records that a court which carried out an execution once in seven years was called destructive, and that some rabbis said they would never have imposed it (Mishnah Makkot 1:10).","Judaism - forgiveness has conditions: teshuvah requires repentance, making amends and asking the wronged person directly, because Yom Kippur does not atone for sins against another person until they are appeased (Mishnah Yoma 8:9).","Judaism - flogging limited: the Torah caps it at forty lashes (Deuteronomy 25:3), and rabbinic law reduced it to thirty-nine to be certain the limit was never passed.","Buddhism - non-harming: the First Moral Precept is to abstain from taking life or harming living beings; the Fifth is to abstain from intoxicants, which are linked to crime.","Buddhism - hatred is self-defeating: 'Hatred is never appeased by hatred' (Dhammapada 5), so punishment motivated by revenge is unskilful.","Buddhism - intention and kamma: an action is judged by the intention behind it, and harmful actions bring their own consequences, so retribution is unnecessary while protection and reform are not.","Buddhism - the death penalty: the fourteenth Dalai Lama, Tenzin Gyatso, has publicly called for its worldwide abolition.","Sikhism - the causes of crime: greed (lobh) and anger (krodh) are among the five evils, and haumai, self-centredness, lies behind law-breaking; langar feeds anyone who comes, so that need does not drive people to steal.","Sikhism - punishment: most Sikhs emphasise reforming the offender and treating them with compassion, while accepting that society must be protected."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: non-religious views and the law in Britain","items":["Atheist and humanist starting point: there is no God and no soul, so morality is worked out from human welfare and the consequences of actions rather than from revelation.","The principle of utility: associated with Jeremy Bentham (1748-1832), it judges an action by whether it produces the greatest happiness or the least suffering overall - the argument the specification names on the death penalty.","Utility applied to execution: it supports the death penalty only if execution deters better than a life sentence; the evidence for that has never been established, and studies have not shown that it deters murder better than a life sentence.","The humanist argument against the death penalty: it is irreversible, so a wrongful conviction cannot be put right, and it treats the offender as beyond change; Humanists UK opposes capital punishment.","Non-religious support for punishment: humanists generally support imprisonment and community sentences because they protect the public and can reform the offender, and support tackling poverty, addiction and poor education as causes of crime.","The death penalty in Britain: abolished for murder by the Murder (Abolition of Death Penalty) Act 1965, made permanent in 1969; the last executions in Britain were in 1964; abolished for all remaining offences by 1998; the UK is bound by Protocol 13 of the European Convention on Human Rights, ratified in 2003.","Corporal punishment in Britain: removed as a court sentence by the Criminal Justice Act 1948; ended in state schools by the Education (No. 2) Act 1986; banned in all schools in England and Wales by 1999, and physical punishment of children by parents is now a criminal offence in Scotland (2020) and Wales (2022).","The aims of sentencing in English law: punishment of offenders, reduction of crime including by deterrence, reform and rehabilitation, protection of the public, and reparation to those affected - close to the aims religions argue about.","Contrasting perspectives in contemporary British society: opinion polls have regularly shown a substantial minority of the British public in favour of restoring the death penalty for murder, while Parliament, the courts and the main churches oppose it - the contrast the specification expects you to know.","Amnesty International opposes the death penalty in all cases, without exception, as a violation of the right to life."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'Prison is the best way to treat criminals.' Evaluate this statement. [12 marks] [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","items":["Decode the statement: 'best' means best at achieving the aims of punishment, so the question is really which aims matter most - retribution, deterrence or reformation - and whether prison serves them better than corporal punishment or community service. 'Criminals' is left general, which is the weakness to exploit: what is best for a violent offender is not what is best for a first-time shoplifter.","Timing: about 12 minutes for the 12 marks, plus a minute at the end to read it back for spelling, punctuation and the specialist terms that the 3 SPaG marks reward - retribution, deterrence, reformation, sanctity of life, qisas, teshuvah.","Do not open with definitions. Open with one sentence naming the test you will apply: a punishment is best if it serves the aims of punishment better than the alternatives.","Paragraph 1, in support: prison protects the public, which no other treatment does, and it deters. Source: Romans 13:1-7, where the ruler carries the sword as God's servant, so Christians can defend a state that locks dangerous people up. Islam adds that protecting the public comes first and that community service does not deter.","Paragraph 2, in support: prison can reform, and religions use it. Christian chaplaincy takes its mandate from 'I was in prison' and you came to me (Matthew 25:36); prisons run education, drug treatment and work schemes, which are exactly what reformation means.","Paragraph 3, a different point of view: prison often fails on its own terms. Reoffending after short sentences is high, prisoners are separated from their families, and prison can teach crime rather than cure it. Judaism's insistence on restitution and Christianity's on making amends are better served by community service, where the offender repays the community directly; the Catholic and Anglican churches both put reformation first.","Paragraph 4, a different point of view: it depends on the offender. Buddhism judges by intention and asks what will actually change the person; poverty, addiction and mental illness are named causes of crime, and prison treats none of them - treatment, restitution and support address the cause, while prison addresses only the symptom.","The non-religious thread the bullets allow: on the principle of utility, prison is justified where it prevents more suffering than it causes, which is clear for dangerous offenders and doubtful for short sentences for petty crime; humanists point to the cost and to the evidence on reoffending.","Evaluation thread running through: keep returning to the three aims. Prison wins on protection and, for serious crime, on retribution; community service wins on reparation; neither automatically wins on reformation, which depends on what is done inside.","What the conclusion must say: not that both sides have a point. It must name which aims it is weighing and give the verdict - for example that prison is best only where the public needs protecting or the crime demands a proportionate penalty, and that for most non-violent offenders community service serves reformation and reparation better, which is what most British churches argue. The conclusion must follow from the arguments already made, with no new material."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T14:13:25.818365Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"All four declared fixes landed and a field-by-field diff against the version I judged shows those edits and nothing else. questions[0] now reads A Deterrence B Reparation C Reformation D Retribution - reparation is a genuine aim of punishment and not a second correct answer, so the key C Reformation stays sound, and sol, hints[1] and the worked example were updated with it. questions[3].hints[0] no longer contradicts its own key. questions[8] is now a real Component 2 AO1:3 contrast whose two halves genuinely oppose each other and are attributed - most Christians, forgiveness commanded before repentance (Matthew 6:12, 18:22, Luke 23:34, all verified), against Orthodox Judaism's teshuvah (Mishnah Yoma 8:9, verified) - which also satisfies the specification's requirement that contrasting beliefs on forgiveness be explained with Christianity plus another tradition. The Proverbs 6:30-31 paraphrases in questions[7].sol and questions[13].sol no longer track the NIV and keep the reference and the sense (restitution is still owed). Re-reading the whole kit: all 27 scripture references, Catechism 2267 (2018) and Evangelium Vitae 56 (1995) remain correct and correctly attributed, no quotation exceeds seven words, all 14 tariffs are 1, 2, 4, 5 or 12, the three 12-mark stems carry the Component 2 bullets with SPaG only there, no June 2023 or specimen Theme E question is reproduced, the Level 4 and Level 2 models are correctly graded against the 2A/2B grid, and the four fact files and every statement of British law hold. Only wording residue is left; publishable as it stands."}}},"3.2.1.6":{"id":"AQA-8062","topic":"3.2.1.6","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Theme F is one of the four themes you answer in Component 2, and it is worth one whole five-part question of 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks, with the 12-mark evaluation carrying the spelling, punctuation and grammar marks as well. It is the theme students most often answer out of their own sense of fairness, and that is exactly how the marks are lost: the examiner rewards named teaching from named traditions, named forms of exploitation and named ethical arguments, not opinions about equality. This room gives you Christianity as the main religious tradition of Great Britain, other traditions to contrast it with, the non-religious views the paper invites, the law of this country where the theme meets it, and the three issues the specification says you must be able to argue on both sides: the status of women in religion, the uses of wealth, and freedom of religious expression.","idea":[{"h":"The rubric that governs every answer on this theme","code":"3.2.1.6 — The theme's own requirement: religious teachings, contrasting perspectives, and the three prescribed issues","t":"The specification asks for three things at once: religious teachings, the religious, philosophical and ethical arguments around them, and contrasting perspectives in contemporary British society. Three issues are named as ones you must be able to contrast: the status of women in religion, the uses of wealth, and freedom of religious expression, and the contrast must use the main religious tradition in Britain (Christianity) plus one or more other religious traditions. You must also be aware of non-religious beliefs such as atheism and humanism, and cite sources of wisdom and authority - the Bible, the Catechism of the Catholic Church, the Qur'an and Hadith, the Torah and Talmud, the Vedas and Upanishads, the Pali Canon, the Guru Granth Sahib. A top-level answer therefore never writes 'religious people think'; it names the tradition, names the group inside that tradition, and says where the teaching comes from."},{"h":"Prejudice and discrimination inside religion: women and homosexuals","code":"3.2.1.6 Human rights — Prejudice and discrimination in religion and belief","t":"Prejudice is the attitude - judging someone before you know them; discrimination is the act that follows, treating them unfairly because of it. The specification points the question inwards, at the status and treatment within religion of women and of homosexuals, so the sharpest contrasts are often inside one religion rather than between two. The Church of England has ordained women as priests since 1994 and consecrated its first woman bishop in 2015, while the Roman Catholic Church ordains only men, holding that the priest acts in the person of Christ, who chose twelve men as apostles. Reform and Liberal Judaism ordain women as rabbis; Orthodox Judaism does not, describing the roles as separate but equal. On homosexuality the Catechism of the Catholic Church calls homosexual acts wrong while insisting that homosexual people be treated with respect, compassion and sensitivity; the Religious Society of Friends (Quakers), and Liberal and Reform Judaism, marry same-sex couples. A top-level answer sets two of these named positions against each other and explains the reasoning each gives, rather than reporting that religions disagree."},{"h":"Equality, freedom of religion and freedom of religious expression","code":"3.2.1.6 Human rights — Issues of equality, freedom of religion and belief","t":"Freedom of religion is the right to hold a belief; freedom of religious expression is the right to act on it in public - to wear religious dress and symbols, to speak about your faith, to worship openly. It is the third of the prescribed contrasting issues and the most live in British society. The Equality Act 2010 makes religion or belief one of nine protected characteristics, and Article 9 of the European Convention, in British law through the Human Rights Act 1998, protects freedom of thought, conscience and religion - but it is a qualified right, limited where public safety or the rights of others require it. Named cases help: in Mandla v Dowell Lee (1983) the House of Lords held that a school could not refuse a Sikh boy who wore a turban, and in Eweida and Others v United Kingdom (2013) the European Court of Human Rights held that the United Kingdom had violated Article 9 by failing to protect Nadia Eweida's right to wear a small cross at work - the case was brought against the United Kingdom, not against British Airways. A top-level answer uses the qualification: it argues where the freedom should end, not only that it exists."},{"h":"Rights bring responsibilities","code":"3.2.1.6 Human rights — Human rights and the responsibilities that come with rights","t":"The Universal Declaration of Human Rights (1948) opens by saying all human beings are born free and equal in dignity and rights, and the specification pairs those rights deliberately with the responsibility to respect the rights of others. Religious traditions more often speak of duties than of rights: Jews and Christians ground human dignity in the teaching that people are made in the image of God (Genesis 1:27), Muslims in the Qur'an's teaching that humanity was made into nations and tribes 'that you may know one another' (Surah 49:13), and all of them attach obligations to that dignity - to the poor, the worker, the stranger. That gives you two arguments to use: religious teaching supports human rights by giving every person equal worth, and it also frames them differently, as duties owed rather than claims made. A top-level answer shows that a right claimed against others is also a right owed to them, and uses that to test statements containing the word 'anything' or 'wherever'."},{"h":"Social justice: the bridge between rights and wealth","code":"3.2.1.6 Human rights — Social justice","t":"Social justice is fairness in how a society shares out wealth, opportunity and power, and every tradition studied makes it a religious duty rather than a political preference. Amos demands 'let justice roll down like waters' (Amos 5:24) and Deuteronomy 16:20 commands 'Justice, justice shall you pursue'; Jewish teaching names tzedek (justice) and tikkun olam, the repairing of the world. Catholic social teaching, running from Rerum Novarum (Pope Leo XIII, 1891) to the Catechism, sets out human dignity, the common good and the preferential option for the poor. Islam builds it into the Five Pillars through zakah; Sikhs practise sewa (selfless service) and langar, the free kitchen Guru Nanak began where everyone eats together regardless of rank. A top-level answer uses social justice to distinguish charity from justice: charity relieves the symptom, justice changes what caused it, and the strongest religious arguments demand both."},{"h":"Racial prejudice and discrimination","code":"3.2.1.6 Human rights — Racial prejudice and discrimination","t":"Every tradition on this specification teaches the equal worth of all people. Genesis 1:27 says humanity is made 'in the image of God'; Paul writes that in Christ there is 'neither Jew nor Greek' (Galatians 3:28); Peter concludes that God shows no favouritism (Acts 10:34); Jesus makes a Samaritan, a foreigner despised by his hearers, the hero of the parable (Luke 10:25-37). The Prophet Muhammad taught in his Final Sermon that no Arab is superior to a non-Arab and no white person to a black person, and the Qur'an says humanity was made into nations and tribes so that people would know one another (Surah 49:13). Sikh langar was begun in part to break the caste distinctions of the time. A top-level answer also admits the failure: religious communities have practised racism - segregated congregations, caste inside communities - which is a breach of their own teaching, and saying so is what turns knowledge into evaluation."},{"h":"Positive discrimination, equality and justice","code":"3.2.1.6 Human rights — Ethical arguments related to racial discrimination","t":"The specification names positive discrimination as the ethical argument to know, and it is the interesting case because equality seems to forbid it while justice seems to require it. Treating people identically looks fair, but if one group starts further back, identical treatment preserves the gap; treating them differently to close the gap is itself treating people by race. British law draws the line precisely: under the Equality Act 2010 positive discrimination - appointing someone because of their race - is unlawful, while positive action, such as encouraging applications from under-represented groups or offering training, is allowed, and an employer may prefer an equally qualified candidate from an under-represented group. Religious arguments run both ways: the preferential option for the poor in Catholic social teaching shows that treating everyone the same is not always just, while 'neither Jew nor Greek' (Galatians 3:28) tells against sorting people by race at all. A top-level answer separates the two terms before judging, because most of the disagreement lives in the confusion between them."},{"h":"The right attitude to wealth and the uses of wealth","code":"3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — Wealth","t":"The uses of wealth is the second prescribed contrasting issue, and the specification's wording is careful: the question is the attitude to wealth and what is done with it, not simply whether wealth is allowed. Christianity warns that 'the love of money' is a root of evil (1 Timothy 6:10) and that it is hard for a rich man to enter the kingdom, easier for a camel to go through 'the eye of a needle' (Mark 10:25) - a warning about attachment, not a ban on property. Islam treats lawfully earned wealth as a trust from God to be purified by zakah. Hinduism lists artha, material prosperity, as one of the four aims of life alongside dharma, kama and moksha, so wealth pursued within dharma is a proper goal. Buddhism warns that greed is one of the three poisons and asks for right livelihood, the fifth step of the Eightfold Path. A top-level answer therefore contrasts traditions that suspect wealth with traditions that accept it, and shows both agreeing about its use."},{"h":"The responsibilities of wealth, including the causes of poverty","code":"3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — The responsibilities of wealth","t":"The specification asks for the duty to tackle poverty and its causes, and those last three words carry the marks. Zakah is a pillar of Islam, normally 2.5 per cent of surplus wealth each year, a due of the poor rather than a favour to them, with voluntary sadaqah on top; Shi'a Muslims also pay khums. Jewish tzedakah comes from the word for righteousness, so giving is justice, not generosity, and the Torah leaves the corners of the field unharvested for the poor (Leviticus 19:9-10). Christians point to the sheep and the goats, where service to 'the least of these' is service to Christ himself (Matthew 25:40), and to the first Christians who held everything in common (Acts 4:32). Catholic social teaching goes further than almsgiving, arguing that unjust structures - wages, debt, trade - must be changed. A top-level answer pairs one act of relief with one change of cause, because that is what 'and its causes' asks for."},{"h":"Exploitation of the poor: pay, interest and trafficking","code":"3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — Exploitation of the poor","t":"Three forms are named and each has its own teaching. Fair pay: the Torah forbids keeping back a hired worker's wages overnight (Leviticus 19:13), and James condemns the rich who withheld the wages of their labourers (James 5:4); in Britain there is a legal minimum wage and a higher voluntary real Living Wage that many churches have adopted. Excessive interest: Exodus 22:25 forbids charging interest to the poor, the Qur'an says God 'permitted trade and forbidden usury' (Surah 2:275), so Islamic banks share risk instead of charging riba, and in 2013 the Archbishop of Canterbury backed credit unions against payday lenders, whose charges the Financial Conduct Authority capped from 2015. People-trafficking: the buying and selling of human beings is condemned by the Catechism as an offence against human dignity, is one of the trades ruled out by Buddhist right livelihood, and is a crime under the Modern Slavery Act 2015. A top-level answer names the form, the teaching and the remedy."},{"h":"The responsibilities of those living in poverty","code":"3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — The responsibilities of those living in poverty","t":"The specification deliberately asks the question from the other side: what those living in poverty may be expected to do to help themselves overcome the difficulties they face. There is religious support for the expectation. Sikh teaching makes kirat karni, earning an honest living, a duty alongside vand chhakna, sharing what you earn; Paul writes that those unwilling to work should not eat (2 Thessalonians 3:10); Maimonides ranks the levels of tzedakah and puts highest of all the gift, loan, partnership or job that ends a person's need for charity. But the limits matter just as much: the same traditions insist that poverty is usually not the fault of the poor, that Deuteronomy 15:11 commands an open hand because the poor will always be there, and that famine, disability, debt and low pay are not cured by effort. A top-level answer uses this idea to avoid a one-sided essay: responsibility without opportunity is blame."},{"h":"Charity, and the arguments about giving","code":"3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — Charity","t":"The duty to give is not in doubt - zakah, sadaqah, tzedakah, Christian almsgiving and tithing, Hindu daan, Buddhist dana - but the specification asks for the issues related to giving money to the poor, and that is where the evaluation lives. Does money handed over relieve need or create dependence? Should it go to individuals or to agencies such as CAFOD (founded 1962), Christian Aid, whose Christian Aid Week has been held since 1957, and Tearfund (founded 1968), which fund long-term development as well as emergency relief? Does charity let governments and companies off the hook for the causes of poverty, and does a food bank, like those in the Trussell Trust network, meet a need or normalise it? Humanists give too, arguing from empathy and consequences rather than from scripture, and often press hardest on effectiveness. A top-level answer never argues whether to give; it argues how, to whom, and alongside what."}],"examples":[{"title":"The one-mark multiple choice: eliminate, then tick","setup":"The question reads: Which one of the following is the yearly Muslim duty to give a share of surplus wealth to the poor? A Khums B Sadaqah C Sawm D Zakah. You put a tick in the box next to the correct answer, and there is a single mark for it.","steps":["Pin down the three things the question insists on before looking at the options: a duty (not a choice), paid yearly, out of wealth.","Clear the option that is not about money at all - sawm is the fast of Ramadan, so it cannot be a share of wealth.","Clear the option that is voluntary - sadaqah is giving over and above what is owed, so it is not a duty.","Of the two obligatory payments left, khums is the fifth paid by Shi'a Muslims, while the pillar every Muslim owes each year is zakah, so tick D and move on inside a minute."],"cues":["What three things must the right answer be?","Which option is not about wealth at all?","Which option is given freely rather than owed?","Which of the two remaining payments is one of the Five Pillars?"]},{"title":"Building the four-mark contrast: the status of women in religion","setup":"The question reads: Explain two contrasting religious beliefs about the status of women in religion. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions. Four marks, marked two plus two, and the mark scheme warns that if two similar beliefs are given only one of them can be credited.","steps":["Choose two positions that genuinely clash, and remember the rule for the three prescribed issues - the status of women in religion, the uses of wealth and freedom of religious expression - that one belief must be Christian and the other from a different religious tradition: the Church of England ordains women, Orthodox Judaism does not.","Write the first belief and earn its second mark with a reason and a fact: the Church of England has ordained women as priests since 1994 and consecrated its first woman bishop in 2015, arguing from Galatians 3:28 that in Christ there is 'neither male nor female'.","Open the second with a word that sets it against the first - 'whereas in Orthodox Judaism' - then give that position and its reason: women are not ordained as rabbis and ten men are needed for a minyan, because Orthodox teaching gives men and women different obligations, which it describes as separate but equal.","Check the instruction has been obeyed: both traditions are named, each belief has a reason attached, and neither paragraph has drifted into the status of women in society, which is a different question."],"cues":["Where will you find two beliefs that really do clash?","What turns a simple explanation into a detailed one here?","Which word will make the contrast visible to the examiner?","What must be named for the Component 2 instruction to be met?"]},{"title":"The five-mark question: two teachings and the mark for the source","setup":"The question reads: Explain two religious teachings about racial equality. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer. Five marks: two for each teaching explained in detail, and a separate mark for a relevant and accurate reference to a source.","steps":["Take the first teaching from Christianity and develop it: all people are made in the image of God (Genesis 1:27), so worth is given by the Creator and cannot be increased or reduced by skin colour.","Take the second from another tradition so the answer is not one religion twice: the Qur'an teaches that God made humanity into nations and tribes 'that you may know one another' (Surah 49:13), so difference exists for acquaintance, not ranking.","Make the source mark unmissable by naming the text and where it is found rather than hinting at it; the two references already written earn it, and the Prophet's Final Sermon, teaching that no Arab is superior to a non-Arab, secures it as a third source supporting the second teaching, not as a third teaching.","Stop at two teachings: only the first two are marked, so a third teaching costs time and earns nothing, even though extra sources for those two teachings are welcome because the fifth mark rewards the reference."],"cues":["Which teaching gives every person the same worth, and why?","Why should the second teaching come from a different tradition?","What exactly does the fifth mark reward?","Why is a third teaching a mistake here?"]},{"title":"The four-mark influence question: what believers actually do","setup":"The question reads: Explain two ways in which belief in social justice influences religious believers today. Four marks, two plus two, and the mark scheme says that for the second mark the influence of the way must be included - a belief stated without its effect scores one.","steps":["Write the first way as belief then consequence: because tzedakah means righteous giving rather than generosity, many Jews set aside a fixed share of income and support agencies working for fairer conditions, not only emergency aid.","Say what changes as a result, which is the part that earns the second mark: giving becomes a budgeted duty rather than an occasional impulse, so it continues when the giver stops feeling moved.","Write the second way from another tradition: Catholic social teaching's preferential option for the poor leads Catholics to campaign on debt, trade and wages through agencies like CAFOD.","Finish that way with its visible effect too - parish groups lobbying members of Parliament, and schools running fundraising and campaigning together - so both halves of the answer show influence rather than belief."],"cues":["How do you begin a way so that the influence is built in?","What is the examiner waiting to see before awarding the second mark?","Where will the second way come from, and why not the same tradition?","What would leave this answer on one mark instead of two?"]},{"title":"Turning a twelve-mark statement into two chains and a judgement","setup":"The question reads: 'Positive discrimination is the fairest way to correct racial inequality.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. There are also three marks for spelling, punctuation and grammar.","steps":["Decode the wording before writing: 'fairest' asks you to compare remedies, so the essay must weigh positive discrimination against other ways of correcting inequality, not simply approve or condemn it.","Build the supporting chain: justice means repairing unfairness, so where one group starts further back identical treatment freezes the gap, and Catholic social teaching's preferential option for the poor shows that treating everyone the same is not always just.","Build the opposing chain with its own religious backing: choosing by race is still choosing by race, which 'neither Jew nor Greek' (Galatians 3:28) and the Prophet's Final Sermon both reject, and in Britain such appointments are unlawful under the Equality Act.","Add the distinction the argument turns on: positive action - outreach, training, preferring an equally qualified candidate from an under-represented group - is lawful and does the work supporters want without choosing by race alone.","Write the judgement from that distinction rather than from preference: the aim is right and the named method is not the fairest available, so positive action carries the argument, and that conclusion follows from the reasons already given."],"cues":["Which word in the statement tells you the essay must compare?","What makes identical treatment look unjust here?","Which teaching cuts against sorting people by race?","What distinction has been missing from both chains so far?","How does the conclusion avoid being a fresh opinion?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which three issues does the specification say you must be able to explain contrasting beliefs about, and which traditions must the contrast use?","a":"The status of women in religion, the uses of wealth, and freedom of religious expression - contrasted using Christianity, the main religious tradition in Britain, plus one or more other religious traditions."},{"q":"What is the difference between prejudice and discrimination?","a":"Prejudice is the attitude, judging someone in advance; discrimination is the unfair action taken because of it."},{"q":"Which three forms of exploitation of the poor does the specification name?","a":"Fair pay, excessive interest on loans, and people-trafficking."},{"q":"Why is 'and its causes' the most important phrase in the responsibilities of wealth?","a":"Because it asks for more than almsgiving: the duty extends to changing the wages, debts and trading conditions that keep people poor, which is where Catholic social teaching and the prophets go beyond charity."},{"q":"What is the difference in British law between positive discrimination and positive action?","a":"Positive discrimination - appointing someone because of their race - is unlawful; positive action, such as encouraging applications or offering training to under-represented groups, is allowed under the Equality Act 2010."}]},"room":{"facts":["Theme F is one five-part question of 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks, and the paper advises 25 minutes on a theme - roughly a minute a mark.","The 12-mark evaluation on Component 2 also carries 3 SPaG marks, and only the best of the four themes' SPaG marks counts towards the paper.","The 4-mark contrasting question on Component 2 adds the instruction to refer to one or more religious traditions, so the tradition must be named in the answer.","The 5-mark question adds the instruction to refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching, and that reference is worth one mark on its own.","The three prescribed contrasting issues for this theme are the status of women in religion, the uses of wealth, and freedom of religious expression, argued from Christianity plus one or more other traditions.","The three named forms of exploitation of the poor are unfair pay, excessive interest on loans, and people-trafficking; the responsibilities of wealth include tackling poverty and its causes."],"questions":[{"q":"Which one of the following is the yearly Muslim duty to give a share of surplus wealth to the poor? A Khums B Sadaqah C Sawm D Zakah","a":"AO1:1. Answer: D Zakah.","sol":"D Zakah, one of the Five Pillars, normally 2.5 per cent of surplus wealth each year. Khums is the fifth paid by Shi'a Muslims; sadaqah is voluntary giving over and above what is owed; sawm is the fast of Ramadan.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The answer has to be a duty, paid yearly, out of wealth.","One option is not about money at all, so it can go first.","Of the payments left, one is voluntary and one is paid only by Shi'a Muslims."],"codes":["3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — The responsibilities of wealth"]},{"q":"Which one of the following means charging excessive interest on a loan? A Riba B Sewa C Tzedakah D Daan","a":"AO1:1. Answer: A Riba.","sol":"A Riba, the interest forbidden in Islam, which is why Islamic banks share risk and profit instead of lending at interest. Sewa is Sikh selfless service, tzedakah is Jewish righteous giving and daan is Hindu giving - all three are forms of giving, not lending.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Three of the four words are about giving something away; one is about lending.","Sort the options by the tradition each comes from, then by what each one does.","The odd one out is the Arabic term forbidden in the Qur'an."],"codes":["3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — Exploitation of the poor"]},{"q":"Give two forms of exploitation of the poor.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points, no development required: unfair pay; excessive interest on loans; people-trafficking; also creditable are slave labour and unfair working conditions.","sol":"Two from: unfair pay, where workers are paid less than they can live on; excessive interest on loans, such as the charges of payday lenders; people-trafficking, the buying and selling of human beings for forced labour or prostitution. Two bare points score the two marks - no explanation is needed or rewarded.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The specification names three forms, and two of them are about money owed to or taken from people.","Think about what someone earns, what someone borrows, and what someone can be treated as.","Two short separate points are enough here; do not develop them."],"codes":["3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — Exploitation of the poor"]},{"q":"Give two religious teachings about the right attitude to wealth.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points: wealth is a trust from God; the love of money is a root of evil; wealth is not wrong but attachment to it is; wealth must be earned honestly; artha is a legitimate aim of life if pursued within dharma.","sol":"Two from: wealth is a trust from God to be used for others, not an absolute possession; the love of money is a root of evil (1 Timothy 6:10); greed is one of the three poisons in Buddhism, so attachment to wealth blocks progress; wealth must be honestly earned, as in the Sikh duty of kirat karni; artha, material prosperity, is one of the four aims of life in Hinduism when pursued within dharma. Two bare points score the two marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The question is about attitude, not about giving it away.","One tradition warns about wanting money; another allows prosperity if it is honestly gained.","Two short separate points are enough; save the explaining for a longer question."],"codes":["3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — Wealth"]},{"q":"Give two human rights set out in the Universal Declaration of Human Rights.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points: the right to life, liberty and security; freedom from slavery; freedom of thought, conscience and religion; freedom of expression; equality before the law; the right to education.","sol":"Two from: the right to life, liberty and security of person; freedom from slavery and servitude; freedom of thought, conscience and religion; freedom of opinion and expression; equality before the law; the right to education. The Declaration was adopted in 1948 and its first article says all human beings are born free and equal in dignity and rights. Two bare points score the two marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think of the freedoms a government is not allowed to take away.","One of them is the freedom this theme argues about most: belief and its expression.","Two short separate points are enough; no development is credited."],"codes":["3.2.1.6 Human rights — Human rights and the responsibilities that come with rights"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting religious beliefs about the status of women in religion. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions.","a":"AO1:3. Two plus two: simple explanation of a relevant contrast 1 mark, detailed 2 marks, for each. This is one of the three prescribed contrasting issues, so one belief must be Christian and one from another tradition: credit Church of England ordination of women against Orthodox Jewish or Muslim practice in worship, or any accurately attributed contrast of that shape. Similar beliefs are credited only once.","sol":"In Christianity, the Church of England believes women should hold every office in the church. It has ordained women as priests since 1994 and consecrated its first woman bishop in 2015, arguing from Galatians 3:28 that in Christ there is 'neither male nor female', so a role that carries authority cannot be closed to half the church. Orthodox Judaism, by contrast, believes women and men have different obligations in worship. Women are not ordained as rabbis, ten men are needed for a minyan, and men and women sit separately in the synagogue; Orthodox teaching describes these roles as separate but equal, since women are exempt from time-bound positive commandments and the home is the centre of Jewish observance.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["On this prescribed issue the contrast must run between Christianity and one other tradition, not inside one religion.","Each belief needs a reason attached to it, because the second mark is for detail, not for a longer sentence.","Set the second paragraph against the first with a word that signals the clash, and name the tradition in both."],"codes":["3.2.1.6 Human rights — Prejudice and discrimination in religion and belief","3.2.1.6 — The theme's own requirement: religious teachings, contrasting perspectives, and the three prescribed issues"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting religious beliefs about freedom of religious expression. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions.","a":"AO1:3. Two plus two. Credit the duty to wear religious dress (the Khalsa Sikh 5 Ks, the hijab) against traditions that treat outward signs as optional or that place modesty and respect for others first; also credit contrasting views on speaking about faith at work.","sol":"Some religious believers hold that expressing their faith openly is a duty they cannot set aside. A Sikh who has taken amrit and joined the Khalsa must wear the five Ks, including uncut hair under a turban, so a rule against religious dress asks that Sikh to choose between faith and work; in Mandla v Dowell Lee (1983) the House of Lords protected exactly that. Other believers hold that outward expression may be limited out of respect for others. Many Christians treat a cross as a personal sign rather than an obligation, and Quakers have long emphasised plainness rather than display, so they accept limits at work or in hospital where safety or the rights of others are at stake.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Start from whether the expression is required by the tradition or chosen by the believer.","One side needs an example of something a believer is obliged to wear; the other needs a reason for accepting limits.","Name the tradition in each paragraph, because Component 2 prints that instruction with the question."],"codes":["3.2.1.6 Human rights — Issues of equality, freedom of religion and belief"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting religious beliefs about the uses of wealth. In your answer you must refer to one or more religious traditions.","a":"AO1:3. Two plus two. Credit traditions that treat wealth as a trust to be given away or used simply against traditions that treat prosperity as a legitimate aim pursued within duty; also credit contrasts over giving to individuals against funding long-term development.","sol":"Christianity teaches that wealth is held in trust and is spiritually dangerous unless it is shared. Jesus taught that it is easier for a camel to go through 'the eye of a needle' than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of God (Mark 10:25), and 1 Timothy 6:10 calls 'the love of money' a root of all kinds of evil, so the first believers in Jerusalem held everything in common and sold property so that it could be distributed 'as any had need' (Acts 4:32-35). Other believers hold that wealth may properly be enjoyed and invested. Hinduism lists artha, material prosperity, as one of the four aims of life alongside dharma, kama and moksha, so earning and spending within one's duty is a legitimate stage of life, and Sikh teaching praises kirat karni, honest work, provided it is matched by vand chhakna, sharing what is earned.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The uses of wealth is one of the three issues you must be able to contrast, so this question is predictable.","One side treats wealth as held in trust; the other treats prosperity as a proper goal.","Develop each side by naming the duty or the aim of life it rests on, and name the tradition."],"codes":["3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — Wealth","3.2.1.6 — The theme's own requirement: religious teachings, contrasting perspectives, and the three prescribed issues"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which belief in social justice influences religious believers today.","a":"AO1:2. Two plus two: simple explanation of a relevant influence 1 mark, detailed 2 marks; for the second mark the influence must be shown - what believers actually do differently. Credit regular budgeted giving, campaigning on debt and wages, volunteering, langar, food banks.","sol":"One way is that giving becomes a fixed duty rather than an impulse. Because tzedakah comes from the word for righteousness, many Jews set aside a share of income before spending on themselves, and support organisations working for fairer conditions as well as emergency relief, so the giving continues when the giver stops feeling moved. A second way is campaigning against the causes of poverty. Catholic social teaching's preferential option for the poor leads Catholics to work through CAFOD on debt, trade and wages, writing to members of Parliament and supporting fair trade purchasing in parishes and schools, so belief changes how people shop and vote as well as what they donate.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["An influence question is only answered when the answer says what somebody does differently.","Take the two ways from different traditions so neither repeats the other.","End each way with the visible consequence, because that is where the second mark is."],"codes":["3.2.1.6 Human rights — Social justice"]},{"q":"Explain two religious teachings about the responsibilities of wealth. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","a":"AO1:1. Two plus two for the teachings, plus one mark for a relevant and accurate source. Credit zakah as a due of the poor, Matthew 25:40 and the sheep and the goats, tzedakah and the corners of the field, Catholic social teaching on the causes of poverty.","sol":"One teaching is that the poor have a claim on the wealth of the rich. In Islam zakah is a pillar, normally 2.5 per cent of surplus wealth each year, and it is described as a due belonging to the poor rather than a favour done to them, which is why it is calculated and paid whether or not the giver feels generous. A second teaching is that service to the poor is service to God. In the parable of the sheep and the goats Jesus tells those who fed the hungry and clothed the naked that whatever they did for 'the least of these' they did for him (Matthew 25:40), so Christians treat relieving need as an obligation owed to Christ himself, and Catholic social teaching extends it to changing the wages and debts that cause the need.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Two teachings only, each from a named tradition, and the fifth mark is separate from both.","One teaching should make giving a duty owed; the other should say why relieving need matters to God.","Name the text or the pillar plainly, because a hinted source is not a reference."],"codes":["3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — The responsibilities of wealth","3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — Charity"]},{"q":"Explain two religious teachings about racial equality. Refer to sacred writings or another source of religious belief and teaching in your answer.","a":"AO1:1. Two plus two plus one for the source. Credit Genesis 1:27 and the image of God, Galatians 3:28, Acts 10:34, the Good Samaritan, Surah 49:13, the Prophet's Final Sermon, Guru Nanak and langar.","sol":"One teaching is that every person is made in the image of God (Genesis 1:27), so worth is given by the Creator and cannot be increased or reduced by skin colour; Christians and Jews argue from this that racism is not merely unkind but an insult to God. A second teaching is that human difference exists for acquaintance and not for ranking: the Qur'an says God made humanity into nations and tribes 'that you may know one another' (Surah 49:13), and in his Final Sermon the Prophet Muhammad taught that no Arab is superior to a non-Arab and no white person to a black person. Both teachings are used today to argue that a religious community which tolerates racism has broken its own rule.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Take one teaching from Christianity and one from another tradition so the answer is not one religion twice.","Each teaching needs its consequence: say what follows for how people must be treated.","Give the reference itself - book and chapter, or surah - because the fifth mark is for the source."],"codes":["3.2.1.6 Human rights — Racial prejudice and discrimination"]},{"q":"'Giving money to the poor is the best way for a religious believer to help.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels-marked. Support: the duty to give - zakah, tzedakah, almsgiving, Matthew 25:40; immediate relief; the giver's own attachment broken. Other views: dependence, the causes of poverty, Maimonides' highest level, agencies and campaigning, the responsibilities of those in poverty. Level 4 needs both sides reasoned and a justified conclusion.","sol":"The case for the statement is that giving is commanded and that it works immediately. In Islam zakah is one of the Five Pillars, normally 2.5 per cent of surplus wealth a year, and it is treated as a due belonging to the poor rather than as generosity; Jewish tzedakah takes its name from the word for righteousness, so handing money over is an act of justice; and in the parable of the sheep and the goats Jesus says that what was done for 'the least of these' was done for him (Matthew 25:40). Supporters add that money is the most flexible help there is: a hungry family can buy what it actually needs, and nothing else relieves need so quickly. Giving also does something to the giver, loosening the attachment that 1 Timothy 6:10 warns about when it calls 'the love of money' a root of evil.\n\nThe opposing case does not deny the duty; it questions the word 'best'. The specification itself speaks of the duty to tackle poverty and its causes, and money handed to individuals leaves those causes untouched - unfair pay, debt at excessive interest, and trafficking go on. Maimonides ranked the levels of tzedakah and put highest of all the gift, loan, partnership or job that ends a person's need for charity, which is an argument from inside the tradition for opportunity rather than cash. Christian agencies work the same way: CAFOD, founded in 1962, and Christian Aid, whose Christian Aid Week has been held since 1957, fund long-term development and campaign on trade and debt as well as sending emergency relief. Critics also argue that constant giving can create dependence and can let governments and employers escape responsibility, and that the person in poverty has responsibilities too - Sikh teaching makes kirat karni, earning an honest living, a duty in its own right. Humanists press the point about effectiveness: give where the evidence shows the most good is done.\n\nWeighing these, the duty to give is not in doubt and no religion treats giving as optional, so an answer that dismisses charity would be arguing against every tradition on the specification. But 'best' is a comparison, and on that test giving money is often the quickest help and rarely the most lasting. My judgement is that the statement is half right: money given generously and regularly is the necessary first response to immediate need, while the better way, where a believer can choose, is the giving that ends the need - training, work, fair wages and campaigning - which is exactly what Maimonides and the churches' own agencies concluded.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["The word to argue about is 'best', which asks you to compare ways of helping, not to approve of charity.","Take the statement seriously first by showing that giving is a duty in more than one tradition.","Then find the argument inside a tradition for a different kind of help, and let your conclusion follow from it."],"codes":["3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — Charity","3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — The responsibilities of those living in poverty","3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — The responsibilities of wealth"]},{"q":"'Positive discrimination is the fairest way to correct racial inequality.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels-marked. Support: justice repairs unfairness, the preferential option for the poor, unequal starting points, representation. Other views: Galatians 3:28, the Final Sermon, positive discrimination unlawful under the Equality Act 2010 while positive action is lawful, tokenism, better remedies. Level 4 needs both sides reasoned and a justified conclusion.","sol":"In support of the statement, justice is not the same as sameness. If one group has been held back for generations, treating everyone identically from today freezes the advantage that unfairness created, so those who support positive discrimination argue that a temporary preference is what repairing the damage actually requires. Religious teaching supports the principle: Catholic social teaching's preferential option for the poor deliberately puts the needs of the disadvantaged first, and the prophets demand that society be changed rather than merely pitied - Amos calls for justice to 'roll down like waters' (Amos 5:24) and Deuteronomy 16:20 commands 'Justice, justice shall you pursue'. Supporters add a practical reason: when a community sees nobody like itself teaching, policing or leading worship, the exclusion continues by itself, and only deliberate action breaks it.\n\nThe opposing case is that choosing by race is still choosing by race. Galatians 3:28 says that in Christ there is 'neither Jew nor Greek', the Qur'an teaches that nations and tribes exist 'that you may know one another' (Surah 49:13), and the Prophet Muhammad taught in his Final Sermon that no Arab is superior to a non-Arab: none of these allows a person's race to decide what they receive, even for a good reason. British law draws the same line. Under the Equality Act 2010 positive discrimination - appointing someone because of their race - is unlawful, while positive action is not: an employer may advertise widely, run training for under-represented groups, and prefer a candidate from such a group where two candidates are equally qualified. Critics also warn about the cost to the person appointed, whose colleagues may treat the appointment as a favour, and argue that the real remedies are enforcing the law against discrimination, improving schools, and removing names from application forms.\n\nWeighing the two, supporters are right that inequality left alone does not correct itself and that the aim is a matter of justice, not preference, so doing nothing is not a neutral option. They are wrong, however, about the method. Positive action achieves what they want - wider pools of candidates, trained applicants, a tie broken in favour of a group long excluded - without deciding anyone's future by their race, and it has the advantage of being lawful. My conclusion is that the statement names the right problem and the wrong solution: the fairest way to correct racial inequality is vigorous positive action, backed by the teaching that every person is made in the image of God, rather than positive discrimination.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["The statement says 'fairest', so the essay has to compare this remedy with others.","One side should explain why identical treatment can preserve unfairness; the other why race should not decide outcomes.","There is a distinction in British law that decides the argument - find it and build the conclusion on it."],"codes":["3.2.1.6 Human rights — Ethical arguments related to racial discrimination","3.2.1.6 Human rights — Racial prejudice and discrimination"]},{"q":"'People should be free to wear religious dress and symbols wherever they want.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels-marked. Support: Article 9 and the Human Rights Act 1998, the Equality Act 2010, obligations such as the 5 Ks and the hijab, Mandla v Dowell Lee, the Eweida judgment. Other views: the right is qualified, safety, identification, the responsibility to respect the rights of others. Level 4 needs both sides reasoned and a justified conclusion.","sol":"The case for the statement rests on what religious dress is. For a Khalsa Sikh the five Ks are not decoration but obligations taken at amrit, so a rule against them does not restrict a preference, it asks the believer to stop being a Sikh at the door; the same is true of the hijab for many Muslim women and the kippah for observant Jewish men. British law has recognised this: in Mandla v Dowell Lee (1983) the House of Lords held that a school could not turn away a Sikh boy who wore his turban, Sikhs wearing turbans are exempt from the motorcycle helmet law, and in Eweida and Others v United Kingdom (2013) the European Court of Human Rights held that the United Kingdom had violated Article 9 by failing to protect Nadia Eweida's right to wear a small cross at work. Article 9 of the European Convention, part of British law through the Human Rights Act 1998, protects freedom of religion, and the Equality Act 2010 makes religion or belief a protected characteristic. Humanists agree: Humanists UK defends freedom of belief and its expression even where it does not share the belief.\n\nThe opposing case takes hold of the word 'wherever'. Article 9 is a qualified right, limited where public safety, public order or the rights of others require it, and the specification pairs human rights with the responsibility to respect the rights of others for exactly this reason. A surgeon cannot wear loose sleeves or a bracelet in theatre, machinery and loose clothing are a known danger, a court or an airport may need to see a face, and a school may reasonably insist that a kirpan is small and secured. Religious arguments exist on this side too: traditions teach modesty rather than display, and Jesus warned against religion practised to be seen by others (Matthew 6:1). Some also argue, as French law does, that a strictly neutral public space protects everyone equally, though critics reply that neutrality in practice falls hardest on minorities whose obligations are visible.\n\nWeighing the two, the presumption must be with the believer: the burden should fall on whoever wants to restrict religious dress to show a real reason, because the cost of a ban is paid by people who cannot obey it without abandoning a duty. But 'wherever they want' cannot survive the cases of safety and identification, and no tradition claims a right to endanger others. My conclusion is that the statement is right in spirit and wrong in wording: people should be free to wear religious dress and symbols everywhere unless a specific, evidenced need of safety or the rights of others requires otherwise, and employers should have to prove that need rather than assume it.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["One word in the statement makes it absolute; the essay is really about whether the exceptions exist.","Start by showing what religious dress means to someone for whom it is an obligation, not a choice.","Then use the responsibility that the specification pairs with rights to find the limits, and let the conclusion say where the line falls."],"codes":["3.2.1.6 Human rights — Issues of equality, freedom of religion and belief","3.2.1.6 Human rights — Human rights and the responsibilities that come with rights"]},{"q":"'Religions should treat women and men exactly the same.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels-marked. Support: Genesis 1:27, Galatians 3:28, Guru Granth Sahib 473, women priests from 1994 and the first woman bishop in 2015, women rabbis in Reform Judaism, the Equality Act 2010. Other views: complementary roles, the Catholic priesthood, Orthodox Jewish separate but equal, the male imam at Friday prayer. Level 4 needs both sides reasoned and a justified conclusion.","sol":"The case for the statement begins with teachings about equal worth. Genesis 1:27 says humanity is made 'in the image of God', male and female together, and Paul writes that in Christ there is 'neither male nor female' (Galatians 3:28). Sikh teaching is blunter: in the Guru Granth Sahib Guru Nanak asks why woman should be called inferior when kings are born from women (Guru Granth Sahib 473), and Sikh women may lead worship and read from the scripture. Supporters argue that identical worth ought to show itself in identical access to roles, and point to what has changed without harm: the Church of England has ordained women as priests since 1994 and consecrated its first woman bishop in 2015, Reform Judaism has ordained women as rabbis in Britain since the 1970s, and in wider society the Equality Act 2010 makes sex discrimination unlawful in work and education. Humanists press the point that a rule reserving a role for men needs evidence, not custom, and that no such evidence has been produced.\n\nA different view is that equal worth does not require identical roles. The Roman Catholic Church ordains only men, teaching that the priest acts in the person of Christ, who chose twelve men as apostles, and holds at the same time that women are not of lesser worth - Mary is honoured above all the saints. Orthodox Judaism describes the roles as separate but equal: men are obliged to time-bound commandments and make up the minyan, while the home through which Jewish identity passes is entrusted to women. In Islam the imam leading mixed congregational prayer is male, and men and women pray in separate spaces, which many Muslims explain as modesty and order rather than rank. On this view treating everyone identically is not equality but sameness, and it can quietly devalue what women in these traditions actually do.\n\nAgainst that, critics reply that separate roles have in practice been unequal ones, because it is always women who are excluded from the roles that carry authority, and a teaching that sounds like difference has functioned as discrimination.\n\nMy judgement is that the statement is right where authority is concerned and too blunt elsewhere. Where a role carries teaching authority or leadership, excluding women cannot easily be squared with Genesis 1:27 or Galatians 3:28, and the traditions that have opened those roles have lost nothing by it. But 'exactly the same' would also forbid the separate spaces and different obligations that many women in Orthodox Judaism and Islam choose and defend, and equality that overrules the people it claims to help is not equality. Equal status, equal access to authority, and freedom to keep distinct practices where women themselves uphold them, is the defensible position.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Separate the two things the statement runs together: equal worth, and identical roles.","Each side has scripture behind it, so cite one text per side and say what follows from it.","Test the opposing view against what has actually happened to women, then let the conclusion say where the statement holds and where it does not."],"codes":["3.2.1.6 Human rights — Prejudice and discrimination in religion and belief","3.2.1.6 — The theme's own requirement: religious teachings, contrasting perspectives, and the three prescribed issues"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is the difference between prejudice and discrimination?","a":"Prejudice is the attitude, judging someone before you know them; discrimination is the unfair action taken because of it."},{"q":"What is zakah, and how much is normally given?","a":"The Muslim duty of almsgiving, one of the Five Pillars: normally 2.5 per cent of surplus wealth each year, treated as a due of the poor."},{"q":"Which verse of Genesis, and which words from it, are used to argue that all races have equal worth?","a":"Genesis 1:27 - humanity is made 'in the image of God'."},{"q":"Which three forms of exploitation of the poor does the specification name?","a":"Unfair pay, excessive interest on loans, and people-trafficking."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Prejudice and discrimination","code":"3.2.1.6 Human rights — Prejudice and discrimination in religion and belief","open":false,"back":"Prejudice is the attitude - judging a person or group in advance. Discrimination is the act that follows: treating them unfairly because of that attitude. One is in the mind, the other is in the world."},{"front":"The status of women in religion: the Christian contrast","code":"3.2.1.6 Human rights — Prejudice and discrimination in religion and belief","open":false,"back":"The Church of England has ordained women as priests since 1994 and had its first woman bishop in 2015. The Roman Catholic Church ordains only men, holding that the priest acts in the person of Christ."},{"front":"The Catechism of the Catholic Church on homosexuality","code":"3.2.1.6 Human rights — Prejudice and discrimination in religion and belief","open":false,"back":"It teaches that homosexual acts are wrong, while insisting that homosexual people be treated with respect, compassion and sensitivity. Quakers, and Liberal and Reform Judaism, marry same-sex couples."},{"front":"Freedom of religious expression in British law","code":"3.2.1.6 Human rights — Issues of equality, freedom of religion and belief","open":false,"back":"Article 9 of the European Convention, in British law through the Human Rights Act 1998, protects freedom of religion but is qualified by safety and the rights of others. The Equality Act 2010 protects religion or belief."},{"front":"Mandla v Dowell Lee (1983)","code":"3.2.1.6 Human rights — Issues of equality, freedom of religion and belief","open":false,"back":"The House of Lords held that Sikhs are protected as an ethnic group, so a school could not refuse a boy who wore a turban. Sikhs wearing turbans are also exempt from the motorcycle helmet law."},{"front":"Universal Declaration of Human Rights (1948)","code":"3.2.1.6 Human rights — Human rights and the responsibilities that come with rights","open":false,"back":"Its first article states that all human beings are born free and equal in dignity and rights. The specification pairs rights with the responsibility to respect the rights of others."},{"front":"Social justice","code":"3.2.1.6 Human rights — Social justice","open":false,"back":"Fairness in how a society shares wealth, opportunity and power, and the duty to work for it. Jewish teaching names tzedek and tikkun olam; Catholic social teaching names human dignity, the common good and the option for the poor."},{"front":"Sewa and langar","code":"3.2.1.6 Human rights — Social justice","open":false,"back":"Sewa is selfless service to others; langar is the free kitchen in every gurdwara, begun by Guru Nanak, where all sit and eat together regardless of rank, caste, wealth or gender."},{"front":"Surah 49:13 and the Final Sermon","code":"3.2.1.6 Human rights — Racial prejudice and discrimination","open":false,"back":"The Qur'an says God made humanity into nations and tribes 'that you may know one another'. In his Final Sermon the Prophet Muhammad taught that no Arab is superior to a non-Arab, nor a white person to a black person."},{"front":"Positive discrimination and positive action","code":"3.2.1.6 Human rights — Ethical arguments related to racial discrimination","open":false,"back":"Positive discrimination, appointing someone because of their race, is unlawful in Britain. Positive action - outreach, training, or preferring an equally qualified candidate from an under-represented group - is allowed under the Equality Act 2010."},{"front":"Artha","code":"3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — Wealth","open":false,"back":"In Hinduism, material prosperity: one of the four aims of life with dharma, kama and moksha. Wealth pursued within dharma is a legitimate goal, which contrasts with Christian warnings about the love of money."},{"front":"Zakah","code":"3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — The responsibilities of wealth","open":false,"back":"The Muslim duty of almsgiving and one of the Five Pillars: normally 2.5 per cent of surplus wealth each year, treated as a due belonging to the poor. Sadaqah is voluntary giving on top; Shi'a Muslims also pay khums."},{"front":"Name one way the poor are exploited, as the specification names it","code":"3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — Exploitation of the poor","open":true,"back":"Unfair pay - wages below what a worker can live on; excessive interest on loans - the charges of payday lenders, forbidden as riba in Islam; people-trafficking - the buying and selling of human beings, a crime under the Modern Slavery Act 2015."},{"front":"Maimonides on tzedakah","code":"3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — The responsibilities of those living in poverty","open":false,"back":"He ranked the levels of giving, putting highest of all the gift, loan, partnership or job that leaves a person able to support themselves and no longer in need of charity."},{"front":"CAFOD, Christian Aid and Tearfund","code":"3.2.1.6 Wealth and poverty — Charity","open":false,"back":"British Christian development agencies: CAFOD (Catholic, founded 1962), Christian Aid (the British and Irish churches, Christian Aid Week since 1957) and Tearfund (evangelical, 1968). All fund long-term development as well as emergency relief."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Evaluate: 'It is wrong for a religious believer to be rich.')","items":["The paragraph: Those who support the statement argue that wealth itself blocks discipleship. Jesus told the rich young man to sell what he had, and said it was easier for a camel to go through 'the eye of a needle' than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of God (Mark 10:25); the first Christians in Jerusalem held everything in common and distributed to those in need (Acts 4:32-35), and 1 Timothy 6:10 calls 'the love of money' a root of evil. Monastic orders have taken this literally for centuries through vows of poverty, and the reasoning is serious: wealth creates attachment, attachment creates fear of losing it, and fear makes generosity difficult, which is close to the Buddhist analysis of greed as one of the three poisons. Against this stands the weight of teaching that treats wealth as a trust rather than a sin. Islam requires zakah, normally 2.5 per cent of surplus wealth each year, which assumes that believers hold wealth in the first place and purifies it rather than forbidding it; Hinduism lists artha, prosperity, among the four aims of life; and Sikh teaching praises kirat karni, honest earning, so long as it is matched by vand chhakna, sharing. On balance the warnings are about attachment rather than possession, since a tradition that commanded poverty could not also command the giving that relieves it - so the honest conclusion is that being rich is not wrong in itself, but staying rich while a neighbour goes hungry is what the texts actually condemn.","Why this is Level 4: it gives reasoned consideration of different points of view, with a chain of reasoning on each side rather than a list of opinions; references to religion are applied to the issue (Mark, Acts, monastic vows, zakah, artha, kirat karni) instead of being mentioned in passing; and it reaches a judgement supported by that knowledge, explaining why the warnings are read as being about attachment. That matches the Level 4 descriptor: 'A well-argued response, reasoned consideration of different points of view. Logical chains of reasoning leading to judgement(s) supported by knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information. Reference to religion applied to the issue.'"]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same statement, visibly weaker)","items":["The paragraph: I do not think it is wrong for a religious believer to be rich because if you have worked hard for your money you should be allowed to keep it, and rich people can give more to charity than poor people can. Some religious people disagree and say you should give everything away to help others, because there are people starving and it is selfish to have a lot when they have nothing. So there are two sides to this, but I think being rich is fine as long as you are a good person with it.","Why this is only Level 2: it does show 'Recognition of different points of view, each supported by relevant reasons/evidence', which is the Level 2 descriptor, and the reasons given are real ones. What it misses is what Level 3 requires - 'Logical chains of reasoning that draw on knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information' and 'Clear reference to religion'. No tradition, text, teaching or practice is named: 'some religious people' is not a reference to religion applied to the issue, and there is no zakah, no Gospel teaching, no attitude to wealth from any named tradition. The last sentence announces a preference rather than justifying one, so it is not the judgement Level 4 asks for. To move up it would need named positions with the reason each gives, and a conclusion that follows from those reasons."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Christianity on human rights, wealth and poverty","items":["Human dignity: every person is made in the image of God (Genesis 1:27), so worth is given by the Creator and is equal in everyone.","Racial equality: in Christ there is 'neither Jew nor Greek' (Galatians 3:28); Peter concludes that God shows no favouritism (Acts 10:34); the hero of the parable of the Good Samaritan is a foreigner despised by Jesus' hearers (Luke 10:25-37).","Wealth: 'the love of money' is a root of evil (1 Timothy 6:10), and it is easier for a camel to pass through 'the eye of a needle' than for a rich man to enter the kingdom (Mark 10:25) - warnings about attachment, not a ban on property.","Charity: in the parable of the sheep and the goats, what is done for 'the least of these' is done for Christ himself (Matthew 25:40).","The early church in Jerusalem held everything in common and distributed to anyone in need (Acts 4:32-35).","Fair pay: James condemns the rich who kept back the wages of the labourers who mowed their fields (James 5:4).","Self-help and its limits: 2 Thessalonians 3:10 says those unwilling to work should not eat, while Deuteronomy 15:11 commands an open hand because the poor will always be in the land.","Catholic social teaching, from Rerum Novarum (Pope Leo XIII, 1891) onwards: human dignity, the common good, subsidiarity, solidarity, and the preferential option for the poor.","Women: the Church of England ordained its first women priests in 1994 and consecrated its first woman bishop in 2015; the Roman Catholic Church ordains only men, holding that the priest acts in the person of Christ, who chose twelve men as apostles.","Homosexuality: the Catechism of the Catholic Church teaches that homosexual acts are wrong while requiring that homosexual people be treated with respect, compassion and sensitivity; the Religious Society of Friends (Quakers) marries same-sex couples.","Agencies: CAFOD (founded 1962, the Catholic Church in England and Wales), Christian Aid (the churches of Britain and Ireland, Christian Aid Week held since 1957), Tearfund (evangelical, 1968), and the Trussell Trust, founded on Christian principles, which supports a network of food banks in the UK.","Slavery: Christian campaigners led by William Wilberforce secured the abolition of the British slave trade in 1807 and of slavery in most of the British Empire in 1833; the Salvation Army today works with victims of modern slavery in England and Wales."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Islam on human rights, wealth and poverty","items":["Racial equality: God made humanity into nations and tribes 'that you may know one another' (Surah 49:13); in his Final Sermon the Prophet Muhammad taught that no Arab is superior to a non-Arab and no white person to a black person.","Wealth is a trust from God, lawfully earned (halal) and purified by giving; hoarding it is a failure of duty.","Zakah: one of the Five Pillars, normally 2.5 per cent of surplus wealth held for a lunar year, treated as a due belonging to the poor rather than a favour to them.","Sadaqah: voluntary giving beyond zakah, which may be money, time or any act of kindness. Shi'a Muslims also pay khums, a fifth of surplus income.","Interest: the Qur'an says God 'permitted trade and forbidden usury' (Surah 2:275), so riba is prohibited and Islamic banks share risk and profit instead of lending at interest.","Women: men and women are created from a single soul (Surah 4:1) and are equal before God in judgement and reward; the imam who leads mixed congregational prayer is male, and men and women pray in separate spaces.","Freedom of expression: for many Muslim women the hijab is an obligation of modesty, so a ban on religious dress is experienced as a ban on obedience rather than on a preference.","Muslim Aid and Islamic Relief, both founded in Britain, distribute zakah and sadaqah and fund long-term development as well as emergency relief."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Judaism on human rights, wealth and poverty","items":["Human dignity: humanity is made in the image of God (Genesis 1:27), and 'love your neighbour as yourself' (Leviticus 19:18) is the rule applied to the stranger as well as the neighbour.","Justice: 'Justice, justice shall you pursue' (Deuteronomy 16:20); Amos demands that justice 'roll down like waters' (Amos 5:24); Micah 6:8 asks people to act justly and love mercy.","Tzedakah takes its name from the word for righteousness, so giving is an act of justice rather than of generosity.","Maimonides ranked the levels of tzedakah, putting highest of all the gift, loan, partnership or job that leaves a person no longer in need of charity.","Tikkun olam: the duty to repair the world, used to argue that believers must change unjust structures and not only relieve need.","The Torah leaves the corners of the field and the gleanings for the poor and the stranger (Leviticus 19:9-10) - a right of the poor rather than the farmer's choice.","Fair pay: a hired worker's wages must not be kept back overnight (Leviticus 19:13). Interest: Exodus 22:25 forbids lending at interest to the poor.","Women: Reform and Liberal Judaism ordain women as rabbis, and Britain's first woman rabbi was ordained in Reform Judaism in 1975; Orthodox Judaism does not ordain women and describes the roles of men and women as separate but equal, with men obliged to time-bound commandments."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Sikhism, Hinduism and Buddhism on this theme","items":["Sikhism - the three duties: naam japna (remembering God), kirat karni (earning an honest living) and vand chhakna (sharing what you earn with others).","Sikhism - sewa, selfless service, and langar, the free kitchen begun by Guru Nanak where all eat together regardless of caste, wealth, gender or religion.","Sikhism - equality of women: the Guru Granth Sahib asks why woman should be called inferior when kings are born from women (Guru Granth Sahib 473); Sikh women may lead worship and read from the scripture.","Sikhism - religious expression: Khalsa Sikhs wear the five Ks (kesh, kangha, kara, kachera, kirpan), so religious dress is an obligation and not a choice; the Khalsa was founded by Guru Gobind Singh in 1699.","Hinduism - artha, material prosperity, is one of the four aims of life with dharma, kama and moksha, so wealth pursued within duty is legitimate; daan is the duty of giving.","Hinduism - ahimsa, non-violence, and the teaching that the same divine reality is in all beings, used against caste discrimination and untouchability, which Gandhi campaigned to end.","Buddhism - greed is one of the three poisons, and craving is the cause of suffering, so attachment to wealth is the danger rather than wealth itself.","Buddhism - right livelihood, the fifth step of the Noble Eightfold Path, rules out ways of earning that harm others, including trading in human beings, weapons and intoxicants.","Buddhism - dana, generosity, is the first of the perfections, and metta and karuna (loving-kindness and compassion) are extended to all beings without distinction."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: non-religious views and the law in Britain","items":["Humanists take their morality from reason, empathy and the consequences of actions rather than from revelation, and argue that human rights rest on our shared humanity and capacity to suffer, not on God.","Humanists UK campaigns for freedom of belief - including the freedom to have none - and for equality on grounds of sex, race and sexual orientation, and supports giving where evidence shows the most good is done.","Some atheists argue that religion has been used to justify inequality, pointing to slavery, caste and the exclusion of women; religious believers reply that religious campaigners also led the movements against those wrongs.","The Universal Declaration of Human Rights (1948) states in its first article that all human beings are born free and equal in dignity and rights.","The Human Rights Act 1998 brings the European Convention into British law; Article 9 protects freedom of thought, conscience and religion but is qualified by public safety, public order and the rights of others.","The Equality Act 2010 makes nine characteristics protected, including race, religion or belief, sex and sexual orientation, and makes discrimination in work, education and services unlawful.","Positive discrimination is unlawful in Britain; positive action - encouraging applications, offering training, or preferring an equally qualified candidate from an under-represented group - is lawful under the Equality Act 2010.","Mandla v Dowell Lee (1983): the House of Lords held that Sikhs are protected as an ethnic group, so a school could not refuse a boy wearing a turban.","In 2013 the European Court of Human Rights held that Britain had failed to protect Nadia Eweida's right to wear a small cross at work as a British Airways employee.","Pay and lending: there is a legal minimum wage, and a higher voluntary real Living Wage that many churches and charities have adopted; the Financial Conduct Authority has capped the cost of payday loans since 2015, a change the Archbishop of Canterbury supported when he backed credit unions in 2013.","People-trafficking: the Modern Slavery Act 2015 made it a serious criminal offence and required large businesses to report on slavery in their supply chains."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'The best way to help the poor is to tackle the causes of poverty.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to religious arguments; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion.","items":["Decode the statement: 'best way' is a comparison, so the essay must weigh tackling causes against relieving need directly; it does not ask whether charity is good. The specification's own wording - the duty to tackle poverty 'and its causes' - tells you this is examinable both ways.","Timing: about twelve minutes, plus the three SPaG marks, so roughly two minutes planning, four paragraphs, and a minute at the end to check spelling and the specialist terms (zakah, sadaqah, tzedakah, riba, sewa, subsidiarity, solidarity).","Paragraph 1, in support: poverty is produced by unfair pay, debt at excessive interest and trafficking, so relieving it without changing them means relieving it for ever. Sources: Amos 5:24, justice to 'roll down like waters'; Deuteronomy 16:20, 'Justice, justice shall you pursue'; Catholic social teaching's common good and preferential option for the poor.","Paragraph 2, in support, developed: Maimonides put the gift, loan, partnership or job that ends a person's need for charity at the top of the levels of tzedakah, which is an argument from inside the tradition that opportunity beats cash; CAFOD, Christian Aid and Tearfund fund development and campaign on debt and trade for the same reason.","Paragraph 3, the different point of view: a hungry family cannot wait for structures to change. Zakah is a pillar of Islam paid yearly to the poor; the sheep and the goats judges people on whether they fed the hungry (Matthew 25:40); langar feeds whoever comes today. Campaigning is uncertain and slow, while a meal is neither.","Paragraph 4, the non-religious voice and the sharpening argument: humanists judge by consequences and ask which pound does the most good, which cuts both ways; and the specification's point about the responsibilities of those living in poverty warns that neither charity nor campaigning works if it treats people as objects rather than agents.","Evaluation thread to run through the essay: keep asking of each method, what happens to the poor person next year? That question makes the comparison concrete and stops the answer becoming two lists.","What the conclusion must say: not 'both are important', but which wins and why. For example - relief is the duty that cannot be postponed, so it is never wrong, but on the test the statement sets, lasting help, tackling causes is the better way, and the traditions themselves say so through Maimonides' highest level and the work of their own agencies."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"The rubric of Theme F in one place","items":["The three prescribed contrasting issues: the status of women in religion; the uses of wealth; freedom of religious expression. On each you must be able to explain contrasting beliefs using Christianity, the main religious tradition in Britain, plus one or more other religious traditions.","Christianity is the main religious tradition of Great Britain and the religious traditions of Britain are diverse: you may draw on Christianity, Buddhism, Hinduism, Islam, Judaism and Sikhism, and on non-religious beliefs such as atheism and humanism.","Sources of wisdom and authority you may cite: the Bible, the Catechism of the Catholic Church, the Qur'an and Hadith, the Torah and Talmud, the Vedas and Upanishads, the Pali Canon, and the Guru Granth Sahib.","Human rights half of the theme: prejudice and discrimination in religion and belief, including the status and treatment within religion of women and homosexuals; equality, freedom of religion and belief; human rights and the responsibility to respect the rights of others; social justice; racial prejudice and discrimination; ethical arguments about racial discrimination, including positive discrimination.","Wealth and poverty half of the theme: the right attitude to wealth and the uses of wealth; the responsibilities of wealth, including the duty to tackle poverty and its causes; exploitation of the poor - fair pay, excessive interest on loans, people-trafficking; the responsibilities of those living in poverty to help themselves; charity, including the issues around giving money to the poor.","Contrasts are strongest when they are attributed exactly: not 'Christians believe' but 'the Roman Catholic Church teaches', 'the Church of England has taught since 1994', 'Orthodox Judaism holds', 'many Sunni Muslims argue'."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T14:14:51.304204Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Re-judged after the fix pass: all eight fixes landed and none introduced an error. Eweida is now stated accurately and identically in all three places (Eweida and Others v United Kingdom (2013), the Court holding that the United Kingdom violated Article 9 by failing to protect her right), with the lesson idea adding that the case was against the state and not British Airways; room.questions[5] now contrasts the Church of England (women priests 1994, first woman bishop 2015, Galatians 3:28) with Orthodox Judaism (no women rabbis, ten men for a minyan, separate seating, women exempt from time-bound positive commandments) - all of that is accurate Orthodox practice - and its a and first hint match; room.questions[7] is now anchored in Christianity (Mark 10:25, 1 Timothy 6:10, Acts 4:32-35 'as any had need') against Hindu artha and Sikh kirat karni and vand chhakna; lesson.examples[1] teaches the prescribed-issue rule instead of the single-religion contrast and its cues still work unchanged; lesson.examples[2] steps 3-4 now distinguish a third source from a third teaching; the plan title carries the five Component 2 bullets. Re-read whole: all 23 scripture and source references correct, no quotation over seven words, every date, denomination, charity and legal claim accurate, tariffs 1, 2, 4, 5, 12 with three or more questions at each difficulty, all four 12-mark stems carrying the bullets and [Plus SPaG 3 marks] and no SPaG elsewhere, all twelve key ideas tested, solution lengths and card limits within the family rules, and no June 2023 or 2026 specimen Theme F question reproduced. Publishable."}}},"3.2.2.1":{"id":"AQA-8062","topic":"3.2.2.1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Theme G is one of the four themes you answer in Component 2, and on the textual studies paper it is compulsory: anyone entered for this route takes both Theme G and Theme H and must also study Christianity or Catholic Christianity in Component 1. It is worth one whole five-part question of 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks, with the spelling, punctuation and grammar marks riding on the 12-mark evaluation. What makes this theme different from every other is that the examiner can ask about a named passage, and the 5-mark question carries the instruction that you must refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer. That fifth mark is given for the reference itself, so the eighteen set passages have to be known closely enough to be cited by chapter and verse, not merely retold. The 12-mark question then asks you to weigh interpretations - whether the miracles happened, what the words at the Last Supper mean, what the empty tomb shows, how much authority the Gospel still carries beside science, history and the law. This room gives you the passages, the four titles, the interpretations that make a genuinely two-sided argument, and the shape of an answer at the top of the levels grid.","idea":[{"h":"The rubric that governs every answer on St Mark's Gospel","t":"Section 3.2.2 sets three demands. You must know the set texts themselves and what they meant for Jesus, for his early followers and for people of the 21st century; you must understand how varied interpretations of the same passage give rise to diversity within Christian traditions; and you must consider how far Christian and non-religious communities give authority to the Gospel beside other sources of contemporary authority. Mark is the shortest Gospel, usually dated to about AD 65-70 and traditionally linked with John Mark, a companion of Peter. Both textual themes are taken together, and Component 1 must be Christianity or Catholic Christianity. A top-level answer therefore never summarises a story for its own sake: it says what the passage shows, cites it by chapter and verse, and shows that Christians read it in more than one way.","code":"3.2.2.1 — The textual studies requirement: the set texts, their interpretation and the authority of the Gospel"},{"h":"John's preparation for Jesus' ministry (Mark 1:1-8)","t":"Mark opens with no birth story at all: he begins with 'the good news of Jesus Christ' (1:1), a prophecy of a messenger sent ahead, and John baptising in the wilderness and preaching repentance for the forgiveness of sins. John wears camel's hair and eats locusts and wild honey, like a prophet of old, and he points away from himself: one is coming who is 'one more powerful than I' (1:7) whose sandals he is not worthy to untie, and who will 'baptise you with the Holy Spirit' (1:8). For early followers this made Jesus the fulfilment of prophecy, not a new idea. A top-level answer uses this passage to argue that Mark presents Jesus from the first verse as Christ and Son of God, so the titles are not later additions to the story.","code":"3.2.2.1 The early ministry of Jesus — John's preparation for Jesus' ministry: 1:1–8"},{"h":"Jesus' baptism and temptation (Mark 1:9-13)","t":"Jesus comes from Nazareth in Galilee and is baptised by John in the Jordan. Mark says the heavens were torn open, the Spirit came down like a dove, and a voice from heaven said 'You are my Son, whom I love' (1:11). At once the Spirit sends him into the wilderness for forty days, tempted by Satan, with the wild animals, and angels attending him. Christians disagree about why a sinless Jesus was baptised: as an example of obedience, as an identification with sinners, or as the moment his mission is publicly declared. A top-level answer links the voice at the baptism to the voice at the transfiguration (9:7) and to the centurion's words at the cross (15:39), because Son of God is the thread Mark runs from beginning to end.","code":"3.2.2.1 The early ministry of Jesus — Jesus' baptism and temptation: 1:9–13"},{"h":"The paralysed man (Mark 2:1-12)","t":"Four friends cannot get through the crowd, so they dig through the roof and lower the man down. Mark says that Jesus saw 'their' faith - the friends' faith, not only the patient's - and said 'Son, your sins are forgiven' (2:5). The teachers of the law think this is blasphemy, because God alone can forgive sins, and Jesus heals the man so that they will know the Son of Man has 'authority on earth to forgive sins' (2:10). The healing is evidence for a claim, not the point of the story. A top-level answer uses this passage to show that the conflict which ends at the trial (14:64) begins here, and that Mark uses miracles to say who Jesus is.","code":"3.2.2.1 The early ministry of Jesus — The paralysed man: 2:1–12"},{"h":"Jairus' daughter (Mark 5:21-24a, 35-43)","t":"Jairus, a synagogue leader, falls at Jesus' feet for his dying daughter. Word comes that she has died, and Jesus tells him 'Don't be afraid; just believe' (5:36). He takes only Peter, James and John, puts the mourners outside, takes the girl's hand and says 'Talitha koum!' (5:41) - Aramaic for 'Little girl, get up'. She is twelve years old, she walks about, and he gives strict orders that no one should know and that she be given something to eat. Note the verse selection: the woman with the haemorrhage sandwiched inside the story is a Theme H text. A top-level answer reads the passage for faith and for the messianic secret, and notes that the order to say nothing is Mark's pattern, not an accident.","code":"3.2.2.1 The early ministry of Jesus — Jairus' daughter: 5:21–24a, 35–43"},{"h":"The rejection at Nazareth (Mark 6:1-6)","t":"In his home synagogue the people are amazed at his teaching but cannot get past what they already know about him: 'Isn't this the carpenter?' and 'Isn't this Mary's son?' (6:3). Familiarity breeds offence, and Jesus answers that 'a prophet is not without honour' (6:4) except in his own town, among his relatives and in his own home. Mark then says something no other evangelist dares: he could not do any miracle there except heal a few sick people, and he was 'amazed at their lack of faith' (6:6). A top-level answer uses this verse on both sides of an argument about miracles - it shows the Gospel itself linking miracle to faith, which supports a non-literal reading, and it shows Mark reporting an awkward fact he had no motive to invent.","code":"3.2.2.1 The early ministry of Jesus — The rejection at Nazareth: 6:1–6"},{"h":"The feeding of the five thousand (Mark 6:30-44)","t":"Jesus has compassion on the crowd because they are 'like sheep without a shepherd' (6:34) and teaches them. The disciples want them sent away; Jesus takes five loaves and two fish, looks up to heaven, gives thanks, breaks the loaves and gives them to the disciples to distribute. All eat and are satisfied and twelve basketfuls of pieces are collected, and the men numbered five thousand. Took, blessed, broke, gave is exactly what he does at the Last Supper (14:22), which is why many Christians read the passage as a eucharistic sign. Others read it as a nature miracle, or as the crowd being moved to share what they had. A top-level answer names those readings as contrasting Christian beliefs about one passage, which is precisely what the 4-mark question asks for.","code":"3.2.2.1 The early ministry of Jesus — The feeding of the five thousand: 6:30–44"},{"h":"The conversation at Caesarea Philippi (Mark 8:27-33)","t":"On the road Jesus asks 'who do people say I am' (8:27), then asks the disciples directly, and Peter answers 'You are the Messiah' (8:29). Jesus warns them to tell no one and begins to teach that 'the Son of Man must suffer' (8:31), be rejected by the elders, chief priests and teachers of the law, be killed and after three days rise. Peter takes him aside and rebukes him; Jesus turns and says 'Get behind me, Satan!' (8:33), because Peter has in mind human concerns and not the concerns of God. This is the hinge of the Gospel: the title is right and Peter's idea of it is wrong. A top-level answer uses it to argue that Jesus accepts Messiah only when he has redefined it as suffering rather than conquest.","code":"3.2.2.1 The later ministry of Jesus — The conversation at Caesarea Philippi: 8:27–33"},{"h":"The transfiguration of Jesus (Mark 9:2-9)","t":"Six days later Jesus takes Peter, James and John up a high mountain, where his clothes become dazzling white and Elijah and Moses appear talking with him. Peter, not knowing what to say because they were so frightened, offers to put up three shelters. A cloud covers them and the voice says 'This is my Son, whom I love' and 'Listen to him!' (9:7). Coming down, Jesus orders them to tell no one until the Son of Man has risen from the dead. Moses stands for the Law and Elijah for the prophets, so the scene places Jesus above both. A top-level answer treats it as the divine answer to Peter's half-right confession: glory is real, but it is reached through the cross.","code":"3.2.2.1 The later ministry of Jesus — The transfiguration of Jesus: 9:2–9"},{"h":"Jesus' passion prediction (Mark 10:32-34)","t":"On the road up to Jerusalem the disciples are astonished and those following are afraid. Jesus takes the Twelve aside and tells them in detail what is coming: the Son of Man will be handed over to the chief priests and teachers of the law, condemned to death, handed to the Gentiles, mocked, spat on, flogged and killed, and after three days he will rise. It is the third and fullest of his predictions in Mark, and the passion narrative then fulfils it point by point. Christians disagree about whether this is foreknowledge or Mark writing after the event. A top-level answer says which it thinks and why, rather than only reporting that the argument exists.","code":"3.2.2.1 The later ministry of Jesus — Jesus' passion prediction: 10:32–34"},{"h":"The request of James and John (Mark 10:35-45)","t":"The two brothers ask for the places at his right and left in glory. Jesus asks whether they can drink the cup he drinks or be baptised with his baptism - images of suffering - and says the places are not his to give. The other ten are indignant, so Jesus contrasts the rulers of the Gentiles who lord it over people with his own community: whoever wants to be great 'must be your servant' (10:43), and the Son of Man came not to be served but to serve and to give his life as 'a ransom for many' (10:45). Verse 45 is the clearest statement in Mark of what the death achieves. A top-level answer uses it for both service and atonement, and notes that the disciples ask for status immediately after a prediction of suffering.","code":"3.2.2.1 The later ministry of Jesus — The request of James and John: 10:35–45"},{"h":"Bartimaeus (Mark 10:46-52)","t":"Outside Jericho a blind beggar, named as Bartimaeus son of Timaeus, shouts 'Jesus, Son of David, have mercy' (10:47) and will not be silenced by the crowd. Jesus calls him; he throws off his cloak - his only shelter and his begging mat - and comes. To the question of what he wants, he answers 'Rabbi, I want to see' (10:51), and Jesus says 'your faith has healed you' (10:52). He then follows Jesus along the road, into Jerusalem and the passion. He is the only healed person in Mark who is named and who becomes a follower, and the first to use the royal title Son of David openly. A top-level answer uses him as Mark's picture of discipleship: seeing, leaving everything and following.","code":"3.2.2.1 The later ministry of Jesus — Bartimaeus: 10:46–52"},{"h":"The entry into Jerusalem (Mark 11:1-11)","t":"Jesus sends two disciples for a colt that no one has ever ridden, which is how animals for sacred use were chosen. People spread cloaks and branches on the road and shout 'Hosanna!' and 'Blessed is he who comes' (11:9) in the name of the Lord, and bless the coming kingdom of 'our father David' (11:10). This is deliberate, public and royal, and it is also anticlimactic in Mark: he goes into the temple, looks round at everything and, because it was late, goes out to Bethany with the Twelve. A top-level answer argues about what kind of claim is being made - a colt rather than a warhorse suggests a king who comes in peace, which is exactly the misunderstanding the crowd later abandons him over.","code":"3.2.2.1 The later ministry of Jesus — The entry into Jerusalem: 11:1–11"},{"h":"The Last Supper (Mark 14:12-26)","t":"On the first day of the Festival of Unleavened Bread two disciples are sent to follow a man carrying a jar of water to a furnished upper room. At the meal Jesus says one of the Twelve who is eating with him will betray him. He takes bread, gives thanks, breaks it and says 'Take it; this is my body' (14:22); he takes the cup and says it is 'my blood of the covenant' (14:24), poured out for many, and that he will not drink again until the kingdom of God. They sing a hymn and go out to the Mount of Olives. Covenant and Passover are the key words. A top-level answer connects the meal to the death - the body given and the blood poured out interpret the cross before it happens - and then to the divergent eucharistic beliefs of the Christian traditions.","code":"3.2.2.1 The final days in Jerusalem — The Last Supper: 14:12–26"},{"h":"Jesus in Gethsemane (Mark 14:32-52)","t":"Jesus takes Peter, James and John and becomes deeply distressed and troubled, saying his soul is overwhelmed with sorrow. He falls to the ground and prays to 'Abba, Father' (14:36) that everything is possible and asks him to 'take this cup from me', then 'yet not what I will' but what God wills. Three times he finds them sleeping and warns that 'the spirit is willing' (14:38) but the flesh is weak. Judas arrives with an armed crowd and betrays him with a kiss; a bystander cuts off a servant's ear; everyone deserts him and flees, and a young man runs away naked. A top-level answer uses the passage for the humanity of Jesus and for obedience, and notes that Mark records the failure of the disciples without softening it.","code":"3.2.2.1 The final days in Jerusalem — Jesus in Gethsemane: 14:32–52"},{"h":"The trial before the Jewish authorities (Mark 14:53, 57-65)","t":"Jesus is taken to the high priest, where the chief priests, elders and teachers of the law are assembled. Witnesses accuse him of saying he would destroy the temple made with hands and build another not made with hands, but their testimony does not agree. The high priest asks directly whether he is the Messiah, 'the Son of the Blessed One' (14:61), and Jesus answers 'I am' (14:62) and speaks of the Son of Man 'sitting at the right hand' of the Mighty One and coming on the clouds of heaven. The high priest tears his clothes, calls it blasphemy, and they all condemn him as worthy of death, then spit at him and strike him. A top-level answer notes that three titles meet in one exchange and that the silence of the Gospel is over: Jesus finally says yes.","code":"3.2.2.1 The final days in Jerusalem — The trial before the Jewish authorities: 14:53, 57–65"},{"h":"The trial before Pilate (Mark 15:1-15)","t":"The whole Sanhedrin hands Jesus over to Pilate, whose only question is political: 'Are you the king of the Jews?' (15:2). Jesus replies that Pilate has said so and then makes no reply to the accusations, so that Pilate is amazed. There is a custom of releasing a prisoner at the festival; the chief priests stir the crowd to ask for Barabbas, a man imprisoned with rebels who had committed murder in an uprising. The crowd shouts 'Crucify him!' (15:13), and Pilate, 'wanting to satisfy the crowd' (15:15), releases Barabbas, has Jesus flogged and hands him over to be crucified. A top-level answer weighs the responsibility Mark distributes here - a governor who thinks him innocent, priests who want him dead and a crowd that can be steered.","code":"3.2.2.1 The final days in Jerusalem — The trial before Pilate: 15:1–15"},{"h":"The crucifixion and burial (Mark 15:21-47)","t":"Simon of Cyrene is forced to carry the cross to Golgotha. The written charge reads 'The King of the Jews' (15:26); two rebels are crucified with him, one on his right and one on his left, and passers-by, chief priests and the rebels mock him for not saving himself. Darkness covers the land from noon until three, and Jesus cries out 'why have you forsaken me?' (15:34), a line from Psalm 22, and dies with a loud cry. 'The curtain of the temple' (15:38) is torn in two from top to bottom, and the centurion says 'this man was the Son of God' (15:39). Joseph of Arimathea, a member of the Council, asks Pilate for the body and lays it in a rock tomb. A top-level answer notices that a Gentile soldier makes the confession Mark's own first verse made.","code":"3.2.2.1 The final days in Jerusalem — The crucifixion and burial: 15:21–47"},{"h":"The empty tomb (Mark 16:1-8)","t":"After the Sabbath, Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James, and Salome buy spices and come at sunrise, asking each other who will roll the stone away. It is already rolled back, and a young man in a white robe tells them 'He has risen! He is not here' (16:6), shows them the place where they laid him, and sends them to the disciples and Peter, because he is going ahead into Galilee. Then the famous ending: trembling and bewildered, 'they said nothing to anyone' (16:8), because they were afraid. The earliest manuscripts stop there; the longer endings appear in later ones. A top-level answer treats the abrupt ending as evidence in an argument - either a story left unfinished for the reader to finish, or a sign that the resurrection appearances came later.","code":"3.2.2.1 The final days in Jerusalem — The empty tomb: 16:1–8"},{"h":"The four titles and what they meant in the 1st century","t":"Son of Man is the title Jesus uses of himself: in Aramaic it can mean simply a human being, but in Daniel 7:13 it is a heavenly figure coming with the clouds who is given authority - and Jesus uses it both of his suffering (8:31) and of his coming in glory (14:62). Christ, Messiah, means the anointed one, and many 1st century Jews expected a warrior king who would free Israel from Rome; Jesus accepts it at Caesarea Philippi and immediately silences it. Son of David is the royal, Davidic claim used by Bartimaeus (10:47) and echoed by the crowd, who bless 'the coming kingdom of our father David' (11:10) without using the title itself - 'Son of David' on the road into Jerusalem is Matthew 21:9, not Mark. Son of God, in the 1st century, could mean a king or one specially close to God. A top-level answer distinguishes what a hearer would have expected from what Jesus does with the title.","code":"3.2.2.1 Significance — The titles Son of Man, Son of God, Christ (Messiah) and Son of David"},{"h":"The titles for 21st century Christians","t":"This is a separate bullet and is examined in its own right. Confessing Jesus as Son of God is, for most Christians, a statement about his divine nature and the doctrine of the incarnation, said every week in the Nicene Creed; Christ has become so familiar that it is used as a name rather than a claim. Son of Man matters because it is the title of a suffering servant, which reshapes Christian ideas of power and leadership - greatness as service (10:43-45) is used to argue about bishops, employers and politicians alike. Son of David roots Jesus in Israel and in the promises of the Old Testament. Others argue the titles are dated and need explaining before they mean anything. A top-level answer says which titles still do work today and why.","code":"3.2.2.1 Significance — The significance for 21st century Christians of Jesus' understanding of the titles"},{"h":"Teacher and miracle worker, and the historicity of the miracles","t":"Mark shows Jesus teaching with authority and working four kinds of miracle: healings, exorcisms, raisings and nature miracles. The specification writes the argument into the content by asking for contrasting views on historicity. Christians who take the accounts literally argue that a God who creates can act within creation, and that the miracles are evidence for Jesus' identity as 2:10 says. Others argue that they are stories shaped to make a theological point, that some have natural explanations, or that they are the language of the time for events that impressed people; David Hume argued in 1748 that the evidence for a law of nature is always stronger than the testimony for its breach. A top-level answer weighs these and still says what it concludes.","code":"3.2.2.1 Significance — St Mark's portrayal of Jesus as a teacher and miracle worker"},{"h":"Differing beliefs about the meaning of the Last Supper","t":"One passage, four settled Christian positions. Roman Catholics teach transubstantiation: the substance of bread and wine becomes the body and blood of Christ while the appearances remain, which is why the reserved sacrament is honoured. The Orthodox affirm a real change but call it a mystery rather than explaining it. Lutherans hold that Christ is truly present in, with and under the bread and wine; many Anglicans speak of a real but spiritual presence received by faith. Most Baptist and other Nonconformist churches follow a memorial understanding: the service is an act of remembrance. Quakers and the Salvation Army keep no outward sacrament at all. A top-level answer names the traditions rather than saying 'some Christians', because that is where the second mark is.","code":"3.2.2.1 Significance — Differing beliefs about the meaning of Jesus' words and actions at the Last Supper"},{"h":"The significance of the death and resurrection, and the empty tomb","t":"Two strands. What the death achieved: a ransom 'for many' (10:45) paid to free people from sin; a sacrifice sealing a new covenant, which is what the words over the cup say (14:24); the example of obedient love; and, for many Christians, atonement that reconciles humanity to God. What happened at the tomb: most Christians hold to a bodily resurrection and point out that women, whose testimony carried little weight, are the first witnesses; others have suggested the women went to the wrong tomb, that the body was moved or stolen, that the appearances were visions, or that resurrection language is a way of saying his cause lives on. A top-level answer connects the two: what Christians think the death achieved depends on what they think happened afterwards.","code":"3.2.2.1 Significance — Differing beliefs about the significance of Jesus' death and resurrection"},{"h":"The authority of the Gospel beside secular authority","t":"This is the framing question of the whole textual route. Christians who treat the Gospel as inspired scripture give it the final word on how to live; Catholics read it within Tradition and alongside the teaching authority of the Church, as Vatican II set out in 1965; liberal Christians read it as a first century document whose message must be translated for a different world. Against it stand other authorities that people in Britain actually use: science, historical scholarship, the law, medical ethics committees and public opinion. Non-religious people may value the Gospel as literature, as history or as ethics without granting it authority at all. A top-level answer distinguishes authority over a believer who accepts it from authority over a society that does not.","code":"3.2.2.1 Significance — Differing views on the authority of St Mark's Gospel relating to the life of Jesus"}],"examples":[{"title":"The one-mark multiple choice: work by elimination","setup":"The opening part of the Theme G question is worth one mark. It reads: Which one of the following titles did blind Bartimaeus shout out as Jesus was leaving Jericho? A Son of God, B Son of Man, C Son of David, D King of the Jews. You put a tick in the box beside the correct answer, and no explanation is credited.","steps":["Place the scene first: a beggar by the roadside who has heard that a famous healer is passing, calling out for mercy and refusing to be quiet (Mark 10:46-48).","Rule out the option Mark reserves for the mockery of outsiders: the charge nailed to the cross reads The King of the Jews (Mark 15:26), and no Jewish beggar greets a healer that way.","Rule out the title Jesus uses only of himself in this Gospel: Son of Man is never put in another person's mouth, so it cannot be a bystander's shout.","Between the last two, ask which one is a royal, Jewish hope rather than a confession of divinity - the crowd will shout for the kingdom of David a few days later at the entry into Jerusalem (Mark 11:10).","Tick C: Son of David, the cry recorded at Mark 10:47, and move on inside a minute."],"cues":["Who is speaking in this passage, and what does he want?","Which of the four is used by Jesus' enemies rather than by those who come to him?","Which title does Jesus keep for himself throughout Mark?","Of the two that are left, which one is a claim about David's line?","Which option survives, and where in Mark is it said?"]},{"title":"The two-mark question: two points, nothing more","setup":"The second part is worth two marks and reads: Give two things that happened on the mountain when Jesus was transfigured. One mark is available for each of two correct points, only your first two answers are marked, and nothing is added for explaining them.","steps":["Picture the scene and list what changes: his clothes become dazzling white, whiter than anyone could bleach them (Mark 9:3).","Then list who appears: Elijah and Moses, talking with Jesus (Mark 9:4).","Then list what is heard: a cloud covers them and a voice says 'This is my Son, whom I love' (Mark 9:7).","Choose the two clearest and write them as two separate short sentences, with no third answer trailing after them, because a wrong first pair cannot be rescued by a correct third point."],"cues":["What is the first thing the three disciples see change?","Which two figures from Israel's past appear?","What is heard from the cloud?","How many points should be on the page, and why not more?"]},{"title":"The four-mark contrasting question: make the two views genuinely differ","setup":"The third part is worth four marks and reads: Explain two contrasting Christian beliefs about the raising of Jairus' daughter. It is marked two plus two, with one mark for a simple explanation of a contrast and two for a detailed one, and if the two beliefs turn out to be similar only one of them can be credited.","steps":["Set the first belief down plainly: many Christians hold that the girl was genuinely dead, as the messengers from the house reported, and that Jesus raised her, showing the power over death that the empty tomb later confirms.","Develop it with the detail the examiner rewards: Jesus takes only Peter, James and John, speaks the Aramaic words 'Talitha koum!' (Mark 5:41), and the girl gets up and walks, so Mark presents it as an act of God, not of medicine.","Now set a contrasting belief against it, using Jesus' own words in the passage: other Christians point out that he says the child is not dead but asleep (Mark 5:39), and read the story as a recovery from a coma or as a story shaped to teach that faith should not give way to fear.","Develop the contrast rather than repeating it: they add that Mark's point is the instruction 'Don't be afraid; just believe' (Mark 5:36), and that the meaning of the passage does not depend on settling the medical question.","Check the two paragraphs actually oppose each other - one says death and divine power, the other says sleep or symbol - and stop, because there is no fifth mark here for a source."],"cues":["What is the most obvious Christian reading of this passage?","What detail from the passage turns that statement into a detailed explanation?","Which sentence inside the passage itself gives a second, different reading?","What does that second view say the story is really for?","Do the two views disagree, and is anything else needed for the four marks?"]},{"title":"The five-mark question: two lessons plus the Gospel reference","setup":"The fourth part is worth five marks and reads: Explain two lessons Christians might learn from the rejection of Jesus at Nazareth. You must refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer. Four marks come from two lessons explained two plus two, and the fifth is given once, for a relevant and accurate reference to the Gospel.","steps":["Take the first lesson from what the townspeople do: faith is not guaranteed by familiarity, since the people who knew the carpenter and his family best were the ones who took offence at him.","Develop it into a lesson for a Christian today: being brought up in a church, or living among believers, is no substitute for actually trusting Jesus, so believers are warned against assuming they already know him.","Take the second lesson from what happens to Jesus: those who speak for God should expect to be rejected by their own, since he says that a prophet is not without honour except at home (Mark 6:4).","Develop it with the consequence the passage records - he could do no miracle there beyond healing a few sick people, and he was amazed at their lack of faith - so Christians learn that their response can limit what they receive.","Make the fifth mark unmissable by naming the reference plainly in the answer, quoting the prophet saying with its chapter and verse rather than leaving the wording unattributed."],"cues":["Why did the people of the town refuse to accept him?","What would that warning mean for a churchgoer now?","What does Jesus say about prophets, and where?","What does Mark say he was and was not able to do there?","Which single mark is still outstanding, and how is it earned?"]},{"title":"The twelve-mark evaluation: planning before writing","setup":"The last part is worth twelve marks plus three for spelling, punctuation and grammar. It reads: 'The entry into Jerusalem shows that Jesus claimed to be a king.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. You have about twelve minutes.","steps":["Decide what the statement would have to be true for: not whether he was a king, but whether the ride into the city was a deliberate public claim to be one.","Build the supporting case from the passage: he arranges the colt in advance, the crowd spreads cloaks on the road, and they bless the coming kingdom of David (Mark 11:10), which is royal language.","Build the opposing case from the same passage: a colt is not a warhorse, the crowd's hopes are not the same as his claim, and he goes into the temple, looks round and leaves for Bethany without seizing anything.","Bring in the Gospel's wider pattern as the deciding evidence: he silences the title at Caesarea Philippi and teaches instead that the Son of Man must suffer, so any kingship he claims has been redefined before this ride.","Add a view from outside religion in one sentence - a historian may read the entry as a small pilgrim procession later written up - and note what that does and does not show.","Reach the verdict: the colt arranged in advance matches the lowly king who comes riding 'on a colt' in Zechariah 9:9, and the crowd blesses the coming kingdom of David (11:10), so the ride is a deliberate royal sign and the statement is largely right - but only if 'king' is read as Mark reads it, because Jesus never uses the word of himself, Mark never quotes Zechariah, and the silence he has kept since Caesarea Philippi means the claim is being made and redefined at once: a king who rides in to die."],"cues":["What exactly would have to be shown for the statement to be true?","Which details of the ride look like a royal claim?","Which details of the same passage count against it?","What does the rest of Mark say about Jesus and the title Messiah?","What might someone who does not treat the Gospel as scripture say?","Weighing the colt and the crowd's shout against Jesus' own silence, which side carries more weight, and with what qualification?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which passage of Mark contains the words 'Talitha koum!', and what do they mean?","a":"Mark 5:41, in the raising of Jairus' daughter; the Aramaic means 'Little girl, get up'."},{"q":"What is the instruction added to the five-mark question on Themes G and H, and what is it worth?","a":"You must refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer - one mark, given once for a relevant and accurate reference."},{"q":"Who is the first human being in Mark to call Jesus the Son of God, and when?","a":"The centurion at the cross, after Jesus dies (Mark 15:39). Before that the words come from God at the baptism and the transfiguration."},{"q":"Name two different explanations Christians and others have given for the empty tomb.","a":"Any two of: a bodily resurrection; the women went to the wrong tomb; the body was moved or stolen; the appearances were visions; resurrection language describes a continuing spiritual reality."}]},"room":{"facts":["Theme G is one five-part question of 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks, and the paper advises 25 minutes on a theme - about a minute a mark.","The 5-mark question on Themes G and H adds the instruction that you must refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer; that fifth mark is awarded once, for a relevant and accurate reference to the Gospel.","The 12-mark evaluation is the only levels-marked question and carries 3 SPaG marks as well; on Component 2 only the best of the four themes' SPaG marks counts towards the paper.","The set passages by chapter and verse: 1:1-8; 1:9-13; 2:1-12; 5:21-24a, 35-43; 6:1-6; 6:30-44; 8:27-33; 9:2-9; 10:32-34; 10:35-45; 10:46-52; 11:1-11; 14:12-26; 14:32-52; 14:53, 57-65; 15:1-15; 15:21-47; 16:1-8.","The four titles the specification names are Son of Man, Son of God, Christ (Messiah) and Son of David, and you must know both what they meant to 1st century Jews and what they mean to Christians now.","An evaluation that never refers to religion is capped at Level 2 (4-6 marks), however well argued it is."],"questions":[{"q":"Which one of the following did the centurion say about Jesus when he saw how he died? A This man was innocent B This man was the Son of God C This man was a prophet D This man was the king of the Jews","a":"AO1:1. Answer: B This man was the Son of God (Mark 15:39).","sol":"B This man was the Son of God. Mark 15:39 gives the words to the Roman centurion standing in front of the cross, and they answer the claim made in the Gospel's opening verse (1:1). The written charge above the cross said 'The King of the Jews' (15:26), but that was Pilate's political charge, not the centurion's confession.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The speaker is a Roman soldier, not a disciple or a priest - ask what would strike him about the way Jesus died.","One option repeats the wording of the notice fixed above the cross, so it belongs to the trial rather than to this moment.","Mark writes the whole Gospel towards the moment when a human character finally says out loud who Jesus is, and this is it, so the answer will be a confession rather than a report."],"codes":["3.2.2.1 The final days in Jerusalem — The crucifixion and burial: 15:21–47","3.2.2.1 Significance — The titles Son of Man, Son of God, Christ (Messiah) and Son of David"]},{"q":"Which one of the following did blind Bartimaeus ask Jesus for? A To be forgiven B To walk again C To see D To sit at his right hand","a":"AO1:1. Answer: C To see (Mark 10:51).","sol":"C To see. Bartimaeus shouts 'Jesus, Son of David, have mercy' (Mark 10:47), throws off his cloak when he is called, and answers the question of what he wants with 'Rabbi, I want to see' (10:51). Jesus tells him his faith has healed him, and he follows Jesus along the road.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Start from what Mark tells you about the man before he speaks - his disability is in the first line of the passage.","Test each option against the Jericho story itself: some of them belong to requests made elsewhere in Mark, so the right one has to come from these seven verses.","Bartimaeus answers in five words, and what he asks for is exactly what his condition lacks."],"codes":["3.2.2.1 The later ministry of Jesus — Bartimaeus: 10:46–52"]},{"q":"Which one of the following did the crowd shout as Jesus rode into Jerusalem? A Hosanna B Talitha koum C Abba D Eloi, Eloi","a":"AO1:1. Answer: A Hosanna (Mark 11:9).","sol":"A Hosanna. As Jesus rides in on the colt the crowd spreads cloaks and branches and shouts 'Hosanna!' and 'Blessed is he who comes' in the name of the Lord (Mark 11:9), blessing the coming kingdom of David (11:10). The other three are Aramaic words from different passages: 'Talitha koum!' at the raising of Jairus' daughter (5:41), 'Abba' in Gethsemane (14:36) and 'Eloi, Eloi' from the cross (15:34).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Three of the four are Aramaic words spoken by or to Jesus in private moments; the answer is a public shout.","Two of the wrong options come from scenes of suffering or death, which do not fit a welcome.","The word you want is a cry of praise still used in Christian worship on Palm Sunday."],"codes":["3.2.2.1 The later ministry of Jesus — The entry into Jerusalem: 11:1–11"]},{"q":"Give two things Jesus said or did at the house of Jairus.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points: told Jairus not to be afraid but to believe; took only Peter, James and John; put the mourners outside; said the child was asleep; took her hand; said 'Talitha koum!'; ordered them to tell no one; told them to give her something to eat.","sol":"Two from: he told Jairus 'Don't be afraid; just believe' (Mark 5:36); he allowed only Peter, James and John to go with him; he put the crowd of mourners outside and said the child was not dead but asleep (5:39); he took her by the hand and said 'Talitha koum!' (5:41); he gave strict orders that no one should know about it; he told them to give her something to eat (5:43).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Work through the scene in order: what he says to the father, who he takes with him, what he does in the room.","Two of the marks are sitting in what he says immediately before and immediately after the girl gets up.","Write two separate short points; explaining them earns nothing and only your first two answers are marked."],"codes":["3.2.2.1 The early ministry of Jesus — Jairus' daughter: 5:21–24a, 35–43"]},{"q":"Give two things that happened when Jesus was baptised by John.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points: Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee and was baptised in the Jordan; the heavens were torn open; the Spirit came down like a dove; a voice from heaven called him the Son whom God loves.","sol":"Two from: Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee and was baptised by John in the river Jordan (Mark 1:9); heaven was torn open (1:10); the Spirit descended on him like a dove (1:10); a voice came from heaven saying 'You are my Son, whom I love' (1:11). Immediately afterwards the Spirit sent him into the wilderness for forty days (1:12-13).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Mark tells this in three quick movements - one you can see above, one that comes down, one you can hear.","John had already promised that the one coming would baptise with something other than water; that promise is pictured here.","Two short points only, one mark each, with no development needed."],"codes":["3.2.2.1 The early ministry of Jesus — Jesus' baptism and temptation: 1:9–13","3.2.2.1 The early ministry of Jesus — John's preparation for Jesus' ministry: 1:1–8"]},{"q":"Give two things the women were told at the empty tomb.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points: not to be alarmed; that Jesus of Nazareth who was crucified has risen; that he is not there; to see the place where they laid him; to go and tell his disciples and Peter; that he is going ahead of them into Galilee.","sol":"Two from: they were told not to be alarmed; that the Jesus they were looking for 'has risen! He is not here' (Mark 16:6); to see the place where they had laid him; to go and tell his disciples and Peter (16:7); and that he was going ahead of them into Galilee, where they would see him. Mark then records that they fled and 'said nothing to anyone' (16:8) because they were afraid.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The whole message comes from one speaker - the young man in the white robe sitting inside the tomb.","Part of what he says is about Jesus, and part of it is an instruction about what the women must now do.","One of the marks is easiest to secure from the place the message tells them to go to next."],"codes":["3.2.2.1 The final days in Jerusalem — The empty tomb: 16:1–8"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting Christian beliefs about what happened at the empty tomb.","a":"AO1:3. Marked 2 + 2: simple explanation of a relevant and accurate contrast 1 mark, detailed 2 marks, for each. Bodily resurrection as an act of God, with the women as first witnesses; against it, a non-literal reading - the wrong tomb, the body moved, visions, or resurrection language for a continuing spiritual reality. Similar beliefs are credited only once.","sol":"Most Christians believe that God raised Jesus bodily from death, and that the tomb was empty because he was no longer in it. The messenger says 'He has risen! He is not here' (Mark 16:6) and shows the women the place where the body had been laid; Mark also names women as the first witnesses at a time when their testimony carried little weight, which is not what an invented story would do.\n\nOther Christians hold that what matters is that Jesus lives with God, not what happened to a body. They point to the abrupt ending at Mark 16:8, where the women say nothing to anyone, and to the earliest manuscripts, which stop there without any appearance of the risen Jesus. On this view the empty tomb is a way of saying that death did not have the last word, so suggestions such as the wrong tomb or a moved body do not damage Christian faith.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The contrast is not between belief and unbelief; it is between two ways Christians themselves read the same eight verses.","Take the first view from what the young man in the tomb actually says, and the second from how Mark chooses to end.","Each view needs a supporting detail from the passage to move from a simple to a detailed explanation, and the two must genuinely differ."],"codes":["3.2.2.1 The final days in Jerusalem — The empty tomb: 16:1–8","3.2.2.1 Significance — Differing beliefs about the significance of Jesus' death and resurrection"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting Christian beliefs about the meaning of the tearing of the temple curtain when Jesus died.","a":"AO1:3. Marked 2 + 2. The way into God's presence opened to everyone, the barrier of the Holy of Holies removed and the temple sacrifices ended; against that, a sign of judgement on the temple and its leaders, which Jesus had already foretold (13:2), or a symbol Mark writes rather than an event he reports, echoing the heavens torn open at the baptism (1:10). Similar beliefs are credited only once.","sol":"Most Christians believe the tearing shows that the way to God is now open to everyone. The curtain shut off the Holy of Holies, which only the high priest entered, and Mark says it was torn 'from top to bottom' (15:38), that is by God rather than by hands, at the moment Jesus dies; the death itself has done what the temple sacrifices could not, which is why the very next voice heard is a Roman soldier's confession (15:39).\n\nOther Christians read the tearing as judgement on the temple rather than welcome. Jesus had already said that not one stone of it would be left on another (13:2), and the men who condemned him ran it; the Romans destroyed it in AD 70, about when Mark was written. Some add that Mark is writing a sign rather than reporting an event, since he uses the same tearing image when heaven is torn open at the baptism (1:10).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The curtain is the one that shut off the innermost part of the temple, so ask what its removal would say about who may now come to God.","One reading looks forward from the death to what has become possible; the other looks back to what Jesus had already said about the temple building.","Attach a detail from Mark to each half, because that is what turns a simple explanation into a detailed one, and make sure the two readings genuinely differ."],"codes":["3.2.2.1 The final days in Jerusalem — The crucifixion and burial: 15:21–47","3.2.2.1 Significance — Differing beliefs about the significance of Jesus' death and resurrection"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which St Mark's picture of Jesus as a servant influences Christians today.","a":"AO1:2. Marked 2 + 2: simple explanation of a relevant and accurate influence 1 mark, detailed 2 marks. The 'ransom for many' and 'must be your servant' teaching (10:43-45) shapes leadership in churches and charitable service. To earn the second mark the influence - what believers actually do differently - must be included.","sol":"One way is in how Christians understand leadership. Jesus told the Twelve that whoever wants to be great 'must be your servant' (Mark 10:43), so church leaders are expected to see office as service rather than status: the Pope takes the title servant of the servants of God, ministers describe their work as serving a congregation, and Christians will criticise a leader in their own church who behaves as the rulers of the Gentiles do in that passage.\n\nA second way is in practical charity. Because the Son of Man came 'to serve' and to give his life as 'a ransom for many' (10:45), Christians take service of the poor as the shape of a Christian life rather than as an optional extra: they volunteer at food banks and night shelters, give through agencies such as Christian Aid and CAFOD, and many choose caring professions for this reason, arguing that following a servant means serving the people he served.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["This form of the question is about influence, so each half has to reach what believers actually do now.","One answer belongs inside the church and concerns who holds authority; the other belongs outside it.","Ground both in the teaching Jesus gives after the two brothers ask for the best seats, and name the source."],"codes":["3.2.2.1 The later ministry of Jesus — The request of James and John: 10:35–45","3.2.2.1 Significance — The significance for 21st century Christians of Jesus' understanding of the titles"]},{"q":"Explain two lessons Christians might learn from the healing of the paralysed man. You must refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer.","a":"AO1:1, AO1:2. Marked 2 + 2 plus 1 for the reference to the Gospel. Lessons: the faith and effort of friends can bring someone to Jesus; forgiveness of sins matters more than physical healing; Jesus has authority to forgive; persistence in prayer; the danger of judging as the teachers of the law did. Maximum of two marks if the student only retells the story.","sol":"The first lesson is that faith is something Christians carry for one another. Mark says that when Jesus saw 'their' faith - the faith of the four men who dug through the roof - he spoke to the paralysed man, so believers learn that praying for another person, and taking trouble on their behalf, is not wasted effort; it is why intercessory prayer and visiting the sick matter in church life.\n\nThe second lesson is about priorities. Jesus deals with the man's sins before his legs, saying 'Son, your sins are forgiven' (Mark 2:5), and only heals him so that the onlookers will know that the Son of Man has 'authority on earth to forgive sins' (2:10). Christians learn from this that spiritual healing is the deeper need, and that Jesus claims an authority which the teachers of the law thought belonged to God alone - which is why the same charge of blasphemy returns at his trial (14:64).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Two lessons, and a lesson is what a Christian should now think or do, not what happened in the story.","One lesson comes from the four men on the roof, the other from the order in which Jesus deals with the man's needs.","The fifth mark is separate: name the Gospel reference plainly so the examiner cannot miss it."],"codes":["3.2.2.1 The early ministry of Jesus — The paralysed man: 2:1–12","3.2.2.1 Significance — The titles Son of Man, Son of God, Christ (Messiah) and Son of David"]},{"q":"Explain two lessons Christians might learn from Jesus' prayer in Gethsemane. You must refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer.","a":"AO1:1, AO1:2. Marked 2 + 2 plus 1 for the reference. Lessons: it is right to bring real fear to God; prayer submits to God's will rather than overriding it; God may be addressed intimately as Abba; watch and pray against temptation; even the closest disciples fail, so self-knowledge is needed. Maximum of two marks if the student only retells the story.","sol":"The first lesson is that Christians may be completely honest with God. Jesus is deeply distressed and asks his Father to 'take this cup from me' (Mark 14:36), which shows that a believer facing illness, bereavement or fear is not required to pretend to be calm; asking God to change a situation is itself a proper prayer.\n\nThe second lesson is about how such a prayer should end. Jesus calls God 'Abba, Father' - an intimate word - and then prays 'yet not what I will' but what God wills, so Christians learn that prayer is not a way of getting their own way but of aligning themselves with God's will, which is why the Lord's Prayer asks that God's will be done. Jesus also warns the sleeping disciples that 'the spirit is willing' (14:38) but the flesh is weak, so believers are taught to watch and pray rather than to trust their own good intentions.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Split the prayer into two halves: what Jesus asks for, and what he says after asking.","One lesson concerns honesty in prayer; the other concerns what a Christian does when the answer is no.","The reference mark is earned once - quote the words of the prayer with the chapter and verse."],"codes":["3.2.2.1 The final days in Jerusalem — Jesus in Gethsemane: 14:32–52","3.2.2.1 The later ministry of Jesus — Jesus' passion prediction: 10:32–34"]},{"q":"Explain two reasons why Peter's answer at Caesarea Philippi is important for Christians. You must refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer.","a":"AO1:1. Marked 2 + 2 plus 1 for the reference. Reasons: it is the first human confession that Jesus is the Messiah; it is the turning point after which Jesus teaches about suffering; it shows that the title has to be redefined, since Peter is rebuked; it introduces the messianic secret. Maximum of two marks if the student only retells the story.","sol":"The first reason is that it is the moment a human being finally gets the identity of Jesus right. To the question of who people say he is, the disciples report Elijah or a prophet; Peter answers 'You are the Messiah' (Mark 8:29), and Christians take this confession as the one the whole Gospel has been working towards and the one every believer must make personally.\n\nThe second reason is that Jesus immediately corrects what Peter means by it. He warns them to tell no one and begins to teach that 'the Son of Man must suffer' (8:31), be rejected, be killed and after three days rise; when Peter rebukes him for this, Jesus answers 'Get behind me, Satan!' (8:33) because Peter has human concerns in mind rather than God's. Christians learn from this that the title Messiah means a suffering servant rather than a conquering king, which is why the cross stands at the centre of Christian faith.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The passage has two halves - a right answer and an immediate correction - and each half gives you one reason.","Ask why Jesus would silence a title that has just been correctly applied to him.","Cite the exchange by chapter and verse so the fifth mark is secure."],"codes":["3.2.2.1 The later ministry of Jesus — The conversation at Caesarea Philippi: 8:27–33","3.2.2.1 Significance — The titles Son of Man, Son of God, Christ (Messiah) and Son of David"]},{"q":"Explain two things Christians learn about Jesus from the transfiguration. You must refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer.","a":"AO1:1. Marked 2 + 2 plus 1 for the reference. Points: his glory is revealed to three disciples; God confirms him as the beloved Son and commands that he be listened to; the presence of Moses and Elijah shows him fulfilling the Law and the prophets; the order of silence until the resurrection ties glory to the cross. Maximum of two marks if the student only retells the story.","sol":"The first thing Christians learn is that Jesus is the Son of God on God's own authority, not only on his followers' say-so. On the mountain a cloud covers Peter, James and John, and the voice says 'This is my Son, whom I love' and 'Listen to him!' (Mark 9:7) - the same declaration made at his baptism, now addressed to the disciples, so that his teaching carries God's authority.\n\nThe second is that he stands above Israel's whole tradition and yet is going to the cross. Moses, who gave the Law, and Elijah, the greatest of the prophets, appear talking with him, which tells Christians that the Law and the prophets point to Jesus rather than compete with him; and as they come down he orders them to tell no one until the Son of Man has risen from the dead (9:9), so the glory they have seen cannot be understood apart from his death and resurrection.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Two of the three things on the mountain are people, and one of them is a voice - each carries a different teaching.","Ask why Jesus tells them to keep quiet until a particular moment, and what that ties the vision to.","Name the passage by chapter and verse so that the reference mark is not lost."],"codes":["3.2.2.1 The later ministry of Jesus — The transfiguration of Jesus: 9:2–9","3.2.2.1 Significance — The significance for 21st century Christians of Jesus' understanding of the titles"]},{"q":"'The miracles in St Mark's Gospel did not really happen.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels-marked. Support: miracles break the laws of nature and cannot be checked; Mark writes decades later; natural explanations for the feeding and the healings; the Gospel's aim is theological. Other views: a creator God can act in creation; Mark's restraint and awkward details; the miracles are evidence for Jesus' identity. Level 4 needs both sides reasoned, the Gospel applied and a justified conclusion.","sol":"There is a serious case for the statement. The miracles describe events that nobody today can check, and Mark's Gospel is usually dated to about AD 65-70, a generation after the events, which leaves time for stories to grow. Several of them have plausible natural explanations: at the feeding of the five thousand (Mark 6:30-44) the crowd may have been moved by the example of the disciples to share food they had brought, and at the house of Jairus Jesus himself says the child is 'not dead but asleep' (5:39), which sounds like a coma. Mark also has a clear theological purpose - his first verse announces Jesus as Christ and Son of God - so an author with that aim may shape his material to prove it, as the healing of the paralysed man does when it is performed to show that the Son of Man has 'authority on earth to forgive sins' (2:10). David Hume argued in 1748 that the evidence for a law of nature is always stronger than the testimony of witnesses that it has been broken, and many Christians, without abandoning their faith, are content to read the miracles as stories that carry a truth about Jesus rather than as reports.\n\nA different point of view is held by most Christians. If God created the natural order, then acting within it is not impossible but simply unusual, so the question is one of evidence rather than of principle. Mark reports details no inventor would choose: Jesus is 'amazed at their lack of faith' at Nazareth and can do almost nothing there (6:5-6), which is an embarrassing admission, and the healings are often private, with orders to say nothing, rather than displays staged for a crowd. Miracles are also woven into the argument of the Gospel rather than decorating it: remove the authority to forgive sins in chapter 2 and the charge of blasphemy at the trial (14:64) makes no sense. Christians add that Jesus refuses to do miracles on demand, which is not how a legend grows.\n\nIn my judgement the statement is too sweeping. The nature miracles are the hardest to defend historically, and a Christian can honestly read the feeding as a sign whose meaning is eucharistic rather than as a conjuring trick. But the healings and exorcisms are so tied to the conflict that leads to the cross that removing them leaves the story unintelligible, and the awkward details suggest a writer reporting rather than inventing. I would therefore reject the statement as it stands: some miracle accounts are better read as signs, but it is not true that none of them happened.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Notice that the statement is an absolute - it says the miracles, not some of them - and decide whether that is what you want to defend.","Build one side completely before starting the other: the claim, the passage it rests on with its chapter and verse, then the reasoning that connects them.","Aim for two developed arguments each way, bring in one view from outside religion, and end by saying which arguments carried more weight and why."],"codes":["3.2.2.1 Significance — St Mark's portrayal of Jesus as a teacher and miracle worker","3.2.2.1 The early ministry of Jesus — The feeding of the five thousand: 6:30–44","3.2.2.1 The early ministry of Jesus — The rejection at Nazareth: 6:1–6","3.2.2.1 The early ministry of Jesus — The paralysed man: 2:1–12"]},{"q":"'St Mark's Gospel has no authority over people in Britain today.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels-marked. Support: Britain is largely secular; science, law and human rights are the working authorities; the Gospel is a 1st century document; even Christians disagree over what passages mean. Other views: it is scripture for Christians; Catholics read it with Tradition and the Church's teaching authority; its ethic of service still shapes charities, hospitals and law. Level 4 needs both sides reasoned, the Gospel applied and a justified conclusion.","sol":"The statement can be defended. Most people in Britain are not practising Christians, and the authorities they actually use are secular: scientific evidence decides medical questions, Parliament and the courts decide legal ones, and the Equality Act 2010 rather than a Gospel decides how an employer must treat people. The Gospel is also a document of the 1st century, written in a world of synagogues, Roman governors and demon possession, and it says nothing directly about most of the questions a modern society argues over. Even among Christians its meaning is disputed, as the differing readings of the words at the Last Supper (Mark 14:22-24) show, so a text that has to be interpreted before it can be applied cannot easily rule anyone. A humanist would add that moral authority should rest on reasoning about consequences and human welfare, not on the testimony of one first century writer, and that where the Gospel's ethic is good we can see that it is good without needing it to be commanded.\n\nA different point of view is taken by Christians, for whom Mark is scripture and therefore carries God's own authority. What Jesus says in it is treated as binding: that greatness means service (10:43-45) and that his death was 'a ransom for many' (10:45) shapes how millions of people in Britain pray, give and vote. Roman Catholics would qualify the claim rather than drop it, since they read the Gospel within the Church's tradition and teaching authority, so its authority is real but never stands alone. Christians also point out that the Gospel's influence on British society is visible outside the churches: hospitals, hospices, food banks and prison reform grew from this ethic, and even people who do not believe often accept that Jesus' teaching on service and on the outsider is worth following.\n\nIn my judgement the statement confuses two kinds of authority. Over British law and public policy it is largely right: no Gospel, read as scripture, can settle a question for a society that does not accept it, and science and law rightly do that work. Over individuals it is clearly wrong, because authority of this kind is accepted rather than imposed, and millions accept it. The fairest conclusion is that St Mark's Gospel has lost the power to compel and keeps the power to persuade - which, given that Jesus in this Gospel refuses to prove himself on demand, may be the only kind of authority it ever claimed.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["The word 'authority' is doing two different jobs here - over a person who accepts it and over a society that does not.","Set the authorities people in Britain actually appeal to against what a Christian means by calling this text scripture.","Two developed arguments each way, at least one non-religious voice, and a conclusion that says which kind of authority survives."],"codes":["3.2.2.1 — The textual studies requirement: the set texts, their interpretation and the authority of the Gospel","3.2.2.1 Significance — Differing views on the authority of St Mark's Gospel relating to the life of Jesus","3.2.2.1 The early ministry of Jesus — John's preparation for Jesus' ministry: 1:1–8","3.2.2.1 The final days in Jerusalem — The Last Supper: 14:12–26","3.2.2.1 Significance — Differing beliefs about the meaning of Jesus' words and actions at the Last Supper"]},{"q":"'Jesus was put to death because of who he claimed to be.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels-marked. Support: the high priest's question and the answer 'I am' (14:61-62); the charge of blasphemy; the royal claim of the entry; the notice above the cross. Other views: political fear of unrest, the crowd and Pilate's weakness, the temple saying, or that his death was God's purpose foretold in the passion predictions. Level 4 needs both sides reasoned, the Gospel applied and a justified conclusion.","sol":"There is strong support for the statement in Mark's account of the trial. The witnesses against Jesus cannot agree, so the case collapses until the high priest asks him directly whether he is the Messiah, 'the Son of the Blessed One' (Mark 14:61). Jesus answers 'I am' (14:62) and adds that they will see the Son of Man 'sitting at the right hand' of the Mighty One and coming on the clouds of heaven. The high priest tears his clothes, calls it blasphemy, and the council condemns him as worthy of death. On Mark's telling, then, Jesus is condemned for a claim about himself, and he is condemned on the only occasion in the Gospel when he states it openly rather than silencing it, as he does at Caesarea Philippi (8:30). The claim carries through to the Roman stage as well: Pilate asks 'Are you the king of the Jews?' (15:2) and the written charge above the cross reads 'The King of the Jews' (15:26).\n\nA different point of view stresses politics rather than theology. Pilate does not care about blasphemy; he asks about kingship because Rome executed people who threatened public order, and Mark says that he handed Jesus over 'wanting to satisfy the crowd' (15:15) after the chief priests had stirred them up. On this reading Jesus died because he was a danger at Passover, when Jerusalem was crowded, and because a governor took the easy way out. Others point to what Jesus did rather than said - the entry into Jerusalem (11:1-11) and his action in the temple looked like an attack on the authorities' position - or argue that the chief priests were protecting their own power. Christians themselves offer a third explanation: Jesus died because God purposed it, which is why he predicts the whole sequence in detail beforehand (10:32-34) and calls his death 'a ransom for many' (10:45).\n\nIn my judgement the statement is right about the Jewish trial and incomplete about everything else. The claim is what makes the condemnation possible - without the answer to the high priest there is no charge - but a claim alone does not get a man crucified by Rome, and Mark is careful to show a political process, a stirred crowd and a governor who thinks him innocent. The deepest Christian answer is that all three explanations are true at once: he died because of who he claimed to be, because of what that claim threatened, and because, as this Gospel says from chapter 8 onwards, the Son of Man came to give his life.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Two trials and two questions: one about blasphemy and one about kingship - decide whether they are asking the same thing.","Weigh what Jesus says at the trial against what Mark says about the crowd, the priests and Pilate's motive.","Two developed arguments each way, with chapter and verse for each, and a conclusion that says how much of the statement survives."],"codes":["3.2.2.1 The final days in Jerusalem — The trial before the Jewish authorities: 14:53, 57–65","3.2.2.1 The final days in Jerusalem — The trial before Pilate: 15:1–15","3.2.2.1 The final days in Jerusalem — The crucifixion and burial: 15:21–47","3.2.2.1 Significance — Differing beliefs about the significance of Jesus' death and resurrection"]}],"exit":[{"q":"At which place does Peter say 'You are the Messiah', and what does Jesus begin to teach immediately afterwards?","a":"Caesarea Philippi (Mark 8:29); that the Son of Man must suffer, be rejected, be killed and after three days rise (8:31)."},{"q":"Which verse of Mark says the Son of Man came to serve and to give his life as a ransom for many?","a":"Mark 10:45, at the end of the reply to James and John."},{"q":"What does Mark say happened to the temple curtain when Jesus died, and who then confessed him as Son of God?","a":"It was torn in two from top to bottom (Mark 15:38); the Roman centurion said 'this man was the Son of God' (15:39)."},{"q":"How does St Mark's Gospel end at 16:8?","a":"The women flee from the tomb trembling and bewildered and 'said nothing to anyone', because they were afraid."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The beginning of the Gospel (Mark 1:1-13)","back":"'The good news of Jesus Christ' (1:1). John baptises in the wilderness, preaching repentance and promising a baptism with the Holy Spirit (1:8). At Jesus' baptism a voice says 'You are my Son' (1:11); forty days tempted by Satan.","code":"3.2.2.1 The early ministry of Jesus — John's preparation for Jesus' ministry: 1:1–8","open":false},{"front":"The paralysed man (Mark 2:1-12)","back":"Lowered through the roof by four friends. Seeing their faith, Jesus says 'Son, your sins are forgiven' (2:5). The teachers of the law call it blasphemy; the healing proves the Son of Man can forgive sins (2:10).","code":"3.2.2.1 The early ministry of Jesus — The paralysed man: 2:1–12","open":false},{"front":"Jairus' daughter and the rejection at Nazareth (Mark 5:21-24a, 35-43; 6:1-6)","back":"'Don't be afraid; just believe' (5:36); the girl is raised with 'Talitha koum!' (5:41) and silence is ordered. At Nazareth they ask 'Isn't this the carpenter?' (6:3), and he is amazed at their lack of faith.","code":"3.2.2.1 The early ministry of Jesus — Jairus' daughter: 5:21–24a, 35–43","open":false},{"front":"The feeding of the five thousand (Mark 6:30-44)","back":"The crowd are 'like sheep without a shepherd' (6:34). Five loaves and two fish are taken, blessed, broken and given; all eat and twelve basketfuls remain. Read as a nature miracle, a eucharistic sign, or a sharing of food.","code":"3.2.2.1 The early ministry of Jesus — The feeding of the five thousand: 6:30–44","open":false},{"front":"Caesarea Philippi and the transfiguration (Mark 8:27-33; 9:2-9)","back":"Peter says 'You are the Messiah' (8:29); Jesus silences them, teaching the Son of Man must suffer (8:31) and telling Peter 'Get behind me, Satan!' (8:33). On the mountain Moses and Elijah appear; the voice says 'Listen to him!' (9:7).","code":"3.2.2.1 The later ministry of Jesus — The conversation at Caesarea Philippi: 8:27–33","open":false},{"front":"The passion prediction and the request of James and John (Mark 10:32-45)","back":"On the road to Jerusalem he foretells the Son of Man handed over, mocked, flogged, killed and risen (10:33-34). James and John want the best seats; greatness means being 'your servant' (10:43), his life 'a ransom for many' (10:45).","code":"3.2.2.1 The later ministry of Jesus — Jesus' passion prediction: 10:32–34","open":false},{"front":"Bartimaeus and the entry into Jerusalem (Mark 10:46-52; 11:1-11)","back":"Bartimaeus shouts 'Jesus, Son of David, have mercy' (10:47) and is told his faith has healed him. Jesus rides a colt into the city; cloaks are spread and the crowd blesses 'the coming kingdom of our father David' (11:10).","code":"3.2.2.1 The later ministry of Jesus — The entry into Jerusalem: 11:1–11","open":false},{"front":"The Last Supper and Gethsemane (Mark 14:12-26; 14:32-52)","back":"At Passover Jesus says over the bread 'Take it; this is my body' (14:22) and of the cup 'my blood of the covenant' (14:24). In Gethsemane he prays 'take this cup from me' (14:36); all desert him and flee.","code":"3.2.2.1 The final days in Jerusalem — The Last Supper: 14:12–26","open":false},{"front":"The two trials (Mark 14:53, 57-65; 15:1-15)","back":"The witnesses disagree; asked if he is 'the Son of the Blessed One' (14:61), Jesus says 'I am' (14:62) and is condemned for blasphemy. Pilate asks about kingship (15:2), then releases Barabbas to satisfy the crowd (15:15).","code":"3.2.2.1 The final days in Jerusalem — The trial before Pilate: 15:1–15","open":false},{"front":"The crucifixion, burial and empty tomb (Mark 15:21-47; 16:1-8)","back":"The charge reads 'The King of the Jews' (15:26); Jesus cries 'why have you forsaken me?' (15:34), the curtain tears (15:38) and the centurion confesses him (15:39). Joseph buries him; the women find the tomb empty and flee (16:6-8).","code":"3.2.2.1 The final days in Jerusalem — The crucifixion and burial: 15:21–47","open":false},{"front":"An example of Jesus as a miracle worker in St Mark's Gospel","back":"healing the paralysed man, who walks out carrying his mat (2:1-12); raising Jairus' daughter with 'Talitha koum!' (5:41); feeding five thousand people from five loaves and two fish (6:30-44).","code":"3.2.2.1 Significance — St Mark's portrayal of Jesus as a teacher and miracle worker","open":true},{"front":"An example of the messianic secret in St Mark's Gospel","back":"Peter's confession at Caesarea Philippi is silenced (8:30); Jairus' household is told to let no one know about the raising (5:43); the three disciples are told to say nothing about the transfiguration until after the resurrection (9:9).","code":"3.2.2.1 Significance — The titles Son of Man, Son of God, Christ (Messiah) and Son of David","open":true},{"front":"The four titles in St Mark's Gospel","back":"Son of Man: Jesus' own title - authority (2:10), suffering (8:31), glory (14:62). Son of God: the first verse (1:1), the voice (1:11; 9:7), the centurion (15:39). Christ (Messiah): Peter (8:29), the high priest (14:61). Son of David: Bartimaeus (10:47).","code":"3.2.2.1 Significance — The titles Son of Man, Son of God, Christ (Messiah) and Son of David","open":false},{"front":"The five parts of a Theme G question and their marks","back":"1 mark, four options; 2 marks 'Give two ...'; 4 marks 'Explain two contrasting ...' (2+2); 5 marks 'Explain two ...' citing St Mark's Gospel (2+2+1); 12 marks 'Evaluate this statement', plus 3 for SPaG.","code":"3.2.2.1 — The textual studies requirement: the set texts, their interpretation and the authority of the Gospel","open":false},{"front":"The key words of the 12-mark level descriptors","back":"Level 4 (10-12): well-argued, different points of view, chains of reasoning leading to judgement, religion applied. Level 3 (7-9): chains of reasoning, clear reference to religion. Level 2 (4-6): one view reasoned, and the ceiling without religion.","code":"3.2.2.1 — The textual studies requirement: the set texts, their interpretation and the authority of the Gospel","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 4 (Evaluate: 'The most important title in St Mark's Gospel is Son of God.')","items":["The paragraph: The strongest case for the statement is that Mark himself frames his whole Gospel with this title. It stands in the opening verse, it is spoken by God at the baptism, 'You are my Son, whom I love' (1:11), repeated at the transfiguration with the command 'Listen to him!' (9:7), and it is finally said by a human being - a Roman centurion at the cross - who sees how Jesus dies and says 'this man was the Son of God' (15:39). A title given by God at the start and confessed by a Gentile at the end is clearly the one Mark wants his readers to reach. Against this, Son of Man is the title Jesus actually uses of himself, and he uses it at the two points where the Gospel turns: he teaches that 'the Son of Man must suffer' (8:31) and tells the high priest that the Son of Man will be seen 'sitting at the right hand' of God (14:62). It carries both the suffering and the glory, which is exactly the combination Mark is trying to teach, and for 21st century Christians it is the title that reshapes power into service (10:43-45). On balance the two titles do different work and the statement is only half right: Son of God says who Jesus is, and Son of Man says what being that person means in practice. Since Mark's own argument is that the second corrects the crowd's misunderstanding of the first, the title that carries most weight in this Gospel is the one Jesus chose, which makes the statement defensible as a claim about Mark's framing but not about his teaching.","Why this is Level 4: it gives reasoned consideration of different points of view rather than a list; each side is a logical chain built on evidence applied to the issue (the three moments in which Son of God is declared, and the two turning points at which Son of Man is used); the references to the Gospel are used as argument rather than decoration; and the judgement is supported by that evidence and says exactly how far the statement stands. That matches 'A well-argued response, reasoned consideration of different points of view. Logical chains of reasoning leading to judgement(s) supported by knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information. Reference to religion applied to the issue.'"]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 2 (the same statement, visibly weaker)","items":["The paragraph: I think Son of God is the most important title in St Mark's Gospel because it is used at the beginning and at the end, and because Christians believe Jesus is the Son of God, which is the main belief of Christianity. The centurion says it at the cross, which shows that even a Roman soldier could see it. Some people might say Son of Man is more important because Jesus says it a lot, and Son of David and Messiah are used too. But Son of God is still the most important one because it is about who Jesus really is and it is what the creed says.","Why this is only Level 2: it does reach 'Recognition of different points of view, each supported by relevant reasons/evidence', and there is a reference to religion, so it is not capped below that. What it misses is the Level 3 requirement of 'Logical chains of reasoning that draw on knowledge and understanding of relevant evidence and information': the alternative view is named ('Jesus says it a lot') but never reasoned or supported from the text, no passage is cited by chapter and verse, and the conclusion simply repeats the opening claim instead of weighing what has been said. To move up it would need the other titles argued with evidence - Mark 8:31 and 14:62 for Son of Man - and a judgement that follows from that argument."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Set text fact file: the early ministry of Jesus","items":["John's preparation for Jesus' ministry: Mark 1:1-8. The good news of Jesus Christ; a messenger sent ahead; John baptising in the wilderness, preaching repentance for the forgiveness of sins, wearing camel's hair and eating locusts and wild honey; one coming who is 'one more powerful than I' (1:7) and will 'baptise you with the Holy Spirit' (1:8).","Jesus' baptism and temptation: Mark 1:9-13. Jesus comes from Nazareth in Galilee and is baptised by John in the Jordan; heaven is torn open; the Spirit descends like a dove; the voice says 'You are my Son, whom I love' (1:11); the Spirit sends him into the wilderness for forty days, tempted by Satan, with the wild animals and angels attending him.","The paralysed man: Mark 2:1-12. Capernaum; four men dig through the roof; Jesus sees their faith and says 'Son, your sins are forgiven' (2:5); the teachers of the law think it blasphemy, since God alone can forgive sins; the healing proves the Son of Man has 'authority on earth to forgive sins' (2:10); the crowd say they have never seen anything like it.","Jairus' daughter: Mark 5:21-24a, 35-43 (the woman with the haemorrhage inside the story is a Theme H text). Jairus is a synagogue leader; news of the death arrives; 'Don't be afraid; just believe' (5:36); only Peter, James and John go in; the mourners are put out and told she is asleep; 'Talitha koum!' (5:41); she is twelve; strict orders to tell no one, and she is to be given something to eat.","The rejection at Nazareth: Mark 6:1-6. Teaching in his home synagogue; 'Isn't this the carpenter?' and 'Isn't this Mary's son?' (6:3); they take offence; 'a prophet is not without honour' (6:4) except at home, among relatives and in his own house; he could do no miracle there except heal a few sick people; he was amazed at their lack of faith.","The feeding of the five thousand: Mark 6:30-44. A remote place; the crowd are 'like sheep without a shepherd' (6:34) and he teaches them; five loaves and two fish; he looks up to heaven, gives thanks, breaks and gives; all eat and are satisfied; twelve basketfuls of pieces are collected; the men numbered five thousand. Took, blessed, broke, gave - the same four actions as at the Last Supper (14:22)."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Set text fact file: the later ministry of Jesus","items":["The conversation at Caesarea Philippi: Mark 8:27-33. 'Who do people say I am' (8:27) - John the Baptist, Elijah, one of the prophets; Peter: 'You are the Messiah' (8:29); they are warned to tell no one; 'the Son of Man must suffer' (8:31), be rejected by the elders, chief priests and teachers of the law, be killed and after three days rise; Peter rebukes him and hears 'Get behind me, Satan!' (8:33).","The transfiguration of Jesus: Mark 9:2-9. Six days later; a high mountain; Peter, James and John; clothes dazzling white; Elijah and Moses appear; Peter offers three shelters; a cloud, and the voice: 'This is my Son, whom I love' and 'Listen to him!' (9:7); coming down, they must tell no one until the Son of Man has risen from the dead.","Jesus' passion prediction: Mark 10:32-34. On the road up to Jerusalem; the disciples astonished and those following afraid; the Twelve taken aside; the Son of Man will be handed over to the chief priests and teachers of the law, condemned, handed to the Gentiles, mocked, spat on, flogged and killed, and after three days will rise.","The request of James and John: Mark 10:35-45. They ask for the seats at his right and left in glory; can they drink the cup he drinks and share his baptism; the places are not his to give; the ten are indignant; the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over people, but whoever wants to be great 'must be your servant' (10:43); the Son of Man came to serve and to give his life as 'a ransom for many' (10:45).","Bartimaeus: Mark 10:46-52. Leaving Jericho; Bartimaeus son of Timaeus, a blind beggar by the roadside; 'Jesus, Son of David, have mercy' (10:47); the crowd try to silence him and he shouts louder; he throws off his cloak; 'Rabbi, I want to see' (10:51); 'your faith has healed you' (10:52); he follows Jesus along the road. The only healed person in Mark who is named and then follows.","The entry into Jerusalem: Mark 11:1-11. Bethphage and Bethany at the Mount of Olives; a colt no one has ever ridden; cloaks and branches spread on the road; 'Hosanna!' and 'Blessed is he who comes' (11:9) in the name of the Lord; blessed is the coming kingdom of 'our father David' (11:10); he goes into the temple, looks round at everything and, because it was late, leaves for Bethany with the Twelve."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Set text fact file: the final days in Jerusalem","items":["The Last Supper: Mark 14:12-26. The first day of the Festival of Unleavened Bread, when the Passover lamb is sacrificed; a man carrying a jar of water leads them to a large upper room; one of the Twelve eating with him will betray him; 'Take it; this is my body' (14:22); the cup is 'my blood of the covenant' (14:24), poured out for many; he will not drink again until the kingdom of God; they sing a hymn and go out to the Mount of Olives.","Jesus in Gethsemane: Mark 14:32-52. Peter, James and John taken aside; he is deeply distressed and troubled, his soul overwhelmed with sorrow; he prays to 'Abba, Father' that everything is possible, asks him to 'take this cup from me' and then 'yet not what I will' (14:36); three times the disciples sleep; 'the spirit is willing' (14:38) but the flesh is weak; Judas betrays him with a kiss; a bystander cuts off the servant's ear; all desert him and flee, and a young man runs away leaving his linen garment.","The trial before the Jewish authorities: Mark 14:53, 57-65. Taken to the high priest, with the chief priests, elders and teachers of the law assembled; witnesses claim he said he would destroy the temple made with hands and build another not made with hands, but their testimony does not agree; asked whether he is the Messiah, 'the Son of the Blessed One' (14:61), Jesus says 'I am' and speaks of the Son of Man 'sitting at the right hand' (14:62) of the Mighty One and coming on the clouds of heaven; the high priest tears his clothes, calls it blasphemy, and all condemn him as worthy of death; he is spat at, blindfolded and struck.","The trial before Pilate: Mark 15:1-15. The whole Sanhedrin hands him over in the morning; 'Are you the king of the Jews?' (15:2) - 'You have said so'; he then makes no reply and Pilate is amazed; the festival custom of releasing a prisoner; Barabbas, imprisoned with rebels who had committed murder in an uprising; the chief priests stir the crowd; 'Crucify him!' (15:13); Pilate, 'wanting to satisfy the crowd' (15:15), releases Barabbas, has Jesus flogged and hands him over to be crucified.","The crucifixion and burial: Mark 15:21-47. Simon of Cyrene, father of Alexander and Rufus, is forced to carry the cross; Golgotha, the place of the skull; wine mixed with myrrh refused; the clothes divided by lot; the written charge 'The King of the Jews' (15:26); two rebels crucified with him, one on his right and one on his left; passers-by and chief priests mock him; darkness from noon until three; 'Eloi, Eloi, lema sabachthani?' - 'why have you forsaken me?' (15:34), the opening of Psalm 22; he dies with a loud cry; 'the curtain of the temple' (15:38) is torn in two from top to bottom; the centurion says 'this man was the Son of God' (15:39); the women watching include Mary Magdalene and Salome; Joseph of Arimathea, a member of the Council, asks Pilate for the body, wraps it in linen and lays it in a tomb cut out of rock with a stone rolled against the entrance.","The empty tomb: Mark 16:1-8. After the Sabbath, Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James, and Salome buy spices; very early on the first day of the week, just after sunrise, they ask who will roll the stone away; it is already rolled back, and it was very large; a young man in a white robe sits on the right side; 'He has risen! He is not here' (16:6) - see the place where they laid him; go and tell his disciples and Peter that he is going ahead into Galilee; trembling and bewildered they flee and 'said nothing to anyone' (16:8), because they were afraid. The earliest manuscripts end here."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the four titles and the Significance bullets","items":["Son of Man: the title Jesus uses of himself and no one else uses of him in Mark. In Aramaic it can mean simply a human being; in Daniel 7:13 it is a heavenly figure who comes with the clouds and is given authority. Mark uses it for authority (2:10), for suffering (8:31; 10:33-34), for service and ransom (10:45) and for coming glory (14:62).","Son of God: Mark's own claim in the first verse; declared by the voice at the baptism (1:11) and the transfiguration (9:7); confessed by the centurion at the cross (15:39). For 1st century Jews it could mean a king or one specially close to God; for Christians today it is a statement of the incarnation, said weekly in the Nicene Creed.","Christ (Messiah): the anointed one. Many 1st century Jews expected a warrior king to free Israel from Rome. Peter uses it at Caesarea Philippi (8:29) and is immediately silenced; the high priest uses it at the trial (14:61) and Jesus finally accepts it. Jesus redefines it through suffering (8:31), which is the messianic secret in action.","Son of David: the royal, Davidic claim. Shouted by Bartimaeus (10:47) and echoed by the crowd blessing the coming kingdom of 'our father David' (11:10). For Christians today it roots Jesus in Israel and in the Old Testament promises; some argue it now says little, since a hereditary Jewish kingship means little in Britain.","Teacher and miracle worker, and the historicity of the miracles: Mark shows healings, exorcisms, raisings and nature miracles. Contrasting views: they happened as told, since a creator God can act within creation; they are accounts shaped to teach who Jesus is; they have natural explanations (shared food at 6:30-44, a coma at 5:39); they are later additions. David Hume argued in 1748 that testimony for a miracle is always weaker than the evidence for the law it breaks.","The meaning of the Last Supper: Roman Catholic transubstantiation - the substance becomes the body and blood, the appearances remain; Orthodox - a real change, kept as mystery; Lutheran - Christ truly present in, with and under bread and wine; many Anglicans - a real but spiritual presence received by faith; most Baptists and Nonconformists - a memorial of the death; Quakers and the Salvation Army keep no outward sacrament.","The death and resurrection, and explanations of the empty tomb: the death as ransom (10:45), as the blood of a new covenant (14:24), as obedient love and as atonement reconciling humanity to God. Explanations of the tomb: bodily resurrection (with women, whose testimony counted for little, as first witnesses); the wrong tomb; the body moved or stolen; visions; resurrection as a way of saying his cause lives on.","The authority of the Gospel: for most Christians it is inspired scripture and carries God's authority; Roman Catholics read it within Tradition and with the teaching authority of the Church, as the Second Vatican Council set out in 1965; liberal Christians read it as a 1st century document to be translated for a different world. Competing contemporary authorities include science, historical scholarship, Parliament and the courts, medical ethics and public opinion; non-religious people may value it as literature, history or ethics without granting it authority."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan - 'The resurrection is the most important part of St Mark's Gospel.' Evaluate this statement.","items":["Decode the statement: 'most important' means most important to the Gospel's purpose, not most dramatic. So the question is what Mark is chiefly trying to say, and whether eight verses at the end (16:1-8) carry more weight than the passion he spends chapters preparing for.","Timing: about twelve minutes, plus the three SPaG marks that ride on this answer alone. Two developed arguments each way and a real conclusion beat six assertions.","Paragraph 1 (support): everything in Mark points forward to it. Each passion prediction ends with rising after three days (8:31; 10:32-34), and the message at the tomb, 'He has risen! He is not here' (16:6), is what makes the death good news rather than a defeat. Without it the centurion's confession is a tribute to a brave man.","Paragraph 2 (support, developed): Christian belief depends on it. If the crucifixion is the ransom (10:45), the resurrection is God's public verdict that the ransom was accepted, and it is why the earliest followers, who all deserted him in Gethsemane (14:50), became preachers.","Paragraph 3 (different point of view): the proportions of the Gospel argue the other way. Mark gives chapters to the final week and only eight verses to the empty tomb, with no appearance of the risen Jesus in the earliest manuscripts; on this reading the cross is the climax and the tomb is the epilogue.","Paragraph 4 (different point of view, developed): others say the most important part is neither, but the teaching about who Jesus is and what following him costs - Caesarea Philippi (8:27-33) and greatness as service (10:43-45) - since that is what a reader is asked to do something about.","Non-religious view, in one or two sentences: a historian may say the tomb is the least verifiable part of the Gospel and therefore the least important as history, which shows that the argument is about the Gospel's purpose rather than its evidence.","The evaluation thread to hold across the whole answer: importance to whom - to Mark's argument, to Christian doctrine, or to a reader deciding whether to follow?","What the conclusion must say: not that both sides have a point, but which weighs more and why. For example: the cross is the climax Mark builds, but the resurrection is what makes the cross mean what he says it means, so the statement is right about significance and wrong about emphasis - and Mark's abrupt ending is deliberate, because the reader is meant to go to Galilee and find out."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"The rubric of Theme G in one place","items":["The five parts: 1 mark multiple choice (four options, tick one); 2 marks 'Give two ...' (one mark each, first two answers only); 4 marks 'Explain two contrasting ...' or 'Explain two ways in which ... influences ... today' (2 + 2); 5 marks 'Explain two ... You must refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer' (2 + 2 + 1); 12 marks 'Evaluate this statement' with the five bullets, plus 3 SPaG.","On the 4-mark contrasting question, if the two beliefs turn out to be similar only one can be credited, up to two marks. On the influence form, the second mark is only given if the answer says what believers actually do or think differently.","On the 5-mark question the fifth mark is given once, for a relevant and accurate reference to St Mark's Gospel - so cite a passage by chapter and verse and make it visible. A 5-mark answer that only retells the story is capped at two marks.","The 12-mark levels: Level 4 (10-12) a well-argued response with reasoned consideration of different points of view, logical chains of reasoning leading to judgements supported by evidence, reference to religion applied to the issue; Level 3 (7-9) different points of view, logical chains, clear reference to religion; Level 2 (4-6) one point of view reasoned, or different views each with reasons - and the ceiling if religion is never mentioned; Level 1 (1-3) a point of view with a reason.","SPaG on the 12-mark answer: 3 marks for consistent accuracy in spelling and punctuation, effective control of meaning and a wide range of specialist terms; 2 for considerable accuracy and a good range; 1 for reasonable accuracy and a limited range. Specialist terms here include messianic secret, Messiah, atonement, ransom, transubstantiation, blasphemy, Sanhedrin and Passover.","Note the difference between the papers: on the non-textual themes the third bullet of the evaluation reads 'should refer to religious arguments', while on Themes G and H it asks you to refer to St Mark's Gospel.","Textual studies route: Themes G and H are taken together, and Component 1 must include Christianity or Catholic Christianity."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T14:23:50.159184Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Every fix landed. The worked example now reaches a verdict on whether the entry was a royal claim instead of giving exam advice; both rewritten hints stop short of the answer (questions[0].hints[2] no longer names the title of 1:1, questions[1].hints[1] no longer eliminates two options); the new questions[7] on the tearing of the curtain is true to Mark throughout - the Holy of Holies barrier, 'from top to bottom' by God rather than by hands (15:38), the centurion's confession next (15:39), judgement foretold at 13:2, the AD 70 destruction, and the same tearing verb as at the baptism (1:10) - with the correct 4-mark contrasting form, honest hints and codes matching the crucifixion and the significance of the death, and it reproduces no printed stem, which the old Last Supper stem came close to doing. The two Last Supper codes went onto the authority evaluation (questions[14], the 'question 15' of the fixed note counting from one), whose answer already argues from the differing readings of 14:22-24, so all twenty-five ideas are still tested. The rebuilt cards are sound: the merged passages restore the passion prediction, the entry and the trial before Pilate, the titles card's fourteen references all check out, the stems-and-tariffs and level-descriptor cards match levels.md, the two open cards each offer three genuinely correct answers, and all fifteen backs are within the word limits. idea[19] now says the crowd blesses the coming kingdom of our father David without using the title and names Matthew 21:9 correctly. The trimmed solutions (157, 159 and 157 words) keep every marking point. Re-reading the whole kit, I again found no wrong chapter or verse in any Mark citation, no scripture quotation over seven words, every tariff 1, 2, 4, 5 or 12, and the Theme G bullet variant the June 2023 8062/2B paper prints on all three evaluations. Only the Zechariah wording and one card's verse range still need a touch."}}},"3.2.2.2":{"id":"AQA-8062","topic":"3.2.2.2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Theme H is one of the two textual studies themes on Component 2 (8062/2B), and the two go together: if you sit Theme G you sit Theme H as well, and you must also study Christianity or Catholic Christianity in Component 1. The theme sets one five-part question of 1, 2, 4, 5 and 12 marks, and the paper advises 25 minutes on it. What makes Theme H unlike Themes A to F is that the evidence is fixed. Eighteen passages of St Mark's Gospel are printed in the specification by chapter and verse, under three headings - the Kingdom of God, Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society, and faith and discipleship - and the 5-mark question tells you that you must refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer. Marks are lost here by vagueness: 'Jesus healed a man' earns nothing that 'Jesus reached out and touched the man with leprosy (1:41)' would not earn twice over. Learn the passages by their references and the theme becomes the most answerable on the paper.","idea":[{"h":"What the textual studies route demands of every answer","t":"Section 3.2.2 governs Themes G and H together: you take both or neither, and you must also study Christianity or Catholic Christianity in Component 1. It asks you to know the set texts, to see that passages are read differently inside Christianity, and to weigh how far Christian and non-religious communities give St Mark's Gospel authority alongside other sources of contemporary authority - law, science, the state and public opinion. Where Theme G follows the life of Jesus, Theme H reads the same Gospel for what it teaches, under three headings: the Kingdom of God, Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society, and faith and discipleship. A top-level answer therefore never says 'the Bible says'. It names the passage by chapter and verse, says what the event meant for Jesus, for the people of his day and for people in the 21st century, and then argues from it.","code":"3.2.2.2 — The textual studies requirement: the set texts, their interpretation and the authority of the Gospel"},{"h":"The parable of the sower: four soils, one word","t":"Mark 4:1-9 gives the parable and 4:14-20 the explanation; the verses between them are not set. Seed falls on the path, on rocky ground, among thorns and on good soil. The explanation makes the seed the word - 'The farmer sows the word' (4:14) - and the soils four kinds of hearer: those from whom Satan takes the word at once, those who fall away when trouble or persecution comes, those choked by 'the worries of this life', 'the deceitfulness of wealth' and the desire for other things, and those who hear, accept and produce 'thirty, sixty or a hundred times' (4:20). A top-level answer uses the explanation and not just the picture: it names the soil it is talking about, and says what that soil costs a believer now - the friend who drops out when faith becomes unpopular is the rocky ground, and the examiner can see that you have read 4:17.","code":"3.2.2.2 The Kingdom of God — Parable of the sower: 4:1–9, 14–20"},{"h":"The parable of the growing seed: growth you cannot explain, and a harvest","t":"Mark 4:26-29. A man scatters seed, sleeps and rises night and day, and the seed sprouts and grows while 'he does not know how' (4:27). The soil produces by itself - first the stalk, then the head, then the full kernel - and when the grain is ripe he puts the sickle to it, 'because the harvest has come' (4:29). Two claims sit in these four verses: the kingdom grows by God's own power rather than by human effort, and it ends in a harvest, which in the prophets is an image of judgement. That is why this is the most useful parable in the theme: a top-level answer can put it on both sides of the present reality and future hope argument and show that Mark holds the two together rather than choosing.","code":"3.2.2.2 The Kingdom of God — Parable of the growing seed: 4:26–29"},{"h":"The parable of the mustard seed: the smallest beginning, the largest shelter","t":"Mark 4:30-32. The mustard seed is 'the smallest of all seeds' (4:31) on earth, yet when planted it grows into the largest of garden plants, with branches big enough for the birds to perch in its shade. The comparison is about scale and about shelter at the same time: the kingdom begins unimpressively, in Galilee, with twelve followers, and becomes something that gives other people somewhere to rest. Christians read it of the growth of the Church from the first disciples, and of small acts of faith that outgrow themselves. A top-level answer notices that the parable says nothing about how quickly the growth comes, which is why it is used to argue patience quite as often as success.","code":"3.2.2.2 The Kingdom of God — Parable of the mustard seed: 4:30–32"},{"h":"Jesus and the children: the kingdom received, not earned","t":"Mark 10:13-16. People bring children for Jesus to touch and the disciples rebuke them; Jesus is indignant and says 'Let the little children come to me' (10:14), for the kingdom of God belongs to such as these, and whoever will not receive the kingdom 'like a little child' (10:15) will never enter it. He takes them in his arms, puts his hands on them and blesses them. Children in that society had no standing from which to claim anything, so the point is that the kingdom is received as a gift rather than earned by achievement. A top-level answer says what 'like a child' means - trust and dependence, not innocence - and then uses the passage on infant baptism and on the welcome children are given in church today.","code":"3.2.2.2 The Kingdom of God — Jesus and the children: 10:13–16"},{"h":"The rich man: one thing lacking","t":"Mark 10:17-27. A man runs up, calls Jesus 'Good teacher' (10:17) and asks what he must do to inherit eternal life. Jesus lists commandments; the man has kept them since he was a boy; Jesus looks at him and loves him and says 'One thing you lack' (10:21) - go, sell everything, give to the poor, then come and follow. He goes away sad, because he had great wealth. Jesus then says it is easier for a camel to go through 'the eye of a needle' (10:25) than for a rich person to enter the kingdom, and, when the disciples ask who can be saved, that 'all things are possible with God' (10:27). A top-level answer weighs whether the demand is made of this man or of everyone, because that is the hinge of every evaluation on wealth in this theme.","code":"3.2.2.2 The Kingdom of God — The rich man: 10:17–27"},{"h":"The greatest commandment: Jesus is asked for one and gives two","t":"Mark 12:28-34. A teacher of the law asks which commandment is the most important of all. Jesus answers with the Shema - 'Hear, O Israel' (12:29), the Lord our God, the Lord is one - and the command to love him with all your heart, soul, mind and strength, and then adds 'Love your neighbour as yourself' (12:31), saying there is no commandment greater than these. The teacher agrees that this matters more than all burnt offerings, and Jesus tells him he is 'not far from the kingdom of God' (12:34). A top-level answer makes the structural point: asked for one commandment, Jesus gives two and refuses to separate them, which is the standard reply to any statement that loving God is all a Christian has to do.","code":"3.2.2.2 The Kingdom of God — The greatest commandment: 12:28–34"},{"h":"The man with leprosy: the touch is the point","t":"Mark 1:40-45. A man with leprosy kneels and says 'If you are willing' (1:40) you can make me clean. Jesus, moved with compassion, reaches out his hand, touches him and says 'I am willing. Be clean!' (1:41). He sends him to the priest to make the offering Moses commanded (1:44) and tells him to say nothing; the man talks freely instead, so that Jesus can no longer enter a town openly (1:45). Under the law of Leviticus a serious skin disease made a person ritually unclean and required them to live apart from the community, so this man had not been touched for years. A top-level answer explains the exclusion before the healing, because without the exclusion the touch is only kindness rather than the reversal of a social rule.","code":"3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The man with leprosy: 1:40–45"},{"h":"The call of Levi: the meal offends more than the call","t":"Mark 2:13-17. Jesus calls Levi son of Alphaeus from the tax collector's booth with 'Follow me' (2:14), and then eats at his house with many tax collectors and sinners. Tax collectors gathered money for the occupying Roman power and were widely regarded as dishonest, so both the call and the meal were public acts with a public meaning: eating with someone said that you accepted them. When the teachers of the law ask the disciples why he eats with such people, Jesus replies that it is 'not the healthy who need a doctor' (2:17) and that he came to call not the righteous but sinners. A top-level answer names the offence of the table, not just the call, because that is what the question in the passage is actually about.","code":"3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The call of Levi: 2:13–17"},{"h":"The Greek (Syro-Phoenician) woman's daughter: the one argument Jesus appears to lose","t":"Mark 7:24-30. In the region of Tyre a Greek woman, Syro-Phoenician by birth, falls at Jesus' feet and begs him to drive a demon out of her daughter. He answers that the children should be fed first, for it is not right to take the children's bread and toss it to the dogs. She replies that 'even the dogs under the table eat' (7:28) the children's crumbs, and he says 'For such a reply, you may go' (7:29) - the demon has left her daughter. She is a Gentile and a woman, outside the covenant and outside the usual conversation, and Christians read the exchange as the opening of the mission beyond Israel. A top-level answer deals with the hard saying honestly rather than skating past it, and says what her persistence adds to the Gospel's picture of faith.","code":"3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The Greek (Syro-Phoenician) woman's daughter: 7:24–30"},{"h":"The epileptic (demon-possessed) boy: faith that admits it is incomplete","t":"Mark 9:14-29. The disciples cannot drive the spirit out of the boy. The father describes the fits and says, 'if you can do anything' (9:22), take pity on us and help us. Jesus turns the words back on him - 'Everything is possible for one who believes' (9:23) - and the father cries out at once that he does believe and asks Jesus to 'help me overcome my unbelief' (9:24). Jesus rebukes the spirit and lifts the boy up by the hand, and afterwards tells the disciples privately that this kind can come out 'only by prayer' (9:29). Faith here is honest and partial rather than complete. A top-level answer uses this verse against any claim that doubt and faith cannot live in the same person, which is worth more than any general sentence about believing hard enough.","code":"3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The epileptic (demon-possessed) boy: 9:14–29"},{"h":"The widow at the treasury: giving measured by what is kept back","t":"Mark 12:41-44. Jesus sits down opposite the treasury and watches the crowd putting in money. Many rich people throw in large amounts; a poor widow puts in two very small copper coins. He calls his disciples over and says she has put in more than all the others, because they gave out of their wealth while she, out of her poverty, put in 'all she had to live on' (12:44). The measure is not the size of the gift but the size of what is left. A top-level answer can use the passage on both sides of an argument about charity: as the model of sacrificial giving, and as a question about a religious system that takes a widow's last two coins, since Jesus has just condemned those who devour widows' houses (12:40).","code":"3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The widow at the treasury: 12:41–44"},{"h":"The anointing at Bethany: a woman who understands before the Twelve do","t":"Mark 14:1-9. Two days before the Passover, in the house of Simon the Leper at Bethany, a woman breaks an alabaster jar of pure nard and pours it over Jesus' head. Some of those present rebuke her harshly: the perfume could have been sold for more than a year's wages and the money given to the poor. Jesus tells them to leave her alone - 'She has done a beautiful thing' (14:6) - says that she has poured the perfume on his body 'to prepare for my burial' (14:8), and promises that wherever the gospel is preached what she has done will be told 'in memory of her' (14:9). An unnamed woman grasps his death before his closest followers do. A top-level answer sets her beside Judas, who goes to the chief priests in the very next verse.","code":"3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The anointing at Bethany: 14:1–9"},{"h":"The call of the first disciples: at once, and at a cost","t":"Mark 1:16-20. By the Sea of Galilee, Simon and Andrew are casting a net; Jesus says follow me and I will send you out to 'fish for people' (1:17), and 'at once they left their nets' (1:18) and followed him. A little further on, James and John are in the boat with their father Zebedee and the hired men, preparing their nets; he calls them, and they leave their father in the boat and go after him. Mark names the two costs for you: the work that fed them and the family that employed them. A top-level answer uses the immediacy as evidence, because every argument about how literally discipleship should be taken today turns on whether 'at once' is a model to copy or a detail of one first-century call.","code":"3.2.2.2 Faith and discipleship — The call of the first disciples: 1:16–20"},{"h":"The woman with a haemorrhage: faith that acts before it asks","t":"Mark 5:24b-34, the story Mark sets inside the raising of Jairus' daughter and the specification sets as a text in its own right. She has bled for twelve years, has spent all she had on doctors and is worse, not better. She comes up behind Jesus in the crowd, saying to herself 'If I just touch his clothes' (5:28) she will be healed. Jesus knows that power has gone out of him and asks who touched him; she comes trembling and tells him the whole truth, and he says 'Daughter, your faith has healed you' (5:34) and 'Go in peace'. Ritually unclean and unnamed, she is the one person in the Gospel he calls daughter. A top-level answer contrasts her faith, which acts first and explains afterwards, with the father's in 9:24, which admits its own gaps.","code":"3.2.2.2 Faith and discipleship — The woman with a haemorrhage: 5:24b–34"},{"h":"The mission of the Twelve: dependence built into the method","t":"Mark 6:7-13. Jesus sends the Twelve out two by two and gives them authority over impure spirits. They are to take nothing for the journey except a staff - no bread, no bag, no money in their belts - to wear sandals but not an extra shirt, to stay in whatever house receives them, and to shake the dust off their feet as they leave a place that will not welcome them. They preach that people should repent, drive out many demons, and anoint many sick people with oil and heal them. The travel rules are not incidental: they force the disciples to depend on God and on the hospitality of strangers. A top-level answer says what the rules are for and then links them to religious orders, missionaries and gap-year mission teams today.","code":"3.2.2.2 Faith and discipleship — The mission of the Twelve: 6:7–13"},{"h":"The cost and rewards of discipleship: the cross and the hundredfold","t":"Mark 8:34-38 and 10:28-31 are set together, and they belong together. To the crowd with his disciples: whoever wants to be his follower must deny themselves, 'take up their cross and follow me' (8:34); whoever wants to save their life will lose it, and whoever loses their life for Jesus and the gospel will save it; what good is it to gain the whole world 'yet forfeit their soul' (8:36)? To Peter, who says 'We have left everything to follow you!' (10:28): no one who has left home, family or fields for Jesus and the gospel will fail to receive a hundred times as much in this present age - 'along with persecutions' (10:30) - and in the age to come eternal life, for 'many who are first will be last' (10:31). A top-level answer never quotes the reward without the persecutions.","code":"3.2.2.2 Faith and discipleship — The cost and rewards of discipleship: 8:34–38; 10:28–31"},{"h":"Peter's denials: the Gospel's honest picture of a disciple","t":"Mark 14:27-31 and 14:66-72. On the way to the Mount of Olives Jesus says 'You will all fall away' (14:27), and tells Peter that that very night, before the cock crows twice, he will disown him three times. Peter insists that even if he has to die he 'will never disown you' (14:31). In the high priest's courtyard he denies knowing Jesus to a servant girl, denies it again to those standing by, and a third time calls down curses and swears he does not know the man; the cock crows the second time, he remembers, and 'he broke down and wept' (14:72). A top-level answer pairs this with the empty tomb, where the young man tells the women to 'tell his disciples and Peter' (16:7): failure followed by being named again is the Christian argument about forgiveness.","code":"3.2.2.2 Faith and discipleship — Peter's denials: 14:27–31, 66–72"},{"h":"The commission and ascension: sent out, and the ending itself disputed","t":"Mark 16:14-20. Jesus appears to the Eleven as they are eating and rebukes their lack of faith and their stubborn refusal to believe those who had seen him after he had risen. He tells them 'Go into all the world' (16:15) and 'preach the gospel to all creation' (16:15): whoever believes and is baptised will be saved, and whoever does not believe will be condemned, with signs accompanying those who believe. Then he is taken up into heaven and sits at the right hand of God, and the disciples go out and preach everywhere, the Lord working with them (16:20). A top-level answer knows that the earliest surviving manuscripts of Mark end at 16:8, which is precisely the kind of textual evidence a question about the Gospel's authority is asking for.","code":"3.2.2.2 Faith and discipleship — The commission and ascension: 16:14–20"},{"h":"Three audiences: Jesus, his contemporaries, and the 21st century","t":"The specification asks for the significance and importance of key events in the life of Jesus recorded in St Mark for Jesus himself, for the people of his day and for people in the 21st century. Those three audiences are a ready-made structure for any set passage. The anointing at Bethany prepared Jesus for burial (14:8); to those watching it looked like a year's wages poured on the floor; for Christians now it is the passage that defends costly worship against the charge of waste. A top-level answer works all three, and it is the third that earns the marks for application, because that is where a mark scheme asks what difference the passage makes to how someone lives.","code":"3.2.2.2 Significance — The significance and importance of key events in the life of Jesus recorded in St Mark"},{"h":"Four ways of understanding the Kingdom of God","t":"The specification names four readings, in two pairs: the kingdom as a present reality and as a future hope, and the kingdom as a personal inner state and as a community. Each can be argued from the set parables. Jesus' first words in Mark are that the kingdom 'has come near' (1:15); the growing seed ends at the harvest (4:29); the child receives the kingdom personally (10:15); the mustard seed becomes a plant in whose shade birds shelter (4:32). Because all four can be supported from the same six passages, this is the richest 12-mark material in the theme. A top-level answer takes two readings, gives each its passage, and then says which the Gospel as a whole supports better and why.","code":"3.2.2.2 Significance — Different ways in which the Kingdom of God might be understood"},{"h":"Why these people were disregarded, and why Jesus was different","t":"The specification asks for the reasons on both sides. On one side: a serious skin disease made a person ritually unclean under the law of Leviticus and required them to live apart; tax collectors collected for the occupying power and were assumed to cheat; Gentiles stood outside the covenant; a woman with a continuous haemorrhage counted as ritually unclean; a widow with no husband had no income and no protector. On the other side, Jesus gives his own reason: it is 'not the healthy who need a doctor' (2:17), and he came to call sinners. A top-level answer gives the first-century reason before the healing or the meal, because the marks lie in the contrast between the two, not in retelling the story.","code":"3.2.2.2 Significance — Reasons for 1st century attitudes and those of Jesus to those disregarded by society"},{"h":"What Jesus' attitudes to the disregarded change for Christians","t":"This is the influence strand of the theme (AO1:2), and influence means what people actually do differently. Christians have built hospitals and hospices: St Christopher's Hospice was opened in 1967 by Dame Cicely Saunders, a Christian, and began the modern hospice movement. Parishes run food banks and street pastor schemes; CAFOD, the Catholic aid agency for England and Wales, Christian Aid and Tearfund work with the poorest overseas; the Missionaries of Charity, founded by Mother Teresa in 1950, care for the dying poor. The argument runs further, to the claim that the Church must side with the excluded in its own society. A top-level answer names the practice and then says what is done differently because of the passage, since that is where the second mark on an influence question is won.","code":"3.2.2.2 Significance — The significance and importance for Christians of Jesus' attitudes to those disregarded"},{"h":"Different views on what discipleship means now","t":"The specification asks for different views on the significance and importance of discipleship both for Jesus' disciples and for 21st century Christians, drawn from the incidents - the call by the lake (1:16-20), the mission of the Twelve (6:7-13), the request of James and John (10:35-45), Peter's denials - and from the teaching about the cross (8:34-38). The divergence is over how literally the cost is to be taken today: members of religious orders take vows of poverty, chastity and obedience and read the demand as written, while most Christians read it as a matter of priority - Jesus must come first when there is a clash. A top-level answer names the two readings and then decides between them with evidence from Mark, rather than concluding that it depends on the person.","code":"3.2.2.2 Significance — Different views on the significance and importance of discipleship"},{"h":"Different views on the nature and importance of faith","t":"Mark's examples of faith are not all alike, which is exactly what the specification wants you to notice. Four men dig through a roof and 'When Jesus saw their faith' (2:5) he forgives the paralysed man - faith shown by other people, and shown by effort. A woman touches a cloak in a crowd (5:28) - faith as trust that acts. A father says he believes and asks for help with his unbelief (9:24) - faith that is partial and admits it. Bartimaeus shouts louder when told to be quiet and is told 'your faith has healed you' (10:52) - faith as persistence. At Nazareth Jesus is amazed at 'their lack of faith' (6:6). A top-level answer defines faith from one passage and then tests that definition against another.","code":"3.2.2.2 Significance — Different views on the nature and importance of faith"},{"h":"The authority of Jesus' teaching against secular authority","t":"The final bullet asks how far Jesus' teaching, as Mark records it, still carries authority beside the secular sources people live by: the law, science, the state and public opinion. Christians differ. Some treat the Gospel as directly inspired and binding word for word; Catholic teaching reads Scripture within the Church's tradition and under the teaching authority of the Magisterium; the Church of England holds that Scripture contains all things necessary to salvation; liberal Christians take some passages as carrying spiritual truth rather than reporting fact, particularly the exorcisms. Non-religious critics point out that Mark was written decades after the events, that the earliest manuscripts end at 16:8, and that morality does not need a divine source. A top-level answer argues from evidence about the text and its use, not from whether religion is a good thing.","code":"3.2.2.2 Significance — Differing views on the authority of Jesus' teaching as recorded by St Mark"}],"examples":[{"title":"The one-mark multiple choice: eliminate, then tick","setup":"The question reads: Which one of the following did Jesus tell the man he had healed of leprosy to do? A Follow him as a disciple. B Sell everything he had. C Show himself to the priest. D Tell everyone in the town. You put a tick in the box next to the correct answer, and there is one mark for it.","steps":["Place the passage before you read the options: this is the healing at the start of the Gospel (1:40-45), where the man kneels and asks to be made clean.","Clear the option that belongs to a different set text - being told to sell everything and give to the poor is said to the rich man (10:21), not to this man.","Clear the option that belongs to the call narratives - 'Follow me' is said to Levi at the tax booth (2:14) and to the fishermen by the lake (1:17).","Clear the remaining wrong option by remembering what Jesus actually forbade: he sent the man away with a strong warning to say nothing to anyone, and the man ignored it.","One option is left, and it is what the text says: Jesus told him to show himself to the priest and make the offering Moses commanded (1:44), so tick C."],"cues":["Which set passage is this question about?","Which option is a command Jesus gives to somebody else in the specification's list?","Which option is the wording of a call to discipleship rather than a healing?","What did Jesus tell the man not to do?","Which option survives, and where in the passage is it?"]},{"title":"The two-mark question: two separate points, nothing more","setup":"The question reads: Give two things Jesus said to the woman who had been subject to bleeding for twelve years. There are two marks, one for each of two correct points, and only the first two answers you write are marked.","steps":["Fix the passage: this is the woman who touches his cloak in the crowd (5:24b-34), and the question asks only for the words Jesus speaks to her, not for what she does.","Take the first mark with his opening words, which name her: he calls her daughter and says her faith has healed her (5:34).","Take the second mark with a separate saying from the same verse rather than a rewording of the first: he tells her to go in peace (5:34).","Stop writing. A third point earns nothing, and a sentence of explanation after the first point cannot rescue a wrong second point, so two short lines are the whole answer."],"cues":["Which passage is this, and whose words are being asked for?","What are the first words Jesus says to her when she tells him the truth?","What is a second saying, not simply the first one reworded?","Is there anything left to gain by writing a third point?"]},{"title":"The four-mark contrasting question: make the two views genuinely differ","setup":"The question reads: Explain two contrasting Christian beliefs about the importance of Jesus blessing the children. There are four marks, two for each belief: a simple explanation of a relevant and accurate contrast earns one mark and a detailed explanation earns two, and if two similar beliefs are given only one of them can be credited.","steps":["Set the first belief down plainly: many Christians read the passage as teaching that the kingdom is received as a gift, since Jesus says it belongs to such as these (10:14).","Develop it into the second mark with a consequence, not a repetition: this is one reason Catholics and Anglicans baptise infants, because the child receives grace before being able to earn anything.","Choose the second belief so that it genuinely opposes the first, not so that it agrees in different words: Baptists and most Pentecostals read the passage as a blessing of children rather than a rule about baptism.","Develop the second belief in the same way: they hold that baptism follows a personal profession of faith, so they dedicate infants and baptise believers later, reading 10:16 as Jesus blessing children, not admitting them.","Check the contrast is visible in the wording by opening the paragraphs with 'Many Christians believe' and 'Other Christians, such as Baptists, believe', so the examiner can see two positions and not one."],"cues":["What is the plainest thing this passage is taken to teach?","What does that belief make a church actually do?","Which Christians read the same passage in a different way?","What practice follows from their reading?","Would a reader see two different positions, or the same one twice?"]},{"title":"The five-mark question: two developed points and an unmistakable reference","setup":"The question reads: Explain two things Christians might learn from the parable of the mustard seed. You must refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer. There are five marks: two for each thing learned, one mark for a simple explanation and two for a detailed one, plus a separate mark for a relevant and accurate reference to St Mark's Gospel.","steps":["Write the first lesson as a point, then develop it: Christians learn that small beginnings are not failures, because Jesus compares the kingdom to a seed that is the smallest of all and yet outgrows every garden plant.","Show the development doing work, which is what turns one mark into two: a congregation of a dozen people, or one person's quiet act of service, is not evidence that God is absent.","Write the second lesson so that it is a different idea rather than the same one enlarged: the grown plant exists for others, since its branches give the birds somewhere to shelter.","Develop the second lesson with a consequence: a church that has grown is meant to be a shelter, which is the reasoning behind parish food banks and night shelters.","Bank the fifth mark on its own by naming the text exactly - the parable of the mustard seed, Mark 4:30-32, with the phrase 'the smallest of all seeds' - rather than leaving the Gospel implied."],"cues":["What is the first lesson, in one sentence?","What would that lesson stop a discouraged believer concluding?","What second idea is in the parable that the first lesson has not used?","What does a church do because of that second idea?","What still has to appear before the answer is finished?"]},{"title":"The twelve-mark evaluation: build both sides, then judge","setup":"The question reads: 'Peter's denials show that a disciple's faith always fails under pressure.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","steps":["Decode the statement into the claim you must test: not whether Peter failed, which the text settles, but whether his failure is the rule for all disciples.","Build the supporting case from the Gospel: Peter promises he will never disown Jesus (14:31), then denies him three times in the courtyard, and Jesus had already said they would all fall away (14:27).","Strengthen it with a second strand rather than repeating the first: the disciples also sleep in Gethsemane and desert him, so the pattern is not one man's weakness.","Build the opposing case to the same standard: the women stay at the cross, the young man at the tomb sends word to 'tell his disciples and Peter' (16:7), and the Eleven are commissioned and preach everywhere (16:20).","Add the non-religious voice the bullets allow: a critic may say the story is included precisely because the early Church needed to explain a leader's failure, which is a reading about the text rather than about faith.","Judge, and say why: the passage shows faith failing and being restored, so it is evidence against the word 'always' - which is the single word the statement stands or falls on."],"cues":["What exactly does the statement claim, beyond saying that Peter failed?","Which verses support the claim most directly?","What second piece of evidence supports it without repeating the first?","What in the Gospel tells against it?","What might someone who does not share the faith say about why this story is told?","Which word in the statement decides the verdict?"]}],"check":[{"q":"In the parable of the sower, what are the three things that choke the word among the thorns?","a":"The worries of this life, the deceitfulness of wealth, and the desire for other things (4:19)."},{"q":"Which four ways of understanding the Kingdom of God does the specification name?","a":"A present reality; a future hope; a personal inner state; a community."},{"q":"Why was it remarkable that Jesus touched the man with leprosy?","a":"A serious skin disease made a person ritually unclean under the law and forced them to live apart, so the man was untouchable; Jesus touched him before healing him (1:41)."},{"q":"What did Jesus say the poor widow had put into the treasury?","a":"More than all the others, because out of her poverty she put in 'all she had to live on' (12:44), while they gave out of their wealth."}]},"room":{"facts":["The eighteen set passages sit under three headings: the Kingdom of God, Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society, and faith and discipleship - six passages each.","The 5-mark question on Theme H carries the instruction that you must refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer, and that reference is worth one separate mark.","The Kingdom of God is to be understood in four ways the specification names: as a present reality, as a future hope, as a personal inner state and as a community.","Every set passage should be answered for three audiences: what it meant for Jesus, for the people of his day, and for people in the 21st century.","Chapter and verse are the currency of this theme: sower 4:1-9 and 4:14-20, growing seed 4:26-29, mustard seed 4:30-32, children 10:13-16, rich man 10:17-27, greatest commandment 12:28-34.","The disregarded: leprosy 1:40-45, Levi 2:13-17, the Greek woman 7:24-30, the epileptic boy 9:14-29, the widow 12:41-44, the anointing 14:1-9. Faith and discipleship: 1:16-20, 5:24b-34, 6:7-13, 8:34-38 with 10:28-31, 14:27-31 with 14:66-72, and 16:14-20."],"questions":[{"q":"Which one of the following did the poor widow put into the temple treasury? A An alabaster jar of perfume B More than a year's wages C Two very small copper coins D A staff and a bag","a":"AO1:1. Answer: C Two very small copper coins.","sol":"C Two very small copper coins. Mark 12:42 says a poor widow put in two very small copper coins while many rich people threw in large amounts, and Jesus said she had given more than all of them because she gave 'all she had to live on' (12:44). The alabaster jar of perfume worth more than a year's wages belongs to the anointing at Bethany (14:3-5), and the staff and bag belong to the instructions for the mission of the Twelve (6:8).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Three of the four options come from other set passages in this theme, so ask which passage each option belongs to.","Two options belong together in a single verse about what the Twelve were and were not to carry on a journey.","One option is a quantity of money large enough to be argued over, which is the opposite of what makes this story remarkable."],"codes":["3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The widow at the treasury: 12:41–44"]},{"q":"Which one of the following does Jesus say the kingdom of God must be received like? A A farmer sowing seed B A hired servant C A little child D A teacher of the law","a":"AO1:1. Answer: C A little child.","sol":"C A little child. In Mark 10:15 Jesus says that anyone who will not receive the kingdom of God 'like a little child' will never enter it, after the disciples had rebuked the people bringing children to him. The farmer sowing seed and the hired servants appear in the parables of chapter 4 and in the call of James and John (1:20), and the teacher of the law is the man who asks about the greatest commandment (12:28).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The question is about how the kingdom is received, so look for the option that describes someone who cannot earn or claim anything.","Two options describe people doing paid work, which is the opposite of receiving a gift.","One option describes a religious expert, and Jesus tells that man only that he is not far from the kingdom."],"codes":["3.2.2.2 The Kingdom of God — Jesus and the children: 10:13–16"]},{"q":"Give two of the four kinds of ground on which the seed fell in the parable of the sower.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points, first two answers only: the path; rocky ground; among thorns; good soil.","sol":"The path, where birds ate the seed, and rocky ground, where it sprang up quickly and withered for lack of root (4:4-6). Thorns, which choked the plants, and good soil, which produced a crop, would also each earn a mark (4:7-8).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Picture the seed leaving the sower's hand and landing in four different places, worst first.","The explanation in 4:14-20 names the four hearers in the same order, so recalling one list gives the other.","Only two answers are marked, so write two of the four and stop."],"codes":["3.2.2.2 The Kingdom of God — Parable of the sower: 4:1–9, 14–20"]},{"q":"Give two things Jesus told the Twelve not to take with them on their mission.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points: bread; a bag; money in their belts; an extra shirt.","sol":"No bread and no bag (6:8). No money in their belts and no second shirt would also each earn a mark (6:8-9). They were allowed a staff and sandals, so neither of those can be offered as an answer.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The instructions are about what makes a traveller independent of the people they visit.","Two of the forbidden items are about food and carrying, and one is about money.","Be careful to give forbidden items rather than the two things they were allowed."],"codes":["3.2.2.2 Faith and discipleship — The mission of the Twelve: 6:7–13"]},{"q":"Give two commandments Jesus named when he was asked which commandment is the most important of all.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points: love the Lord your God with all your heart, soul, mind and strength; love your neighbour as yourself.","sol":"Love the Lord your God with all your heart, soul, mind and strength, which Jesus quotes from the Shema after 'Hear, O Israel' (12:29-30). Love your neighbour as yourself, of which he says there is no commandment greater than these (12:31).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Jesus is asked for one commandment and does not give only one.","The first answer he gives is the prayer Jewish people recite, beginning with a call to Israel to listen.","The second names a person other than God."],"codes":["3.2.2.2 The Kingdom of God — The greatest commandment: 12:28–34"]},{"q":"Give two things Peter did in the courtyard of the high priest.","a":"AO1:1. One mark for each of two correct points: denied knowing Jesus three times; denied it to a servant girl and then to bystanders; called down curses and swore; wept when the cock crowed the second time.","sol":"He denied knowing Jesus three times, first to a servant girl who recognised him and then to those standing nearby (14:66-71). He broke down and wept when the cock crowed the second time and he remembered what Jesus had said (14:72).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Mark tells you who challenged Peter as well as what he said, and either can earn a mark.","The scene ends with a sound and with Peter's reaction to it.","Two points, each in a short sentence, are the whole answer."],"codes":["3.2.2.2 Faith and discipleship — Peter's denials: 14:27–31, 66–72"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting Christian beliefs about the meaning of the parable of the growing seed.","a":"AO1:3. First belief and second belief, each 1 mark simple, 2 marks detailed. Growth comes by God's power alone, so believers wait and trust; against: the harvest is judgement, so believers must work and be ready. Similar beliefs credited once only.","sol":"Many Christians read the parable as teaching that the kingdom grows by God's power and not by human effort. The farmer sleeps and rises night and day and the seed grows while 'he does not know how' (4:27), so Christians are meant to sow faithfully and leave the results to God rather than judging a church by its numbers. Other Christians take the emphasis to fall on the ending rather than the growing: when the grain is ripe the man puts the sickle to it 'because the harvest has come' (4:29), and harvest in the prophets is an image of judgement. On that reading the parable is a warning that time is limited, which is why it is used in preaching about repentance and about being ready.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The parable has two movements: something grows quietly, and then something is cut.","A contrast has to oppose, so pick readings that pull the believer in different directions - one towards patience, the other towards urgency.","Each belief needs a consequence as well as a statement, because the second mark is for the development."],"codes":["3.2.2.2 The Kingdom of God — Parable of the growing seed: 4:26–29","3.2.2.2 Significance — Different ways in which the Kingdom of God might be understood"]},{"q":"Explain two contrasting Christian beliefs about the importance of the anointing at Bethany.","a":"AO1:3. Each belief 1 mark simple, 2 marks detailed. Costly worship offered to Jesus is right and is defended by him; against, the objectors' view that resources belong to the poor, and the reading that the passage is about his burial rather than about worship.","sol":"Many Christians treat the passage as a defence of costly worship. Jesus says 'She has done a beautiful thing' (14:6) and refuses to let the woman be scolded, so beautiful buildings, music and vestments are held to be a proper offering rather than money wasted. Other Christians give weight to the objection Mark records, that the perfume could have been sold for more than a year's wages and given to the poor (14:5). Quakers worship very plainly because of their testimony to simplicity, and some Christians argue from 14:5 that what is spent on display would be better spent on relief; they point out that Jesus says the poor will always be with them, which they read as a fact to be acted on rather than an excuse.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Mark gives you the argument itself: two groups of people in the room disagree about the same act.","One side is about what is owed to Jesus and the other about what is owed to the poor.","Naming a church that worships plainly turns a simple explanation into a detailed one."],"codes":["3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The anointing at Bethany: 14:1–9"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which Jesus' treatment of those disregarded by society influences Christians today.","a":"AO1:2. Each way 1 mark simple, 2 marks detailed; the second mark only if the influence - what Christians actually do - is included. Credit any accurate way: care for the sick after 1:40-45; welcome at the table after 2:13-17; work with outsiders and refugees after the Greek woman's daughter, 7:24-30; giving measured by sacrifice after 12:41-44.","sol":"One influence is medical and social care for people others avoid. Because Jesus touched the man with leprosy rather than healing him at a distance (1:41), Christians have founded hospitals and hospices: St Christopher's Hospice was opened in 1967 by Dame Cicely Saunders, a Christian, and began the modern hospice movement, and Christian volunteers staff night shelters and food banks. A second influence is in who a church makes welcome. Because Jesus ate with tax collectors and sinners and said it is 'not the healthy who need a doctor' (2:17), many churches deliberately include people others exclude - running groups for prisoners' families, refugees or people in recovery - on the grounds that a congregation that only admits the respectable is not doing what Jesus did.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["An influence question is answered with what people do, not only with what they believe.","Take one passage about healing and one about who is welcomed at a meal.","Name a real organisation or activity, then say which passage it is a response to."],"codes":["3.2.2.2 Significance — The significance and importance for Christians of Jesus' attitudes to those disregarded","3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The man with leprosy: 1:40–45","3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The call of Levi: 2:13–17","3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The Greek (Syro-Phoenician) woman's daughter: 7:24–30"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which the parable of the sower influences Christians today.","a":"AO1:2. Each way 1 mark simple, 2 marks detailed; the influence must be stated for the second mark.","sol":"One influence is self-examination. The thorns are 'the worries of this life', 'the deceitfulness of wealth' and the desire for other things (4:19), so Christians use the parable to ask which of those is choking their own faith, and Lent is kept partly by giving up the things that crowd prayer out. A second influence is on how Christians evangelise. Because the sower scatters widely and only some seed produces a crop, churches expect a mixed response to preaching and mission and are not discouraged when most people are uninterested; groups such as Alpha invite anyone rather than only those thought likely to respond, since the parable says the yield cannot be predicted from the sowing.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The explanation of the parable in 4:14-20 names the dangers for you, so use them rather than the picture of the field.","One influence is about the believer's own life; the other is about what the church does to other people.","The second mark comes from saying what changes, not from retelling the soils."],"codes":["3.2.2.2 The Kingdom of God — Parable of the sower: 4:1–9, 14–20"]},{"q":"Explain two lessons Christians might learn from the call of Levi. You must refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer.","a":"AO1:1, AO1:2. Each lesson 1 mark simple, 2 marks detailed, plus 1 mark for a relevant and accurate reference to St Mark's Gospel.","sol":"The first lesson is that no one's past disqualifies them. Levi collected taxes for the occupying Roman power and was assumed to be dishonest, yet Jesus called him from the booth with 'Follow me' (2:14), so Christians hold that a person's history is not a reason to keep them out of the Church, which is why prison chaplaincies and recovery groups matter to congregations. The second lesson is that acceptance has to be public. Jesus did not merely call Levi; he ate at his house with many tax collectors and sinners, and when challenged said he came to call sinners rather than the righteous (2:17). Sharing a table said publicly that these people were accepted, so Christians argue that welcome must be visible - sitting with people, not sending help from a distance. Reference: Mark 2:13-17.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Mark records two separate actions here, and the second caused more offence than the first.","For each lesson, say what it makes a Christian do now as well as what it says about Jesus.","One mark is held back for the Gospel reference, so the passage has to be named as well as used."],"codes":["3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The call of Levi: 2:13–17","3.2.2.2 Significance — Reasons for 1st century attitudes and those of Jesus to those disregarded by society"]},{"q":"Explain two things Christians might learn about faith from the father of the epileptic boy. You must refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer.","a":"AO1:1, AO1:2. Each thing 1 mark simple, 2 marks detailed, plus 1 mark for the reference to St Mark's Gospel.","sol":"The first thing is that faith need not be complete to be real. The father says, 'if you can do anything' (9:22), and when Jesus answers that 'Everything is possible for one who believes' (9:23) he cries out that he does believe and asks Jesus to 'help me overcome my unbelief' (9:24). Christians take this as permission to pray honestly while doubting, which is why doubt is not treated as the opposite of faith in most Christian teaching; his faith is not like the woman with a haemorrhage, who simply acts, saying 'If I just touch his clothes' (5:28), and is told 'Daughter, your faith has healed you' (5:34). The second thing is that faith is sustained by prayer rather than by effort of will. When the disciples ask privately why they could not drive the spirit out, Jesus tells them this kind comes out 'only by prayer' (9:29), so Christians treat regular prayer as the ordinary means by which faith is kept alive rather than as an emergency measure. Reference: Mark 9:14-29.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The father's most famous sentence contains two opposite things at once - start there.","The second lesson comes from the private conversation after the crowd has gone.","Say what a Christian does differently because of each lesson, and name the passage."],"codes":["3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The epileptic (demon-possessed) boy: 9:14–29","3.2.2.2 Significance — Different views on the nature and importance of faith","3.2.2.2 Faith and discipleship — The woman with a haemorrhage: 5:24b–34"]},{"q":"Explain two things Christians might learn from the widow at the treasury. You must refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer.","a":"AO1:1, AO1:2. Each thing 1 mark simple, 2 marks detailed, plus 1 mark for the reference to St Mark's Gospel.","sol":"The first thing is that God measures a gift by what is kept back. Many rich people threw in large amounts and the widow put in two very small copper coins, yet Jesus said she had put in more than all of them because she gave 'all she had to live on' (12:44). Christians therefore judge their giving as a proportion of what they have, which is the reasoning behind tithing and behind Christian Aid Week collections that ask for sacrifice rather than surplus. The second thing is that God notices what nobody else does. Jesus was sitting opposite the treasury watching the crowd, and only he saw the widow; Christians take from this that small acts of faithfulness done unseen are not wasted, and that the Church should pay attention to the people at the edges of its own congregations. Reference: Mark 12:41-44.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Compare the two amounts and then compare what each giver had left afterwards.","The second lesson is in what Jesus is doing at the start of the passage, before anyone gives anything.","Turn each lesson into a practice, and name the passage for the fifth mark."],"codes":["3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The widow at the treasury: 12:41–44","3.2.2.2 Significance — The significance and importance for Christians of Jesus' attitudes to those disregarded"]},{"q":"'The kingdom of God is a future hope, not a present reality.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, marked by levels on the whole answer. Support: the harvest of 4:29, the growth of the mustard seed, the age to come and eternal life in 10:30. Other views: 'has come near' (1:15), 'not far from the kingdom' (12:34), the kingdom belonging to children (10:14). Judgement required.","sol":"There is real force in reading the kingdom as future. The parable of the growing seed ends at harvest: when the grain is ripe the man puts the sickle to it 'because the harvest has come' (4:29), and harvest in the prophets is a standing image of judgement, so the parable points forward to something completed rather than something finished. The mustard seed works the same way: the whole comparison is with what the smallest of seeds becomes (4:31-32), not with what it already is. Jesus' promise to Peter also divides time in two, offering a hundredfold in this present age 'along with persecutions' (10:30) and eternal life only in the age to come. On this reading Christians live in hope, which is why the Lord's Prayer asks for the kingdom to come and why the Church has always prayed for a world it does not yet see.\n\nAgainst this, Mark opens the ministry with Jesus announcing that the kingdom 'has come near' (1:15) and calling for repentance now, not later. He tells the teacher of the law who agrees about the two greatest commandments that he is 'not far from the kingdom of God' (12:34) - said to a living man, about his present state. He says the kingdom belongs to the children being brought to him (10:14), and drives out demons as evidence that God's rule has arrived. Many Christians therefore describe the kingdom as inaugurated: genuinely present wherever people are healed, forgiven and included, and still awaiting completion. The four readings the specification names fit here too, because a kingdom that is a personal inner state is necessarily present, while a kingdom that is a community grows through history.\n\nA non-religious reply is worth noting. A critic may argue that a kingdom permanently postponed is unfalsifiable: the harvest can always be said to be coming, and the claim costs nothing. A humanist would add that the good these parables describe - shelter, welcome, generosity - can be worked for without any expectation of a harvest at the end.\n\nThe statement is too tidy. Mark's own evidence will not let the two halves be separated: the same short parable has both a growth nobody can explain and a harvest that has not yet come. The word that breaks the statement is 'not'. A fair conclusion is that the kingdom in Mark is a present reality that is incomplete, and a future hope grounded in what is already happening - and that this is why the Gospel expects Christians both to act now and to wait.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first what the statement is denying, because the argument is about the word 'not' rather than about the word 'future'.","Take one parable that seems to prove the statement and look for the half of it that does not.","Give each side two pieces of evidence with chapter and verse before you start weighing them, and keep the judgement for the end."],"codes":["3.2.2.2 Significance — Different ways in which the Kingdom of God might be understood","3.2.2.2 The Kingdom of God — Parable of the growing seed: 4:26–29","3.2.2.2 The Kingdom of God — Parable of the mustard seed: 4:30–32","3.2.2.2 — The textual studies requirement: the set texts, their interpretation and the authority of the Gospel"]},{"q":"'Christians today should give away their wealth, as Jesus told the rich man to.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels on the whole answer. Support: 'One thing you lack' (10:21), the camel and the needle (10:25), the widow (12:44), the mission of the Twelve (6:8). Other views: the demand was personal, 'all things are possible with God' (10:27), the anointing (14:6), the practical need for givers. Judgement required.","sol":"The case for the statement is strong and it comes straight from the text. Jesus looks at the man, loves him, and says 'One thing you lack' (10:21) before telling him to sell everything, give to the poor and follow. He then generalises: it is easier for a camel to go through 'the eye of a needle' (10:25) than for a rich person to enter the kingdom, which is not a comment about one man's character but about wealth itself. The pattern runs through the Gospel. The Twelve are sent out with no bread, no bag and no money in their belts (6:8), and the widow is praised for giving 'all she had to live on' (12:44) while the rich are not praised for giving more. Peter speaks for the Twelve when he says 'We have left everything to follow you!' (10:28), and Jesus does not correct him but promises a hundred times as much in this age 'along with persecutions' (10:30), so leaving possessions behind is treated as the normal shape of discipleship rather than as one man's special case. Religious orders have taken this literally for centuries through vows of poverty, and the Church's teaching on the preferential option for the poor grows from the same root.\n\nThere is a serious case on the other side. The command in Mark is addressed to one man who has kept the commandments and is still not free, and Jesus does not repeat it to anyone else: Simon and Andrew leave their nets 'at once' (1:18) and James and John leave their father in the boat with the hired men (1:20), but none of them is told to sell what they own and give the money away. When the disciples ask in alarm who can be saved, Jesus does not answer that everyone must sell up but that 'all things are possible with God' (10:27), which locates salvation in God rather than in a transaction. At Bethany he defends a woman who pours away perfume worth more than a year's wages instead of selling it for the poor, saying 'She has done a beautiful thing' (14:6). Practically, a church with no money runs no food bank; Christian Aid, CAFOD and Tearfund exist because Christians earn and give rather than because they are all destitute.\n\nA non-religious argument can be added on either side: economists point out that giving away capital can reduce the good done over a lifetime, while others argue that extreme inequality is itself the moral problem and that a call to redistribute is simply right, with or without religion.\n\nThe better conclusion is that the statement takes a demand made to one man and makes a rule of it, but that the softer reading concedes too little. Mark clearly treats wealth as dangerous, not neutral: it is what choked the seed among the thorns (4:19) and what sent the rich man away sad. So the honest position is that Christians are not all commanded to give everything away, but that every Christian is commanded to know what they would refuse to give up - because that, in Mark, is the thing that keeps a person out.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Notice who the command in 10:21 is spoken to, and then notice who in the Gospel is not given it.","The strongest evidence for the statement is not the command itself but what Jesus says immediately afterwards about rich people in general.","Build both sides from passages before judging, and let the conclusion take a position rather than splitting the difference."],"codes":["3.2.2.2 The Kingdom of God — The rich man: 10:17–27","3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The widow at the treasury: 12:41–44","3.2.2.2 Significance — Different views on the significance and importance of discipleship","3.2.2.2 Significance — The significance and importance of key events in the life of Jesus recorded in St Mark","3.2.2.2 Faith and discipleship — The call of the first disciples: 1:16–20","3.2.2.2 Faith and discipleship — The cost and rewards of discipleship: 8:34–38; 10:28–31"]},{"q":"'St Mark's Gospel has no authority over people's lives today.' Evaluate this statement. In your answer you: should give reasoned arguments in support of this statement; should give reasoned arguments to support a different point of view; should refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer; may refer to non-religious arguments; should reach a justified conclusion. [12 marks] [Plus SPaG 3 marks]","a":"AO2, levels on the whole answer. Support: written decades later, earliest manuscripts end at 16:8, exorcisms read as first-century medicine, law and science as rival authorities. Other views: inspiration, the Gospel read at every Eucharist, its continuing effect on charity and care, Catholic and Anglican teaching on Scripture. Judgement required.","sol":"Much can be said for the statement. Mark was written decades after the events it describes, so a critic argues it records what a community believed rather than what a reporter saw. The manuscript evidence is a real difficulty at exactly the point where authority is claimed: the earliest surviving manuscripts of Mark end at 16:8, so the commission to 'Go into all the world' (16:15) and the ascension come in an ending many scholars think was added later. Where the Gospel and modern knowledge conflict, most people follow modern knowledge: in 9:14-29 a spirit throws the boy to the ground (9:18) and Jesus drives it out (9:25-26), where a doctor today would diagnose epilepsy and prescribe treatment. In Britain, law, medicine and public opinion, not a first-century text, decide how schools, hospitals and courts behave, and a humanist would say that people can be generous to the excluded without needing Jesus to have told them to be.\n\nThe case against the statement is also strong. For Christians the Gospel's authority does not rest on it being a modern report. Catholic teaching reads Scripture within the tradition and under the teaching authority of the Church, so a difficult ending does not unsettle the whole; the Church of England holds that Scripture contains all things necessary to salvation; and in almost every denomination a passage of the Gospel is read aloud at the Eucharist, with the congregation standing, which is a claim about authority acted out weekly. The effects are visible and not confined to believers: St Christopher's Hospice, opened in 1967 by Dame Cicely Saunders, began the modern hospice movement, and CAFOD, Christian Aid and Tearfund raise and spend money on exactly the people Mark says Jesus went to. A text that changes how millions give, vote and volunteer is exercising authority in any ordinary sense of the word.\n\nThe statement is also ambiguous, and that ambiguity decides the argument. If 'authority' means legal force over everyone in Britain, the statement is plainly true, and few Christians would want it otherwise. If it means the power to shape how people live, the statement is false: it is contradicted every time a parish opens a night shelter because of 1:40-45 or 2:13-17. The fairest conclusion is that the Gospel has no coercive authority over anyone and considerable moral authority over those who accept it - and that the second kind has proved more durable than the critics of the first expected.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["The word doing the work in the statement is 'authority', and it can mean at least two different things.","The strongest argument for the statement is evidence about the text itself rather than an opinion about religion.","For the other side, look for authority that can be seen in practice - what is read aloud, and what gets built or funded."],"codes":["3.2.2.2 Significance — Differing views on the authority of Jesus' teaching as recorded by St Mark","3.2.2.2 Faith and discipleship — The commission and ascension: 16:14–20","3.2.2.2 Significance — The significance and importance for Christians of Jesus' attitudes to those disregarded","3.2.2.2 — The textual studies requirement: the set texts, their interpretation and the authority of the Gospel"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In the parable of the sower, what do the thorns stand for?","a":"The worries of this life, the deceitfulness of wealth and the desire for other things, which choke the word (4:18-19)."},{"q":"What did the father of the epileptic boy say about his own faith?","a":"That he did believe, and he asked Jesus to 'help me overcome my unbelief' (9:24)."},{"q":"Why was Jesus criticised for eating at Levi's house?","a":"Because he ate with tax collectors and sinners; he replied that it is 'not the healthy who need a doctor' (2:17)."},{"q":"What did Jesus say the woman at Bethany had done with the perfume?","a":"A beautiful thing: she had poured it on his body 'to prepare for my burial' (14:8), and it would be told 'in memory of her' (14:9)."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Parable of the sower (4:1-9, 14-20)","back":"Seed falls on the path, rocky ground, thorns and good soil. The seed is the word; the soils are hearers. Thorns are worry, wealth and other desires; good soil yields thirty, sixty or a hundred times.","code":"3.2.2.2 The Kingdom of God — Parable of the sower: 4:1–9, 14–20","open":false},{"front":"The rich man (10:17-27)","back":"He has kept the commandments; Jesus loves him and says 'One thing you lack' - sell everything, give to the poor, follow. He leaves sad. Camel and 'the eye of a needle'; but 'all things are possible with God'.","code":"3.2.2.2 The Kingdom of God — The rich man: 10:17–27","open":false},{"front":"The greatest commandment (12:28-34)","back":"Asked for one, Jesus gives two: the Shema, 'Hear, O Israel', love God with heart, soul, mind and strength; and 'Love your neighbour as yourself'. The teacher agrees; Jesus says he is 'not far from the kingdom of God'.","code":"3.2.2.2 The Kingdom of God — The greatest commandment: 12:28–34","open":false},{"front":"The man with leprosy (1:40-45)","back":"'If you are willing' - Jesus touches him: 'I am willing. Be clean!' He sends him to the priest with Moses' offering and tells him to say nothing. The touch reverses the exclusion of the ritually unclean.","code":"3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The man with leprosy: 1:40–45","open":false},{"front":"The call of Levi (2:13-17)","back":"Jesus calls a tax collector from his booth - 'Follow me' - and eats with tax collectors and sinners. Challenged, he says it is 'not the healthy who need a doctor' and that he came to call sinners.","code":"3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The call of Levi: 2:13–17","open":false},{"front":"The Greek (Syro-Phoenician) woman's daughter (7:24-30)","back":"A Gentile woman begs for her daughter. Jesus speaks of children's bread and dogs; she answers that 'even the dogs under the table eat' the crumbs. 'For such a reply, you may go' - the demon has left.","code":"3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The Greek (Syro-Phoenician) woman's daughter: 7:24–30","open":false},{"front":"The epileptic (demon-possessed) boy (9:14-29)","back":"The disciples cannot drive the spirit out; it throws the boy down. The father says 'if you can do anything'; Jesus answers 'Everything is possible for one who believes'. 'Help me overcome my unbelief!' Jesus drives it out (9:25-26).","open":false,"code":"3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The epileptic (demon-possessed) boy: 9:14–29"},{"front":"The anointing at Bethany (14:1-9)","back":"A woman pours pure nard over Jesus' head; some say it should have been sold for the poor. 'She has done a beautiful thing' - it prepares 'for my burial', and will be told 'in memory of her'.","code":"3.2.2.2 Jesus' relationships with those disregarded by society — The anointing at Bethany: 14:1–9","open":false},{"front":"The call of the first disciples (1:16-20)","back":"Jesus calls Simon and Andrew from their nets by the Sea of Galilee, promising to make them 'fish for people'; they leave their nets at once. James and John leave their father Zebedee in the boat.","open":false,"code":"3.2.2.2 Faith and discipleship — The call of the first disciples: 1:16–20"},{"front":"The woman with a haemorrhage (5:24b-34)","back":"Twelve years of bleeding, her money spent on doctors and she is worse. 'If I just touch his clothes' - Jesus knows power has gone out of him. She tells the whole truth: 'Daughter, your faith has healed you'.","open":false,"code":"3.2.2.2 Faith and discipleship — The woman with a haemorrhage: 5:24b–34"},{"front":"The mission of the Twelve (6:7-13)","back":"Sent out two by two: a staff and sandals only - no bread, bag, money or second shirt. Stay in one house; shake the dust off your feet where unwelcomed. They preach repentance and anoint the sick with oil.","code":"3.2.2.2 Faith and discipleship — The mission of the Twelve: 6:7–13","open":false},{"front":"The cost and rewards of discipleship (8:34-38; 10:28-31)","back":"Deny yourself, 'take up their cross and follow me'; lose your life to save it; what good to gain the world 'yet forfeit their soul'? To Peter: a hundred times now, 'along with persecutions', and eternal life to come.","code":"3.2.2.2 Faith and discipleship — The cost and rewards of discipleship: 8:34–38; 10:28–31","open":false},{"front":"Peter's denials (14:27-31, 66-72)","back":"'You will all fall away'; before the cock crows twice Peter will disown him three times. Peter swears he never will, then denies Jesus to a servant girl and bystanders; the cock crows and 'he broke down and wept'.","code":"3.2.2.2 Faith and discipleship — Peter's denials: 14:27–31, 66–72","open":false},{"front":"The commission and ascension (16:14-20)","back":"Jesus appears to the Eleven and rebukes their lack of faith: 'Go into all the world' and preach the gospel; whoever believes and is baptised will be saved. He is taken up and sits at God's right hand.","open":false,"code":"3.2.2.2 Faith and discipleship — The commission and ascension: 16:14–20"},{"front":"The four ways of understanding the Kingdom of God","back":"A present reality (1:15, 'has come near'); a future hope (the harvest, 4:29); a personal inner state (received 'like a little child', 10:15); a community (the mustard seed's shade, 4:32).","code":"3.2.2.2 Significance — Different ways in which the Kingdom of God might be understood","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 4 (Evaluate: 'The kingdom of God is a future hope, not a present reality.')","items":["There is real force in reading the kingdom as future. The parable of the growing seed ends at harvest: when the grain is ripe the man puts the sickle to it 'because the harvest has come' (4:29), and harvest in the prophets is a standing image of judgement, so the parable points forward to a kingdom that is completed rather than one already finished. The mustard seed works the same way, since the whole comparison is with what the smallest of seeds becomes (4:31-32). Against this, Mark opens the ministry with Jesus saying the kingdom 'has come near' (1:15), and he tells a living man that he is 'not far from the kingdom of God' (12:34). The better reading is therefore that Mark holds both together: the kingdom is genuinely present wherever people are healed, forgiven and welcomed, and still awaits its harvest - which is exactly why Christians pray for the kingdom to come while claiming already to live in it.","Why this is Level 4: it is a well-argued response with reasoned consideration of different points of view, a logical chain of reasoning on each side, and a judgement supported by the evidence, with references to religion applied to the issue - four Gospel references, each used to make a point rather than decorate one. The mini-judgement at the end does not say that both sides have a point; it says which reading the Gospel supports and why."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 2 (the same statement, visibly weaker)","items":["I think the kingdom of God is in the future because Jesus talks about a harvest at the end of the parable of the growing seed, and a harvest is something that happens later. That is a good reason to say the kingdom has not arrived yet. Some Christians disagree and say the kingdom is already here because Jesus did miracles and healed people. So there are two different points of view about it.","Why this is only Level 2: it reaches 'Recognition of different points of view, each supported by relevant reasons/evidence', which is the Level 2 descriptor, and there is one chain of reasoning about the harvest. What it misses is the Level 3 and 4 requirement of logical chains of reasoning on both sides drawing on knowledge of relevant evidence, and the Level 4 judgement: the second view is asserted with no passage behind it, no reference is given by chapter and verse, and the paragraph stops without deciding anything. Add 1:15 and 12:34 to the second view and end with a reasoned verdict and the same material reaches Level 4."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Kingdom of God - the six set passages with chapter and verse","items":["Parable of the sower, Mark 4:1-9 and 4:14-20: path, rocky ground, thorns, good soil; 'The farmer sows the word' (4:14); thorns are 'the worries of this life', 'the deceitfulness of wealth' and desires for other things (4:19); good soil yields 'thirty, sixty or a hundred times' (4:20).","Parable of the growing seed, Mark 4:26-29: the man sleeps and rises night and day; the seed grows while 'he does not know how' (4:27); stalk, then head, then full kernel; the sickle is put in 'because the harvest has come' (4:29).","Parable of the mustard seed, Mark 4:30-32: 'the smallest of all seeds' (4:31) becomes the largest of garden plants, with branches where the birds can perch in its shade (4:32).","Jesus and the children, Mark 10:13-16: the disciples rebuke those bringing children; 'Let the little children come to me' (10:14); the kingdom must be received 'like a little child' (10:15); he takes them in his arms and blesses them (10:16).","The rich man, Mark 10:17-27: 'Good teacher' (10:17); the commandments kept since boyhood (10:20); Jesus loves him and says 'One thing you lack' (10:21); 'the eye of a needle' (10:25); 'all things are possible with God' (10:27).","The greatest commandment, Mark 12:28-34: the Shema, 'Hear, O Israel' (12:29), love God with all your heart, soul, mind and strength (12:30); 'Love your neighbour as yourself' (12:31); more than all burnt offerings (12:33); 'not far from the kingdom of God' (12:34).","The four readings the specification names: present reality, future hope, personal inner state, community. Anchor verses: 1:15, 4:29, 10:15 and 4:32."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Jesus and those disregarded by society - the six set passages with chapter and verse","items":["The man with leprosy, Mark 1:40-45: 'If you are willing' (1:40); Jesus is moved with compassion, reaches out his hand and touches him, 'I am willing. Be clean!' (1:41); he is sent to the priest with the offering Moses commanded (1:44); he talks freely, so Jesus can no longer enter a town openly (1:45). Background: a serious skin disease made a person ritually unclean under the law of Leviticus and required them to live apart.","The call of Levi, Mark 2:13-17: Levi son of Alphaeus is called from the tax collector's booth with 'Follow me' (2:14); Jesus eats at his house with many tax collectors and sinners (2:15); 'not the healthy who need a doctor' (2:17), and he came to call sinners, not the righteous. Background: tax collectors gathered money for the occupying Roman power.","The Greek (Syro-Phoenician) woman's daughter, Mark 7:24-30: in the region of Tyre; she is Greek, Syro-Phoenician by birth (7:26); the children's bread and the dogs (7:27); 'even the dogs under the table eat' the crumbs (7:28); 'For such a reply, you may go' (7:29); the daughter is found lying on the bed, free of the demon (7:30).","The epileptic (demon-possessed) boy, Mark 9:14-29: the disciples cannot drive it out; 'if you can do anything' (9:22); 'Everything is possible for one who believes' (9:23); 'help me overcome my unbelief' (9:24); Jesus takes the boy by the hand and lifts him up (9:27); 'only by prayer' (9:29).","The widow at the treasury, Mark 12:41-44: Jesus sits opposite the treasury and watches; the rich throw in large amounts; the widow puts in two very small copper coins (12:42); she has put in more than all the others, 'all she had to live on' (12:44). It follows his warning about those who devour widows' houses (12:40).","The anointing at Bethany, Mark 14:1-9: two days before the Passover, in the house of Simon the Leper (14:1-3); an alabaster jar of pure nard poured over his head; worth more than a year's wages (14:5); 'She has done a beautiful thing' (14:6); 'to prepare for my burial' (14:8); told 'in memory of her' wherever the gospel is preached (14:9). Judas goes to the chief priests immediately afterwards (14:10).","The influence today: St Christopher's Hospice, opened in 1967 by Dame Cicely Saunders, a Christian, began the modern hospice movement; the Missionaries of Charity, founded by Mother Teresa in 1950, care for the dying poor; CAFOD is the Catholic aid agency for England and Wales; Christian Aid and Tearfund work with the poorest overseas; parishes run food banks, night shelters and street pastor schemes."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: faith and discipleship - the six set passages with chapter and verse","items":["The call of the first disciples, Mark 1:16-20: Simon and Andrew casting a net by the Sea of Galilee; 'fish for people' (1:17); 'at once they left their nets' (1:18); James and John leave their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men (1:20).","The woman with a haemorrhage, Mark 5:24b-34: twelve years of bleeding, all her money spent on doctors and she is worse (5:25-26); 'If I just touch his clothes' (5:28); Jesus knows power has gone out of him (5:30); she tells him the whole truth (5:33); 'Daughter, your faith has healed you' and 'Go in peace' (5:34).","The mission of the Twelve, Mark 6:7-13: sent out two by two with authority over impure spirits (6:7); nothing but a staff - no bread, no bag, no money in their belts (6:8); sandals but not an extra shirt (6:9); stay in one house (6:10); shake the dust off your feet (6:11); they preach repentance, drive out demons and anoint the sick with oil (6:12-13).","The cost and rewards of discipleship, Mark 8:34-38 and 10:28-31: 'take up their cross and follow me' (8:34); save your life and lose it, lose it for Jesus and the gospel and save it (8:35); 'yet forfeit their soul' (8:36); 'We have left everything to follow you!' (10:28); a hundred times as much in this age 'along with persecutions' (10:30) and eternal life in the age to come; 'many who are first will be last' (10:31).","Peter's denials, Mark 14:27-31 and 14:66-72: 'You will all fall away' (14:27); before the cock crows twice, three denials (14:30); 'I will never disown you' (14:31); the servant girl and the bystanders (14:66-70); curses and swearing (14:71); the cock crows a second time and 'he broke down and wept' (14:72). At the tomb the young man says to 'tell his disciples and Peter' (16:7).","The commission and ascension, Mark 16:14-20: Jesus appears to the Eleven as they are eating and rebukes their lack of faith (16:14); 'Go into all the world' and 'preach the gospel to all creation' (16:15); whoever believes and is baptised will be saved (16:16); signs will accompany believers (16:17-18); he is taken up into heaven and sits at the right hand of God (16:19); they preach everywhere, the Lord working with them (16:20).","Other faith passages Mark supplies for comparison: 'When Jesus saw their faith' (2:5), the four men who dig through the roof; Bartimaeus, told 'your faith has healed you' (10:52); Nazareth, where Jesus is amazed at 'their lack of faith' (6:6); the request of James and John, answered with the Son of Man coming 'to serve, and to give his life' (10:45)."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the authority of the Gospel, diversity among Christians, and non-religious views","items":["The specification's own question for this route: how far Christian and non-religious communities give authority to St Mark's Gospel, especially in relation to other sources of contemporary authority - law, science, the state and public opinion.","Literalist and fundamentalist Christians: the Gospel is directly inspired by God and binding as it stands, so its account of exorcisms and of the resurrection is accepted as fact.","Catholic teaching: Scripture is read within the tradition of the Church and under the teaching authority of the Magisterium, so an individual verse is never interpreted on its own; a Gospel passage is read at every Mass.","Church of England: the Thirty-Nine Articles hold that Scripture contains all things necessary to salvation, so the Gospel is authoritative for salvation without settling every scientific or historical question.","Liberal Christians: some passages carry spiritual truth rather than reporting fact, and the exorcisms in particular are read as a first-century description of illness such as the epilepsy in 9:14-29.","Textual evidence a non-religious critic will use: Mark was written decades after the events, and the earliest surviving manuscripts end at 16:8, so the commission and ascension of 16:14-20 come in an ending widely regarded as added later.","Humanist and atheist arguments: morality does not need a divine source, and the compassion Mark records can be practised without believing any of its claims; in Britain, law, medicine and public opinion, not a first-century text, govern schools, hospitals and courts.","Evidence of authority in practice, for the other side: the Gospel is read aloud at the Eucharist with the congregation standing; Christian charities such as CAFOD, Christian Aid and Tearfund raise and spend money on exactly the people Mark says Jesus went to; the modern hospice movement began with a Christian, Dame Cicely Saunders, at St Christopher's Hospice in 1967."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan - 'Christians today should give away their wealth, as Jesus told the rich man to.' Evaluate this statement.","items":["Decode the statement: it does not ask whether Jesus told the rich man to sell everything - the text settles that (10:21). It asks whether a command given to one man is a rule for all Christians now. The word doing the work is 'should', and the argument is about how a particular command becomes a general duty.","Paragraph 1, in support: the command itself. Jesus looks at him, loves him, and says 'One thing you lack' (10:21) - sell everything, give to the poor, come and follow. The man goes away sad because he had great wealth, so the command is the one thing he cannot do.","Paragraph 2, in support and stronger: Jesus generalises. It is easier for a camel to go through 'the eye of a needle' (10:25) than for a rich person to enter the kingdom. Support from elsewhere in Mark: the Twelve take no money in their belts (6:8) and the widow is praised for giving 'all she had to live on' (12:44). Religious orders take vows of poverty on this basis.","Paragraph 3, against: the command is addressed to one man and is not repeated to Levi, to the fishermen's families or to the women who supported Jesus. When the disciples ask who can be saved, the answer is not a rule about selling but 'all things are possible with God' (10:27) - salvation comes from God, not from a transaction.","Paragraph 4, against and practical: at Bethany Jesus defends a woman who pours out perfume worth more than a year's wages instead of selling it for the poor - 'She has done a beautiful thing' (14:6). A church with no money runs no food bank, and CAFOD, Christian Aid and Tearfund depend on Christians who earn and give.","Non-religious arguments, which the bullets allow: some argue that giving away capital reduces the good a person can do over a lifetime; others argue that extreme inequality is itself the moral problem, so redistribution is right with or without religion.","The evaluation thread to keep running: is wealth dangerous in Mark, or neutral? Every paragraph should feed that question, because the conclusion turns on it - 'the deceitfulness of wealth' chokes the seed in 4:19, which tells against treating money as morally neutral.","The conclusion must decide and say why: the statement over-generalises a command made to one man, but the soft answer concedes too little. Mark treats wealth as a spiritual danger, so the defensible position is that Christians are not all obliged to give everything away, yet each is obliged to identify what they would refuse to give up - which is what kept the rich man out.","Timing: about twelve minutes, plus the three SPaG marks, so leave a minute to check spelling and use the theme's own vocabulary - kingdom, discipleship, sacrificial giving, parable - accurately."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"The shape of a Theme H answer in one place","items":["Always name the passage. 'Mark 1:40-45' costs four characters more than 'the Bible' and is the difference between a reference mark awarded and withheld.","Answer every set passage for three audiences: what it meant for Jesus, for the people of his day, and for people in the 21st century.","On a contrasting question, make the two views genuinely oppose each other, because if similar beliefs are given only one of them can be credited.","On an influence question, say what Christians actually do because of the passage - a belief with no consequence attached earns the simple mark and not the detailed one.","On the evaluation, argue from the Gospel on both sides before judging, and let the conclusion say which arguments were stronger and why, rather than that both sides have a point."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T14:16:04Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"All six fixes landed and none broke anything of substance: the three 12-mark stems and examples[4] now print 'should refer to St Mark's Gospel in your answer' exactly as 80622B-QP-JUN23 does, questions[9] carries only the sower code, questions[7].sol gives two genuine contrasts with the Quaker attribution corrected to their testimony to simplicity, questions[15].sol states 9:18 and 9:25-26 correctly, and the four new cards on 9:14-29, 1:16-20, 5:24b-34 and 16:14-20 are accurate against Mark - but the first-disciples card imported a nine-word quotation of 1:17 that should be cut back to 'fish for people'."}}}} };
